[{"id":"features","title":"Feature overview","html":"<p>What <code>@simple-html/grid</code> can do, at a glance: a table per area, each feature in a line, with a link to\nthe guide that explains it and a live sample to try it in. The <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">Full grid</a> sample has\nmost of it in one grid: 50 columns, 5 000 rows. New to the grid? Start with\n<a href=\"#guide/getting-started\">Getting started</a>.</p>\n<p>For the people who use a grid there is the <a href=\"#user-guide/getting-started\">user guide</a>: what to click, by\nkind of action, with a live grid to try it on each page.</p>\n<p>On a Mac, Ctrl is Cmd and Alt is Option.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-and-setup\">Data and setup</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>No framework needed</td>\n<td>Plain TypeScript or JavaScript: <code>createGrid()</code> puts a grid in any element of the page. Its only dependency is lit-html</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/getting-started/a-first-grid\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/basic-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typed fields</td>\n<td>Text, number, date, date and time, and yes/no fields: each sorts, filters, aligns and is edited as its type</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/datasource/fields-and-types\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/basic-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Config as plain data</td>\n<td>The columns, the options and the fields are JSON: store them in a backend. What has to be code is a hook beside them</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many rows</td>\n<td>Only the rows on screen are drawn, so thousands of rows scroll fast</td>\n<td><a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load and add rows</td>\n<td>Replace the rows, add more at the end, or merge what a server sends</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/datasource/data-in-setdata\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/rows-data\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keys</td>\n<td>A key field ties the selection and the edits to the row, also when fresh rows come from the server</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/datasource/keys\">guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The current row</td>\n<td>The row the user is on, for a panel or form beside the grid</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/datasource/the-current-row\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/detail-form\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two grids, one set of rows</td>\n<td>Two grids share their rows, each with its own filter, sort and selection</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/datasource/sharing-the-rows-between-two-datasources\">guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hooks</td>\n<td>Your code for what data can not say: readonly cells and css classes from the row, changes to the menus</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/cell-styling\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Events</td>\n<td>Know when rows change, are sorted, filtered or selected, and when cells are clicked or focused</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/events\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/events\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-columns-and-layout\">Columns and layout</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Pinned columns</td>\n<td>Columns pinned left or right stay in place while the rest scroll sideways</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/columns-and-layout\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/column-layout\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stacked fields</td>\n<td>One column shows several fields, one above the other</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/columns-and-layout\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/column-layout\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Move, resize, pin, hide</td>\n<td>Drag a label to move a column, drag its edge to resize it; pin and hide it from the header menu</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/column-layout\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Column chooser</td>\n<td>Every column in a list with a search: drag one into the header to show it, rename it</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/the-column-chooser\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/column-layout\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fit to the content</td>\n<td>Columns as wide as their text and rows as tall as their lines, like Excel&#39;s AutoFit - or a double click on a column&#39;s edge</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-widths-and-row-heights\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/multiline\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sizes and parts</td>\n<td>Row and header heights; hide the labels, the filters, the column numbers or the footer</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/grid-options\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/misc-options\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-display-and-formatting\">Display and formatting</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Formatters</td>\n<td>Numbers with a decimal point or comma, dates as <code>2026-11-22</code> or <code>22.11.2026</code>: how values are shown, and how what is typed is read</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-built-in-formatters\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/formatters\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A formatter of your own</td>\n<td>Show and read a field your way - a country in upper case, a phone number without spaces - registered once by a name</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-formatter-of-your-own\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/custom-formatter\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Display formats</td>\n<td>Show a date as Norwegian, US, a week or the days until; a number with decimals and a thousands separator. The value stays as it is</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Templates and links</td>\n<td>A text column shows a template of fields - <code>{name} ({id})</code> - and links to a web address, an email or a phone number</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colour rules</td>\n<td>Colour a cell from its value - a negative balance in red, waiting orders on orange - from 14 colours or any colour picked; or from other fields of the row, or a formula. No code</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data bars</td>\n<td>A bar in a number cell as long as the value&#39;s place among the column&#39;s numbers, negative values left of 0, as Excel&#39;s</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/data-bars\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colour scales</td>\n<td>A heat map: the cell&#39;s background from red to yellow to green - two or three colours - by the value</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-scales\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon sets</td>\n<td>Arrows, traffic lights, flags or ✓ ! ✕ before the value by thresholds, said in words to a screen reader</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/icon-sets\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calculated columns</td>\n<td>A column computed from other fields with a safe formula - <code>{price} * {qty}</code>, <code>IF(...)</code>, <code>DAYS({due}, TODAY())</code> - sorted, filtered, grouped and exported as any column, never saved</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/calculated-columns\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labels and placeholders</td>\n<td>Rename a column; show a hint in an empty cell or an empty header filter</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/label-placeholder-blank--0-checkboxes-multiline-read-only\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/required-placeholders\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checkboxes</td>\n<td>Yes/no fields are checkboxes; so can a column of <code>1</code> / <code>0</code> or <code>Y</code> / <code>N</code> be</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/checkboxes\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiline text</td>\n<td>Notes and addresses: the first line in the cell, more lines in its tooltip and in a bigger editor</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/multiline\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blank or 0</td>\n<td>Show 0 as an empty cell, or an empty cell as 0</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/showing-0-or-an-empty-cell-numberoverride\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/misc-options\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dim repeated values</td>\n<td>A value the same as in the row above is greyed out, easy to read when sorted</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/grid-options\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/misc-options\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cell and row styling</td>\n<td>Css classes on cells and rows from their values: a tint, bold, italic</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/cell-styling\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom cells</td>\n<td>Draw cells with your own code - plain DOM, lit-html, React, Solid: a badge, a progress bar, a select</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/custom-cells\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/custom-cells\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A type per row</td>\n<td>One column whose cells are text, a number, a date or a checkbox, by another field of the row</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-type-from-another-field\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/dynamic-cell-types\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltips</td>\n<td>On the buttons, validation messages on a cell, the lines of a multiline cell</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/grid-options\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/tooltips\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-filtering\">Filtering</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Header filters</td>\n<td>A filter under each label: type and press Enter. <code>*</code> and <code>_</code> are wildcards</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/header-filters\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checkbox filter</td>\n<td>A yes/no column, or one shown as checkboxes, filters with a checkbox: checked, unchecked, no filter</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/header-filters\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operators</td>\n<td>Equals, contains, begins with, greater than, between, blank ... picked from the symbol in the filter or its menu</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filter-operator-header\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value list</td>\n<td>Tick the values to show, as in a spreadsheet, each with its count. A search narrows the list, or filters on the text</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-value-list\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Range block</td>\n<td>For numbers and dates: an operator and a value, or from and to, in one go</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-block\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date and range pickers</td>\n<td>A calendar for a date filter; two months side by side for a range</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-picker\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quick dates</td>\n<td>One click for Today, This week, Last month, This quarter, Last 30 days, Overdue ... - kept as &quot;this month&quot;, so a saved view still means this month next month</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter as shown</td>\n<td>Type the filter as the cells show it: <code>2026-W47</code> is that week, <code>1 234,5</code> that number</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-shown\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter on the text you see</td>\n<td>A column with a template or a link filters on the text its cells show - <code>*(12)*</code> finds <code>Acme Corp (12)</code> - and its value list lists those texts</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/what-a-template-and-a-link-change\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter as you type</td>\n<td>Filters after a short pause, no Enter needed - per column, turned on by the user or you</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filter-operator-header\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Match case</td>\n<td><code>Oslo</code> matches <code>Oslo</code>, not <code>OSLO</code>: upper and lower case as typed</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/match-case\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter on a cell</td>\n<td>Right click a cell: filter on its value, or add it to the filter there is</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/filters-from-the-row-menu\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/context-menu\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter editor</td>\n<td>A dialog for any filter: AND and OR groups, nested, with undo and redo</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-editor\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter in the footer</td>\n<td>The row count and the filter as chips: click them to edit, ✕ to clear</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-footer\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changed rows only</td>\n<td>Show only the new, edited and deleted rows, to check them before saving</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/changed-rows-only\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/editing\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filters from code</td>\n<td><code>where()</code>, <code>and()</code>, <code>or()</code>: plain data you can store, or send to a server</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/filters-from-code\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The filter as text</td>\n<td>One line that says what the rows are filtered by</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-as-text\">guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-sorting-and-grouping\">Sorting and grouping</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Sort</td>\n<td>Click a label; again for the other way; Shift+click to sort on one more column</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/sorting\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sorting-grouping\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sort on the text you see</td>\n<td>A column with a template sorts on its text - <code>{last}, {first}</code> by the last name; grouped, on its value</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/sorting\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group</td>\n<td>Drag a label to the panel above the header; group in groups, drag the chips to reorder them</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/grouping\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sorting-grouping\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expand and collapse</td>\n<td>Each group, or all of them from the header menu</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/grouping\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sorting-grouping\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aggregates</td>\n<td>Sum, average, min, max or count of a column, in every group row</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/aggregates/in-the-group-rows\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/aggregates\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Totals row</td>\n<td>A row under the rows with the aggregates of every row the filter lets through</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/aggregates/the-totals-row\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/aggregates\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aggregates from the menu</td>\n<td>The user turns each aggregate and the totals row on and off in the header menu</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/aggregates/from-the-header-menu\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/aggregates\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Select rows</td>\n<td>Click a row number, Ctrl+click for one more, Shift+click for the rows between; the corner selects all. Many, one or none</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/selecting-rows\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/selection\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Select columns</td>\n<td>Click the column numbers under the filters: Ctrl+click and Shift+click as for rows</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/selecting-columns\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ranges of cells</td>\n<td>Drag over cells, Shift+click to extend, Ctrl+click for one more range - or Alt+Shift+arrows</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/ranges-of-cells\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Summary in the footer</td>\n<td>Count, sum, average, min and max of the selected cells, as a spreadsheet&#39;s status bar</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-summary-in-the-footer\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy</td>\n<td>Ctrl+C copies the cells with their column labels: a table in Excel, Google Sheets and Word</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/copy\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paste</td>\n<td>Ctrl+V pastes cells copied in Excel or in the grid; one value fills a whole range</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/paste\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fill down</td>\n<td>Ctrl+D, or drag the small square at the corner of a range, as in a spreadsheet</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/fill-down\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clear</td>\n<td>Delete empties every cell of the ranges, readonly cells skipped</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/delete\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-editing\">Editing</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Edit in the cells</td>\n<td>Turn editing on and type: what is typed is read as the field&#39;s type, a number or a date</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/how-the-user-edits\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/editing\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Editors</td>\n<td>A date picker, checkboxes, a bigger editor for text of several lines</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/how-the-user-edits\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lookups</td>\n<td>Pick a value from a list with a search: store a code, show its name, copy more fields from the row picked</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/lookups\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/lookups\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dropdown grid</td>\n<td>Pick from a grid of its own under the cell - several columns, its filters - or just type to search it</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/lookups/the-dropdown-grid\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/dropdown-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Validation</td>\n<td>Rules per field: required, min, max (a number or a day), length, a pattern, a whole number, decimals, one of a list, an email, unique (one field or several together), or your own. The error shows on the cell and its row</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/validation\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rules of a grid&#39;s columns</td>\n<td><code>validate</code> of a column in the grid&#39;s config: added to the field&#39;s, set by code - the user can not change them, the grid config dialog shows them read only</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/rules-of-a-grids-columns\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/validation\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Required fields</td>\n<td>Empty cells that must be filled in are striped until they have a value</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/required-fields-and-placeholders\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/required-placeholders\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undo and redo</td>\n<td>Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y for typing, paste, clear, fill and delete; the grid goes to what changed</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/editing\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Put a value back</td>\n<td>Click the marker of an edited cell for its original value, or undo the edits of a row</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/editing\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change tracking</td>\n<td>New, edited and deleted rows are shown on the row number, edited cells get a marker</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/change-tracking\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/editing\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New and deleted rows</td>\n<td>Add empty rows; a delete marks the row, so it can be undone until it is saved</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/new-rows\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/rows-data\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saving</td>\n<td>Collect the new, edited and deleted rows for the server in one call, then take in the ids it gives</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/saving\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Errors from the server</td>\n<td>Show a server&#39;s message on the rows it refused</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/errors-from-a-server\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/validation\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Edit permissions</td>\n<td>Allow adding, changing and deleting rows each on its own: add only, say</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/edit-permissions\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Readonly columns and rows</td>\n<td>A column readonly, or cells readonly from their row with a hook</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/cell-styling\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-find\">Find</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Find in the grid</td>\n<td>Ctrl+F opens a find bar: every cell that shows the text is marked, with a count; Enter goes to the next</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/find/the-keys\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/find\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Match case</td>\n<td>Find the text with upper and lower case as typed</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/find/match-case\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/find\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter rows</td>\n<td>Show only the rows with a match</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/find/filter-rows\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/find\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rows loaded later</td>\n<td>Filter rows filters the rows a backend sends after it was turned on, in the browser</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/find/filter-rows-and-a-backend\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/find\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-detail-form\">Detail form</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>The row as a form</td>\n<td>A panel at the right of the rows: a label and a value for each field picked - also fields that are no column, and long notes in full</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/detail-form\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Edit in the form</td>\n<td>The same rules, validation and undo as the cells</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/detail-form\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resize and collapse</td>\n<td>Drag its edge, fold it to a strip; on a narrow screen it goes under the rows</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/detail-form\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-grid-config-dialog\">Grid config dialog</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Column settings</td>\n<td>The gear in the footer: per column its label, display format, template and link, checkbox, multiline, read only, placeholder and colour rules</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fits a small screen</td>\n<td>Starts within 80 % of the window, scrolls inside, resized from its edges; on a phone the columns and a column&#39;s settings take turns. Colour rules fold, the colours are panels, one open at a time</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insert a function</td>\n<td>The <strong>ƒ</strong> button by a formula: the functions in a menu by kind, each with a tooltip of what it does</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns/in-the-grid-config-dialog\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/calculated-columns\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detail form settings</td>\n<td>The fields of the detail form and their order, its width</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/detail-form\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kept with the state</td>\n<td>What the user sets there is saved and restored with the rest of the state</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/state/display-formats-in-the-state\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-menus\">Menus</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Row menu</td>\n<td>Right click a cell or a row number: undo, select, copy, paste, clear, delete, filter on the value, sizes, export</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/row-menu-menu_idsrow\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/context-menu\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Header menu</td>\n<td>Right click a label: pin, hide, remove the sort, the column chooser, sizes, display format, aggregates, grouping</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/header-menu-menu_idsheader\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/context-menu\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter menu</td>\n<td>Right click a header filter: the operator, blank, match case, the value list or range block, the filter editor</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/filter-menu-menu_idsfilter\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/filtering\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Column number menu</td>\n<td>Right click a column number: select columns, copy them or their labels</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/column-menu-menu_idscolumn\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change the menus</td>\n<td>A hook hides, reorders, renames or adds entries</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/changing-the-grids-menus\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/context-menu\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Menus of your own</td>\n<td>Open a menu in the same style, with submenus - or a grid in it, to pick from</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/a-menu-of-your-own-openmenu-and-closemenu\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/context-menu\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Submenus and keys</td>\n<td>Submenus open beside their item; arrows, Enter and Esc work in every menu</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/the-keyboard\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/context-menu\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-export\">Export</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Excel</td>\n<td>An .xlsx file of real numbers and dates, the header row frozen with Excel&#39;s filter, the columns as wide as in the grid</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/export/excel\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/export\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colours in Excel</td>\n<td>The colour rules as the cells&#39; colours, the data bars, colour scales and icon sets as Excel&#39;s own conditional formatting, the number formats too</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/export/colours\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CSV</td>\n<td>Text for any program: the values as shown or as they are, a comma, a semicolon or a tab between them</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/export/csv\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/export\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe for spreadsheets</td>\n<td>Text starting with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code> or <code>@</code> is written so a spreadsheet does not run it as a formula</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/export/formulas-csv-injection\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/export\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>From the menu</td>\n<td>Right click a cell: Export › Excel, all rows or the selected rows</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/export/from-the-row-menu\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/export\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What you see</td>\n<td>The rows the filter lets through, sorted, the columns in their order - or the selected rows</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/export/the-rows\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/export\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Import</td>\n<td>Rows pasted from Excel, or a CSV or Excel file, become new rows: see where each column goes, what is wrong, then import - one undo step</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/import\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/import\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Import a file from code</td>\n<td><code>importFile(file)</code>: an Excel workbook (its first sheet) or a CSV file into new rows, no library of your own</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/import/from-code\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/import\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-state\">State</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Save and restore</td>\n<td>What the user changed - column order and widths, sorting, grouping, filters, formats, aggregates, the detail form - as plain data to store and give back</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/state/getstate-setstate-and-resetstate\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/grid-state\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Start and reset</td>\n<td>The state a grid starts in, and a reset back to it</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/state/the-state-the-grid-starts-in\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/grid-state\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Named views</td>\n<td>&quot;My open orders&quot;, &quot;Late deliveries&quot;: save views of the grid and switch with one click in the footer - Save, Save as, Revert, Rename, Delete, a default view; shared views of yours; kept in the browser or your backend</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/state/views\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/views\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-live-data\">Live data</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Merge rows from a server</td>\n<td>Update, add and remove rows by key; the selection, open groups and scroll position stay</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/live-data/merging-rows-by-key-upsert\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/live-data\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changes flash</td>\n<td>A cell a merge changed flashes once</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/live-data/how-the-grid-shows-them\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/live-data\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The user&#39;s edits are kept</td>\n<td>A merge never overwrites an edit not yet saved; it reports the clash instead</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/live-data/conflicts\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/live-data\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No refresh needed</td>\n<td>A value set from code shows in the grid on its own</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/live-data/values-show-on-their-own\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/live-data\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-theming\">Theming</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Two themes</td>\n<td>The default theme and TreDeSpace, each light and dark: four looks</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/theming/themes\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/theming\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dark mode</td>\n<td>The grid follows the page&#39;s colour scheme: one line of css</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/theming/dark-mode\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/theming\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colours of your own</td>\n<td>Every colour is a css variable: change the ones you want</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/theming/a-theme-of-your-own\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/theming\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>On the docs site the sidebar&#39;s <em>Grid theme</em> and <em>Toggle dark/light</em> switch every grid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-texts-and-translation\">Texts and translation</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Every text translatable</td>\n<td>Menus, dialogs, footer, tooltips, date picker, validation messages and what a screen reader reads</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/texts/translating\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/translation\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The page or one grid</td>\n<td>Texts for every grid of the page, or for one grid in its config</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/texts/where-a-text-comes-from\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/translation\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names of months and days</td>\n<td>Dates in a display format follow the language set</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-language-of-the-names\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/translation\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Messages of your own</td>\n<td>Your validation messages are texts too, translated like the rest</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/texts/messages-of-your-own\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/validation\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-accessibility\">Accessibility</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Screen readers</td>\n<td>A grid of rows, cells and column headers, as WAI-ARIA describes it - a tree grid when grouped; the sort, the selection and the row state are read out</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/accessibility/what-a-screen-reader-gets\">guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The keyboard</td>\n<td>Cells, filters, headers, menus, dialogs and the find bar all work from the keyboard</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Focus</td>\n<td>A visible focus ring, and Tab goes through the grid as it is shown</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/accessibility/focus\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/theming\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shortcuts help</td>\n<td><code>?</code> (where it types nothing) or Ctrl+/ shows every key of the grid, grouped and searchable - as a Mac writes them on a Mac</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-touch\">Touch</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Swipe</td>\n<td>Scroll the rows or the columns with a finger; the swipe coasts on</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/getting-started/touch\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long press</td>\n<td>Holding a finger still opens the menu a right click opens</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/menus/with-a-finger\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/full-grid\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Select with a finger</td>\n<td>Tap row or column numbers; <em>Select ›</em> in the menus does what Shift and Ctrl do</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/ranges\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Move columns</td>\n<td>Hold a label a moment, then drag it</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/column-layout\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-sql-helpers\">SQL helpers</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>See</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>A WHERE from the filter</td>\n<td><code>toSqlWhere()</code> turns the grid&#39;s filter into the WHERE of a query and its parameters, for a server that loads only the rows asked for</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/a-where-from-the-filter\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Four databases</td>\n<td>PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQLite and SQL Server</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/the-dialects\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe from SQL injection</td>\n<td>Values are always parameters, the columns a list you give, names checked and quoted, the size limited</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/security\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The same rows as the grid</td>\n<td>Case, blanks, dates and time zones compare as the grid compares them</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/how-each-type-compares\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calculated columns in SQL</td>\n<td>A formula as an SQL expression of the database - <code>LEFT({name}, 2)</code>, <code>DAYS(...)</code>, <code>IF(...)</code> - so a filter on a calculated column runs on the server too</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/calculated-columns\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A column that keeps its case</td>\n<td><code>caseSensitive</code> of a field, set by code: Match case always on, <code>toSqlWhere()</code> compares with the case - an index on the column is used</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/a-column-that-keeps-its-case-casesensitive\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A LOB column as text</td>\n<td><code>clobType</code> of a field: an Oracle <code>CLOB</code> filtered with <code>LIKE</code> only - Equal, Contains, Begins with ... no value list, no formula</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/a-lob-column-clobtype\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Templates in SQL</td>\n<td>A column filtered on the text its template shows, as an SQL expression: <code>getShownFormulas()</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/a-column-filtered-on-its-template\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The filter to send</td>\n<td><code>getServerFilter()</code>: the filter without what the backend can not run - Find&#39;s rows, fields it does not have, calculated columns unless kept</td>\n<td><a href=\"#guide/sql/the-filter-to-send-getserverfilter\">guide</a> · <a href=\"#sample/sql\">sample</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-keyboard-shortcuts\">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>\n<p>The ones used most - <code>?</code> or Ctrl+/ in the grid shows them all. Every key, by where the focus is: <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard\">The keyboard</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Keys</th>\n<th>What they do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the next / previous cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>in a cell that can be edited: the cell below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+arrows</td>\n<td>the cell beside, above or below - the plain arrows move the caret in the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End</td>\n<td>the first / last cell of the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Home / Alt+End</td>\n<td>the first / last cell of the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PageUp / PageDown</td>\n<td>a page of rows up / down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+ArrowUp / Ctrl+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the edge of the data in the column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+arrows</td>\n<td>a range of cells; Shift with the keys above moves its end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V</td>\n<td>copy / paste cells, to and from Excel too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete</td>\n<td>empties the cells of the ranges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+D</td>\n<td>fill down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y</td>\n<td>undo / redo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+F, F3 / Shift+F3</td>\n<td>find, the next / previous match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter, F2</td>\n<td>in a multiline cell: its editor, where Ctrl+Enter saves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowDown, F4</td>\n<td>in a lookup cell: its list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F4</td>\n<td>in a header filter: pick the operator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter / Space on a label</td>\n<td>sort; with Shift, sort on one more column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the menu of the cell, label, header filter or column number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes a menu; clears the ranges</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},{"id":"getting-started","title":"Getting started","html":"<p><code>@simple-html/grid</code> is a data grid for plain TypeScript or JavaScript apps, no framework needed. Its\nonly dependency is lit-html, which is installed with it. This page installs it and puts a first grid\non a page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-install\">Install</h2>\n<p>The package is not on npmjs.com. Every release has it as a tgz on its\n<a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/releases\">GitHub Release</a>: install it from its address, no\ntoken and no alias - the imports are <code>@simple-html/grid</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>npm install https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/releases/download/v6.0.0/simple-html-grid-6.0.0.tgz</code></pre>\n<p>Or download the tgz into your project and install the file: <code>npm install ./libs/simple-html-grid-6.0.0.tgz</code>.</p>\n<p>It is also on GitHub Packages as\n<a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/pkgs/npm/simple-html-grid\"><code>@vegarringdal/simple-html-grid</code></a>:\ninstall it under its usual name with an npm alias, so the imports stay <code>@simple-html/grid</code>. GitHub\nPackages needs a token that may read packages (a personal access token with <code>read:packages</code>), in an\n<code>.npmrc</code> next to your package.json:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ini\"><code>@vegarringdal:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com\n//npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>npm install @simple-html/grid@npm:@vegarringdal/simple-html-grid</code></pre>\n<p>Or install it from a git branch: the <code>prepare</code> script builds <code>dist</code> when it is installed.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"json\"><code>&quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;@simple-html/grid&quot;: &quot;github:vegarringdal/simple-html#&lt;branch&gt;&quot;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Or make a tgz in a clone of the repo with <code>npm run build:tgz</code>, copy it from <code>build/</code> into your\nproject and point to the file:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"json\"><code>&quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;@simple-html/grid&quot;: &quot;file:./libs/simple-html-grid-X.Y.Z.tgz&quot;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-css\">Css</h2>\n<p>The grid needs its stylesheet. Date cells and date header filters open a date picker, which has a\nstylesheet of its own. Both are imported from the package root:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/date.css'; // the date picker</code></pre>\n<p>A bundler such as esbuild or Vite handles the css import. Without one, link the files from\n<code>node_modules/@simple-html/grid/dist/</code> in your html.</p>\n<p>The grid is light, or dark with <code>color-scheme: dark</code> on the page. A second theme comes with it, the\ncolours of the TreDeSpace UI widget library: <code>import &#39;@simple-html/grid/theme-tredespace.css&#39;</code> after\nthe two, and <code>data-grid-theme=&quot;tredespace&quot;</code> on the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, see\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/themes\">Themes</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-first-grid\">A first grid</h2>\n<p>A grid needs two things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <code>Datasource</code>: your rows, and the type of each field that is not text</li>\n<li>a <code>GridConfig</code>: the columns to show and the options of the grid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both take plain data, JSON, so they can be stored. What has to be code - a cell readonly from its\nrow, css classes from the row, changes to the menus - are hooks, given next to them:\n<code>createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks })</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<p><code>createGrid()</code> puts the grid in a container and returns the grid interface, the object you work\nwith the grid through. The grid fills the container, so the container needs a height:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"html\"><code>&lt;div id=&quot;grid&quot; style=&quot;height: 400px&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/date.css';\n\n// the fields that are not text, with their type. A field that is not listed is text\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: {\n        age: 'number',\n        born: { type: 'date', label: 'Born' },\n        active: 'boolean'\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.setData([\n    { id: 1, name: 'Ann', age: 36, born: new Date(1990, 4, 17), active: true },\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', age: 41, born: new Date(1985, 0, 2), active: false },\n    { id: 3, name: 'Cid', age: 25, born: new Date(2001, 11, 24), active: true }\n]);\n\n// layout: where the columns go and how wide they are. columns: the options of a column, by field\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { label: 'Name' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['age'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['born'], width: 110 },\n            { fields: ['active'], width: 80 }\n        ]\n    }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(document.getElementById('grid')!, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"basic-grid\"></div>\n<p>Out of the box the user can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>sort: click a header label, shift+click to sort on one more column</li>\n<li>filter: type in the row under the labels and press Enter, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering\">Filtering</a></li>\n<li>resize a column (drag its right edge) and move it (drag its label) - with a mouse, a pen or a\nfinger, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a></li>\n<li>group: drag a label to the panel above the header, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping\">Sorting and grouping</a></li>\n<li>right click a cell, a label or a header filter for a menu, see <a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a></li>\n<li>all of it on a touch screen too, see <a href=\"#guide/getting-started/touch\">Touch</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The grid is <strong>readonly by default</strong>. Set <code>readonly: false</code> in the config to edit cells, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-touch\">Touch</h2>\n<p>On a phone or a tablet the grid works with a finger, nothing to set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scrolling</strong>: a swipe scrolls the rows, or the columns sideways - locked to the way it started (a\nswipe that is mostly up and down does not drift sideways). Let go while it moves, it coasts on and\nslows down; a touch stops it, and that touch is no tap. The page does not scroll along while the\ngrid scrolls. When the grid is at its edge that way (its first row, swiped down) the swipe scrolls\nthe page instead, as a scroll box of the browser does; the grid keeps a swipe it started to the end.\nTwo fingers are the browser&#39;s: a pinch zooms the page.</li>\n<li><strong>Menus</strong>: a long press (held still for half a second) opens the menu a right click opens - of a\ncell, a row number, a header label, a header filter, a column number. In a menu a tap opens a\nsubmenu, a tap outside closes it. On a touch screen (css <code>pointer: coarse</code>) the items are 28px high,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/menus/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Selecting</strong>: a finger has no Shift and no Ctrl. A tap on a row number toggles the row - the first\ntap selects, more taps add or remove (in <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode a tap selects the one row) - and a tap on a\ncolumn number toggles the column. <em>Select ›</em> in the row and column menus does the rest: rows or\ncolumns from the last one to here, all, a range of cells from the active cell to here, a cell added\nto the ranges, clear - see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Columns</strong>: a label held still for 200ms is dragged, a tap sorts, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a>. The column chooser&#39;s\nlist scrolls with a finger, a field held still is dragged out of it.</li>\n<li><strong>The rest</strong>: a tap on a cell puts the focus in it - a phone&#39;s keyboard opens only where the cell\ncan be edited (a readonly cell&#39;s input is <code>readOnly</code>). The filter editor is moved by its title bar\nand resized by its edges and corners. A finger drags no range of cells, it makes them with\n<em>Select ›</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tried in Chromium&#39;s touch emulation (Chrome on Android is Chromium); Safari on iOS is not tried on a\ndevice. iOS sends no <code>contextmenu</code> for a long press, the grid times it itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-why-the-fields-matter\">Why the fields matter</h2>\n<p>The datasource sorts, filters, groups and formats a field by its type. A <code>number</code> field sorts as a\nnumber and is right aligned, a <code>date</code> field gets a date picker and filters on the day, a <code>boolean</code>\nfield is a checkbox. A field you do not list is <code>text</code>. List every field that is not text:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        balance: 'number',\n        born: 'date', // the day: 2026-11-20\n        lastLogin: 'datetime', // the time too: 2026-11-20T08:15:00\n        active: 'boolean'\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The values must have the type: numbers for a <code>number</code> field, <code>Date</code> objects for <code>date</code> and\n<code>datetime</code>. Convert dates from JSON when you load them. See <a href=\"#guide/datasource\">Datasource</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-done-with-the-grid\">Done with the grid</h2>\n<p><code>destroy()</code> takes the grid off the page and disconnects it from the datasource. The datasource\nkeeps its rows, it can be shown in a new grid.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.destroy();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-placing-the-element-yourself\">Placing the element yourself</h2>\n<p><code>createGrid()</code> makes a <code>&lt;simple-html-grid&gt;</code> element that fills the container. To place and size it\nyourself, make the element and connect a <code>GridInterface</code> to it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { GridElement, GridInterface, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = { layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] } };\n\nconst element = document.createElement('simple-html-grid') as GridElement;\nelement.style.width = '100%';\nelement.style.height = '100%';\nelement.connectInterface(new GridInterface(config, datasource));\nhost.appendChild(element);</code></pre>\n<p>Removing such an element from the page disconnects the grid too, it connects again when the\nelement is put back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-where-to-go-next\">Where to go next</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/features\">Feature overview</a> - everything the grid can do, each feature linked to its guide and a\nsample</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a> - columns, layout, every option, the hooks and saving the config</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/state\">State</a> - save and restore what the user changed: sorting, filters, column order</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/datasource\">Datasource</a> - data in and out, keys, fields, selection</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a> - editing, change tracking, deleting rows, saving</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/filtering\">Filtering</a> - the header filters, the filter editor, filters from code</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping\">Sorting and grouping</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a> - how values are shown and parsed</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a> - every event and what it sends</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a> - change the right click menus, open menus of your own</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/migrating-from-v5\">Migrating from v5</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a> - translate the grid: every text by id, <code>setTexts()</code></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"grid-config","title":"Grid config","html":"<p>The <code>GridConfig</code> is what you write: which columns go where, the options of each column, and the\noptions of the grid. What the user changes while working - column order and widths, sorting,\nfilters - is the <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>, not the config.</p>\n<p>The config is plain JSON: it has no functions, so it can be stored and loaded, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>. The functions of a grid - readonly and css classes from the\nrow, the menus - are <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">hooks</a>, given next to the config.</p>\n<p>The grid does not change the config object you pass in. It keeps its own copy.</p>\n<p><strong><code>null</code> is an option left out.</strong> A config from a backend or a database often has <code>null</code> for what is not\nset: <code>placeholderRow: null</code>, <code>operator: null</code>, <code>format: null</code>, <code>readonly: null</code>. The grid takes each as not\ngiven - the column&#39;s own default (the field&#39;s label, the formatter&#39;s format, the default operator) or the\ngrid&#39;s (<code>readonly: null</code> is <code>true</code>). In <code>updateConfig()</code>, <code>null</code> sets an option back to its default, as\n<code>undefined</code> does. A value that is wrong still throws; <code>layout</code> can not be <code>null</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-columns-and-layout\">Columns and layout</h2>\n<p>A config has two parts for the columns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>layout</code> says where the columns go and how wide they are</li>\n<li><code>columns</code> has the options of a column, keyed by the field name</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a value the user has to fill in is a rule of the field: required\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { name: { validate: ['required'] }, age: 'number', born: 'date', balance: 'number' } });\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { label: 'Name' },\n        age: { readonly: true },\n        phone: { label: 'Phone' } // not in the layout: the user can add it from the column chooser\n    },\n    layout: {\n        left: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }], // pinned left, does not scroll sideways\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['age', 'born'], width: 100 }, // two fields stacked in one column\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 200 }\n        ],\n        right: [{ fields: ['balance'], width: 100 }] // pinned right\n    }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"column-layout\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code>layout.center</code> is required, <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> can be left out.</li>\n<li>Each entry of a list is one column, from left to right. <code>width</code> is in px.</li>\n<li>A column with more than one field shows them stacked, top to bottom. Every column gets as many\ncells as the column with the most fields, a column with fewer fields leaves the rest empty.</li>\n<li>A field in the layout without an entry in <code>columns</code> gets the default options. <code>company</code> and\n<code>born</code> above have none.</li>\n<li>A field in <code>columns</code> that is not in the layout is not shown. The column chooser in the header\nmenu lists every field the grid knows - its search finds a field by its label or its name - and the\nuser can drag one from it into the header (each field has a drag handle at its left), and rename a\ncolumn with the ✎ of its field (see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-column-chooser\">The column chooser</a>).\n<code>gridInterface.getHiddenFields()</code> returns these fields (<code>[&#39;phone&#39;]</code> above).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The type of a column comes from the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code>, not from the config, see\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/fields-and-types\">Datasource</a>. So do the validation rules: a field the user has to\nfill in has the <code>required</code> rule, which also shows its empty cells with stripes - see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>. The <code>mandatory</code> column option of v5 is gone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</h3>\n<p>The user changes the layout in the header, with a mouse, a pen or a finger:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Move</strong>: drag a label. While it is dragged the header shows where it can go: the left or right\nedge of a label makes the field a column of its own on that side, the top or bottom edge stacks it\nin that column, the middle swaps the two fields. Dropped on the panel above the header, the rows\nare grouped on it, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/grouping\">Grouping</a>. Dragged past the left or right\nside of the grid, the columns scroll.</li>\n<li><strong>Resize</strong>: drag the edge of a column&#39;s header - the right edge, the left one for a column pinned\nright. A column is at least 25px wide. A double click on the edge fits the column to its content,\nas in Excel: see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-widths-and-row-heights\">Column widths and row heights</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sort</strong>: click a label, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/sorting\">Sorting</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A press on a label is a click until it is a drag: with a mouse or a pen once it has moved 5px, with a\nfinger once it has been held still for 200ms. A finger that moves sooner scrolls the grid, as a swipe\non the rows does. A finger still held at 500ms is a long press: the header menu opens, the drag ends\nand nothing moves. Only the left mouse button, the first finger or the pen&#39;s tip drag and resize. The\ncolumn chooser&#39;s list scrolls with a finger, a field held still for 200ms is dragged out of it, see\n<a href=\"#guide/getting-started/touch\">Touch</a>.</p>\n<p>What the user makes of the layout is part of the grid&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>: <code>getState().layout</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-widths-and-row-heights\">Column widths and row heights</h3>\n<p>Like Excel&#39;s AutoFit, a column can be made as wide as its content, and a row as high as its\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">multiline</a> cells need - and both go back to their size:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.fitColumns(); // every column\ngridInterface.fitColumns(['company', 'notes']); // the columns of these fields\ngridInterface.resetColumnWidths(); // the widths of the config again\n\ngridInterface.fitRows(); // every row as high as its multiline cells need\ngridInterface.fitRows(datasource.getSelectedRows());\ngridInterface.resetRowHeights(); // every row one line again</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>method</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>fitColumns(fields?, { rows? })</code></td>\n<td>each column as wide as the widest text of its fields - every line of a multiline text, the widest field of a column with stacked fields - and its header label. 25px to 750px. Without fields: every column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fitColumns([field], { rows: [row] })</code></td>\n<td>the column as wide as the text of these rows&#39; cells, the label left out: a column fitted to a cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>resetColumnWidths(fields?)</code></td>\n<td>the width the column has in the layout <code>resetState()</code> goes back to (<code>initialState.layout</code>, else the config&#39;s <code>layout</code>). A column added from the column chooser: 100px</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fitRows(rows?)</code></td>\n<td>each row as many lines high as its tallest column needs: a multiline cell the lines of its text, every other cell a line, stacked fields added up. At most 20 lines. The lines of the layout&#39;s row are <code>cellHeight</code> each, every line more <code>multilineLineHeight</code> (a line of text). Without rows: every row the filter lets through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>resetRowHeights(rows?)</code></td>\n<td>the rows back to the height of the layout. Without rows: every row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The user does the same with the mouse:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The header menu</strong> (right click a label), &quot;Size ›&quot;: Fit column width, Fit all columns, Reset column\nwidth, Reset all column widths.</li>\n<li><strong>The row menu</strong> (right click a cell), &quot;Size ›&quot;: Fit column to this cell, Fit row height, Fit row\nheight (selected rows) - the selected rows and the rows of the <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">ranges of cells</a> -\nFit all rows, Reset row height, Reset all row heights.</li>\n<li><strong>A double click on the edge</strong> of a column&#39;s header fits that column.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What to know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The text measured</strong> is what the cells show: formatted, with <code>numberOverride</code>. It is measured\nwith the grid&#39;s font on a canvas - for each field the 20 longest texts by their number of\ncharacters, as measuring every text of 100 000 rows takes the browser half a second per field.\nFitting every column of a grid of 100 000 rows and 10 columns takes about 0.2s, the rebuild\nincluded. A column with nothing to measure keeps its width.</li>\n<li><strong>The widths</strong> are part of the <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>, <code>getState().layout</code>, as a resize with the mouse.</li>\n<li><strong>A row&#39;s height</strong> is a number of lines. Fitting counts the line breaks of the cells&#39; text, nothing\nis measured: about 0.1s for every row of 100 000. A long line is not wrapped, it is cut at the\nedge of the cell as in a row of one line.</li>\n<li><strong>A line of text</strong> after the first is <code>multilineLineHeight</code> px (15), not a whole <code>cellHeight</code>: the\nfirst line of a cell is its input, <code>cellHeight</code> high, the lines under it read as a paragraph. A note\nof three lines makes a row of 22 + 15 + 15 = 52px. With a larger font make both larger, e.g.\n<code>cellHeight: 28, multilineLineHeight: 18</code> for <code>--simple-html-grid-font-size: 13px</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>In a taller row</strong> a multiline cell shows as many lines as it has room for, under its first line,\nand its marker when some are not shown. Every other cell has its text on the first line. Editing\nis the same: Enter, F2 or a double click opens the editor over the cell.</li>\n<li><strong>The heights are kept by the row&#39;s key</strong> (<code>DatasourceOptions.key</code>, or the key the datasource\ngenerates without one), so they follow a row through a sort, a filter, grouping and new data with\nthe same keys. A row keeps its height when its text changes: fit it again. A full <code>fitRows()</code> drops\nthe heights of rows that are no longer in the data.</li>\n<li><strong>The heights are not part of the state</strong>: they belong to the rows loaded now, and a saved state is\ngiven to other rows. <code>resetState()</code> does not change them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"#sample/multiline\">Multiline text</a> sample has buttons for each, and the menus.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-column-label\">The column label</h3>\n<p>A column&#39;s header shows the first of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the label the user gave it in the grid config dialog or with the ✎ of the column chooser: <code>format.label</code>,\nstate (<code>GridState.formats</code>)</li>\n<li>its <code>label</code> in <code>columns</code></li>\n<li>the field&#39;s <code>label</code> in the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code> - for a calculated column its <code>label</code></li>\n<li>the field name, with the first letter in upper case and <code>_</code> as a space: <code>first_name</code> shows as\n<code>First Name</code>, but <code>firstName</code> as <code>Firstname</code>. Give such a field a label.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The grid names the column with it everywhere: the header, the menus, the column chooser, the filter\neditor, the footer&#39;s filter, copy with headers and the headers of the CSV and Excel exports.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-options\">Column options</h3>\n<p>Every option of a <code>ColumnConfig</code> is optional.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>label</code></td>\n<td>the header text. The user&#39;s label from the column settings (<code>format.label</code>) wins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>readonly</code></td>\n<td>the cell can not be edited, also when the grid can. Shown with stripes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderRow</code></td>\n<td>text shown in an empty cell, by default on the current row only (<code>placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly</code>). The user&#39;s from the grid config dialog (<code>format.placeholderRow</code>, or the label with <code>format.placeholderRowLabel</code>) wins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderFilter</code></td>\n<td>text shown in the empty header filter. Default: the formatter&#39;s placeholder (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> for dates), else the field&#39;s type - <code>Text</code> or <code>Number</code> (texts <code>filter.placeholder.*</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>operator</code></td>\n<td>the operator the header filter starts with. Default <code>EQUAL</code> for text (the whole text: <code>ann</code> does not find <code>Anna</code>), <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code> for a number or date. One of the column&#39;s operators the header filter&#39;s input takes - not <code>IN</code>, <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code> - else a <code>TypeError</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/header-filters\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterOperators</code></td>\n<td>the operators the column offers in the header filter&#39;s menu and the filter editor, e.g. <code>[&#39;CONTAINS&#39;, &#39;BEGIN_WITH&#39;, &#39;IS_BLANK&#39;]</code>: some of its type&#39;s (<code>operatorsFor(type)</code>), with one the header filter takes. Default: all of its type&#39;s. A <code>TypeError</code> for an operator of another type. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operators-a-column-offers\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validate</code></td>\n<td>the column&#39;s validation rules - <code>[&#39;required&#39;, { id: &#39;maxLength&#39;, value: 50 }]</code>, as a field&#39;s - added after the field&#39;s own. Set by code, the user can not change them: not in the state, the grid config dialog shows them read only. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/rules-of-a-grids-columns\">Editing</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterCaseSensitive</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code>: the grid&#39;s conditions on the column match case - the header filter, its value list, the row menu&#39;s filter and the filter editor. Its Match case is on and can not be turned off. Text columns only, else a <code>TypeError</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/match-case\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterOnType</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code>: the header filter filters as you type, after a short pause - no Enter needed. Left out, the grid&#39;s <code>filterOnType</code>. The user turns it on and off (the operator choice, the filter menu, the grid config dialog). Not a boolean column. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePresets</code></td>\n<td>the quick dates of a date or datetime column&#39;s filter menu, in this order: <code>[&#39;today&#39;, &#39;thisWeek&#39;, &#39;overdue&#39;]</code> (<code>DATE_PRESETS</code> has them all). Default: every one but <code>&#39;overdue&#39;</code>; <code>false</code> none. A <code>TypeError</code> for an id that is none, another type, or a column without <code>BETWEEN</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">Quick dates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>numberOverride</code></td>\n<td>number fields only: <code>&#39;ZERO_TO_BLANK&#39;</code> shows 0 as an empty cell, <code>&#39;BLANK_TO_ZERO&#39;</code> shows an empty cell as 0. The value is not changed. The user&#39;s from the column settings (<code>format.numberOverride</code>) wins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>format</code></td>\n<td>how the cells show the value, a display format: <code>{ date: &#39;norwegian&#39; }</code> (22.11.2026), <code>{ date: &#39;weekIso&#39; }</code> (2026-W47) for a date or datetime field, <code>{ number: { decimals: 2, thousands: &#39; &#39;, decimal: &#39;,&#39; } }</code> (1 234 567,89) for a number field; for a text field a template of its text and a link, <code>{ text: &#39;{name} ({id})&#39;, link: { url: &#39;https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}&#39;, newTab: true } }</code>. Only the text shown: editing, filtering, sorting and copy keep to the field&#39;s formatter - but the header filter takes a date or number format&#39;s text as the cells show it, <code>2026-W47</code> is the week (<code>filterAsShown</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a>). Where the grid starts - the user picks another in the header menu (Format ›) or the grid config dialog, it is state (<code>GridState.formats</code>). It has the rest of the column settings too: <code>label</code>, <code>placeholderRow</code> / <code>placeholderRowLabel</code>, <code>numberOverride</code>, <code>checkbox</code> (a text or number column of <code>1</code> / <code>0</code> as checkboxes), <code>multiline</code>, <code>readonly</code> and <code>rules</code>, the colour rules of any column (<code>[{ operator: &#39;LESS_THAN&#39;, value: &#39;0&#39;, color: &#39;red&#39; }]</code>). A <code>TypeError</code> for a format that does not fit the field, and for a link of another scheme than https, http, mailto, tel. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>, <a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">Templates and links</a> <a href=\"#guide/formatting/label-placeholder-blank--0-checkboxes-multiline-read-only\">Label, placeholder, blank / 0, checkboxes, multiline, read only</a> and <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">Colour rules</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>allowPasteClearOnly</code></td>\n<td>typing in the cell does nothing, paste and clear from the row menu work. For a field you set from a dropdown of your own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>editor</code></td>\n<td>the value is picked from a list: <code>{ type: &#39;select&#39; | &#39;grid&#39;, lookup: &#39;&lt;id&gt;&#39;, set? }</code>, a select or a dropdown grid of a lookup registered with <code>registerLookup()</code>. The cell shows the label. See <a href=\"#guide/lookups\">Lookups</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>focusButton</code></td>\n<td>shows a <code>...</code> button in the focused cell, see <a href=\"#guide/events/cell-events\">Events</a>. Not with <code>editor</code>, it has its own button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code></td>\n<td>another field of the row holds the type of this cell, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-type-from-another-field\">Formatting</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate</code></td>\n<td>sums the column up in the group rows and the totals row: <code>&#39;sum&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;avg&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;min&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;max&#39;</code> (number fields) or <code>&#39;count&#39;</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>focusButton</code> takes <code>&#39;ALWAYS&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code> or\n<code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_AND_CELL_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</h3>\n<p><code>calculatedColumns</code> adds columns whose value a formula computes from the row - a field of their own,\nput in the <code>layout</code> as any field, with <code>columns</code> options as any column:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    calculatedColumns: { total: { formula: '{price} * {qty}', label: 'Total' } },\n    columns: { total: { aggregate: 'sum', format: { number: { decimals: 2 } } } },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['price'], width: 80 }, { fields: ['qty'], width: 60 }, { fields: ['total'], width: 90 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>They are read only, part of the state (<code>GridState.calculatedColumns</code>), and the user adds and changes\nthem in the grid config dialog. See <a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"required-placeholders\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-grid-options\">Grid options</h2>\n<p>Every option is optional.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>readonly</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the whole grid is readonly. Set <code>false</code> to edit, see <a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>edit</code></td>\n<td>all allowed</td>\n<td>what the user may do in an editable grid: <code>{ add, change, delete }</code>, each <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> - <code>{ add: true, change: false, delete: false }</code> lets them add rows and fill them in, and leaves the rows that are there as they are. A permission of yours: not state, no menu or dialog changes it. A <code>TypeError</code> for anything else. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">Edit permissions</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>22</code></td>\n<td>height of a cell in px</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>multilineLineHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>15</code></td>\n<td>the height in px of a line of text after the first in a multiline cell of a row made taller (<code>fitRows()</code>), and in its editor. A whole number of 1 or more, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-widths-and-row-heights\">Column widths and row heights</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellHeaderLabelHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>cellHeight</code></td>\n<td>height of a header label cell, make it taller for labels that wrap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>panelHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>25</code></td>\n<td>height of the grouping panel above the header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footerHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>45</code></td>\n<td>height of the footer: the row count and the filter, or the button &quot;No filter&quot; without one, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-footer\">Filtering</a>. <code>0</code>: no footer, nothing in it for Tab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>selectSizeHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>18</code></td>\n<td>height of the row of column numbers under the header filters (click one to select a column). <code>0</code> hides the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hideLabels</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>hides the row of header labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hideFilter</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>hides the row of header filters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterOperatorIndicator</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>shows the operator of each header filter as a small symbol in its cell (<code>=</code>, <code>≥</code>, <code>*a*</code> ...): on the right of a text filter, on the left of a number or date filter. A click (F4 in the filter) opens the operator choice. Not on a boolean column nor on a column under 60px. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterOnType</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>every header filter filters as you type, after a short pause - the default of the columns, a column&#39;s <code>filterOnType</code> wins. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>settingsButton</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a gear at the right end of the footer, the last stop of Tab there: it opens the grid config dialog - the display formats, a text column&#39;s template and link, the detail form, saved in the state. <code>false</code> hides the gear only: <em>Column settings…</em> stays in the header menu (<code>header.settings</code>, the <code>menus</code> hook takes it out) and <code>openColumnSettings()</code> opens the dialog. Not in a footer of no height. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">The grid config dialog</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcutHelp</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the keyboard shortcuts help: <code>?</code> where the key types nothing (a readonly cell, a column header) and Ctrl+/ anywhere in the grid open it, the header menu has <em>Keyboard shortcuts…</em> (<code>header.shortcuts</code>). <code>false</code> turns the keys and the entry off, <code>openShortcutHelp()</code> still opens the help. See <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">The keyboard shortcuts help</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>the named views the grid starts with, <code>{ id, name, state, readonly?, default? }</code>: snapshots of the state the user switches between with the views button at the left end of the footer. <code>readonly</code>: a view of yours the user can apply, not change; <code>default</code>: applied when the grid is made. See <a href=\"#guide/state/views\">Views</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>viewsStorageKey</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>keeps the user&#39;s views in localStorage under this key - read when the grid is made, written when they change. See <a href=\"#guide/state/views\">Views</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>viewsButton</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> with <code>views</code> or <code>viewsStorageKey</code>, else <code>false</code></td>\n<td>the views button at the left end of the footer, the first stop of Tab there: the name of the view shown, a <code>*</code> when it changed, a click opens the views menu. <code>false</code> hides it, the views API still works. Not in a footer of no height</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>importMenu</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td><em>Import rows…</em> in the header menu and <em>Paste › As new rows…</em> in the row menu: the import dialog, a CSV or Excel file or rows pasted from a spreadsheet as new rows. In an editable grid where <code>edit</code> allows <code>add</code>. <code>openImport()</code> opens the dialog without it. See <a href=\"#guide/import\">Import</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dimRepeatedValues</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>dims a cell that has the same value as the row above. The row menu can toggle it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>shows the state of each row on its row number: new, edited, marked for deletion, an error. The row numbers are 14px wider for the icon. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>copyHeaders</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a copy of more than one cell starts with a line of the column labels (Word and Excel show the headers); pasted into the grid that line is skipped. See <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>editedMarker</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a marker in the corner of an edited cell: its tooltip has the original value, a click puts it back. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mergeHighlight</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a cell whose value <code>datasource.upsert()</code> changed flashes once. See <a href=\"#guide/live-data/how-the-grid-shows-them\">Live data</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>shows <code>placeholderRow</code> on the current row only. <code>false</code> shows it on every row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>autoRemoveContextMenuOnScrollEvent</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>closes the open menu when the grid scrolls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tooltips</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the built-in tooltips: on the buttons of the filter editor, and the validation messages on a cell and its row number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>multilineTooltipLines</code></td>\n<td><code>5</code></td>\n<td>the lines of a multiline cell&#39;s text its tooltip shows, then <code>… 7 more lines</code>. A whole number of 1 or more, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>texts</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>the texts of this grid by id, e.g. <code>{ &#39;row.copy&#39;: &#39;Kopier&#39; }</code>: they win over the page&#39;s (<code>setTexts()</code>). The tooltips are texts too, their ids end with <code>.tooltip</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePickerConfig</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>week start and sizes of the date picker: only the fields to change (<code>Partial&lt;IDateConfig&gt;</code>), e.g. <code>{ weekStart: 0, isoWeek: false }</code>. The rest are the defaults: weeks from monday with their numbers. The names of the months and days are texts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>initialState</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>the sorting, grouping and filter the grid starts with, see <a href=\"#guide/state/the-state-the-grid-starts-in\">State</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>groupAggregates</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the group rows show the columns&#39; <code>aggregate</code>s. <code>false</code>: the name of the group only. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>totalsRow</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>a row under the rows with the columns&#39; <code>aggregate</code>s over the rows the filter lets through. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/the-totals-row\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm</code></td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>the current row as a form at the right of the rows: <code>{ show: true, fields: [&#39;name&#39;, &#39;notes&#39;], width: 300 }</code>, and <code>expanded</code>, <code>minWidth</code>, <code>maxWidth</code>, sections in <code>fields</code>, <code>fieldOptions</code> (lines, room). It is state, the user changes it in the grid config dialog, drags its width and collapses it. See <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">The detail form</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>How rows are selected is not a grid option: the datasource has the selection mode, see\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/the-selection-mode\">Datasource</a>. Readonly cells and css classes from the row, and\nchanges to the menus, are not options either: they are functions, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">Hooks</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"misc-options\"></div>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"tooltips\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-detail-form\">The detail form</h2>\n<p>The detail form is a panel inside the grid, at the right of the rows, with the current row as a form: a\nlabel and a value for each field picked, in their order. A text of several lines gets a field tall\nenough to read all of it - a column of notes can be narrow in the grid, or not in it at all.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['city'], width: 120 }] },\n    // notes is no column of the layout: the form shows it, all its lines. The user drags it between\n    // 200 and 600px wide\n    detailForm: { show: true, fields: ['name', 'notes', 'city'], width: 320, minWidth: 200, maxWidth: 600 }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>The user sets it</strong> in the grid config dialog (the gear in the footer), tab <em>Detail form</em>: <em>Show the\ndetail form</em>, <em>Expanded</em>, its width with its min and max, and a list of the fields - the columns of\nthe layout and the other columns of the config - each with a checkbox, in the order of the form. A\nfield is dragged to another place by the handle at its right (a line shows where it goes), and <em>Move\nup</em> / <em>Move down</em> (Alt+↑ / Alt+↓ in the list, Space checks a field) move it too.\n<code>gridInterface.openDetailFormSettings()</code> opens the dialog on that tab. See\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">The grid config dialog</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>It is state</strong>: <code>GridConfig.detailForm</code> is the one the grid starts with, <code>getState().detailForm</code>\nthe one it shows - what the user did with it too: the fields, the width dragged, collapsed or\nexpanded. Save it with the rest, see <a href=\"#guide/state/the-detail-form-in-the-state\">State</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Collapsed</strong> (<code>expanded: false</code>) the form is a strip of 24px at the right of the rows with its title\ngoing down - under the rows a bar over the footer. Its title is a button with a chevron (<code>aria-expanded</code>):\nit collapses the form, and expands it again. The button changes <code>expanded</code> in the state, as the\ndialog&#39;s <em>Expanded</em> does: there is one setting, what the form is now, and the grid starts with the\nconfig&#39;s (<code>true</code> when it is left out) or the state saved.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The row</strong> is the current row (<code>datasource.currentEntity</code>): a click on a row, the arrows, code. The\nform follows it, and a value that changes - an edit in a cell, undo, <code>upsert()</code>, code - a filter and\na sort. Without a current row, or with one the filter hides, it says <em>No row</em>. Its title has the\nrow&#39;s number, as the row numbers show it.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The values</strong> are what the cells show: the display format, a template, a lookup&#39;s label. A text of\nseveral lines (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>, or a value with a line break) is a textarea as tall as its\nlines. A boolean is a checkbox. A row with a link has the link under the value. A value has the\ncolours of its column&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">colour rule</a>, as its cell has them.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Editing</strong>: in an editable grid a field is edited as its cell is - the same readonly rules\n(<code>readonly</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>ColumnConfig.readonly</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> hook, <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code>), the value\nparsed by the field&#39;s formatter (a field with the focus shows its <code>formatForEdit</code>), validated, its\nerrors under it. What is typed in a field is one step of the undo history; Ctrl+Z in a field undoes\nthe last step. A lookup column is shown, not edited in the form: its cell picks the value.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The fields</strong> are on the inputs&#39; background in a quiet frame - the controls&#39; frame under the\npointer, the focus outline with the focus, the error colour and the errors under it with an error.\nThe frame is <code>--simple-html-grid-detail-input-border-color</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/the-variables-of-the-grid\">Theming</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Its width</strong> is <code>width</code> px, 300 by default, between <code>minWidth</code> (150 by default, never less) and\n<code>maxWidth</code> (none by default). The header, the rows, their scrollbars and the totals row end where it\nstarts; the grouping panel and the footer stay as wide as the grid.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The user drags its left edge</strong> - a mouse, a pen or a finger - to make it wider or narrower: the rows\nfollow as it moves, and the width let go is the state&#39;s <code>width</code>. It stops at <code>minWidth</code>, at <code>maxWidth</code>\nand where the rows would get less room than the form (half the grid) or the scrolling columns less\nthan 150px: a drag does not put it under the rows. The edge is a splitter for the keyboard too: Tab\ncomes to it after the title, ArrowLeft makes the form 10px wider, ArrowRight narrower.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A narrow grid</strong> - where the rows would get less room than the form beside it, or the scrolling\ncolumns less than 150px (a phone, wide pinned columns) - has the form under the rows instead, over the\nfooter, 40 % of the grid&#39;s height. It is laid out again when the grid gets wider or narrower. Its edge\nis not dragged there.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Sections</strong> are entries of <code>fields</code> between the field names (<code>DetailFormSection</code>): <code>{ section: &#39;Address&#39; }</code>\nhas the fields after it, up to the next section; the fields before the first are in none. Its header is\na button with a chevron (<code>aria-expanded</code>, its fields a <code>group</code> named by it) that collapses the section\nand expands it again. <code>collapsed: true</code> is its default: it starts collapsed (<em>Open at start</em> off in the\ndialog). The header&#39;s button is the user&#39;s, while the form shows these sections: it does not change\n<code>collapsed</code>, and a detail form set again (the dialog&#39;s Apply, <code>setState</code>, <code>resetState()</code>) starts from\nthe defaults. <code>panel: true</code> puts a frame around it,\n<code>paddingTop</code> / <code>paddingBottom</code> give it room in px. In the dialog&#39;s tab <em>Detail form</em> a section is a row of\nthe list: <em>Add section</em> adds one under the row picked, the fields are moved under it as any row is, and\nits name, <em>Panel</em>, <em>Collapsed</em> and room are set under the list (<em>Remove section</em> takes it out, its fields\nstay).</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A field&#39;s options</strong> (<code>fieldOptions</code>, under its name - <code>DetailFormFieldOptions</code>): <code>minRows</code>, the least\nlines of a field of several lines (1 to 30, default 3), and <code>paddingTop</code> / <code>paddingBottom</code>, room in px.\nThe dialog sets them for the field picked - and <em>Min lines in the detail form</em> is under <em>Multiline</em> on\nthe Columns tab too, while Multiline is on (the same setting; the Detail form tab follows Multiline as\nthe dialog has it, before Apply). A field taken out of the form keeps its options for when it is put in\nagain.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.setState({\n    detailForm: {\n        show: true,\n        fields: ['name', { section: 'Address', panel: true, paddingTop: 8 }, 'country', 'city', { section: 'Notes', collapsed: true }, 'notes'],\n        fieldOptions: { notes: { minRows: 6 } }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Keys and screen readers</strong>: the form is a region named by its title (<em>Details</em>), each value&#39;s label\ntied to it. Tab comes to it after the footer - the button of its title first, then its edge (a\n<code>separator</code> with its width as <code>aria-valuenow</code>, the least and most as <code>aria-valuemin</code> /\n<code>aria-valuemax</code>), then its fields - and Shift+Tab from the button goes back to the footer. The wheel\nand a finger over it scroll the form, not the rows. Its texts are <code>detailForm.*</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/detail-form\">Texts</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"detail-form\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-hooks\">Hooks</h2>\n<p>The hooks are the functions of a grid, the parts of it that can not be written as data. They are a\n<code>GridHooks</code> object, given next to the config:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>hook</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>isReadonly</code></td>\n<td>makes cells readonly, or editable, from their row, <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellClass</code></td>\n<td>adds css classes to cells from their row, <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowClass</code></td>\n<td>adds css classes to whole rows from their values, <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/css-classes-for-a-whole-row-rowclass\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>menus</code></td>\n<td>changes the right click menus, see <a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const config: GridConfig;\n\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: { row: (defaults) =&gt; defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; !entry.id.startsWith('row.export')) }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });</code></pre>\n<p>Every hook is optional. <code>new GridInterface(config, datasource, hooks)</code> takes them too, for an\nelement you place yourself. A config that has <code>isReadonly</code>, <code>cellClass</code>, <code>rowClass</code> or <code>menus</code> throws:\nthey are hooks.</p>\n<p>With a typed datasource the hooks get typed rows: <code>GridHooks&lt;Person&gt;</code> for a <code>Datasource&lt;Person&gt;</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">Readonly and css classes from the row</h3>\n<p><code>isReadonly</code> and <code>cellClass</code> are called for every cell when it is rendered, with the cell&#39;s row\nand field. Keep them fast.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>isReadonly(row, field, readonlyByConfig)</code> returns <code>true</code> (readonly) or <code>false</code> (editable).\n<code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code> leaves it to the column&#39;s <code>readonly</code> option, which is passed in as\n<code>readonlyByConfig</code>. A readonly grid (<code>readonly: true</code>) stays readonly, whatever it returns - and so\ndoes a row the <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">edit permissions</a> lock\n(<code>change: false</code>, a row that is not new).</li>\n<li><code>cellClass(row, field)</code> returns <code>{ input, background }</code>: classes for the cell&#39;s <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> (the\ntext) and for the element behind it (the background). Both are optional, <code>null</code> adds nothing.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['balance'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['isActive'], width: 80 }\n        ]\n    }\n};\n\n// .negative { position: absolute; inset: 0; background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.2); }\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    // company can not be edited on an inactive row, the other cells follow the config\n    isReadonly: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'company' &amp;&amp; row.isActive === false ? true : null),\n    cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.balance &lt; 0 ? { background: 'negative' } : null)\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });</code></pre>\n<p>The background element has no size on an editable cell: give its class\n<code>position: absolute; inset: 0</code>, as above.</p>\n<p>The row is always a data row, a group row has no cells. When what the hooks read changes, call\n<code>gridInterface.refresh()</code> to render the cells again.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"cell-styling\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-css-classes-for-a-whole-row-rowclass\">CSS classes for a whole row: <code>rowClass</code></h3>\n<p><code>rowClass(row, rowIndex)</code> returns class names for the whole row - one or more, separated by spaces -\nor <code>null</code> for none: a late order, a blocked customer, a row to look at. The classes go on the row&#39;s\nelement in every section (the row number, the pinned left, middle and pinned right columns), so one\nselector reaches the whole row:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Order {\n    due: Date;\n    shipped: boolean;\n}\n\n// .simple-html-grid-row-even.late, .simple-html-grid-row-odd.late { background-color: #fdecec; }\n// .late .simple-html-grid-col-cell input { font-style: italic; }\nconst hooks: GridHooks&lt;Order&gt; = {\n    rowClass: (row) =&gt; (!row.shipped &amp;&amp; row.due &lt; new Date() ? 'late' : null)\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The row element is behind the cells - their inputs are transparent - so a background on it shows.\nGive it with the even/odd class (<code>.simple-html-grid-row-even.late</code>), as above: that leaves a selected\nrow its own colour, and it is more specific than the grid&#39;s row colours.</li>\n<li>The classes follow the row: it is called when the row is rendered - scrolled in, <code>refresh()</code> - and\nafter its values changed (an edit, a paste, an upsert, an undo), in the next frame. A group row gets\nnone, it is not called for one.</li>\n<li>The grid&#39;s own classes of the row element stay.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-changing-the-hooks-updatehooks\">Changing the hooks: <code>updateHooks()</code></h3>\n<p><code>updateHooks()</code> changes the hooks while the grid is shown:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.updateHooks({ cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.balance &lt; 0 ? { input: 'red' } : null) });\ngridInterface.updateHooks({ isReadonly: undefined }); // removed: the column's readonly decides again\ngridInterface.updateHooks({ menus: undefined }); // the menus show their defaults</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the keys you pass change, the other hooks stay. A key passed as <code>undefined</code> removes that\nhook.</li>\n<li>The visible cells are rendered again once. A menu uses its new function the next time it opens.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-changing-options-updateconfig\">Changing options: <code>updateConfig()</code></h2>\n<p><code>updateConfig()</code> changes options while the grid is shown and builds the grid again once:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.updateConfig({ readonly: false });\ngridInterface.updateConfig({ hideFilter: true, cellHeight: 30 });\ngridInterface.updateConfig({ tooltips: undefined }); // back to the default</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the keys you pass change. A key passed as <code>undefined</code> goes back to its default:\n<code>updateConfig({ readonly: undefined })</code> makes the grid readonly again.</li>\n<li><code>columns</code> replaces the options of every column, nothing is merged. A field you leave out gets\nthe defaults.</li>\n<li><code>layout</code> is shown at once and replaces the column order, pinning and widths the user made. It is\nalso the layout <code>resetState()</code> goes back to from then on. <code>layout: undefined</code> is ignored.</li>\n<li><code>initialState</code> changes nothing now, it is what the next <code>resetState()</code> goes back to.</li>\n<li><code>edit</code> replaces the permissions as a whole: <code>updateConfig({ edit: { delete: false } })</code> allows adding\nand changing again. An open menu or editor is closed, what was typed in the editor not saved. See\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">Edit permissions</a>.</li>\n<li>The sorting, grouping, filter and header filters stay as they are, and so do the handlers you\nsubscribed with <code>on()</code> and the hooks. Change a hook with <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/changing-the-hooks-updatehooks\"><code>updateHooks()</code></a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-saving-the-config\">Saving the config</h2>\n<p>The config, the datasource&#39;s fields and formatters, and the <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a> are plain JSON.\nThey can be stored - in a backend, say - and given back as they are. What is code stays in the\napp: the hooks, and the formatters of your own, registered by id with <code>registerFormatter()</code> (see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-formatter-of-your-own\">Formatting</a>).</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import {\n    createGrid,\n    Datasource,\n    type DatasourceOptions,\n    type GridConfig,\n    type GridHooks,\n    type GridState,\n    NumberFormatterDot,\n    registerFormatter\n} from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// what is stored, as JSON\ninterface SavedGrid {\n    config: GridConfig;\n    datasource: Pick&lt;DatasourceOptions, 'key' | 'fields' | 'formatters' | 'selectionMode'&gt;;\n    state: GridState | null;\n}\n\n// code: the formatters the stored fields name, registered before the datasource is made\nregisterFormatter('money', {\n    ...NumberFormatterDot,\n    format: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? value.toFixed(2) : '')\n});\n\n// code: the hooks\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    isReadonly: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.locked === true ? true : null)\n};\n\n// e.g. { &quot;config&quot;: { &quot;layout&quot;: ... }, &quot;datasource&quot;: { &quot;fields&quot;: { &quot;balance&quot;: { &quot;formatter&quot;: &quot;money&quot; ... } } } ... }\nconst saved: SavedGrid = await (await fetch('/api/grids/accounts')).json();\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource(saved.datasource);\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config: saved.config, datasource, hooks });\nif (saved.state) {\n    gridInterface.setState(saved.state);\n}\n\n// later: store what the user changed\nconst body: SavedGrid = { ...saved, state: gridInterface.getState() };\nawait fetch('/api/grids/accounts', { method: 'PUT', body: JSON.stringify(body) });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A formatter id that is not registered throws when the datasource is made, with the ids that are.</li>\n<li>The texts are JSON too: <code>texts</code> in the config for one grid, <code>setTexts()</code> with the texts of the page -\nsee <a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a>.</li>\n<li><code>container</code> is the one datasource option that is not data: a <code>DataContainer</code> is made in code.</li>\n<li>A <code>Date</code> does not come back from JSON as a <code>Date</code>, but as its ISO text: a <code>Date</code> in a filter of\nthe state, or of <code>initialState</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/state/dates-in-a-saved-state\">Dates in a saved state</a> for what the grid does\nwith it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-exporting-with-a-version-exportconfig--importconfig\">Exporting with a version: <code>exportConfig()</code> / <code>importConfig()</code></h3>\n<p><code>exportConfig()</code> gives the config and the state in one, with the version of the format: what\n<code>importConfig()</code> takes back. A saved grid of an older version is converted on the way in, so an app keeps\nloading what it saved before.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// save: { &quot;version&quot;: 2, &quot;config&quot;: { ... }, &quot;state&quot;: { ... } }\nlocalStorage.setItem('my-grid', JSON.stringify(gridInterface.exportConfig()));\n\n// load: one exportConfig() made, or a config v5 saved\nconst saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('my-grid') ?? 'null');\nif (saved) {\n    gridInterface.importConfig(saved);\n}</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>version</code></strong> is <code>GRID_CONFIG_VERSION</code>, 2. <strong>A saved grid without a <code>version</code> is version 1: a config\nv5 saved (<code>saveConfig()</code>) or was written for.</strong> It is converted: <code>attributes</code> become <code>columns</code> (its\nplaceholders with their new names), the <code>columns*</code> the <code>layout</code>, and the state v5 kept in the config -\nthe sort, the grouping, the groups expanded, the filter (v5&#39;s shape), the header filters&#39; values -\n<code>initialState</code> and the state. A v5 column&#39;s <code>type</code> and <code>mandatory</code> are the datasource&#39;s fields&#39; in v6:\nthey are left out, give the datasource the field&#39;s type and its <code>required</code> rule.</li>\n<li><strong><code>config</code></strong> is the config as the app gave it - the constructor&#39;s, with what <code>updateConfig()</code> changed\nsince - and <strong><code>state</code></strong> is <code>getState()</code>. The hooks and the datasource are not in it.</li>\n<li><strong><code>views</code></strong> are the named views - every one: the config&#39;s (<code>config.views</code>) and the ones the user saved,\nrenamed and deleted with the views button (<code>viewsStorageKey</code>) - as <code>getViews()</code> has them. Left out when\nthere are none. <code>importConfig()</code> gives them back with <code>setViews()</code> (written to <code>viewsStorageKey</code>); a saved\ngrid without <code>views</code> leaves the grid&#39;s. So a grid saved in a backend keeps the user&#39;s views with it.</li>\n<li><strong><code>importConfig()</code></strong> gives the config to <code>updateConfig()</code> - an option it leaves out stays as it is -\nthen the state to <code>setState()</code>; a saved grid without a state starts as its config says (<code>resetState()</code>),\nthen its views to <code>setViews()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A version newer than the grid&#39;s</strong> throws a <code>TypeError</code> (it was saved by a newer grid), and so does a\nsaved grid without a version that has <code>layout</code>, <code>columns</code> or <code>initialState</code>: that is a v6 config, save it\nas <code>{ version: 2, config }</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>convertGridConfig(saved)</code></strong> is the conversion alone, for a grid made from a saved one:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { convertGridConfig, createGrid, Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const host: HTMLElement;\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { age: 'number' } });\nconst { config, state } = convertGridConfig(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('my-grid') ?? 'null'));\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });\nif (state) {\n    gridInterface.setState(state);\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-when-the-sources-columns-change-reconcilecolumns--updatecolumns\">When the source&#39;s columns change: <code>reconcileColumns()</code> / <code>updateColumns()</code></h3>\n<p>A grid made from a source - the columns of a database view, an API - and saved names the fields the source\nhad then. When the source gets columns and loses some, bring the saved grid up to date with the columns it\nhas now, before the grid is made from it - or on the grid that is shown:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { convertGridConfig, createGrid, Datasource, reconcileColumns, type SourceColumn } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const host: HTMLElement;\n// the columns of the view now: a ColumnConfig and its field\nconst columns: SourceColumn[] = [\n    { field: 'company', label: 'Company' },\n    { field: 'country', label: 'Country' },\n    { field: 'region', label: 'Region' }\n];\n\nconst saved = convertGridConfig(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('my-grid') ?? 'null'));\nconst { grid, report } = reconcileColumns(saved, columns);\n// report: { added: ['region'], removed: ['city'], removedCalculated: [...], removedRules: [{ field, rule }] }\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { region: 'text' } }); // the new fields' types\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config: grid.config, datasource });\nif (grid.state) {\n    gridInterface.setState(grid.state);\n}\n\n// a grid that is shown: the same, its views saved since too - returns the report\ngridInterface.updateColumns(columns, { showNew: true });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>config.columns</code></strong> becomes the source&#39;s columns: each one&#39;s options as given, over the options the\nsaved config had for it (a <code>readonly</code> kept, the source&#39;s <code>label</code> wins). A calculated column stays, it is\nthe grid&#39;s.</li>\n<li><strong>A field that is gone</strong> leaves everything that names it - the config, its <code>initialState</code>, the state and\nevery view (<code>config.views</code>; <code>updateColumns()</code> the views saved since too, written back): the layout (a\ncolumn left without a field goes), the sort, the grouping, the header filters, the filter&#39;s conditions\n(an AND / OR group left empty goes, a condition comparing with the field - <code>valueField</code> - too), the\nformats, the aggregates, the detail form&#39;s fields and options.</li>\n<li><strong>Colour rules</strong>: a condition on the field goes, a rule left without a condition goes, a formula that\nnames the field goes - each rule taken out is in <code>report.removedRules</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A calculated column</strong> whose formula names a field that is gone goes, and so does one that names it\n(<code>report.removedCalculated</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>A new field</strong> is in <code>config.columns</code>: the column chooser lists it, hidden. <code>showNew: true</code> shows it at\nthe end of the scrolling columns, <code>width</code> px wide (100).</li>\n<li>The datasource keeps its fields: give it the new fields&#39; types; a field it has no type of is text.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"reconcile-columns\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-rendering-again\">Rendering again</h2>\n<p>The grid renders again by itself when the datasource changes: new data, sorting, filtering,\nselection - and a value set on a row from code, in the next animation frame (see\n<a href=\"#guide/live-data/values-show-on-their-own\">Live data</a>). When something changes that it can not see,\ntell it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>gridInterface.refresh()</code> renders the visible cells again. Call it when what the <code>isReadonly</code> /\n<code>cellClass</code> hooks read has changed. It is cheap.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.rebuild()</code> builds the whole grid again: sizes, header, rows, footer. Call it when a\nformatter shows values differently now, after <code>setTexts()</code> (see <a href=\"#guide/texts/when-a-changed-text-shows\">Texts</a>),\nor after you changed the size of the grid&#39;s container without a resize event.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.fitColumns()</code> fits every column to its content (at most 750px) and rebuilds, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-widths-and-row-heights\">Column widths and row heights</a>. It measures with the grid&#39;s font,\nso call it once the grid is on the page. <code>autoResizeColumns()</code> is the same, and deprecated.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>let highlighted = 'ACME CORP';\ngridInterface.updateHooks({ cellClass: (row) =&gt; (row.company === highlighted ? { input: 'highlight' } : null) });\n\nhighlighted = 'GLOBEX'; // the hook reads it, the grid does not know\ngridInterface.refresh();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-fields-in-the-layout\">The fields in the layout</h2>\n<p><code>getLayoutFields()</code> lists the fields in the layout now, in the order the user sees them: pinned\nleft, center, pinned right. With <code>{ selectedOnly: true }</code> it lists only the fields of the columns\nthe user selected by clicking the column numbers under the header filters (ctrl+click for more).\nIt is <code>[]</code> when none is selected.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const selected = gridInterface.getLayoutFields({ selectedOnly: true });\nconst fieldsToExport = selected.length ? selected : gridInterface.getLayoutFields();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-done-with-a-grid-destroy\">Done with a grid: <code>destroy()</code></h2>\n<p><code>gridInterface.destroy()</code> removes the grid&#39;s element from the page and disconnects the grid: from\nthe datasource, the document and the handlers subscribed with <code>on()</code>. The datasource is not\nchanged. Do not use the interface after it, make a new one with <code>createGrid()</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"state","title":"State","html":"<p>The config is what you write. The state is what the user changes while working with the grid. Read\nit with <code>getState()</code>, give it back with <code>setState()</code>, and go back to the start with <code>resetState()</code>.\nThe user can keep several under names and switch between them in the footer: <a href=\"#guide/state/views\">Views</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-the-state-is\">What the state is</h2>\n<p><code>gridInterface.getState()</code> returns a <code>GridState</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>layout</code></td>\n<td>the column order, pinning and widths: the user drags, pins, hides and resizes columns, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a>. The widths <code>fitColumns()</code> gives are here too; the row heights of <code>fitRows()</code> are not part of the state, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-widths-and-row-heights\">Column widths and row heights</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sorting</code></td>\n<td>the sort, as <code>datasource.getLastSorting()</code>. When grouped, the grouped fields come first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grouping</code></td>\n<td>the grouping, as <code>datasource.getGrouping()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>expandedGroups</code></td>\n<td>the ids of the expanded groups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter</code></td>\n<td>the filter the rows are filtered by, as <code>datasource.getFilter()</code>. <code>null</code> when there is none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>headerFilters</code></td>\n<td>what each header filter cell shows: the value, the operator picked in its menu and its Match case - and a quick date (This month)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregates</code></td>\n<td>the aggregate functions of the columns, keyed by field (<code>{ balance: [&#39;sum&#39;, &#39;avg&#39;] }</code>): what <code>ColumnConfig.aggregate</code> starts with and the header menu changes, see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/from-the-header-menu\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>totalsRow</code></td>\n<td>whether the totals row is shown: <code>GridConfig.totalsRow</code>, turned on and off in the header menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formats</code></td>\n<td>the display formats of the columns, keyed by field (<code>{ born: { date: &#39;weekIso&#39; } }</code>, <code>{ email: { link: { url: &#39;mailto:{email}&#39; } } }</code>): what <code>ColumnConfig.format</code> starts with and the header menu&#39;s Format ›, the grid config dialog and the column chooser&#39;s ✎ (a label) change, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a> and <a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">Templates and links</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>typeFormats</code></td>\n<td>the formats by type (<code>{ date: &#39;norwegian&#39;, number: { decimals: 2 } }</code>): how every date, datetime and number column without a format of its own shows its values - what <code>GridConfig.typeFormats</code> starts with and the Defaults tab of the grid config dialog changes, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/formats-by-type\">Formats by type</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>calculatedColumns</code></td>\n<td>the calculated columns, keyed by field (<code>{ total: { formula: &#39;{price} * {qty}&#39;, label: &#39;Total&#39; } }</code>): what <code>GridConfig.calculatedColumns</code> starts with and the grid config dialog adds, changes and removes, see <a href=\"#guide/state/calculated-columns-in-the-state\">Calculated columns in the state</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm</code></td>\n<td>the detail form: whether it is shown, its fields in their order, its width (dragged too) and its least and most width, expanded or collapsed (<code>{ show: true, fields: [&#39;name&#39;, &#39;notes&#39;], width: 300 }</code>) - what <code>GridConfig.detailForm</code> starts with and the grid config dialog, the form&#39;s edge and its title change, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">The detail form</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const state = gridInterface.getState();\nstate.layout.center.map((column) =&gt; column.fields); // [['name'], ['age', 'born']]\nstate.sorting; // [{ attribute: 'name', ascending: true }]\nstate.filter; // { group: 'AND', children: [{ field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO', value: 30 }] }\nstate.headerFilters; // { age: { value: 30 } }</code></pre>\n<p><code>filter</code> is what the rows are filtered by. <code>headerFilters</code> is only what the header cells show: the\nconditions a header filter made are in <code>filter</code> already. Keep both, as <code>getState()</code> gives them, so\nthe header shows what the rows are filtered by. A quick date is the one exception: it is computed\nfrom today when the state is set, see <a href=\"#guide/state/quick-dates-in-the-state\">below</a>.</p>\n<p>These are <strong>not</strong> state: the options (<code>readonly</code>, <code>dimRepeatedValues</code>, sizes ... see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a>), the data and its edits, the selected rows, the current row, the\nselected columns and the scroll position.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-getstate-setstate-and-resetstate\"><code>getState()</code>, <code>setState()</code> and <code>resetState()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const saved = gridInterface.getState();\n\n// ... the user sorts, filters and moves columns around\n\ngridInterface.setState(saved); // back to what it was\ngridInterface.resetState(); // back to the state the grid started in</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getState()</code> returns a copy. Changing it changes nothing in the grid, and what the user does\nlater does not change it.</li>\n<li><code>setState()</code> applies the parts it is given and <strong>keeps the rest</strong>. Within a part nothing is\nmerged: <code>layout</code> replaces the whole layout, <code>headerFilters</code> every header filter.</li>\n<li><code>setState()</code> copies what it is given with <code>structuredClone</code>, so a <code>Date</code> stays a <code>Date</code>. The\nstate must be plain data: no functions, no rows from the datasource.</li>\n<li><code>resetState()</code> goes back to <code>initialState</code> (below), with the config&#39;s <code>layout</code>. What\n<code>initialState</code> leaves out is cleared: no sorting, no grouping, no filter.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.setState({ sorting: [{ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }] }); // only the sort\ngridInterface.setState({ grouping: [{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }], expandedGroups: [] });\ngridInterface.setState({ filter: null, headerFilters: {} }); // no filter, empty header filters</code></pre>\n<p>An empty <code>sorting</code> gives the rows back the order they were set in.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-saving-it\">Saving it</h2>\n<p>The state is plain data, so it can be stored as JSON - with the config, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridState } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst KEY = 'orders-grid-state';\n\n// e.g. when the page is left\nwindow.addEventListener('beforeunload', () =&gt; {\n    localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(gridInterface.getState()));\n});\n\n// when the grid is made\nconst saved: GridState | null = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) ?? 'null');\nif (saved) {\n    gridInterface.setState(saved);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>A saved layout is the layout the user had. When you add a column to the config later, a state saved\nbefore does not have it. Put a version in the key, and change it when the layout in the config\nchanges.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-dates-in-a-saved-state\">Dates in a saved state</h3>\n<p>JSON has no dates: a <code>Date</code> in the filter or in a header filter is stored as its ISO text,\n<code>&#39;2026-11-20T08:15:00.000Z&#39;</code>, and comes back as that text. <code>setState()</code> does not turn it back into a\n<code>Date</code>, it keeps what it is given. What that means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code> field reads the text as the same moment. The same rows are filtered, and\nthe header filter and <code>getFilterString()</code> show the same date. <code>getState()</code> gives the text from\nthen on, not a <code>Date</code>.</li>\n<li>A field that is not a <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code> field in the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code> filters on a <code>Date</code>\nas a date only while it is a <code>Date</code>. After JSON it is compared as text, and other rows are\nfiltered. List the type of every date field in <code>fields</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same goes for a <code>Date</code> in the config&#39;s <code>initialState</code>, when the config is stored as JSON.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"grid-state\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-state-the-grid-starts-in\">The state the grid starts in</h2>\n<p><code>initialState</code> in the config is the state the grid starts in, and the one <code>resetState()</code> goes back\nto. It takes the same parts as <code>setState()</code>. The layout comes from the config&#39;s <code>layout</code>, unless\n<code>initialState</code> has one.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type GridConfig, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['age'], width: 80 }\n        ]\n    },\n    initialState: {\n        sorting: [{ attribute: 'name', ascending: true }],\n        filter: where('age', 'GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO', 18),\n        headerFilters: { age: { value: 18 } } // what the age header filter shows\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>The grid applies <code>initialState</code> to the datasource when it is made: a filter, sort or grouping\nset on the datasource before is replaced, and <strong>cleared when <code>initialState</code> leaves it out</strong>. Set\nthem in <code>initialState</code>, or on the datasource after the grid is made.</p>\n<p><code>updateConfig({ initialState })</code> changes what the next <code>resetState()</code> goes back to, it changes\nnothing now. Call <code>resetState()</code> after it to apply it at once.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-display-formats-in-the-state\">Display formats in the state</h2>\n<p><code>formats</code> is keyed by field: how each column shows its values - a view setting of the user, like a\ncolumn&#39;s width. Only the columns with a format are listed; a column left out shows its field&#39;s\nformatter (the menu&#39;s <em>Default</em>).</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridState } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst formats: GridState['formats'] = {\n    born: { date: 'norwegian' }, // 22.11.2026\n    due: { date: 'weekIso' }, // 2026-W47\n    balance: { number: { decimals: 2, thousands: ' ', decimal: ',' } }, // 1 234 567,89\n    name: { text: '{name} ({id})' }, // Ann (12)\n    company: { link: { url: 'https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}', newTab: true } } // a link\n};\ngridInterface.setState({ formats });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>It starts as the columns&#39; <code>format</code> in the config (or <code>initialState.formats</code>), and <code>resetState()</code>\ngoes back to it. <code>updateConfig({ columns })</code> gives the columns new options: their formats are the\nnew config&#39;s from then on.</li>\n<li>The user changes it in the header menu (Format ›) and the grid config dialog (the gear in the\nfooter): its Apply is a <code>setState({ formats })</code>. Save <code>getState()</code> to keep what the user set, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">The grid config dialog</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A saved state runs nothing</strong>: the templates of a text column (<code>text</code>, <code>link.url</code>) are text with\n<code>{field}</code> placeholders, not code. A state from storage or another user can have a link to\n<code>javascript:</code> in it - <code>setState()</code> throws a <code>TypeError</code> for it - or a field that holds one, which is\nshown as text, not as a link. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-not-code\">Templates, not code</a>.</li>\n<li><code>setState({ formats })</code> replaces every column&#39;s format - start from <code>getState().formats</code> to change\none. A format that does not fit its field throws a <code>TypeError</code>, and nothing of the state changes.</li>\n<li>It is plain JSON: a state saved with <code>JSON.stringify</code> gives the same formats back.</li>\n<li>The rest of what the user sets in the grid config dialog is in a column&#39;s format too - its\n<code>label</code>, the empty cells&#39; <code>placeholderRow</code> / <code>placeholderRowLabel</code>, a number column&#39;s <code>numberOverride</code>,\n<code>checkbox</code>, <code>multiline</code>, <code>readonly</code>, the colour <code>rules</code> - so it is saved and given back with the formats, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/label-placeholder-blank--0-checkboxes-multiline-read-only\">Label, placeholder, blank / 0, checkboxes, multiline, read only</a> and\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">Colour rules</a>. A rule&#39;s value is text as it is typed in the header\nfilter (<code>&#39;2026-11-22&#39;</code>), so a saved rule reads the same.</li>\n<li><code>typeFormats</code> is next to it: the format of each type, for the columns without one of their own -\n<code>setState({ typeFormats: { date: &#39;norwegian&#39; } })</code> shows every such date column as <code>22.11.2026</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/formats-by-type\">Formats by type</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-detail-form-in-the-state\">The detail form in the state</h2>\n<p><code>detailForm</code> is the detail form at the right of the rows: whether it is shown, the fields it shows in\ntheir order with the sections between them (<code>{ section, panel?, collapsed?, ... }</code> - <code>collapsed</code> is\nthe default a section starts with, the header&#39;s button does not change it), its width and its least and most width, whether it is expanded or collapsed, and the options of its\nfields (<code>fieldOptions</code>: the least lines, room above and under) - a view setting of the user, like the\ncolumns.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.setState({ detailForm: { show: true, fields: ['company', 'notes', 'orderDate'], width: 300 } });\ngridInterface.getState().detailForm; // { show: true, fields: ['company', 'notes', 'orderDate'], width: 300 }\n\n// collapsed to a strip, dragged between 200 and 500px\ngridInterface.setState({\n    detailForm: { show: true, fields: ['company', 'notes'], width: 300, expanded: false, minWidth: 200, maxWidth: 500 }\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>It starts as <code>GridConfig.detailForm</code> (or <code>initialState.detailForm</code>), none by default\n(<code>{ show: false, fields: [] }</code>), and <code>resetState()</code> goes back to it. <code>updateConfig({ detailForm })</code>\nsets it, <code>updateConfig()</code> without it keeps the user&#39;s.</li>\n<li>The user changes it in the grid config dialog, tab <em>Detail form</em>: its Apply is a\n<code>setState({ detailForm })</code>. Save <code>getState()</code> to keep it.</li>\n<li>And on the form itself: the width dragged at its left edge (or its arrow keys) is <code>width</code>, the button\nof its title sets <code>expanded</code> - <code>false</code> collapsed, <code>true</code> expanded; left out it is expanded. The\ndialog&#39;s <em>Expanded</em> is the same <code>expanded</code>: one setting, what the form is now.</li>\n<li><code>width</code> is laid out between <code>minWidth</code> (at least 150) and <code>maxWidth</code>, the state keeps what it was\ngiven. <code>minWidth</code> and <code>maxWidth</code> are left out when they are none, <code>expanded</code> when it was not set.</li>\n<li>A field is listed once; a field that is no column is shown too, labelled as the column chooser\nlabels it. <code>show</code> or <code>expanded</code> that is not a boolean, <code>fields</code> that is not a list of names, a\n<code>width</code>, <code>minWidth</code> or <code>maxWidth</code> that is no number of px, or a <code>minWidth</code> more than the <code>maxWidth</code>\nthrows a <code>TypeError</code>, and nothing of the state changes - so does a section or an option of\n<code>fieldOptions</code> that does not fit (a section&#39;s name a text, <code>minRows</code> 1 to 30, the room 0 or more px).\nA field&#39;s empty options are left out.</li>\n<li>It is plain JSON.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-calculated-columns-in-the-state\">Calculated columns in the state</h2>\n<p><code>calculatedColumns</code> is keyed by field: the columns whose value a formula computes from the row, see\n<a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const saved = gridInterface.getState().calculatedColumns; // { total: { formula: '{price} * {qty}', label: 'Total' } }\n\n// one more, at the end of the layout\nconst layout = gridInterface.getState().layout;\nlayout.center.push({ fields: ['half'], width: 90 });\ngridInterface.setState({ calculatedColumns: { ...saved, half: { formula: '{price} / 2' } }, layout });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>It starts as <code>GridConfig.calculatedColumns</code> (or <code>initialState.calculatedColumns</code>), and <code>resetState()</code>\ngoes back to it. <code>updateConfig({ calculatedColumns })</code> shows new ones at once.</li>\n<li>The user changes it in the grid config dialog: its Apply is a <code>setState({ calculatedColumns })</code>, with\nthe layout of a new one.</li>\n<li><code>setState({ calculatedColumns })</code> replaces all of them, and the rows are filtered, sorted and grouped\nagain with their values. One left out is taken out of the layout, the sort, the grouping, the filter,\nthe header filters and the detail form; its format and aggregates go.</li>\n<li><strong>A saved state runs nothing</strong>: a formula is text read by the grid&#39;s own small language - no <code>eval</code>.\nA formula with a problem, a name that is a field of the rows, anything that is no calculated column\nthrows a <code>TypeError</code>, and nothing of the state changes - so does a section or an option of\n<code>fieldOptions</code> that does not fit (a section&#39;s name a text, <code>minRows</code> 1 to 30, the room 0 or more px).\nA field&#39;s empty options are left out. See\n<a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns/security\">Security</a>.</li>\n<li>It is applied before the formats and aggregates of the same state: a <code>format</code> of a calculated column\nis checked against its type.</li>\n<li>It is plain JSON.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-header-filters-in-the-state\">Header filters in the state</h2>\n<p><code>headerFilters</code> is keyed by field. Only the columns whose header filter has a value, an operator\npicked in its menu, Match case on or filter as you type turned on or off by the user are listed:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridState } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst headerFilters: GridState['headerFilters'] = {\n    name: { value: 'ann*' }, // typed in the name column\n    age: { value: 30, operator: 'LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO' }, // the operator picked in the menu\n    city: { value: 'Oslo', caseSensitive: true }, // Match case on in its menu: OSLO is left out\n    balance: { value: [1000, 5000], operator: 'BETWEEN' }, // typed as 1000..5000\n    active: { value: true }, // a boolean column's checkbox\n    notes: { value: null, filterOnType: true }, // filter as you type turned on in its operator choice\n    due: { value: new Date(2026, 10, 16), operator: 'EQUAL' } // `2026-W47` typed, the column shown as weekIso\n};</code></pre>\n<p>The operator picked in a header filter&#39;s menu is state. The column&#39;s <code>operator</code> in the config is\nonly the operator its header filter starts with, the grid never changes it. An operator of the state the\ncolumn does not offer (its <code>filterOperators</code> changed since it was saved) is passed by: the column&#39;s\n<code>operator</code> is used. Match case (<code>caseSensitive</code>) is state too, <code>true</code> or left out; a column with\n<code>filterCaseSensitive</code> always matches case, whatever the state has. A <code>BETWEEN</code>&#39;s value is\n<code>[from, to]</code>, an open end <code>null</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/header-filters\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<p>Filter as you type (<code>filterOnType</code>) is the user&#39;s, <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>: listed only when it differs from\nthe column&#39;s default (its <code>filterOnType</code>, else the grid&#39;s), so <code>resetState()</code> goes back to the config&#39;s.\nSee <a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">Filter as you type</a>.</p>\n<p>A column that filters as shown - a display format such as <code>weekIso</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a> - stores its value as any date column does: a\n<code>Date</code>, the <strong>start of the period typed</strong>. <code>2026-W47</code> is Monday 2026-11-16 00:00, <code>2026-11</code> November 1,\n<code>2026-W40..2026-W47</code> the Mondays of both weeks (<code>[from, to]</code>). The cell shows it in the format again,\n<code>2026-W47</code>; through JSON the Date is its ISO text (<code>&#39;2026-11-15T23:00:00.000Z&#39;</code> in Oslo), which the cell\nreads the same. The conditions of the whole week are in <code>filter</code>: the state has both, as <code>getState()</code>\ngives them. A state of a week typed when the column was shown as weeks, set after its format changed,\nshows the date in the new format - the filter is what was saved until the header filter runs again.</p>\n<p>To set a filter from code that the header also shows, set both:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { and, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ngridInterface.setState({\n    filter: and(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway')),\n    headerFilters: { country: { value: 'Norway' } }\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-quick-dates-in-the-state\">Quick dates in the state</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">quick date</a> picked in a date column&#39;s filter menu - This month, Last\nweek, Overdue ... - is kept as itself, <code>datePreset</code>, with the operator <code>BETWEEN</code> and the days it was\ncomputed to last:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridState } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// getState() on 22 November 2026, after Quick dates › This month on orderDate\nconst saved: Pick&lt;GridState, 'headerFilters'&gt; = {\n    headerFilters: {\n        orderDate: { value: [new Date(2026, 10, 1), new Date(2026, 10, 30)], operator: 'BETWEEN', datePreset: 'thisMonth' }\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>It is <strong>relative</strong>: <code>setState()</code> (and <code>initialState</code>, <code>resetState()</code>) computes its days from the day it\nis set - the same state set on 2 December filters on December - and its <code>BETWEEN</code> replaces the\ncolumn&#39;s conditions in the state&#39;s <code>filter</code> (in the filter the grid has, when the state has none), the\nother conditions kept. The <code>value</code> stored is only what it was last; give <code>value: null</code> and the grid\nfills it in. So <code>setState({ headerFilters: { orderDate: { value: null, datePreset: &#39;today&#39; } } })</code>\nfilters on today, without a <code>filter</code>. <code>filter</code> itself is plain - <code>getFilter()</code> and\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/a-quick-date\"><code>toSqlWhere()</code></a> see the dates of the day - and through JSON its Dates are ISO\ntext, as any. A quick date a column can not take (not a date column, no <code>BETWEEN</code> in its\n<code>filterOperators</code>, an id that is none) is left out, the rest of the header filter kept. A page open\nover midnight: <code>gridInterface.refreshDatePresets()</code>, which the grid calls when the window gets the\nfocus back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-views\">Views</h2>\n<p>A view is a snapshot of the state under a name: &quot;My open orders&quot;, &quot;Late deliveries&quot;. The user saves\nthe views they need and switches between them in the footer - the columns, widths, filters, sorting,\ngrouping, formats and colour rules, the totals row and the detail form of each come with it. A view is\nplain JSON, a <code>GridView</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridView } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst view: GridView = {\n    id: 'mine', // unique in the list, never shown\n    name: 'My open orders',\n    state: gridInterface.getState(), // what it applies\n    readonly: false, // true: a view of yours the user can apply, not change\n    default: false // true: the view the grid starts in\n};</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"views\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-views-button\">The views button</h3>\n<p><code>views</code> or <code>viewsStorageKey</code> in the config shows the views button at the left end of the footer, on\nthe line of the filter - the first stop of Tab in the footer. It shows the name of the view shown,\n<strong>Standard</strong> when none is: the config&#39;s state (<code>initialState</code> and <code>layout</code>). A <code>*</code> after the name says\nthe state changed since the view was applied or saved - a filter, a column moved or resized, a sort; its\nname for a screen reader says it in words, &quot;View: My open orders (changed)&quot;. A click opens the views\nmenu: the views to switch to - one click - then <em>Save</em>, <em>Save as new view…</em>, <em>Revert changes</em>,\n<em>Rename…</em>, <em>Delete</em> and <em>Default view</em>, see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-views-menu\">The views menu</a> - the default\nview can be picked on the Defaults tab of the grid config dialog too. On a narrow\ngrid the filter goes first: the name is cut where the filter&#39;s row starts, its tooltip has all of it.\n<code>viewsButton: false</code> hides the button, the API below still works.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-where-the-views-are-kept\">Where the views are kept</h3>\n<p>The grid keeps the list; where it is stored is the app&#39;s choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>localStorage</strong>: <code>viewsStorageKey: &#39;orders-grid-views&#39;</code> - read when the grid is made, written\neach time the list changes. Stored are the views that are not readonly, and which one is the default.\nA browser that refuses (blocked storage) gives a warning in the console, the views stay until the\npage is left.</li>\n<li><strong>a backend</strong>: <code>views-change</code> sends the list each time the user changes it, give it back as\n<code>views</code> next time - or later with <code>setViews()</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridView } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const config: GridConfig;\n\nconst saved: GridView[] = await (await fetch('/api/settings/orders-grid-views')).json();\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config: { ...config, views: saved }, datasource });\n\ngridInterface.on('views-change', async ({ views }) =&gt; {\n    await fetch('/api/settings/orders-grid-views', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(views.filter((view) =&gt; !view.readonly))\n    });\n});</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Views of yours</strong> go in <code>views</code> with <code>readonly: true</code>: views the company shares, say. The user\napplies them and can save one as a view of their own (<em>Save as new view…</em>), not save over, rename or\ndelete it. With <code>viewsStorageKey</code> they come from the config every time - change them and every user\ngets the new ones - while the user&#39;s own come from the storage once the user has saved one: a view of\n<code>views</code> that is not readonly and was deleted stays deleted.</p>\n<p>A view keeps the layout it was saved with. When the columns of the config change much, put a version\nin the storage key, as for a saved state (see <a href=\"#guide/state/saving-it\">Saving it</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-applying-saving-modified\">Applying, saving, modified</h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const late = gridInterface.saveViewAs('Late deliveries'); // the state now, the view shown from now on\ngridInterface.applyView(late.id); // shows it\ngridInterface.getCurrentView()?.name; // 'Late deliveries'\n\ngridInterface.setState({ sorting: [{ attribute: 'dueDate', ascending: true }] });\ngridInterface.isViewModified(); // true: the * on the button\ngridInterface.saveView(); // saved over the view shown\ngridInterface.renameView(late.id, 'Late');\ngridInterface.setDefaultView(late.id); // the grid starts in it next time\ngridInterface.deleteView(late.id);\ngridInterface.applyView(null); // Standard: the config's state, as resetState()</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A view is all of the state.</strong> Applied, what its <code>state</code> leaves out is the config&#39;s state - its\n<code>initialState</code> and <code>layout</code> - not what was shown before: a view of a filter and a sort only is the\nconfig&#39;s columns with that filter and sort.</li>\n<li><strong>Modified</strong> compares the state as JSON with the one the view was applied or saved with. Opening and\nclosing groups is no change (the expanded groups are left out); the current row, the scroll\nposition and Find are no state. Applying the view again (<em>Revert changes</em>) takes the change back.</li>\n<li><strong><code>setState()</code> from code is a change</strong> like the user&#39;s: the view stays the view shown, marked as\nchanged. The grid config dialog&#39;s Apply too.</li>\n<li><strong><code>resetState()</code></strong> goes back to the config&#39;s state: Standard, no view shown - also when the grid\nstarted in a default view.</li>\n<li><strong>The default view</strong> (<code>default: true</code>, one at most) is applied when the grid is made, instead of\n<code>initialState</code> alone - on top of it. One that can not be applied (a format that does not fit its\nfield any more) is passed by with a warning in the console: the grid starts in the config&#39;s state.</li>\n<li><strong>Deleting the view shown</strong> leaves the state as it is: Standard is shown, marked as changed - <em>Save\nas new view…</em> makes it a view again. <code>setViews()</code> without the view shown does the same.</li>\n<li>A readonly view, or no view shown, can not be saved over, renamed or deleted: those throw a\n<code>TypeError</code>, and the menu greys them out. An id no view has throws too.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"datasource","title":"Datasource","html":"<p>The <code>Datasource</code> holds the rows. It sorts, filters and groups them, keeps the selection and the\ncurrent row, and tracks the edits. The grid shows what the datasource gives it, and most of what you\ndo from code goes through the datasource.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-making-one\">Making one</h2>\n<p>Every option is optional, <code>new Datasource()</code> works.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id', // the field that identifies a row\n    fields: {\n        age: 'number',\n        born: { type: 'date', label: 'Born' },\n        lastLogin: 'datetime',\n        active: 'boolean'\n    },\n    formatters: { date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }, // the default formatter of every date field, by its id\n    selectionMode: 'multiple' // the grid showing it uses this\n});</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>key</code></td>\n<td>the field that identifies a row, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/keys\">Keys</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fields</code></td>\n<td>the type, label and formatter id of each field that is not plain text, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/fields-and-types\">Fields and types</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formatters</code></td>\n<td>the id of the default formatter per type, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>selectionMode</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> (default), <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;none&#39;</code>. A grid showing the datasource uses it, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/selection\">Selection</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>container</code></td>\n<td>a <code>DataContainer</code> to share the rows with another datasource, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/sharing-the-rows-between-two-datasources\">Sharing the rows</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>All but <code>container</code> are plain JSON: a formatter is named by its id (<code>&#39;dateDDMMYYYY&#39;</code>, or one of your\nown registered with <code>registerFormatter()</code>), so the options can be stored, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>.</p>\n<p>With TypeScript, give it the type of your rows. The rows it hands out are then typed:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Person {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n    born: Date | null;\n}\n\nconst people = new Datasource&lt;Person&gt;({ key: 'id', fields: { born: 'date' } });\nconst names = people.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; row.name); // string[]</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-in-setdata\">Data in: <code>setData()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const rowsFromServer: object[];\ndeclare const moreRows: object[];\n\ndatasource.setData(rowsFromServer); // replaces the rows\ndatasource.setData(moreRows, { add: true }); // adds them after the rows there are\ndatasource.setData(moreRows, { add: true, refilter: false }); // shown even if the filter leaves them out</code></pre>\n<p>The new rows are filtered, sorted and grouped the way the datasource is: a filter that is set - from\ncode, the header filters or the filter editor - applies to them too. <code>refilter: false</code> shows every row\nset, also one the filter would leave out, while the filter stays set (the header filters and the\nfooter still show it) until it runs again. v5 did that by default.</p>\n<p>The datasource wraps each of your objects in a proxy that tracks its edits. The rows it hands out\nare those proxies, not your objects: set values on the rows you get from the datasource, a change\nmade on your original object is not seen as an edit. See <a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<p>Dates must be <code>Date</code> objects, numbers must be numbers. Convert values from JSON when you load them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>interface OrderJson {\n    id: number;\n    amount: string;\n    ordered: string;\n}\ndeclare const json: OrderJson[];\n\ndatasource.setData(json.map((row) =&gt; ({ ...row, amount: Number(row.amount), ordered: new Date(row.ordered) })));</code></pre>\n<p>To delete rows, mark them: <code>markForDeletion(rows)</code> keeps them, struck through, so the delete can be\nundone (<code>unmarkForDeletion(rows)</code>), and <code>getChanges()</code> sends it to the server - see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/deleting-rows\">Deleting rows</a> and <a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a>.\n<code>removeData(rows)</code> and <code>removeAllData()</code> remove rows for good, without tracking: for rows that never\ncame from a server.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"rows-data\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-out\">Data out</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>method</th>\n<th>returns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>getAllData()</code></td>\n<td>every row, unfiltered and in the order they were set. Rows marked for deletion included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getDataRows()</code></td>\n<td>the rows that pass the filter, sorted. No group rows, and the rows of collapsed groups included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getRows()</code></td>\n<td>the rows as the grid shows them: when grouped, a group row in front of each group, and the rows of a collapsed group left out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getRow(n)</code></td>\n<td>one row of <code>getRows()</code>, 0 based. <code>undefined</code> out of range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>length()</code></td>\n<td><code>getRows().length</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getSelectedRows()</code></td>\n<td>the selected rows, in displayed order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>currentEntity</code></td>\n<td>the current row, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate(field, fn, rows?)</code></td>\n<td>the sum, average, smallest or largest number of a field, or how many values it has: over <code>getDataRows()</code>, a group row&#39;s rows or rows you give. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/from-code\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>getRows()</code> and <code>getRow()</code> can return a group row when the datasource is grouped. <code>isGroupRow(row)</code>\ntells them apart, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/group-rows\">Sorting and grouping</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nfor (const row of datasource.getRows()) {\n    if (isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log(row.__groupName, row.__groupTotal);\n    } else {\n        console.log(row.name);\n    }\n}\n\nconst visibleNames = datasource.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; row.name);</code></pre>\n<p><code>getDataRows()</code> and <code>getRows()</code> return the datasource&#39;s own lists. Read them, do not change them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-current-row\">The current row</h3>\n<p><code>datasource.currentEntity</code> is the current row: the one the user clicked, or moved to with the\nkeyboard, or the last one selected from code. It is <code>null</code> until a row is made current. Use it for a\ndetail form next to the grid:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.on('current-entity', () =&gt; {\n    const row = datasource.currentEntity;\n    if (row &amp;&amp; !isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log('show in the form', row);\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.setRowAsCurrentEntity(0); // the first displayed row, 0 based</code></pre>\n<p>When grouped it can be a group row: moving the focus with the keyboard from the first data row up\ninto the header filters makes row 0 current, and that is a group row. Check with <code>isGroupRow()</code>.</p>\n<p><code>setData()</code> sets <code>currentEntity</code> to <code>null</code>, and sends <code>current-entity</code>, when the current row is no\nlonger in the data: replaced rows are new objects, also when a row with the same key comes back. Rows\nadded with <code>add: true</code>, or the same row objects set again, keep it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-keys\">Keys</h2>\n<p><code>key</code> names the field that identifies a row, e.g. <code>&#39;id&#39;</code>. With a key:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the selection follows the key, so it survives <code>setData()</code> with fresh rows from the server</li>\n<li><code>getChanges()</code> gives each edited row by its key, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/change-tracking\">Editing</a></li>\n<li>a row without a value in the key field gets a generated one, a negative number, written into\nthat field. A new row from <code>addNewEmpty()</code> gets one too, until the server gives it its own:\n<code>acceptChanges({ newKeys })</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without a key every row gets a generated key that is not written into the row (<code>row.__KEY</code> reads\nit). It is new every time the data is set, so the selection is lost on <code>setData()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-fields-and-types\">Fields and types</h2>\n<p><code>fields</code> lists the fields that are not plain text, with their type. Writing only the type\n(<code>born: &#39;date&#39;</code>) is the same as <code>born: { type: &#39;date&#39; }</code>. A field that is not listed is <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>,\nlabelled with its name.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>value</th>\n<th>sorts and filters</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>edited with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;text&#39;</code></td>\n<td>string</td>\n<td>as text, filters ignore case</td>\n<td>the text; the first line of a text of several lines</td>\n<td>an input; the editor over the cell for a text of several lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;number&#39;</code></td>\n<td>number</td>\n<td>as numbers</td>\n<td><code>12.5</code>, right aligned</td>\n<td>an input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code></td>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td>true / false</td>\n<td>a checkbox</td>\n<td>the checkbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;date&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>Date</code></td>\n<td>on the day, the time is ignored</td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code></td>\n<td>an input and a date picker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetime&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>Date</code></td>\n<td>on the full timestamp</td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20T08:15:00</code></td>\n<td>an input and a date picker</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong><code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code>?</strong> Both hold a <code>Date</code>. A <code>date</code> field is about the day: a filter\n<code>born = 2026-11-20</code> matches any time on that day, sorting and grouping ignore the time, and a change\nof the time only is not an edit. A <code>datetime</code> field keeps the time everywhere: filters, sorting,\ngrouping, and a change of the time is an edit (to the second).</p>\n<p>A <code>FieldConfig</code> can also have a <code>label</code> (the grid&#39;s column label falls back to it, and so does\n<code>getFilterString()</code>), a <code>formatter</code>: the id of a registered formatter, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>, <code>validate</code>: the field&#39;s validation rules, see below, and\n<code>multiline</code>: a text of several lines, a text field only - see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline\ntext</a>. Two text options lock a field, set by code and not by the grid&#39;s\nconfig: <code>caseSensitive</code> - every filter on it matches case, for a column with an index (see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/match-case\">Match case</a>) - and <code>clobType</code> - a long text the database holds as a LOB (an\nOracle <code>CLOB</code>), filtered with <code>LIKE</code> only (see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operators-a-column-offers\">the operators a column\noffers</a>). One more locks a field of any type: <code>readonly</code> -\nno grid lets the user edit it, and they can not turn that off (see <a href=\"#guide/editing/editing-on\">Editing on</a>).\n<code>getField()</code> and <code>getFormatter()</code> give the formatter object.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        born: { type: 'date', label: 'Born' },\n        price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberComma', validate: ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }] },\n        notes: { multiline: true },\n        projectCode: { caseSensitive: true }, // its index is used\n        memo: { clobType: true }, // an Oracle CLOB: LIKE only\n        created: { type: 'date', readonly: true } // set by the server, never edited\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.getField('born'); // { name: 'born', type: 'date', label: 'Born', formatter: DateFormatterYYYYMMDD, validate: [], multiline: false, caseSensitive: false, clobType: false, readonly: false }\ndatasource.getField('name'); // { name: 'name', type: 'text', label: 'name', formatter: TextFormatter, validate: [], multiline: false, caseSensitive: false, clobType: false, readonly: false }\ndatasource.getField('notes').multiline; // true\ndatasource.getField('price').validate; // [{ id: 'required' }, { id: 'min', value: 0 }]\ndatasource.getFormatter('born').format(new Date(2026, 10, 20)); // '2026-11-20'\ndatasource.getFormatter('price').format(12.5); // '12,5'</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-rows-from-json-parse\">Rows from JSON: <code>parse</code></h3>\n<p>JSON has no dates, and a backend can send numbers as text. <code>setData(rows, { parse: true })</code> reads each\nrow&#39;s values as the types of the fields first - <code>datasource.parseRow(row)</code> does it to one row:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { born: 'date', amount: 'number', active: 'boolean' } });\ndatasource.setData([{ born: '2026-11-22', amount: '12.5', active: 'true' }], { parse: true });\n// born: a Date, 22 November 00:00 local time - amount: 12.5 - active: true</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>becomes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>date</code>, <code>datetime</code></td>\n<td>a <code>Date</code>: <code>&#39;2026-11-22&#39;</code> the start of that day in local time (not UTC, as <code>new Date(&#39;2026-11-22&#39;)</code> reads it); <code>&#39;2026-11-22T08:30:00&#39;</code> and <code>&#39;2026-11-22 08:30:00&#39;</code> local time; with <code>Z</code> or an offset that instant; a number the milliseconds since 1970</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>number</code></td>\n<td>the number of text that is one: <code>&#39;12.5&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;-3&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;1e3&#39;</code> - not <code>&#39;12,5&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;true&#39;</code> / <code>&#39;false&#39;</code> (any case), <code>1</code> / <code>0</code>, <code>&#39;1&#39;</code> / <code>&#39;0&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>&#39;&#39;</code> is <code>null</code> for those types. A value that can not be read as its type stays as it is, the cell shows\nit; the fields not listed and the text fields are not touched. <code>parseRow()</code> changes the object: use it on\nrows before <code>setData()</code> - a row in the datasource is a proxy, its values set would be edits.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-fields-known-later-setfields\">Fields known later: <code>setFields()</code></h3>\n<p>When the columns are known only after the datasource is made - the metadata of a query comes with its\nrows - <code>datasource.setFields(fields)</code> replaces the fields, as <code>fields</code> of the constructor gives them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ key: 'ID' });\n// ... the grid is made on it; then the metadata of the query comes\ndatasource.setFields({ ID: 'number', BORN: 'date', NAME: { type: 'text', label: 'Name' } });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The rows stay. They are filtered, sorted and grouped again by the new types, after the event\n<code>fields-change</code>; a grid on the datasource shows them.</li>\n<li>A field not given is a text field now. A display format of a column that its new type does not take\n(a date format on a text) is dropped, with a warning - its label, placeholder and read only stay.</li>\n<li>The rows that have validation errors are checked again with the new rules; <code>validate()</code> checks the rest.</li>\n<li>What is wrong - an unknown type, a formatter that is not registered, a field another datasource on\nthe same container gives another type - throws, and nothing changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-validation-rules\">Validation rules</h3>\n<p><code>validate</code> lists the field&#39;s rules: the id of a validator as text (<code>&#39;required&#39;</code>), or an object with\nthe id, its options and a message of its own (<code>{ id: &#39;max&#39;, value: 150, message: &#39;too old&#39; }</code>). The\nbuilt-ins are <code>required</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>minLength</code>, <code>maxLength</code> and <code>pattern</code>; your own are\nregistered by id with <code>registerValidator()</code>, like the formatters, so the fields stay plain JSON. An\nid that is not registered throws when the datasource is made.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: {\n        name: { validate: ['required'] },\n        age: { type: 'number', validate: ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }, { id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'too old' }] },\n        zip: { validate: [{ id: 'pattern', value: '^[0-9]{4}$', message: 'Four digits' }] }\n    }\n});\n\npeople.setData([{ id: 1, name: 'Ann', age: 36, zip: '0150' }]);\npeople.getDataRows()[0].age = -1; // an edit is validated\npeople.getErrors(); // [{ row: &lt;Ann&gt;, field: 'age', messages: ['Must be at least 0'] }]\npeople.validate(); // every row, before a save\npeople.hasErrors(); // true</code></pre>\n<p>An edit is validated as it is made, <code>validate()</code> checks every row, and the grid shows the errors on\nthe cells and the row numbers. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a> for the rules, the\nmessages, when they run and rules of your own.</p>\n<p>To sort text with the rules of a language, set a collator: <code>datasource.setLocaleCompare(&#39;nb&#39;)</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-selection\">Selection</h2>\n<p>The user selects rows by clicking them: ctrl+click adds a row, shift+click a range. A click on the\ntop left corner of the grid selects every row, ctrl+click there deselects them. That is the default\n<code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> mode, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/the-selection-mode\">below</a> for the others. From code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.select(1); // the first displayed row: select() counts from 1, getRow() from 0\ndatasource.select(5, { notify: true }); // and the grid scrolls to it\ndatasource.selectNext(); // also selectFirst(), selectPrev(), selectLast()\ndatasource.selectAll(); // 'multiple' mode only\ndatasource.deselectAll({ notify: true }); // notify: the grid shows it at once\n\nconst selected = datasource.getSelectedRows();</code></pre>\n<p><code>select()</code> and the <code>select*()</code> methods make the row current too. A group row can not be selected,\n<code>select()</code> takes the first data row after it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-selection-mode\">The selection mode</h3>\n<p>The datasource has the selection mode, the grid uses it - a grid has no option for it. Give it to\n<code>new Datasource()</code>, or change it later, also while a grid shows the datasource:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({ key: 'id', selectionMode: 'single' });\nconst config: GridConfig = { layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] } };\ncreateGrid(host, { config, datasource: people });\n\npeople.setSelectionMode('none'); // a click makes the row current, selects nothing</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>mode</th>\n<th>a click</th>\n<th>ctrl+click, shift+click</th>\n<th><code>selectAll()</code>, the top left corner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> (default)</td>\n<td>selects the row</td>\n<td>adds a row, selects a range</td>\n<td>selects every displayed row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;single&#39;</code></td>\n<td>selects the row</td>\n<td>selects the row, as a click does</td>\n<td>does nothing, sends no event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n<td>selects nothing</td>\n<td>selects nothing</td>\n<td>does nothing, sends no event</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>In every mode a click makes the row current.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"selection\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-sharing-the-rows-between-two-datasources\">Sharing the rows between two datasources</h2>\n<p>Two datasources can share their rows through one <code>DataContainer</code>, e.g. two grids that show the same\ndata with a different filter. Each has its own filter, sort, grouping and selection. The key goes to\nthe container:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { DataContainer, Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst container = new DataContainer('id');\nconst left = new Datasource({ container, fields: { born: 'date' } });\nconst right = new Datasource({ container, fields: { born: 'date' } });\n\nleft.setData([{ id: 1, name: 'Ann', born: new Date(1990, 4, 17) }]);\nright.reloadDatasource(); // right shows the new rows once it rebuilds its own</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The rows and their edits are the container&#39;s: an edit is the same edit in both, and\n<code>getChanges()</code> gives the same result on both. So is the undo history: <code>undo()</code> on one takes back\nan edit made through the other, and both send <code>history-changed</code>. The grid of the other one shows\nan undo too, it renders its cells again on that event.</li>\n<li>Each datasource keeps its own list of displayed rows. After <code>setData()</code>, <code>removeData()</code>,\n<code>acceptChanges()</code> or <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code> on one, call <code>reloadDatasource()</code> (or <code>filter()</code>)\non the other. Its selection is kept by key: a new row that <code>acceptChanges({ newKeys })</code> gave the\nserver&#39;s key is no longer selected there.</li>\n<li>A field listed in both must have the same type in both, the second datasource throws if not.\nThe validation rules are the container&#39;s too: a field with rules in one datasource is validated\non every edit of the rows, and two datasources that both give a field rules must give it the same.\nThe errors are the container&#39;s, like the changes - <code>validate()</code> on one sends its event on that one\nonly: <code>refresh()</code> the other grid.</li>\n<li>A <code>key</code> given to a datasource with a <code>container</code> must be the container&#39;s key, or it throws.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-events\">Events</h2>\n<p>The datasource sends events when its rows change, are sorted, filtered or grouped, and when the\nselection or the current row changes. Subscribe with <code>datasource.on()</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-rows-from-a-server-upsert\">Rows from a server: <code>upsert()</code></h2>\n<p><code>setData()</code> replaces the rows. To merge what a server sends into the rows the grid shows - a poll\nevery second, changes pushed to the page - use <code>upsert()</code>: it updates the rows by their key, adds the\nnew keys and, with <code>removeMissing</code>, removes the rows the server no longer has. The selection, the\nexpanded groups and the scroll position stay, and an edit the user has not saved is not overwritten.\nSee <a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const rowsFromServer: object[];\n\ndatasource.upsert(rowsFromServer, { removeMissing: true });</code></pre>\n"},{"id":"editing","title":"Editing","html":"<p>The grid is readonly by default. With editing on, the user changes values in the cells, and the\ndatasource tracks every change: which rows are new, edited or marked for deletion, and what changed.\nThe rules of the fields validate the edits, <code>validate()</code> checks every row before a save.\n<code>getChanges()</code> collects the changes for a server, and <code>acceptChanges()</code> takes them in once they are\nsaved.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-editing-on\">Editing on</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false, // the default is true\n    columns: {\n        id: { readonly: true } // this column stays readonly\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['id'], width: 60 },\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['born'], width: 110 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>Or turn it on and off while the grid is shown: <code>gridInterface.updateConfig({ readonly: false })</code>.</p>\n<p>In an editable grid, a cell is readonly when the <code>isReadonly(row, field)</code> hook returns <code>true</code>,\nand editable when it returns <code>false</code>. When it returns nothing (or there is none), the column&#39;s\n<code>readonly</code> option decides. See <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">Grid config</a>\nfor <code>isReadonly</code>. Readonly cells of an editable grid are shown with stripes. What the user may do at\nall - add rows, change values, delete rows - is <code>edit</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">Edit permissions</a>.</p>\n<p>A column&#39;s <code>readonly</code> is a default: the user can turn it off in the grid config dialog (<em>Read only</em>).\nA field that must never be edited - an id, a value the server sets - is locked in the datasource&#39;s\nfields, <code>readonly: true</code> (<code>FieldConfig.readonly</code>). No grid on the datasource lets the user edit it,\nthe <code>isReadonly</code> hook is not asked for it, the import leaves it out, and <em>Read only</em> is checked and\ngreyed out in the dialog. Code still sets its values.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ key: 'id', fields: { id: { type: 'number', readonly: true }, name: 'text' } });</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"editing\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">Edit permissions: add, change, delete</h2>\n<p><code>readonly: false</code> lets the user edit, <code>edit</code> says what of it they may do: add rows, change values,\ndelete rows - any of them. Each is <code>true</code> when it is left out:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    // new rows only: the user adds rows and fills them in, the rows that are there stay as they are\n    edit: { add: true, change: false, delete: false },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const user: { isAdmin: boolean };\n\n// after a login check: an admin may delete too\ngridInterface.updateConfig({ edit: { add: true, change: false, delete: user.isAdmin } });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A permission is yours, not the user&#39;s</strong>: it is not part of the state (<code>getState()</code> has none), and\nno menu or dialog changes it. A column&#39;s <code>readonly</code> is different: it is a default the user can turn\noff in the grid config dialog (<em>Read only</em>) - a field&#39;s <code>readonly</code> (<code>FieldConfig.readonly</code>) is not.</li>\n<li><strong><code>updateConfig({ edit })</code> replaces all three</strong>: one left out is allowed again. The cells follow at\nonce; what is open - a menu, the date picker, a lookup&#39;s list, the editor of a multiline cell - is\nclosed, what was typed in the editor is not saved.</li>\n<li><strong>A <code>TypeError</code></strong> for anything but an object of <code>add</code>, <code>change</code> and <code>delete</code>, each <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>:\na misspelt <code>chnage: false</code> would leave the user allowed without a word.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>permission</th>\n<th><code>false</code> stops</th>\n<th>still allowed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>change</code></td>\n<td>changing a value of a row that was there: typing, the editor of a multiline cell, the date picker, a lookup&#39;s list or grid, a checkbox, the editor of a custom cell (its renderer is told <code>readonly</code>), paste (Ctrl+V, <em>Paste ›</em>), clear (Delete on ranges, <em>Clear ›</em>), fill down (Ctrl+D, the fill handle, <code>fillDown()</code>), the <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">detail form</a>. The cells look as in a readonly grid: no stripes</td>\n<td><strong>a new row</strong> (<code>__rowState</code> <code>&#39;N&#39;</code>) is filled in as any row while <code>add</code> is allowed - who may add a row may fill it in. Once <code>acceptChanges()</code> took it in it is a row that was there, locked like the rest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>delete</code></td>\n<td>marking a row that was there for deletion: <em>Delete › Row</em> and <em>Rows (selected rows)</em> are greyed out on them - the menu keeps its shape - and Delete on a row number leaves them</td>\n<td>taking out <strong>a new row</strong>: it is not on the server, nothing is deleted - it is the undo of an add. <em>Undo (selected rows)</em> takes a mark off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>add</code></td>\n<td>nothing in the grid: the grid has no add of its own. Your add button follows it (below), and with <code>change: false</code> too the new rows your code adds can not be filled in</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>With <code>change</code> and <code>add</code> both <code>false</code> no value can change at all: the row menu has no <em>Paste ›</em> and\n<em>Clear ›</em>, there is no fill handle, and Ctrl+V, Delete and Ctrl+D are the browser&#39;s - as in a\nreadonly grid. <em>Delete ›</em> stays in an editable grid: a new row can always be taken out.</p>\n<p><strong>What every permission leaves</strong>: undo and redo (Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, <em>Undo / Redo ›</em>), the reverts\n(<em>Undo cell edit</em>, <em>Undo row edits</em>, a click on the marker of an edited cell) and taking a deletion\nmark off. They put back what was, they make nothing new - and the history has only what the user was\nallowed to do, and the edits of your code. A permission taken away while the grid is shown leaves the\nhistory as it is: call <code>datasource.clearHistory()</code> with it when a redo must not bring back what was done\nbefore.</p>\n<p><strong>Your code is not stopped</strong>: the permissions are about the grid&#39;s own ways of editing - the user&#39;s\ntyping, the menus, the keys. A value set from code, <code>addNewEmpty()</code>, <code>markForDeletion()</code>, <code>undo()</code> go\nthrough as before, so an &quot;Add row&quot; or &quot;Delete&quot; button of your own follows the permissions as you make it.\n<code>gridInterface.fillDown()</code> goes through the grid, it follows them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const addButton: HTMLButtonElement;\ndeclare const canAdd: boolean;\n\ngridInterface.updateConfig({ edit: { add: canAdd } });\naddButton.hidden = !canAdd;\naddButton.onclick = () =&gt; datasource.addNewEmpty();</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>isReadonly</code> hook, a column&#39;s <code>readonly</code> and <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code> still make cells readonly where\na permission allows the change. The hook can not make a cell of a locked row editable.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"edit-permissions\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-the-user-edits\">How the user edits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Text, number and date cells</strong> are inputs. What the user types is parsed with the field&#39;s\nformatter (<code>parse</code>) and stored in the row as they type: a number field stores a number, a date\nfield a <code>Date</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Emptying a cell</strong> stores what the formatter&#39;s <code>parse(&#39;&#39;)</code> gives: <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for text, <code>null</code> for a\ndate, and <strong><code>0</code> for a number</strong> - the number formatters parse text that is no number as 0. The row\nmenu&#39;s clear (below) stores <code>null</code> in any field. For an emptied number cell to store <code>null</code>, give\nthe field a formatter of your own, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-defaults-per-type\">Formatting</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Enter</strong> in an editable cell moves to the cell below - in a cell of several lines it opens the\neditor, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Date cells</strong> also open a date picker on click, when they are editable: &quot;Use&quot; stores the picked\ndate and time, &quot;Clear&quot; stores <code>null</code>. The picker needs <code>@simple-html/grid/date.css</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Boolean cells</strong> are checkboxes.</li>\n<li><strong>Paste and clear</strong> are in the row menu (right click a cell): paste puts the clipboard text into\nthat column of every selected row, clear sets it to <code>null</code>. Rows where the cell is readonly are\nskipped. The grid sends a <code>paste</code> event per row and a <code>clear</code> event, see <a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lookup columns</strong> (<code>editor</code>): the value is picked from a list - a select, or a dropdown grid - of\na lookup registered with <code>registerLookup()</code>. The cell shows the label of the value (<code>Norway</code> for\n<code>NO</code>) and is not typed in: a character, the button at its right, Alt+ArrowDown or F4 open the list.\nA pick, with the other fields its <code>set</code> copies, is one step of the undo history. See\n<a href=\"#guide/lookups\">Lookups</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Custom cells</strong> (<code>renderer</code>): a renderer of your own draws the cell. Where it only draws, Enter, F2,\ntyping or a double click open the grid&#39;s editor of one line over the cell (Enter saves), and a click\non a date cell the date picker; a renderer can also edit the value itself, a select say. See\n<a href=\"#guide/custom-cells\">Custom cells</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A column with <code>allowPasteClearOnly: true</code> can not be typed in, only pasted into and cleared. Use it\nfor a field the user sets with a picker of your own, such as a <a href=\"#guide/menus/a-dropdown-grid-as-a-block\">dropdown grid</a>.</p>\n<p>The grid sends no event with the edit itself. Read the changes when you need them, with\n<code>getChanges()</code> or the row&#39;s <code>__rowState</code> (below). <code>history-changed</code> says that an edit was recorded\nfor undo, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-multiline-text\">Multiline text</h2>\n<p>Notes, an address, a comment: a text of several lines. The input of a cell can not hold a line break -\nthe browser strips them from the value of <code>&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot;&gt;</code> - so a cell of several lines shows its\nfirst line, and is edited in an editor over the cell, a bigger textarea.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        notes: { label: 'Notes', multiline: true }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['notes'], width: 220 }] },\n    multilineTooltipLines: 10 // the tooltip shows 10 lines, then how many more there are\n};\n\n// every row as high as its notes need, after the data is loaded\ngridInterface.fitRows();</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The option</strong>: <code>multiline: true</code> in the field&#39;s <code>FieldConfig</code>, for a text field only - on another\ntype it throws when the datasource is made. <code>getField(&#39;notes&#39;).multiline</code> says it.</li>\n<li><strong>Automatic</strong>: a text cell whose value has a line break is multiline for that value, without the\noption. CR LF, CR and LF are line breaks. The option is for a field whose values have no line break\nyet: its cells are edited in the editor from the start.</li>\n<li><strong>The cell</strong> shows the first line, readonly (no typing in it), with a marker at its right when there\nare more lines. The rows keep their height, unless they are made taller (below).</li>\n<li><strong>The tooltip</strong> of the cell shows the first 5 lines of the text, then how many more there are -\n<code>… 7 more lines</code> (the text <code>cell.multiline.moreLines</code>), one line more is shown instead. The empty\nlines count, the ones at the end are left out; a line longer than 100 characters is cut with <code>…</code>.\nThe validation errors come first when the cell has any, an empty line between them. More or fewer\nlines: <code>multilineTooltipLines</code> in the config, a whole number of 1 or more (anything else throws a\n<code>TypeError</code>). The tooltip of the <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">edited marker</a> shows the original value\nthe same way.</li>\n<li><strong>Taller rows</strong>: <code>gridInterface.fitRows()</code>, or &quot;Fit row height&quot; / &quot;Fit all rows&quot; in the row menu,\nmakes a row as many lines high as its multiline cells have (at most 20); <code>resetRowHeights()</code> and\n&quot;Reset row height&quot; take it back. In a taller row the cell shows its lines under the first one, as\nmany as there is room for, and the marker only when some are not shown. The lines read as a\nparagraph: the first is the cell&#39;s input, <code>cellHeight</code> high, each line after it\n<code>multilineLineHeight</code> (15px, about 1.35 times the font) - so a note of three lines is a row of\n52px. The editor has the same line height. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-widths-and-row-heights\">Column widths and row heights</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Opening the editor</strong>: Enter, F2, a double click - or typing a character, in a cell that can be\nedited: the editor opens with the text and the character at its end (nothing is replaced). A click\nonly focuses the cell; Tab, Alt+arrows and the row menu (Shift+F10) work as in any cell.</li>\n<li><strong>In the editor</strong> the textarea has the text and the focus, the caret at the end. It is as wide as\nthe cell, at least 300px, six lines high, and can be resized. It is put over the cell, its top left\ncorner on the cell&#39;s, and kept in the window.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>in the editor</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>a new line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Enter, Cmd+Enter</td>\n<td>saves and closes, the focus back in the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes without saving, the focus back in the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>saves and closes, and goes on to the next / previous cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a click outside it</td>\n<td>saves and closes, the focus goes where the click put it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the ✕ at the right of the hint under it</td>\n<td>saves and closes, the focus back in the cell - for a mouse and a finger, which has no Ctrl+Enter. In a readonly editor it closes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scrolling the grid (the wheel, a swipe, the scrollbars)</td>\n<td>saves and closes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Opening a menu or another editor saves and closes it too - one is open at a time - and so does\n<code>destroy()</code>, or the grid leaving the page.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Saving</strong> writes the row once, and only when the text changed: one step of the <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">undo\nhistory</a> (Ctrl+Z in the cell takes it back), validated as an edit, and the cell gets\nthe edited marker. The line breaks of a saved text are LF (<code>\\n</code>): the textarea gives them so (the\nHTML spec normalises the value of a textarea to LF), a text from a server with CR LF keeps them\nuntil the user changes it. The value goes through the field&#39;s formatter (<code>parse</code>), as what is typed\nin a cell.</li>\n<li><strong>Readonly</strong>: in a readonly grid, a readonly column or cell (<code>readonly</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> hook) and\na column with <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code>, Enter, F2 and a double click open the editor read-only, to read\nthe whole text: Esc (or Ctrl+Enter, Tab, a click outside) closes it. Typing opens nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Paste</strong> from the row menu writes the clipboard text as it is, line breaks and all.</li>\n<li>The texts of the editor are <code>cell.multiline.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/multiline-cells\">Texts</a>, the\ncolour of the marker <code>--simple-html-grid-multiline-color</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/multiline-text\">Theming</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"multiline\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-edits-from-code\">Edits from code</h2>\n<p>Set the value on a row you got from the datasource. The grid shows it on its own, in the next\nanimation frame (see <a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>) - no <code>refresh()</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nrow.name = 'Anna';</code></pre>\n<p>The datasource wraps your objects in proxies that track the edits. A value set on the object you\ngave to <code>setData()</code> reaches the row, but it is not tracked as an edit. Always use the rows the\ndatasource hands out: <code>getDataRows()</code>, <code>getSelectedRows()</code>, <code>currentEntity</code>, the <code>rowData</code> of an\nevent.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-new-rows\">New rows</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.addNewEmpty(); // an empty row at the end, selected, and the grid scrolls to it\ndatasource.addNewEmpty({ company: 'NEW COMPANY' }); // with values\ndatasource.addNewEmpty({ company: 'NEW COMPANY' }, { scrollTo: false }); // selection unchanged</code></pre>\n<p>A new row is listed in <code>getChanges().newEntities</code>. When the datasource has a <code>key</code>, the key field\nof the new row gets a generated negative number. When grouped, the row goes into its group and the\ngroup is expanded.</p>\n<p>Many rows at once: <code>addNewRows()</code> - the rows are sorted and grouped once, not once per row. The grid&#39;s\n<a href=\"#guide/import\">import</a> adds its rows with it.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const added = datasource.addNewRows([{ company: 'NEW 1' }, { company: 'NEW 2' }]); // the first one selected\ndatasource.validate(added); // new rows are not validated until they are edited</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-deleting-rows\">Deleting rows</h2>\n<p>Deleting a row marks it, so the delete can be undone:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.markForDeletion(datasource.getSelectedRows()); // a row or a list of rows\ndatasource.unmarkForDeletion(datasource.getSelectedRows()); // undo\ndatasource.markAllForDeletion(); // every row\ndatasource.unmarkAllForDeletion(); // undo for every row</code></pre>\n<p>A row marked for deletion stays in the grid, with the state <code>&#39;D&#39;</code> - struck through, with a trash can\non its row number, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a> - until the changes are saved.\nIt is listed in <code>getChanges().deletedEntities</code>. Unmarked, it is as it was before: its edits are kept.</p>\n<p>A new row is marked too. While it is marked it is in none of the lists of <code>getChanges()</code>: it was\nnever saved, there is nothing to send. Unmarked, it is a new row again.</p>\n<p><strong>From the keyboard</strong>: a click on a row number selects the row and puts the focus on the number, as a\nspreadsheet&#39;s row header (Shift+click and Ctrl+click select more). There <strong>Delete</strong> or <strong>Backspace</strong>\nmarks the selected rows for deletion - the number&#39;s row alone when it is not selected - and Ctrl+Z on\nthe number takes it back. In a cell Delete stays the input&#39;s own key (or empties a range of cells).\nA row the <code>isReadonly</code> hook locks is left out, as below, and with <code>edit: { delete: false }</code> every row\nbut a new one, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">Edit permissions</a>. Only in an editable grid.</p>\n<p>In an editable grid the row menu (right click a cell) has a <strong>Delete ›</strong> submenu: <em>Rows (selected\nrows)</em> marks the selected rows, <em>Undo (selected rows)</em> takes the mark off - shown when a selected row\nis marked. Ctrl+Z takes a delete back too, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>. A row the <code>isReadonly</code> hook returns <code>true</code> for in every column is locked: the menu does not\nmark it. The columns&#39; <code>readonly</code> option does not lock a row, so a grid of readonly columns can still\ndelete rows - <code>edit: { delete: false }</code> is what stops deleting. The entries are <code>row.delete</code>, <code>row.delete.rows</code> and <code>row.delete.undo</code>, change or hide\nthem with the <a href=\"#guide/menus\">menus hook</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>datasource.acceptChanges()</code> removes the marked rows once the deletes are saved, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a>. <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code> removes them too, and leaves the other changes.</li>\n<li><code>datasource.removeData(rows)</code> and <code>removeAllData()</code> remove rows for good, without marking them:\nno undo, and not listed in <code>getChanges()</code>. Use them for rows that never came from a server.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-change-tracking\">Change tracking</h2>\n<p>Every row has a state, <code>row.__rowState</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>state</th>\n<th>the row</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;N&#39;</code></td>\n<td>is new: <code>addNewEmpty()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;M&#39;</code></td>\n<td>has a field whose value differs from the original</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;D&#39;</code></td>\n<td>is marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>undefined</code></td>\n<td>is as it was set</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>getChanges()</code> returns copies of the changed rows, what to send to a server:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Person {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n    born: Date | null;\n}\n\nconst people = new Datasource&lt;Person&gt;({ key: 'id', fields: { born: 'date' } });\npeople.setData([\n    { id: 1, name: 'Ann', born: new Date(1990, 4, 17) },\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', born: new Date(1985, 0, 2) }\n]);\n\nconst [ann, bob] = people.getDataRows();\nann.name = 'Anna';\nbob.born = new Date(1985, 0, 2, 13, 30); // the same day: no edit on a date field\npeople.addNewEmpty({ name: 'Cid' }, { scrollTo: false });\n\nconst { newEntities, modifiedEntities, deletedEntities } = people.getChanges();\n// newEntities:      [{ name: 'Cid', id: -1 }] - a generated key, the number varies\n// modifiedEntities: [{ id: 1, name: 'Anna' }] - the key and the changed fields\n// deletedEntities:  []</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>newEntities</code> and <code>deletedEntities</code> are copies of the whole rows. A row in <code>modifiedEntities</code> has\nthe key (under the key field&#39;s name, or <code>__KEY</code> without a key) and only the fields that changed.</li>\n<li>The copies are shallow and keep the types: a <code>Date</code> stays a <code>Date</code>. <code>JSON.stringify()</code> turns it\ninto its ISO text.</li>\n<li>A field set back to its original value is not a change any more. Setting an empty field\n(<code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code>) to <code>&#39;&#39;</code> is not a change either.</li>\n<li>A <code>date</code> field compares on the day: a date typed into a cell gets the current time of day, and\ntyping the same day again is no change. A <code>datetime</code> field, and a field not listed in <code>fields</code>,\ncompares to the second: a change of the time only is a change.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After a save, <code>acceptChanges()</code> makes the changes the new starting point, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a>.\nBefore it, <code>validate()</code> checks the rows, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-one-kind-of-change-the-rows-the-fields\">One kind of change, the rows, the fields</h3>\n<p><code>getChanges()</code>, <code>hasChanges()</code> and <code>getChangedRows()</code> take the kinds of change they look at,\n<code>{ new?, modified?, deleted? }</code> (<code>ChangeKinds</code>):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>kinds</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>left out, <code>null</code>, <code>{}</code></td>\n<td>all of them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ deleted: true }</code></td>\n<td>only those set to <code>true</code>: the rows marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ new: true, modified: true }</code></td>\n<td>the new and edited rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ deleted: false }</code></td>\n<td>only kinds <code>false</code>: all but those - the new and edited rows too</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Person {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n    city: string;\n}\ndeclare const datasource: Datasource&lt;Person&gt;;\n\n// the lists not asked for are []: one request for the deletions, one for the rest\nconst { deletedEntities } = datasource.getChanges({ deleted: true });\nconst { newEntities, modifiedEntities } = datasource.getChanges({ deleted: false });\n\n// a confirm only when something is deleted\nconst confirmFirst = datasource.hasChanges({ deleted: true });\n\n// the rows themselves, not copies: validate what a save sends\ndatasource.validate(datasource.getChangedRows({ deleted: false }));\n\n// a row's edited fields and what they were\nconst [ann] = datasource.getDataRows();\ndatasource.getChangedFields(ann); // ['name', 'city'] - in the order they were edited\ndatasource.getOriginalValue(ann, 'name'); // 'Ann' - the value now when the field is not edited</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getChangedRows()</code> lists the rows <code>getChanges()</code> would - a new row marked for deletion is in none, a\nrow edited back to its original values neither - in the order of the data.</li>\n<li><code>getChangedFields(row)</code> is <code>[]</code> for a new row (it has no original) and a row not edited;\n<code>getOriginalValue(row, field)</code> is undefined for a new row. After <code>acceptChanges()</code> the values now are\nthe originals.</li>\n<li>Kinds that are not <code>{ new?, modified?, deleted? }</code> of booleans (or <code>null</code>) throw a <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-throwing-changes-away\">Throwing changes away</h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.resetData(); // every edit undone, marked rows unmarked, new rows removed\ndatasource.resetDataSelectionOnly(); // the same, for the selected rows only</code></pre>\n<p>To undo a delete and keep the edits, use <code>unmarkForDeletion(rows)</code>. To revert one cell, or rows\nwithout removing the new ones, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>.\n<code>resetData()</code> clears the undo history; <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> is a step of it, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</h3>\n<p>The grid shows the state of each row on its row number, and marks the edited cells. Both are on by\ndefault:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>the row</th>\n<th>its row number</th>\n<th>its cells</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>new</td>\n<td>a plus</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>edited</td>\n<td>a pencil</td>\n<td>an edited cell: a small triangle in its top left corner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>marked for deletion</td>\n<td>a trash can, the number struck through</td>\n<td>the text struck through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>with an error</td>\n<td>an exclamation mark, and a red bar</td>\n<td>a cell with an error: a red border and a tint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in conflict with the server</td>\n<td>two arrows</td>\n<td>a field in conflict: a larger triangle in the conflict colour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One icon</strong>: a row marked for deletion shows the trash can (it is not saved, its errors do not\nstop a save), else a row with an error the exclamation mark, else a row in conflict the arrows,\nelse the plus or the pencil.</li>\n<li><strong>A conflict</strong> is a value the server changed while the user had it edited, see\n<a href=\"#guide/live-data/how-the-grid-shows-them\">Live data</a>: the marker&#39;s tooltip has the server&#39;s value, a\nclick takes it.</li>\n<li><strong>The tooltip of the row number</strong> has the state, the conflicts, then the errors, a line each: <code>Edited</code> and\n<code>Age: Must be at least 18</code>. A screen reader reads them with the row number, see\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/what-a-screen-reader-gets\">Accessibility</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>An edited cell</strong> is a cell whose value differs from its original value, as <code>getChanges()</code> compares\nthem: a cell edited back is not edited. A new row has no markers, all of it is new.</li>\n<li><strong>The marker&#39;s tooltip</strong> has the original value, shown with the field&#39;s formatter (a boolean as\n<code>true</code> / <code>false</code>: give the field a formatter of your own for other words, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>), after the cell&#39;s errors. A blank value is <code>(blank)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A click on the marker</strong> puts the original value back - the row menu&#39;s <em>Undo cell edit</em>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>. In an editable grid only: a readonly grid\nshows the markers of the rows it is given, a click does nothing. The marker is not a stop of Tab\nand a screen reader does not see it; Ctrl+Z takes an edit back from the keyboard.</li>\n<li><strong>They follow the edits</strong>: as the user types, after undo and redo, a paste, a delete, and an edit\nfrom code.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"#sample/editing\">editing sample</a> shows them. Both can be turned off:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    rowStatus: false, // the plain row number: no icon, no state in its tooltip\n    editedMarker: false, // no markers\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>rowStatus</code> makes the row numbers 14px wider, for the icon. <code>rowStatus: false</code> gives them the width\nthey had.</li>\n<li><strong>The classes are there either way</strong>, for a look of your own: <code>row-is-new</code>, <code>row-is-modified</code> and\n<code>row-is-deleted</code> on the element around the row number, <code>simple-html-row-error</code> on the row number of\na row with an error, <code>simple-html-cell-edited</code> on an edited cell. With <code>rowStatus</code> the cells of a row\nmarked for deletion get <code>simple-html-cell-deleted</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The colours are variables of the theme</strong>: <code>--simple-html-grid-new-color</code>,\n<code>--simple-html-grid-edited-color</code> (the pencil and the markers), <code>--simple-html-grid-deleted-color</code>\nand <code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code>, each with a light and a dark colour - see\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/the-row-state-and-errors\">Theming</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A look of your own, with <code>rowStatus: false</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.row-is-modified .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#4f46e5, #a5b4fc);\n}\n.row-is-deleted .simple-html-selector-text {\n    text-decoration: line-through;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Or show <code>__rowState</code> in a column of its own: put <code>&#39;__rowState&#39;</code> in the layout.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-changed-rows-only\">Changed rows only</h3>\n<p><code>filterChangedOnly(true)</code> shows only the changed rows - new, edited and marked for deletion - of the\nrows the filter lets through: the changes made before a save, to check them.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.filter(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'));\ndatasource.filterChangedOnly(true); // the changed rows in Norway\ndatasource.isChangedOnly(); // true\ndatasource.filterChangedOnly(false); // every row in Norway</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In the grid</strong>: the row menu&#39;s <em>View › Changed rows only</em> turns it on and off (a ✓ while it is\non), and the footer shows a chip <em>Changed rows only</em> in front of the filter&#39;s. The footer&#39;s <em>Clear</em>\nand the filter menu&#39;s <em>Clear All Filters</em> turn it off with the filter.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not part of the filter</strong>, it is ANDed with it: <code>getFilter()</code>, <code>getFilterString()</code>, the filter\neditor and the grid&#39;s state (<code>GridState</code>) do not have it, and a header filter, the filter editor\nand <code>filter()</code> leave it on. It is not saved with the state: the changes are not either.</li>\n<li><strong>It is applied when the rows are filtered</strong>, as the filter is: <code>filter()</code>, a header filter,\n<code>setData()</code>, <code>reloadDatasource()</code>, <code>markForDeletion()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code> ... An edit, undo and a\nrevert do not filter the rows: <strong>a row edited back to its original values stays shown until the\nrows are filtered next</strong>, it does not disappear while the user types in it. A row changed from code\nwhile it is not shown comes in on the next filter run too.</li>\n<li>After <code>setData()</code> with the rows from the server, or <code>acceptChanges()</code>, no row is changed: nothing\nis shown until it is turned off. The footer&#39;s chip says why.</li>\n<li>It is the datasource&#39;s, as the filter is: other datasources on the same container are not filtered.</li>\n<li>The filter menu&#39;s value list, with <em>Only values in filtered rows</em>, lists the values of the\nchanged rows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For one state only, filter on <code>__rowState</code>: <code>where(&#39;__rowState&#39;, &#39;IN&#39;, [&#39;N&#39;])</code> is the new rows. That\nis a condition of the filter, in <code>getFilter()</code> and the state.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</h2>\n<p>The datasource keeps a history of the edits: <code>undo()</code> takes the last step back, <code>redo()</code> does it\nagain. In the grid the user has them too:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> (Cmd+Z on a Mac) undoes, <strong>Ctrl+Y</strong> and <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Z</strong> (Cmd+Y, Cmd+Shift+Z) redo - with\nthe focus in a cell of an editable grid. Not in the header filters: the keys are the input&#39;s own\nthere. The filter editor has an undo history of its own, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/undo-and-redo-in-the-filter-editor\">Filtering</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The focus goes to what changed</strong>, as in a spreadsheet: after Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y and the row menu&#39;s\n<em>Undo last change</em> / <em>Redo</em> the grid scrolls to the first cell of the step - the first change made\n(the top left cell of a paste) whose row is displayed - puts it in the middle of the view when it was\nout of it, focuses it and makes its row the current row. A deletion mark, or a field that is not in\nthe layout, is its row in the column of the cell the key was pressed in (the menu opened on). When\nno row of the step is displayed - filtered out, in a collapsed group - the focus stays.\n<code>datasource.undo()</code> / <code>redo()</code> from code (an Undo button of your own) do not move it.</li>\n<li><strong>While the user types in a cell, Ctrl+Z is the input&#39;s own undo</strong> of the typing. The grid writes\nevery key to the row as it is typed, so what was typed in the cell is the last step of the history;\nonce the typing is undone back to the value the cell started with, or the focus left the cell,\nCtrl+Z undoes the history.</li>\n<li>The row menu (right click a cell) starts with the submenu <em>Undo / Redo ›</em>: <em>Undo last change\n(Ctrl+Z)</em> and <em>Redo (Ctrl+Y)</em> - <em>(⌘Z)</em> and <em>(⇧⌘Z)</em> on a Mac, see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/the-ids\">Texts</a> - greyed out when there is nothing to undo or redo, and the reverts, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>. The ids are <code>row.edit</code> (the submenu),\n<code>row.edit.undo</code>, <code>row.edit.redo</code>, <code>row.edit.revertCell</code>, <code>row.edit.revertRow</code> and\n<code>row.edit.revertRows</code>, change or hide them with the <a href=\"#guide/menus\">menus hook</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nrow.name = 'Changed';\n\ndatasource.canUndo(); // true\ndatasource.undo(); // the name it had\ndatasource.redo(); // 'Changed' again</code></pre>\n<p><strong>A step</strong> is what one undo takes back:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>what the user typed in one cell, from when it got the focus until the focus left it</li>\n<li>a paste or a clear from the row menu, over every selected row</li>\n<li>a paste (Ctrl+V), a Delete or a clear of ranges of cells, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a></li>\n<li>a delete or its undo: <code>markForDeletion()</code>, <code>markAllForDeletion()</code>, <code>unmarkForDeletion()</code>,\n<code>unmarkAllForDeletion()</code> - from the row menu or from code</li>\n<li>a revert: <code>resetField()</code>, <code>resetRows()</code>, <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code></li>\n<li><strong>what code sets on the rows in one synchronous run</strong>: <code>row.name = &#39;x&#39;</code> is an edit like the user&#39;s,\nand is recorded. The changes made before the code awaits are one step, after an <code>await</code> the next\none starts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A value set that does not change the field - as the change tracking compares, so a <code>date</code> field on\nthe day - is not recorded.</p>\n<p><strong>Undo sets the values back the way an edit does</strong>: the change tracking follows (a value back at its\noriginal is no change, the <code>__rowState</code> goes back with it) and so does the validation - an error an\nedit made goes away when it is undone. The displayed rows are not filtered, sorted or grouped again,\nthe rows stay where they are, as after an edit. The grid renders its cells again.</p>\n<p><strong>What is not in it</strong>: new rows - <code>addNewEmpty()</code>, <code>setData(rows, { add: true })</code>. Undo never removes\na row and never adds one back; the edits of a new row are steps like any other. Rows removed for good\n(<code>removeData()</code>, <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code>, a new row <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> removed) take their\nchanges out of the history, and a step left without changes is dropped.</p>\n<p><strong>It is cleared</strong> - nothing to undo or redo, the edits stay as they are - by <code>setData()</code> without\n<code>add</code>, <code>removeAllData()</code>, <code>resetData()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code> (after a save the saved values are the\noriginals, also when only some rows were accepted) and <code>clearHistory()</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>The last 100 steps</strong> are kept. A new step empties what there is to redo.</p>\n<p>The history is the <code>DataContainer</code>&#39;s, like the edits: datasources that share a container share it.</p>\n<p><code>history-changed</code> is sent when the history changes - for Undo and Redo buttons of your own:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const undoButton: HTMLButtonElement;\ndeclare const redoButton: HTMLButtonElement;\n\nconst showHistory = () =&gt; {\n    undoButton.disabled = !datasource.canUndo();\n    redoButton.disabled = !datasource.canRedo();\n};\ndatasource.on('history-changed', showHistory);\nshowHistory();\nundoButton.onclick = () =&gt; datasource.undo();\nredoButton.onclick = () =&gt; datasource.redo();</code></pre>\n<p>Its <code>info</code> says what changed it, see <a href=\"#guide/events/datasource-events\">Events</a>. A new step is told once\nthe code that made it is done (<code>&#39;edit&#39;</code>, in a microtask): a paste over many rows is one event, and so\nis what is typed in a cell.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</h2>\n<p><code>resetField(row, field)</code> puts one field back to its original value: the value it had when the data\nwas set, or when the changes were last accepted. <code>resetRows(rows)</code> does it for every field of the\nrows, and takes their deletion marks off:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\ndatasource.resetField(row, 'name');\ndatasource.resetRows(datasource.getSelectedRows());</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A revert is a step of the history: <code>undo()</code> brings the edits back.</li>\n<li><strong>A new row is marked for deletion</strong>, with the values it was added with: it is not on the server,\nso that is where a revert takes it. Struck through, it is left out of <code>getChanges()</code>, and it goes\nwith the other marked rows when the changes are saved. It is not removed at once: a revert can be\nundone, and <code>undo()</code> brings the new row back with its edits. <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> removes the\nnew rows it resets, and undo does not bring those back.</li>\n<li>The fields are validated as for an edit.</li>\n<li>A field that is not edited (<code>getChanges()</code> would not list it) is left as it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In an editable grid the row menu (right click a cell) starts with the submenu <em>Undo / Redo ›</em>:\n<em>Undo last change</em> (Ctrl+Z) and <em>Redo</em> (Ctrl+Y) go through the undo history; <em>Undo cell edit</em> puts\nthe original value of the cell right clicked back, greyed out while it is not edited - a click on the\nmarker of an edited cell does the same, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a>;\n<em>Undo row edits</em> puts the row right clicked back - no need to select it first - and\n<em>Undo row edits (selected rows)</em> the selected rows, each greyed out while none of its rows is edited\nor marked for deletion. Like the delete&#39;s undo they also work on readonly cells: they put values back,\nreadonly is about typing new ones.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-validation\">Validation</h2>\n<p>The rules are part of the datasource&#39;s fields, plain JSON like the rest of them - they can be\nstored in a backend with the fields. A field lists its rules in <code>validate</code>: the id of a validator as\ntext, or an object with the id, its options and a message of its own.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: {\n        name: { validate: ['required', { id: 'maxLength', value: 50 }] },\n        age: { type: 'number', validate: ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }, { id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'too old' }] },\n        email: { validate: ['email'] },\n        code: { validate: [{ id: 'pattern', value: '^[A-Z]{2}-\\\\d{3}$', message: 'Like AB-123' }] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The validators are named by id, like the formatters: the built-in ones below, and your own with\n<code>registerValidator()</code>. An id that is not registered throws when the datasource is made, and so does\na built-in rule with a wrong option (<code>min</code> without a number, a <code>pattern</code> that is no regular\nexpression). <code>getField(name).validate</code> gives the rules of a field, written out as objects.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"validation\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-rules-of-a-grids-columns\">Rules of a grid&#39;s columns</h3>\n<p>A column can have rules in the grid&#39;s config too, <code>ColumnConfig.validate</code> - the same rules, for what this\ngrid edits: a datasource shared by two screens, a field of a backend&#39;s metadata without rules, a rule of\nthe screen (a project code of this form). They are <strong>set by code and the user can not change them</strong>: they\nare not in the state or a view - <code>getState()</code> has none, <code>setState()</code> and <code>resetState()</code> keep them,\n<code>updateConfig({ columns })</code> replaces them. The grid config dialog shows them, read only: see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/the-rules-in-the-dialog\">The rules in the dialog</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    columns: {\n        projectCode: { validate: ['required', { id: 'pattern', value: '^[A-Z]{2}-\\\\d{3}$', message: 'Like AB-123' }] },\n        hours: { validate: [{ id: 'min', value: 0 }, { id: 'max', value: 24 }, { id: 'decimals', value: 1 }] },\n        status: { validate: [{ id: 'oneOf', value: ['Open', 'Closed'] }] }\n    },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['projectCode'], width: 120 }, { fields: ['hours'], width: 80 }, { fields: ['status'], width: 100 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>They are <strong>added after the field&#39;s own</strong> (<code>FieldConfig.validate</code>): an edit of a cell or in the detail\nform, an import and <code>datasource.validate()</code> check both, <code>getField(name).validate</code> lists both - a\n<code>&#39;required&#39;</code> of a column gives its cells the stripes. The rows that have errors are validated again when\nthey change.</li>\n<li>Checked when the grid is made, and by <code>updateConfig()</code>: an id that is not registered, or wrong options,\nthrow a <code>TypeError</code> that names the column (<code>GridConfig: columns.hours has the rule &#39;max&#39;, ...</code>). A\ncalculated column has none, it is not edited.</li>\n<li><strong>Two grids on one datasource</strong>: the rules of the last one made (or given its columns) that has some are\nused, and its <code>destroy()</code> gives the other one&#39;s back. A grid without any - a second view of the rows -\nleaves the other one&#39;s in place.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-built-in-rules\">The built-in rules</h3>\n<p>They take their option as <code>value</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>rule</th>\n<th>the value fails when</th>\n<th>message</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;required&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, only spaces, an invalid Date, or 0</td>\n<td><code>Required</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;min&#39;, value: 0 }</code></td>\n<td>it is a number below <code>value</code> - or, with a day (<code>&#39;2026-01-01&#39;</code>), a date before it</td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;max&#39;, value: 150 }</code></td>\n<td>it is a number above <code>value</code> - or, with a day, a date after it (a datetime on the day is within)</td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;integer&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is a number with decimals</td>\n<td><code>Must be a whole number</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;decimals&#39;, value: 2 }</code></td>\n<td>it is a number with more decimals than <code>value</code> (0 to 20)</td>\n<td><code>Must have at most {value} decimals</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;oneOf&#39;, value: [&#39;Open&#39;, &#39;Closed&#39;] }</code></td>\n<td>it is not in the list, compared as text (with its case)</td>\n<td><code>Must be one of {value}</code>: <code>Open, Closed</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;email&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is text that is no email address: something <code>@</code> something with a dot, no spaces</td>\n<td><code>Not an email address</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;minLength&#39;, value: 2 }</code></td>\n<td>it is text shorter than <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value} characters</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;maxLength&#39;, value: 50 }</code></td>\n<td>it is text longer than <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value} characters</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;pattern&#39;, value: &#39;^[A-Z]{2}$&#39; }</code></td>\n<td>it is text the regular expression does not find</td>\n<td><code>Not in the expected format</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;unique&#39;</code></td>\n<td>another row has the same value - see <a href=\"#guide/editing/unique-no-two-rows-with-the-same-value\">Unique</a></td>\n<td><code>Another row has the same value</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;unique&#39;, with: [&#39;year&#39;] }</code></td>\n<td>another row has the same value and the same <code>year</code></td>\n<td><code>Another row has the same value and {with}</code>: <code>Year</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Only spaces fail <code>required</code></strong>: <code>&#39;  &#39;</code> is as empty as <code>&#39;&#39;</code> - tabs and line breaks too. The stripes\nof a required cell show it as empty.</li>\n<li><strong>0 fails <code>required</code>.</strong> The filter takes 0 as blank too (see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/0-is-blank\">0 is blank</a>),\nand an emptied number cell stores 0 - the number formatters parse <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as 0 - so with 0 as a value\na required number would never fail in the grid. <code>false</code> passes: it is a value, and a checkbox has\nno empty state. For a number where 0 is a real value, use a rule of your own (<code>present</code> below).</li>\n<li><strong>The other rules pass an empty value</strong> (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>): an optional field can be\nempty, add <code>&#39;required&#39;</code> to make it fail. They check the values of their kind only - <code>min</code> and\n<code>max</code> numbers and dates, <code>integer</code> and <code>decimals</code> numbers, <code>oneOf</code> any value, the others text - and\npass the rest.</li>\n<li><strong><code>decimals</code> takes the floating point as it is</strong>: <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> (<code>0.30000000000000004</code>) has two decimals.</li>\n<li><strong><code>email</code> is a light check</strong>, not every rule of RFC 5322: a typo like a missing <code>@</code> or <code>.com</code>. Use a\n<code>pattern</code> or a rule of your own for a stricter one.</li>\n<li><strong><code>pattern</code> is not anchored</strong>: <code>&#39;[0-9]&#39;</code> finds a digit anywhere in the text, <code>&#39;^[0-9]+$&#39;</code> is\ndigits only. Written as text, so it stays JSON. In TypeScript a <code>\\</code> is written twice.</li>\n<li>Every rule runs: a value can fail more than one, and gets a message for each.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-unique-no-two-rows-with-the-same-value\">Unique: no two rows with the same value</h3>\n<p><code>&#39;unique&#39;</code> compares the rows: a value that another row has fails, in <strong>every</strong> row that has it - the\nuser sees both, and fixes the one that is wrong. Change one of them (or delete it) and the error goes\nfrom the others too, without a <code>validate()</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        email: { validate: ['required', 'email', { id: 'unique', ignoreCase: true }] },\n        // a code once per year: the error on the code\n        code: { validate: [{ id: 'unique', with: ['year'] }] },\n        year: { type: 'number', label: 'Year' }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>with</code></strong> lists other fields whose values go with it, two or more columns as one check: <code>code</code> with\n<code>with: [&#39;year&#39;]</code> is unique per year. The error is on the field that has the rule; give the rule to\n<code>year</code> as well (<code>with: [&#39;code&#39;]</code>) to mark both cells. The message names the other fields by their\nlabels: <code>Another row has the same value and Year</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A blank value is no duplicate</strong>, as NULL in a UNIQUE constraint of SQL: two empty rows pass - add\n<code>&#39;required&#39;</code> to make them fail. With <code>with</code>, a row with a blank in one of the fields passes.</li>\n<li><strong>How values compare</strong>: text as it is, with its case - <code>ignoreCase: true</code> takes <code>Ann</code> and <code>ANN</code> as the\nsame; dates by their time; numbers and other values as text (<code>3</code> and <code>&#39;3&#39;</code> are the same). 0 is a value.</li>\n<li><strong>The rows</strong> are the rows of the datasource&#39;s container, also the ones a filter hides. A row marked for\ndeletion does not count: it is not saved. Rows set with <code>setData()</code> are not validated (below), and\nneither is a loaded duplicate - until a row is edited to its value, or <code>validate()</code> runs.</li>\n<li><strong>An edit checks the other rows too</strong>: the rows with the old value lose the error when it was theirs,\nthe rows with the new value get it - they are validated again on that field, and the grid repaints them.\nIt looks the value up in an index of the rows, not by going through them on every key.</li>\n<li><strong>An import</strong> compares the rows of the file with the grid&#39;s rows and with each other: two rows of the\nfile with the same code both have the problem.</li>\n<li>The datasource checks the rows it has. A value can still be taken on the server - by a row that is\nnot loaded, or saved by another user meanwhile: keep the unique constraint in the database.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-rules-in-the-dialog\">The rules in the dialog</h3>\n<p>The grid config dialog shows a column&#39;s rules on its General tab, under <em>Read only</em>: a list under\n<em>Validation</em>, read only, with the hint that the application sets them - the field&#39;s own rules\n(<code>FieldConfig.validate</code>) and the grid&#39;s (<code>ColumnConfig.validate</code>). A rule is shown as its own <code>message</code>\n(<code>Like AB-123</code>), else as the built-in&#39;s message (<code>Must be at least 0</code>) - a <code>pattern</code> as its expression,\n<code>unique</code> as <code>Unique</code> (<code>Unique together with Year</code>) - and a rule of your own as the text of\n<code>validation.&lt;its id&gt;</code> when <code>setTexts()</code> has one, else its id. There is no control to change one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-messages\">Messages</h3>\n<p>A rule&#39;s <code>message</code> replaces the default, and <code>{name}</code> in it is replaced by the rule&#39;s option <code>name</code>:\n<code>{ id: &#39;max&#39;, value: 150, message: &#39;at most {value}&#39; }</code> gives <code>at most 150</code>.</p>\n<p>The default messages are texts: <code>&#39;validation.required&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.min&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.max&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;validation.minLength&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.maxLength&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.pattern&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.integer&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;validation.decimals&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.oneOf&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.email&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.unique&#39;</code> and\n<code>&#39;validation.uniqueWith&#39;</code> in <code>TEXTS</code>; a list option is its items with <code>, </code> in a message. Translate\nthem with <code>setTexts()</code> - for the page, the datasource has no grid, so a grid&#39;s <code>texts</code> do not change\nthem. A rule&#39;s <code>message</code> can be a text id as well, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/messages-of-your-own\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({ 'validation.required': 'Må fylles ut', 'validation.max': 'Kan være høyst {value}' });</code></pre>\n<p>A message is made when the value is validated: after <code>setTexts()</code> an error already there keeps its\ntext until the field is validated again.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-rules-of-your-own\">Rules of your own</h3>\n<p>A validator is a function <code>(value, row, rule) =&gt; message | null</code>: <code>null</code> when the value is valid,\nelse the message - or a text id, the way the built-ins return <code>&#39;validation.required&#39;</code>. An id of your\nown gets its text from <code>setTexts()</code>, so it can be translated. <code>rule</code> is\nthe rule written out, so a rule can carry options of its own. Register it before the datasource that\nnames it is made:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, registerValidator } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a number that must be there, 0 included\nregisterValidator('present', (value) =&gt; (value === null || value === undefined ? 'validation.required' : null));\n\n// { id: 'inList', values: [...] }: the options come from the rule (the built-in oneOf does this)\nregisterValidator('inList', (value, _row, rule) =&gt; {\n    if (value === null || value === undefined || value === '') {\n        return null; // an empty value is the required rule's job\n    }\n    return Array.isArray(rule.values) &amp;&amp; rule.values.includes(String(value)) ? null : 'Must be one of {values}';\n});\n\n// another field of the row: the end can not be before the start\nregisterValidator('afterStart', (value, row) =&gt;\n    value instanceof Date &amp;&amp; row.start instanceof Date &amp;&amp; value &lt; row.start ? 'Before the start' : null\n);\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        discount: { type: 'number', validate: ['present'] },\n        country: { validate: [{ id: 'inList', values: ['Norway', 'Sweden'] }] },\n        start: 'date',\n        end: { type: 'date', validate: ['afterStart'] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Keep a validator fast, it runs on every key the user types. Registering an id again replaces it, for\nthe datasources made after.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-when-it-runs\">When it runs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An edit is validated</strong>, from the grid as the user types and from code (<code>row.age = -1</code>): the\nfield&#39;s rules run, and so do the rules of the row&#39;s fields that have an error - an error that\ndepends on the edited value (<code>afterStart</code> above) clears once it is fixed. An edit that makes the\nfield valid clears its error.</li>\n<li><strong>The rows set with <code>setData()</code> are not validated, and neither are the fields of a new row from\n<code>addNewEmpty()</code></strong> until they are edited: an empty new row would be all errors before the user\ntyped anything. The stripes of the required fields show what to fill in, and <code>validate()</code> before\nthe save checks the fields never edited.</li>\n<li><strong><code>validate()</code> checks every row</strong>, or the rows given: every field with rules. It keeps the errors,\nthe grid shows them, and returns them - one <code>{ row, field, messages }</code> per field with an error.</li>\n<li><code>getErrors(rows?)</code> and <code>hasErrors()</code> read the errors there are now, without validating.</li>\n<li><code>acceptChanges()</code> and <code>resetData()</code> clear the errors, <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> those of the\nselected rows.</li>\n<li>Undo, redo, <code>resetField()</code> and <code>resetRows()</code> set values the way an edit does: the fields they set\nare validated.</li>\n<li>A row marked for deletion is not saved, so it has no errors: <code>validate()</code> skips it, <code>getErrors()</code>\nand <code>hasErrors()</code> leave it out. Its errors are back when it is unmarked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Validate before a save:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare function showErrors(lines: string[]): void;\n\nconst errors = datasource.validate();\nif (errors.length) {\n    showErrors(errors.map(({ row, field, messages }) =&gt; `${row.name}, ${datasource.getField(field).label}: ${messages.join(', ')}`));\n} else {\n    const response = await fetch('/api/save', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(datasource.getChanges()) });\n    if (response.ok) {\n        datasource.acceptChanges();\n    }\n}\n\n// only what a save sends: the new and edited rows\ndatasource.validate(datasource.getAllData().filter((row) =&gt; row.__rowState === 'N' || row.__rowState === 'M'));</code></pre>\n<p><code>validate()</code> checks every row, also the rows the filter leaves out and the rows that were not\nedited: data from a server that breaks a rule blocks the save too. Pass the new and edited rows to\ncheck what the save sends only.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-how-the-grid-shows-the-errors\">How the grid shows the errors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A cell with an error</strong> gets the class <code>simple-html-cell-error</code> - a red border inside the cell\nand a tint - and its messages as the tooltip, one per line.</li>\n<li><strong>The row number</strong> of a row with an error gets <code>simple-html-row-error</code> - a red bar on its left and\na tint - and an exclamation mark (<code>rowStatus</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a>).\nIts tooltip lists the row&#39;s state, then the server&#39;s error, then a line per field:\n<code>Age: Must be at least 0</code>.</li>\n<li>The colors are css variables: <code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code> and\n<code>--simple-html-grid-error-bg-color</code>.</li>\n<li>The tooltips are the grid&#39;s own, as on the filter editor&#39;s buttons: <code>tooltips: false</code> in the\nconfig turns them off. The <code>data-tooltip</code> attribute is written either way, for a tooltip library\nof your own.</li>\n<li>An edit in the grid shows its errors at once, and so does <code>validate()</code>. An edit from code shows\nthem in the next animation frame, with its value.</li>\n<li>A field with the <code>required</code> rule shows stripes on its empty editable cells, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/required-fields-and-placeholders\">Required fields and placeholders</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-saving\">Saving</h2>\n<p>A save usually sends everything in one transaction: the deleted, edited and new rows of\n<code>getChanges()</code>. After a successful save, <code>acceptChanges()</code> makes the grid show what was saved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the rows marked for deletion are removed</li>\n<li>the new rows become ordinary rows</li>\n<li>the values the rows have now become their original values: nothing counts as edited</li>\n<li>the row errors (<code>setErrors()</code>) and the validation errors are cleared</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The displayed rows are rebuilt once, and <code>hasChanges()</code> is <code>false</code> after.</p>\n<p><strong>Keys of new rows.</strong> Until it is saved, a new row has a temporary key: a generated negative number,\nin the key field (<code>id: -1</code>), or as <code>__KEY</code> in <code>getChanges().newEntities</code> when the datasource has no\n<code>key</code>. The server gives the row its real key. <code>acceptChanges({ newKeys })</code> takes a <code>Map</code> from each\ntemporary key to the server&#39;s key, and puts the server&#39;s key in the row - in the key field, or as the\nrow&#39;s <code>__KEY</code>. Setting it is no edit, and a selected row stays selected.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Person {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n}\n\n// what the server answers: the id it gave each new row\ninterface SaveResult {\n    newIds: { temporaryId: number; id: number }[];\n}\n\nconst people = new Datasource&lt;Person&gt;({ key: 'id' });\npeople.setData([\n    { id: 1, name: 'Ann' },\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }\n]);\n\n// the user edits, deletes and adds rows\nconst [ann, bob] = people.getDataRows();\nann.name = 'Anna';\npeople.markForDeletion(bob);\npeople.addNewEmpty({ name: 'Cid' }, { scrollTo: false }); // a temporary id: -1, say\n\n// one transaction\nconst changes = people.getChanges();\n// newEntities:      [{ name: 'Cid', id: -1 }]\n// modifiedEntities: [{ id: 1, name: 'Anna' }]\n// deletedEntities:  [{ id: 2, name: 'Bob' }]\nconst response = await fetch('/api/people/save', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(changes) });\nif (response.ok) {\n    const result: SaveResult = await response.json();\n    people.acceptChanges({ newKeys: new Map(result.newIds.map((row) =&gt; [row.temporaryId, row.id])) });\n}\n\npeople.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; [row.id, row.name]); // [[1, 'Anna'], [7, 'Cid']] - Cid has the server's id\npeople.hasChanges(); // false</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>newKeys</code> compares the keys as text: <code>-1</code> and <code>&#39;-1&#39;</code> are the same key, so a map made from the keys\nof a JSON object works too - <code>new Map(Object.entries(json))</code>.</li>\n<li>A key in <code>newKeys</code> that is not the key of a new row throws, and so does a blank server key - before\nanything changes. A map the wrong way round (server key to temporary key) is caught that way. A\nnew row that is not in <code>newKeys</code> keeps its key.</li>\n<li>When only some rows were saved, pass them: <code>acceptChanges({ rows: savedRows, newKeys })</code>. The\nother rows keep their changes.</li>\n<li>Keep the grid readonly while the save runs - <code>gridInterface.updateConfig({ readonly: true })</code>, and\n<code>false</code> after - or an edit made in between is accepted without being saved.</li>\n<li>Validate first: <code>validate()</code> returns the errors, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>.</li>\n<li>When the save fails, do not call <code>acceptChanges()</code>: the changes stay, to be saved again. Show the\nserver&#39;s errors on the rows with <a href=\"#guide/editing/errors-from-a-server\"><code>setErrors()</code></a>.</li>\n<li>Without a <code>key</code>, the server&#39;s key becomes the row&#39;s <code>__KEY</code>: a later edit of the row is sent with\nit, as <code>__KEY</code> in <code>modifiedEntities</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>hasChanges()</code> is <code>true</code> while there is something to save, for an &quot;unsaved changes&quot; indicator. The\ngrid sends no event for an edit, so check it when it matters:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>window.addEventListener('beforeunload', (event) =&gt; {\n    if (datasource.hasChanges()) {\n        event.preventDefault();\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Setting the data again with the rows from the server works too: <code>setData(freshRows)</code> starts the state\nof every row over, and with a <code>key</code> the selection is kept. <code>acceptChanges()</code> keeps the rows, so the\ncurrent row, the scroll position and the expanded groups stay as they are.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"saving\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-errors-from-a-server\">Errors from a server</h2>\n<p><code>setErrors()</code> on the datasource&#39;s container puts an error text on rows, by key - what a server said\nabout a save it refused. It is a <code>Map</code> from the key as text to the message. Every row not in the map\ngets <code>null</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] }\n};\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    // the cells of the row too: .row-error { position: absolute; inset: 0; background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.2); }\n    cellClass: (row) =&gt; (row.__rowError ? { background: 'row-error' } : null)\n};\nconst grid = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });\n\ndatasource.getDataContainer().setErrors(new Map([['2', 'The name is taken']]));\n\nconst row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nconsole.log(row.__rowError); // the message, or null</code></pre>\n<p>The grid shows <code>__rowError</code> on the row number, with the row&#39;s validation errors: the class\n<code>simple-html-row-error</code>, and the message as the first line of its tooltip, in the next animation\nframe after <code>setErrors()</code>. To mark the cells of the row too, use the <code>cellClass</code> hook as above.</p>\n<p><strong>Server errors and validation errors are kept apart.</strong> A server error is the row&#39;s <code>__rowError</code>,\nset with <code>setErrors()</code>; a validation error is a field&#39;s, from the rules (<code>getErrors()</code>). <code>setErrors()</code>\ndoes not change the validation errors, and <code>validate()</code>, <code>getErrors()</code> and <code>hasErrors()</code> do not read\n<code>__rowError</code> - a row the server refused is not invalid by the rules, and the next save may well work.\n<code>acceptChanges()</code> clears both.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-required-fields-and-placeholders\">Required fields and placeholders</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A field with the <code>required</code> rule (<code>validate: [&#39;required&#39;]</code> in the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>) shows its empty cells with stripes, until they have a value - empty as\nthe rule sees it, so 0 too. Only in an editable grid, and only on editable cells. Restyle the\nstripes with the <code>simple-html-mandatory</code> class. It replaces the <code>mandatory</code> column option of v5.</li>\n<li><code>placeholderRow</code> is text shown in an empty cell. By default only on the current row, set\n<code>placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly: false</code> in the config to show it on every row.</li>\n<li><code>placeholderFilter</code> is text shown in the empty header filter.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst companies = new Datasource({ fields: { company: { validate: ['required'] } } });\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly: false,\n    columns: {\n        company: { placeholderRow: 'required' },\n        phone: { placeholderRow: '+47 ...', placeholderFilter: 'search phone' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['phone'], width: 120 }\n        ]\n    }\n};\ncreateGrid(host, { config, datasource: companies });</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"required-placeholders\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-edits-and-live-data\">Edits and live data</h2>\n<p>A value set from code shows in the grid on its own, in the next animation frame: the datasource sends\n<code>rows-changed</code> with the rows changed, and the grid renders the ones on screen - <code>refresh()</code> is not\nneeded after an edit from code, an undo or a revert. The cell the user is typing in is never rendered\nover. When rows from a server are merged with <code>upsert()</code>, an edit the user has not saved stays: a\nfield the server changed too is a conflict, resolved with <code>resolveConflict()</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"filtering","title":"Filtering","html":"<p>The datasource has one filter: a condition, or a group of conditions and groups. The user builds it\nin the grid - with the header filters, the menus and the filter editor - and you set it from code.\nEverything works on the same filter, <code>datasource.getFilter()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-header-filters\">Header filters</h2>\n<p>The row under the header labels has a filter cell per column. The user types a value and presses\nEnter, or leaves the cell, and the grid filters on it:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field type</th>\n<th>the cell</th>\n<th>the operator it starts with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>an input</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>: the whole text, ignoring case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>number</td>\n<td>an input</td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>date, datetime</td>\n<td>an input, and a date picker on click - a range picker with Between</td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td>a checkbox: no filter, then checked (<code>true</code>), then unchecked (<code>false</code>), then no filter again</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A text filter matches the whole text</strong> (<code>EQUAL</code>, ignoring case): <code>ann</code> finds <code>Ann</code> and <code>ANN</code>, but\nnot <code>Anna</code>. <code>*</code> and <code>%</code> are wildcards: <code>ann*</code> starts with ann, <code>*ann*</code> has ann in it. <code>_</code> is one\ncharacter, any - as in SQL <code>LIKE</code>: <code>p_mp</code> finds <code>pump</code> and <code>PUMP</code>, <code>pump-__</code> finds <code>pump-01</code> and\n<code>pump-AB</code> but not <code>pump-1</code>. With <a href=\"#guide/filtering/match-case\">Match case</a> on, <code>Ann</code> does not find <code>ANN</code>.</li>\n<li>A number or date is typed as the field&#39;s formatter shows it (<code>12,5</code> works too, the date in the\nformat of the placeholder) and parsed with the formatter. A column with a display format takes it as\nits cells show it - <code>2026-W47</code> is the week, <code>1 234,5</code> a number - see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>BETWEEN</code></strong> (Filter › Operator › Between, number and date columns, or Between in the\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-block\">range block</a>) takes both ends in the one input, with <code>..</code> between them: <code>10..20</code> is 10 to 20, both included; <code>10..</code> is 10 or more, <code>..20</code> 20\nor less; <code>10</code> alone is 10 to 10 (a date: that day). Each end is parsed with the formatter, as a\nsingle value is: <code>2026-01-01..2026-03-31</code>. The placeholder says <code>from..to</code>, and the cell shows the\nvalue that way (<code>10..20</code>). See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-picker\">The range picker</a> for a date column.</li>\n<li>The <code>operator</code> option of a column sets the operator its header filter starts with, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/a-contains-search\">below</a>; <code>filterOperators</code> the ones it offers, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operators-a-column-offers\">The operators a column offers</a>.</li>\n<li>The cell shows the operator it uses as a small symbol beside the input, a click on it changes it, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">The operator in the cell</a>.</li>\n<li>Emptying the cell removes the column&#39;s condition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">Filter as you type</a>, per column, filters without Enter, after a short pause.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-range-picker\">The range picker</h3>\n<p>A click in the header filter of a date or datetime column opens the grid&#39;s date picker under it, on the\ndate the cell has: Use filters on the date picked, Clear removes the filter. With <strong>Between</strong> it opens\nthe <strong>range picker</strong> instead, on the <code>from..to</code> the cell has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two months side by side - one on a screen too narrow for two (a phone) - with « ‹ › » once, around\nthem: they move both months.</li>\n<li>The first day clicked is <strong>From</strong>, the next one <strong>To</strong> - swapped when it is before From - and a\nthird click starts again with a new From. From and To are framed as the selected day, the days\nbetween them have a fill. The line under the months shows the From and To picked (<code>22 November 2026</code>, <code>–</code> for an end not picked).</li>\n<li><strong>Use</strong> filters on the range, as if <code>from..to</code> was typed: <code>2026-11-22..2026-11-28</code>, both days\nincluded. One day picked is an open end: <code>2026-11-22..</code> is that day or later. <strong>Clear</strong> removes the\nfilter, the operator stays Between. Esc or a click outside closes it, the filter stays as it was.</li>\n<li>The range is whole days: a datetime column gets From at 00:00:00 and To at 23:59:59 -\n<code>2026-11-22T00:00:00..2026-11-28T23:59:59</code>. Change the times in the cell to filter on hours.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The range block of the filter menu opens the same pickers from its inputs, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-block\">The range block</a>. The range picker is a <code>DateInterface</code> with <code>rangepicker: true</code>:\nuse it on your own page too, see <a href=\"#guide/events/the-date-picker\">Events</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</h3>\n<p>A column with a <a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">display format</a> - a week, a month, the days until, a\nnumber with a thousands separator - takes the text in its header filter <strong>as its cells show it</strong>, and\nfilters on the whole of what it stands for:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>typed, the column shown as <code>weekIso</code></th>\n<th>the filter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>= 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td><code>BETWEEN</code> Monday 2026-11-16 and Sunday 2026-11-22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≥ 2026-W47</code> / <code>&gt; 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>from Monday / after Sunday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt; 2026-W47</code> / <code>≤ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>before Monday / to Sunday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>an OR group: before Monday, after Sunday or blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>2026-W40..2026-W47</code> (Between)</td>\n<td>Monday of week 40 to Sunday of week 47</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A month (<code>2026-11</code>), a quarter (<code>2026 Q4</code>), a year, a day of any day format (<code>22.11.2026</code>, <code>Nov 22, 2026</code>), the days until (<code>≤ 5</code>: up to the day in 5 days), a minute (<code>2026-11-22 13:45</code>) and a second go\nthe same way; a number is read with the separators of its format (<code>1 234,5</code>, <code>50 %</code>). The day of the\nweek and a time of day are no period: those columns take the field&#39;s format (<code>2026-11-22</code>), as does a\ncolumn with <code>filterAsShown: false</code> in its format. It is on by default; the grid config dialog has it as\n<em>Filter as shown</em>.</p>\n<p>The filter is ordinary date conditions - the footer&#39;s chips and the filter editor show the dates, and\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/a-week-typed-in-the-grid\">toSqlWhere()</a> makes a range of the column of them, no date\nfunctions. The cell shows <code>2026-W47</code> again after a render, and so does a saved state: the state holds the\nstart of the period, see <a href=\"#guide/state/header-filters-in-the-state\">Header filters in the state</a>. The\nplaceholder is an example (<code>2026-W47</code>), the date pickers write the format, and a change of the format by\nthe user runs the filter again. Every format and rule is in the formatting guide,\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-as-you-type\">Filter as you type</h3>\n<p>A header filter filters when Enter is pressed or the cell is left. With <strong>Filter as you type</strong> it filters\nwhile the user types, after a short pause (300ms) - no Enter needed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Per column</strong>, turned on and off by the user in the <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">operator choice</a> (at\nits bottom, under a line), in the header filter menu&#39;s Filter › (beside Match case) and in the\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">grid config dialog</a>, next to the operator. A ✓ while it is\non. It is state: <code>headerFilters[field].filterOnType</code>, listed when the user&#39;s differs from the default.</li>\n<li><strong>The default</strong> of a column is its <code>filterOnType</code>, else the grid&#39;s <code>filterOnType</code>, else off.</li>\n<li><strong>A number or date column</strong> filters when the text is a number or a date: a date half typed\n(<code>2026-1</code>, <code>202</code>, <code>2026-W</code> filtered as shown) does not filter, nor a <code>-</code> alone - the rows stay until it\nis one. Empty text removes the filter at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Enter and leaving the cell</strong> filter at once, as before, and end the pause: the text is filtered on\nonce. Enter on a text already filtered while typing does nothing.</li>\n<li>The rows are rendered again while the user types: the header filter keeps the focus and its caret.</li>\n<li>Not for a boolean column: its checkbox filters on a click.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    filterOnType: true, // every column filters while typing\n    columns: {\n        name: { operator: 'CONTAINS' },\n        notes: { filterOnType: false } // this one on Enter\n    },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['notes'], width: 200 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-contains-search\">A contains search</h3>\n<p>For a search anywhere in the text, give the column <code>operator: &#39;CONTAINS&#39;</code> in <code>columns</code>. Each column\nhas its own, the text columns without one keep <code>EQUAL</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { operator: 'CONTAINS' }, // `ann` finds Ann, Anna and Joanna\n        city: { operator: 'BEGIN_WITH' } // `osl` finds Oslo\n        // company: EQUAL, the default\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['city'], width: 120 },\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 150 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>The user can also pick the operator of a column in the header filter&#39;s menu (Filter › Operator ›,\nbelow), with the <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">operator in the cell</a>, or in the\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">grid config dialog</a> - it shows the column&#39;s default\ntoo - and type <code>*ann*</code> in any text header filter.</p>\n<p>A header filter only replaces the conditions on its own column, at the top of the filter. The rest\nof the filter is kept, and the column&#39;s condition is ANDed with it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-match-case\">Match case</h3>\n<p>A text compares ignoring case by default: <code>oslo</code> finds <code>Oslo</code> and <code>OSLO</code>. A condition with\n<code>caseSensitive: true</code> compares upper and lower case as they are, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-format\">the filter format</a>.\nIn the grid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Match case</strong> in a text column&#39;s header filter menu (Filter › Match case, a ✓ while it is on): the\ncolumn&#39;s header filter gives its conditions <code>caseSensitive: true</code> - the value typed, and the\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-value-list\">value list</a>, which then lists texts that differ in case (<code>Oslo</code>, <code>OSLO</code>) apart.\nTurning it on or off runs the header filter again with the value its cell has, as picking an operator\ndoes. It is state: <code>headerFilters[field].caseSensitive</code>. A lookup column has it too: the label is\nmatched with its case, and the <code>IN</code> of the values found has <code>caseSensitive: true</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>filterCaseSensitive: true</code></strong> on a column: its conditions always match case - the header filter,\nthe value list, the row menu&#39;s &quot;Filter:&quot; and the filter editor. Its Match case is shown on, greyed\nout, with the hint <em>(always)</em>; the filter editor&#39;s toggle too. For a text column (and a lookup column),\n<code>true</code> on another type throws a <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made.</li>\n<li><strong>A field&#39;s <code>caseSensitive: true</code></strong> (<code>new Datasource({ fields: { code: { caseSensitive: true } } })</code>): the\nsame, locked by code - not in the grid&#39;s config, which the grid config dialog, <code>updateConfig()</code> or a\nsaved config can change. For a column the database has an index on (a project code): its conditions\nalways have <code>caseSensitive: true</code> - the datasource keeps a condition set without it (<code>filter()</code>,\n<code>setFilter()</code>, a saved state) as a copy with it - and <code>toSqlWhere()</code> compares the column with its case\n(<a href=\"#guide/sql/a-column-that-keeps-its-case-casesensitive\">SQL</a>). Match case is on and greyed out, the\ndialog&#39;s hint says <em>Always for this column: set by the application</em>\n(<code>columnSettings.matchCase.locked</code>). Text fields only, else the datasource throws.</li>\n<li><strong>Aa in the header</strong>: a header filter that matches case - <code>filterCaseSensitive</code>, or Match case on -\nshows <strong>Aa</strong> before its operator, <code>Aa =</code>, in the link colour, so it is seen before anything is typed;\nits name for a screen reader says &quot;match case&quot; (<code>filter.indicator.matchCase</code>). The operator choice (a\nclick on it, or F4) has <strong>Match case</strong> at its end, under <em>Filter as you type</em>. The column&#39;s header\nfilter needs its operator indicator for it (<code>filterOperatorIndicator</code>, a column of 60px or more).</li>\n<li><strong>The grid config dialog</strong>, tab <em>Header filter</em> of a text column: a <em>Match case</em> checkbox under <em>Filter\nas you type</em> - Apply sets it as the menu does; checked and greyed out when the column always matches\ncase, its hint saying why. Reset column turns it off (on for <code>filterCaseSensitive</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Aa</strong> in the filter editor, on each condition of a text field: pressed (filled) while it matches\ncase, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-editor\">the filter editor</a>.</li>\n<li>The footer shows <strong>Aa</strong> after the value of a chip that matches case, <code>getFilterString()</code> keeps the\ncase of its value and adds <code>&lt;&lt;MATCH_CASE&gt;&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        sku: { filterCaseSensitive: true, operator: 'BEGIN_WITH' } // `AB` does not find `ab-100`\n    },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['sku'], width: 120 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-operators-a-column-offers\">The operators a column offers</h3>\n<p>Each type has its operators, <code>operatorsFor(type)</code> - see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/operators\">Operators</a>. The header filter\nmenu&#39;s Operator › offers the ones its input takes (not <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code>, the value list&#39;s, nor\n<code>IS_BLANK</code> / <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code>, the Blank submenu&#39;s), the filter editor&#39;s operator picker all of them.</p>\n<p>A field with <strong><code>clobType: true</code></strong> (<code>fields: { notes: { clobType: true } }</code>, set by code) is a long text the\ndatabase holds as a LOB - an Oracle <code>CLOB</code> - that SQL compares with <code>LIKE</code> only: it offers <code>EQUAL</code>,\n<code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code>, <code>END_WITH</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code> and <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code>\n(<code>CLOB_OPERATORS</code>) - no value list, an <code>IN</code> of <code>filterOperators</code> is not offered -, the filter editor has no\n&quot;compare with a field&quot; for it, no formula reads it (a calculated column&#39;s, a colour rule&#39;s: the problem\n<code>clobField</code>, <em>{notes} is a long text: a formula can not read it</em>), and a column whose template reads it is\nfiltered on the value. See <a href=\"#guide/sql/a-lob-column-clobtype\">SQL</a>. Text fields only, else the datasource\nthrows.</p>\n<p><code>filterOperators</code> on a column offers fewer, in the type&#39;s order:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // a search only: no Equal or Ends with, no Not blank, no value list (it needs IN)\n        name: { filterOperators: ['CONTAINS', 'BEGIN_WITH', 'IS_BLANK'] },\n        // an exact number or a range, BETWEEN to start with\n        age: { filterOperators: ['EQUAL', 'BETWEEN', 'IS_BLANK', 'IS_NOT_BLANK'], operator: 'BETWEEN' }\n    },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['age'], width: 80 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Operator › offers those its input takes; Blank › its Is blank / Is not blank when it has them (and is\nleft out with neither); the value list needs <code>IN</code>, and uses <code>NOT_IN</code> for the unchecked values only\nwhen it has <code>NOT_IN</code> too (else always an <code>IN</code> of the checked ones). The filter editor&#39;s picker offers\nthem for the field.</li>\n<li>The header filter starts with its <code>operator</code>, else the type&#39;s default (<code>EQUAL</code>, or\n<code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code> for a number or date) when the list has it, else its first operator the\nheader filter takes. An operator of the state that the list does not have is passed by.</li>\n<li>A <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made or <code>updateConfig()</code> is given the column: an operator the type does\nnot have (<code>CONTAINS</code> on a number), an empty list, a list without an operator for the header filter&#39;s\ninput, an <code>operator</code> that is not in it. A lookup column filters as text in its header: its list is\nof text operators.</li>\n<li>It is what the user can pick, not a rule of the filter: <code>datasource.filter()</code> and a state from\n<code>setState()</code> can have any condition, and the row menu&#39;s &quot;Filter:&quot; is <code>EQUAL</code> (<code>IS_BLANK</code> for an empty\ncell) whatever the column offers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What each header cell shows is part of the <a href=\"#guide/state/header-filters-in-the-state\">state</a>:\n<code>getState().headerFilters</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"filtering\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-operator-in-the-cell\">The operator in the cell</h3>\n<p>Each header filter shows the operator it uses, as a small symbol in its cell: on the right of a text\nfilter (and of a <a href=\"#guide/lookups\">lookup column</a>&#39;s, it filters as text), clear of its text; on the left\nof a number or date filter, where it reads as the condition does: <code>≥ 2026-01-01</code>. The input&#39;s padding\nkeeps the text typed clear of it. A boolean column has none, its filter is a checkbox.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>symbol</th>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>symbol</th>\n<th>operator</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>=</code></td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>*a*</code></td>\n<td>Contains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠</code></td>\n<td>Not Equal</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>!*a*</code></td>\n<td>Does not contain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;</code> <code>≤</code></td>\n<td>Less than (or equal)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>a*</code></td>\n<td>Begins with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&gt;</code> <code>≥</code></td>\n<td>Greater than (or equal)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>*a</code></td>\n<td>Ends with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a..b</code></td>\n<td>Between</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The text symbols are the wildcards a text filter takes: <code>*a*</code> is what <code>*ann*</code> typed with Equal finds.\nThe tooltip names the operator (<code>Operator: Contains (F4)</code>), and so does the input&#39;s description for a\nscreen reader.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A click</strong> on it - a tap on a phone - opens the <strong>operator choice</strong> right under it: the operators of\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-header-filter-menu\">Filter › Operator ›</a> (the column&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code>, else its type&#39;s), the\none in use with a ✓. Picking one does exactly what Operator › does: the operator is state\n(<code>headerFilters[field].operator</code>), the filter runs again with the value in the cell, the grid sends\n<code>filter-operator-change</code>. A text typed and not filtered yet is filtered first, as a right click does.\nUnder a line at its bottom: <strong>Filter as you type</strong>, a ✓ while it is on, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">Filter as you type</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>F4</strong> in the header filter opens the choice from the keyboard, the focus on its first item: the\n<a href=\"#guide/menus/the-keyboard\">menu keys</a> move in it, Enter picks - the focus goes back to the filter -\nand Esc closes it. The symbol is no stop of Tab: Tab goes from filter to filter as before.</li>\n<li>A right click or a long press on it opens the filter menu, as on the input.</li>\n<li><strong>A column with one operator</strong> (<code>filterOperators: [&#39;CONTAINS&#39;, &#39;IN&#39;]</code>) shows its symbol, but it can\nnot be clicked: there is nothing to choose.</li>\n<li><strong>A narrow column</strong> - under 60px - shows none: the input keeps its room. F4 opens the choice there\ntoo, under the cell.</li>\n<li><strong><code>filterOperatorIndicator: false</code></strong> in the config shows none at all, and F4 opens nothing: the\noperator is picked in the filter menu only. The dropdown grid of a <a href=\"#guide/lookups\">lookup</a> has it\noff, a picker has no menus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The symbols, the name and the tooltip are texts: <code>filter.indicator.&lt;FilterOperator&gt;</code>, <code>filter.indicator</code>\nand <code>filter.indicator.tooltip</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/header-filters\">Texts</a>. The choice is made of\nOperator ›&#39;s items: the <code>menus.filter</code> hook changes the filter menu, not the choice - offer fewer\noperators with <code>filterOperators</code>, it is what both show.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    filterOperatorIndicator: false, // plain inputs, the operator in the filter menu only\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-header-filter-menu\">The header filter menu</h3>\n<p>Right click a header filter for its menu: the submenu <strong>Filter ›</strong>, then the main tool, in the menu\nitself: the <strong>value list</strong> for text fields and <a href=\"#guide/lookups\">lookup columns</a>, the\n<strong>range block</strong> for number, date and datetime fields - a boolean column has neither, its header\nfilter is a checkbox. A date or datetime column has its <a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">quick dates</a> under the range\nblock. In Filter ›:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Operator ›</strong> picks the operator the column&#39;s header filter uses, the one in use has a ✓: for text\nEqual, Not Equal, Contains, Does not contain, Begins with and Ends with; for numbers and dates Equal,\nNot Equal, Less than, Less than or equal, Greater than, Greater than or equal and Between - or the\nones of the column&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code>. A boolean column has none. The filter runs again with it, and\nthe grid sends a <code>filter-operator-change</code> event. The operator picked is state\n(<code>headerFilters[field].operator</code>). The symbol in the cell opens the same choice, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">The operator in the cell</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Match case</strong> (text columns) turns matching case on and off, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/match-case\">Match case</a></li>\n<li><strong>Filter as you type</strong> (not a boolean column) turns it on and off, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">Filter as you type</a></li>\n<li><strong>Blank ›</strong> <strong>Is blank</strong> / <strong>Is not blank</strong> replace the column&#39;s conditions with <code>IS_BLANK</code> /\n<code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></li>\n<li><strong>Clear Filter</strong> removes the conditions on this column, <strong>Clear All Filters</strong> the whole filter</li>\n<li><strong>Advanced Filter…</strong> opens the <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-editor\">filter editor</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>From the keyboard: Shift+F10 (or the menu key) on the header filter opens the menu with the focus on\nFilter ›, ArrowRight goes into it; Tab goes from Filter › into the value list or the range block (and\non to the quick dates), Shift+Tab back.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"filter-operator-header\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-value-list\">The value list</h3>\n<p>The value list is the part of the filter menu of a text column, and of a lookup column, with a\ncheckbox per value, as in a spreadsheet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It lists <strong>what the cells show</strong>: the text of the field&#39;s formatter, the label of a\n<a href=\"#guide/lookups\">lookup column</a> (<code>Norway</code>, not <code>NO</code>) - for the rows the <em>other</em> columns&#39; filters let\nthrough, in the order of the texts (<code>localeCompare</code>), with the number of rows of each on its right.\nOver the list: how many values it has.</li>\n<li>Values shown the same are <strong>one entry</strong>, their rows counted together: two values a formatter shows\nas <code>1234</code>, two codes with one label. So are texts that differ in case only (<code>Oslo</code>, <code>OSLO</code>): <code>IN</code>\ncompares ignoring case, it can not filter them apart. The entry shows the text of the first row. With\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/match-case\">Match case</a> on they are entries of their own, and Apply&#39;s <code>IN</code> matches case.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Blank&quot;</strong> (in italics), first, is every empty value - <code>null</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> and also <code>0</code> (see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/0-is-blank\">0 is blank</a>) - and a value whose cell shows no text. <code>false</code> is a value of its own.</li>\n<li>It lists the first 100 texts, and then says so. The counts are only shown when every value is\nlisted.</li>\n<li><strong>Only values in filtered rows</strong>, when other columns filter rows: uncheck it to list the values of\nevery row.</li>\n<li>The search box narrows the list to the texts with the search text in them, ignoring case (<code>*</code> and\n<code>%</code> are left out); Blank, which has no text, is not listed then.</li>\n<li><strong>Filter on the text: contains &quot;…&quot;</strong>, under the search box when it has text: checked, Apply filters\non the <strong>text</strong>, not the values - the header filter gets the operator <code>CONTAINS</code> (<em>Contains</em>, the <code>*a*</code>\nin the cell) and the text, as if picked in Operator › and typed in the cell, and so rows added later\nwith the text are found too. <code>*</code>, <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> are wildcards there. The list stays as a preview of the\nvalues with the text, greyed out. Not for a column whose <code>filterOperators</code> have no <code>CONTAINS</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Select all</strong>, the header of the list, is checked when every value is, mixed when some are: a\nclick checks every value, or none when every value is checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply</strong> filters the column on the <strong>values</strong> behind the checked texts - a filter as any other:\nthe footer, <code>getFilterString()</code>, the filter editor and the state have it. An <code>IN</code> condition with\nthem, or a <code>NOT_IN</code> with the values of the unchecked ones when there are fewer of those: the values\npast the first 100 stay then. Blank is <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code> in the list. After a search it is an <code>IN</code> of the\nvalues the search found and are checked - of the first 100: type more to narrow it. Nothing\nchecked, or nothing unchecked: no condition from the list. It replaces the column&#39;s conditions,\nas the header filter does, and empties the header filter cell. Enter in the search box or on a\ncheckbox does the same. <strong>Cancel</strong> (or Esc, or a click outside) closes the menu, the filter stays\nas it was: nothing is filtered while you check and uncheck.</li>\n<li>The keyboard: Tab goes from the menu&#39;s items into the search box, the checkboxes and the buttons;\nArrowDown / ArrowUp move between the search box and the checkboxes, Space checks one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-range-block\">The range block</h3>\n<p>The range block is the part of the filter menu of a number, date or datetime column - where a text\ncolumn has the value list - to filter on a range in one go: an operator and a value, without picking\nOperator › first and then typing in the header filter.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>[ = ][ ≠ ][ &lt; ][ ≤ ][ &gt; ][ ≥ ][ Between ]       [ = ][ ≠ ][ &lt; ][ ≤ ][ &gt; ][ ≥ ][ Between ]\n[ 30                                  ]       From [ 2026-01-01                    ]\n                     [Cancel] [Apply]         To   [ 2026-03-31                    ]\n                                                                  [Cancel] [Apply]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The operators</strong> are those of Operator ›, in the same order - <code>=</code>, <code>≠</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>≤</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>≥</code> and\nBetween - or the ones of the column&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code>. The one picked is filled. Each is named for\na screen reader by its text in Operator › (&quot;Greater than&quot;), which is its tooltip too.</li>\n<li><strong>One input</strong>, two for <strong>Between</strong>: <strong>From</strong> and <strong>To</strong>. Either can be left empty, an open end: From\n10 alone is 10 or more, To 20 alone 20 or less. A value is typed as in the header filter, parsed with\nthe field&#39;s formatter; the placeholder says how (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, <code>Number</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>A date or datetime column</strong> has the date picker too: a click in the input opens it under the\ninput, on the date the input holds - Use puts the date picked in the input, Clear empties it. A click\nin From or To of Between opens the <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-picker\">range picker</a> under them, on the two dates\nthey hold: Use puts From and To in them (a datetime&#39;s To at 23:59:59). The menu stays open: what is\npicked is a draft as what is typed, Apply filters. Esc closes the picker only - a second Esc the\nmenu - and so does a click in the menu outside it, another operator say. The focus stays in the\ninput, Enter still applies; a date can still be typed.</li>\n<li>It opens with what the header filter has: its operator, its value - a <code>BETWEEN</code>&#39;s ends in From and\nTo. Picking Between takes the value as From; another operator after Between the first end as the\nvalue.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply</strong> - or Enter in an input, or on an operator - does what the header filter does when the\noperator is picked in Operator › and the value typed in the cell (<code>10..20</code> for Between): the\noperator, when it changed, is state (<code>headerFilters[field].operator</code>) and the grid sends\n<code>filter-operator-change</code>; the value replaces the column&#39;s conditions, as typing does, and the cell\nshows it (<code>10..20</code>). The state, the footer&#39;s chip, <code>getFilterString()</code> and the filter editor have\nwhat typing gives. An empty input is no filter on the column, as an empty cell. <strong>Cancel</strong> (or Esc,\nor a click outside) closes the menu, the filter stays as it was: nothing is filtered while you pick\nand type.</li>\n<li>The keyboard: Tab goes from the menu&#39;s items into the block, Shift+Tab back. The operators are a\nradio group, one stop of Tab: ArrowLeft / ArrowRight (or ArrowUp / ArrowDown) pick another one. Then\nthe input (From, To), Cancel and Apply. An operator clicked with the mouse puts the focus in the\ninput: a click, the value, Enter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is a block of the menu, <code>filter.range</code>: a <code>menus.filter</code> hook can hide it, move it or put a block\nof its own in its place, see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-built-in-ids\">Menus</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-quick-dates\">Quick dates</h3>\n<p>Under the range block of a date or datetime column: the date ranges filtered on all day, one click\neach - no Apply, the menu closes and the rows are filtered.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>[ Today        ][ Yesterday    ]\n[ This week    ][ Last week    ]\n[ This month   ][ Last month   ]\n[ This quarter ][ Last quarter ]\n[ This year    ][ Last year    ]\n[ Last 7 days  ][ Last 30 days ]\n[ Next 7 days  ][ Overdue      ]</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>quick date</th>\n<th>the days, from today</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Today, Yesterday</td>\n<td>the day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>This week, Last week</td>\n<td>7 days from the first day of the week: the date picker&#39;s <code>datePickerConfig.weekStart</code>, Monday by default (ISO weeks). <code>weekStart: 0</code> makes Sunday the first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>This month, Last month</td>\n<td>the 1st to the last day of the month</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>This quarter, Last quarter</td>\n<td>January-March, April-June, July-September, October-December</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>This year, Last year</td>\n<td>1 January to 31 December</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Last 7 days, Last 30 days</td>\n<td>today and the 6 (29) days before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Next 7 days</td>\n<td>today and the 6 days after</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overdue</td>\n<td>every day before today - an open start. Only on a column that lists it in <code>datePresets</code>: it means something on a due date, not on a birthday</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A quick date is relative.</strong> The header filter keeps it as itself -\n<code>headerFilters[field].datePreset: &#39;thisMonth&#39;</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/state/header-filters-in-the-state\">the state</a><ul>\n<li>and its days are computed from today whenever it filters: when it is picked, when a state with it\nis set (<code>setState()</code>, <code>initialState</code>, <code>resetState()</code>), when Enter is pressed on it. A view saved with\nThis month shows the month it is opened in. A page left open over midnight: the grid computes them\nagain when the window gets the focus back or the page is shown again, and filters again when their\ndays changed - <code>gridInterface.refreshDatePresets()</code> does the same from code (a timer for a screen\non the wall).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The filter is plain</strong>: a <code>BETWEEN</code> of the days, the column&#39;s conditions replaced as typing does,\nthe rest of the filter kept. A <code>date</code> field compares the days, a <code>datetime</code> from 00:00:00 of the\nfirst day to 23:59:59 of the last, as the <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-picker\">range picker</a>. Overdue is a <code>BETWEEN</code>\nwith an open start: <code>[null, yesterday]</code>. <code>getFilter()</code>, <code>getFilterString()</code>, the filter editor and\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/a-quick-date\"><code>toSqlWhere()</code></a> see those dates, never the quick date. The display format of\nthe column plays no part: This month on a column shown as weeks is the month.</li>\n<li><strong>What shows it</strong>: the header filter cell its name (<em>This month</em>), the operator indicator Between\n(<code>a..b</code>) - a quick date sets the operator to Between, and the grid sends <code>filter-operator-change</code>\nwhen it was another one - the footer&#39;s chip <code>Order date = This month</code>, the range block its days in\nFrom and To. The button of the quick date in use is filled. The tooltip of a button has its days,\nas the field writes a date: <code>2026-11-16 – 2026-11-22</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>What replaces it</strong>: a date typed or picked in the cell, the range block&#39;s Apply with a date\nchanged (Apply with nothing changed keeps it), another operator (its first day stays: This week\nwith ≥ is ≥ Monday), Clear Filter, Blank ›, the filter editor&#39;s Apply when it changed the column&#39;s\nconditions (unchanged, the quick date stays). The name of a quick date typed in the cell - <code>today</code>,\n<code>last week</code>, any case - is that quick date.</li>\n<li><strong>Which a column offers</strong>: <code>datePresets</code> - a list in the order shown (<code>DATE_PRESETS</code> has every one),\n<code>false</code> for none. Left out: every one but Overdue. Only a column whose header filter offers\n<code>BETWEEN</code> (<code>filterOperators</code>); a lookup column filters as text, it has none.</li>\n<li>The keyboard: Tab goes on from the range block to the quick dates, one stop: the one in use, else\nthe first. ArrowLeft / ArrowRight the one before / after, ArrowUp / ArrowDown the one above / below,\nHome / End the first / last; Enter or Space filters on it.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { DATE_PRESETS, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // every quick date on the due date, Overdue too\n        dueDate: { datePresets: [...DATE_PRESETS] },\n        // a few on the order date, in this order\n        orderDate: { datePresets: ['today', 'thisWeek', 'thisMonth', 'last30Days'] },\n        // none on the date of birth\n        born: { datePresets: false }\n    },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['dueDate'], width: 110 }, { fields: ['orderDate'], width: 110 }, { fields: ['born'], width: 110 }] }\n};\n\n// from code, as if picked: the grid fills in the operator, the days and the filter\ngridInterface.setState({ headerFilters: { orderDate: { value: null, datePreset: 'thisMonth' } } });</code></pre>\n<p>It is a block of the menu, <code>filter.dates</code>: a <code>menus.filter</code> hook can hide it or move it, see\n<a href=\"#guide/menus/the-built-in-ids\">Menus</a>. The texts are <code>filter.dates.*</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/the-quick-dates\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-filters-from-the-row-menu\">Filters from the row menu</h3>\n<p>Right click a cell for the row menu. Its &quot;Filter:&quot; part filters on the value of that cell:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Replace filter (all)</strong> replaces the whole filter with <code>field = value</code>, and empties the header\nfilters</li>\n<li><strong>Add to filter (and)</strong> ANDs <code>field = value</code> with the filter there is</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An empty cell gives <code>IS_BLANK</code> instead of <code>= value</code>. On a column with <code>filterCaseSensitive</code> the\ncondition matches case.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-filter-editor\">The filter editor</h2>\n<p>The filter editor is a dialog where the user builds any filter: conditions and AND/OR groups,\nnested. It opens from the header filter menu (Filter › Advanced Filter…), from a click on the filter\nin the footer - or on <strong>No filter</strong> there while the rows are not filtered - and from code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>document.getElementById('advanced-filter')!.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; gridInterface.openFilterEditor());</code></pre>\n<p>It starts with the filter there is. A condition is one row: a field (every field the grid knows),\nan operator and a value - or another field to compare with, the arrows switch between the two - then\nthe trash can. The field and the operator are pickers: a button that opens a menu under it, the\nfield&#39;s with a search box. A group is a line down the left of what is in it, its header on top:\n<strong>AND</strong> / <strong>OR</strong> (the one in use filled), <strong>+ Condition</strong>, <strong>+ Group</strong>, then wrap it in a new group\nand delete it. &quot;Apply &amp; close&quot; and &quot;Apply&quot; apply it, &quot;Cancel&quot; leaves the filter as it was. A condition\nwith no field or operator picked, or no value, is left out when it is applied: an empty value input is\nno value. Applying it empties the header filter cells of the columns whose conditions it changed; a\nheader filter whose conditions are as they were keeps its text.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The operators of the field</strong>: the picker offers the operators of the field&#39;s type (see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/operators\">Operators</a>) - or of its column&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code> - and a text&#39;s while no field is\npicked. A lookup column&#39;s field has the operators of its type: the editor edits the values (<code>NO</code>),\nnot the labels. Picking a field whose type does not have the operator picked takes the operator\naway: pick one again. A condition with an operator its type does not offer - from a filter saved\nwith v5, from code - shows it, and keeps it when it is applied.</li>\n<li><strong>Aa</strong>, Match case, after the value of a condition on a text field (not Is Blank / Is Not Blank):\npressed (filled) while the condition matches case, a click turns it on or off. It gives the condition\n<code>caseSensitive: true</code>. On a column with <code>filterCaseSensitive</code> it is pressed and greyed out.</li>\n<li><strong>Between</strong> has two value inputs, <em>From</em> and <em>To</em>, each read with the field&#39;s formatter (a date opens\nthe date picker under it): one left empty is an open end. It compares with its values, so it has no\nswitch to another field. Picking Between takes the value there was as its start; another operator\npicked after it takes the start as its value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every control is a button or an input, Tab goes through them in the order they are shown: the <code>✕</code>,\nthen each group&#39;s header and its conditions, then Undo, Redo, Cancel, Apply, Apply &amp; close. A\npicker opened from the keyboard has the focus in its menu - the field picker in its search box: type,\nthen ArrowDown to the fields or Enter for the first one - and gives it back to the picker when it\ncloses. The icons and the pickers have names for a screen reader: <code>Field: Country</code>, <code>Delete this condition</code>. The dialog is as high as what is in it, up to the window, then the groups scroll; a\nnested group is indented, its rows as wide as what is left, and a row wraps when the dialog is\nnarrow.</p>\n<p>It is a modal <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, over everything on the page: the grid behind it can not be used while it is\nopen. The focus moves to the dialog when it opens, and goes back to where it was when it closes. Esc\ncloses it - or the field, operator or date picker open in it, then Esc again closes the editor. Drag\nit by its title bar, resize it from any edge or corner: with the mouse, a finger or a pen. It keeps\nits place and size while it is open, and opens in the middle of the window again. The dialog is\n<code>&lt;dialog class=&quot;simple-html-grid filter-editor-content&quot;&gt;</code>, the grey layer behind it its <code>::backdrop</code>.</p>\n<p>Its texts - the buttons, the labels, the names of the operators, the tooltips - are <code>filterEditor.*</code>,\nthose of the header filter menu and its value list <code>filter.*</code>: see <a href=\"#guide/texts/filter-editor\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-undo-and-redo-in-the-filter-editor\">Undo and redo in the filter editor</h3>\n<p>The editor keeps an undo history of what is done in it: <strong>↶ Undo</strong> and <strong>↷ Redo</strong>, the icons at the\nleft end of the footer, and the keys <strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> (Cmd+Z) to undo, <strong>Ctrl+Y</strong> and <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Z</strong>\n(Cmd+Y, Cmd+Shift+Z) to redo, with the focus anywhere in the dialog. The icons are greyed out\n(<code>aria-disabled</code>) while there is nothing to undo or redo; their tooltips say the keys, <code>⌘Z</code> and <code>⇧⌘Z</code>\non a Mac.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A step</strong> is one edit: a condition or a group added, deleted or wrapped, AND / OR, a field, an\noperator or a column picked, value / column switched, clear all, a date picked - and <strong>what is typed\nin one value</strong>, from the first key since the input got the focus until the focus leaves it (or Enter).\nAn edit that changes nothing (a click on the AND in use) is no step, nor is a value typed back to what\nit was. A new step empties redo; up to 100 steps are kept.</li>\n<li><strong>While the user types in a value, Ctrl+Z is the input&#39;s own</strong>: the browser undoes the typing. Once it\nis undone back to where it started, Ctrl+Z undoes the editor&#39;s edit before it; Ctrl+Y stays the\ninput&#39;s until the focus leaves it, the browser redoes the typing. Everywhere else in the dialog the keys\nare the editor&#39;s - also with nothing to undo: the browser&#39;s own undo is the page&#39;s, Ctrl+Z on a button\nwould undo the typing in a value input. In the field menu&#39;s search box the keys are its own while it\nhas text; elsewhere in a picker&#39;s menu they undo the editor&#39;s edit and close the menu.</li>\n<li><strong>The focus</strong>: after Ctrl+Z or Ctrl+Y it goes to the control the edit was made with - the picker, the\ninput, the trash can of the condition deleted - or to the dialog when that is not there any more (a\ncondition deleted again by a redo). A click on Undo or Redo keeps the focus on the button.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply keeps the history</strong>: the dialog stays open, an edit applied can be undone and applied again.\nUndo changes what the editor shows, not the filter of the grid, until Apply. Each time the editor\nopens it starts with an empty history. It is the editor&#39;s own: the grid&#39;s undo (<code>datasource.undo()</code>,\nCtrl+Z in a cell) does not see it, and the editor&#39;s keys do not reach the grid behind it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-footer\">The footer</h2>\n<p>The footer shows the number of rows shown (<code>datasource.length()</code>, group rows included) and the\nnumber of rows there are (<code>12/500</code>), and the\nfilter as a row of chips: field, operator, value, with the groups in brackets. &quot;✕ Clear&quot; removes the\nwhole filter, a click on the chips opens the filter editor. A <code>BETWEEN</code> is <code>Age between 10 and 20</code>\n(an open end: <code>Age ≥ 10</code>), a <a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">quick date</a> its name - <code>Order date = This month</code> - and a condition that matches case has <strong>Aa</strong> after its value. The labels are the column labels, the\nrest are texts (<code>footer.*</code>, the operators <code>footer.operator.*</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/texts/footer\">Texts</a>. The\nvalues of a lookup column are shown as its cells show them, by their labels (<code>Country in Norway</code>);\nthe filter editor shows the values (<code>NO</code>), they are what it edits.</p>\n<p>While only the changed rows are shown (below), a chip <em>Changed rows only</em> comes first, ANDed with the\nfilter&#39;s chips. &quot;✕ Clear&quot; turns it off too. While Find&#39;s <em>Filter rows</em> hides rows (below), a chip\n<em>Find: denma</em> with its text comes next; &quot;✕ Clear&quot; turns it off and leaves the find bar open.</p>\n<p>Without any of them the footer has a button <strong>No filter</strong> in Clear&#39;s place: a click (Enter, Space)\nopens the filter editor, so a filter can be built without going through the menus - also with the\nheader filters hidden (<code>hideFilter</code>), where the filter menu can not be opened. The count is at the\ntop of the footer either way, the button or Clear and the chips on the line under it: nothing moves\nwhen a filter comes or goes. It is one button that changes: the focus stays on it, and goes back to it\nwhen the editor it opened closes. A footer of no height (<code>footerHeight: 0</code>) has none. Its texts are\n<code>footer.noFilter</code> and <code>footer.noFilter.tooltip</code>.</p>\n<p>The summary of the selected cells is at the footer&#39;s right, on the line of the filter (see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-summary-in-the-footer\">Selection and clipboard</a>). On a narrow\ngrid the chips collapse to one box when they would reach under it, and the summary is cut where the\nfilter&#39;s line ends, its tooltip has all of it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-find-filter-rows\">Find: Filter rows</h2>\n<p>Find (Ctrl+F in the grid) has a toggle <em>Filter rows</em> in its bar: only the rows with a cell that shows\nthe text are shown - the text as the cells show it, formatted, so the rows shown are the rows of the\nmarked cells. Like <em>Changed rows only</em> it is on top of the filter (AND) and not part of it:\n<code>getFilter()</code>, the filter editor and the state do not have it. It filters the rows again when the\ntext changes and whenever the rows are filtered; closing the find bar turns it off. With <em>Match case</em>\n(<code>Aa</code>) it finds the text as it is typed. See <a href=\"#guide/find/filter-rows\">Find</a>.\nIt filters in the browser only: a backend does not get it, the rows it sends are filtered by it, see\n<a href=\"#guide/find/filter-rows-and-a-backend\">Find</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.find('denma', { filterRows: true }); // only the rows with a cell that shows &quot;denma&quot;\ngridInterface.closeFind(); // every row again</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-changed-rows-only\">Changed rows only</h2>\n<p><code>datasource.filterChangedOnly(true)</code> shows only the new, edited and deleted rows of the rows the\nfilter lets through - the row menu&#39;s <em>View: Changed rows only</em> in the grid. It is not a condition of\nthe filter but on top of it (AND): <code>getFilter()</code>, the filter editor and the state do not have it, and\nsetting the filter leaves it on. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/changed-rows-only\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.filterChangedOnly(true);\ndatasource.isChangedOnly(); // true\ndatasource.filterChangedOnly(false);</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-filters-from-code\">Filters from code</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { and, or, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.filter(where('balance', 'GREATER_THAN', 2500)); // set a filter and run it\n\n// country = 'Norway' AND (age &lt; 30 OR age &gt; 60)\ndatasource.filter(\n    and(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'), or(where('age', 'LESS_THAN', 30), where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 60)))\n);\n\ndatasource.filter(); // run the filter that is set again, e.g. after rows were edited\ndatasource.filter(null); // no filter: every row\n\ndatasource.setFilter(where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 18)); // set it without running it\ndatasource.getFilter(); // the filter that is set, null when there is none</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>filter(expression)</code> replaces the filter, also the header filters&#39; conditions. The header cells\nkeep showing their text: to change both, use <code>gridInterface.setState({ filter, headerFilters })</code>,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/state/header-filters-in-the-state\">State</a>.</li>\n<li><code>setData()</code> runs the filter on the new rows. <code>{ refilter: false }</code> shows them all, the filter stays\nset, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/data-in-setdata\">Datasource</a>.</li>\n<li>The grid shows the result by itself, it follows the datasource&#39;s events.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-filter-format\">The filter format</h2>\n<p>A filter is a <code>FilterExpression</code>: a condition or a group. It is plain data, so it can be stored and\ncompared like any object, and stored as JSON - and sent to a backend, which makes the <code>WHERE</code> of a SQL\nquery of it with <code>toSqlWhere()</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/sql\">SQL</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { FilterCondition, FilterExpression, FilterGroup } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst condition: FilterCondition = { field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18 };\n\nconst group: FilterGroup = {\n    group: 'OR', // 'AND': every child must match. 'OR': one is enough\n    children: [\n        { field: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Norway' },\n        { field: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Sweden' }\n    ]\n};\n\nconst filter: FilterExpression = { group: 'AND', children: [condition, group] };\ndatasource.filter(filter);</code></pre>\n<p><code>where(field, operator, value?, { caseSensitive?, literal? })</code>, <code>and(...children)</code> and\n<code>or(...children)</code> build the same objects. <code>where(&#39;email&#39;, &#39;IS_BLANK&#39;)</code> leaves <code>value</code> out,\n<code>caseSensitive</code> and <code>literal</code> are only there when they are <code>true</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nwhere('name', 'EQUAL', 'Ann', { caseSensitive: true }); // { field: 'name', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Ann', caseSensitive: true }\nwhere('discount', 'CONTAINS', '50%', { literal: true }); // finds '50% off', not '500'\nwhere('age', 'BETWEEN', [18, 30]); // { field: 'age', operator: 'BETWEEN', value: [18, 30] }\nwhere('age', 'BETWEEN', [18, null]); // 18 or more</code></pre>\n<p><code>where()</code> throws a <code>TypeError</code> for a <code>BETWEEN</code> whose value is not <code>[from, to]</code>.</p>\n<p>A condition has no type of its own. It is compared as the type of its field in the datasource&#39;s\n<code>fields</code> (<code>text</code> when the field is not listed). A number value always compares as a number, and a\n<code>Date</code> value as a date.</p>\n<p><strong>Compare with another field</strong> of the same row with <code>valueField</code>. It wins over <code>value</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// rows changed after they were approved\ndatasource.filter({ field: 'changed', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', valueField: 'approved' });</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Ignored</strong>: these filter nothing out, and <code>getFilterString()</code>, <code>getFilterTree()</code> and the footer leave\nthem out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a condition without <code>field</code> or <code>operator</code></li>\n<li>a condition without the value it needs: a blank <code>value</code> - null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> or an invalid\n<code>Date</code> - and no <code>valueField</code>, for every operator but <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code>, <code>IN</code> and <code>NOT_IN</code>.\n<code>{ field: &#39;name&#39;, operator: &#39;EQUAL&#39;, value: &#39;&#39; }</code> does not find the empty names: use <code>IS_BLANK</code></li>\n<li>a <code>BETWEEN</code> whose value is not <code>[from, to]</code>, or whose two ends are blank (a <code>valueField</code> is not used\nby <code>BETWEEN</code>)</li>\n<li>an empty group. It is kept as it is (<code>filter(and())</code> keeps every row); use <code>filter(null)</code> for no\nfilter</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code> without a list (or with <code>[]</code>) are not ignored: <code>IN</code> matches no row, <code>NOT_IN</code> every row.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-operators\">Operators</h2>\n<p>The field&#39;s type (the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code>, <code>text</code> when the field is not listed) decides how a\ncondition compares the row&#39;s value - <em>row</em> below - with its value. This is the exact rule set: a\nbackend that filters the same rows (a SQL <code>WHERE</code> made from the filter) has to follow it. The SQL in\nthe tables is a sketch of the meaning, <code>col</code> the column and <code>:v</code> the value: case, collation and how a\ndatabase compares texts are the backend&#39;s. <code>toSqlWhere()</code> of <code>@simple-html/grid/sql</code> writes that <code>WHERE</code>\nfor PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQLite and SQL Server, its values as parameters, see <a href=\"#guide/sql\">SQL</a>.</p>\n<p>Two rules come first:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The type of the value wins over the field&#39;s</strong>: a number value compares as a number and a <code>Date</code>\nvalue as a date, also on a text field or a field that is not listed. <code>where(&#39;code&#39;, &#39;GREATER_THAN&#39;, 6)</code> on a text field compares numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>A condition without a value is ignored</strong>, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-format\">the filter format</a>: a blank\nvalue never means &quot;is blank&quot;, that is <code>IS_BLANK</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-operators-of-each-type\">The operators of each type</h3>\n<p><code>operatorsFor(type)</code> gives them, in this order. The grid offers these, and only these, in the header\nfilter menu and the filter editor; a column&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code> can offer fewer.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>operators</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code>, <code>END_WITH</code>, <code>IN</code>, <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>number, date, datetime</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>LESS_THAN</code>, <code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>IN</code>, <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { operatorsFor } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\noperatorsFor('number').includes('CONTAINS'); // false\noperatorsFor(datasource.getField('name').type); // the operators of the name field</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-text\">Text</h3>\n<p><em>row</em> is the row value as text: null and undefined are <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, anything else <code>String(value)</code> (<code>5</code> is\n<code>&#39;5&#39;</code>). Both texts are compared in lower case (<code>toLowerCase()</code>), or as they are with\n<code>caseSensitive: true</code>. <code>*</code> and <code>%</code> in the value stand for any text - none too, line breaks too, as\n<code>%</code> in SQL <code>LIKE</code>; <code>_</code> stands for one character, any - a line break too, a letter past U+FFFF (an\nemoji) is one - as <code>_</code> in <code>LIKE</code>. <strong><code>\\</code> makes the next <code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> or <code>\\</code> a plain character</strong>; a <code>\\</code>\nbefore anything else, or at the end, is a <code>\\</code> (<code>C:\\temp</code> is <code>C:\\temp</code>). The rest of the value is taken\nas it is:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>typed</th>\n<th>finds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>S__den</code></td>\n<td><code>S__den</code>, <code>Sweden</code> - each <code>_</code> any one character</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>S\\_\\_den</code></td>\n<td><code>S__den</code> only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>*\\%*</code> (<code>CONTAINS \\%</code>)</td>\n<td>a text with <code>%</code> in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>50\\%*</code></td>\n<td><code>50%</code>, <code>50% off</code> - not <code>500</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a\\*b</code></td>\n<td><code>a*b</code> only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a\\\\*</code></td>\n<td>starts with <code>a\\</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>escapeTextPattern(text)</code> gives the value that finds <code>text</code> as it is (<code>&#39;50% off&#39;</code> → <code>&#39;50\\% off&#39;</code>), for a\nheader filter an app fills in. <strong><code>literal: true</code></strong> on the condition takes all of the value as it is,\n<code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>\\</code> too: <code>CONTAINS &#39;50%&#39;</code> finds <code>50% off</code>, not <code>500</code> - the row menu&#39;s <em>Filter</em> on a cell&#39;s value has it, the header filters and the\nfilter editor do not make it (the filter editor leaves it out of a condition it applies).\nA text of several lines is one text: <code>BEGIN_WITH</code> is the start of the whole value, not of a line.</p>\n<p>In the SQL, <code>t</code> is <code>LOWER(COALESCE(col, &#39;&#39;))</code> and <code>v</code> the value in lower case with its <code>*</code> and <code>%</code>\nas <code>%</code>, its <code>_</code> as it is, a <code>\\%</code>, <code>\\_</code> or <code>\\*</code> of it escaped for the database&#39;s pattern\n(<code>ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code>, brackets on SQL Server and in SQLite&#39;s <code>GLOB</code>) - grid and SQL select the same rows:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>matches</th>\n<th>SQL, ignoring case</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>the whole text. With a wildcard: the pattern, the whole text</td>\n<td><code>t = &#39;v&#39;</code>, with a wildcard <code>t LIKE &#39;v&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>not <code>EQUAL</code>: a blank row matches</td>\n<td><code>NOT (t = &#39;v&#39;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td>the value somewhere in it</td>\n<td><code>t LIKE &#39;%v%&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td>not <code>CONTAINS</code>: a blank row matches</td>\n<td><code>NOT (t LIKE &#39;%v%&#39;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td>starts with the value</td>\n<td><code>t LIKE &#39;v%&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>END_WITH</code></td>\n<td>ends with the value</td>\n<td><code>t LIKE &#39;%v&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IN</code></td>\n<td>one of a list, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/in-and-not_in\">below</a></td>\n<td><code>UPPER(col) IN (...)</code>, and <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code> as <code>col IS NULL OR col = &#39;&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IN</code>: none of the list</td>\n<td><code>NOT (...)</code> of <code>IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> (and the number 0, in plain js data)</td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL OR col = &#39;&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>col IS NOT NULL AND col &lt;&gt; &#39;&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>With <code>caseSensitive: true</code> leave out the <code>LOWER()</code> / <code>UPPER()</code>: compare the texts as they are.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-number\">Number</h3>\n<p><em>row</em> is <code>Number(row value)</code>; a blank (null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>) and what is no number is <strong>0</strong>. The value\nis <code>Number(value)</code>, what is no number 0 too.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>matches</th>\n<th>SQL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code> / <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>the same number / not</td>\n<td><code>COALESCE(col, 0) = :v</code> / <code>&lt;&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN</code>, <code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>smaller, smaller or the same, larger, larger or the same</td>\n<td><code>COALESCE(col, 0) &lt; :v</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td>from <code>value[0]</code> to <code>value[1]</code>, both included</td>\n<td><code>COALESCE(col, 0) BETWEEN :from AND :to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td>one of a list, compared as the formatter shows the number</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> or 0</td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL OR col = 0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>col IS NOT NULL AND col &lt;&gt; 0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>So <code>where(&#39;age&#39;, &#39;LESS_THAN&#39;, 5)</code> matches the rows without an age, and <code>EQUAL 0</code> matches them too.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-date-and-datetime\">Date and datetime</h3>\n<p><em>row</em> and the value are compared as timestamps: a <code>date</code> field on the day (the time of both is left\nout), a <code>datetime</code> field to the second (the milliseconds are left out). A value of text (a <code>Date</code> after\nJSON, ISO text) is read as a date. <strong>A blank row date</strong> - null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid date - is\nnot on, before or after any date: of the comparisons it only matches <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>matches</th>\n<th>SQL, a <code>date</code> field</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>the same day (<code>date</code>) or second (<code>datetime</code>)</td>\n<td><code>CAST(col AS DATE) = :v</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>not <code>EQUAL</code>: a blank row date matches</td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL OR CAST(col AS DATE) &lt;&gt; :v</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN</code> ... <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>earlier, earlier or the same, later, later or the same. Never a blank</td>\n<td><code>CAST(col AS DATE) &lt; :v</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td>a <code>date</code>: from the start of <code>from</code>&#39;s day to the end of <code>to</code>&#39;s day. A <code>datetime</code>: from <code>from</code> to <code>to</code>, to the second. Never a blank</td>\n<td><code>CAST(col AS DATE) BETWEEN :from AND :to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td>one of a list, compared as the formatter shows the date (<code>2026-01-31</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid date (and 0)</td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>col IS NOT NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-boolean\">Boolean</h3>\n<p><em>row</em> is the value, a blank (null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, 0) is <strong>false</strong>: the cell shows it unchecked. The\nvalue is <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, compared with <code>===</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>matches</th>\n<th>SQL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL true</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td><code>col = TRUE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL false</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code> and the blanks</td>\n<td><code>col = FALSE OR col IS NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>not <code>EQUAL</code>: <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO true</code> is <code>false</code> and the blanks</td>\n<td><code>NOT (...)</code> of <code>EQUAL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> (and 0). <code>false</code> is a value</td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IS_BLANK</code>: <code>true</code> and <code>false</code></td>\n<td><code>col IS NOT NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-between\">BETWEEN</h3>\n<p><code>value</code> is <code>[from, to]</code>, both ends included. It is <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO from</code> and\n<code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO to</code>, each with the rules of the type - a blank number is 0, a blank date is in\nno range.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A blank end is open</strong>: <code>[10, null]</code> is <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO 10</code>, <code>[null, 20]</code> is\n<code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO 20</code>. Blank is null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> or an invalid date - 0 is an end. Both ends\nblank: the condition is ignored.</li>\n<li><strong><code>from</code> after <code>to</code></strong> (<code>[20, 10]</code>) matches no row, as <code>BETWEEN 20 AND 10</code> in SQL: the range is\nempty. The ends are not swapped - that would hide a typo.</li>\n<li>A <code>valueField</code> is not used: the ends are the value.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-in-and-not_in\">IN and NOT_IN</h3>\n<p><code>value</code> is a list, or a text with one value per line. Each value of the list and the row value are\ncompared as text, in upper case (<code>toUpperCase()</code>) - as they are with <code>caseSensitive: true</code> on a text\nfield. No wildcards.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A blank row value</strong> - null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> and 0 in a text field - is the text <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code>: <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code>\nin the list matches the blanks. A row whose text is <code>null</code> (in any case; <code>NULL</code> with <code>caseSensitive</code>)\nis matched by it too. With <code>caseSensitive</code> write the blank as <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code>, in upper case.</li>\n<li><strong>A number or date field</strong> compares the text its formatter shows: the row value and a number in the\nlist are formatted (<code>[2]</code> finds the 2 a formatter shows as <code>2.00</code>), a text in the list is taken as\nthe text shown (<code>&#39;2.00&#39;</code>). There a 0 is <code>&#39;0&#39;</code>, not <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code>. <code>caseSensitive</code> is not used.</li>\n<li>No list, or <code>[]</code>: <code>IN</code> matches no row, <code>NOT_IN</code> every row.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nwhere('country', 'IN', ['Norway', 'Sweden']); // ignoring case\nwhere('country', 'IN', ['Norway', 'NULL']); // or blank\nwhere('code', 'NOT_IN', ['AB', 'ab'], { caseSensitive: true }); // as they are</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-case\">Case</h3>\n<p><code>caseSensitive: true</code> is for the conditions that compare text: a text field (also one that is not\nlisted) with a value that is no number and no <code>Date</code>, and <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code> on a text field. It is not\nused on a number, date, datetime or boolean field, nor by <code>IS_BLANK</code> / <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code>; only <code>true</code> turns\nit on. Without it the texts are compared in lower case (<code>IN</code>: upper case), as JavaScript does - a\nbackend&#39;s case-insensitive collation may differ for a few letters (<code>ß</code>, the Turkish <code>İ</code>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-operators-a-type-does-not-offer\">Operators a type does not offer</h3>\n<p>The datasource still filters on an operator the field&#39;s type does not offer - a filter saved with\nv5, a filter of code - the way v5 did. The grid does not offer them, the filter editor shows one and\nkeeps it. They are legacy; a backend can treat them as these:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>number, date, datetime</td>\n<td><code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code> (and an operator of plain js that is none)</td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>number, date, datetime</td>\n<td><code>END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>number, date, datetime</td>\n<td><code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN</code> ... <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td>the two texts compared as JavaScript does (code unit by code unit), in lower case or as they are with <code>caseSensitive</code>. No wildcards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td>any other operator</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td><code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td>the text <code>TRUE</code> / <code>FALSE</code> / <code>NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-0-is-blank\">0 is blank</h3>\n<p>A row value of <code>0</code> counts as blank, on purpose: in ERP style data 0 and empty mean the same.\n<code>IS_BLANK</code> matches 0 and <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code> leaves it out, in every field that holds the number 0, and\nthe value list puts 0 under &quot;Blank&quot;. The text <code>&#39;0&#39;</code> is not blank. <code>IN</code> on a number field compares\nthe formatted text, so there 0 is <code>&#39;0&#39;</code>, not <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code>. The <code>required</code> validation rule takes 0 as\nmissing too, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/the-built-in-rules\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<p>The other way round, a blank compares as the empty value of its type: a blank number as 0\n(<code>LESS_THAN 5</code> matches it), a blank boolean as <code>false</code> (<code>EQUAL false</code> matches it), a blank text as\n<code>&#39;&#39;</code>. A blank date is no date at all. So, per type:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>blank (<code>IS_BLANK</code>)</th>\n<th>a blank row value compares as</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>number</td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, 0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>date, datetime</td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid date</td>\n<td>no date: only <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> matches it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td>null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> - <code>false</code> is a value</td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-filter-as-text\">The filter as text</h2>\n<p><code>getFilterString()</code> gives the filter as one line of text, <code>getFilterTree()</code> as a tree for your own\nrendering. Both leave out what the filter ignores, and use the fields&#39; labels unless you pass a\nfunction for them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { and, Datasource, or, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({ fields: { age: { type: 'number', label: 'Age' } } });\npeople.filter(and(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'), or(where('age', 'LESS_THAN', 20), where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 60))));\n\npeople.getFilterString();\n// ([COUNTRY] &lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt; 'NORWAY' AND ([AGE] &lt;&lt;LESS_THAN&gt;&gt; '20' OR [AGE] &lt;&lt;GREATER_THAN&gt;&gt; '60'))\n\npeople.getFilterString((field) =&gt; (field === 'country' ? 'Land' : field));\n// ([LAND] &lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt; 'NORWAY' AND ([AGE] &lt;&lt;LESS_THAN&gt;&gt; '20' OR [AGE] &lt;&lt;GREATER_THAN&gt;&gt; '60'))\n\npeople.getFilterTree();\n// { kind: 'group', group: 'AND', children: [\n//     { kind: 'condition', field: 'country', label: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Norway', hasValue: true, caseSensitive: false },\n//     { kind: 'group', group: 'OR', children: [ ...the two age conditions ] }\n// ] }</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getFilterString()</code> is <code>&#39;&#39;</code> and <code>getFilterTree()</code> is <code>null</code> when no filter is set, or nothing of\nit is left.</li>\n<li>A condition that matches case keeps the case of its value in <code>getFilterString()</code> and ends with\n<code>&lt;&lt;MATCH_CASE&gt;&gt;</code>: <code>[NAME] &lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt; &#39;Ann&#39; &lt;&lt;MATCH_CASE&gt;&gt;</code>; in the tree it has <code>caseSensitive: true</code>\n(only where it is used: a number condition with it is <code>false</code>).</li>\n<li>A <code>BETWEEN</code> is <code>[AGE] &lt;&lt;BETWEEN&gt;&gt; &#39;10&#39; &lt;&lt;AND&gt;&gt; &#39;20&#39;</code>, an open end <code>&#39;&#39;</code>. In the tree <code>value</code> is the\nstart and <code>valueTo</code> the end, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for an open end.</li>\n<li>The values are formatted with the field&#39;s formatter, the values of <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code> are shown as\nthey are. The tree&#39;s root is always a group, also for a single condition.</li>\n<li><code>getFilterTree(label, formatterOf)</code> takes a formatter for the values of a field too, the <code>IN</code> /\n<code>NOT_IN</code> values included (<code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code> stays): <code>formatterOf(field)</code> gives one, or <code>undefined</code> for the\nfield&#39;s own.</li>\n<li>The grid&#39;s footer uses <code>getFilterTree()</code> with the column labels, and the formatter of its lookup\ncolumns: a chip shows <code>Country in Norway</code> where the filter has <code>IN [&#39;NO&#39;]</code>.</li>\n<li>The operators and AND / OR of <code>getFilterString()</code> are texts, <code>filterString.*</code>: change them with\n<code>setTexts()</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/getfilterstring\">Texts</a>. <code>getFilterTree()</code> has the operators as they\nare (<code>&#39;EQUAL&#39;</code>), for your own rendering.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"sorting-grouping","title":"Sorting and grouping","html":"<p>Sorting and grouping are done by the datasource. The user does them in the grid&#39;s header, you do\nthem from code, and the grid shows the result.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-sorting\">Sorting</h2>\n<p>The user clicks (or taps) a header label to sort on that column, ascending. A second click sorts it\ndescending. Shift+click adds the column to the sort there is. A press that moves the label is a drag,\nnot a click, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a>. The header menu&#39;s &quot;Remove sorting&quot;\nremoves the sort, apart from the grouped fields.</p>\n<p>From code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.sort({ attribute: 'company', ascending: true }); // replaces the sort there was\ndatasource.sort({ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }, { add: true }); // company, then age\n\ndatasource.sort([\n    { attribute: 'country', ascending: true },\n    { attribute: 'company', ascending: false }\n]);\n\ndatasource.sort(); // sort again with the sort there is, e.g. after edits\ndatasource.getLastSorting(); // [{ attribute: 'country', ascending: true }, { attribute: 'company', ascending: false }]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>With <code>{ add: true }</code>, a field that is in the sort already gets the new direction and keeps its\nplace. A list (<code>sort([a, b])</code>) always replaces the sort.</li>\n<li><code>setOrderBy(args, { add })</code> sets the sort without sorting, <code>sort()</code> sorts.</li>\n<li><code>resetSort()</code> clears the sort, the rows keep their order. <code>filter()</code> after it gives them back the\norder they were set in - sorted on the grouped fields when grouped.</li>\n<li>The sort stays when the data changes: <code>setData()</code> sorts the new rows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>How values compare:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Empty values (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid date) come first when ascending, last when\ndescending. <code>0</code> and <code>false</code> are values, not empty.</li>\n<li>Text is compared with <code>localeCompare</code>. <code>datasource.setLocaleCompare(&#39;nb&#39;)</code> sorts it with the\nrules of a language (an <code>Intl.Collator</code>).</li>\n<li>A <code>date</code> field sorts on the day, the time is ignored. A <code>datetime</code> field sorts on the full time.</li>\n<li>In a grid, a <code>text</code> column with a <a href=\"#guide/formatting/what-a-template-and-a-link-change\">template or a link</a>\nsorts on the text its cells show (<code>{last}, {first}</code> sorts by the last name), a column grouped by on\nits value. A lookup column sorts on the name it shows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The sort is part of the grid&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>: <code>getState().sorting</code>. To start sorted, put it\nin the config&#39;s <code>initialState</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-grouping\">Grouping</h2>\n<p>The user groups by dragging a header label to the panel above the header. A group shows as a chip\nin the panel: its icon removes it, and the chips can be dragged to change the order. A group row\nshows the group&#39;s name and its number of rows, and its <code>+</code> / <code>-</code> box expands or collapses it. The\nheader menu has &quot;Expand all&quot;, &quot;Collapse all&quot; and &quot;Clear all&quot;.</p>\n<p>Left of the rows each level of grouping has a column, like a tree: a group row has its box in the\ncolumn of its level, and the line of an expanded group runs from under its box down through its rows,\nso where a group ends shows. The colour of the lines and the box&#39;s frame is\n<code>--simple-html-grid-group-line-color</code> (see <a href=\"#guide/theming\">Theming</a>).</p>\n<p>From code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.group([{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }]);\ndatasource.group([{ attribute: 'company', title: 'Company' }], { add: true }); // country, then company\n\ndatasource.expandGroup(); // every group\ndatasource.collapseGroup(); // every group\ndatasource.expandGroup('Norway'); // one group, by its id\n\ndatasource.group([]); // no grouping. removeGroup() does the same</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>attribute</code> is the field, <code>title</code> the text in front of the value in the group row:\n<code>Country: Norway</code>. Its first letter is shown in upper case.</li>\n<li><code>group()</code> sorts the rows on the grouped fields, ascending, and replaces the sort there was. A\n<code>sort()</code> after it sorts within the groups, the grouped fields stay first.</li>\n<li><strong>Groups start collapsed</strong>, only the ids given to <code>setExpanded()</code> start expanded. <code>getExpanded()</code>\nlists the expanded ids.</li>\n<li>A row is grouped on its value as the field&#39;s formatter shows it: a <code>date</code> field groups by day, a\nnumber with its decimals as the formatter shows them. In a grid, a date column shown as weeks,\nmonths, quarters or years (its display format <code>weekIso</code>, <code>weekUs</code>, <code>month</code>, <code>quarter</code>, <code>year</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>) groups by them: <code>Due: 2026-W47</code>. Another format\nfor the column groups the rows again. The rows with an empty value are one group\nat the top, its id is <code>&#39; blank&#39;</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code>group()</code> to group. <code>setGrouping()</code> only stores the grouping: the next call that builds the\ndisplayed rows (<code>group()</code>, <code>sort()</code>, <code>filter()</code>, <code>setData()</code> ...) groups them.</li>\n<li><strong>The rows are always grouped sorted on the grouped fields</strong>, whatever led there: <code>group()</code>,\n<code>sort()</code>, <code>filter()</code> after <code>setGrouping()</code>, <code>setOrderBy()</code> or <code>resetSort()</code>. The grouped fields come\nfirst in the sort, each with the direction the sort gives it (ascending when it has none), the other\nfields of the sort after them. A group is built from each run of equal values, so the rows of a\ngroup have to be next to each other.</li>\n<li><code>removeGroup(group)</code> removes one level, found by its field: the object from <code>getGrouping()</code> or a\ncopy, e.g. from a saved state. A field that is not grouped on changes nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Sums in the group rows</strong>: a column with an <code>aggregate</code> (<code>&#39;sum&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;avg&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;min&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;max&#39;</code> or\n<code>&#39;count&#39;</code>) shows it in its place in every group row, over the group&#39;s rows - collapsed sub groups\nincluded. The name stays at the left. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The grouping and the expanded groups are part of the grid&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>:\n<code>getState().grouping</code> and <code>expandedGroups</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"sorting-grouping\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-group-rows\">Group rows</h2>\n<p>When the datasource is grouped, <code>getRows()</code> has a group row in front of each group, and leaves out\nthe rows of a collapsed group. A group row is a <code>GroupRow</code>, made by the datasource, not one of your\nobjects. <code>isGroupRow(row)</code> tells it from a data row, and narrows the type:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.group([{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }]);\ndatasource.expandGroup();\n\nfor (const row of datasource.getRows()) {\n    if (isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log(row.__groupName, row.__groupTotal); // 'Country: Norway', 2\n    } else {\n        console.log('  ', row.name);\n    }\n}\n\n// the data rows only, the rows of collapsed groups included\nconst names = datasource.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; row.name);</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>__group</code></td>\n<td>always <code>true</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupID</code></td>\n<td>the id for <code>expandGroup(id)</code> / <code>collapseGroup(id)</code>: the value as formatted, e.g. <code>&#39;Norway&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupName</code></td>\n<td>the text the grid shows: <code>&#39;Country: Norway&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupLvl</code></td>\n<td>the level, 0 for the first grouped field. The grid indents by it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupTotal</code></td>\n<td>the number of data rows in the group, its sub groups included. More sums of a group: <code>datasource.aggregate(field, fn, groupRow)</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/from-code\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupChildren</code></td>\n<td>its rows: data rows, or the group rows of the next level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupExpanded</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> when the group is expanded</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Read the fields, do not change them. A group row can not be selected, edited or marked for\ndeletion: <code>getSelectedRows()</code>, <code>getDataRows()</code> and <code>getAllData()</code> never have one. <code>getRow(n)</code> and\n<code>currentEntity</code> can, check them with <code>isGroupRow()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-groups-in-groups\">Groups in groups</h2>\n<p>Each field given to <code>group()</code> is one level. The id of a sub group starts with the ids of the groups\nabove it, joined by <code>-</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.group([\n    { attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' },\n    { attribute: 'company', title: 'Company' }\n]);\n\ndatasource.expandGroup('Norway'); // the first level\ndatasource.expandGroup('Norway-ACME'); // company ACME in Norway, and Norway if it was collapsed\n\nconst ids = datasource\n    .getRows()\n    .filter(isGroupRow)\n    .map((row) =&gt; `${'  '.repeat(row.__groupLvl)}${row.__groupID}`);</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Expanding a sub group expands the groups above it too, so it is shown:\n<code>expandGroup(&#39;Norway-ACME&#39;)</code> while <code>Norway</code> is collapsed expands both.</li>\n<li>A sub group stays expanded when the group above it is collapsed and expanded again.</li>\n<li><code>expandGroup()</code> and <code>collapseGroup()</code> without an id work on every level.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"formatting","title":"Formatting","html":"<p>A formatter turns the values of a field into text, and text back into values. Every field has one:\nits own, or the datasource&#39;s default for the field&#39;s type.</p>\n<p>The datasource&#39;s options name a formatter by its id: <code>formatter: &#39;numberComma&#39;</code>. The built-in\nformatters are registered under ids from the start, your own you register with\n<code>registerFormatter()</code>. So the options are plain JSON and can be stored, the formatters stay code.</p>\n<p>A formatter gives text. To draw a cell another way - a badge, a bar, a select - give the column a cell\nrenderer, see <a href=\"#guide/custom-cells\">Custom cells</a>: it gets the formatter&#39;s text too.</p>\n<p>How a column <em>shows</em> its values - a date as <code>22.11.2026</code> or as the week <code>2026-W47</code>, a number as\n<code>1 234 567,89</code> - the user can pick in the header menu, without a formatter of your own: see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>. A column can show a template of the row&#39;s fields,\n<code>{firstname} {lastname}</code>, and be a link, <code>https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}</code> or <code>mailto:{email}</code>:\nsee <a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">Templates and links</a>. A cell can take a colour from its value - red text\nbelow 0, an orange background for <code>waiting</code>: see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">Colour rules</a>. A number column can draw\na bar, a colour scale or an icon by the value&#39;s place among its numbers, as a spreadsheet&#39;s conditional\nformatting: see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/data-bars\">Data bars</a>, <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-scales\">Colour scales</a> and <a href=\"#guide/formatting/icon-sets\">Icon sets</a>. The\nuser sets all of them in the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">grid config dialog</a>, the gear in the footer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-formatter-does\">What a formatter does</h2>\n<p>A formatter is a plain object with two methods and three optional parts:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { Formatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst percent: Formatter = {\n    // value -&gt; text: a cell, the header filter, a group row, the footer, getFilterString()\n    format: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? `${Math.round(value * 100)} %` : ''),\n    // optional: the text while a cell is edited, the plain value to type over. Left out, format is used\n    formatForEdit: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? String(Math.round(value * 100)) : ''),\n    // text the user typed or pasted -&gt; the value stored in the row\n    parse: (text) =&gt; (text.trim() === '' ? null : Number(text.replace('%', '').trim()) / 100),\n    // optional: text typed in a filter -&gt; the value filtered on. Left out, parse is used\n    parseFilter: (text) =&gt; Number(text.replace('%', '').trim()) / 100,\n    // optional: shown in the empty header filter\n    placeholder: '0 %'\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> gets whatever the row holds and returns text: <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for an empty value.</li>\n<li><code>formatForEdit</code> is what a cell shows while it is edited - from when its input gets the focus until it\nloses it - and the text the editor over a cell starts from. Give the plain value where <code>format</code> is\nhard to type over: thousands separators, a currency sign, a <code>%</code>. <code>parse</code> must read it back. The cell\nshows <code>format</code> again when it loses the focus. A readonly cell always shows <code>format</code>.</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> and <code>parseFilter</code> always get text from the grid.</li>\n<li>A boolean field is shown as a checkbox from the raw value. Its formatter is used where it is\nshown as text: the filter string, group rows, and text pasted into the column.</li>\n<li>Do not use <code>this</code> in the methods: the datasource can call them on their own.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-defaults-per-type\">The defaults per type</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>default formatter</th>\n<th>shows</th>\n<th>parses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>text</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;text&#39;</code>, <code>TextFormatter</code></td>\n<td>the text, <code>0</code> and <code>false</code> as <code>0</code> and <code>false</code></td>\n<td>the text as it is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>number</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code>, <code>NumberFormatterDot</code></td>\n<td><code>1234.5</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;12.5&#39;</code> and <code>&#39;12,5&#39;</code> as 12.5. Text that is no number, and <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, as 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code>, <code>BooleanFormatter</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;true&#39;</code> (any case) as true, other text as false, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>date</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;dateYYYYMMDD&#39;</code>, <code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDD</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;2026-11-20&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeYYYYMMDD&#39;</code>, <code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20T13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;2026-11-20T13:45:12&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Every formatter shows <code>null</code> and <code>undefined</code> as <code>&#39;&#39;</code>.</p>\n<p>An emptied number cell stores <code>0</code>, not <code>null</code>: the number formatters parse <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as 0. &quot;Clear&quot; in the\nrow menu stores <code>null</code>. Both count as blank in a filter, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/0-is-blank\">0 is blank</a>. For <code>null</code> from an emptied cell, give the field a formatter\nthat parses <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as <code>null</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type Formatter, NumberFormatterDot, registerFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst emptyIsNull: Formatter = {\n    ...NumberFormatterDot,\n    parse: (text) =&gt; (text.trim() === '' ? null : NumberFormatterDot.parse(text))\n};\nregisterFormatter('numberEmptyIsNull', emptyIsNull);\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { amount: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberEmptyIsNull' } } });</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-built-in-formatters\">The built-in formatters</h2>\n<p>Each is registered under an id, the options use the id. The object is exported too: call it\nyourself (<code>NumberFormatterComma.format(12.5)</code>), or build a variant of it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>formatter</th>\n<th>shows</th>\n<th>placeholder</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;text&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>TextFormatter</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>BooleanFormatter</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>NumberFormatterDot</code></td>\n<td><code>1234.5</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;numberComma&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>NumberFormatterComma</code></td>\n<td><code>1234,5</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;dateYYYYMMDD&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDD</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code></td>\n<td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;dateDDMMYYYY&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterDDMMYYYY</code></td>\n<td><code>20.11.2026</code></td>\n<td><code>DD.MM.YYYY</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeYYYYMMDD&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20T13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeDDMMYYYY&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS</code></td>\n<td><code>20.11.2026T13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>DD.MM.YYYYTHH:MM:SS</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The date formatters fill in what the typed text leaves out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a missing month is the current month, a missing day (or year, for the <code>DD.MM.YYYY</code> ones) the\ncurrent one</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> gives the date the current time of day, for the parts of the time the text has not.\n<code>parseFilter</code> gives them 0, so a filter starts at the start of the day.</li>\n<li><code>&#39;&#39;</code> is <code>null</code>, text that is not a date gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The number formatters read both <code>12.5</code> and <code>12,5</code>, whichever separator they show.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-different-default-for-a-type\">A different default for a type</h2>\n<p><code>formatters</code> in the datasource&#39;s options sets the formatter of every field of a type that has none\nof its own, by id:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: 'number', amount: 'number', born: 'date' },\n    formatters: { number: 'numberComma', date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }\n});</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"formatters\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-formatter-for-one-field\">A formatter for one field</h2>\n<p>Give the field a <code>formatter</code>, the id of a registered formatter:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberComma' },\n        born: { type: 'date', formatter: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.getFormatter('price').format(12.5); // '12,5'</code></pre>\n<p><code>datasource.getFormatter(field)</code> returns the formatter of a field - the object, not the id -\n<code>getField(field).formatter</code> too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-formatter-of-your-own\">A formatter of your own</h2>\n<p>Register it under an id with <code>registerFormatter(id, formatter)</code>, and name the id in the options. A\nbuilt-in formatter is a plain object, so a variant is a spread away:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type Formatter, NumberFormatterDot, registerFormatter, TextFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// shown in upper case, stored as typed\nconst upperCase: Formatter = {\n    ...TextFormatter,\n    format: (value) =&gt; TextFormatter.format(value).toUpperCase()\n};\n\n// two decimals and a space between the thousands: 12345.6 -&gt; '12 345.60', edited as '12345.6'\nconst money: Formatter = {\n    ...NumberFormatterDot,\n    format: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? value.toFixed(2).replace(/\\B(?=(\\d{3})+(?!\\d))/g, ' ') : ''),\n    formatForEdit: (value) =&gt; NumberFormatterDot.format(value),\n    parse: (text) =&gt; NumberFormatterDot.parse(text.replaceAll(' ', ''))\n};\n\n// when the page loads, before a datasource names them\nregisterFormatter('upperCase', upperCase);\nregisterFormatter('money', money);\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        country: { formatter: 'upperCase' }, // a text field: no type needed\n        price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'money' }\n    },\n    formatters: { date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' } // an id works for the default of a type too\n});\n\ndatasource.getFormatter('price').format(12345.6); // '12 345.60'</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Register before the datasource is made.</strong> The datasource looks its ids up once, when it is\nmade, and keeps the formatters it found. An id that is not registered throws there, and the\nmessage lists the ids that are.</li>\n<li><strong>Registering an id again replaces the formatter</strong>, for the datasources made after; one made\nbefore keeps the formatter it has. A built-in id can be replaced too, and replacing a default\n(<code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code> ...) changes every field of that type without a formatter of its own. Use an id of\nyour own unless that is what you want.</li>\n<li>There is one registry for the page, every datasource uses it.</li>\n<li>The id is any text. The options stay plain JSON, so <code>fields</code> and <code>formatters</code> can be stored - in a\nbackend, say - and the formatters registered in code. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"custom-formatter\"></div>\n<p>A formatter that reads a setting of your own (the number of decimals, say) can change what it\nshows: call <code>gridInterface.rebuild()</code> after the setting changed, so the cells and the header\nfilters show it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-display-formats\">Display formats</h2>\n<p>A display format is how a column shows its values, picked by the user: right click a column label,\n<strong>Format ›</strong>. A date column shows its dates as ISO, Norwegian, US ... or a part of them - the week,\nthe day of the week, the days until; a number column picks its decimals, its thousands separator and\nits decimal mark. It is a view setting, like a column&#39;s width: only the text shown changes, the value\nstays as it is - an edited cell shows the value. The header filter takes the text as the cells show it:\n<code>2026-W47</code> in a column shown as weeks is that week, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a>. It is part\nof the state, so <code>getState()</code> keeps it.</p>\n<p>Give a column the format it starts with in the config, <code>ColumnConfig.format</code>: an object with a part\nfor the field&#39;s type, <code>date</code> for a <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code> field, <code>number</code> for a <code>number</code> field.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        born: { format: { date: 'norwegian' } }, // 22.11.2026\n        due: { format: { date: 'daysUntil' } }, // 3: in three days\n        updated: { format: { date: 'norwegianTime' } }, // 22.11.2026 13:45 (a datetime field)\n        balance: { format: { number: { decimals: 2, thousands: ' ', decimal: ',' } } } // 1 234 567,89\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['born'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['due'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['updated'], width: 130 },\n            { fields: ['balance'], width: 120 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>A format that does not fit the field throws a <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made, or <code>updateConfig()</code>\nor <code>setState()</code> is given it: an id that is not a date format, a part for another type (<code>date</code> on a\nnumber field), a time format on a <code>date</code> field, a number format with a part it does not have, and a\nformat on a lookup column (<code>editor</code>: its cells show a label). Text and boolean columns have no\nformat, and no Format › in their header menu.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"full-grid\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-date-formats\">The date formats</h3>\n<p>What 2026-11-22 13:45:12, a Sunday, is shown as. The time formats are for a <code>datetime</code> field only.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>date</code></th>\n<th>shows</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;iso&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-22</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;norwegian&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.2026</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;norwegianShort&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.26</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;us&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>11/22/2026</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;uk&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>22/11/2026</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;medium&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>Nov 22, 2026</code></td>\n<td><code>22. nov. 2026</code> with <code>format.locale</code> <code>nb-NO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;long&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>November 22, 2026</code></td>\n<td><code>22. november 2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;full&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>Sunday, November 22, 2026</code></td>\n<td><code>søndag 22. november 2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;isoTime&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-22 13:45</code></td>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;isoSeconds&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-22 13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;norwegianTime&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.2026 13:45</code></td>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;norwegianSeconds&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.2026 13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;time&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>13:45</code></td>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;timeSeconds&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;weekday&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>Sunday</code></td>\n<td><code>søndag</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;weekIso&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>ISO 8601, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;weekUs&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-W48</code></td>\n<td>see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;month&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;quarter&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026 Q4</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;year&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>2026</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;daysUntil&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n<td>whole days from today, see below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The local day and time are shown, as the date formatters show them. A <code>datetime</code> column with a date\nformat shows the date only: <code>&#39;norwegian&#39;</code> is <code>22.11.2026</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Week, ISO</strong> (ISO 8601, as in Norway and most of Europe): a week starts on Monday, week 1 is the\nweek with the year&#39;s first Thursday. A week belongs to one year, the year of its Thursday: the first\ndays of January can be the last week of the year before, the last days of December week 1 of the\nnext. 2027-01-01 is <code>2026-W53</code>, 2024-12-30 is <code>2025-W01</code>. A year has 52 or 53 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Week, US</strong> (as Excel&#39;s <code>WEEKNUM</code>): a week starts on Sunday, week 1 is the week of January 1, and\na week across New Year is split - 2026-12-31 is <code>2026-W53</code>, 2027-01-01 <code>2027-W01</code>. 53 weeks, 54\nin a leap year that starts on a Saturday (2000).</li>\n<li><strong>Days until</strong>: the whole days from today to the date - <code>1</code> tomorrow, <code>0</code> today, <code>-1</code> yesterday.\nThe time of day and summer time do not count. Today is the day the cell is rendered: a grid left\nopen over midnight shows yesterday&#39;s count until its cells are rendered again - a scroll, an edit,\n<code>gridInterface.refresh()</code>. It has no timer of its own; call <code>refresh()</code> from one of yours to keep it\nup to date.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"section-the-language-of-the-names\">The language of the names</h4>\n<p>The names of the months and the days - <code>medium</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>full</code>, <code>weekday</code> - are made by\n<code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> in the language of the text <code>format.locale</code>, <code>&#39;en-US&#39;</code> by default. It is set\nwith the other texts (see <a href=\"#guide/texts/display-formats\">Texts</a>): the Norwegian texts of an app give\n<code>&#39;nb-NO&#39;</code>, and the dates follow. A tag Intl does not take (<code>&#39;nb_NO&#39;</code>) shows English.</p>\n<p>How a week and a quarter are written are texts too, with <code>{year}</code>, <code>{week}</code> (two digits) and\n<code>{quarter}</code> filled in:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({\n    'format.locale': 'nb-NO', // søndag 22. november 2026\n    'format.weekIso': 'uke {week} {year}', // uke 47 2026\n    'format.quarter': '{quarter}. kvartal {year}' // 4. kvartal 2026\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-number-formats\">The number formats</h3>\n<p>A <code>number</code> part has four optional parts; <code>{}</code> shows the number as it is, without separators:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part</th>\n<th>takes</th>\n<th>left out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>decimals</code></td>\n<td><code>0</code> to <code>4</code></td>\n<td>the decimals the number has, as the field&#39;s formatter shows them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>thousands</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39; &#39;</code>, <code>&#39;,&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;.&#39;</code> or <code>&quot;&#39;&quot;</code></td>\n<td>none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>decimal</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;.&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;,&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;.&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>percent</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code>: the number times 100, with <code> %</code> after it</td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>What 1234567.891 is shown as:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>number</code></th>\n<th>shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>{ thousands: &#39; &#39;, decimal: &#39;,&#39; }</code></td>\n<td><code>1 234 567,891</code> (the menu&#39;s Norwegian)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ thousands: &#39;,&#39; }</code></td>\n<td><code>1,234,567.891</code> (English)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ thousands: &quot;&#39;&quot; }</code></td>\n<td><code>1&#39;234&#39;567.891</code> (Swiss)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{}</code></td>\n<td><code>1234567.891</code> (Plain)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ decimals: 2, thousands: &#39; &#39;, decimal: &#39;,&#39; }</code></td>\n<td><code>1 234 567,89</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ decimals: 0 }</code></td>\n<td><code>1234568</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ decimals: 1, percent: true }</code> on 0.256</td>\n<td><code>25.6 %</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rounded half away from zero, of the number as it is written</strong>: <code>2.5</code> is <code>3</code>, <code>-2.5</code> is <code>-3</code>,\n<code>1.005</code> with two decimals is <code>1.01</code> - <code>toFixed(2)</code> gives <code>1.00</code>, of the binary value\n1.00499999.... A negative number rounded to 0 has no minus: <code>-0.001</code> is <code>0.00</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The decimals as is</strong> are the ones the number has, at most 20, as the field&#39;s formatter writes\nthem: <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is <code>0.30000000000000004</code>. No exponent: <code>1e21</code> is a 1 and 21 zeros.</li>\n<li><strong>The characters are plain ASCII</strong>: the space is U+0020, not the no-break space\n<code>Intl.NumberFormat(&#39;nb-NO&#39;)</code> writes - a cell is one line, and Find finds a space typed. The minus\nis <code>-</code>, not the <code>−</code> (U+2212) of <code>nb-NO</code>. The apostrophe is <code>&#39;</code>, not the <code>’</code> of Chromium&#39;s <code>de-CH</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>One character can not be both separators</strong>: <code>{ thousands: &#39;,&#39;, decimal: &#39;,&#39; }</code> throws a\n<code>TypeError</code>, and so does <code>{ thousands: &#39;.&#39; }</code> (the decimal mark is <code>.</code> when it is left out). In\nthe menu a separator the other one has is greyed out.</li>\n<li><strong>From the menu</strong>, a column without a format starts with the decimal mark its formatter shows:\n<em>Decimals › 2</em> on a <code>numberComma</code> field gives <code>{ decimals: 2, decimal: &#39;,&#39; }</code>.</li>\n<li>A value that is no finite number, and a blank, is shown by the field&#39;s formatter - a blank as an\nempty cell. So is a date column&#39;s value that is no date.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-what-a-display-format-changes\">What a display format changes</h3>\n<p>Only what the grid shows as the cell&#39;s text, and what follows the cells. The value, and everything\nthat reads or writes it, keep to the field&#39;s formatter:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>where</th>\n<th>shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>the cells, a readonly cell with the focus too</td>\n<td>the display format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a cell being edited (it has the focus)</td>\n<td>the value, as the field&#39;s formatter writes it for editing (<code>formatForEdit</code>): a cell shown as <code>2026-W47</code> is edited as <code>2026-11-22</code>, <code>1 234 567,89</code> as <code>1234567.891</code>. What is typed is parsed by the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a renderer of your own (<code>ColumnConfig.renderer</code>)</td>\n<td><code>text</code> is the display format, <code>value</code> the value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the edited marker&#39;s tooltip, &quot;Original value&quot;</td>\n<td>the display format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the aggregates in the group rows and the totals row</td>\n<td>the display format of a number column, see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/how-they-are-shown\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the summary of the selected cells in the footer</td>\n<td>the display format of the first number field, its decimals - as is: two at most</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Find, the fitted width (<code>fitColumns()</code>), the dimmed repeated values</td>\n<td>the text the cells show</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the header filter, its placeholder, the range block of its menu, the date pickers</td>\n<td>the display format - <strong>you type a week</strong>, <code>2026-W47</code>, and the filter is the whole week (<code>filterAsShown</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a>). <code>filterAsShown: false</code>, the day of the week and a time of day: the field&#39;s formatter, <code>2026-11-22</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the filter chips, the filter editor, <code>getFilterString()</code></td>\n<td>the field&#39;s formatter: a filter is on the value - <code>2026-W47</code> typed is a BETWEEN of <code>2026-11-16</code> and <code>2026-11-22</code> there</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the names of the group rows, grouping</td>\n<td>the field&#39;s formatter (a day of a date) - but a date column shown as weeks, months, quarters or years groups by them: one group a week, <code>Due: 2026-W47</code>. Not the day of the week nor a time of day: their rows are not next to each other in the sort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sorting</td>\n<td>the value: a column shown as weeks sorts by its dates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>copy (Ctrl+C, Copy ›) and the CSV export</td>\n<td>the field&#39;s formatter: the value, so a paste reads it back - <code>2026-W47</code> and <code>1 234 567,89</code> it could not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the Excel export</td>\n<td>the value, with an Excel number format like the display format where Excel has one, see <a href=\"#guide/export/typed-cells\">Export</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-in-the-state\">In the state</h3>\n<p>The formats are <code>GridState.formats</code>, keyed by field: what the columns&#39; <code>format</code> starts with and the\nmenu changes. <code>setState({ formats })</code> replaces them all - to change one column, start from the ones\nthere are:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const { formats } = gridInterface.getState(); // { born: { date: 'norwegian' } }\n\ngridInterface.setState({ formats: { ...formats, balance: { number: { decimals: 2 } } } });\ngridInterface.setState({ formats: {} }); // every column shows its field's formatter\ngridInterface.resetState(); // the formats of the config again</code></pre>\n<p>See <a href=\"#guide/state/display-formats-in-the-state\">State</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-formats-by-type\">Formats by type</h3>\n<p><code>typeFormats</code> in the config gives every column of a type a format: the date, datetime and number\ncolumns without a date or number format of their own show it. The user picks them on the Defaults\ntab of the grid config dialog.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: Partial&lt;GridConfig&gt; = {\n    typeFormats: {\n        date: 'norwegian', // 22.11.2026\n        datetime: 'norwegianTime', // 22.11.2026 13:45\n        number: { decimals: 2, thousands: ' ', decimal: ',' } // 1 234 567,89\n    },\n    // due keeps its own: 2026-W47\n    columns: { due: { format: { date: 'weekIso' } } }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A column&#39;s own format wins</strong>: <code>date</code> or <code>number</code> in its <code>format</code>. The rest of its format stays -\na column with only a label, a template or blank / 0 shows its type&#39;s format, <code>{value}</code> of a template\nin it.</li>\n<li><strong>Not</strong> a number column shown as checkboxes, nor a lookup column: they show no number or date.</li>\n<li>It is a display format like a column&#39;s own, with all that follows: the header filter\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">as shown</a>, grouping by a week, month, quarter or year, the Excel number format.</li>\n<li>Format › of a column without its own says <em>Default</em>, its example the type&#39;s format: <em>Default</em> is\n&quot;as the grid&#39;s default&quot;. In the dialog the column&#39;s picker says <code>Default: Norwegian</code>.</li>\n<li>A type left out shows the field&#39;s formatter. <code>date</code> takes no time of day (<code>&#39;isoTime&#39;</code> ...), and a\nformat a type does not take throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li>It is state: <code>GridState.typeFormats</code>, saved with <code>getState()</code> and the views, <code>resetState()</code> goes back\nto the config&#39;s. <code>updateConfig({ typeFormats })</code> changes it. See\n<a href=\"#guide/state/display-formats-in-the-state\">Display formats in the state</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</h3>\n<p>The header filter of a column with a display format takes the text <strong>as the cells show it</strong>, and\nfilters on what it stands for - a week, a month, a minute is the whole of it. It is on by default:\n<code>filterAsShown: false</code> in the column&#39;s format turns it off, the grid config dialog has it as a checkbox.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>format</th>\n<th>typed</th>\n<th>filters on</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>weekIso</code>, <code>weekUs</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>the week: Monday 16 to Sunday 22 November (US: Sunday to Saturday, within the year)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>month</code>, <code>quarter</code>, <code>year</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11</code>, <code>2026 Q4</code>, <code>2026</code></td>\n<td>the month, the quarter, the year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>iso</code>, <code>norwegian</code>, <code>norwegianShort</code>, <code>us</code>, <code>uk</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.2026</code>, <code>11/22/2026</code></td>\n<td>the day - a whole day of a <code>datetime</code> column too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>medium</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>full</code></td>\n<td><code>Nov 22, 2026</code>, <code>22. november 2026</code></td>\n<td>the day, the names in the language of <code>format.locale</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>daysUntil</code></td>\n<td><code>5</code></td>\n<td>the day in 5 days: <code>≤ 5</code> is up to then - overdue too, <code>≥ 0</code> from today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>isoTime</code>, <code>norwegianTime</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-22 13:45</code></td>\n<td>the minute, 13:45:00 to 13:45:59</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>isoSeconds</code>, <code>norwegianSeconds</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-22 13:45:12</code></td>\n<td>the second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a number format</td>\n<td><code>1 234,5</code>, <code>50 %</code></td>\n<td>the number read with the format&#39;s separators: 1234.5; a percent is a hundredth, 0.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>weekday</code>, <code>time</code>, <code>timeSeconds</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>not filtered as shown: a Sunday and 13:45 are of any week, any day - typed as the field&#39;s formatter reads it (<code>2026-11-22</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The operator works on the whole period, and the filter is made of ordinary date conditions:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>typed</th>\n<th>the filter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>= 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td><code>BETWEEN</code> 2026-11-16 and 2026-11-22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≥ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>from Monday, <code>≥ 2026-11-16</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&gt; 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>after Sunday, <code>&gt; 2026-11-22</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt; 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>before Monday, <code>&lt; 2026-11-16</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≤ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>to Sunday, <code>≤ 2026-11-22</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>an OR group: <code>&lt; 2026-11-16</code> or <code>&gt; 2026-11-22</code> or blank - as <code>≠</code> a day, a blank date is not in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>2026-W40..2026-W47</code> (Between)</td>\n<td>Monday of week 40 to Sunday of week 47 - an end left out is open</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The end of a period is its last day for a <code>date</code> column, its last second (23:59:59) for a <code>datetime</code>\ncolumn. A period of one day on a <code>date</code> column, one second on a <code>datetime</code> column, is the condition as\nwithout a format: <code>EQUAL 2026-11-22</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No date functions in SQL.</strong> The filter is plain conditions on dates, so\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/a-week-typed-in-the-grid\"><code>toSqlWhere()</code></a> makes a range of the column of them - an index on\nit works, in every dialect - and so does a backend of your own.</li>\n<li><strong>The cell keeps what was typed.</strong> The state has the value as before: a date, the start of the period\ntyped - <code>2026-W47</code> is stored as Monday 2026-11-16 00:00, a BETWEEN as the starts of its two ends - and\nthe cell shows it in the format again, <code>2026-W47</code>, after a render and after a saved state. See\n<a href=\"#guide/state/header-filters-in-the-state\">Header filters in the state</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The text in full</strong>, as shown: <code>2026-W4</code> is week 4, <code>2026-W</code> is no week and no filter, a column of\nminutes takes the minute. A leading 0 can be left out (<code>22.1.2026</code>), case does not matter\n(<code>2026-w47</code>, <code>2026 q4</code>), a week and a quarter are written as the texts <code>format.weekIso</code>,\n<code>format.weekUs</code> and <code>format.quarter</code> have them (<code>uke 47 2026</code>). An ISO date is read too: <code>2026-11-18</code>\ntyped in a column of weeks is its week.</li>\n<li><strong>The placeholder</strong> of the empty header filter is an example, today in the format: <code>2026-W47</code>,\n<code>1 234,5</code>; <em>Days from today</em> for <code>daysUntil</code> (the text <code>filter.placeholder.daysUntil</code>). A column&#39;s own\n<code>placeholderFilter</code> wins.</li>\n<li><strong>The date pickers</strong> write the format: a day picked in a column of weeks is <code>2026-W47</code>, the range\npicker of Between <code>2026-W40..2026-W47</code>, and it opens from Monday of the first week to Sunday of the\nlast. The range block of the filter menu reads and writes it too.</li>\n<li><strong>The format changed</strong> by the user - Format › in the header menu, the grid config dialog - runs a\nheader filter with a value again, as the cell shows it then: <code>2026-W47</code> switched to <em>Month</em> is\n<code>2026-11</code>, November; to <em>Default</em> <code>2026-11-16</code>, that day. <code>setState({ formats })</code> from code leaves the\nfilter as it is.</li>\n<li>The footer&#39;s chips, the filter editor and <code>getFilterString()</code> show the conditions: the dates.</li>\n<li>A number format reads the number only, the operators are as without it: <code>≥ 1 234,5</code> is <code>≥ 1234.5</code>.\nThe cell shows the number with the format&#39;s separators and the decimals it has: <code>12.34</code> typed in a\ncolumn of one decimal stays <code>12.34</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        due: { format: { date: 'weekIso' }, operator: 'EQUAL' }, // `2026-W47` is the week\n        born: { format: { date: 'norwegian', filterAsShown: false } }, // typed as YYYY-MM-DD\n        balance: { format: { number: { thousands: ' ', decimal: ',' } } } // `1 234,5`\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['due'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['born'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['balance'], width: 120 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-templates-and-links\">Templates and links</h2>\n<p>A column has two more parts in its format: <code>text</code>, a template of what its cells show - a text, number,\ndate or datetime column - and <code>link</code>, which makes its cells links - a text or number column. Both are\ntemplates: text with <code>{field}</code> placeholders, each filled in with the value of a field of the row.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // Ann Lee: the text of two fields\n        firstname: { format: { text: '{firstname} {lastname}' } },\n        // 1 234,5 kg: the cell's own value, {value}, in the column's number format, and text\n        weight: { format: { number: { decimals: 1, thousands: ' ', decimal: ',' }, text: '{value} kg' } },\n        // a link from an order number: order 1042, to https://shop.example.com/order/1042\n        order: { format: { text: 'order {value}', link: { url: 'https://shop.example.com/order/{value}' } } },\n        // due 2026-W47: the column's date format in the template\n        due: { format: { date: 'weekIso', text: 'due {value}' } },\n        // a link to the customer, its text the company and the id: Acme Corp (12)\n        company: {\n            format: { text: '{company} ({id})', link: { url: 'https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}', newTab: true } }\n        },\n        // a link that writes an email, one that calls, one to the address the field holds\n        email: { format: { link: { url: 'mailto:{email}' } } },\n        phone: { format: { link: { url: 'tel:{phone}' } } },\n        website: { format: { link: { url: '{website}', newTab: true } } }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['firstname'], width: 140 },\n            { fields: ['weight'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['order'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['due'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 160 },\n            { fields: ['email'], width: 180 },\n            { fields: ['phone'], width: 120 },\n            { fields: ['website'], width: 180 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"full-grid\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-placeholders\">The placeholders</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>{field}</code></strong> is the value of a field of the row, as text: as the field&#39;s formatter writes it -\n<code>2026-11-22</code> for a date, <code>1234.5</code> for a number - not in a display format, and the value of a lookup\nfield, not its label. The field does not have to be a column: <code>{id}</code> can be a field the grid does not\nshow. A field the row does not have is empty.</li>\n<li><strong><code>{value}</code></strong> is the cell&#39;s own value: a template that fits any column. In a number or date column\nwith a display format (<code>number</code>, <code>date</code>) it is written in that format - <code>1 234,5</code>, <code>2026-W47</code> -\nexcept in the address of a link, where it is the field&#39;s formatter&#39;s (<code>1042</code>). An edited cell shows\nthe value, not the template. A field named <code>value</code> can not\nbe reached from another column.</li>\n<li><strong>A brace that is not a placeholder is text</strong>: <code>{}</code>, <code>{a</code> and <code>}</code> are shown as they are. A name is\neverything between the braces, spaces too: <code>{ id }</code> is the field <code>id</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A name that starts with <code>__</code></strong> is the grid&#39;s own (<code>{__rowState}</code>): a template with one throws a\n<code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li><code>text</code> is not <code>&#39;&#39;</code>: leave it out for the cell&#39;s own text.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-templates-not-code\">Templates, not code</h3>\n<p>The formats are part of the state, and a saved state can come from storage, a backend or another user.\nSo a template runs nothing: there are no expressions, no <code>eval</code>, no <code>new Function</code>, no html. A value is\nalways text - <code>&lt;img src=x onerror=...&gt;</code> in a field is shown as those characters - and the text of a\ncell and of a link is put in the page as text, never as html.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-address-of-a-link\">The address of a link</h3>\n<p><code>link.url</code> is where the cells link to. The grid builds it for each row, and checks it:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>url</code></th>\n<th>Acme Corp, id 12, <a href=\"mailto:ann@example.com\">ann@example.com</a>, +47 123 45 678</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>https://crm.example.com/customer/12</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;https://duckduckgo.com/?q={company}&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>https://duckduckgo.com/?q=Acme%20Corp</code></td>\n<td>the values are encoded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;mailto:{email}&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>mailto:ann@example.com</code></td>\n<td>the <code>@</code> of a mailto: stays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;tel:{phone}&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>tel:+47%20123%2045%20678</code></td>\n<td>the <code>+</code> of a tel: stays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;{website}&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the address the field holds, <code>https://acme.example.com</code></td>\n<td>one placeholder alone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It starts with its scheme, written out</strong>: <code>https://</code>, <code>http://</code>, <code>mailto:</code> or <code>tel:</code>. Or it is one\nplaceholder alone, a field that holds the whole address. Anything else throws a <code>TypeError</code> when the\ngrid is made or <code>setState()</code> / <code>updateConfig()</code> is given it: <code>javascript:...</code>, <code>data:...</code>, <code>ftp:...</code>,\na relative <code>/customer/{id}</code>, a scheme from a value <code>{scheme}://...</code>, <code>{base}/customer/{id}</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The values are encoded</strong> with <code>encodeURIComponent</code>: a value can not end the path, add a query or a\nheader, or change the host - <code>a/b?c</code> is <code>a%2Fb%3Fc</code>. In a <code>mailto:</code> and a <code>tel:</code> address the <code>@</code> and\nthe <code>+</code> stay as they are: they are part of the address and add nothing (a header or a parameter needs\n<code>?</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>=</code> or <code>;</code>, which are encoded).</li>\n<li><strong>A whole address from a field</strong> is taken as it is, its spaces at the ends trimmed.</li>\n<li><strong>Then it is checked</strong> with <code>new URL()</code>: it is an absolute address of one of the four schemes - after\nthe browser&#39;s own clean up (tabs and line breaks taken out, the scheme in lower case: <code>java script:</code>\nis <code>javascript:</code>). The address linked to is the one <code>new URL()</code> gives, so what was checked is what the\nbrowser opens; an international host is written as punycode, a lookalike <code>exаmple.com</code> (with a\nCyrillic <code>а</code>) shows as <code>xn--exmple-4nf.com</code> where the browser shows the address.</li>\n<li><strong>A row whose address is not one of those shows its text, no link</strong>: a field of the address that is\nempty in the row, a value <code>.</code> or <code>..</code> (the browser would step up the path), a field whose whole\naddress is <code>javascript:alert(1)</code>, <code>data:...</code> or relative, text that is no address.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A field that holds a whole address can link anywhere on the web. When the data is not yours to trust,\nwrite the site in the template - <code>https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}</code> - and the field only fills in\na part of it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-link-cell\">A link cell</h3>\n<p>The cell shows an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> with the cell&#39;s text (its template&#39;s, with <code>text</code>), underlined in\n<code>--simple-html-grid-link-color</code>. In a new tab with <code>newTab: true</code> (<code>target=&quot;_blank&quot;</code>,\n<code>rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;</code>), else in the page.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>what</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a click on the text</td>\n<td>opens the link, and makes the row the current row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl/Cmd+click, a middle click on the text</td>\n<td>the browser&#39;s: opens it in a new tab - no range of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+click on the text</td>\n<td>a range of cells, as on any cell: does not open it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+click, the second click of a double click</td>\n<td>does not open it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a click beside the text</td>\n<td>the cell, as any cell: the focus, the current row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Enter on the cell</td>\n<td>opens the link, as a click on it (Google Sheets&#39; key)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter, F2, a character typed, a double click beside the text</td>\n<td>edit the cell&#39;s value - not the text of the template - in the grid&#39;s editor of one line over the cell, Enter saves, Esc cancels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a right click</td>\n<td>the row menu</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The cell is a custom cell the grid draws itself (see <a href=\"#guide/custom-cells\">Custom cells</a>): it has the\nfocus - the link in it is out of the tab order and a press on its text focuses the cell - so the\narrows, Tab, the ranges, copy, paste, Delete, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+Z and Find work on it as on any cell. In a\nreadonly grid the cells are links too, and nothing opens the editor. A screen reader finds the link in\nthe cell, its name its text; the cell has <code>aria-keyshortcuts=&quot;Alt+Enter&quot;</code>.</p>\n<p>A column with a <code>renderer</code> of its own draws its cells: it gets the template&#39;s text as <code>text</code>, the\ngrid draws no link. The default cell&#39;s <code>focusButton</code> and <code>placeholderRow</code> are not on a link cell.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-what-a-template-and-a-link-change\">What a template and a link change</h3>\n<p>As a display format: only what the cells show, and what follows the cells.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>where</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>the cells</td>\n<td>the template&#39;s text; the link with its address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a cell being edited</td>\n<td>the value, as the field&#39;s formatter writes it: a cell shown as <code>Acme Corp (12)</code> is edited as <code>Acme Corp</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Find, the fitted width, the dimmed repeated values</td>\n<td>the text the cells show: Find finds <code>(12)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the edited marker&#39;s tooltip, &quot;Original value&quot;</td>\n<td>the template with the original value as <code>{value}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a renderer of your own</td>\n<td><code>text</code> is the template&#39;s text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>copy, paste, the CSV export, the Excel export</td>\n<td>the value - no template, no link: a paste reads it back. Excel gets text, not a <code>HYPERLINK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the filters - the header filter, the filter menu&#39;s value list, the filter editor, IN</td>\n<td><strong>the text shown</strong> on a <code>text</code> field: <code>= Ann Berg</code>, <em>contains</em> <code>(12)</code> - what you see. <code>filterAsShown: false</code> in the format (<em>Filter as shown</em> in the grid config dialog) filters the value instead. A backend compares the same text: <a href=\"#guide/sql/a-column-filtered-on-its-template\"><code>getShownFormulas()</code></a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sorting</td>\n<td><strong>the text shown</strong> on a <code>text</code> field - <code>{last}, {first}</code> sorts by the last name - as it is filtered; the value of a column grouped by</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>grouping</td>\n<td>the value: a group is a run of one value</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A template is for a <code>text</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code> field, a link for a <code>text</code> or <code>number</code>\nfield, neither for a lookup column (<code>editor</code>): a format with them on another type throws a\n<code>TypeError</code>. The filters and the sort compare the text shown on a <code>text</code> field only - a number or a\ndate is filtered and sorted as its value. A cell of another type in a <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code> is\nshown as its type, without them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-grid-config-dialog\">The grid config dialog</h2>\n<p>The gear at the right end of the footer opens it - and <em>Column settings…</em> in the header menu, on that\ncolumn, and <code>gridInterface.openColumnSettings(field)</code> from a button of your own. <code>settingsButton: false</code>\nhides the gear (<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/grid-options\">Grid config</a>).</p>\n<p>Its title is <em>Grid config</em>. Under the title bar are three tabs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Columns</strong>, below: the settings of each column. <code>openColumnSettings()</code> opens the dialog on it.</li>\n<li><strong>Detail form</strong>: the detail form at the right of the rows - <em>Show the detail form</em>, <em>Expanded</em>, its\nwidth with its <em>Min width</em> and <em>Max width</em> (empty: none; the one typed keeps its value, the others\nfollow it), and the fields it shows in their order - each with a drag handle at its right that drags\nit to another place, a line showing where. <code>openDetailFormSettings()</code> opens the dialog on it. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">The detail form</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Defaults</strong>: the settings of the grid, not of a column.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Small screens.</strong> The dialog fits a laptop of 1366x768 or 1280x720 at 150 % zoom, and a phone: it opens\nwithin 80 % of the window&#39;s height and width (a phone: its whole width), what does not fit scrolls inside\nit - also when more opens in it - and its buttons stay at the bottom. Resized by its edges or corners, it\ncan be as large as the window. The grid&#39;s other dialogs (the filter editor, the import, the keyboard\nshortcuts) open within 80 % too. Side by side, the list of the columns stays in\nview while the settings scroll. Narrower than some 520px (a phone), the list and a column&#39;s settings take\nturns: a column clicked (or Enter in the list) shows its settings, <strong>‹ Columns</strong> the list again.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Formats by type</em>: a picker of the format of every date, date and time, and number column without a\nformat of its own - the entries of Format ›, <em>Default</em> the field&#39;s formatter (<code>GridState.typeFormats</code>,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/formatting/formats-by-type\">Formats by type</a>). The Columns tab shows it at once: a column&#39;s picker says\n<code>Default: Norwegian</code>, its preview shows it.</li>\n<li><em>Show the views button</em>: the button at the left end of the footer that saves, switches, renames and\ndeletes the views (<code>viewsButton</code>, set with <code>updateConfig()</code> on Apply - <code>exportConfig()</code> keeps it).</li>\n<li><em>Default view</em>: the view the grid opens with - <em>Standard</em> (the config&#39;s state) or one of the views,\nas <em>Default view</em> in the views menu (<a href=\"#guide/state/views\">Views</a>). Greyed out without views.</li>\n<li><em>Reset formats</em> in the footer goes back to the formats by type the grid starts with.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The tabs are a tab list: Tab comes to the tab shown, ArrowLeft / ArrowRight (Home / End) show another.\nThe footer is the dialog&#39;s: <em>Apply</em> applies every tab, <em>Cancel</em> drops what was changed on all of them. Its\nleft end has the resets of the tab shown.</p>\n<p>The Columns tab has the columns at the left and the settings of the one picked at the right - those\nin tabs of their own, under the column&#39;s name:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>tab</th>\n<th>the settings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>General</strong></td>\n<td>Label - or a calculated column&#39;s field name, label and formula - Multiline, Read only, Placeholder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Display</strong></td>\n<td>what the cells show: the format, Filter as shown, blank / 0, Text shown, Show as link, Show as checkbox. Not for a checkbox or a lookup column: General says they have no format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Header filter</strong></td>\n<td>Filter operator, Filter as you type. Not for a checkbox column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Colours</strong></td>\n<td>Colour rules; for a number column four panels, one open at a time - <em>Colour rules</em>, <em>Data bar</em>, <em>Colour scale</em>, <em>Icons</em> - each a line saying what it has (<em>2 rules</em>, <em>On</em>, <em>Off</em>, a red square for a problem in it), a click opens it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A tab with a problem in it has a small red square after its name (<em>Error</em> for a screen reader): <em>Apply</em>\nwaits for it. The tab picked stays when another column is picked - General for a column without it. A\ncalculated column waiting for <em>Apply</em> has its own settings only, no tabs. The preview is under every\ntab. The tabs are a tab list too: Tab comes to the tab shown, ArrowLeft / ArrowRight (Home / End) show\nanother.</p>\n<p>The settings, from the top:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The columns</strong> of the layout are at the left, in their order, each with its label, its type and\nwhat it has set - <em>Calculated</em>, <em>Format</em>, <em>Text</em>, <em>Link</em>, <em>Checkbox</em>, <em>Blank / 0</em>, <em>Label</em>, <em>Placeholder</em>,\n<em>Multiline</em>, <em>Read only</em>, <em>Colours</em>, <em>Bar</em>, <em>Scale</em>, <em>Icons</em>, or <em>Error</em>. The arrows, Home, End, PageUp / PageDown and a letter pick one, a\nclick too.</li>\n<li><strong>Add calculated column</strong>, under the list: a column whose value a formula computes from the row. A\ncalculated column has its field name, label and formula at the top of its settings, the type it gives\nand its value in the row of the preview, its problems at once, and <em>Remove calculated column</em>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns/in-the-grid-config-dialog\">Calculated columns</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Label</strong>, every column: the column&#39;s label - empty is the column&#39;s own, which the input shows. The ✎ of\nthe column chooser sets it too (<a href=\"#guide/menus/the-column-chooser\">The column chooser</a>). It\nis the label wherever the grid names the column, the exports&#39; headers too.</li>\n<li><strong>A date, datetime or number column</strong> has its format: a picker that opens the entries of Format ›,\nthe same list as the header menu. Under it, with a format, <em>Filter as shown</em> (on): the header filter\ntakes the text as the cells show it, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a> - off, the format has\n<code>filterAsShown: false</code>. It is off and greyed out for the day of the week and a time of day, the hint\nunder it says why. A number column has <em>Show blank as 0</em> and <em>Show 0 as blank</em> too,\none or the other: they start as the column shows them, its <code>numberOverride</code> too, and turning the\ncolumn&#39;s own off is kept as <code>&#39;NONE&#39;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A text column</strong> has <em>Show as link</em>, and under it while it is on <em>Text shown</em> (the link&#39;s text, a\ntemplate - empty for the cell&#39;s own text, with <em>Filter as shown</em>), <em>Address</em> and <em>Open in a new tab</em>.\nTurned off, the link&#39;s text goes with it. A number column has them too, under its format. A template\na column has without a link - the config&#39;s (<code>text: &#39;{value} kg&#39;</code>), a date column&#39;s - is shown there\ntoo, to be seen and emptied; the dialog makes no new one without a link. The <code>{ }</code> button beside a template opens a menu of the fields -\nthe columns&#39; and those of the row, by their labels, with a search - and puts the one picked in at the\ncaret: no need to know the names of the fields.</li>\n<li><strong>Show as checkbox</strong>, a text or number column: its cells are checkboxes - for a flag of <code>1</code> / <code>0</code>.\nThe <em>Checked value</em> (<code>1</code>) and the <em>Unchecked value</em> (<code>0</code>, empty empties the cell) are under it. A\ncheckbox shows no text: while it is on, the template and the link of a text column, the format and\nblank / 0 of a number column are gone from the column and from the dialog. A checked value that is\nempty, the same two values, a value of a number column that is no number are problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Multiline</strong>, a text column that is not shown as checkboxes: its cells are text of several lines -\nthe first line shown, the text editor to edit it (Enter a new line), taller rows with <code>fitRows()</code>. It\nstarts as the field has it (<code>multiline</code> of its field); off, only a value with a line break is shown\nas several lines, as always.</li>\n<li><strong>Read only</strong>, every column: its cells can not be edited, also in an editable grid - or, unchecked, a\ncolumn that is <code>readonly</code> in the config can. It starts as the column has it. The <code>isReadonly</code> hook\nstill has the last word.</li>\n<li><strong>Placeholder</strong>, for every column but a checkbox: the placeholder of the column&#39;s empty cells, the\ntext an empty cell shows greyed - empty is the column&#39;s own (<code>placeholderRow</code>), which the input shows -\nor <em>Use the column label</em>: the label is the placeholder, the input is off. As the column&#39;s own it is\nshown on the current row only, unless <code>placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly: false</code>. The header filter\nkeeps its own placeholder (<code>placeholderFilter</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Filter operator</strong>, for every column but a checkbox: the operator of the column&#39;s header filter, a\npicker of the operators it offers - the same as Filter › Operator › - with <em>Default:</em> and the operator\nthe column starts with under it (its <code>operator</code>, else the type&#39;s: <code>≥</code> for a number or date, <code>=</code> for a\ntext). A column with one operator (<code>filterOperators</code>) shows it, there is nothing to pick.</li>\n<li><strong>Filter as you type</strong>, after the operator, every column but a checkbox: the header filter filters\nafter a short pause in typing, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-you-type\">Filter as you type</a>. It starts\nas the column has it (its <code>filterOnType</code>, the grid&#39;s, or the user&#39;s from the operator choice).</li>\n<li><strong>Colour rules</strong>, every column: the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">colour rules</a> of its cells, in their order - the\nfirst that matches wins. <strong>One rule is open</strong>, the others are a line each - a sample in their colours\n(<em>Abc</em>), what they match (<code>Greater than 100</code>, a formula as written) and their buttons: a click on the\nline opens it, <em>▾</em> closes it. The first rule is open (the first with a problem), a rule added is open,\nremoving the open one opens the next. So a column of rules fits a small screen. Each rule is a group.\nIts first line: the kind of its condition - <em>Value</em>,\n<em>Conditions</em> or <em>Formula</em>, radio buttons - and <em>Move up</em>, <em>Move down</em> and <em>Remove rule</em> at its right.\n<em>Value</em>: its operator (a picker of the operators the column&#39;s filter offers, not In / Not In) and its\nvalue as it is typed in the header filter - <em>From</em> and <em>To</em> for Between, none for Is Blank, a checkbox\n<em>Checked</em> for a checkbox column. <em>Conditions</em>: a line for each - its field (a picker of the columns and\nthe calculated columns, with a search), its operator and value as for that field, a ✕ - <em>Add\ncondition</em> under them, and over two or more <em>All of them</em> / <em>Any of them</em>. <em>Formula</em>: a formula that\nis true or false, with the <code>{ }</code> button and the <strong>ƒ</strong> button (a menu of the functions, see\n<a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns/the-functions\">Calculated columns</a>), and under it whether it is true in the\nrow of the preview.\nThen <em>Text colour</em> and <em>Background</em>, a row of square swatches each (<em>None</em> and the palette; one stop\nof Tab, the arrows pick, each named by its colour for a screen reader and in its tooltip) with the\ncolour picker of a custom colour after them (a <code>+</code> until one is picked), and <em>Bold</em>. <em>Add rule</em> under\nthe rules (red text on a red background, the column&#39;s header filter operator; the focus goes to its\noperator). A rule without its value, without a condition, with a condition on a field the grid does\nnot know, with a formula that has a problem or is not true / false, or with nothing to colour, is a\nproblem; a value the column does not read (a date of another format) and a custom text colour under\n4.5:1 are said, they hold nothing back.</li>\n<li><strong>Data bars, scales, icons</strong>, a number column not shown as checkboxes: the Colours tab is four panels,\none open at a time - <em>Colour rules</em>, <em>Data bar</em>, <em>Colour scale</em>, <em>Icons</em> - each a line saying what it has\n(<em>2 rules</em>, <em>On</em>, <em>Off</em>, a red square for a problem in it). In the panel of <em>Data bar</em>, <em>Colour scale</em> or\n<em>Icons</em> a checkbox of its name turns it on, its settings under it: the colour swatches (of the palette&#39;s\nbackgrounds, a custom colour), <em>Min</em>, <em>Midpoint</em> and <em>Max</em> (empty: <em>Automatic</em>, from the rows), the\n<em>Icon set</em> (radio buttons that show its icons), <em>Middle from</em> and <em>High from</em>, <em>Thresholds</em> in <em>% of\nthe range</em> or <em>Values</em>, where the <em>Icon</em> goes and <em>Reverse</em>. Under them five cells from the lowest\nvalue of the rows to the highest, drawn as the column will be. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting/data-bars\">Data bars</a> and\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/bars-scales-icons-and-the-colour-rules\">Bars, scales, icons and the colour rules</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The preview</strong> shows what the cell shows in the current row (else the first row shown), in the\ncolours of the rule its value matches - with its bar, scale colour and icon: the text, the link - a click opens it in a new tab - and its\naddress, and why the row has no link (a field that is empty in it, an address that is not allowed, a\nfield that does not exist). A checkbox and a lookup column have one when they have rules.</li>\n<li><strong>A problem of a template</strong> is shown under it at once: no address, one that does not start with its\nscheme, <code>javascript:</code> and the other schemes that are not allowed, a field of the grid&#39;s own - and so\nis a problem of the checkbox values, of a colour rule and of the numbers of a bar, a scale or the icons. The column is marked <em>Error</em>, and Apply\ndoes nothing until it is fixed.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply</strong> sets the formats of the state, <code>setState({ formats })</code>: <code>getState()</code> has them - to save\nthem, see <a href=\"#guide/state/saving-it\">Saving it</a>. A filter operator that changed is switched as Operator ›\ndoes it: the header filter&#39;s value is filtered on again with it, the state has it\n(<code>headerFilters[field].operator</code>) and the grid sends <code>filter-operator-change</code>. A header filter whose\ncolumn reads its text in another way now (another format filtered as shown, <em>Filter as shown</em> turned\non or off) runs again, as the cell shows it. <em>Filter as you type</em> is state too\n(<code>headerFilters[field].filterOnType</code>). <em>Apply &amp; close</em> applies and closes, <em>Cancel</em> (and the ✕, Esc)\ncloses without applying. <em>Reset column</em> and <em>Reset all</em> go back to the formats the grid starts with -\n<code>ColumnConfig.format</code>, or <code>initialState.formats</code> - and to the column&#39;s default operator and filter as\nyou type, in the dialog, until Apply.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is a modal dialog of the filter editor&#39;s kind: the focus goes into it when it opens and back to\nwhere it was when it closes, it is moved by its title bar and resized from every edge and corner. The\ntexts are <code>columnSettings.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/grid-config-dialog\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<p>A developer sets the formats the grid starts with in <code>ColumnConfig.format</code>; what the user sets in the\ndialog is the state. To keep the user&#39;s settings, save <code>getState()</code> (or its <code>formats</code>) and give it back\nwith <code>setState()</code> the next time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-label-placeholder-blank--0-checkboxes-multiline-read-only\">Label, placeholder, blank / 0, checkboxes, multiline, read only</h2>\n<p>The rest of what the grid config dialog sets is in the column&#39;s format too - so the state keeps it\nwith the formats, and a developer can start a column with it in <code>ColumnConfig.format</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // the header, and wherever the grid names the column: Firm\n        company: { label: 'Company', format: { label: 'Firm', placeholderRowLabel: true } },\n        // the placeholder of an empty cell\n        city: { format: { placeholderRow: 'Name of the city' } },\n        // a number column: a blank cell shows 0\n        discount: { format: { numberOverride: 'BLANK_TO_ZERO' } },\n        // '1' / '0' (text) or 1 / 0 (a number field) as checkboxes\n        newsletter: { format: { checkbox: { checked: '1', unchecked: '0' } } }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['city'], width: 120 },\n            { fields: ['discount'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['newsletter'], width: 90 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part</th>\n<th>for</th>\n<th>wins over</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>label</code></td>\n<td>any column: the label wherever the grid names the column - the header, the menus, the column chooser, the filter editor, the footer&#39;s filter, copy with headers, the headers of the CSV and Excel exports</td>\n<td><code>ColumnConfig.label</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderRow</code></td>\n<td>any column but a <code>boolean</code> one: the placeholder of its empty cells</td>\n<td><code>ColumnConfig.placeholderRow</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderRowLabel</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code>: the placeholder of the empty cells is the column&#39;s label - not with <code>placeholderRow</code></td>\n<td>the same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>numberOverride</code></td>\n<td>a <code>number</code> column: <code>&#39;BLANK_TO_ZERO&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;ZERO_TO_BLANK&#39;</code>, or <code>&#39;NONE&#39;</code> - neither</td>\n<td><code>ColumnConfig.numberOverride</code>: <code>&#39;NONE&#39;</code> turns it off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>checkbox</code></td>\n<td>a <code>text</code> or <code>number</code> column: its cells are checkboxes, see below</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>multiline</code></td>\n<td>a <code>text</code> column: <code>true</code> text of several lines, <code>false</code> one line (a value with a line break is shown as several lines still) - not with <code>checkbox</code></td>\n<td>the field&#39;s <code>multiline</code> (<code>FieldConfig</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>readonly</code></td>\n<td>any column: <code>true</code> its cells are not edited, also in an editable grid; <code>false</code> they are</td>\n<td><code>ColumnConfig.readonly</code> - the <code>isReadonly</code> hook gets this and has the last word</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Each is checked as the rest of a format: an empty label or placeholder, a placeholder of a checkbox\ncolumn, <code>numberOverride</code> on another type, checkboxes on a date column throw a <code>TypeError</code> when the grid\nis made, or <code>updateConfig()</code> or <code>setState()</code> is given it. A lookup column (<code>editor</code>) takes a label,\na placeholder and <code>readonly</code>, not the rest.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-checkboxes\">Checkboxes</h3>\n<p><code>checkbox: { checked?, unchecked? }</code> makes each cell of a text or number column a checkbox: for a flag\na database keeps as <code>1</code> / <code>0</code>, <code>&#39;Y&#39;</code> / <code>&#39;N&#39;</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A cell is <strong>checked</strong> when its value is the checked value (<code>&#39;1&#39;</code> left out): its text trimmed, <code>&#39; 1 &#39;</code>\ntoo, or a number equal to it - <code>1</code> in a number field. Anything else is unchecked, a blank too.</li>\n<li>A click, or Space, on an editable cell <strong>writes the other value</strong>: the checked value, or the unchecked\none (<code>&#39;0&#39;</code> left out) - a number in a number field (<code>1</code>, <code>0</code>), else the text. An unchecked value <code>&#39;&#39;</code>\nempties the cell (null), as Delete does. Shift+click and Ctrl+click make ranges, as on a boolean\ncell; a readonly grid or cell and <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code> write nothing. Undo takes it back.</li>\n<li><strong>The header filter is a checkbox</strong>, as a boolean column&#39;s: a click checks it - the rows whose value is\nthe checked value, as it is (<code>&#39;Y&#39;</code>, not <code>&#39;y&#39;</code> or <code>&#39; Y &#39;</code>; a number equal to it) - another unchecks it -\nevery other row, a blank too - and a third clears it. Plain conditions, <code>flag = &#39;Y&#39;</code> and\n<code>flag &lt;&gt; &#39;Y&#39;</code>: a backend&#39;s <code>toSqlWhere()</code> selects the same rows.</li>\n<li><strong>The value stays the value</strong>: copy, paste, the CSV and Excel exports, sorting, grouping and the filter\nmenu&#39;s value list have <code>1</code> and <code>0</code>. Find does not look in a checkbox, <code>fitColumns()</code> gives it a\ncheckbox&#39;s room.</li>\n<li>A checkbox shows no text: not with <code>text</code>, <code>link</code>, <code>number</code> or <code>numberOverride</code> (a <code>TypeError</code>), and a\nnumber column with checkboxes has no Format ›. A renderer of your own (<code>renderer</code>) wins, and a cell of\nanother type in a <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code> is shown as its type.</li>\n<li>The checked value is not <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, the two are not the same, and a number field takes numbers - <code>&#39;1&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;-1&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for the unchecked one: else a <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-colour-rules\">Colour rules</h2>\n<p>A column&#39;s colour rules colour a cell from its row - its text, its background, bold: a negative\nbalance in red, the orders that wait on an orange background, the name of a customer who is late and\nowes. No code: a rule is a condition and a style from a palette (or a custom colour). The condition is\none of three kinds:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Value</strong> (<code>operator</code>, <code>value</code>): the cell&#39;s own value, as the column&#39;s header filter tests it - below.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditions</strong> (<code>conditions</code>, <code>combine</code>): conditions on fields of the row - the column&#39;s own, others, a\n<a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns\">calculated column</a> - each tested as the header filter of its field tests\nit; all of them (<code>combine: &#39;AND&#39;</code>, the default) or any (<code>&#39;OR&#39;</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Formula</strong> (<code>expression</code>): a <a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns/the-formulas\">formula</a> that is true or false -\n<code>{balance} &lt; 0 AND {status} = &quot;late&quot;</code>, <code>DAYS({due}, TODAY()) &gt; 7</code>. True colours the cell; a row where\nit can not be computed is not coloured. Two columns compared: <code>{shipped} != {ordered}</code>; a part of a text:\n<code>LEFT({account}, 2) = &quot;NO&quot;</code>, <code>CONTAINS({note}, &quot;urgent&quot;)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The user makes them in the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">grid config dialog</a> (<em>Colour rules</em>), a developer\nstarts a column with them in <code>ColumnConfig.format</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // red text below 0\n        balance: { format: { rules: [{ operator: 'LESS_THAN', value: '0', color: 'red' }] } },\n        // the first rule that matches wins\n        status: {\n            format: {\n                rules: [\n                    { operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'waiting', background: 'orange' },\n                    { operator: 'CONTAINS', value: 'late', color: 'red', bold: true }\n                ]\n            }\n        },\n        // both ends included, an end '' is open\n        due: { format: { rules: [{ operator: 'BETWEEN', value: ['2026-11-01', '2026-11-30'], background: 'yellow' }] } },\n        // empty - or 0, as in the filter\n        notes: { format: { rules: [{ operator: 'IS_BLANK', background: 'grey' }] } },\n        // conditions on other fields: late AND owing; a formula with a custom colour\n        name: {\n            format: {\n                rules: [\n                    {\n                        conditions: [\n                            { field: 'status', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'late' },\n                            { field: 'balance', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: '0' }\n                        ],\n                        background: 'red',\n                        bold: true\n                    },\n                    { expression: 'DAYS({due}, TODAY()) &gt; 30', background: '#c4b5fd' }\n                ]\n            }\n        }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['balance'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['status'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['due'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['notes'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part of a rule</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>operator</code></td>\n<td>how the value is compared: an operator of the column&#39;s type - <code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code>, <code>LESS_THAN</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code> ... (<a href=\"#guide/filtering/operators\">Operators</a>). Not <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code>: a rule compares one value, give each value a rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>value</code></td>\n<td>what it is compared with, text as it is typed in the header filter: <code>&#39;100&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;late&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;2026-11-22&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;true&#39;</code> / <code>&#39;false&#39;</code> for a checkbox column. <code>[from, to]</code> for <code>BETWEEN</code>. Left out for <code>IS_BLANK</code> / <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePreset</code></td>\n<td>a date column: a quick date in place of <code>value</code> - <code>&#39;today&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;thisWeek&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;lastWeek&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;thisMonth&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;lastMonth&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;thisQuarter&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;lastQuarter&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;thisYear&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;lastYear&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;last7Days&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;last30Days&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;next7Days&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;overdue&#39;</code> - its days today. Not with <code>BETWEEN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>week</code></td>\n<td>a date column: <code>&#39;iso&#39;</code> (Monday, ISO 8601 numbers) or <code>&#39;us&#39;</code> (Sunday, week 1 has January 1) - the weeks of a <code>value</code> like <code>&#39;2026-W47&#39;</code> and of a quick date&#39;s week. Left out: the column&#39;s <code>weekIso</code> / <code>weekUs</code> format, else the date picker&#39;s <code>weekStart</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conditions</code></td>\n<td>the kind <em>Conditions</em>, in place of <code>operator</code>: 1 to 20 of <code>{ field, operator, value? }</code> - a field of the grid, an operator of its type, a value as typed in its header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>combine</code></td>\n<td>with <code>conditions</code>: <code>&#39;AND&#39;</code> (the default) all of them, <code>&#39;OR&#39;</code> one of them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>expression</code></td>\n<td>the kind <em>Formula</em>, in place of <code>operator</code>: a formula that is true or false</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>color</code></td>\n<td>the text colour: <code>&#39;red&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;orange&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;amber&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;yellow&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;lime&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;green&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;teal&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;cyan&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;blue&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;indigo&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;purple&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;pink&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;brown&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;grey&#39;</code> - or a custom colour, <code>&#39;#rrggbb&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>background</code></td>\n<td>the background, a colour of the same palette or <code>&#39;#rrggbb&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bold</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code>: bold text</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>The condition is the header filter&#39;s.</strong> The value is read as the column&#39;s header filter reads what\nis typed in it: a text as it is - <code>*</code>, <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> are wildcards, case is ignored unless the column\nhas <code>filterCaseSensitive</code> - a number or a date with the field&#39;s formatter (<code>1,5</code> with a decimal comma,\n<code>2026-11-22</code>, the day of a date), a Between&#39;s two ends each. Then the datasource&#39;s own filter tests\nit: <code>≥ 100</code> or <em>Contains</em> <code>late</code> keeps the cells the header filter would keep, and 0 is blank as there\n(<a href=\"#guide/filtering/0-is-blank\">0 is blank</a>) - a blank number is 0, <code>&lt; 1</code> colours it.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A date takes a period, as the header filter of a column shown as weeks or months does</strong> - and a\nquick date, as its filter menu offers them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { ColorRule } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst rules: ColorRule[] = [\n    // a due date this week: Sunday to Saturday\n    { operator: 'EQUAL', datePreset: 'thisWeek', week: 'us', background: 'green' },\n    // before this week's Monday\n    { operator: 'LESS_THAN', datePreset: 'thisWeek', week: 'iso', color: 'red', bold: true },\n    // ISO week 47 of 2026, Monday 16 to Sunday 22 November\n    { operator: 'EQUAL', value: '2026-W47', week: 'iso', background: 'yellow' },\n    // November, the fourth quarter, from week 40 to the end of November\n    { operator: 'EQUAL', value: '2026-11', background: 'blue' },\n    { operator: 'EQUAL', value: '2026 Q4', background: 'teal' },\n    { operator: 'BETWEEN', value: ['2026-W40', '2026-11'], background: 'purple' }\n];</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>on a period (a week, a month, a quick date ...)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>not in it, or blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN</code> / <code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>before its first day / to its last</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN</code> / <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>after its last day / from its first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td>from the first day of the one to the last of the other (typed values; an end may be a day)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A week, a month (<code>2026-11</code>), a quarter (<code>2026 Q4</code>) and a year (<code>2026</code>) are read whatever the column\nshows; a week and a quarter also as the grid&#39;s texts write them (<code>format.weekIso</code>, <code>format.quarter</code>:\n<code>uke 47 2026</code> in Norwegian) - <code>2026-W47</code> is read in every language, a saved rule means the same. A day\nis a day, as before. On a <code>datetime</code> a period ends at 23:59:59 of its last day. <strong>ISO or US weeks</strong>:\n<code>week: &#39;iso&#39;</code> - Monday to Sunday, week 1 has the year&#39;s first Thursday; <code>&#39;us&#39;</code> - Sunday to Saturday,\nweek 1 has January 1 (Excel&#39;s <code>WEEKNUM</code>). A rule without <code>week</code> takes the column&#39;s week format\n(<code>weekIso</code>, <code>weekUs</code>), else the date picker&#39;s first day (<code>datePickerConfig.weekStart</code>: Sunday is US).\n<strong>A quick date is today&#39;s</strong>: the cells are rendered with today&#39;s days; the grid renders them again on\na new day when the window gets the focus back, or the page is shown again\n(<code>refreshDatePresets()</code>). In the dialog: <em>Compare with</em> - <em>A date or period typed</em> or a quick date\n(picked after a typed value it is Equal, in it) - and <em>Weeks</em>: as the column, <em>ISO - Monday</em>,\n<em>US - Sunday</em>. A condition on a date field (<em>Conditions</em>) takes both too.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A lookup column</strong> (<code>editor</code>) compares the label its cells show, with a text&#39;s operators - as its\nheader filter does; a value no row of the lookup has matches no rule that compares. Is Blank / Is Not\nBlank look at the value.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The first rule that matches wins</strong>: the order of the list, <em>Move up</em> / <em>Move down</em> in the dialog. A\ncell no rule matches has the grid&#39;s colours.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A value the column does not read</strong> - a date of another format, <code>22.11.2026</code> in a <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>\ncolumn - matches no cell (the header filter filters nothing with it); the dialog says so.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A condition on another field</strong> (<em>Conditions</em>) is that field&#39;s header filter&#39;s: its type, its\nformatter, a lookup column&#39;s label, its Match case. A calculated column is a field like the others.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The palette is the theme&#39;s</strong>: 14 colours, each a text colour and a background, css variables with a\nlight and a dark colour, in both themes - readable: a text colour has 4.5:1 to the rows, the selected\nrows and every background of the palette, the grid&#39;s text 4.5:1 to the backgrounds. A theme changes\nthe palette, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/colour-rules\">Theming, colour rules</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A custom colour</strong> is <code>#</code> and six hex digits - nothing else (<code>&#39;red;&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;url(...)&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;#fff&#39;</code> throw a\n<code>TypeError</code>). It is one colour in light and in dark, so the grid keeps it readable: a custom\nbackground without a text colour gets black or white text, whichever reads better on it; a custom\ntext colour is the colour picked in light and lighter in dark, until it has 4.5:1 to the rows. The\ndialog warns when a custom text colour has less than 4.5:1 to the light rows or to its background.\nIt reaches the page as a css custom property, never as css text.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Where</strong>: every cell of the column - an input, a multiline cell, a link (the rule&#39;s text colour in\nplace of the link&#39;s, still underlined), a template, a custom cell (its text takes the colour; a\nrenderer that sets a colour of its own keeps it), a checkbox (its background). The values of the\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">detail form</a> and the dialog&#39;s preview have them too. The Excel\nexport writes them as the cells&#39; own colours (<a href=\"#guide/export/colours\">Excel, colours</a>); not the CSV -\ntext has no colours - nor a group row or the totals row.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>What wins over a rule</strong>: a cell with a validation error and a match of Find show as without it -\ntheir tint and the grid&#39;s text - so they stand out; a range&#39;s tint is laid over the rule&#39;s\nbackground. A selected row keeps its colour in the other cells; the rule&#39;s background is the cell&#39;s,\nover it. The focus outline, the marker of an edited cell and of more lines are on top. Bold stays.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Checked</strong> as the rest of a format: no kind of condition or two, an operator the type does not\nhave, <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code>, a rule without the value its operator needs, a <code>datePreset</code> or <code>week</code> on a\ncolumn that is no date, a <code>datePreset</code> with a value or with <code>BETWEEN</code>, a condition on a field the grid\ndoes not know, a formula with a problem or that is not true / false, one that colours nothing, a\ncolour that is neither of the palette nor <code>#rrggbb</code> throw a <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made, or\n<code>updateConfig()</code> or <code>setState()</code> is given it.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>It is state</strong> with the rest of the column&#39;s format: Apply in the dialog, <code>getState()</code>, a state\nsaved as JSON (the value is text, a date too), Reset column / Reset all to the config&#39;s.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-bars\">Data bars</h2>\n<p>A number column can draw a bar in each cell, as long as the value&#39;s place among the column&#39;s numbers -\na spreadsheet&#39;s data bar. The number stays where it is, over a light tint of the bar&#39;s colour (a dark one\nin dark mode), so it reads as without the bar.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // from the smallest number of the rows to the largest, and 0: a bar starts at 0\n        sales: { format: { bar: { color: 'green' } } },\n        // a fixed range: 50 is half a cell, a value over 100 a whole one\n        progress: { format: { bar: { color: '#0f766e', min: 0, max: 100 } } }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['sales'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['progress'], width: 100 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part of <code>bar</code></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>color</code></td>\n<td>a colour of the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">palette</a> - its background - or a custom colour <code>&#39;#rrggbb&#39;</code>, drawn as a tint of it. Left out: <code>&#39;blue&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>min</code></td>\n<td>the value of the bar&#39;s low end. Left out: the smallest value of the rows, or 0 when that is above 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>max</code></td>\n<td>the value of a whole bar. Left out: the largest value of the rows, or 0 when that is below 0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The range</strong> is the rows the filter lets through - grouped, every one of them, the rows of collapsed\ngroups too, as the <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">totals row</a> counts them. A filter changes it, so does an edit\nof the largest value: every cell on screen is drawn again. The datasource keeps the smallest and the\nlargest value (<code>datasource.aggregate(field, &#39;min&#39;)</code>) until a value of the field changes or other rows\nare filtered: 100 000 rows are read once, not for every cell.</li>\n<li><strong>0 is in the range</strong> when it is left out: a bar starts at 0. With negative values the axis is where 0\nis, a negative value&#39;s bar goes to the left of it, a positive one&#39;s to the right. A fixed range without\n0 (<code>min: 900</code>) starts at its low end. A value out of a fixed range is drawn as its end.</li>\n<li><strong>No bar</strong> for a cell without a number, a group row and the totals row.</li>\n<li><strong>A calculated column</strong> of numbers can have one too (<a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Square</strong>, as everything of the grid: no rounded corners.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-colour-scales\">Colour scales</h2>\n<p>A colour scale gives the cell&#39;s background a colour by the value&#39;s place from the smallest number to the\nlargest: two colours mixed (<code>minColor</code>, <code>maxColor</code>), or three with a midpoint (<code>midColor</code>) - red, yellow,\ngreen as a spreadsheet&#39;s heat map.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // red for the smallest number of the rows, yellow halfway, green for the largest\n        margin: { format: { scale: { minColor: 'red', midColor: 'yellow', maxColor: 'green' } } },\n        // a fixed range and the midpoint at 80\n        load: { format: { scale: { minColor: 'grey', midColor: 'amber', maxColor: 'red', min: 0, mid: 80, max: 100 } } }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['margin'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['load'], width: 100 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part of <code>scale</code></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>minColor</code></td>\n<td>the colour of the smallest value: a colour of the palette - its background - or <code>&#39;#rrggbb&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>midColor</code></td>\n<td>the colour of the midpoint: three colours. Left out: two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>maxColor</code></td>\n<td>the colour of the largest value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>min</code>, <code>max</code></td>\n<td>the values of <code>minColor</code> and <code>maxColor</code>. Left out: the smallest and the largest value of the rows the filter lets through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mid</code></td>\n<td>with <code>midColor</code>, the value of the midpoint. Left out: halfway between <code>min</code> and <code>max</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The colours are the palette&#39;s backgrounds</strong>, mixed in the cell (<code>color-mix()</code>): light tints in light\nmode, dark ones in dark mode, in both themes. The grid&#39;s text reads on every one of them and on their\nmixes - the theme tests compute it. <strong>A custom colour</strong> is a tint of it: a fifth of the colour and the\nrest white in light mode, black in dark mode - whatever colour is picked, the text reads on it.</li>\n<li><strong>The range</strong> is the one of the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/data-bars\">data bars</a> - the rows the filter lets through, kept by the\ndatasource - without 0 in it. A range of one value has the middle: the midpoint&#39;s colour, or halfway.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-icon-sets\">Icon sets</h2>\n<p>An icon before the value (or after it) by two thresholds: from the second the high icon, from the first\nthe middle one, under it the low one - in percent of the range from the smallest number of the rows to the\nlargest, or as numbers.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        // ▲ from 67 % of the range, ► from 33 %, ▼ under\n        growth: { format: { icons: { set: 'arrows' } } },\n        // a green light from 50 in stock, amber from 10, red under - after the number\n        stock: { format: { icons: { set: 'traffic', thresholds: [10, 50], unit: 'number', position: 'after' } } },\n        // the other way round: a high cost has the red cross\n        cost: { format: { icons: { set: 'symbols', reverse: true } } }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['growth'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['stock'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['cost'], width: 100 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>set</th>\n<th>high</th>\n<th>middle</th>\n<th>low</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;arrows&#39;</code></td>\n<td>▲ green</td>\n<td>► amber</td>\n<td>▼ red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;traffic&#39;</code></td>\n<td>● green</td>\n<td>● amber</td>\n<td>● red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;flags&#39;</code></td>\n<td>⚑ green</td>\n<td>⚑ amber</td>\n<td>⚑ red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;symbols&#39;</code></td>\n<td>✓ green</td>\n<td>! amber</td>\n<td>✕ red</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part of <code>icons</code></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>set</code></td>\n<td>the icons, above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>thresholds</code></td>\n<td><code>[middle, high]</code>: from the first the middle icon, from the second the high one (<code>&gt;=</code>, as a spreadsheet&#39;s). Left out: <code>[33, 67]</code>, the thirds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>unit</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;percent&#39;</code> (the default): the thresholds are percent of the range, 0 to 100. <code>&#39;number&#39;</code>: values of the column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>reverse</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code>: the icons the other way round - the high values have the low icon, for a column where lower is better</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>position</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;before&#39;</code> (the default): at the left of the cell; <code>&#39;after&#39;</code>: at its right. The number moves clear of it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Svg icons</strong> in the theme&#39;s icon colours, <code>--simple-html-grid-icon-green-color</code>, <code>-amber-</code> and <code>-red-</code>:\n3:1 or more to the rows, the selected rows and the palette&#39;s backgrounds, light and dark, in both themes\n(<a href=\"#guide/theming/data-bars-colour-scales-and-icons\">Theming</a>). The shapes differ too - but the traffic\nlights and the flags, said in words for a screen reader.</li>\n<li><strong>For a screen reader</strong> the cell says the level after its value: <em>High</em>, <em>Middle</em> or <em>Low</em> (the texts\n<code>cell.icon.*</code>), the description of its input.</li>\n<li><strong>The range</strong> is the one of the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/data-bars\">data bars</a>, without 0.</li>\n<li><strong>Not in a custom cell</strong> (<code>ColumnConfig.renderer</code>): the renderer draws the cell. A bar and a scale are\nthe cell&#39;s background, a custom cell has them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-bars-scales-icons-and-the-colour-rules\">Bars, scales, icons and the colour rules</h2>\n<p>A column can have colour rules, a data bar, a colour scale and an icon set together. What wins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A rule&#39;s background wins over the scale</strong>: a cell whose rule has a background has the rule&#39;s - the\nrule is the exception the user asked for. A rule&#39;s text colour and bold show over the scale.</li>\n<li><strong>The bar is drawn over a rule&#39;s background</strong> and the scale&#39;s colour, under a range&#39;s tint: both are\nseen. The grid&#39;s text and the palette&#39;s text colours read on all of them.</li>\n<li><strong>The icons show with any rule.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>An error and a match of Find</strong> show as without a bar and a scale - as without a rule&#39;s colours - so\nthey stand out. The icons stay.</li>\n<li><strong>The detail form</strong> shows a value in its rule&#39;s colours, without a bar, a scale or an icon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the grid config dialog a number column has <em>Data bars, scales, icons</em> under its colour rules: a\ncheckbox each, the colour swatches, Min / Max (empty: <em>Automatic</em>), the icon set, the thresholds and what\nthey are, Reverse and where the icon goes - and five cells from the lowest value of the rows to the\nhighest, drawn as the column will be. A number typed that is no number, a min not below the max, a\nmidpoint out of them or thresholds the wrong way round are said at once: Apply waits.</p>\n<p>They are <strong>state</strong> with the rest of the column&#39;s format (<code>getState().formats</code>), and <strong>checked</strong> as it is -\na <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made, or <code>updateConfig()</code> or <code>setState()</code> is given one that does not fit: a\ncolumn that is not a number column (a lookup column neither), a part with <code>checkbox</code>, a key it does not\nknow, a colour that is neither of the palette nor <code>#rrggbb</code>, a number that is not finite, a min not below\nthe max, a <code>mid</code> without <code>midColor</code> or out of the range, thresholds that are not two numbers - the first\nabove the second, out of 0 to 100 in percent - and a <code>set</code>, <code>unit</code> or <code>position</code> it does not have.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"#guide/export/colours\">Excel export</a> writes them as Excel&#39;s own conditional formatting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-type-from-another-field\">The type from another field</h2>\n<p>For property/value style data - a column of values of different types - a text column can take the\ntype of each cell from another field of its row, with <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource(); // value and valueType are text fields: not listed\ndatasource.setData([\n    { property: 'Name', valueType: 'text', value: 'Ann' },\n    { property: 'Age', valueType: 'number', value: 36 },\n    { property: 'Born', valueType: 'date', value: new Date(1990, 4, 17) },\n    { property: 'Active', valueType: 'boolean', value: true }\n]);\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    columns: {\n        value: { dynamicCellTypeColumn: 'valueType' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['property'], width: 120 },\n            { fields: ['valueType'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['value'], width: 150 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The field must be a <code>text</code> field, which is what a field not listed in <code>fields</code> is.</li>\n<li>The other field holds <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;number&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;date&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;datetime&#39;</code>. Anything else\nleaves the cell text.</li>\n<li>Each cell is shown and edited as its type, with the datasource&#39;s default formatter for it:\n<code>datasource.getFormatter(field, type)</code>. A date cell gets the date picker, a boolean a checkbox.</li>\n<li>Sorting, grouping and filtering treat the field as text - the text the cell shows: a date is\n<code>2026-11-25</code> (<code>2026-11-25T14:30:00</code> with a time of day), a number <code>36</code>, a boolean <code>true</code>. So\n<code>= 1990-05-17</code> and <em>contains</em> <code>1990-05</code> find a date, and values of different types sort by that text.\n<code>&gt; 30</code> compares texts, not numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>A backend</strong> keeps the column as text, with the same ISO text for a date: <code>toSqlWhere()</code> with it as a\ntext column selects the rows the grid does.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"dynamic-cell-types\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-showing-0-or-an-empty-cell-numberoverride\">Showing 0 or an empty cell: <code>numberOverride</code></h2>\n<p>The column option <code>numberOverride</code> changes what a number cell shows, not the value:\n<code>&#39;ZERO_TO_BLANK&#39;</code> shows 0 as an empty cell, <code>&#39;BLANK_TO_ZERO&#39;</code> an empty cell as <code>0</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-options\">Grid config</a>. The user sets it in the grid config dialog (<em>Show\nblank as 0</em>, <em>Show 0 as blank</em>): <code>format.numberOverride</code> in the state, which wins over the column&#39;s -\n<code>&#39;NONE&#39;</code> turns it off, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/label-placeholder-blank--0-checkboxes-multiline-read-only\">Label, placeholder, blank / 0, checkboxes, multiline, read only</a>.\nWith a display format the 0 is the format&#39;s: <code>&#39;BLANK_TO_ZERO&#39;</code> with two decimals shows <code>0,00</code>, and\n<code>&#39;ZERO_TO_BLANK&#39;</code> empties what shows as <code>0,00</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"events","title":"Events","html":"<p>The datasource and the grid interface both send events. The datasource&#39;s are about the rows: they\nchanged, were sorted, filtered or grouped, the selection or the current row changed. The grid&#39;s are\nabout the cells: clicks, focus, the ranges of cells and the columns selected, copy and paste from the\nrow menu.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-subscribing-on\">Subscribing: <code>on()</code></h2>\n<p><code>on(name, handler)</code> subscribes to one event. The handler gets the event&#39;s payload, typed from the\nevent name. <code>on()</code> returns a function that unsubscribes:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const offFiltered = datasource.on('collection-filtered', (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log('filtered by', data.info, '-', datasource.getDataRows().length, 'rows left');\n});\n\nconst offClick = gridInterface.on('cell-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log(data.attribute, 'clicked in row', data.row, data.rowData);\n});\n\n// later\noffFiltered();\noffClick();</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The handler stays subscribed until you call the function <code>on()</code> returned. What it returns is\nignored.</li>\n<li>To listen once, unsubscribe in the handler:\n<code>const off = datasource.on(&#39;select&#39;, () =&gt; { off(); ... })</code>.</li>\n<li>The same function subscribed twice to the same event is called once.</li>\n<li>An error thrown by a handler is not caught: it stops the handlers after it for that event.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.destroy()</code> unsubscribes every handler of the grid. The handlers on the datasource\nstay, the datasource lives on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To type a handler you write on its own, use <code>EventHandler</code> with the event map:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { DatasourceEvents, EventHandler, GridEvents } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst onSelect: EventHandler&lt;DatasourceEvents['select']&gt; = (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log('row', data.row, 'selected by', data.info);\n};\nconst onPaste: EventHandler&lt;GridEvents['paste']&gt; = (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log('pasted into', data.attribute, data.entity);\n};\n\ndatasource.on('select', onSelect);\ngridInterface.on('paste', onPaste);</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"events\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-datasource-events\">Datasource events</h2>\n<p><code>DatasourceEvents</code> lists them. An event with nothing to tell sends an empty object.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>collection-changed</code></td>\n<td>rows were added, replaced or removed</td>\n<td><code>{ added?, removed?, info?, data? }</code>, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-filtered</code></td>\n<td>the displayed rows were rebuilt</td>\n<td><code>{ info }</code>, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-sorted</code></td>\n<td><code>sort()</code> ran</td>\n<td><code>{}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-grouped</code></td>\n<td>the grouping changed: <code>group()</code>, <code>removeGroup()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ info: &#39;group&#39;, groupings }</code> or <code>{ info: &#39;removeGroup&#39; }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-expand</code></td>\n<td><code>expandGroup()</code> expanded one or every group</td>\n<td><code>{}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-collapse</code></td>\n<td><code>collapseGroup()</code> collapsed one or every group</td>\n<td><code>{}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>current-entity</code></td>\n<td><code>currentEntity</code> was set: a row was clicked or selected, or <code>setRowAsCurrentEntity()</code>. Also when <code>setData()</code> set it to <code>null</code>, the current row no longer in the data, and when a method that removes rows removed it (<code>removeData()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code>, <code>resetData()</code> ...) - after their own event</td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>datasource.currentEntity</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>selection-change</code></td>\n<td>the selected rows changed. <code>deselectAll()</code> sends it only with <code>{ notify: true }</code>, <code>selectAll()</code> only in the <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> selection mode. Also when a method that removes rows removed a selected one, after its own event</td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>datasource.getSelectedRows()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>select</code></td>\n<td>a row was selected from code, the grid scrolls to it</td>\n<td><code>{ info, row }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validate</code></td>\n<td><code>validate()</code> ran, the grid shows the errors. An edit is validated without an event</td>\n<td><code>{ errors }</code>, what <code>validate()</code> returned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>history-changed</code></td>\n<td>the undo history changed: read <code>canUndo()</code> and <code>canRedo()</code>. Sent by every datasource on the same <code>DataContainer</code>, the history is the container&#39;s</td>\n<td><code>{ info }</code>, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rows-changed</code></td>\n<td>values of rows changed - set from code, by the user, by undo/redo, a revert, <code>upsert()</code> - or a row&#39;s deletion mark, server error or conflict. Once the code that changed them is done (a microtask), one event for all of them. The grid renders the rows on screen, see <a href=\"#guide/live-data/values-show-on-their-own\">Live data</a>. Sent by every datasource on the same <code>DataContainer</code></td>\n<td><code>{ rows, fields }</code>: the rows, and <code>fields.get(row)</code> the fields set on each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conflicts-changed</code></td>\n<td>the conflicts of <code>upsert()</code> changed: one came or went. Once per run of code, after <code>rows-changed</code>. Sent by every datasource on the same <code>DataContainer</code></td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>datasource.getConflicts()</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>collection-changed</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>method</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>setData(rows)</code></td>\n<td><code>{ added: false }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>setData(rows, { add: true })</code>, <code>addNewEmpty()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ added: true }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>removeData(rows)</code></td>\n<td><code>{ removed: true, info: &#39;removeData&#39;, data: rows }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>removeAllData()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ removed: true, info: &#39;removeData&#39;, data: null }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ removed: true, info: &#39;clearMarkedForDeletion&#39; }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>upsert(rows)</code></td>\n<td><code>{ info: &#39;upsert&#39;, added }</code>, and <code>removed: true</code> when it removed rows. Only when it added or removed rows or the displayed rows changed (filter, sort order, groups): an upsert that only changed values sends <code>rows-changed</code> only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>collection-filtered</code>, by <code>info</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>info</code></th>\n<th>sent by</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;filter&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>filter()</code>, <code>reloadDatasource()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;resetData&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>resetData()</code>, <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;markForDeletion&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>markForDeletion()</code>, <code>markAllForDeletion()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;unmarkForDeletion&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>unmarkForDeletion()</code>, <code>unmarkAllForDeletion()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;acceptChanges&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>acceptChanges()</code>. It sends no <code>collection-changed</code>, also when it removed the rows marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;__internalUpdate, forced&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>setData(rows, { refilter: false })</code> and <code>addNewEmpty()</code> while there is no filter, sort or grouping, before their <code>collection-changed</code>. A <code>setData()</code> that runs the filter (the default) sends only <code>collection-changed</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>history-changed</code>, by <code>info</code> - see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>info</code></th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;edit&#39;</code></td>\n<td>a step was recorded: an edit, what was typed in a cell, a paste, a clear, a (un)mark for deletion, a revert. Sent once the code that made it is done (in a microtask), one event per step. Also when what was typed in a cell went back to the value it started from, and its step was dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;undo&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>undo()</code>. The grid renders its cells again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;redo&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>redo()</code>. The grid renders its cells again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;clear&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the history was emptied: <code>clearHistory()</code>, <code>setData()</code> without <code>add</code>, <code>removeAllData()</code>, <code>resetData()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code>. Only when there was something in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;remove&#39;</code></td>\n<td>rows were removed for good (<code>removeData()</code>, <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code>, a new row <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> removed, <code>upsert(rows, { removeMissing: true })</code>) and their changes left the history. Or <code>upsert()</code> gave an edited field the server&#39;s value as its original, and a change of the history that does nothing now was dropped</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>select</code>: <code>row</code> is the index in the displayed rows (<code>getRows()</code>), 0 based. <code>info</code> is\n<code>&#39;select-first&#39;</code> (from <code>select(row, { notify: true })</code> and <code>selectFirst()</code>), <code>&#39;select-prev&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;select-next&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;select-last&#39;</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.on('selection-change', () =&gt; {\n    const count = datasource.getSelectedRows().length;\n    document.getElementById('delete-button')!.toggleAttribute('disabled', count === 0);\n});\n\ndatasource.on('collection-grouped', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.info === 'group') {\n        console.log('grouped on', data.groupings?.map((group) =&gt; group.attribute));\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>rows-changed</code> has the rows an edit changed, and the fields: an edit of a cell, by the user or from\ncode, is in it - see <a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>. <code>history-changed</code> says a step was recorded, not\nwhat it is.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-grid-events\">Grid events</h2>\n<p><code>GridEvents</code> lists them. Most send the cell the event happened on, a <code>GridCellEventData</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cell</code></td>\n<td>the cell element</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row</code></td>\n<td>the index of the row in the displayed rows (<code>datasource.getRows()</code>), 0 based. 0 for a header cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column</code></td>\n<td>the index of the column in its part of the grid, 0 based</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>celno</code></td>\n<td>the index of the cell in a column with stacked fields, 0 based</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>colType</code></td>\n<td>the part of the grid: <code>&#39;left-pinned&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;middle-pinned&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;right-pinned&#39;</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellType</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;row-cell&#39;</code> for a data cell, <code>&#39;filter&#39;</code> for a header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>attribute</code></td>\n<td>the field of the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowData</code></td>\n<td>the cell&#39;s row, always a data row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-cell-events\">Cell events</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cell-click</code></td>\n<td>a data cell was clicked. Sent after the click, so the row is current already</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>originalEvent</code>: the click</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell-dblclick</code></td>\n<td>a data cell was double-clicked. Sent after it - after the <code>cell-click</code> of its two clicks - and after what the grid does with it (the editor of a multiline cell, a lookup&#39;s list)</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>originalEvent</code>: the dblclick</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell-row-focus</code></td>\n<td>a data cell got focus, by a click or the keyboard</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>originalEvent</code>: the focus event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell-focus-button-click</code></td>\n<td>the <code>...</code> button of a cell was clicked, or Space pressed in the cell</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>target</code>: the cell&#39;s input, <code>originalEvent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell-header-focus</code></td>\n<td>a header filter got focus</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>rowData</code>: see below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>cell-click</code>, <code>cell-dblclick</code> and <code>cell-row-focus</code> are not sent for a checkbox (boolean) cell.</p>\n<p>The focus button is shown in the focused cell by the column option <code>focusButton</code> (<code>&#39;ALWAYS&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_AND_CELL_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code>). Use it to open a picker of\nyour own, and focus <code>target</code> to give the focus back to the cell:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('cell-focus-button-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.attribute === 'company') {\n        console.log('pick a company for', data.rowData);\n        data.target.focus();\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>cell-header-focus</code> sends as <code>rowData</code> the first displayed row, not a row of the header: a group\nrow when the datasource is grouped, <code>undefined</code> when there are no rows. Check it with\n<code>isGroupRow()</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-menu-events\">Menu events</h3>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell) sends these when the user picks an entry:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>copy-cell</code></td>\n<td>Copy › Cell Value</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>copy-column</code></td>\n<td>Copy › Column (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>copy-row</code></td>\n<td>Copy › Row (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>copy-row-col</code></td>\n<td>Copy › Row (selected rows/col)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>paste</code></td>\n<td>Paste: Paste cell (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell, without <code>rowData</code>. <code>entity</code>: the row pasted into. Sent once per row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear: Clear cell (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The entries of the ranges of cells (Range, Paste range, Clear range) and Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V / Delete on\na range send none of these: the cells changed are in <code>rows-changed</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>.</p>\n<p><code>filter-operator-change</code> is sent when the user picks an operator in a header filter&#39;s menu, after\nthe filter ran with it. It sends <code>{ field, operator, column, rebuildHeader }</code>: <code>column</code> is the\ncolumn&#39;s options as the grid uses them, change them and call <code>rebuildHeader()</code> to show it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('filter-operator-change', (data) =&gt; {\n    data.column.placeholderFilter = data.operator; // show the operator in the empty filter cell\n    data.rebuildHeader();\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-range-change\"><code>range-change</code></h3>\n<p><code>range-change</code> is sent when the ranges of cells change: the user dragged over the cells, Shift+clicked,\nCtrl/Cmd+clicked, pressed Alt+Shift+arrows or Esc, moved to another cell or typed in one, or\n<code>setRanges()</code> / <code>clearRanges()</code> were called. Also when the grid cleared them because the displayed rows\nor the layout changed. While the mouse drags it is sent each time the range reaches another cell; it is\nnot sent when nothing changed. The payload is <code>{ ranges }</code>, what <code>getRanges()</code> gives now - <code>[]</code> when\nthey were cleared. See <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-api\">Selection and clipboard</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('range-change', ({ ranges }) =&gt; {\n    const last = ranges[ranges.length - 1];\n    console.log(last ? `rows ${last.fromRow}-${last.toRow}, ${last.fromField} to ${last.toField}` : 'no cells selected');\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-selection-change\"><code>column-selection-change</code></h3>\n<p><code>column-selection-change</code> is sent when the selected columns change: the user clicked a column number\n(the row of numbers under the header filters, Ctrl/Cmd+click and Shift+click too) or pressed Esc on\none, or <code>selectColumns()</code> was called. Also when the grid cleared them because the fields of the\nlayout changed - a column moved, pinned, hidden or added. Not when nothing changed. The payload is\n<code>{ columns, fields }</code>: the numbers, what <code>getSelectedColumns()</code> gives, and their fields. See\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-api-of-the-columns\">Selection and clipboard</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('column-selection-change', ({ columns, fields }) =&gt; {\n    console.log(columns.length ? `columns ${columns.join(', ')}: ${fields.join(', ')}` : 'no column selected');\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-find-change\"><code>find-change</code></h3>\n<p><code>find-change</code> is sent when what Find (Ctrl+F in the grid) has found changes: the bar opened or closed,\nthe typing stopped, the current match moved, <em>Match case</em> or <em>Filter rows</em> turned on or off. The payload\nis what <code>getFind()</code> gives.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-views-change\"><code>views-change</code></h3>\n<p><code>views-change</code> is sent when the list of named views changes: the user saved a view (over the one shown,\nor as a new one), renamed or deleted one, or picked the default view - in the views menu of the footer,\nor your code called <code>saveView()</code>, <code>saveViewAs()</code>, <code>renameView()</code>, <code>deleteView()</code> or <code>setDefaultView()</code>.\nThe payload is <code>{ views }</code>, what <code>getViews()</code> gives: store it, and give it back as <code>GridConfig.views</code>\nnext time. Not sent by <code>setViews()</code> and <code>updateConfig({ views })</code> - you gave the list - nor when the\nuser only switches views. See <a href=\"#guide/state/views\">Views</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('views-change', ({ views }) =&gt; {\n    // the readonly views are yours, they come from the config every time\n    const own = views.filter((view) =&gt; !view.readonly);\n    localStorage.setItem('orders-grid-views', JSON.stringify(own));\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-grid-connected\"><code>grid-connected</code></h3>\n<p><code>grid-connected</code> is sent when a grid element connects to the interface: <code>connectInterface()</code>, or\nthe element is put back on the page. <code>createGrid()</code> connects before it returns, so a handler\nsubscribed after <code>createGrid()</code> does not hear the first one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-date-picker\">The date picker</h2>\n<p>A <code>DateInterface</code> - a date picker of your own, outside a grid - sends <code>date-select</code> when the user\npicks a date: the &quot;Use&quot; button sends the date and time, &quot;Clear&quot; sends <code>null</code>. Removing its element\nfrom the page unsubscribes every handler.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type DateElement, DateInterface, type IDateConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/date.css';\n\nconst config: IDateConfig = {\n    datepicker: true,\n    datepickerDate: new Date(),\n    monthsToShow: 1,\n    monthColumns: 1,\n    startMonth: 0,\n    startYear: 2026,\n    weekStart: 1,\n    showWeek: true,\n    isoWeek: true,\n    rowHeight: 22,\n    monthWidth: 230,\n    monthMargin: 10,\n    hideDimmedDates: false,\n    showYearInMonth: false,\n    headerTitle: ''\n};\n\nconst dateInterface = new DateInterface(config);\nconst element = document.createElement('simple-html-date') as DateElement;\nelement.classList.add('simple-html-date');\nelement.connectInterface(dateInterface);\nhost.appendChild(element);\n\ndateInterface.on('date-select', (date) =&gt; {\n    console.log(date ? date.toISOString() : 'cleared');\n});</code></pre>\n<p>A range picker - <code>rangepicker: true</code> with <code>datepicker: true</code> - picks From and To: two months side by\nside (one in a window too narrow for two), the first day clicked is From, the next one To. It sends\n<code>range-select</code>: &quot;Use&quot; sends <code>[from, to]</code>, the days at 00:00 - an end not picked is <code>null</code>, an open end</p>\n<ul>\n<li>and &quot;Clear&quot; sends <code>null</code>. <code>datepickerRange</code> is the range it opens on.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type DateElement, DateInterface, type IDateConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const config: IDateConfig; // as above\n\nconst rangeInterface = new DateInterface({\n    ...config,\n    datepicker: true,\n    rangepicker: true,\n    datepickerRange: [new Date(2026, 10, 22), new Date(2026, 10, 28)]\n});\nconst rangeElement = document.createElement('simple-html-date') as DateElement;\nrangeElement.classList.add('simple-html-date');\nrangeElement.connectInterface(rangeInterface);\nhost.appendChild(rangeElement);\n\nrangeInterface.on('range-select', (range) =&gt; {\n    if (!range) {\n        console.log('cleared');\n        return;\n    }\n    const [from, to] = range;\n    console.log(from?.toDateString() ?? 'open', to?.toDateString() ?? 'open');\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The names of the months and days and the buttons are texts, <code>datePicker.*</code>: the page&#39;s\n(<code>setTexts()</code>), or the picker&#39;s own in <code>texts</code> of its config - see <a href=\"#guide/texts/date-picker\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<p>The date pickers the grid opens in date cells and date header filters are its own: they write the\ndate into the cell or the filter, you do not subscribe to them. They show the grid&#39;s texts.</p>\n"},{"id":"menus","title":"Menus","html":"<p>The grid has four right click menus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>row menu</strong>, on a cell or a row number: the submenus undo and redo, select, copy, paste,\nclear, delete, filter on the value, view, sizes, export. On a row number it is about rows: undo and\nredo, select, copy, delete and sizes, without the entries of a cell. The summary of selected cells is in the footer, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-summary-in-the-footer\">Selection and clipboard</a></li>\n<li>the <strong>header menu</strong>, on a header label: pin, hide, remove sorting, the column chooser, then the\nsubmenus sizes, format (how a date or number column shows its values), aggregates, grouping</li>\n<li>the <strong>filter menu</strong>, on a header filter: the submenu filter (the operator, is blank, clear, the\nfilter editor), then the value list of a text column or the range block of a number or date column</li>\n<li>the <strong>column menu</strong>, on a column number (the row of numbers under the header filters): the\nsubmenu select, then copy the column, the selected columns, the header names - see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-column-menu\">Selection and clipboard</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Select ›</em>, in the row menu and the column menu, does what Shift and Ctrl do with the mouse - for a\nfinger, which has neither, and for every other input too: see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>.</p>\n<p>Each is a tree of entries with ids: most entries are in submenus, so a menu is short and opens\nbeside it what you point at. The <code>menus</code> hook changes them: hide entries, reorder them, change their\nlabels, add your own. <code>openMenu()</code> opens a menu of your own, in the same style, with submenus too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-a-menu-opens-and-closes\">How a menu opens and closes</h2>\n<p>Every menu of the grid - the four right click menus, <code>openMenu()</code>, the column chooser, the field\nand operator pickers of the filter editor and the date picker - is a popover (<code>popover=&quot;manual&quot;</code>):\nthe browser shows it in the top layer, over everything on the page. A container with\n<code>overflow: hidden</code> does not cut it off, and no z-index puts something over it. It is on\n<code>document.body</code>, or inside the <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> (the filter editor, or one of yours) or the popover of yours\nthat the element it opens from is in: everything outside a modal dialog is inert, a menu there could\nnot be clicked. A menu has the classes <code>simple-html-grid simple-html-grid-popover</code>. A submenu, and the\ndate picker opened from an input of the filter menu&#39;s range block, are popovers put in the menu&#39;s\npopover: the menu stays open while they are, and they close with it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is one menu at a time: opening one closes the one before.</li>\n<li>It closes on a click outside it, a finger touching the page outside it, on Esc, when another menu\nopens, on scroll (with\n<code>autoRemoveContextMenuOnScrollEvent: true</code>) and when the grid leaves the page. Clicking an item\ncloses it too, then runs the item&#39;s action; a click in a block does not.</li>\n<li>Opened with the mouse, the focus stays on the cell the menu was opened from: an item does not take\nit. Opened from the keyboard (Shift+F10, the menu key) it takes the focus, see\n<a href=\"#guide/menus/the-keyboard\">The keyboard</a>. When the focus is in the menu as it closes - an item, a search\nfield - it goes back to the element that had it when the menu opened.</li>\n<li>The column chooser is a panel more than a menu: it stays open while you drag columns from it into\nthe header, and when another menu opens. It closes with its Close button, Esc in its search field\n(or on a ✎), and when the grid leaves the page. See <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-column-chooser\">The column chooser</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why <code>manual</code> and not <code>auto</code>, which would close it on a click outside and Esc by itself: where the\nbrowser sends <code>contextmenu</code> on the button press (macOS, Linux), the pointerup that follows is outside\nthe menu, and an <code>auto</code> popover would be closed by it the moment a right click opened it. So the grid\ncloses its menus itself, the same way everywhere.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-column-chooser\">The column chooser</h3>\n<p><em>Column chooser…</em> in the header menu opens a list of every column of the config, by their labels, in\nthe order of the labels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The search</strong> at its top is framed as the inputs of the dialogs, a magnifying glass in front of\n<em>Search</em>. It finds a column by its label - a label the user gave it too - or its field name, in any\ncase: <code>fir</code> finds <em>Firm</em> and <em>first_name</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>A field</strong> has a drag handle at its left, the six dots of the Detail form tab&#39;s handles: the whole\nrow is dragged into the header to add the column, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The ✎</strong> at the right of a field (under the pointer and with the focus; always on a touch screen)\nturns its label into an input with the label. Enter - or leaving the input - gives the column that\nlabel, Esc leaves it as it was, and an empty input gives the column its own label again (the\ninput&#39;s placeholder). It is the label of the grid config dialog&#39;s <em>Label</em>: <code>ColumnFormat.label</code> in\n<code>GridState.formats</code>, the other parts of the column&#39;s format kept - the header and everything that\nnames the column follow, <code>getState()</code> keeps it, <code>resetState()</code> takes it away. The ✎ is a button named\n<em>Rename Firm</em>, a stop of Tab after the search.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-views-menu\">The views menu</h3>\n<p>The views button at the left end of the footer (<code>GridConfig.viewsButton</code>, shown with <code>views</code> or\n<code>viewsStorageKey</code>) opens the views menu above it - a second click closes it. It is a menu of the grid\nas the others: the arrows, Home and End, the first letter of an entry, Enter and Esc work the same, and\nopened from the keyboard (Enter or Space on the button) the focus is on its first entry:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Standard and the views</strong>, a ✓ on the one shown: a click applies it, one click to switch. The hint\n<em>default</em> is on the view the grid starts in (Standard when no view is), <em>shared</em> on a readonly view.</li>\n<li><strong>Save</strong> saves the state over the view shown, <strong>Save as new view…</strong> makes a new view of it, <strong>Revert\nchanges</strong> applies the view shown again. Save is greyed out while nothing changed, and on Standard and a\nreadonly view; Revert changes while nothing changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Rename…</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong> the view shown - greyed out on Standard and a readonly view - and\n<strong>Default view</strong>, a toggle with a ✓: the view shown is the one the grid starts in.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Save as new view…</em> and <em>Rename…</em> ask for the name in a menu of its own at the button: an input with\nthe focus, Cancel and Save (Rename). Enter saves, Esc closes it, the focus goes back to the button. A\nname of only spaces, or one another view has (in any case), is not taken: the line under the input\nsays why. The views menu is not one of the <code>menus</code> hook: hide the button (<code>viewsButton: false</code>) and\nbuild a control of your own on <code>getViews()</code>, <code>applyView()</code>, <code>saveViewAs()</code> ... See\n<a href=\"#guide/state/views\">Views</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-submenus\">Submenus</h3>\n<p>An item with a <code>›</code> opens a submenu beside it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>when the pointer rests on it (150ms: moving the pointer across other items on the way to an open\nsubmenu does not swap it), on a click, and with ArrowRight, Enter or Space</li>\n<li>on the right of the menu, its first entry level with the item; on the left when there is no room\non the right. It is kept in the window as a menu is, and scrolls when it is taller than the window</li>\n<li>one at a time in a menu: pointing at another item closes it, and so do ArrowLeft and Esc - the\nfocus goes back to its item</li>\n<li>a click on an item in a submenu closes the whole menu, then runs the action. A click outside, Esc in\nthe menu itself, another menu: everything closes</li>\n<li>a submenu is a popover of its own, put in the menu&#39;s popover; submenus can have submenus</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-with-a-finger\">With a finger</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A long press</strong> - a finger held still (10px) for 500ms - opens the menu a right click opens, at the\nfinger, while it is still down. Lifting it after that is no tap: the cell under it does not take the\nfocus (no keyboard pops up), nothing is selected, the menu stays open. A finger that moves is a\nswipe, a second finger a pinch: no menu.</li>\n<li>The long press sends a <code>contextmenu</code> event (a <code>PointerEvent</code> with <code>pointerType: &#39;touch&#39;</code>) to the\nelement the finger is on, so a <code>contextmenu</code> listener of your own in the grid works with a finger as\nit is. Safari on iOS sends no <code>contextmenu</code> for a long press; Chrome on Android does - whichever comes\nfirst opens the menu, the other is canceled: one menu. A long press on an element without a menu (a\ngroup row&#39;s number) stays a tap.</li>\n<li><strong>Select ›</strong> of the row menu and the column menu is the finger&#39;s Shift and Ctrl: rows from the\nanchor to here, a range of cells from the active cell to here, a cell added to the ranges - and a\ntap on a row or column number toggles it, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Taps</strong>: a tap on an item with a <code>›</code> opens its submenu, a tap on an item runs it, a tap outside\ncloses the menu (as soon as the finger touches the page, a swipe of the page too).</li>\n<li>On a label the hold makes a drag first (200ms, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a>): still held at 500ms\nthe header menu opens and the drag ends, nothing moves.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigger targets</strong>: on a touch screen (css <code>@media (pointer: coarse)</code>) the items of the menus and\nsubmenus, the column chooser&#39;s fields and Close, and the rows of the filter menu&#39;s value list\nare 28px high (<code>--simple-html-grid-touch-row-height</code>), and the filter editor&#39;s edges and corners a finger can take (10px, 20px). The buttons,\nthe range block&#39;s operators and inputs and the searches (the column chooser&#39;s too) keep their size, 24px - the WCAG 2.2 minimum\ntarget - in line with the rest of a dialog. With a mouse everything stays as it is.</li>\n<li><strong>The column chooser</strong>: its list scrolls with a finger; a field held still for 200ms is dragged, and\nis shown over the chooser while it is (the dragged field is a popover too). The ✎ of each field is\nalways shown, 24px.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-keyboard\">The keyboard</h3>\n<p>The menus follow the WAI-ARIA <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/menubar/\">menu pattern</a>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>opens the menu of the cell, header label, header filter or column number with the focus, the focus on its first item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowDown / ArrowUp</td>\n<td>the next / previous item, round. Lines and section headers are passed by; a disabled item gets the focus too, it can not be used. In a menu opened with the mouse, the first ArrowDown (ArrowUp) gets into it: the first (last) item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Home / End</td>\n<td>the first / last item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a letter</td>\n<td>the next item whose label starts with it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter, Space</td>\n<td>on an item: as a click - the menu closes, the focus goes back, the action runs. On an item with a submenu: opens it, the focus on its first item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowRight</td>\n<td>opens the submenu of the item, the focus on its first item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowLeft</td>\n<td>closes the submenu, the focus back on its item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>in a submenu: closes it, the focus back on its item. In the menu: closes it, the focus goes back where it was</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab, Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>closes the menu, the focus goes back where it was. In a menu with a block - the filter menu&#39;s value list, range block or quick dates - Tab is the browser&#39;s: it goes on into the block&#39;s inputs, and Shift+Tab back to the item the keyboard was on</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>For a screen reader the menu is a <code>menu</code>, an item a <code>menuitem</code> - a <code>menuitemcheckbox</code> with\n<code>aria-checked</code> when it has a <code>selected</code> callback (the aggregates, the dimmed values, the operator in\nuse) - with <code>aria-disabled</code> when it is disabled. An item with a submenu has <code>aria-haspopup=&quot;menu&quot;</code> and\n<code>aria-expanded</code>, its submenu is labelled by it. In the filter menu&#39;s value list the search box,\nthe checkboxes, <em>Cancel</em> and <em>Apply</em> are stops of Tab; ArrowDown / ArrowUp move between the search\nbox and the checkboxes, Enter in them applies, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-value-list\">The value list</a>. In\nthe range block the operators are a radio group - one stop of Tab, the arrows pick another one - then\nthe input (From and To), <em>Cancel</em> and <em>Apply</em>; Enter applies, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-range-block\">The range block</a>. The quick dates after it are one stop of Tab:\nthe arrows move between them (two columns), Enter or Space filters, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">Quick dates</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-changing-the-grids-menus\">Changing the grid&#39;s menus</h2>\n<p>The <code>menus</code> hook has a function per menu: <code>row</code>, <code>header</code>, <code>filter</code> and <code>column</code>. It gets the grid&#39;s own\nentries (<code>defaults</code>) and what the menu was opened on (<code>context</code>), and returns the entries to show.\nIt is called every time the menu opens. Like the other <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">hooks</a> it is given\nnext to the config, not in it: the config stays plain JSON.</p>\n<p><code>defaults</code> is a tree: a submenu (<code>type: &#39;submenu&#39;</code>) has its own <code>entries</code>. Its id is the start of\nthe ids in it - <code>row.copy.cell</code> is in <code>row.copy</code> - so a filter on the start of an id hides a submenu\nwith what is in it, as it hid a section before. An entry in a submenu is changed in that submenu,\nwith <code>map</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks, isGroupRow, type MenuEntry } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['age'], width: 80 }] }\n};\n\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: {\n        row: (defaults, context) =&gt; [\n            // hide built-in entries by id: here the export submenu, with what is in it\n            ...defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; !entry.id.startsWith('row.export')),\n            // add your own\n            {\n                id: 'my.logRow',\n                label: 'Log row',\n                visible: () =&gt; !isGroupRow(context.row),\n                action: () =&gt; console.log(context.field, context.row)\n            }\n        ],\n        // reorder: the value list first\n        filter: (defaults) =&gt; [\n            ...defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; entry.id === 'filter.values'),\n            ...defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; entry.id !== 'filter.values')\n        ],\n        // in a submenu: hide an entry of the size submenu, add one of your own to it\n        header: (defaults, context) =&gt;\n            defaults.map((entry): MenuEntry =&gt; {\n                if (entry.type !== 'submenu' || entry.id !== 'header.size') {\n                    return entry;\n                }\n                return {\n                    ...entry,\n                    entries: [\n                        ...entry.entries.filter((inner) =&gt; inner.id !== 'header.size.resetAll'),\n                        { id: 'my.wide', label: 'Wide', action: () =&gt; console.log('wide', context.field) }\n                    ]\n                };\n            })\n    }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>defaults</code> always has every entry of the menu. The ones that do not apply here have a <code>visible</code>\ncallback that hides them: paste and clear in a readonly grid (or where the edit permissions let no\nvalue change), delete in a readonly grid, the operators of another type. A submenu with nothing\nleft to show is hidden too.</li>\n<li>A section header or a separator is shown as long as it is in the list: hide it together with the\nentries under it.</li>\n<li>To move an entry out of its submenu, take it from the submenu&#39;s <code>entries</code>:\n<code>defaults.flatMap((entry) =&gt; (entry.type === &#39;submenu&#39; ? entry.entries : [entry]))</code> is every entry\nof the first level of submenus, in a flat list.</li>\n<li>The entries in <code>defaults</code> are new objects every time, change them as you like. Their labels are\nthe texts of their ids, already looked up: to translate the menus use the texts (see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a>), the hook is for changes in one menu.</li>\n<li><code>updateHooks({ menus })</code> changes the functions while the grid is shown,\n<code>updateHooks({ menus: undefined })</code> gives back the defaults.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"context-menu\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-entries\">Entries</h2>\n<p>A <code>MenuEntry</code> is one of:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>{ id, label, action, hint?, tooltip?, visible?, disabled?, selected? }</code> - a <code>MenuItem</code></td>\n<td>an entry to click. <code>type: &#39;item&#39;</code> can be left out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ type: &#39;submenu&#39;, id, label, entries, visible? }</code> - a <code>MenuSubmenu</code></td>\n<td>an entry with a <code>›</code> that opens <code>entries</code> beside it, see <a href=\"#guide/menus/submenus\">Submenus</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ type: &#39;section&#39;, id, label, visible? }</code> - a <code>MenuSection</code></td>\n<td>a section header with a line under it, <code>Pick a fruit:</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ type: &#39;separator&#39;, id, visible? }</code> - a <code>MenuSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a dashed line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ type: &#39;block&#39;, id, element, visible? }</code> - a <code>MenuBlock</code></td>\n<td>your element, as it is</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Clicking an item closes the menu, then calls <code>action(context)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>hint</code> is shown after the label, in italics and in parentheses: <code>Paste cell (selected rows)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>tooltip</code> says what the item does, longer than a label: a tooltip while the pointer rests on it, and\nits description for a screen reader (<code>aria-description</code>).</li>\n<li><code>visible(context)</code>: <code>false</code> leaves the entry out. <code>disabled(context)</code>: <code>true</code> shows the item greyed\nout, a click does nothing. <code>selected(context)</code>: <code>true</code> shows a ✓ in front of the label - a toggle\nthat is on, the choice in use. They are called when the menu (or submenu) opens.</li>\n<li>Labels are shown as they are given. The grid&#39;s own come from the texts, by the id of the entry.</li>\n<li>Every entry is rendered with its id in a <code>data-menu-id</code> attribute, for css and tests. A disabled\nitem has the class <code>simple-html-grid-menu-item-disabled</code>, a selected one\n<code>simple-html-grid-menu-item-selected</code>, an item with a submenu <code>aria-haspopup=&quot;menu&quot;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every callback gets a <code>MenuContext</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>menu</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;row&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;header&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;filter&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;column&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;custom&#39;</code> (opened with <code>openMenu()</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>field</code></td>\n<td>the field of the cell the menu was opened on. The column menu: the column&#39;s first field, a column of stacked fields has more. <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for <code>openMenu()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row</code></td>\n<td>row menu: the cell&#39;s row. Header, filter and column menus: the first displayed row (a group row when grouped, <code>undefined</code> without rows). <code>openMenu()</code>: <code>undefined</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowIndex</code></td>\n<td>the index of <code>row</code> in the displayed rows: 0 in the header, filter and column menus, -1 for <code>openMenu()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell</code></td>\n<td>the cell the menu was opened on, <code>undefined</code> for <code>openMenu()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gridInterface</code></td>\n<td>the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datasource</code></td>\n<td>the grid&#39;s datasource</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-built-in-ids\">The built-in ids</h2>\n<p><code>MENU_IDS</code> lists the ids of every built-in entry, per menu: a flat list in the order of the tree, a\nsubmenu then what is in it. An entry in a submenu starts with the id of the submenu (<code>row.copy.cell</code>\nis in <code>row.copy</code>); the pin and blank submenus kept the ids their entries had, which share the start\nof their part of the menu (<code>header.column.pinLeft</code> is in <code>header.column.pin</code>, <code>filter.filter.isBlank</code>\nin <code>filter.filter.blank</code>). The operator submenu <code>filter.operator</code> is in the filter menu&#39;s submenu\n<code>filter.filter</code> with the ids it had before it was there: a hook that hides <code>filter.filter</code> hides it\ntoo. The ids stay the same between versions, so a menu changed by id keeps working.</p>\n<p>The ids are the ids of the texts too: the label of <code>row.copy.cell</code> is the text <code>row.copy.cell</code>, its\nhint <code>&lt;id&gt;.hint</code>. Change them with <code>setTexts()</code> or the grid&#39;s <code>texts</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/row-menu\">Texts</a>.\nThe &quot;shown as&quot; below is the English text.</p>\n<p>The menus as they open, a <code>›</code> is a submenu:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>row menu                          header menu                     filter menu\nUndo / Redo ›                     Pin ›                           Filter ›\nSelect ›                          Hide column                     the value list / the range block\nCopy ›                            Remove sorting\nPaste ›                           ----                            in Filter ›:\nClear ›                           Column chooser…                 Operator ›\nDelete ›                          Size ›                          Match case\nFilter ›                          Format ›                        Blank ›\nView ›                            Column settings…                Clear Filter\nSize ›                            Aggregate ›                     Clear All Filters\nExport ›                          Grouping ›                      ----\n                                                                  Advanced Filter…\n\ncolumn menu                       in Select › of the row menu:    in Select › of the column menu:\nSelect ›                          This row                        This column\n----                              Rows from N to here             Columns from N to here\nCopy column (all rows)            All rows                        All columns\nCopy selected columns (all rows)  ----                            Clear column selection\n----                              Range from the active cell to here\nCopy header names                 Add this cell to the ranges\nCopy header name                  ----\nCopy selected header names        Clear selection\n\nin Format › of a date column:     in Format › of a number column:\nDefault                           Default\n----                              ----\nISO ... Day and month name        Norwegian\n----  (a datetime column)         English\nISO with time ... Time            Swiss\n----                              Plain\nDay of week ... Days until        ----\n                                  Decimals ›\n                                  Thousands separator ›\n                                  Decimal mark ›\n                                  Percent</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-row-menu-menu_idsrow\">Row menu (<code>MENU_IDS.row</code>)</h3>\n<p>Right click on a cell, or on a row number. On a row number the menu has no cell: <code>context.field</code> is\n<code>&#39;&#39;</code> and <code>context.cell</code> is the row number&#39;s cell. It is about rows - <em>Undo / Redo ›</em>, <em>Select ›</em>,\n<em>Copy ›</em>, <em>Delete ›</em> and <em>Size ›</em> - and what needs a cell or is not about rows is not in it: <em>Paste ›</em>,\n<em>Clear ›</em>, <em>Filter ›</em>, <em>View ›</em>, <em>Export ›</em>, <em>Undo cell edit</em>, <em>Copy › Cell Value</em>, <em>Column</em> and\n<em>Range</em>, the cell entries of <em>Select ›</em>, <em>Fit column to this cell</em>. They are left out with <code>visible</code>:\nthe <code>menus.row</code> hook still gets them, by their ids. A group row&#39;s number has no menu.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>row.edit</code></td>\n<td>Undo / Redo ›: a submenu, first in the menu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo</code></td>\n<td>Undo last change (Ctrl+Z, ⌘Z on a Mac): <code>datasource.undo()</code>, and the focus goes to the cell it changed, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>. Greyed out when there is nothing to undo</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo</code></td>\n<td>Redo (Ctrl+Y, ⇧⌘Z on a Mac): <code>datasource.redo()</code>, the focus to the cell it changed. Greyed out when there is nothing to redo</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertCell</code></td>\n<td>Undo cell edit: the cell&#39;s original value, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>. Greyed out when the cell is not edited</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRow</code></td>\n<td>Undo row edits: the row right clicked back to its original values, its deletion mark taken off - no selection needed. A new row is marked for deletion: it is not on the server. Greyed out when the row is not edited, not marked and not new</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRows</code></td>\n<td>Undo row edits (selected rows): the selected rows back, deletion marks taken off, the new ones marked for deletion. Greyed out when no selected row is edited, marked or new</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select</code></td>\n<td>Select › - a submenu: what Shift and Ctrl do with the mouse, for a finger and every input, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.row</code></td>\n<td>This row: the row in or out of the selection, as Ctrl+click on its number, a ✓ while it is selected. In <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode it replaces the row selected</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.rowsTo</code></td>\n<td>Rows from N to here: from the anchor (N, the row selected last without Shift) to this row, as Shift+click on its number. Greyed out without an anchor (N is this row then), when the anchor is this row, and in <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.allRows</code></td>\n<td>All rows: <code>datasource.selectAll()</code>. Greyed out when every row is selected, and in <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.cellsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.rangeTo</code></td>\n<td>Range from the active cell to here: as Shift+click on the cell - from where the last range starts, or from the cell with the focus, to this cell. Greyed out when there is neither, or when it would start at this cell. Not on a row number</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.addCell</code></td>\n<td>Add this cell to the ranges: as Ctrl+click on the cell - the active cell kept as a range when there is none yet. Greyed out when the cell is in a range. Not on a row number</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.clearSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear selection: no row selected, no range of cells. Greyed out when there is neither</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy</code></td>\n<td>Copy › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.cell</code></td>\n<td>Cell Value</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.column</code></td>\n<td>Column (selected rows). Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.row</code></td>\n<td>Row (selected rows). Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td>Row (selected rows/col): the selected columns, every column when none is selected. Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.range</code></td>\n<td>Range: the ranges of cells, as Ctrl+C copies them - see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/copy\">Selection and clipboard</a>. Greyed out without a range</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste</code></td>\n<td>Paste › - a submenu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.current</code></td>\n<td>Paste cell: the cell right clicked. Greyed out when it is readonly - also on a row the <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">edit permissions</a> lock (<code>change: false</code>, a row that is not new)</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.cell</code></td>\n<td>Paste cell (selected rows): the column of the selected rows, readonly cells skipped. Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.range</code></td>\n<td>Paste range: the clipboard as Ctrl+V pastes it with ranges - one value into every cell of them, a block from the top left of the last one. Reads the clipboard with <code>navigator.clipboard.readText()</code>, the browser may ask the user first. Greyed out without a range</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.fillDown</code></td>\n<td>Fill down (Ctrl+D): as Ctrl+D (<code>gridInterface.fillDown()</code>) - in every range the values of its top row go into the rows under it, a range of one row gets the row above it; without a range the cell right clicked gets the value of the cell above it. Readonly cells skipped, one step of undo. Greyed out without a range when the cell is readonly</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.newRowsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td><code>importMenu</code>, the grid is editable and <code>edit</code> allows <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.newRows</code></td>\n<td>As new rows…: the import dialog with the rows on the clipboard (<code>gridInterface.openImport(text)</code>) - their columns from this cell&#39;s column on, or by the names of a header row - added as new rows on <em>Import</em>, see <a href=\"#guide/import\">Import</a></td>\n<td><code>importMenu</code>, the grid is editable and <code>edit</code> allows <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear › - a submenu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.current</code></td>\n<td>Clear cell: empties the cell right clicked. Greyed out when it is readonly, as Paste cell</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.cell</code></td>\n<td>Clear cell (selected rows): empties the column of the selected rows, readonly cells skipped. Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.range</code></td>\n<td>Clear range: every cell of the ranges, as Delete. Greyed out without a range</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete</code></td>\n<td>Delete › - a submenu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.row</code></td>\n<td>Row: marks the row right clicked for deletion. Greyed out when it is marked, when <code>edit.delete</code> is <code>false</code> and the row is not new - a new row can always be taken out - or locked by the <code>isReadonly</code> hook</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.rows</code></td>\n<td>Rows (selected rows): marks them for deletion, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/deleting-rows\">Deleting rows</a> - with <code>edit.delete: false</code> the new ones only. Greyed out when no selected row is left to mark</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.undo</code></td>\n<td>Undo (selected rows): takes the mark off. Greyed out when no selected row is marked</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter</code></td>\n<td>Filter › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.replace</code></td>\n<td>Replace filter (all)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.add</code></td>\n<td>Add to filter (and)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view</code></td>\n<td>View › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.dimRepeatedValues</code></td>\n<td>Use dimmed values, with a ✓ when it is on</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.changedOnly</code></td>\n<td>Changed rows only, with a ✓ when it is on: the new, edited and deleted rows of the filter, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/changed-rows-only\">Changed rows only</a></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size</code></td>\n<td>Size › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitColumn</code></td>\n<td>Fit column to this cell: the column as wide as the cell&#39;s text, <code>gridInterface.fitColumns([field], { rows: [row] })</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-widths-and-row-heights\">Column widths and row heights</a></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitRow</code></td>\n<td>Fit row height: the row right clicked as high as its multiline cells need, <code>fitRows(row)</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitRows</code></td>\n<td>Fit row height (selected rows): the selected rows and the rows of the ranges of cells. Greyed out without either</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitAllRows</code></td>\n<td>Fit all rows: every row the filter lets through, <code>fitRows()</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.resetRow</code></td>\n<td>Reset row height: the row right clicked, <code>resetRowHeights(row)</code>. Greyed out when it has the height of the layout</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.resetAllRows</code></td>\n<td>Reset all row heights: <code>resetRowHeights()</code>. Greyed out when no row has a height of its own</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export</code></td>\n<td>Export › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.excel</code></td>\n<td>Excel, all rows: <code>gridInterface.downloadExcel()</code>, an .xlsx file, see <a href=\"#guide/export\">Export</a></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.excelSelected</code></td>\n<td>Excel, selected rows: <code>downloadExcel(&#39;export.xlsx&#39;, { rows: &#39;selected&#39; })</code>. Greyed out when no row is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-header-menu-menu_idsheader\">Header menu (<code>MENU_IDS.header</code>)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pin</code></td>\n<td>Pin › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeft</code></td>\n<td>Pin left</td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeftCopy</code></td>\n<td>Pin left (copy): pinned, and kept where it is</td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRight</code></td>\n<td>Pin right</td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRightCopy</code></td>\n<td>Pin right (copy)</td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.unpin</code></td>\n<td>Unpin</td>\n<td>pinned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.hide</code></td>\n<td>Hide column</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.removeSorting</code></td>\n<td>Remove sorting: keeps the sort of the grouped fields</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooserSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooser</code></td>\n<td>Column chooser…: every field, drag one into the header</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size</code></td>\n<td>Size › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.column</code></td>\n<td>Fit column width: to its content and its label, <code>gridInterface.fitColumns([field])</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.all</code></td>\n<td>Fit all columns: <code>fitColumns()</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.resetColumn</code></td>\n<td>Reset column width: the width of the config, <code>resetColumnWidths([field])</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.resetAll</code></td>\n<td>Reset all column widths: <code>resetColumnWidths()</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format</code></td>\n<td>Format › - a submenu: how the column&#39;s cells show the value, the display format in use with a ✓ (<code>GridState.formats</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>. Each hint is an example: today, or 1234567.891</td>\n<td>a date, datetime or number field, not a lookup column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.default</code></td>\n<td>Default: no display format, the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.defaultSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.iso</code></td>\n<td>ISO (2026-11-22) - <code>{ date: &#39;iso&#39; }</code>, a choice with a ✓</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegian</code></td>\n<td>Norwegian (22.11.2026)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegianShort</code></td>\n<td>Norwegian, short year (22.11.26)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.us</code></td>\n<td>US (11/22/2026)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.uk</code></td>\n<td>UK (22/11/2026)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.medium</code></td>\n<td>Short month name (Nov 22, 2026), in the language of the text <code>format.locale</code></td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.long</code></td>\n<td>Month name (November 22, 2026)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.full</code></td>\n<td>Day and month name (Sunday, November 22, 2026)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.timeSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.isoTime</code></td>\n<td>ISO with time (2026-11-22 13:45)</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.isoSeconds</code></td>\n<td>ISO with seconds (2026-11-22 13:45:12)</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegianTime</code></td>\n<td>Norwegian with time (22.11.2026 13:45)</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegianSeconds</code></td>\n<td>Norwegian with seconds (22.11.2026 13:45:12)</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.time</code></td>\n<td>Time (13:45)</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.timeSeconds</code></td>\n<td>Time with seconds (13:45:12)</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.partSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.weekday</code></td>\n<td>Day of week (Sunday)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.weekIso</code></td>\n<td>Week, ISO (2026-W47)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.weekUs</code></td>\n<td>Week, US (2026-W48)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.month</code></td>\n<td>Month (2026-11)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.quarter</code></td>\n<td>Quarter (2026 Q4)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.year</code></td>\n<td>Year (2026)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.daysUntil</code></td>\n<td>Days until: the whole days from today, no hint</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.norwegian</code></td>\n<td>Norwegian (1 234 567,891): a space between the thousands, a decimal comma - the decimals and percent kept</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.english</code></td>\n<td>English (1,234,567.891)</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.swiss</code></td>\n<td>Swiss (1&#39;234&#39;567.891)</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.plain</code></td>\n<td>Plain (1234567.891): no thousands separator, a decimal dot</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.settingsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals</code></td>\n<td>Decimals › - a submenu</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.asIs</code></td>\n<td>As is: the decimals the number has</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.0</code></td>\n<td>0: no decimals, rounded half away from zero</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.1</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.2</code></td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.3</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.4</code></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands</code></td>\n<td>Thousands separator › - a submenu</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.none</code></td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.space</code></td>\n<td>Space. Greyed out while the decimal mark is that character - for every separator</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.comma</code></td>\n<td>Comma</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.dot</code></td>\n<td>Dot</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.apostrophe</code></td>\n<td>Apostrophe</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimalMark</code></td>\n<td>Decimal mark › - a submenu</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimalMark.dot</code></td>\n<td>Dot. Greyed out while the thousands separator is that character</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimalMark.comma</code></td>\n<td>Comma</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.percent</code></td>\n<td>Percent, a toggle: the number times 100 with <code> %</code></td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.settings</code></td>\n<td>Column settings… - opens the grid config dialog on this column (<code>gridInterface.openColumnSettings(field)</code>): its display format, a text column&#39;s template and link, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">The grid config dialog</a></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.shortcuts</code></td>\n<td>Keyboard shortcuts… - opens the keyboard shortcuts help (<code>gridInterface.openShortcutHelp()</code>), its hint the key that opens it anywhere in the grid: Ctrl+/ (⌘/ on a Mac). See <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">The keyboard shortcuts help</a></td>\n<td><code>shortcutHelp</code> (the default)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.import</code></td>\n<td>Import rows… - opens the import dialog (<code>gridInterface.openImport()</code>): a CSV or Excel file, or rows pasted from a spreadsheet, as new rows, see <a href=\"#guide/import\">Import</a></td>\n<td><code>importMenu</code>, the grid is editable and <code>edit</code> allows <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate</code></td>\n<td>Aggregate › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.sum</code></td>\n<td>Sum - a toggle with a ✓ when on: the column&#39;s sum in the group rows and the totals row, see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a></td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.avg</code></td>\n<td>Average, a toggle</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.min</code></td>\n<td>Min, a toggle</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.max</code></td>\n<td>Max, a toggle</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.count</code></td>\n<td>Count, a toggle: the values that are not blank</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.totalsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.totalsRow</code></td>\n<td>Totals row, a toggle</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping</code></td>\n<td>Grouping › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.expandAll</code></td>\n<td>Expand all</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.collapseAll</code></td>\n<td>Collapse all</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear all: no grouping</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-menu-menu_idsfilter\">Filter menu (<code>MENU_IDS.filter</code>)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter</code></td>\n<td>Filter › - a submenu, first in the menu: the entries below up to the value list</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator</code></td>\n<td>Operator › - a submenu in Filter ›, the operator in use has a ✓. The column&#39;s operators the header filter&#39;s input takes: its <code>filterOperators</code>, else its type&#39;s</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.equal</code></td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.notEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Not Equal</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThan</code></td>\n<td>Less than</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Less than or equal</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThan</code></td>\n<td>Greater than</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Greater than or equal</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.between</code></td>\n<td>Between: typed as <code>from..to</code></td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.contains</code></td>\n<td>Contains</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.doesNotContain</code></td>\n<td>Does not contain</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.beginWith</code></td>\n<td>Begins with</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.endWith</code></td>\n<td>Ends with</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.matchCase</code></td>\n<td>Match case - a toggle with a ✓ (<code>headerFilters[field].caseSensitive</code>). On and greyed out, with the hint &quot;always&quot;, for a column with <code>filterCaseSensitive</code></td>\n<td>a text field, a lookup column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.blank</code></td>\n<td>Blank › - a submenu in Filter ›</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isBlank</code></td>\n<td>Is blank</td>\n<td>the column has <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isNotBlank</code></td>\n<td>Is not blank</td>\n<td>the column has <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear Filter: the column&#39;s conditions</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clearAll</code></td>\n<td>Clear All Filters</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advancedSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advanced</code></td>\n<td>Advanced Filter…: opens the filter editor</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values</code></td>\n<td>the value list: a search box, &quot;Select all&quot; and a checkbox per value, &quot;Cancel&quot; and &quot;Apply&quot;. One <code>MenuBlock</code>, its texts are <code>filter.values.*</code></td>\n<td>a text field, a lookup column - that has <code>IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.range</code></td>\n<td>the range block: the operators of Operator › as a row of buttons (=, ≠, &lt;, ≤, &gt;, ≥, Between), an input - From and To for Between - &quot;Cancel&quot; and &quot;Apply&quot;: as the operator picked in Operator › and the value typed in the header filter. One <code>MenuBlock</code>, its texts are <code>filter.range.*</code></td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field, not a lookup column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates</code></td>\n<td>the quick dates, under the range block: a button each - Today, Yesterday, This week ... - one click filters on its days and closes the menu. One <code>MenuBlock</code>, its texts are <code>filter.dates.*</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">Quick dates</a></td>\n<td>a date or datetime field with <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>datePresets</code> not <code>false</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-header-filter-menu\">Filtering</a> for what the filter menu&#39;s entries do.</p>\n<p>The operator indicator in a header filter cell opens a menu of its own, the operator choice: the\nitems of Operator › (<code>filter.operator.*</code>), with the context of the filter menu (<code>menu: &#39;filter&#39;</code>). The\n<code>filter</code> function of the <code>menus</code> hook is not called for it: it changes the filter menu only. Offer\nfewer operators with the column&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code>, both show those; turn the indicator off with\n<code>filterOperatorIndicator: false</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-menu-menu_idscolumn\">Column menu (<code>MENU_IDS.column</code>)</h3>\n<p>Right click on a column number, the row of numbers under the header filters, or Shift+F10 on one with\nthe focus. <em>Select ›</em>, then the copies, flat: a <em>Copy ›</em> around them would only add a step. The\ncolumns are copied as the row menu copies rows - tab separated text and an html table, a header line\nof the labels with <code>copyHeaders</code> - every displayed data row; the names as one line of text, a tab\nbetween them. See <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-column-menu\">Selection and clipboard</a>. The column\nnumbers have no selection mode: <em>Select ›</em> is always there.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>column.select</code></td>\n<td>Select › - a submenu: what Shift and Ctrl do on a column number, for a finger and every input</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.column</code></td>\n<td>This column: the column in or out, as Ctrl+click, a ✓ while it is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.columnsTo</code></td>\n<td>Columns from N to here: from the anchor (N, the column clicked last without Shift) to this one, as Shift+click. Greyed out without an anchor (N is this column then) and when the anchor is this column</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.all</code></td>\n<td>All columns. Greyed out when every column is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear column selection: <code>selectColumns([])</code>. Greyed out with none selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.separator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.column</code></td>\n<td>Copy column (all rows): the column right clicked. A column of stacked fields: each of its fields</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td>Copy selected columns (all rows): the selected columns, <code>getSelectedColumns()</code>. Greyed out with no column selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.namesSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.headers</code></td>\n<td>Copy header names: the label of every field of the layout</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.header</code></td>\n<td>Copy header name: the label of the column right clicked, each of its fields</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.selectedHeaders</code></td>\n<td>Copy selected header names: the labels of the selected columns. Greyed out with no column selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// the column menu without the copies of the selected columns, and an entry of your own\nconst columnHooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: {\n        column: (defaults, context) =&gt; [\n            ...defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; !entry.id.startsWith('column.copy.selected')),\n            { id: 'my.fit', label: 'Fit column width', action: () =&gt; context.gridInterface.fitColumns([context.field]) }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type GridHooks, MENU_IDS } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a row menu with the copy submenu only\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: { row: (defaults) =&gt; defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; entry.id.startsWith('row.copy')) }\n};\n\nMENU_IDS.row.includes('row.paste.cell'); // true</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-menu-of-your-own-openmenu-and-closemenu\">A menu of your own: <code>openMenu()</code> and <code>closeMenu()</code></h2>\n<p><code>gridInterface.openMenu(at, entries)</code> opens a menu of your own, with the same entries, submenus too:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('cell-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.attribute !== 'fruit') {\n        return;\n    }\n    const pick = (fruit: string | null) =&gt; {\n        data.rowData.fruit = fruit;\n        gridInterface.refresh();\n    };\n    gridInterface.openMenu(data.originalEvent, [\n        { type: 'section', id: 'fruit', label: 'Pick a fruit:' },\n        { id: 'fruit.apple', label: 'Apple', action: () =&gt; pick('Apple') },\n        { id: 'fruit.banana', label: 'Banana', action: () =&gt; pick('Banana') },\n        {\n            type: 'submenu',\n            id: 'fruit.more',\n            label: 'More',\n            entries: [\n                { id: 'fruit.kiwi', label: 'Kiwi', action: () =&gt; pick('Kiwi') },\n                { id: 'fruit.mango', label: 'Mango', action: () =&gt; pick('Mango') }\n            ]\n        },\n        { type: 'separator', id: 'fruit.separator' },\n        { id: 'fruit.none', label: 'None', hint: 'clears the cell', action: () =&gt; pick(null) }\n    ]);\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>at</code> is where it opens:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a mouse event: its top left corner at the mouse</li>\n<li>a <code>MenuPosition</code>, <code>{ x, y }</code> in px from the window&#39;s left and top edges: its top left corner there</li>\n<li>an element: under the element, lined up with its left edge</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The menu is kept in the window, 4px from its edges - every menu of the grid, the long row menu too:\nit opens below the point, above it when there is no room below, and next to it (on its right, or on\nits left when there is no room on the right) when there is room neither below nor above. A menu taller\nthan the window gets the window&#39;s height and scrolls.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is one menu at a time: opening one closes the one before, the grid&#39;s own too.</li>\n<li>The grid closes it as its own menus: on a click outside it, on Esc, when another menu opens, on\nscroll (with <code>autoRemoveContextMenuOnScrollEvent: true</code>) and when the grid leaves the page, see\n<a href=\"#guide/menus/how-a-menu-opens-and-closes\">above</a>. Opened from a click handler, it stays open: that click does\nnot count as a click outside.</li>\n<li>Opened from an element in a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> of yours, or a popover, it is put in that element, so it can\nbe used there.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.closeMenu()</code> closes the open menu from code.</li>\n<li>The callbacks of its entries get a context with <code>menu: &#39;custom&#39;</code> and no field, row or cell: let\nthem use what they were made for, as <code>pick</code> above.</li>\n<li>Opened from the keyboard - a button that calls <code>openMenu()</code> on Enter - it takes the focus, on its\nfirst item; opened with the mouse it leaves the focus where it is. See <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-keyboard\">The keyboard</a>.</li>\n<li>It does nothing while the grid is not on the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-dropdown-grid-as-a-block\">A dropdown grid as a block</h2>\n<p>To pick a cell&#39;s value from a list, a column&#39;s <code>editor</code> does it all - a select or a dropdown grid, its\nbutton, the keys, writing the value as one undo step: see <a href=\"#guide/lookups\">Lookups</a>. This is the way to\ndo it yourself, for a picker the editor does not have.</p>\n<p>A <code>MenuBlock</code> shows an element of yours in the menu: a second grid as a dropdown, a form, a color\npicker. The grid puts it on the page, places it and closes it as any menu - Esc too, also when the\nfocus is in the block. A click in the block does not close the menu: call <code>closeMenu()</code> when the user\nhas picked. The dropdown grid&#39;s own menus open over it.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type GridConfig, type GridElement, GridInterface } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// in the main grid's config:\n// readonly: false, columns: { company: { readonly: true, focusButton: 'SHOW_IF_GRID_NOT_READONLY' } }\n\nconst companies = new Datasource();\ncompanies.setData([{ name: 'ACME' }, { name: 'GLOBEX' }, { name: 'INITECH' }]);\n\nconst dropdownConfig: GridConfig = {\n    panelHeight: 0,\n    footerHeight: 10,\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 250 }] }\n};\n\ngridInterface.on('cell-focus-button-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.attribute !== 'company') {\n        return;\n    }\n\n    const dropdown = document.createElement('simple-html-grid') as GridElement;\n    dropdown.style.width = '280px';\n    dropdown.style.height = '250px';\n    dropdown.connectInterface(new GridInterface(dropdownConfig, companies));\n\n    // a click on a company picks it: the cell's click made its row current\n    dropdown.addEventListener('click', (event) =&gt; {\n        if (event.target instanceof HTMLElement &amp;&amp; event.target.classList.contains('simple-html-grid-cell-input')) {\n            gridInterface.closeMenu();\n            data.rowData.company = companies.currentEntity?.name;\n            gridInterface.refresh();\n            data.target.focus();\n        }\n    });\n\n    gridInterface.openMenu(data.cell, [{ type: 'block', id: 'company-picker', element: dropdown }]);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Give the element its size, the menu&#39;s frame gives it a border and a shadow. The column is readonly,\nso the dropdown is the only way to change the value. <code>allowPasteClearOnly: true</code> instead of\n<code>readonly</code> keeps paste and clear working too.</p>\n"},{"id":"migrating-from-v5","title":"Migrating from v5","html":"<p>v6 changes the API: the datasource knows its fields, the grid config is split from its state, events\nare subscribed one by one, and filters, menus and options are plain data. The config and the\ndatasource&#39;s fields are plain JSON: formatters are named by id, and the functions of a grid are\nhooks next to the config. TypeScript points to most of the places to change.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md\">MIGRATION2V6.md</a> has every breaking change with code before and after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#package\">Package</a>: the css path, installing from git</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#renamed\">Renamed</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#removed\">Removed</a>: <code>Formater</code> is\n<code>Formatter</code>, <code>deSelectAll</code> is <code>deselectAll</code> ...</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#events\">Events</a>: <code>on()</code> instead of <code>addEventListener</code>, kebab case names</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#group-rows-are-not-entities\">Group rows are not entities</a>: <code>GroupRow</code>,\n<code>isGroupRow()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#fields-and-formatters\">Fields and formatters</a>: <code>new Datasource(options)</code>, types in\n<code>fields</code>, <code>date</code> and <code>datetime</code>, formatters per field by id, <code>registerFormatter()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#filters\">Filters</a>: one filter format, <code>and()</code> / <code>or()</code> / <code>where()</code>, and\n<a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#saved-configs\">a converter for saved v5 filters</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#options-objects-instead-of-boolean-flags\">Options objects instead of boolean flags</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#change-tracking-getchanges\">Change tracking</a>: the typed <code>getChanges()</code>, a new\nrow marked for deletion stays (marked), <code>unmarkForDeletion()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#validation-mandatory-is-the-required-rule\">Validation</a>: <code>mandatory</code> is the\nfield&#39;s <code>required</code> rule, <code>registerValidator()</code>, <code>validate()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#grid-config\">Grid config</a>: <code>columns</code> and <code>layout</code>, the state, and\n<a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#a-v5-config-converted\">a converter for a v5 config</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#setup-callbacks-and-methods-of-the-grid\">Setup, callbacks and methods of the grid</a>:\n<code>createGrid()</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> / <code>cellClass</code> hooks, <code>updateConfig()</code>, <code>updateHooks()</code>, renamed\nmethods</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#menus\">Menus</a>: the <code>menus</code> hook, <code>openMenu()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#texts\">Texts</a>: every text has an id, <code>setTexts()</code> and <code>GridConfig.texts</code>;\n<code>tooltipText</code> is <code>texts</code> with <code>.tooltip</code> ids, <code>OPERATORS</code> and the month and day names are texts</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#other-behaviour-changes\">Other behaviour changes</a>: <code>currentEntity</code> after\n<code>setData()</code>, <code>selectAll()</code>, <code>removeGroup()</code>, <code>expandGroup()</code>, grouping always sorted</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#internals-are-no-longer-in-the-typings\">Internals are no longer in the typings</a>\nand <a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#types-are-stricter\">Types are stricter</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below are the ten changes almost every app has to make.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1-css-and-setup\">1. Css and setup</h2>\n<p>The css is imported from the package root, and <code>createGrid()</code> replaces the element, its class and\n<code>connectInterface()</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nimport '@simple-html/grid/dist/grid.css';\n\nconst gridInterface = new GridInterface(gridConfig, datasource);\nconst element = document.createElement('simple-html-grid') as GridElement;\nelement.style.width = '100%';\nelement.style.height = '100%';\nelement.classList.add('simple-html-grid');\nelement.connectInterface(gridInterface);\ncontainer.appendChild(element);</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { createGrid, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\n\ndeclare const gridConfig: GridConfig;\n\nconst grid = createGrid(host, { config: gridConfig, datasource });\ngrid.destroy(); // when done with it, new in v6</code></pre>\n<p>See <a href=\"#guide/getting-started\">Getting started</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-2-the-datasource-takes-one-options-object-with-the-types\">2. The datasource takes one options object, with the types</h2>\n<p><code>new Datasource(new DataContainer(key), options)</code> is <code>new Datasource({ key, ... })</code>. The types of\nthe fields move from the grid config&#39;s <code>attributes</code> and <code>setDates()</code> to <code>fields</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nconst datasource = new Datasource(new DataContainer('id'));\ndatasource.setDates(['born']);\n// and in the grid config: selectionMode: 'single',\n// attributes: [{ attribute: 'age', type: 'number' }, { attribute: 'born', type: 'date' }]</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: { age: 'number', born: 'date' }, // a field not listed is text\n    selectionMode: 'single'\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The selection mode is the datasource&#39;s: <code>GridConfig.selectionMode</code> is removed. v5&#39;s grid set its own\n(default <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code>) on the datasource, replacing the datasource&#39;s. See\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/the-selection-mode\">Datasource</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-3-columns-and-layout-replace-attributes-and-columns\">3. <code>columns</code> and <code>layout</code> replace <code>attributes</code> and <code>columns*</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nconst gridConfig: GridConfig = {\n    columnsPinnedLeft: [{ rows: ['name'], width: 150 }],\n    columnsCenter: [{ rows: ['age', 'born'], width: 100 }],\n    attributes: [\n        { attribute: 'name', label: 'Name', placeHolderFilter: 'search' },\n        { attribute: 'age', readonly: true }\n    ]\n};</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst gridConfig: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { label: 'Name', placeholderFilter: 'search' },\n        age: { readonly: true }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        left: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }],\n        center: [{ fields: ['age', 'born'], width: 100 }]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p><code>placeHolderRow</code> and <code>placeHolderFilter</code> are <code>placeholderRow</code> and <code>placeholderFilter</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<p><strong><code>mandatory</code> is a validation rule of the datasource field.</strong> v5&#39;s <code>mandatory: true</code> only drew\nstripes; v6 validates the field - the edits, and <code>validate()</code> before a save - and draws the same\nstripes. A config with <code>mandatory</code> throws, naming the field:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nattributes: [{ attribute: 'company', mandatory: true }]</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst companies = new Datasource({ fields: { company: { validate: ['required'] } } });</code></pre>\n<p><code>required</code> takes 0 as missing, like the filter&#39;s <code>IS_BLANK</code>: a number field of 0 is striped now,\nand a checkbox that is unchecked (<code>false</code>) is not. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-4-events-on\">4. Events: <code>on()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.addEventListener({\n    handleEvent: (e) =&gt; {\n        if (e.type === 'selectionChange') {\n            updateToolbar();\n        }\n        return true; // keep listening\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\ndeclare function updateToolbar(): void;\n\nconst off = datasource.on('selection-change', () =&gt; updateToolbar());\noff(); // unsubscribe</code></pre>\n<p>The handler gets the payload, not <code>{ type, data }</code>. What it returns is ignored. <code>selectionChange</code>,\n<code>currentEntity</code> and <code>gridConnected</code> are kebab case now: <code>selection-change</code>, <code>current-entity</code> and\n<code>grid-connected</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-5-filters-one-format-and-builders\">5. Filters: one format and builders</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.filter({\n    type: 'GROUP',\n    logicalOperator: 'AND',\n    filterArguments: [\n        { type: 'CONDITION', logicalOperator: 'NONE', attribute: 'age', attributeType: 'number', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18, valueType: 'VALUE' }\n    ]\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { and, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.filter(and(where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 18)));\ndatasource.filter(null); // no filter. In v5 filter(null) ran the filter again</code></pre>\n<p>The type comes from the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code>. A filter saved by v5 is ignored, MIGRATION2V6.md has a\n<a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#saved-configs\">converter</a>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-6-options-objects-instead-of-boolean-flags\">6. Options objects instead of boolean flags</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.setData(rows, true);\ndatasource.sort({ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }, true);\ndatasource.markForDeletion(null, true);\nconst dataRows = datasource.getRows(true);</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\ndeclare const rows: object[];\n\ndatasource.setData(rows, { add: true });\ndatasource.sort({ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }, { add: true });\ndatasource.markAllForDeletion();\nconst dataRows = datasource.getDataRows();</code></pre>\n<p>A boolean passed the v5 way throws a <code>TypeError</code> that shows the v6 way to write it.</p>\n<p><strong><code>setData()</code> runs the filter that is set</strong> on the new rows. v5 showed them all (<code>reRunFilter</code>\ndefaulted to <code>false</code>), also the ones the filter leaves out. <code>setData(rows, { refilter: false })</code> does\nwhat v5 did. See <a href=\"#guide/datasource/data-in-setdata\">Datasource</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-7-formatters-per-field\">7. Formatters per field</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.setNumberFormater(NumberFormaterComma);\ndatasource.setDateFormater(DateFormaterDDMMYYYY);</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst orders = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: 'number', ordered: 'date' },\n    formatters: { number: 'numberComma', date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>Formater</code> is spelled <code>Formatter</code>, and a formatter is an object with <code>format</code> and <code>parse</code>. The\noptions name it by its id: the built-in ones are registered from the start, register your own with\n<code>registerFormatter(&#39;myId&#39;, formatter)</code> and give <code>formatter: &#39;myId&#39;</code> to the field. See\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-8-the-state-getstate-and-setstate\">8. The state: <code>getState()</code> and <code>setState()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nlocalStorage.setItem('grid', JSON.stringify(gridInterface.saveConfig()));\ngridInterface.loadConfig(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('grid')));\ngridInterface.loadInitConfig();</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nlocalStorage.setItem('grid', JSON.stringify(gridInterface.getState()));\ngridInterface.setState(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('grid') ?? '{}'));\ngridInterface.resetState();</code></pre>\n<p>The state has no options: change an option with <code>updateConfig()</code>. A grid that starts sorted,\ngrouped or filtered sets it in <code>initialState</code>, not as <code>sorting</code> / <code>grouping</code> / <code>filter</code> in the\nconfig. See <a href=\"#guide/state\">State</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-9-callbacks-are-hooks-renamed-methods\">9. Callbacks are hooks, renamed methods</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ngridInterface.readonlySetter((attribute, rowData) =&gt; (attribute === 'company' &amp;&amp; !rowData.isActive ? true : null));\ngridInterface.cellAppendClassSetter((attribute, rowData) =&gt;\n    attribute === 'balance' &amp;&amp; rowData.balance &lt; 0 ? { inputClass: 'red', dimmedClass: '' } : { inputClass: '', dimmedClass: '' }\n);\ngridInterface.triggerScrollEvent();\ngridInterface.triggerRebuild();</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst gridConfig: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['company'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['balance'], width: 100 }] }\n};\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    isReadonly: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'company' &amp;&amp; !row.isActive ? true : null),\n    cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.balance &lt; 0 ? { input: 'red' } : null)\n};\nconst grid = createGrid(host, { config: gridConfig, datasource, hooks });\n\ngrid.updateHooks({ cellClass: undefined }); // was cellAppendClassSetter(null)\ngrid.refresh(); // was triggerScrollEvent()\ngrid.rebuild(); // was triggerRebuild()</code></pre>\n<p>The row comes first. The hooks are given next to the config, so the config stays plain JSON.\n<code>getAttributeColumns()</code> is <code>getLayoutFields()</code>, <code>getOptionalAttributes()</code> is <code>getHiddenFields()</code>.\nSee <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-10-menus\">10. Menus</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ngridInterface.contextMenuCustom(data.originalEvent, [{ value: 'Apple', label: 'Apple', isHeader: false }], data.cell, (value) =&gt; {\n    data.rowData.fruit = value;\n    gridInterface.triggerScrollEvent();\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\ngridInterface.on('cell-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    gridInterface.openMenu(data.originalEvent, [\n        {\n            id: 'fruit.apple',\n            label: 'Apple',\n            action: () =&gt; {\n                data.rowData.fruit = 'Apple';\n                gridInterface.refresh();\n            }\n        }\n    ]);\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>setContextMenuElement(element)</code> is a block of a menu: <code>openMenu(cell, [{ type: &#39;block&#39;, id, element }])</code>.\nThe grid&#39;s own menus are changed with the <code>menus</code> hook. See <a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-group-rows\">Group rows</h2>\n<p>One more change that hits code that reads rows: when grouped, the group rows in <code>getRows()</code> are a\n<code>GroupRow</code>, not an entity. <code>isGroupRow(row)</code> replaces <code>row.__group</code>, and <code>getDataRows()</code> gives the\ndata rows only. See <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/group-rows\">Sorting and grouping</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"texts","title":"Texts","html":"<p>Every text the grid shows has an id: the menus, the filter editor, the column chooser, the footer,\nthe tooltips, the date picker and the labels a screen reader reads - and so do the texts the\ndatasource makes, the validation messages and <code>getFilterString()</code>. <code>TEXTS</code> has them all, with their\nEnglish text. Change them with <code>setTexts()</code> for the page, and with <code>texts</code> in the config for one\ngrid. A translation is a JSON object of texts by id: it can be stored with the config, in a backend.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"translation\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-translating\">Translating</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts, type Texts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a text for each id to change, the rest stay English\nconst norwegian: Texts = {\n    'row.copy': 'Kopier',\n    'footer.summary.count': 'Antall: {count}',\n    'filterEditor.addCondition.tooltip': 'Legg til betingelse',\n    'validation.required': 'Må fylles ut'\n};\n\n// once, when the page loads: before the datasources and grids are made\nsetTexts(norwegian);</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>TEXTS</code> is every id with its English text: <code>JSON.stringify(TEXTS, null, 2)</code> is a file to hand a\ntranslator. The ids are <a href=\"#guide/texts/all-ids\">below</a>.</li>\n<li><code>Texts</code> takes some of the ids. Typed <code>Record&lt;TextId, string&gt;</code> instead, a translation must have every\none: the compiler names those missing, also after an update of the grid adds ids. The\n<a href=\"#sample/translation\">Translation sample</a> does that.</li>\n<li>A translation is plain JSON, it can come from a backend as it is:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst response = await fetch('/api/texts/nb');\nsetTexts(await response.json());</code></pre>\n<p><code>setTexts()</code> throws a <code>TypeError</code> when it gets something that is not an object, or a text that is not\na string - and so does a grid given such <code>texts</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-where-a-text-comes-from\">Where a text comes from</h2>\n<p>A text is looked up in this order, the first that has the id wins:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>GridConfig.texts</code> - the texts of one grid, for a grid that differs from the rest of the page</li>\n<li><code>setTexts()</code> - the texts of the page</li>\n<li><code>TEXTS</code> - the English defaults</li>\n</ol>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({ 'row.copy': 'Kopier', 'row.paste': 'Lim inn' }); // every grid of the page\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] },\n    texts: { 'row.copy': 'Copy' } // this grid: Copy and Lim inn\n};\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<p><strong>The datasource has no grid</strong>: the texts it makes - the validation messages (<code>validation.*</code>),\n<code>getFilterString()</code> (<code>filterString.*</code>) and the name of the group of blank values (<code>grouping.blank</code>) -\ncome from <code>setTexts()</code> and the defaults only. A grid&#39;s <code>texts</code> does not change them, also not where\nthat grid shows them. There is no <code>texts</code> option on the datasource: the validation runs in its\n<code>DataContainer</code>, which datasources can share, and a page has one language.</p>\n<p><strong>Each replaces what was there</strong>: <code>setTexts()</code> replaces the texts set before, so an id left out shows\nits default again and <code>setTexts({})</code> is English. <code>updateConfig({ texts })</code> replaces the grid&#39;s texts\nas a whole, <code>texts: undefined</code> removes them. The object given is copied by <code>setTexts()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-texts-with-values\">Texts with values</h2>\n<p>A text with <code>{name}</code> in it gets a value when it is shown: <code>&#39;footer.summary.count&#39;: &#39;Count: {count}&#39;</code>\nshows <code>Count: 3</code>. The tables <a href=\"#guide/texts/all-ids\">below</a> name the values of each text. Keep the names in a\ntranslation, a value can go anywhere in the text; a name the text has no value for is shown as it is.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({ 'footer.summary.count': '{count} celler', 'footer.rowCount': 'Viser {shown} av {total}' });</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-ids\">The ids</h2>\n<p>The ids are grouped by area, the area first. Within an area:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A menu entry&#39;s text has the entry&#39;s id</strong>, see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-built-in-ids\">Menus</a>: the text of the\nentry <code>row.copy.cell</code> is <code>row.copy.cell</code>, the label of the submenu <code>row.copy</code> is <code>row.copy</code>. The\ntext in parentheses after an entry, its hint, is <code>&lt;id&gt;.hint</code>. The separators and the blocks - the\nvalue list (<code>filter.values</code>) and the range block (<code>filter.range</code>) - have no text of their own, the\nparts of the value list are <code>filter.values.*</code>, those of the range block <code>filter.range.*</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A tooltip is <code>&lt;id&gt;.tooltip</code></strong>: <code>filterEditor.filterOnly</code> is the button, <code>filterEditor.filterOnly.tooltip</code>\nits tooltip. A tooltip of an icon has no text without <code>.tooltip</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>An operator has a name in each place it is shown</strong>, the <code>FilterOperator</code> at the end:\n<code>filterEditor.operator.EQUAL</code> in the filter editor, <code>footer.operator.EQUAL</code> on a chip in the footer\n(symbols, so a chip stays short) and <code>filterString.operator.EQUAL</code> in <code>getFilterString()</code>. AND and OR\nas well: <code>filterEditor.operatorAnd</code>, <code>footer.and</code>, <code>filterString.and</code>. The filter menu&#39;s operators\nare menu entries: <code>filter.operator.equal</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A text that names a key has a text for a Mac, <code>&lt;id&gt;.mac</code></strong>: <code>row.edit.undo.hint</code> is <code>Ctrl+Z</code>,\n<code>row.edit.undo.hint.mac</code> is <code>⌘Z</code>. The grid shows the <code>.mac</code> one on a Mac - and an iPad or iPhone -\nfrom the platform the browser gives (<code>navigator.userAgentData.platform</code>, else <code>navigator.platform</code>).\nThe grid takes both keys everywhere (Ctrl+Z and Cmd+Z), the texts say what a user there presses.\nTranslate both: a <code>.mac</code> text left out shows its English default on a Mac. They are\n<code>row.edit.undo.hint</code>, <code>row.edit.redo.hint</code>, <code>find.previous.tooltip</code>, <code>find.matchCase.tooltip</code>,\n<code>cell.multiline.hint</code>, <code>filterEditor.undo.tooltip</code>, <code>filterEditor.redo.tooltip</code>,\n<code>header.shortcuts.hint</code>, <code>shortcuts.hint</code> and the modifiers of the keyboard shortcuts help,\n<code>shortcuts.key.ctrl</code>, <code>shortcuts.key.alt</code> and <code>shortcuts.key.shift</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-names-of-the-months-and-days\">The names of the months and days</h3>\n<p>The date picker shows the names of the months, and of the days over its columns. Each name is a text\nof its own - <code>datePicker.month.january</code> to <code>datePicker.month.december</code>, <code>datePicker.day.sunday</code> to\n<code>datePicker.day.saturday</code> - not a list in the date picker&#39;s config. So a translation is one flat\nobject of strings for every part of the grid, and <code>setTexts()</code> reaches the date pickers of every grid\nwith the rest. The day names are in javascript&#39;s order, Sunday first; <code>weekStart</code> picks the first\ncolumn.</p>\n<p>A <code>DateInterface</code> of your own shows the same texts: the page&#39;s, or its own in <code>IDateConfig.texts</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-messages-of-your-own\">Messages of your own</h2>\n<p>A validator returns the message, or a text id: the built-ins return the <code>validation.*</code> ids. Return an\nid of your own and give it a text with <code>setTexts()</code>, and the message is translated like the\nbuilt-ins&#39;. A rule&#39;s <code>message</code> can be a text id too. An id no text is found for is shown as it is, so\na message written out works as well.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, registerValidator, setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nregisterValidator('phoneNO', (value) =&gt;\n    typeof value === 'string' &amp;&amp; value !== '' &amp;&amp; !/^\\d{8}$/.test(value) ? 'validation.phoneNO' : null\n);\n\nsetTexts({\n    'validation.phoneNO': 'Må være 8 siffer',\n    'age.tooOld': 'For gammel, høyst {value}'\n});\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        phone: { validate: ['phoneNO'] },\n        age: { type: 'number', validate: [{ id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'age.tooOld' }] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>See <a href=\"#guide/editing/messages\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-when-a-changed-text-shows\">When a changed text shows</h2>\n<p>Nothing is shown again when a text changes, it is read when what shows it is made:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>what</th>\n<th>shows a changed text</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>the grid: header, rows, footer, the find bar</td>\n<td>after its next rebuild. <code>updateConfig({ texts })</code> rebuilds, after <code>setTexts()</code> call <code>gridInterface.rebuild()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a menu, the column chooser, the filter editor, the date picker</td>\n<td>the next time it opens (the filter editor also when it is rendered again after an edit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getFilterString()</code></td>\n<td>on its next call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a validation message</td>\n<td>when the value is validated: an edit of the field, or <code>validate()</code>. An error already there keeps its text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the name of the group of blank values</td>\n<td>the next time the rows are grouped: a filter, a sort, <code>setData()</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>So <code>setTexts()</code> goes before the datasources and grids are made. To switch the language of a page\nthat is shown:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts, type Texts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const swedish: Texts;\n\nsetTexts(swedish);\ngridInterface.rebuild(); // every grid of the page</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-not-texts\">Not texts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>What the config and the data have: the column labels (<code>columns.&lt;field&gt;.label</code>, <code>FieldConfig.label</code>),\n<code>placeholderRow</code> and <code>placeholderFilter</code>, the group titles, the values.</li>\n<li>The placeholders of the date formatters, <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> and <code>DD.MM.YYYY</code> in an empty date header\nfilter: give the column a <code>placeholderFilter</code>, or use a formatter of your own with another\n<code>placeholder</code> (see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-formatter-of-your-own\">Formatting</a>).</li>\n<li>The hints of Format › in the header menu: examples of each format, made from today&#39;s date and from\n1234567.891 - in the language of <code>format.locale</code>.</li>\n<li>Symbols and numbers: <code>✕</code> closes the filter editor and the find bar, <code>↑</code> and <code>↓</code> of the find bar,\n<code>✓</code> marks a menu item that is on (an aggregate, the dimmed values, the operator in use), <code>›</code> an item\nthat opens a submenu, <code>?</code> a footer chip without a field or operator, <code>(12)</code> the count of a value in\nthe value list.</li>\n<li><code>headerTitle</code> of a <code>DateInterface</code> calendar: your own text.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-all-ids\">All ids</h2>\n<h3 id=\"section-row-menu\">Row menu</h3>\n<p>Right click on a cell or a row number.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>row.copy</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy</code></td>\n<td>the copy submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>Cell Value</code></td>\n<td>copies the value of the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.column</code></td>\n<td><code>Column</code></td>\n<td>copies the column of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.column.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.row</code></td>\n<td><code>Row</code></td>\n<td>copies the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.row.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td><code>Row</code></td>\n<td>copies the selected columns of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.selectedColumns.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows/col</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.range</code></td>\n<td><code>Range</code></td>\n<td>copies the last range of cells, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter</code></td>\n<td>the filter submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.replace</code></td>\n<td><code>Replace filter</code></td>\n<td>filters on the value of the cell, replacing the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.replace.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>all</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.add</code></td>\n<td><code>Add to filter</code></td>\n<td>ANDs the value of the cell with the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.add.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>and</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view</code></td>\n<td><code>View</code></td>\n<td>the view submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.dimRepeatedValues</code></td>\n<td><code>Use dimmed values</code></td>\n<td>turns <code>dimRepeatedValues</code> on and off, with a ✓ in front while it is on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.changedOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Changed rows only</code></td>\n<td>turns <code>datasource.filterChangedOnly()</code> on and off: the new, edited and deleted rows only. A ✓ in front while it is on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste</code></td>\n<td>the paste submenu, an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.current</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste cell</code></td>\n<td>pastes into the cell right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste cell</code></td>\n<td>pastes into the column of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.cell.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.range</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste range</code></td>\n<td>pastes the clipboard into the ranges of cells, as Ctrl+V</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.fillDown</code></td>\n<td><code>Fill down</code></td>\n<td>fills down, as Ctrl+D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.fillDown.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+D</code></td>\n<td>its hint, the key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.fillDown.hint.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⌘D</code></td>\n<td>its hint on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.newRows</code></td>\n<td><code>As new rows…</code></td>\n<td>opens the import dialog with the rows on the clipboard (<code>GridConfig.importMenu</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/texts/import-dialog\">Import dialog</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear</code></td>\n<td>the clear submenu, an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.current</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear cell</code></td>\n<td>empties the cell right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear cell</code></td>\n<td>empties the column of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.cell.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.range</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear range</code></td>\n<td>empties the cells of the ranges, as Delete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete</code></td>\n<td>the delete submenu, an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.row</code></td>\n<td><code>Row</code></td>\n<td>marks the row right clicked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.rows</code></td>\n<td><code>Rows</code></td>\n<td>marks the selected rows for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.rows.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.undo</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo</code></td>\n<td>takes the mark off the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.undo.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo / Redo</code></td>\n<td>the row menu&#39;s submenu of undo, redo and the reverts, in an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo last change</code></td>\n<td>undoes the last step of the undo history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+Z</code></td>\n<td>its hint, the key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo.hint.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⌘Z</code></td>\n<td>its hint on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo</code></td>\n<td><code>Redo</code></td>\n<td>redoes the last step undone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+Y</code></td>\n<td>its hint, the key. Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo.hint.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⇧⌘Z</code></td>\n<td>its hint on a Mac. ⌘Y redoes too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertCell</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo cell edit</code></td>\n<td>puts the original value of the cell back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRow</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo row edits</code></td>\n<td>puts the original values of the row right clicked back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo row edits</code></td>\n<td>puts the original values of the selected rows back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRows.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size</code></td>\n<td><code>Size</code></td>\n<td>the size submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitColumn</code></td>\n<td><code>Fit column to this cell</code></td>\n<td>the column as wide as the text of the cell right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitRow</code></td>\n<td><code>Fit row height</code></td>\n<td>the row right clicked as high as its multiline cells need</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Fit row height</code></td>\n<td>the selected rows and the rows of the ranges of cells, the same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitRows.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitAllRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Fit all rows</code></td>\n<td>every row the filter lets through, the same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.resetRow</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset row height</code></td>\n<td>the row right clicked back to the height of the layout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.resetAllRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset all row heights</code></td>\n<td>every row back to the height of the layout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export</code></td>\n<td><code>Export</code></td>\n<td>the export submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.excel</code></td>\n<td><code>Excel, all rows</code></td>\n<td>downloads the rows the filter lets through as an .xlsx file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.excelSelected</code></td>\n<td><code>Excel, selected rows</code></td>\n<td>downloads the selected rows as an .xlsx file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select</code></td>\n<td><code>Select</code></td>\n<td>the select submenu: what Shift and Ctrl do with the mouse, for a finger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.row</code></td>\n<td><code>This row</code></td>\n<td>the row in or out of the selection, with a ✓ in front while it is selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.rowsTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Rows from {row} to here</code></td>\n<td>selects the rows from the anchor to this row, <code>{row}</code>: the anchor&#39;s row number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.allRows</code></td>\n<td><code>All rows</code></td>\n<td>selects every row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.rangeTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Range from the active cell to here</code></td>\n<td>a range of cells from the active cell to this cell, as Shift+click</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.addCell</code></td>\n<td><code>Add this cell to the ranges</code></td>\n<td>a range of this cell added, as Ctrl+click</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear selection</code></td>\n<td>no row selected, no range of cells</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-header-menu\">Header menu</h3>\n<p>Right click on a header label.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pin</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin</code></td>\n<td>the pin submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeft</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin left</code></td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeftCopy</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin left (copy)</code></td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRight</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin right</code></td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRightCopy</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin right (copy)</code></td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.unpin</code></td>\n<td><code>Unpin</code></td>\n<td>pinned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.hide</code></td>\n<td><code>Hide column</code></td>\n<td>takes the column out of the layout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.removeSorting</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove sorting</code></td>\n<td>keeps the sort of the grouped fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooser</code></td>\n<td><code>Column chooser…</code></td>\n<td>opens the column chooser</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size</code></td>\n<td><code>Size</code></td>\n<td>the size submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.column</code></td>\n<td><code>Fit column width</code></td>\n<td>fits the column to its content and its label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.all</code></td>\n<td><code>Fit all columns</code></td>\n<td>fits every column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.resetColumn</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset column width</code></td>\n<td>the column back to the width of the config</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.resetAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset all column widths</code></td>\n<td>every column back to the width of the config</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format</code></td>\n<td><code>Format</code></td>\n<td>the format submenu: how the column&#39;s cells show the value, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.default</code></td>\n<td><code>Default</code></td>\n<td>no display format: the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.iso</code></td>\n<td><code>ISO</code></td>\n<td>a date column: <code>2026-11-22</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegian</code></td>\n<td><code>Norwegian</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegianShort</code></td>\n<td><code>Norwegian, short year</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.26</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.us</code></td>\n<td><code>US</code></td>\n<td><code>11/22/2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.uk</code></td>\n<td><code>UK</code></td>\n<td><code>22/11/2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.medium</code></td>\n<td><code>Short month name</code></td>\n<td><code>Nov 22, 2026</code>, in the language of <code>format.locale</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.long</code></td>\n<td><code>Month name</code></td>\n<td><code>November 22, 2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.full</code></td>\n<td><code>Day and month name</code></td>\n<td><code>Sunday, November 22, 2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.isoTime</code></td>\n<td><code>ISO with time</code></td>\n<td>a datetime column: <code>2026-11-22 13:45</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.isoSeconds</code></td>\n<td><code>ISO with seconds</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-22 13:45:12</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegianTime</code></td>\n<td><code>Norwegian with time</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.2026 13:45</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.norwegianSeconds</code></td>\n<td><code>Norwegian with seconds</code></td>\n<td><code>22.11.2026 13:45:12</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.time</code></td>\n<td><code>Time</code></td>\n<td><code>13:45</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.timeSeconds</code></td>\n<td><code>Time with seconds</code></td>\n<td><code>13:45:12</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.weekday</code></td>\n<td><code>Day of week</code></td>\n<td><code>Sunday</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.weekIso</code></td>\n<td><code>Week, ISO</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-W47</code>, written as <code>format.weekIso</code> says</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.weekUs</code></td>\n<td><code>Week, US</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-W48</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.month</code></td>\n<td><code>Month</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.quarter</code></td>\n<td><code>Quarter</code></td>\n<td><code>2026 Q4</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.year</code></td>\n<td><code>Year</code></td>\n<td><code>2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.daysUntil</code></td>\n<td><code>Days until</code></td>\n<td>the days from today: <code>3</code>, <code>-1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.norwegian</code></td>\n<td><code>Norwegian</code></td>\n<td>a number column: <code>1 234 567,891</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.english</code></td>\n<td><code>English</code></td>\n<td><code>1,234,567.891</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.swiss</code></td>\n<td><code>Swiss</code></td>\n<td><code>1&#39;234&#39;567.891</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.plain</code></td>\n<td><code>Plain</code></td>\n<td><code>1234567.891</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals</code></td>\n<td><code>Decimals</code></td>\n<td>the decimals submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.asIs</code></td>\n<td><code>As is</code></td>\n<td>the decimals the number has</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.0</code></td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n<td>no decimals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.1</code></td>\n<td><code>1</code></td>\n<td>one decimal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.2</code></td>\n<td><code>2</code></td>\n<td>two decimals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.3</code></td>\n<td><code>3</code></td>\n<td>three decimals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals.4</code></td>\n<td><code>4</code></td>\n<td>four decimals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands</code></td>\n<td><code>Thousands separator</code></td>\n<td>the thousands separator submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.none</code></td>\n<td><code>None</code></td>\n<td>no thousands separator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.space</code></td>\n<td><code>Space</code></td>\n<td><code>1 234</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.comma</code></td>\n<td><code>Comma</code></td>\n<td><code>1,234</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.dot</code></td>\n<td><code>Dot</code></td>\n<td><code>1.234</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands.apostrophe</code></td>\n<td><code>Apostrophe</code></td>\n<td><code>1&#39;234</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimalMark</code></td>\n<td><code>Decimal mark</code></td>\n<td>the decimal mark submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimalMark.dot</code></td>\n<td><code>Dot</code></td>\n<td><code>1.5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimalMark.comma</code></td>\n<td><code>Comma</code></td>\n<td><code>1,5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.percent</code></td>\n<td><code>Percent</code></td>\n<td>turns percent on or off: 0.256 is <code>25.6 %</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.settings</code></td>\n<td><code>Column settings…</code></td>\n<td>opens the grid config dialog on the column, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/grid-config-dialog\">Grid config dialog</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.shortcuts</code></td>\n<td><code>Keyboard shortcuts…</code></td>\n<td>opens the keyboard shortcuts help, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/keyboard-shortcuts-help\">Keyboard shortcuts help</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.shortcuts.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+/</code></td>\n<td>its hint: the key that opens the help anywhere in the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.shortcuts.hint.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⌘/</code></td>\n<td>its hint on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.import</code></td>\n<td><code>Import rows…</code></td>\n<td>opens the import dialog (<code>GridConfig.importMenu</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/texts/import-dialog\">Import dialog</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate</code></td>\n<td><code>Aggregate</code></td>\n<td>the aggregate submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.sum</code></td>\n<td><code>Sum</code></td>\n<td>turns the column&#39;s sum on or off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.avg</code></td>\n<td><code>Average</code></td>\n<td>turns its average on or off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.min</code></td>\n<td><code>Min</code></td>\n<td>turns its smallest value on or off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.max</code></td>\n<td><code>Max</code></td>\n<td>turns its largest value on or off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.count</code></td>\n<td><code>Count</code></td>\n<td>turns its count on or off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.totalsRow</code></td>\n<td><code>Totals row</code></td>\n<td>shows or hides the totals row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping</code></td>\n<td><code>Grouping</code></td>\n<td>the grouping submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.expandAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Expand all</code></td>\n<td>expands every group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.collapseAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Collapse all</code></td>\n<td>collapses every group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear all</code></td>\n<td>removes the grouping</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-menu\">Filter menu</h3>\n<p>Right click on a header filter.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter</code></td>\n<td>the filter submenu, first in the menu: the operator, match case, blank, clear and the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear Filter</code></td>\n<td>removes the column&#39;s conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clearAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear All Filters</code></td>\n<td>removes the whole filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>Blank</code></td>\n<td>the blank submenu, in the filter submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isBlank</code></td>\n<td><code>Is blank</code></td>\n<td>replaces the column&#39;s conditions with <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isNotBlank</code></td>\n<td><code>Is not blank</code></td>\n<td>replaces the column&#39;s conditions with <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advanced</code></td>\n<td><code>Advanced Filter…</code></td>\n<td>opens the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator</code></td>\n<td>the operator submenu, in the filter submenu, not a boolean field. The column&#39;s operators (<code>filterOperators</code>). Also the name of the row of operators of the range block</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.equal</code></td>\n<td><code>Equal</code></td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.notEqualTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Not Equal</code></td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThan</code></td>\n<td><code>Less than</code></td>\n<td>a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Less than or equal</code></td>\n<td>a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThan</code></td>\n<td><code>Greater than</code></td>\n<td>a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Greater than or equal</code></td>\n<td>a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.between</code></td>\n<td><code>Between</code></td>\n<td>a number or date field, typed as <code>from..to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.contains</code></td>\n<td><code>Contains</code></td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.doesNotContain</code></td>\n<td><code>Does not contain</code></td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.beginWith</code></td>\n<td><code>Begins with</code></td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.endWith</code></td>\n<td><code>Ends with</code></td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case</code></td>\n<td>the toggle of a text column in the filter submenu, a ✓ while it is on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.matchCase.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>always</code></td>\n<td>its hint, on a column that always matches case (<code>filterCaseSensitive</code>, a field&#39;s <code>caseSensitive</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filterOnType</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter as you type</code></td>\n<td>the toggle of filter as you type, a ✓ while it is on: in the filter submenu and at the bottom of the operator choice, not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-value-list\">The value list</h3>\n<p>The part of the filter menu with a checkbox per value - the text its cells show - for a text field\nand a lookup column.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.search</code></td>\n<td><code>Search values</code></td>\n<td>the name of the search box, for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.searchPlaceholder</code></td>\n<td><code>Search values</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of the search box</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.availableOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Only values in filtered rows</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox that lists only the values the other filters let through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.textSearch</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter on the text: contains &quot;{text}&quot;</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox under the search box that filters on the text typed (<code>CONTAINS</code>), not the values found; <code>{text}</code> is the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.rowsFound</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} values</code></td>\n<td>under the search box, <code>{count}</code>: the number of values listed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.rowsFound.one</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} value</code></td>\n<td>the same, when one value is listed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.truncated</code></td>\n<td><code>Only the first {max} values are shown</code></td>\n<td>instead, <code>{max}</code>: the most values listed (100), when there are more</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.selectAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Select all</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox over the values that checks every value - or none, when every value is checked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>Blank</code></td>\n<td>the value of the empty cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.run</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply</code></td>\n<td>the button that filters on the checked values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.cancel</code></td>\n<td><code>Cancel</code></td>\n<td>the button that closes the menu without filtering</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-range-block\">The range block</h3>\n<p>The part of the filter menu of a number, date or datetime column with its operators as buttons and\nan input - From and To for Between. The buttons show the symbols <code>=</code>, <code>≠</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>≤</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>≥</code> - no\ntexts - and the text <code>filter.operator.between</code>. Each is named by the text of its entry in Operator ›\n(<code>filter.operator.equal</code> ...), for a screen reader and as its tooltip; the row of them by\n<code>filter.operator</code>. An empty input shows the placeholder of an empty header filter: the date format, or\n<code>filter.placeholder.number</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.range.value</code></td>\n<td><code>Value</code></td>\n<td>the name of the input, for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.range.from</code></td>\n<td><code>From</code></td>\n<td>the label of the first input, with Between</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.range.to</code></td>\n<td><code>To</code></td>\n<td>the label of the second input, with Between</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.range.run</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply</code></td>\n<td>the button that filters, as typing in the header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.range.cancel</code></td>\n<td><code>Cancel</code></td>\n<td>the button that closes the menu without filtering</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-quick-dates\">The quick dates</h3>\n<p>The part of the filter menu of a date or datetime column under the range block: a button for each\nquick date (<code>ColumnConfig.datePresets</code>). A quick date&#39;s name is shown on its button, in the header\nfilter cell while it filters - typed there, it is that quick date - and on the footer&#39;s chip\n(<code>Order date = This month</code>). The tooltip of a button is its days, written as the field shows a date:\n<code>{from}</code> and <code>{to}</code>, the day alone when they are one.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates</code></td>\n<td><code>Quick dates</code></td>\n<td>the name of the buttons together, for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.today</code></td>\n<td><code>Today</code></td>\n<td>today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.yesterday</code></td>\n<td><code>Yesterday</code></td>\n<td>yesterday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.thisWeek</code></td>\n<td><code>This week</code></td>\n<td>the week of today, from the date picker&#39;s first day of the week (Monday)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.lastWeek</code></td>\n<td><code>Last week</code></td>\n<td>the week before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.thisMonth</code></td>\n<td><code>This month</code></td>\n<td>this month</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.lastMonth</code></td>\n<td><code>Last month</code></td>\n<td>the month before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.thisQuarter</code></td>\n<td><code>This quarter</code></td>\n<td>this quarter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.lastQuarter</code></td>\n<td><code>Last quarter</code></td>\n<td>the quarter before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.thisYear</code></td>\n<td><code>This year</code></td>\n<td>this year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.lastYear</code></td>\n<td><code>Last year</code></td>\n<td>last year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.last7Days</code></td>\n<td><code>Last 7 days</code></td>\n<td>today and the 6 days before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.last30Days</code></td>\n<td><code>Last 30 days</code></td>\n<td>today and the 29 days before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.next7Days</code></td>\n<td><code>Next 7 days</code></td>\n<td>today and the 6 days after</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.overdue</code></td>\n<td><code>Overdue</code></td>\n<td>every day before today - only on a column that lists it in <code>datePresets</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.range</code></td>\n<td><code>{from} – {to}</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of a button: its first and last day (a datetime&#39;s first and last second)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates.before</code></td>\n<td><code>Before {date}</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of Overdue, <code>{date}</code> today</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-menu\">Column menu</h3>\n<p>Right click on a column number, the row of numbers under the header filters.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.column</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy column</code></td>\n<td>copies the column right clicked, every displayed row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.column.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>all rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy selected columns</code></td>\n<td>copies the selected columns, every displayed row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.selectedColumns.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>all rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.headers</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy header names</code></td>\n<td>copies the label of every column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.header</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy header name</code></td>\n<td>copies the label of the column right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.selectedHeaders</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy selected header names</code></td>\n<td>copies the labels of the selected columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select</code></td>\n<td><code>Select</code></td>\n<td>the select submenu: what Shift and Ctrl do on a column number, for a finger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.column</code></td>\n<td><code>This column</code></td>\n<td>the column in or out of the selection, with a ✓ in front while it is selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.columnsTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Columns from {column} to here</code></td>\n<td>selects the columns from the anchor to this one, <code>{column}</code>: the anchor&#39;s number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.all</code></td>\n<td><code>All columns</code></td>\n<td>selects every column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear column selection</code></td>\n<td>selects no column</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-chooser\">Column chooser</h3>\n<p><code>{label}</code> is the column&#39;s label, as the list shows it - in <code>columnChooser.label</code> the column&#39;s own label\n(what an empty input gives it).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.title</code></td>\n<td><code>All Fields:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.search</code></td>\n<td><code>Search</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of the search box, its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close</code></td>\n<td>the button that closes it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.drag</code></td>\n<td><code>Drag it into the header to add the column</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of a field&#39;s drag handle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.rename</code></td>\n<td><code>Rename {label}</code></td>\n<td>the ✎ of a field: its name and tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Label of {label}</code></td>\n<td>the input the ✎ opens: its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.label.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Enter saves, Esc cancels. Empty: the column has its own label</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-editor\">Filter editor</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.title</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter Editor</code></td>\n<td>the title</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.title.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Drag to move the dialog</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the title</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.close.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Close without filtering</code></td>\n<td>tooltip and name of the <code>✕</code> in the title bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorAnd</code></td>\n<td><code>AND</code></td>\n<td>the button that makes a group AND, filled while it is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorAnd.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>All conditions in this group must match</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorOr</code></td>\n<td><code>OR</code></td>\n<td>the button that makes a group OR, filled while it is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorOr.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Any condition in this group may match</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.wrapGroup.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Wrap everything in this group inside a new parent group</code></td>\n<td>tooltip and name of the bracket icon of a group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.addSubGroup</code></td>\n<td><code>+ Group</code></td>\n<td>the button of a group that adds a sub group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.addSubGroup.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Add a sub group inside this group</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.addCondition</code></td>\n<td><code>+ Condition</code></td>\n<td>the button of a group that adds a condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.addCondition.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Add a condition to this group</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.deleteGroup.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete this group and everything in it</code></td>\n<td>tooltip and name of the trash icon of a sub group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.clearAll.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear all conditions</code></td>\n<td>tooltip and name of the trash icon of the top group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.empty</code></td>\n<td><code>No conditions: every row is shown</code></td>\n<td>in the top group while it has nothing in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.field</code></td>\n<td><code>Field</code></td>\n<td>the name of the field picker of a condition, for a screen reader: <code>Field: Country</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.operator</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator</code></td>\n<td>the name of the operator picker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.value</code></td>\n<td><code>Value</code></td>\n<td>the name and the placeholder of the value input, the name of the column picker that replaces it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.from</code></td>\n<td><code>From</code></td>\n<td>the name and the placeholder of the first value input of a <code>BETWEEN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.to</code></td>\n<td><code>To</code></td>\n<td>of its second value input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickAttribute</code></td>\n<td><code>Field</code></td>\n<td>in the field picker of a condition without a field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickAttribute.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick which column this condition looks at</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickOperator</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator</code></td>\n<td>in the operator picker of a condition without an operator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickOperator.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick how the value is compared, for example equal or greater than</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickValueAttribute</code></td>\n<td><code>Column</code></td>\n<td>in the column picker, compared with a field that is not picked yet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickValueAttribute.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick which column to compare against</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.deleteCondition.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete this condition</code></td>\n<td>tooltip and name of the trash icon of a condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.useValue.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Compare against a typed in value instead of another column</code></td>\n<td>tooltip and name of the icon that switches to a value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.useAttribute.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Compare against another column instead of a typed in value</code></td>\n<td>tooltip and name of the icon that switches to another field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>Aa</code></td>\n<td>the toggle of a condition on a text field, filled while the condition matches case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.matchCase.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip and name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.matchCaseAlways.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case: always, for this column</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip and name on a column that always matches case (<code>filterCaseSensitive</code>, a field&#39;s <code>caseSensitive</code>): on, greyed out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.undo</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo</code></td>\n<td>the name of the undo icon in the footer: takes the last edit back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.undo.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo (Ctrl+Z)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip, with the key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.undo.tooltip.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo (⌘Z)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.redo</code></td>\n<td><code>Redo</code></td>\n<td>the name of the redo icon: does the edit undone again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.redo.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Redo (Ctrl+Y)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip, with the key. Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.redo.tooltip.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>Redo (⇧⌘Z)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip on a Mac. ⌘Y redoes too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterAndClose</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply &amp; close</code></td>\n<td>button, the primary one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterAndClose.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply this filter to the grid and close the dialog</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply</code></td>\n<td>button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterOnly.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply this filter to the grid but keep the dialog open, so you can keep adjusting it</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.closeButton</code></td>\n<td><code>Cancel</code></td>\n<td>button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.closeButton.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Close the dialog and leave the current filter as it is</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.fieldMenu</code></td>\n<td><code>Available Fields:</code></td>\n<td>section header of the menu of fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.fieldMenu.search</code></td>\n<td><code>Search fields</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder and the name of its search box</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorMenu</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator:</code></td>\n<td>section header of the menu of operators</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The names of the operators, in the operator menu and on a condition:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.EQUAL</code></td>\n<td><code>Equal</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>Less Than Or Equal To</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>Greater Than Or Equal To</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>Less Than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>Greater Than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td><code>Between</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>Contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>Not Equal To</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>Does Not Contain</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>Begin With</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>End With</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.IN</code></td>\n<td><code>In</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td><code>Not In</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>Is Blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>Is Not Blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-footer\">Footer</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>footer.rowCount</code></td>\n<td><code>{shown}/{total}</code></td>\n<td><code>{shown}</code>: the rows shown, <code>{total}</code>: the rows there are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>✕ Clear</code></td>\n<td>the button that removes the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.clear.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear filter</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip (<code>title</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.noFilter</code></td>\n<td><code>No filter</code></td>\n<td>the button in Clear&#39;s place while the rows are not filtered: a click opens the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.noFilter.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Open the filter editor</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip (<code>title</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.filter.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Edit filter</code></td>\n<td>tooltip (<code>title</code>) of the chips, a click opens the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.collapsed.one</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} filter — click to edit</code></td>\n<td><code>{count}</code>: the chips do not fit, one condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.collapsed.other</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} filters — click to edit</code></td>\n<td><code>{count}</code>: the chips do not fit, or there are more than 5 conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.and</code></td>\n<td><code>AND</code></td>\n<td>between the chips of an AND group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.or</code></td>\n<td><code>OR</code></td>\n<td>between the chips of an OR group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.changedOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Changed rows only</code></td>\n<td>the chip in front of the filter&#39;s while only the changed rows are shown (<code>datasource.filterChangedOnly()</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.betweenAnd</code></td>\n<td><code>and</code></td>\n<td>between the two values of a <code>BETWEEN</code> chip: <code>Age between 10 and 20</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>Aa</code></td>\n<td>the marker after the value of a condition that matches case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.matchCase.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip (<code>title</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.findFilter</code></td>\n<td><code>Find: {text}</code></td>\n<td>the chip while Find&#39;s <em>Filter rows</em> hides rows. <code>{text}</code>: the text the rows are filtered with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.summary</code></td>\n<td><code>Selected cells</code></td>\n<td>the name of the summary of the selected cells at the footer&#39;s right, for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.summary.count</code></td>\n<td><code>Count: {count}</code></td>\n<td>the cells of the ranges with a value, any type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.summary.sum</code></td>\n<td><code>Sum: {sum}</code></td>\n<td>the sum of the numbers of the number fields in the ranges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.summary.avg</code></td>\n<td><code>Average: {avg}</code></td>\n<td>their average</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.summary.min</code></td>\n<td><code>Min: {min}</code></td>\n<td>the smallest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.summary.max</code></td>\n<td><code>Max: {max}</code></td>\n<td>the largest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.settings</code></td>\n<td><code>Grid config</code></td>\n<td>the name and tooltip (<code>title</code>) of the gear at the footer&#39;s right end (<code>GridConfig.settingsButton</code>): it opens the grid config dialog</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The short names of the operators, on a chip:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.EQUAL</code></td>\n<td><code>=</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≤</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≥</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>&lt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td><code>between</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≠</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>does not contain</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>starts with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>ends with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.IN</code></td>\n<td><code>in</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td><code>not in</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>is blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>is not blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-views\">Views</h3>\n<p>The named views (<code>GridConfig.views</code>): the views button at the footer&#39;s left end, its menu, and the\nname asked for by <em>Save as new view…</em> and <em>Rename…</em>, see <a href=\"#guide/state/views\">Views</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>views.standard</code></td>\n<td><code>Standard</code></td>\n<td>the name of no view: the config&#39;s state - on the button, and first in the menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.button</code></td>\n<td><code>View: {name}</code></td>\n<td>the name (<code>aria-label</code>) and tooltip of the button, <code>{name}</code>: the view shown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.button.modified</code></td>\n<td><code>View: {name} (changed)</code></td>\n<td>the same while the state changed since the view was applied or saved: the <code>*</code> after the name on the button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.default.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>default</code></td>\n<td>the hint after the view the grid starts in (<code>GridView.default</code>), Standard when no view is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.shared.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>shared</code></td>\n<td>the hint after a readonly view (<code>GridView.readonly</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.save</code></td>\n<td><code>Save</code></td>\n<td>saves the state over the view shown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.saveAs</code></td>\n<td><code>Save as new view…</code></td>\n<td>saves the state as a new view, asks for its name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.revert</code></td>\n<td><code>Revert changes</code></td>\n<td>applies the view shown again: what changed since is taken back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.rename</code></td>\n<td><code>Rename…</code></td>\n<td>asks for another name for the view shown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.delete</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete</code></td>\n<td>deletes the view shown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.setDefault</code></td>\n<td><code>Default view</code></td>\n<td>a toggle with a ✓: the view shown is the one the grid starts in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.name</code></td>\n<td><code>Name of the view</code></td>\n<td>the label of the input of the name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.name.empty</code></td>\n<td><code>Give the view a name</code></td>\n<td>under the input while it is empty, or only spaces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.name.taken</code></td>\n<td><code>Another view has this name</code></td>\n<td>under the input while another view has the name (in any case)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.name.cancel</code></td>\n<td><code>Cancel</code></td>\n<td>closes it, nothing saved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.name.save</code></td>\n<td><code>Save</code></td>\n<td>saves the new view (<em>Save as new view…</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.name.rename</code></td>\n<td><code>Rename</code></td>\n<td>gives the view the name (<em>Rename…</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-aggregates\">Aggregates</h3>\n<p>The aggregates of the columns (<code>ColumnConfig.aggregate</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>. A\nscreen reader reads the label with the value, the column&#39;s header with them.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.sum</code></td>\n<td><code>Sum</code></td>\n<td>in front of a sum, in a group row and the totals row: <code>Sum 1234.5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.avg</code></td>\n<td><code>Avg</code></td>\n<td>in front of an average</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.min</code></td>\n<td><code>Min</code></td>\n<td>in front of the smallest value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.max</code></td>\n<td><code>Max</code></td>\n<td>in front of the largest value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.count</code></td>\n<td><code>Count</code></td>\n<td>in front of a count</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.total</code></td>\n<td><code>Total</code></td>\n<td>the label of the totals row, at its left under the row numbers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-display-formats\">Display formats</h3>\n<p>The display formats of the columns (<code>ColumnConfig.format</code>, Format › in the header menu), see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>. <code>format.locale</code> is not shown: it is the language\nof the names in a date - the months and the days of <code>medium</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>full</code> and <code>weekday</code> - a BCP 47\nlanguage tag for <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code>. <code>&#39;nb-NO&#39;</code> shows <code>søndag 22. november 2026</code>. A tag Intl does\nnot take (<code>&#39;nb_NO&#39;</code>) shows English. The others write a week and a quarter: <code>{week}</code> has two digits.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>format.locale</code></td>\n<td><code>en-US</code></td>\n<td>not shown: the language of the names, <code>nb-NO</code> for Norwegian</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>format.weekIso</code></td>\n<td><code>{year}-W{week}</code></td>\n<td>a week, ISO: <code>2026-W47</code>. Norwegian, say: <code>uke {week} {year}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>format.weekUs</code></td>\n<td><code>{year}-W{week}</code></td>\n<td>a week, US: <code>2026-W48</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>format.quarter</code></td>\n<td><code>{year} Q{quarter}</code></td>\n<td>a quarter: <code>2026 Q4</code>. Norwegian, say: <code>{quarter}. kvartal {year}</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-grid-config-dialog\">Grid config dialog</h3>\n<p>The grid config dialog: the gear in the footer, <em>Column settings…</em> in the header menu,\n<code>gridInterface.openColumnSettings()</code> and <code>openDetailFormSettings()</code> - see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">The grid config dialog</a>. Its Columns tab, then its Detail form\ntab.\nIts format picker shows the texts of Format › (<code>header.format.*</code>). The placeholders and the hints of\nthe templates show a template as it is written: their <code>{value}</code>, <code>{field}</code>, <code>{id}</code> are not filled in,\nkeep the braces - <code>{value}</code> is the cell&#39;s own value in a template, keep it as it is. In the problems\n<code>{field}</code> is filled in: the placeholder as it was written, <code>{firstnme}</code>, or the label of a column.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.title</code></td>\n<td><code>Grid config</code></td>\n<td>the title of the dialog, its name for a screen reader, and the name of its tabs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.title.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Drag to move the dialog</code></td>\n<td>the title&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.close.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Close without applying</code></td>\n<td>the ✕: its name and tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.columns</code></td>\n<td><code>Columns</code></td>\n<td>the name of the list of the columns, for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.insertFunction</code></td>\n<td><code>Insert a function</code></td>\n<td>the ƒ beside a formula: a menu of the functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.functions.logic</code></td>\n<td><code>Logic</code></td>\n<td>a submenu of the function menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.functions.numbers</code></td>\n<td><code>Numbers</code></td>\n<td>a submenu of the function menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.functions.dates</code></td>\n<td><code>Dates</code></td>\n<td>a submenu of the function menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.functions.text</code></td>\n<td><code>Text</code></td>\n<td>a submenu of the function menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.IF</code></td>\n<td><code>IF(condition, a, b)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.IF.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>a when the condition is true, else b - a and b of one type</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.COALESCE</code></td>\n<td><code>COALESCE(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.COALESCE.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the first value that is not blank: COALESCE(x, 0) is 0 for a blank x</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.ISBLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>ISBLANK(x)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.ISBLANK.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>true when x is blank</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.ROUND</code></td>\n<td><code>ROUND(x, n)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.ROUND.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>x rounded to n decimals, halves away from 0 - a negative n rounds to tens, hundreds</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.ABS</code></td>\n<td><code>ABS(x)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.ABS.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>x without its sign</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MIN</code></td>\n<td><code>MIN(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MIN.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the least of numbers or dates, a blank skipped</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MAX</code></td>\n<td><code>MAX(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MAX.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the greatest of numbers or dates, a blank skipped</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.TODAY</code></td>\n<td><code>TODAY()</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.TODAY.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>today, at 00:00</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.DAYS</code></td>\n<td><code>DAYS(from, to)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.DAYS.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the days from one date to another: negative when to is before</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.YEAR</code></td>\n<td><code>YEAR(date)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.YEAR.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the year of a date</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MONTH</code></td>\n<td><code>MONTH(date)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MONTH.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the month of a date, 1 to 12</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.CONCAT</code></td>\n<td><code>CONCAT(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.CONCAT.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the values as one text, as &amp; joins them</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.LEFT</code></td>\n<td><code>LEFT(text, n)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.LEFT.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the first n characters</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.RIGHT</code></td>\n<td><code>RIGHT(text, n)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.RIGHT.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the last n characters</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MID</code></td>\n<td><code>MID(text, start, n)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.MID.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>n characters from the start-th, the first is 1</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.SUBSTRING</code></td>\n<td><code>SUBSTRING(text, start, n)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.SUBSTRING.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the same as MID: n characters from the start-th</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.LEN</code></td>\n<td><code>LEN(text)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.LEN.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>how many characters - 0 for a blank</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.UPPER</code></td>\n<td><code>UPPER(text)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.UPPER.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the text in upper case</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.LOWER</code></td>\n<td><code>LOWER(text)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.LOWER.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the text in lower case</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.TRIM</code></td>\n<td><code>TRIM(text)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.TRIM.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>the text without the spaces around it</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>CONTAINS(text, part)</code></td>\n<td>the function in the menu, as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula.function.CONTAINS.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>true when the text has the part, in any case</code></td>\n<td>what it does: the entry&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.back</code></td>\n<td><code>‹ Columns</code></td>\n<td>back to the list of the columns, where they take turns with a column&#39;s settings: a narrow dialog (a phone)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.type.text</code></td>\n<td><code>Text</code></td>\n<td>the type of a text column, in the list and over its settings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.type.number</code></td>\n<td><code>Number</code></td>\n<td>a number column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.type.boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>Checkbox</code></td>\n<td>a boolean column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.type.date</code></td>\n<td><code>Date</code></td>\n<td>a date column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.type.datetime</code></td>\n<td><code>Date and time</code></td>\n<td>a datetime column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.type.lookup</code></td>\n<td><code>Lookup</code></td>\n<td>a lookup column (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.format</code></td>\n<td><code>Format</code></td>\n<td>in the list: the column has a date or number format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.text</code></td>\n<td><code>Text</code></td>\n<td>the column has a template of its text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.link</code></td>\n<td><code>Link</code></td>\n<td>the column is a link</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.problem</code></td>\n<td><code>Error</code></td>\n<td>a template or a checkbox value of the column has a problem: Apply waits for it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.multiline</code></td>\n<td><code>Multiline</code></td>\n<td>in the list of the columns: a text column made multiline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.readonly</code></td>\n<td><code>Read only</code></td>\n<td>in the list of the columns: a column made read only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.multiline</code></td>\n<td><code>Multiline</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox of a text column: its cells are text of several lines (<code>ColumnFormat.multiline</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.minRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Min lines in the detail form</code></td>\n<td>under Multiline while it is on: <code>DetailForm.fieldOptions[field].minRows</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.minRows.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>1 to 30, the least lines of its box in the detail form - empty: 3</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.readonly</code></td>\n<td><code>Read only</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox of every column: its cells are not edited, also in an editable grid (<code>ColumnFormat.readonly</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.readonly.locked</code></td>\n<td><code>Always for this column: set by the application</code></td>\n<td>under it when the field is locked (<code>FieldConfig.readonly</code>): checked and greyed out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.validation</code></td>\n<td><code>Validation</code></td>\n<td>the heading of the column&#39;s validation rules, read only - only a column whose field has rules (<code>FieldConfig.validate</code>, <code>ColumnConfig.validate</code>). A rule is its own <code>message</code>, else the built-in&#39;s message (<code>validation.*</code>), else its id</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.validation.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Set by the application: checked on every edit, not changed here</code></td>\n<td>under them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.validation.pattern</code></td>\n<td><code>Must match {value}</code></td>\n<td>a <code>pattern</code> rule without a message: <code>{value}</code> is its regular expression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.validation.unique</code></td>\n<td><code>Unique</code></td>\n<td>a <code>unique</code> rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.validation.uniqueWith</code></td>\n<td><code>Unique together with {with}</code></td>\n<td>a <code>unique</code> rule with <code>with</code>: the labels of the columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.validation.ignoreCase</code></td>\n<td><code>{rule}, in any case</code></td>\n<td>a <code>unique</code> rule with <code>ignoreCase</code>: <code>{rule}</code> is one of the two above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.checkbox</code></td>\n<td><code>Checkbox</code></td>\n<td>the column is shown as checkboxes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.numberOverride</code></td>\n<td><code>Blank / 0</code></td>\n<td>the column shows a blank as 0, or 0 as blank (or neither, over its <code>numberOverride</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Label</code></td>\n<td>the column has a label of the user&#39;s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>Placeholder</code></td>\n<td>the column&#39;s empty cells have a placeholder of the user&#39;s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.rules</code></td>\n<td><code>Colours</code></td>\n<td>the column has colour rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Label</code></td>\n<td>the label of the input of the column&#39;s label, every column: empty is the column&#39;s own, the input shows it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.format</code></td>\n<td><code>Format</code></td>\n<td>the label of the format picker of a date or number column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.format.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>How the cells show the value</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.format.ofType</code></td>\n<td><code>Default: {format}</code></td>\n<td>the format picker of a column without a format of its own whose type has one (the Defaults tab): <code>{format}</code> the type&#39;s format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.filterAsShown</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter as shown</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox under the format of a date or number column: the header filter takes the text as the cells show it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.filterAsShown.template.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>The filters compare the text the cells show, the template filled in - off: the value of the field</code></td>\n<td>under Filter as shown of a text column with a template</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.filterAsShown.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>The header filter takes the text as the cells show it: a week, a month, a year is the whole of it</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.filterAsShown.off</code></td>\n<td><code>Not for the day of the week or the time of day: the header filter takes the field&#39;s format</code></td>\n<td>under it instead, greyed out: a format that is no period of time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.text</code></td>\n<td><code>Text shown</code></td>\n<td>the label of a text column&#39;s template of its text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.text.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>{firstname} {lastname}</code></td>\n<td>its placeholder, a template as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.text.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Empty: the cell&#39;s own text. {value} is the cell&#39;s value, {field} the value of another field</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.link</code></td>\n<td><code>Show as link</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox that makes the column a link</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.url</code></td>\n<td><code>Address</code></td>\n<td>the label of the address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.url.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>https://example.com/customer/{id}</code></td>\n<td>its placeholder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.url.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Starts with https://, http://, mailto: or tel: - or is one {field} that holds the whole address</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.newTab</code></td>\n<td><code>Open in a new tab</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox of <code>newTab</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.insertField</code></td>\n<td><code>Insert a field</code></td>\n<td>the <code>{ }</code> button beside a template: its name and tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.none.boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>A checkbox column has no format</code></td>\n<td>the settings of a boolean column, under its label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.none.lookup</code></td>\n<td><code>A lookup column shows the label of its value: it has no format</code></td>\n<td>the settings of a lookup column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.blankToZero</code></td>\n<td><code>Show blank as 0</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox of a number column: a blank cell shows <code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.zeroToBlank</code></td>\n<td><code>Show 0 as blank</code></td>\n<td>a number column: 0 shows as an empty cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.checkbox</code></td>\n<td><code>Show as checkbox</code></td>\n<td>a text or number column: its cells are checkboxes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.checked</code></td>\n<td><code>Checked value</code></td>\n<td>the label of the value of a checked cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.unchecked</code></td>\n<td><code>Unchecked value</code></td>\n<td>the label of the value written when a cell is unchecked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.checkbox.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>A click writes the other value. An empty unchecked value empties the cell</code></td>\n<td>under the two values: a cell is checked when its value is the checked value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>Placeholder</code></td>\n<td>the label of the placeholder of the column&#39;s empty cells, every column but a checkbox: empty is the column&#39;s own (<code>placeholderRow</code>), the input shows it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.placeholderLabel</code></td>\n<td><code>Use the column label</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox: the placeholder of the empty cells is the column&#39;s label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.operator</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter operator</code></td>\n<td>the label of the operator picker, every column but a checkbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.operator.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>The operator the header filter of the column uses</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.operator.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Default: {operator}</code></td>\n<td>under it: the operator the column starts with, its name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.filterOnType</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter as you type</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox after the operator, every column but a checkbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.filterOnType.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>After a short pause, no Enter needed</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case</code></td>\n<td>the dialog&#39;s checkbox of a text column&#39;s header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.matchCase.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ann does not find ANN - the header shows Aa beside the operator</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.matchCase.always</code></td>\n<td><code>Always for this column: filterCaseSensitive in its config</code></td>\n<td>under it when the column always matches case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.matchCase.locked</code></td>\n<td><code>Always for this column: set by the application</code></td>\n<td>under it when the field&#39;s case is locked (<code>FieldConfig.caseSensitive</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules</code></td>\n<td><code>Colour rules</code></td>\n<td>the heading of the column&#39;s colour rules, every column, and their name for a screen reader. A rule&#39;s operator picker has the header filter&#39;s names of the operators (<code>filter.operator.*</code>, <code>filter.filter.isBlank</code>, <code>filter.filter.isNotBlank</code>), its label and the value inputs the filter editor&#39;s (<code>filterEditor.label.*</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>The first rule that matches colours the cell. The value is compared as the header filter compares it</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.empty</code></td>\n<td><code>No rules</code></td>\n<td>a column without rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.add</code></td>\n<td><code>Add rule</code></td>\n<td>the button that adds a rule at the end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.rule</code></td>\n<td><code>Rule {number}</code></td>\n<td>the name of a rule for a screen reader, <code>{number}</code> its place: 1 is the first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.checked</code></td>\n<td><code>Checked</code></td>\n<td>the value of a rule of a boolean column: a checkbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.date.compare</code></td>\n<td><code>Compare with</code></td>\n<td>a rule on a date: the picker of what it compares with, its name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.date.typed</code></td>\n<td><code>A date or period typed</code></td>\n<td>in it: the value typed - else a quick date (<code>filter.dates.*</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.date.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>Date, 2026-W47, 2026-11, 2026 Q4, 2026</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of a date rule&#39;s value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.week</code></td>\n<td><code>Weeks</code></td>\n<td>the picker of the weeks of a rule on a date (<code>week</code>), its name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.week.column</code></td>\n<td><code>As the column: {week}</code></td>\n<td>no <code>week</code>: the column&#39;s - <code>{week}</code> the name of the one it takes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.week.iso</code></td>\n<td><code>ISO - Monday</code></td>\n<td><code>week: &#39;iso&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.week.us</code></td>\n<td><code>US - Sunday</code></td>\n<td><code>week: &#39;us&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.textColor</code></td>\n<td><code>Text colour</code></td>\n<td>the label of a rule&#39;s text colours, a row of swatches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.background</code></td>\n<td><code>Background</code></td>\n<td>the label of its backgrounds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.bold</code></td>\n<td><code>Bold</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox: the text is bold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.none</code></td>\n<td><code>None</code></td>\n<td>the first swatch: no text colour, no background - the name and tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.red</code></td>\n<td><code>Red</code></td>\n<td>a swatch of the palette: its name and tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.orange</code></td>\n<td><code>Orange</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.amber</code></td>\n<td><code>Amber</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.yellow</code></td>\n<td><code>Yellow</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.lime</code></td>\n<td><code>Lime</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.green</code></td>\n<td><code>Green</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.teal</code></td>\n<td><code>Teal</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.cyan</code></td>\n<td><code>Cyan</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.blue</code></td>\n<td><code>Blue</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.indigo</code></td>\n<td><code>Indigo</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.purple</code></td>\n<td><code>Purple</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.pink</code></td>\n<td><code>Pink</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.brown</code></td>\n<td><code>Brown</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.color.grey</code></td>\n<td><code>Grey</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.custom</code></td>\n<td><code>Custom colour</code></td>\n<td>the colour picker after the swatches, a custom colour (<code>#rrggbb</code>): its name and tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.contrast.rows</code></td>\n<td><code>The text colour has {ratio}:1 to the light rows, under 4.5:1: hard to read</code></td>\n<td>a custom text colour that is hard to read on the light rows, <code>{ratio}</code> its contrast (<code>3.2</code>). Apply is not held back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.contrast.background</code></td>\n<td><code>The text colour has {ratio}:1 to the background, under 4.5:1: hard to read</code></td>\n<td>the same on the rule&#39;s custom background</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.kind</code></td>\n<td><code>Condition</code></td>\n<td>the label of the kind of a rule&#39;s condition, three radio buttons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.kind.value</code></td>\n<td><code>Value</code></td>\n<td>the kind: the cell&#39;s value, an operator and a value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.kind.conditions</code></td>\n<td><code>Conditions</code></td>\n<td>the kind: conditions on fields of the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.kind.formula</code></td>\n<td><code>Formula</code></td>\n<td>the kind: a formula that is true or false - also the name of its input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.combine.AND</code></td>\n<td><code>All of them</code></td>\n<td>the picker over two conditions or more: all must be true (<code>combine: &#39;AND&#39;</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.combine.OR</code></td>\n<td><code>Any of them</code></td>\n<td>one is enough (<code>combine: &#39;OR&#39;</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.combine.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Whether all the conditions must be true, or one of them</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.condition</code></td>\n<td><code>Condition {number}</code></td>\n<td>the name of a condition for a screen reader, <code>{number}</code> its place: 1 is the first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.condition.field</code></td>\n<td><code>Field</code></td>\n<td>the picker of a condition&#39;s field: its tooltip, and its name before the field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.condition.add</code></td>\n<td><code>Add condition</code></td>\n<td>the button under the conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.condition.remove</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove condition</code></td>\n<td>the ✕ of a condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.formula.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>{balance} &lt; 0 AND {status} = &quot;late&quot;</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of a rule&#39;s formula, a formula as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.formula.true</code></td>\n<td><code>True in this row: the rule colours the cell</code></td>\n<td>under the formula: what it is in the row of the preview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.formula.false</code></td>\n<td><code>False in this row</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.problem.conditions</code></td>\n<td><code>Add a condition</code></td>\n<td>a rule of the kind Conditions without one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.problem.expression</code></td>\n<td><code>Write the formula, true or false</code></td>\n<td>a rule of the kind Formula without a formula</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.moveUp</code></td>\n<td><code>Move up</code></td>\n<td>the button that moves a rule before the one above it: its name and tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.moveDown</code></td>\n<td><code>Move down</code></td>\n<td>after the one under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.remove</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove rule</code></td>\n<td>the ✕ of a rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.colours.rules</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} rules</code></td>\n<td>the line of the Colour rules panel of a number column: how many it has</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.colours.oneRule</code></td>\n<td><code>1 rule</code></td>\n<td>the line of the Colour rules panel with one rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.colours.on</code></td>\n<td><code>On</code></td>\n<td>the line of the Data bar, Colour scale or Icons panel: it is set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.colours.off</code></td>\n<td><code>Off</code></td>\n<td>the line of the Data bar, Colour scale or Icons panel: it is not set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.open</code></td>\n<td><code>Edit the rule</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of a closed rule&#39;s line: a click opens it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close the rule</code></td>\n<td>the ▾ of the open rule: one line again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.sample</code></td>\n<td><code>Abc</code></td>\n<td>the sample in a closed rule&#39;s colours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.unchecked</code></td>\n<td><code>Not checked</code></td>\n<td>a closed rule on a checkbox column whose box is not checked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.problem.value</code></td>\n<td><code>Write the value to compare with</code></td>\n<td>a rule&#39;s operator compares, its value is empty (a Between: both)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.problem.style</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick a text colour, a background or bold</code></td>\n<td>a rule that colours nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.rules.unread</code></td>\n<td><code>The column does not read {value}: the rule colours no cell</code></td>\n<td><code>{value}</code>: a value the field&#39;s formatter does not read - a date of another format. Apply is not held back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.bars</code></td>\n<td><code>Data bars, scales, icons</code></td>\n<td>the heading of a number column&#39;s data bar, colour scale and icon set, and their name for a screen reader. The swatches have the names of the rules&#39; colours (<code>columnSettings.rules.color.*</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.bars.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>By the value&#39;s place among the numbers of the rows the filter lets through. A colour rule&#39;s background wins over a scale</