n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.defaultLabel</code></td>\n<td><code>Calculated {number}</code></td>\n<td>the label of a calculated column added, <code>{number}</code> the one of its field <code>calc_&lt;number&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.name</code></td>\n<td><code>Field name</code></td>\n<td>the label of its field name - an input while it is new, its name after</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.name.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>A letter, then letters, digits, _ and #. Not changed once applied</code></td>\n<td>under the input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.formula</code></td>\n<td><code>Formula</code></td>\n<td>the label of its formula</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.formula.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>{price} * {qty}</code></td>\n<td>its placeholder, a formula as it is written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.formula.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Fields in braces, numbers, &quot;text&quot;, + - * / &amp;, = &lt;&gt; != &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=, AND OR NOT, IF ROUND ABS MIN MAX TODAY DAYS YEAR MONTH CONCAT COALESCE ISBLANK LEFT RIGHT MID SUBSTRING LEN UPPER LOWER TRIM CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td>under it: the language. The keywords and the names of the functions are the language&#39;s, keep them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.gives</code></td>\n<td><code>Gives: {type}</code></td>\n<td>the type of its values, <code>{type}</code> the name of a type (<code>columnSettings.type.*</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.value</code></td>\n<td><code>In this row: {value}</code></td>\n<td>its value in the row of the preview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>(blank)</code></td>\n<td>the value when it is blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.applyFirst</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply first: then the format and the colour rules of a {type} column</code></td>\n<td>a calculated column new, or of another type now: its other settings after Apply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.remove</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove calculated column</code></td>\n<td>the button: it leaves the draft, and on Apply the layout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.problem.name</code></td>\n<td><code>The field name is a letter, then letters, digits, _ and # - 64 at most</code></td>\n<td>a field name typed that a calculated column can not have</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.calculated.problem.taken</code></td>\n<td><code>The field name is a field already: give the column a name of its own</code></td>\n<td>the name typed is a field of the datasource, of the rows or of another column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.badge.calculated</code></td>\n<td><code>Calculated</code></td>\n<td>in the list of the columns: a calculated column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.type.number</code></td>\n<td><code>a number</code></td>\n<td>the name of a type in a problem of a formula (<code>{got}</code>, <code>{expected}</code>, <code>{type}</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.type.text</code></td>\n<td><code>a text</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.type.date</code></td>\n<td><code>a date</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.type.boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>true / false</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.empty</code></td>\n<td><code>Write the formula</code></td>\n<td>the problems of a formula - of a calculated column and of a rule of the kind Formula</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.length</code></td>\n<td><code>The formula is longer than {max} characters</code></td>\n<td>over the limit, 1000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.depth</code></td>\n<td><code>The formula nests deeper than {max} levels</code></td>\n<td>brackets, functions, a minus or NOT in front, 32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.size</code></td>\n<td><code>The formula has more than {max} parts</code></td>\n<td>500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.character</code></td>\n<td><code>{text} at {position} is no part of a formula</code></td>\n<td>a character no part of the language: <code>$</code>, <code>;</code>. <code>{position}</code>: the character, 1 the first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.bigNumber</code></td>\n<td><code>The number at {position} is too large</code></td>\n<td>a number of more digits than a number holds, over 1.8 × 10³⁰⁸</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.unexpected</code></td>\n<td><code>{text} at {position} can not be there</code></td>\n<td><code>1 2</code>, a <code>)</code> too many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.end</code></td>\n<td><code>The formula ends too soon</code></td>\n<td><code>1 +</code>, <code>ROUND(1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.text</code></td>\n<td><code>The text at {position} has no closing quote</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.field</code></td>\n<td><code>The field at {position} has no closing brace</code></td>\n<td>a <code>{</code> without its <code>}</code>, and <code>{}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.function</code></td>\n<td><code>{name} is no function: {names}</code></td>\n<td><code>{names}</code>: the functions of the language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.arguments</code></td>\n<td><code>{name} has {count} values: it is written {usage}</code></td>\n<td><code>{usage}</code>: how it is written, <code>ROUND(x, n)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.name</code></td>\n<td><code>{name} at {position} is no function - a field is written in braces</code></td>\n<td>a word that is no keyword and has no <code>(</code> after it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.internal</code></td>\n<td><code>{field} is the grid&#39;s own, not a field</code></td>\n<td><code>{field}</code>: a field that starts with <code>__</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.unknownField</code></td>\n<td><code>No field {field}</code></td>\n<td>a field that is no column, no field of the datasource, no calculated column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.cycle</code></td>\n<td><code>{field} is computed from itself</code></td>\n<td>a calculated column read by its own formula, straight or through another</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.fieldProblem</code></td>\n<td><code>{field} has a problem of its own</code></td>\n<td>a calculated column read whose formula has a problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.clobField</code></td>\n<td><code>{field} is a long text: a formula can not read it</code></td>\n<td>a field with <code>clobType</code> (<code>FieldConfig.clobType</code>), filtered with LIKE only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.number</code></td>\n<td><code>{what} takes a number: {part} is {got}</code></td>\n<td><code>{what}</code>: an operator or a function, <code>{part}</code>: the part as written, <code>{got}</code>: its type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.date</code></td>\n<td><code>{what} takes a date: {part} is {got}</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>{what} takes true / false: {part} is {got}</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.same</code></td>\n<td><code>{what} takes values of one type: {part} is {got}, {other} is {expected}</code></td>\n<td><code>=</code>, IF, COALESCE ... on two types</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.order</code></td>\n<td><code>{what} compares numbers, texts and dates, not true / false</code></td>\n<td><code>&lt;</code> on true / false</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.chain</code></td>\n<td><code>Compare two values at a time ({position}): a &lt; b AND b &lt; c</code></td>\n<td><code>a &lt; b &lt; c</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.digits</code></td>\n<td><code>ROUND takes a whole number of digits from -10 to 10, not {part}</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.result</code></td>\n<td><code>The formula gives {got}, it must give {expected}</code></td>\n<td>a rule&#39;s formula that is not true / false, a column&#39;s <code>type</code> that is not its formula&#39;s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.divide</code></td>\n<td><code>Divides by 0 in this row: no value</code></td>\n<td>the preview: the formula in the row of the preview. The cell is empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.value</code></td>\n<td><code>{field} is not {type} in this row: no value</code></td>\n<td>a value that is not of its field&#39;s type, a text in a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.overflow</code></td>\n<td><code>A number too big in this row: no value</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.formula.problem.long</code></td>\n<td><code>A text of more than {max} characters in this row: no value</code></td>\n<td><code>&amp;</code>, CONCAT: 10000 at most</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.preview.current</code></td>\n<td><code>Preview, the current row</code></td>\n<td>the title of the preview of the current row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.preview.first</code></td>\n<td><code>Preview, the first row</code></td>\n<td>of the first row, without a current row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.preview.none</code></td>\n<td><code>No rows to preview</code></td>\n<td>without rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.urlEmpty</code></td>\n<td><code>Write the address</code></td>\n<td>Show as link is on, the address is empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.urlStart</code></td>\n<td><code>Start the address with https://, http://, mailto: or tel: - or give one field alone</code></td>\n<td>the address does not start with its scheme</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.protocol</code></td>\n<td><code>Not a link the grid opens: only https:, http:, mailto: and tel:</code></td>\n<td>the address, or the one a field holds, has another scheme: <code>javascript:</code>, <code>data:</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.internal</code></td>\n<td><code>{field} is the grid&#39;s own, not a field</code></td>\n<td><code>{field}</code>: a placeholder that starts with <code>__</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.unknownField</code></td>\n<td><code>No field {field} in this row</code></td>\n<td><code>{field}</code>: a placeholder that is no column, no field and not in the row of the preview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>{field} is empty in this row: no link, the text only</code></td>\n<td><code>{field}</code>: the label of the column of the address that is empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.invalid</code></td>\n<td><code>No valid address in this row: no link, the text only</code></td>\n<td>what the row fills in is no address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.checkedEmpty</code></td>\n<td><code>Write the checked value</code></td>\n<td>the checked value of a column&#39;s checkboxes is empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.notNumber</code></td>\n<td><code>{value} is no number: a number column writes numbers</code></td>\n<td><code>{value}</code>: a checked or unchecked value of a number column, as typed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.problem.sameValues</code></td>\n<td><code>The checked and the unchecked value are the same: a click could not uncheck a cell</code></td>\n<td>the two values are the same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.resetColumn</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset column</code></td>\n<td>the button: the column&#39;s settings as the grid starts with them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.resetColumn.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>The settings the column starts with</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.resetAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset all</code></td>\n<td>the button: every column&#39;s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.resetAll.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>The settings every column starts with</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.cancel</code></td>\n<td><code>Cancel</code></td>\n<td>closes the dialog without applying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.apply</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply</code></td>\n<td>sets the formats of the grid&#39;s state, the dialog stays open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.apply.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply to the grid, the dialog stays open</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.applyAndClose</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply &amp; close</code></td>\n<td>applies and closes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.tab.columns</code></td>\n<td><code>Columns</code></td>\n<td>the tab of the columns&#39; settings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.tab.detailForm</code></td>\n<td><code>Detail form</code></td>\n<td>the tab of the detail form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.tab.defaults</code></td>\n<td><code>Defaults</code></td>\n<td>the tab of the settings of the grid: the formats by type, the default view</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.section.general</code></td>\n<td><code>General</code></td>\n<td>the tab of a column&#39;s label, read only, multiline and placeholder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.section.display</code></td>\n<td><code>Display</code></td>\n<td>the tab of what a column&#39;s cells show: format, text, link, checkboxes, blank / 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.section.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Header filter</code></td>\n<td>the tab of a column&#39;s header filter operator and filter as you type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.section.colours</code></td>\n<td><code>Colours</code></td>\n<td>the tab of a column&#39;s colour rules, data bar, colour scale and icon set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.sections</code></td>\n<td><code>Settings of {column}</code></td>\n<td>the name of a column&#39;s tabs, for a screen reader. <code>{column}</code>: its label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.show</code></td>\n<td><code>Show the detail form</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox that shows it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.expanded</code></td>\n<td><code>Expanded</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox that expands it, off: collapsed (<code>DetailForm.expanded</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.expanded.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Collapsed, the form is a narrow strip: the button of its title expands it, and collapses it again</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.width</code></td>\n<td><code>Width (px)</code></td>\n<td>the label of its width</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.minWidth</code></td>\n<td><code>Min width (px)</code></td>\n<td>the label of its least width (<code>minWidth</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.maxWidth</code></td>\n<td><code>Max width (px)</code></td>\n<td>the label of its most width (<code>maxWidth</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.maxWidth.none</code></td>\n<td><code>None</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of an empty most width</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.width.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>At least 150, between the min and the max width - a drag of the left edge of the form changes it too. Where the rows would get too little room beside it, the form is under them</code></td>\n<td>under the widths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.fields</code></td>\n<td><code>Fields of the form</code></td>\n<td>the name of the list of the fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.fields.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Space puts a field in the form or takes it out, Alt+↑ and Alt+↓ move it - or drag it by its handle</code></td>\n<td>under the list: its keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.fields.hint.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>Space puts a field in the form or takes it out, ⌥↑ and ⌥↓ move it - or drag it by its handle</code></td>\n<td>the same on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.drag</code></td>\n<td><code>Drag to move it</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of the drag handle at the right of a field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.multiline</code></td>\n<td><code>Several lines</code></td>\n<td>in the list: a field of several lines (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.hidden</code></td>\n<td><code>Hidden</code></td>\n<td>in the list: a column that is not in the layout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.moveUp</code></td>\n<td><code>Move up</code></td>\n<td>the button: the field picked one up, in the form too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.moveDown</code></td>\n<td><code>Move down</code></td>\n<td>one down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.field</code></td>\n<td><code>Settings of {field} in the form</code></td>\n<td>the heading of the settings of the field picked, under the list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.minRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Min lines</code></td>\n<td>the least lines of a field of several lines (<code>minRows</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.paddingTop</code></td>\n<td><code>Space above (px)</code></td>\n<td>room above the field (<code>paddingTop</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.paddingBottom</code></td>\n<td><code>Space below (px)</code></td>\n<td>room under it (<code>paddingBottom</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.addSection</code></td>\n<td><code>Add section</code></td>\n<td>the button under the list: a section under the row picked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.newSection</code></td>\n<td><code>Section</code></td>\n<td>the name of a section added</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.sectionType</code></td>\n<td><code>Section</code></td>\n<td>in the list: the type of a section&#39;s row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.panelBadge</code></td>\n<td><code>Panel</code></td>\n<td>in the list: a section that is a panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.sectionSettings</code></td>\n<td><code>Settings of the section {section}</code></td>\n<td>the heading of the settings of the section picked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.section</code></td>\n<td><code>Name</code></td>\n<td>the label of its name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.section.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>The fields under it in the list, up to the next section, are in it. Its header collapses it</code></td>\n<td>under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.panel</code></td>\n<td><code>Panel: a frame around it</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox of <code>panel</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.open</code></td>\n<td><code>Open at start</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox of a section&#39;s default: off is <code>collapsed</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.removeSection</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove section</code></td>\n<td>the button: the section out of the form, its fields stay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.reset</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset form</code></td>\n<td>the button at the footer&#39;s left on the Detail form tab: the detail form the grid starts with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.detailForm.reset.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>The detail form the grid starts with</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.formats</code></td>\n<td><code>Formats by type</code></td>\n<td>the Defaults tab: the heading over the formats of a date, a datetime and a number column (<code>GridState.typeFormats</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.formats.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>A column without a format of its own shows the format of its type</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.format.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>How every column of this type shows its values</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of each type&#39;s format picker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.views</code></td>\n<td><code>Views</code></td>\n<td>the heading over the default view</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.viewsButton</code></td>\n<td><code>Show the views button</code></td>\n<td>the Defaults tab: the views button at the footer&#39;s left end on or off (<code>GridConfig.viewsButton</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.viewsButton.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>At the left end of the footer: save, switch, rename and delete views</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.defaultView</code></td>\n<td><code>Default view</code></td>\n<td>the picker of the view the grid opens with: Standard or a view</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.defaultView.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>The view the grid opens with</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.noViews</code></td>\n<td><code>No views saved yet: save one from the views button</code></td>\n<td>its hint without views, the picker greyed out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.reset</code></td>\n<td><code>Reset formats</code></td>\n<td>the button at the footer&#39;s left on the Defaults tab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnSettings.defaults.reset.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>The formats by type the grid starts with</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-detail-form\">Detail form</h3>\n<p>The detail form at the right of the rows (<code>GridConfig.detailForm</code>), see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">The detail form</a>. <code>{row}</code> is the number of the row it shows, as the\nrow numbers show it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>detailForm.title</code></td>\n<td><code>Details</code></td>\n<td>its title, its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm.row</code></td>\n<td><code>Row {row}</code></td>\n<td>after the title: the row it shows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm.noRow</code></td>\n<td><code>No row</code></td>\n<td>without a current row, or one the filter hides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm.noFields</code></td>\n<td><code>No fields: pick them with the gear in the footer, tab Detail form</code></td>\n<td>shown without fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm.collapse</code></td>\n<td><code>Collapse the details</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of the button of its title while it is expanded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm.expand</code></td>\n<td><code>Expand the details</code></td>\n<td>the same while it is collapsed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detailForm.resize</code></td>\n<td><code>Width of the details</code></td>\n<td>the splitter of its left edge: its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-import-dialog\">Import dialog</h3>\n<p>The import dialog (<code>gridInterface.openImport()</code>, the menus&#39; entries with <code>GridConfig.importMenu</code>), see\n<a href=\"#guide/import\">Import</a>. <code>{size}</code>, <code>{rows}</code>, <code>{columns}</code> and <code>{cells}</code> in the error of a file too large are\nthe limits (<code>IMPORT_LIMITS</code>); <code>{row}</code> is the row of the file, from 1; <code>{field}</code> the label of a column;\n<code>{text}</code> a value as the file has it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>import.title</code></td>\n<td><code>Import rows</code></td>\n<td>its title, its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.title.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Drag to move the dialog</code></td>\n<td>the title&#39;s tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.close.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Close without importing</code></td>\n<td>the ✕: its tooltip and its name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.chooseFile</code></td>\n<td><code>Choose file…</code></td>\n<td>the button that opens the file picker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Drop a CSV or Excel (.xlsx) file here, or paste rows copied from a spreadsheet</code></td>\n<td>the drop zone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.clipboard</code></td>\n<td><code>Pasted rows</code></td>\n<td>where the rows came from, for pasted rows (a file has its name)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.reading</code></td>\n<td><code>Reading the file…</code></td>\n<td>while a file is read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.error.tooLarge</code></td>\n<td><code>The file is too large to import: at most {size} MB, {rows} rows, {columns} columns and {cells} cells</code></td>\n<td>a file over a limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.error.xls</code></td>\n<td><code>An Excel 97-2003 file (.xls), or one protected by a password, can not be read: save it as .xlsx or CSV</code></td>\n<td>an .xls file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.error.broken</code></td>\n<td><code>The file can not be read: it is not CSV or an Excel workbook (.xlsx), or it is damaged</code></td>\n<td>a file that is neither, or damaged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.error.unsupported</code></td>\n<td><code>This browser can not read Excel files: save the file as CSV</code></td>\n<td>an .xlsx file in a browser without <code>DecompressionStream</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.error.clipboard</code></td>\n<td><code>No rows on the clipboard: copy them in a spreadsheet, then paste them here</code></td>\n<td><em>As new rows…</em> without rows on the clipboard, or a clipboard the browser does not give</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.header</code></td>\n<td><code>First row is a header</code></td>\n<td>the option: the first row names the columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.separator</code></td>\n<td><code>Separator</code></td>\n<td>the option of a CSV file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.separator.auto</code></td>\n<td><code>Found: {separator}</code></td>\n<td>the separator found, <code>{separator}</code> its name below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.separator.comma</code></td>\n<td><code>Comma</code></td>\n<td>a separator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.separator.semicolon</code></td>\n<td><code>Semicolon</code></td>\n<td>a separator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.separator.tab</code></td>\n<td><code>Tab</code></td>\n<td>a separator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.sheet</code></td>\n<td><code>Sheet</code></td>\n<td>the option of a workbook of more sheets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.decimalMark</code></td>\n<td><code>Decimal mark</code></td>\n<td>the option when a number field is picked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.decimalMark.auto</code></td>\n<td><code>As the columns</code></td>\n<td>each number column&#39;s own decimal mark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.decimalMark.dot</code></td>\n<td><code>Dot: 1,234.5</code></td>\n<td>a decimal mark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.decimalMark.comma</code></td>\n<td><code>Comma: 1 234,5</code></td>\n<td>a decimal mark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.dateOrder</code></td>\n<td><code>Dates with /</code></td>\n<td>the option when a date field is picked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.dateOrder.DMY</code></td>\n<td><code>Day first: 31/12/2026</code></td>\n<td>an order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.dateOrder.MDY</code></td>\n<td><code>Month first: 12/31/2026</code></td>\n<td>an order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.byName</code></td>\n<td><code>The first row names the columns: they are matched to the fields by name</code></td>\n<td>over the table, with a header that names fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.byPosition</code></td>\n<td><code>The columns are matched to the fields by position, from {field}</code></td>\n<td>over the table, without</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.column</code></td>\n<td><code>Column {number}</code></td>\n<td>a column without a header, <code>{number}</code> from 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.field</code></td>\n<td><code>Field of {column}</code></td>\n<td>the name of a column&#39;s field picker for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.skip</code></td>\n<td><code>Skip</code></td>\n<td>the picker&#39;s entry for a column not imported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.count</code></td>\n<td><code>{rows} rows</code></td>\n<td>the rows to import</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.count.problems</code></td>\n<td><code>{rows} rows, {problems} with problems</code></td>\n<td>the same, with the rows that have problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.problem</code></td>\n<td><code>Row {row}, {field}: {message}</code></td>\n<td>a problem in the list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.problems.more</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} more</code></td>\n<td>after the problems listed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.problem.number</code></td>\n<td><code>Not a number: {text}</code></td>\n<td>a value of a number field that is not read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.problem.date</code></td>\n<td><code>Not a date: {text}</code></td>\n<td>the same for a date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.problem.boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>Not true or false: {text}</code></td>\n<td>the same for a checkbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.invalidRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Rows with problems</code></td>\n<td>the option of the rows with problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.invalidRows.import</code></td>\n<td><code>Import them, marked</code></td>\n<td>they are imported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.invalidRows.skip</code></td>\n<td><code>Skip them</code></td>\n<td>they are not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.noFields</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick a field for at least one column</code></td>\n<td>no column has a field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.noRows</code></td>\n<td><code>No rows to import</code></td>\n<td>an empty sheet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.cancel</code></td>\n<td><code>Cancel</code></td>\n<td>the button that closes it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.import</code></td>\n<td><code>Import {rows} rows</code></td>\n<td>the button that imports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>import.rowError</code></td>\n<td><code>Not imported: {problems}</code></td>\n<td>the row error of a new row with values that were not read, on its row number</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-header-filters\">Header filters</h3>\n<p>What an empty header filter shows, when its column has no <code>placeholderFilter</code> of its own. A date\nfilter shows its formatter&#39;s format (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>) - filtered as shown, an example of its display format\n(<code>2026-W47</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/filter-as-shown\">Filtering</a>) - a boolean filter is a checkbox.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.placeholder.text</code></td>\n<td><code>Text</code></td>\n<td>an empty header filter of a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.placeholder.number</code></td>\n<td><code>Number</code></td>\n<td>an empty header filter of a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.placeholder.between</code></td>\n<td><code>from..to</code></td>\n<td>an empty header filter whose operator is <code>BETWEEN</code>, any type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.placeholder.daysUntil</code></td>\n<td><code>Days from today</code></td>\n<td>an empty header filter of a date column shown as <code>daysUntil</code>, filtered as shown - a number of days is typed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The operator indicator in a header filter cell (<code>filterOperatorIndicator</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">Filtering</a>): the symbol of the operator the filter uses,\nits name for a screen reader and its tooltip. <code>{operator}</code> is the operator as Filter › Operator ›\nnames it (<code>filter.operator.*</code>). A symbol is short: the indicator is about two characters wide, a\nlonger one is cut off.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator: {operator}</code></td>\n<td>the indicator&#39;s name for a screen reader, and the description of the header filter&#39;s input; the tooltip of one that can not be clicked (a column with one operator)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.case</code></td>\n<td><code>Aa</code></td>\n<td>in the header, before the operator of a header filter that matches case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>{operator}, match case</code></td>\n<td>the operator&#39;s name in <code>filter.indicator</code> when it matches case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator: {operator} (F4)</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of the indicator: a click, or F4 in the filter, opens the operator choice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.EQUAL</code></td>\n<td><code>=</code></td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≠</code></td>\n<td>Not Equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>&lt;</code></td>\n<td>Less than, a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≤</code></td>\n<td>Less than or equal, a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Greater than, a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≥</code></td>\n<td>Greater than or equal, a number or date field - the operator a number or date filter starts with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td><code>a..b</code></td>\n<td>Between, a number or date field - typed as <code>from..to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>*a*</code></td>\n<td>Contains, a text field - as the wildcards a text filter takes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>!*a*</code></td>\n<td>Does not contain, a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>a*</code></td>\n<td>Begins with, a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.indicator.END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>*a</code></td>\n<td>Ends with, a text field</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-find\">Find</h3>\n<p>The find bar of Ctrl+F in the grid, see <a href=\"#guide/find\">Find</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>find.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Find in the grid</code></td>\n<td>the name of the find bar (a search region) for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.placeholder</code></td>\n<td><code>Find</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of its input, and the input&#39;s name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.count</code></td>\n<td><code>{current} of {count}</code></td>\n<td>the count of the matches. <code>{current}</code>: the number of the current match, <code>{count}</code>: how many cells match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.none</code></td>\n<td><code>No matches</code></td>\n<td>the count when no cell matches the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case</code></td>\n<td>the <code>Aa</code> button, a toggle: its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.matchCase.text</code></td>\n<td><code>Aa</code></td>\n<td>what the button shows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.matchCase.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case (Alt+C)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.matchCase.tooltip.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip on a Mac: Option+C types <code>ç</code> there, it is no key of the bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.filterRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter rows</code></td>\n<td>the funnel button, a toggle: its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.filterRows.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Show only the rows with a match</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.previous</code></td>\n<td><code>Previous match</code></td>\n<td>the <code>↑</code> button: its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.previous.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Previous match (Shift+Enter)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.previous.tooltip.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>Previous match (⇧Enter)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.next</code></td>\n<td><code>Next match</code></td>\n<td>the <code>↓</code> button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.next.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Next match (Enter)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close</code></td>\n<td>the <code>✕</code> button, closes the find bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.close.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Close (Esc)</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">Keyboard shortcuts help</h3>\n<p>The keyboard shortcuts help - <code>?</code> and Ctrl+/ in the grid, <em>Keyboard shortcuts…</em> in the header menu - see\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">The keyboard shortcuts help</a>. Its title bar, its search\nand the hint under it, then the names of the keys: a key with a name of its own has a text, a letter, a\nfunction key (F2) and the arrows are shown as they are. The modifiers have a text for a Mac. Then each\ngroup of keys: its title, and what each of its keys does.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.title</code></td>\n<td><code>Keyboard shortcuts</code></td>\n<td>its title, its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.title.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Drag to move the dialog</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of its title</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close</code></td>\n<td>the <code>✕</code> button: its name for a screen reader and its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.search</code></td>\n<td><code>Search</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of the search, and its name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.none</code></td>\n<td><code>No key matches the search</code></td>\n<td>under the search when no key has what is typed in it, a status</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>The arrows, Home and End move the caret in a cell. With Alt they move between the cells</code></td>\n<td>under the search, its description for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.hint.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>The arrows, Home and End move the caret in a cell. With ⌥ they move between the cells</code></td>\n<td>the same on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.ctrl</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl</code></td>\n<td>the key Ctrl - the grid takes Cmd for it too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.ctrl.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⌘</code></td>\n<td>the key Ctrl on a Mac: Cmd</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.alt</code></td>\n<td><code>Alt</code></td>\n<td>the key Alt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.alt.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⌥</code></td>\n<td>the key Alt on a Mac: Option</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.shift</code></td>\n<td><code>Shift</code></td>\n<td>the key Shift</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.shift.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⇧</code></td>\n<td>the key Shift on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.enter</code></td>\n<td><code>Enter</code></td>\n<td>the key Enter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.esc</code></td>\n<td><code>Esc</code></td>\n<td>the key Esc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.tab</code></td>\n<td><code>Tab</code></td>\n<td>the key Tab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.space</code></td>\n<td><code>Space</code></td>\n<td>the space bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.home</code></td>\n<td><code>Home</code></td>\n<td>the key Home</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.end</code></td>\n<td><code>End</code></td>\n<td>the key End</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.pageUp</code></td>\n<td><code>Page Up</code></td>\n<td>the key PageUp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.pageDown</code></td>\n<td><code>Page Down</code></td>\n<td>the key PageDown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.delete</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete</code></td>\n<td>the key Delete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.backspace</code></td>\n<td><code>Backspace</code></td>\n<td>the key Backspace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.key.menu</code></td>\n<td><code>Menu</code></td>\n<td>the key the menu key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.move</code></td>\n<td><code>Moving around</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.tab</code></td>\n<td><code>The next / previous cell</code></td>\n<td>what Tab / Shift+Tab do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.sideways</code></td>\n<td><code>The cell to the left / right</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+ArrowLeft / Alt+ArrowRight do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.upDown</code></td>\n<td><code>The cell above / below</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.enter</code></td>\n<td><code>The cell below, from a cell that can be edited</code></td>\n<td>what Enter does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.row</code></td>\n<td><code>The first / last cell of the row</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+Home / Alt+End do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.grid</code></td>\n<td><code>The first / last cell of the grid</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.page</code></td>\n<td><code>A page of rows up / down</code></td>\n<td>what PageUp / PageDown do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.columns</code></td>\n<td><code>A page of columns left / right</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+PageUp / Alt+PageDown do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.move.edge</code></td>\n<td><code>The edge of the data in the column, up / down</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+ArrowUp / Ctrl+ArrowDown do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.select</code></td>\n<td><code>Selecting cells</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.select.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>A range of cells, one cell further each time</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+Shift+Arrows does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.select.page</code></td>\n<td><code>The range a page up / down</code></td>\n<td>what Shift+PageUp / Shift+PageDown do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.select.row</code></td>\n<td><code>The range to the first / last cell of the row</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+Shift+Home / Alt+Shift+End do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.select.grid</code></td>\n<td><code>The range to the first / last cell of the grid</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+Shift+Home / Ctrl+Shift+End do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.select.edge</code></td>\n<td><code>The range to the edge of the data, up / down</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+Shift+ArrowUp / Ctrl+Shift+ArrowDown do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.select.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>No range of cells any more</code></td>\n<td>what Esc does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>In a cell</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.cell.editor</code></td>\n<td><code>Open the editor of a cell of several lines, or of a custom cell</code></td>\n<td>what Enter / F2 do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.cell.save</code></td>\n<td><code>In the editor of several lines: save the text</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+Enter does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.cell.cancel</code></td>\n<td><code>Close an editor or the list of a lookup cell, nothing saved</code></td>\n<td>what Esc does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.cell.list</code></td>\n<td><code>Open the list of a lookup cell</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+ArrowDown / F4 do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.cell.check</code></td>\n<td><code>Check or uncheck a checkbox</code></td>\n<td>what Space does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.cell.link</code></td>\n<td><code>Open the link of a link cell</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+Enter does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.edit</code></td>\n<td><code>Changing values</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.edit.undo</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo, the focus to the cell it changed</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+Z does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.edit.redo</code></td>\n<td><code>Redo</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+Y / Ctrl+Shift+Z do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.edit.fillDown</code></td>\n<td><code>Fill down: the values of the row above, or of the top row of the range</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+D does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.edit.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Empty the cells of a range</code></td>\n<td>what Delete / Backspace do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.edit.deleteRows</code></td>\n<td><code>On a row number: mark its row for deletion, or the selected rows</code></td>\n<td>what Delete / Backspace do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.clipboard</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy and paste</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.clipboard.copy</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy the range of cells, or the value of the cell</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+C does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.clipboard.paste</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste cells copied here or in a spreadsheet</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+V does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.clipboard.columns</code></td>\n<td><code>On a column number: copy the selected columns</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+C does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.find</code></td>\n<td><code>Find</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.find.open</code></td>\n<td><code>Open the find bar</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+F does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.find.next</code></td>\n<td><code>The next match</code></td>\n<td>what Enter / F3 do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.find.previous</code></td>\n<td><code>The previous match</code></td>\n<td>what Shift+Enter / Shift+F3 do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.find.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>Match case on or off</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+C does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.find.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close the find bar</code></td>\n<td>what Esc does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.header</code></td>\n<td><code>Filters, headers and groups</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.header.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>In a header filter: filter</code></td>\n<td>what Enter does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.header.operator</code></td>\n<td><code>In a header filter: choose the operator</code></td>\n<td>what F4 does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.header.label</code></td>\n<td><code>From a header filter: up to its column header</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+ArrowUp does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.header.sort</code></td>\n<td><code>On a column header: sort, again the other way</code></td>\n<td>what Enter / Space do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.header.addSort</code></td>\n<td><code>On a column header: add the column to the sort</code></td>\n<td>what Shift+Enter / Shift+Space do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.header.group</code></td>\n<td><code>On a group row: expand or collapse it</code></td>\n<td>what Enter / Space do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.menus</code></td>\n<td><code>Menus</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.menus.open</code></td>\n<td><code>The menu of the cell, header, filter or number with the focus</code></td>\n<td>what Shift+F10 / the menu key do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.menus.move</code></td>\n<td><code>The next / previous item</code></td>\n<td>what ArrowDown / ArrowUp do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.menus.use</code></td>\n<td><code>Use the item, or open its submenu</code></td>\n<td>what Enter / Space do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.menus.submenu</code></td>\n<td><code>Open / close a submenu</code></td>\n<td>what ArrowRight / ArrowLeft do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.menus.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close the menu</code></td>\n<td>what Esc does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.group.dialogs</code></td>\n<td><code>Dialogs and this help</code></td>\n<td>the title of a group of keys, the caption of its table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.dialogs.help</code></td>\n<td><code>This help, where the key types nothing</code></td>\n<td>what ? does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.dialogs.helpAnywhere</code></td>\n<td><code>This help, also while typing in a cell</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+/ does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.dialogs.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close a dialog</code></td>\n<td>what Esc does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.dialogs.undo</code></td>\n<td><code>In the filter editor: undo / redo</code></td>\n<td>what Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.dialogs.tabs</code></td>\n<td><code>In the grid config dialog: the tab before / after</code></td>\n<td>what ArrowLeft / ArrowRight do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.dialogs.moveField</code></td>\n<td><code>The fields of the detail form: move the field up / down</code></td>\n<td>what Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.dialogs.toggleField</code></td>\n<td><code>The fields of the detail form: in the form or out</code></td>\n<td>what Space does</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-screen-readers\">Screen readers</h3>\n<p>Labels (<code>aria-label</code>) that are read out, not shown. The <a href=\"#guide/accessibility\">keyboard and accessibility\nguide</a> has what a screen reader gets.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>grid.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Data grid</code></td>\n<td>the name of the grid. Give each grid its own in its config: <code>texts: { &#39;grid.label&#39;: &#39;Orders&#39; }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.rowSelector</code></td>\n<td><code>row selector</code></td>\n<td>the column header of the row numbers, the top left corner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter {label}</code></td>\n<td>a header filter. <code>{label}</code>: the column&#39;s label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.groupToggle</code></td>\n<td><code>{name} ({total})</code></td>\n<td>the expand/collapse button of a group row, which also says whether it is expanded. <code>{name}</code>: the group&#39;s name, <code>Country: Norway</code>; <code>{total}</code>: its number of rows. The row shows <code>{name} ({total})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.removeGroup</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove the grouping on {title}</code></td>\n<td>the x on a group chip in the panel above the grid. <code>{title}</code>: the title of the grouping</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-row-state-and-edited-cells\">Row state and edited cells</h3>\n<p>The state of a row on its row number (<code>GridConfig.rowStatus</code>) and the marker of an edited cell\n(<code>GridConfig.editedMarker</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Editing</a>. The state is the\ntooltip&#39;s first line and the text a screen reader reads with the row number.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.new</code></td>\n<td><code>New row</code></td>\n<td>a new row: the tooltip of its row number, and for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.edited</code></td>\n<td><code>Edited</code></td>\n<td>an edited row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.deleted</code></td>\n<td><code>Marked for deletion</code></td>\n<td>a row marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.conflict</code></td>\n<td><code>Changed on the server too: {fields}</code></td>\n<td>a row with fields in conflict (<code>datasource.getConflicts()</code>): the tooltip of its row number, and for a screen reader. <code>{fields}</code>: their column labels, joined with <code>, </code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.removedOnServer</code></td>\n<td><code>Removed on the server, it has changes here</code></td>\n<td>a row the server removed (<code>upsert(rows, { removeMissing: true })</code>) that has changes here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.error</code></td>\n<td><code>Error: {errors}</code></td>\n<td>a row with an error, for a screen reader (the tooltip has the errors, a line each). <code>{errors}</code>: the server&#39;s error and the fields&#39; errors, <code>Age: Must be at least 18</code>, joined with <code>, </code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.editedMarker.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Original value: {value}</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of the marker of an edited cell. <code>{value}</code>: the value before the edit, shown with the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.editedMarker.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>(blank)</code></td>\n<td>the <code>{value}</code> of an original value that is blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.editedMarker.revert</code></td>\n<td><code>Click to revert</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip&#39;s last line, in an editable grid: a click on the marker puts the original value back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.conflictMarker.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Changed on the server to {value} while you edited it</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of the marker of a field in conflict. <code>{value}</code>: the server&#39;s value, shown with the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.conflictMarker.take</code></td>\n<td><code>Click to take the server&#39;s value</code></td>\n<td>its last line, in an editable grid: a click on the marker takes the server&#39;s value</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-multiline-cells\">Multiline cells</h3>\n<p>A cell of several lines (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>, or a value with a line break) and the editor that\nopens over it, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cell.icon.high</code></td>\n<td><code>High</code></td>\n<td>for a screen reader: the icon of an icon set (<code>ColumnFormat.icons</code>) of a value from the second threshold, said after the value. Hidden on screen, the cell has the icon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.icon.middle</code></td>\n<td><code>Middle</code></td>\n<td>from the first threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.icon.low</code></td>\n<td><code>Low</code></td>\n<td>below it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.more</code></td>\n<td><code>More lines, Enter shows all</code></td>\n<td>for a screen reader: the description of a cell that shows the first of its lines. Hidden on screen, the cell has a marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.moreLines</code></td>\n<td><code>… {count} more lines</code></td>\n<td>the last line of the cell&#39;s tooltip when its text has more lines than the tooltip shows (<code>multilineTooltipLines</code>). <code>{count}</code>: how many are not shown, 2 or more - one line more is shown instead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+Enter to save, Esc to cancel</code></td>\n<td>under the textarea of the editor. Cmd+Enter saves too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.hint.mac</code></td>\n<td><code>⌘Enter to save, Esc to cancel</code></td>\n<td>the same on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.hintReadonly</code></td>\n<td><code>Esc to close</code></td>\n<td>under the textarea of the editor of a readonly cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Save and close</code></td>\n<td>the ✕ at the right of the editor&#39;s hint: its name and tooltip - it saves and closes, as a click beside the editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.closeReadonly</code></td>\n<td><code>Close</code></td>\n<td>the same ✕ in the editor of a readonly cell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-lookup-columns\">Lookup columns</h3>\n<p>A column whose value is picked from a list (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>), see\n<a href=\"#guide/lookups\">Lookups</a>. The dropdown grid has the texts of a grid, the labels of its columns are\nits config&#39;s.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>lookup.open</code></td>\n<td><code>Open the list</code></td>\n<td>for a screen reader: the name of the button at the right of the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lookup.search</code></td>\n<td><code>Search</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of the search field of the select</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lookup.noMatch</code></td>\n<td><code>No matches</code></td>\n<td>under the search of the select when no label has the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lookup.more</code></td>\n<td><code>{shown} of {count} shown, type to search</code></td>\n<td>under the list of the select when it has more options than it shows. <code>{shown}</code>: the options shown (500), <code>{count}</code>: the options the search finds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-custom-cells\">Custom cells</h3>\n<p>A cell drawn by a renderer of your own (<code>ColumnConfig.renderer</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/custom-cells/editing\">Custom cells</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cell.editor.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Enter to save, Esc to cancel</code></td>\n<td>under the input of the editor of one line that opens over the cell. A cell of several lines gets the multiline editor and its texts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-date-picker\">Date picker</h3>\n<p>Of a date cell, a date header filter and the filter menu&#39;s range block, and a <code>DateInterface</code> of your\nown. The range picker shows its From and To as the day, the month&#39;s name and the year: <code>22 November 2026</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.hours</code></td>\n<td><code>HH</code></td>\n<td>label in front of the hours, and their name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.minutes</code></td>\n<td><code>MM</code></td>\n<td>label in front of the minutes, and their name for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.week</code></td>\n<td><code>W</code></td>\n<td>header of the week numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear</code></td>\n<td>button, empties the date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.now</code></td>\n<td><code>Now</code></td>\n<td>button, goes to now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.use</code></td>\n<td><code>Use</code></td>\n<td>button, picks the date (the range of a range picker)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.from</code></td>\n<td><code>From</code></td>\n<td>a range picker (a date header filter with Between, From and To of the filter menu): the label over the first day picked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.to</code></td>\n<td><code>To</code></td>\n<td>a range picker: the label over the last day picked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.rangeOpen</code></td>\n<td><code>–</code></td>\n<td>a range picker: under From or To while that day is not picked, an open end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.previousYear</code></td>\n<td><code>Previous year</code></td>\n<td>the « button left of the month: its name for a screen reader and its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.previousMonth</code></td>\n<td><code>Previous month</code></td>\n<td>the ‹ button left of the month</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.nextMonth</code></td>\n<td><code>Next month</code></td>\n<td>the › button right of the month</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.nextYear</code></td>\n<td><code>Next year</code></td>\n<td>the » button right of the month</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.january</code></td>\n<td><code>January</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.february</code></td>\n<td><code>February</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.march</code></td>\n<td><code>March</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.april</code></td>\n<td><code>April</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.may</code></td>\n<td><code>May</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.june</code></td>\n<td><code>June</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.july</code></td>\n<td><code>July</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.august</code></td>\n<td><code>August</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.september</code></td>\n<td><code>September</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.october</code></td>\n<td><code>October</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.november</code></td>\n<td><code>November</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.december</code></td>\n<td><code>December</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.sunday</code></td>\n<td><code>Su</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.monday</code></td>\n<td><code>Mo</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.tuesday</code></td>\n<td><code>Tu</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.wednesday</code></td>\n<td><code>We</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.thursday</code></td>\n<td><code>Th</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.friday</code></td>\n<td><code>Fr</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.saturday</code></td>\n<td><code>Sa</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-validation-messages\">Validation messages</h3>\n<p>Made by the datasource: <code>setTexts()</code> only. <code>{value}</code> is the rule&#39;s <code>value</code>, and every option of the\nrule can be in a message by its name.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>validation.required</code></td>\n<td><code>Required</code></td>\n<td><code>required</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.min</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value}</code></td>\n<td><code>min</code>, <code>{value}</code> is the rule&#39;s value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.max</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value}</code></td>\n<td><code>max</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.minLength</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value} characters</code></td>\n<td><code>minLength</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.maxLength</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value} characters</code></td>\n<td><code>maxLength</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.pattern</code></td>\n<td><code>Not in the expected format</code></td>\n<td><code>pattern</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.integer</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be a whole number</code></td>\n<td><code>integer</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.decimals</code></td>\n<td><code>Must have at most {value} decimals</code></td>\n<td><code>decimals</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.oneOf</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be one of {value}</code></td>\n<td><code>oneOf</code>: the list, <code>Norway, Sweden</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.email</code></td>\n<td><code>Not an email address</code></td>\n<td><code>email</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.unique</code></td>\n<td><code>Another row has the same value</code></td>\n<td><code>unique</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.uniqueWith</code></td>\n<td><code>Another row has the same value and {with}</code></td>\n<td><code>unique</code> with <code>with</code>: <code>{with}</code> is the labels of those fields, <code>Year, Month</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-getfilterstring\">getFilterString()</h3>\n<p>Made by the datasource: <code>setTexts()</code> only. The whole string is upper cased - the values of a condition\nthat matches case excepted: <code>&#39;filterString.operator.EQUAL&#39;: &#39;equal_to&#39;</code> is shown as <code>&lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt;</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterString.and</code></td>\n<td><code>AND</code></td>\n<td>joins the conditions of an AND group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.or</code></td>\n<td><code>OR</code></td>\n<td>joins the conditions of an OR group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.betweenAnd</code></td>\n<td><code>and</code></td>\n<td>between the two values of a <code>BETWEEN</code>: <code>&#39;10&#39; &lt;&lt;AND&gt;&gt; &#39;20&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.matchCase</code></td>\n<td><code>match_case</code></td>\n<td>after a condition that matches case: <code>[NAME] &lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt; &#39;Ann&#39; &lt;&lt;MATCH_CASE&gt;&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.EQUAL</code></td>\n<td><code>equal_to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>less_or_equal</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>greater_than_or_equal</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>less_than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>greater_than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.BETWEEN</code></td>\n<td><code>between</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>not_equal_to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>does_not_contain</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>start_with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>end_with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.IN</code></td>\n<td><code>IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td><code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-grouping\">Grouping</h3>\n<p>Made by the datasource: <code>setTexts()</code> only.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>grouping.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>blank</code></td>\n<td>in <code>&#39;Country: blank&#39;</code>, the name of the group of the empty values</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},{"id":"theming","title":"Theming","html":"<p>Every colour of the grid is a css variable: the grid itself, its menus, the column chooser, the\ntooltips, the filter editor and the date picker. Each variable has a light and a dark colour, and the\ngrid follows the <code>color-scheme</code> of the page: dark mode is one line of css. Two themes come with the\ngrid, the default and <a href=\"#guide/theming/themes\">TreDeSpace</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"theming\"></div>\n<p>The variables of the grid are set on <code>.simple-html-grid</code> in <code>grid.css</code>, the ones of the date picker\non <code>.simple-html-date</code> in <code>date.css</code>. The tables at the end list them all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-themes\">Themes</h2>\n<p>The grid comes with two themes, each light and dark - four looks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Default</strong> - <code>grid.css</code> and <code>date.css</code>, the colours of the tables below</li>\n<li><strong>TreDeSpace</strong> - <code>theme-tredespace.css</code> on top of them: the colours of the TreDeSpace UI widget\nlibrary (<code>@treDeSpaceUI</code>), for a page built with it. Its panels, menus, dialogs and fields, its\nselection and focus blue, its reds, ambers and greens</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are flat and square: no rounded corners, in light and in dark - the widgets have none either.\nThe sizes are the grid&#39;s in both (11px text, the row heights of the grid options).</p>\n<p>The docs site has all four: the sidebar&#39;s <em>Grid theme</em> (Default, TreDeSpace) and <em>Toggle dark/light</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-turning-tredespace-on\">Turning TreDeSpace on</h3>\n<p>Load the file after the grid&#39;s, and put the attribute <code>data-grid-theme=&quot;tredespace&quot;</code> on the root of\nthe page:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/date.css';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/theme-tredespace.css';</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"html\"><code>&lt;html data-grid-theme=&quot;tredespace&quot;&gt;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Every grid and date picker under the attribute takes it. Put it on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>, as\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/dark-mode\"><code>color-scheme</code></a>: the menus, the column chooser, the tooltips and the filter editor\nare put on the body (or in the dialog or popover the grid is in). On the grid&#39;s container only, the\ngrid would have the theme and its menus the default</li>\n<li>Without the attribute the grid has the default theme. So both can be loaded, and the attribute\nswitches: <code>document.documentElement.dataset.gridTheme = &#39;tredespace&#39;</code>, and\n<code>delete document.documentElement.dataset.gridTheme</code> for the default</li>\n<li>Light or dark follows <code>color-scheme</code>, as in the default theme. The widget library sets it itself\n(dark is its default, <code>data-theme=&quot;light&quot;</code> on the root makes it light), so the grid switches with it:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"html\"><code>&lt;!-- dark, the library's default --&gt;\n&lt;html data-grid-theme=&quot;tredespace&quot;&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- light --&gt;\n&lt;html data-theme=&quot;light&quot; data-grid-theme=&quot;tredespace&quot;&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>On a page without the library, set <code>color-scheme</code> as for the default theme.</p>\n<p>To change a colour of the TreDeSpace theme, use its selector - it is stronger than\n<code>.simple-html-grid</code>, so a rule on <code>.simple-html-grid</code> does not reach a TreDeSpace grid:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>[data-grid-theme='tredespace'] .simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-focus-color: light-dark(#b45309, #fbbf24);\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-colours-of-tredespace\">The colours of TreDeSpace</h3>\n<p>The colours are the library&#39;s tokens: its <code>styles.css</code> (its light theme, and its dark surfaces lifted\nhalf a step) over the Tailwind palette it is built on. Where the grid has a part the widgets do not,\nthe colour is made from the library&#39;s scale. The light colour is the token in the library&#39;s light\ntheme, the dark one in its dark theme. A Tailwind colour the library does not change is written as\nTailwind&#39;s own <code>oklch()</code> in the file, so it is the same colour as in the widgets; the table has it as\na screen shows it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>the grid&#39;s</th>\n<th>token (as in the library)</th>\n<th>light</th>\n<th>dark</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>bars - labels, panel, footer, row numbers - menus, filter editor, date picker</td>\n<td>slate-900 (its Menu, dialogs, side bars)</td>\n<td><code>#f8fafc</code></td>\n<td><code>#172033</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rows, the filter row, the inputs, the filter chips</td>\n<td>slate-950 (the ground, the search field of its Select)</td>\n<td><code>#f1f5f9</code></td>\n<td><code>#080f21</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>every other row</td>\n<td>midway slate-950 - slate-900</td>\n<td><code>#f5f7fb</code></td>\n<td><code>#0f182a</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lines between cells and rows, menu frames</td>\n<td>slate-800 (<code>--dock-line</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#e2e8f0</code></td>\n<td><code>#283548</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stronger lines: pinned columns, under the header, the filter editor&#39;s frame</td>\n<td>slate-700 (<code>--dock-line-hi</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#cbd5e1</code></td>\n<td><code>#314158</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>slate-200 (<code>--dock-text</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#1e293b</code></td>\n<td><code>#e2e8f0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>placeholders, quiet text, row state &quot;deleted&quot;, aggregate labels</td>\n<td>slate-400 (<code>--dock-text-dim</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#475569</code></td>\n<td><code>#90a1b9</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>selected rows, menu item under the pointer, the active option</td>\n<td>blue-950 (its selected TreeView row, Select)</td>\n<td><code>#dbeafe</code></td>\n<td><code>#162556</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the other selected rows</td>\n<td>midway blue-950 - blue-900</td>\n<td><code>#cde2fe</code></td>\n<td><code>#192f72</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>focus outline, the line of a range, Apply, the value list&#39;s checkboxes</td>\n<td>blue-400 (<code>--dock-accent</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#2563eb</code></td>\n<td><code>#51a2ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>links (a link column, the column settings&#39; preview)</td>\n<td>blue-800, blue-100</td>\n<td><code>#1e40af</code></td>\n<td><code>#dbeafe</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the colour rules&#39; text colours</td>\n<td>red, blue, indigo, purple, pink 800, orange, amber, yellow, lime, green, teal, cyan 900, slate-700 in light; their 300 in dark. Brown, which Tailwind does not have: orange&#39;s hue with less chroma</td>\n<td><code>#9f0712</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>#ffa2a2</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the colour rules&#39; backgrounds</td>\n<td>red, orange, yellow, lime, green, teal, sky, indigo, purple, pink 200, amber and cyan 300 (apart from yellow and blue) and slate-300 in light; their 950, slate-800 in dark. Blue is sky: blue-200 and blue-950 are the selected rows</td>\n<td><code>#ffc9c9</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>#460809</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the icons of an icon set: green, amber, red</td>\n<td>green-800, amber-800, red-700 in light; their 300 in dark</td>\n<td><code>#166534</code> <code>#92400e</code> <code>#b91c1c</code></td>\n<td><code>#7bf1a8</code> <code>#ffd230</code> <code>#ffa2a2</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>text on Apply</td>\n<td>slate-950 (its picked day)</td>\n<td><code>#f1f5f9</code></td>\n<td><code>#080f21</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>frames of the controls of the value list and the filter editor</td>\n<td>slate-500</td>\n<td><code>#64748b</code></td>\n<td><code>#62748e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a range&#39;s tint</td>\n<td>blue-500 at 15% (its info chip)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>errors</td>\n<td>red-400, red-500 at 15% (its danger chip)</td>\n<td><code>#dc2626</code></td>\n<td><code>#ff6467</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>row state: edited, new, conflict</td>\n<td>amber-400, green-400, violet-400 (light: violet-600)</td>\n<td><code>#b45309</code> <code>#15803d</code> <code>#7c3aed</code></td>\n<td><code>#ffb900</code> <code>#05df72</code> <code>#a684ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Find</td>\n<td>amber-500 at 25% and 45%, a line in amber-200</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>totals row</td>\n<td>slate-800, text slate-100</td>\n<td><code>#e2e8f0</code></td>\n<td><code>#283548</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scrollbar thumbs</td>\n<td>blue-400 45% into slate-800 (its scrollbars)</td>\n<td><code>#8dacee</code></td>\n<td><code>#3a669d</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tooltips</td>\n<td>its Tooltip, dark in both</td>\n<td><code>#242933</code></td>\n<td><code>#242933</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>shadows, the layer behind the filter editor</td>\n<td>black at 50%, 45% (tooltips), 40%</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>font</td>\n<td><code>ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif</code> (its panels)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>theme-tredespace.css</code> names the token of every variable. The contrasts are the default theme&#39;s:\ntext 4.5:1 or more to its background, lines and marks 3:1, light and dark (WCAG AA). Six colours\ndiffer from the widgets for that, or because the grid has one colour where the widgets have two:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>placeholders and quiet text are slate-400: the library&#39;s placeholder slate-500 is under 4.5:1</li>\n<li>the frames of the value list&#39;s and the filter editor&#39;s controls are slate-500: the library&#39;s field\nframes (slate-700) are under 3:1 to a menu</li>\n<li>the filled controls (Apply, the AND / OR in use) are blue-400 with slate-950 text, as the\nlibrary&#39;s checked box and picked day - its primary Button is blue-950 in a blue-400 frame, a fill\nunder 3:1 to the dialog</li>\n<li>the date picker&#39;s selected day is blue-950 in a blue-400 frame (the library&#39;s range fill): its\npicked day (blue-400) has slate-950 text, the date picker&#39;s text is slate-200. The days between From\nand To of a range picker are blue-950 too, the library&#39;s span: From and To have the frame</li>\n<li>the line of the current match of Find is amber-200: amber-400 is under 3:1 to the match&#39;s tint on a\nselected row</li>\n<li>the links are blue-800 in light and blue-100 in dark, not the accent blue-400: a link&#39;s text keeps\n4.5:1 under the tints of Find and a range on a selected row, blue-400 has 2.2:1 there</li>\n</ul>\n<p>And: a menu&#39;s frame is the grid&#39;s line (slate-800, the library&#39;s Menu has slate-700), a menu item\nunder the pointer is the selected blue (the library&#39;s Menu has slate-800, its Select blue-950), the\ncheckboxes in the cells are the browser&#39;s (in both themes), and the text is 11px (the widgets&#39; 12px).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-theme-of-your-own\">A theme of your own</h2>\n<p>Set the variables you want to change on <code>.simple-html-grid</code>, in a css file loaded after <code>grid.css</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-odd: light-dark(#b9e4dc, #0f5c52);\n    --simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-even: light-dark(#a7dbd1, #11685d);\n    --simple-html-grid-focus-color: light-dark(#b45309, #fbbf24);\n    --simple-html-grid-font-size: 12px;\n}</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>light-dark(&lt;light&gt;, &lt;dark&gt;)</code> gives a colour for each, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/dark-mode\">Dark mode</a>. A plain colour\n(<code>#b9e4dc</code>) is used in both.</li>\n<li>Same selector, so the rule that comes last wins: load your css after <code>grid.css</code>, or make your\nselector stronger (<code>.simple-html-grid.simple-html-grid</code>).</li>\n<li>The menus, the column chooser, the tooltips and the filter editor&#39;s dialog are not inside the grid&#39;s\nelement: the grid puts them on the body (or in the dialog or popover the grid is in). They are\nelements with the <code>simple-html-grid</code> class too, so a rule on <code>.simple-html-grid</code> reaches them.\nA rule on the grid&#39;s container (<code>.my-page .simple-html-grid</code>) only reaches the grid itself, its\nmenus keep the default colours.</li>\n<li>The grid&#39;s date picker is a <code>.simple-html-date</code> in a menu: set its own variables, see\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/the-date-picker\">The date picker</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What the classes and parts of the grid are, if you want to restyle more than the colours: read\n<code>grid.css</code>, it is commented.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-dark-mode\">Dark mode</h2>\n<p>Each colour is set with the css function <code>light-dark()</code>: the first colour when the element&#39;s\n<code>color-scheme</code> is light, the second when it is dark. <code>color-scheme</code> is inherited, so it is set once\nfor the page:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* always dark */\n:root {\n    color-scheme: dark;\n}\n\n/* dark when the page has a dark class */\nbody.dark {\n    color-scheme: dark;\n}\n\n/* follow the setting of the OS / browser */\n:root {\n    color-scheme: light dark;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>&lt;meta name=&quot;color-scheme&quot; content=&quot;light dark&quot;&gt;</code> in the page&#39;s head does the same as the last one.\nWithout any of these the <code>color-scheme</code> is <code>normal</code>, and the grid is light: the colours it had before\nv6.</p>\n<p>Set it on <code>:root</code> or the body, not on the grid&#39;s container. The menus, the column chooser, the\ntooltips and the filter editor are put on the body (or in the dialog or popover the grid is in): set\non the container only, the grid would be dark and its menus light.</p>\n<p>The browser&#39;s own parts follow <code>color-scheme</code> on their own: the checkboxes, the number inputs of the\ndate picker, scrollbars. The grid&#39;s scrollbars have their colours from the grid&#39;s variables.</p>\n<p><code>light-dark()</code> is in Chrome and Edge 123, Firefox 120 and Safari 17.5 (May 2024). In an older browser\na colour set with it is not valid: the grid has no backgrounds and the text colour of the page. To\nsupport one, set the variables with plain colours for it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>@supports not (color: light-dark(white, black)) {\n    .simple-html-grid {\n        --simple-html-grid-main-bg-color: #f5f5f5;\n        /* ... the light colour of every variable in the table below */\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keyboard-focus\">The keyboard focus</h2>\n<p>The element that has the keyboard focus gets an outline of 2px in <code>--simple-html-grid-focus-color</code>:\na cell, a filter input, a checkbox, a menu item, a button of the filter editor, the date picker. The\noutline is inside the element (<code>outline-offset: -2px</code>): the cells are packed edge to edge, an outline\noutside the cell would be painted over by the next one or cut off at the edge of the grid. A checkbox\nin a cell has it on the cell. The buttons of the filter editor and of the filter menu&#39;s value list,\nand the value list&#39;s checkboxes, have room around them: theirs is outside (<code>outline-offset: 1px</code>),\nan outline inside a filled button would not show.</p>\n<p>It is drawn for <code>:focus-visible</code>, so the browser decides when it shows: when you move with the\nkeyboard, and in an input you type in; not when you click a checkbox or a button. It replaces the\ndashed border a focused input had before v6.</p>\n<p>To change more than the colour, override the rule:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid :focus-visible {\n    outline-width: 3px;\n    outline-offset: -3px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The date picker draws the same outline in <code>--simple-html-date-focus-color</code>. In the grid it takes the\ngrid&#39;s colour: <code>grid.css</code> sets <code>--simple-html-date-focus-color</code> to <code>--simple-html-grid-focus-color</code> on\n<code>.simple-html-grid .simple-html-date</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-row-state-and-errors\">The row state and errors</h2>\n<p>The grid shows the state of each row on its row number and marks the edited cells (<code>rowStatus</code> and\n<code>editedMarker</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a>), in these\nvariables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-new-color</code> - the plus of a new row</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-edited-color</code> - the pencil of an edited row, the marker of an edited cell</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-deleted-color</code> - the trash can of a row marked for deletion</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-conflict-color</code> - a conflict with the server: the arrows of the row number, the\nmarker of a field in conflict, see <a href=\"#guide/live-data/how-the-grid-shows-them\">Live data</a></li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-merged-color</code> - the flash of a cell a merge changed (<code>mergeHighlight</code>), it fades\nout: a see-through colour</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code> and <code>--simple-html-grid-error-bg-color</code> - a cell and a row with an\nerror: the border of the cell, the bar and the exclamation mark of the row number, the tint, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/how-the-grid-shows-the-errors\">How the grid shows the errors</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The icons and the marker are graphics: the new, edited, deleted and conflict colours have a contrast of at least\n3:1 to the rows, the row numbers and the selected rows, in light and in dark, also under an error&#39;s\ntint (WCAG 1.4.11). Keep that when you change them. Each state has a shape of its own too, the colour is\nnot the only thing that tells them apart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-multiline-text\">Multiline text</h2>\n<p>A cell of several lines (see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>) has a marker at its\nright, lines of text, in <code>--simple-html-grid-multiline-color</code> - at least 3:1 to the rows and the\nselected rows too. In a row made taller (<code>fitRows()</code>) the cell shows its other lines under the first\none, in the cell&#39;s text colour (<code>--simple-html-grid-main-font-color</code>): an element of their own,\n<code>simple-html-grid-cell-lines</code>, with the classes the <code>cellClass</code> hook gives the input. Their line\nheight is the grid option <code>multilineLineHeight</code> (15px), not a css variable: the grid needs it to\nplace the rows. With a larger <code>--simple-html-grid-font-size</code> make it and <code>cellHeight</code> larger too. The editor that\nopens over the cell uses the colours of the rest: the textarea is\n<code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code> with <code>--simple-html-grid-main-font-color</code>, its hint\n<code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-color</code>, its frame <code>--simple-html-grid-pinned-border</code> and its shadow\n<code>--simple-html-grid-dialog-shadow-color</code>. The editor is on the body (or in the dialog the grid is in),\nlike the menus: a theme set on <code>.simple-html-grid</code> reaches it, one set on the grid&#39;s container does not.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-edited-color: light-dark(#1d4ed8, #93c5fd);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The classes are there to restyle more: <code>row-is-new</code>, <code>row-is-modified</code> and <code>row-is-deleted</code> on the\nelement around the row number, <code>simple-html-row-status-icon</code> on the icon (with\n<code>simple-html-row-status-new</code>, <code>-edited</code>, <code>-deleted</code> or <code>-error</code>), <code>simple-html-cell-edited-marker</code> on the\nmarker. With <code>rowStatus: false</code> the classes <code>row-is-*</code> are still there for a look of your own:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.row-is-modified .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#4f46e5, #a5b4fc);\n}\n\n.row-is-new .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#16a34a, #86efac);\n}\n\n.row-is-deleted .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#dc2626, #fca5a5);\n    text-decoration: line-through;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</h2>\n<p>A range of cells the user selected (see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>) is\na tint over its cells and a 2px line around it, in two variables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-range-bg-color</code> - laid over the cells, under their text: over the colour of\nthe row, a selected row&#39;s and an error&#39;s tint. A colour with alpha, so those still show through</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-range-border-color</code> - the line on the outer sides of the range</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The defaults keep the text at 4.5:1 or more over the tint, and the line at 3:1 or more against the\nrows, the selected rows and an error&#39;s tint, in light and in dark (WCAG 1.4.3 and 1.4.11). Keep that\nwhen you change them.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-range-bg-color: light-dark(rgba(22, 163, 74, 0.12), rgba(74, 222, 128, 0.18));\n    --simple-html-grid-range-border-color: light-dark(#166534, #bbf7d0);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The classes: <code>simple-html-cell-in-range</code> on every cell in a range, and <code>simple-html-cell-range-top</code>,\n<code>-bottom</code>, <code>-left</code> and <code>-right</code> on the cells whose side is an edge of the ranges. The line is a\n<code>::after</code> of the cell, so a rule of your own can restyle it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* a dashed line, as a cut in a spreadsheet */\n.simple-html-grid .simple-html-cell-in-range::after {\n    border-style: dashed;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-find\">Find</h2>\n<p>Find (Ctrl+F in the grid, see <a href=\"#guide/find\">Find</a>) marks the cells that show the text with a tint, the\ncurrent match with a stronger tint and a 2px line inside its edge:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-find-match-color</code> - laid over a cell that matches, under its text: yellow in\nlight, amber in dark. A colour with alpha, so the row&#39;s colour and a range&#39;s tint show through</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-find-current-color</code> - laid over the current match instead: orange</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-find-current-border-color</code> - the line around the current match</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The defaults keep the text at 4.5:1 or more over both tints (on the rows and on the selected rows), and\nthe line at 3:1 or more against the rows, the selected rows and the current match&#39;s tint, in light and\nin dark (WCAG 1.4.3 and 1.4.11): the line tells the current match, not the colour alone. The tint of the\nother matches is a highlighter&#39;s, not a line - it is not 3:1 against the rows. Keep that when you change\nthem.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-find-match-color: light-dark(rgba(34, 197, 94, 0.3), rgba(21, 128, 61, 0.5));\n    --simple-html-grid-find-current-border-color: light-dark(#14532d, #bbf7d0);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The classes: <code>simple-html-cell-find-match</code> on every match, <code>simple-html-cell-find-current</code> on the\ncurrent one too. The find bar is <code>simple-html-grid-find</code>, in the grid&#39;s colours: the panel&#39;s background\n(<code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-color</code>), the input of the header filters (<code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code>),\nthe frame and shadow of the tooltips. Its <em>Match case</em> button (<code>simple-html-grid-find-case</code>, the text\n<code>Aa</code>) and <em>Filter rows</em> button (<code>simple-html-grid-find-filter</code>) are filled with\n<code>--simple-html-grid-accent-color</code> while they are on (<code>aria-pressed=&quot;true&quot;</code>), the <code>Aa</code> and the funnel in\n<code>--simple-html-grid-accent-font-color</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-lookup-columns\">Lookup columns</h2>\n<p>A column whose value is picked from a list (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/lookups\">Lookups</a>) has a\nbutton at the right of its cells, on hover and on the focused cell: square, inside the cell&#39;s focus\noutline, <code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-button-bg-color</code> with its triangle in\n<code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-icon-color</code> - at least 3:1 to the button - and\n<code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-even</code> under the pointer. The select under the cell has the colours\nof the menus (<code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-color</code>), its search field the header filters&#39;\n(<code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code>), its frame and shadow the editor of a multiline cell&#39;s; the active\noption is <code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-active-bg-color</code>, the text on it 4.5:1. The dropdown grid is a\ngrid, in the grid&#39;s colours.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-lookup-icon-color: light-dark(#1d4ed8, #93c5fd);\n    --simple-html-grid-lookup-active-bg-color: light-dark(#fef3c7, #78350f);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The classes: <code>simple-html-grid-lookup-button</code> on the button, <code>simple-html-grid-cell-lookup</code> on the input\n(its text stops before the button), <code>simple-html-grid-lookup</code> on the popover (with\n<code>simple-html-grid-lookup-select</code> or <code>simple-html-grid-lookup-grid</code>), <code>simple-html-grid-lookup-option</code> on\nan option, <code>-active</code> and <code>-current</code> (the cell&#39;s value, bold with a check) on top.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-colour-rules\">Colour rules</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">colour rules</a> of a column colour its cells from a palette: 14\ncolours, each a text colour and a background - two variables of the theme each, light and dark:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-&lt;colour&gt;-color</code> - the text colour: red, orange, amber, yellow, lime,\ngreen, teal, cyan, blue, indigo, purple, pink, brown, grey (the order of the swatches). At least 4.5:1\nto the rows and the selected rows, under a range&#39;s tint too, to the inputs (the detail form, the\ndialog&#39;s preview) and to every background of the palette</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-&lt;colour&gt;-bg-color</code> - the background: the grid&#39;s text and a link at least\n4.5:1 on it, under a range&#39;s tint too; the focus outline, the row state, the marker of an edited cell\nand of more lines 3:1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So any text colour on any background stays readable, in light and in dark. The rows of the default\ntheme&#39;s dark look are grey (<code>#4b5563</code>, <code>#5a6370</code>): 4.5:1 to them is a light tint of a colour, so its\ntext colours are pale there - the backgrounds show the colours better. TreDeSpace&#39;s dark rows are near\nblack: its text colours are stronger (Tailwind 300). The grid writes no colour of its own for the\npalette: a rule takes a name of it.</p>\n<p><strong>A custom colour</strong> (<code>#rrggbb</code>, picked in the dialog&#39;s colour picker after the swatches) is no variable\nof the theme: it is one colour, in light and in dark, so the grid keeps it readable itself. It is set on\nthe cell as a custom property with <code>style.setProperty()</code> - its value checked, never css text:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-rule-bg</code> - a custom background, the same in light and dark. A rule without a text\ncolour gets black or white text on it, whichever has the more contrast.</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-rule-text-light</code>, <code>--simple-html-grid-rule-text-dark</code> - a custom text colour: the\ncolour picked in light, and in dark the same colour lighter (its lightness raised) until it has 4.5:1\nto <code>#5a6370</code>, the lightest dark row of the two themes. On a custom background it is the colour picked\nin both. The grid config dialog warns when a custom text colour has less than 4.5:1 to the light rows,\nor to its custom background - the pick is the user&#39;s, the grid keeps it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The classes are <code>simple-html-grid-cell-text-custom</code> and <code>simple-html-grid-cell-bg-custom</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* grid.css */\n.simple-html-grid .simple-html-grid-cell-text-custom {\n    color: light-dark(var(--simple-html-grid-rule-text-light), var(--simple-html-grid-rule-text-dark));\n}\n.simple-html-grid .simple-html-grid-cell-bg-custom {\n    background-color: var(--simple-html-grid-rule-bg);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Keep those contrasts when you change one:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* a lighter orange background in light, a stronger one in dark */\n.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-palette-orange-bg-color: light-dark(#ffedd5, #9a3412);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The classes, on the cell element (<code>simple-html-grid-col-cell</code>) and on a value of the detail form:\n<code>simple-html-grid-cell-text</code> and <code>simple-html-grid-cell-text-&lt;colour&gt;</code> for a text colour,\n<code>simple-html-grid-cell-bg</code> and <code>simple-html-grid-cell-bg-&lt;colour&gt;</code> for a background,\n<code>simple-html-grid-cell-bold</code> for bold. The cell&#39;s text - its input, the lines of a multiline cell, a\ncustom cell&#39;s host, a link - takes the cell&#39;s colour (<code>color: inherit</code>). A cell with a validation error\n(<code>simple-html-cell-error</code>) or a match of Find (<code>simple-html-cell-find-match</code>) shows as without a rule:\nthe classes are there, the colours wait. A rule of your own can restyle them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* the cells with grey text in italics too */\n.simple-html-grid .simple-html-grid-cell-text-grey input {\n    font-style: italic;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The swatches of the grid config dialog are <code>column-settings-swatch</code> radio buttons, square, in the\npalette&#39;s variables; the colour picker of a custom colour is <code>column-settings-swatch-custom</code>, an\n<code>&lt;input type=&quot;color&quot;&gt;</code> in a box of the swatches&#39; frame (a <code>+</code> until a colour is picked).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-bars-colour-scales-and-icons\">Data bars, colour scales and icons</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"#guide/formatting/data-bars\">data bars</a> and <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-scales\">colour scales</a> of a\nnumber column are drawn in the <strong>backgrounds of the palette</strong>, <code>--simple-html-grid-palette-&lt;colour&gt;-bg-color</code>:\nthe grid&#39;s text reads on every one of them, and on the mix of two of them a scale gives a cell, light\nand dark, in both themes - the theme tests compute it. A theme that changes the palette changes them\ntoo. They have no variables of their own but the icons&#39;:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-icon-green-color</code>, <code>--simple-html-grid-icon-amber-color</code>,\n<code>--simple-html-grid-icon-red-color</code> - the icons of an <a href=\"#guide/formatting/icon-sets\">icon set</a>: the high,\nthe middle and the low one. Marks, at least 3:1 to the rows, the selected rows (under a range&#39;s tint\ntoo) and every background of the palette. Tailwind&#39;s green-800, amber-800 and red-700 in light, green\nand amber 300 and red-200 in dark (red-300 is under 3:1 to a range&#39;s tint on the light dark row) -\nTreDeSpace&#39;s are the same in light and its 300 in dark</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A custom colour</strong> of a bar or a scale is a tint of it, computed by the grid: a fifth of the colour and\nthe rest white in light mode, black in dark mode - so the text reads on it whatever colour is picked (its\nchannels are 0.8 or more in light, 0.2 or less in dark).</p>\n<p>The grid sets them on the cell element as custom properties, with <code>style.setProperty()</code> - their values a\npalette variable or a custom colour&#39;s two tints in <code>light-dark()</code>, built from a palette name or a checked\n<code>#rrggbb</code> only:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>class</th>\n<th>custom properties</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>simple-html-grid-cell-bar</code></td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-bar-start</code>, <code>--simple-html-grid-bar-end</code> (<code>12.5%</code>), <code>--simple-html-grid-bar-color</code></td>\n<td>the bar: a background image of the cell from start to end, under a range&#39;s tint (<code>--simple-html-grid-cell-bar-image</code>), over a rule&#39;s background. Square</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>simple-html-grid-cell-scale</code></td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-scale-from</code>, <code>--simple-html-grid-scale-to</code>, <code>--simple-html-grid-scale-at</code> (<code>37.5%</code>)</td>\n<td>the scale: the cell&#39;s background, <code>color-mix(in srgb, to at, from)</code>. A rule&#39;s background wins: such a cell does not get the class</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The icon is <code>simple-html-grid-cell-icon</code> (and <code>-before</code> / <code>-after</code>, <code>-green</code> / <code>-amber</code> / <code>-red</code>), a 12px\nsvg at the left or the right of the cell, its input <code>simple-html-grid-cell-with-icon-before</code> (or\n<code>-after</code>) with room for it. A cell with a validation error or a match of Find shows as without a bar\nand a scale, as without a rule&#39;s colours; the icon stays.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* bars a little stronger in light: the backgrounds of the palette stay as they are */\n.simple-html-grid .simple-html-grid-cell-bar {\n    --simple-html-grid-bar-color: light-dark(#93c5fd, #1e3a8a);\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-variables-of-the-grid\">The variables of the grid</h2>\n<p>On <code>.simple-html-grid</code>, in <code>grid.css</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>variable</th>\n<th>colours</th>\n<th>light</th>\n<th>dark</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the labels, the panel, the footer, the row selector, group rows, the menus, the filter editor and the grid config dialog</td>\n<td><code>#f5f5f5</code></td>\n<td><code>#374151</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the filter row, the inputs, pickers and buttons of the filter editor and the value list, the value list&#39;s box, the column chooser&#39;s search, the fields of the detail form, the filter chips, the list of the columns and the preview of the column settings, the tooltips (through <code>--simple-html-grid-tooltip-bg-color</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#ffffff</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-alt-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>scrollbar tracks, the focus button of a cell</td>\n<td><code>#ffffff</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-even</code></td>\n<td>the even rows</td>\n<td><code>#ffffff</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-odd</code></td>\n<td>the odd rows</td>\n<td><code>#f7f7f7</code></td>\n<td><code>#5a6370</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-odd</code></td>\n<td>selected rows (odd), the number of a selected column, scrollbar thumbs (through <code>--simple-html-grid-scrollbar-thumb-color</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#bdd4e7</code></td>\n<td><code>#234882</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-even</code></td>\n<td>selected rows (even), menu items and buttons under the pointer, the menu item the keyboard is on and the one whose submenu is open</td>\n<td><code>#afc7db</code></td>\n<td><code>#274e8f</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-border</code></td>\n<td>the lines between cells, rows and menu sections, the frame of the grid and the menus</td>\n<td><code>#e6e5e5</code></td>\n<td><code>#1f2937</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-pinned-border</code></td>\n<td>the lines at the pinned columns and under the header, the frame of the filter editor, the tooltips (through <code>--simple-html-grid-tooltip-border-color</code>) and the column chooser&#39;s button, the line of a group (the bracket) in the filter editor</td>\n<td><code>#d1d0d0</code></td>\n<td><code>#1f2937</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-border</code></td>\n<td>the line right of the grouping columns</td>\n<td><code>#e6e5e5</code></td>\n<td><code>#1f2937</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>#2e2d2d</code></td>\n<td><code>#f9f7f7</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-sec-font-color</code></td>\n<td>placeholders - the filter row, the search boxes, a picker of the filter editor with nothing picked - the AND/OR between the filter chips, the &quot;too many conditions&quot; box, the drag handles of the column chooser and the Detail form tab, the column chooser&#39;s magnifying glass and ✎</td>\n<td><code>#6b6b6b</code></td>\n<td><code>#c8c8c8</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-boxshadow</code></td>\n<td>the inset shadow of the labels, the panel and the row selector</td>\n<td><code>#fffdfd</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-dropzone-color</code></td>\n<td>where a dragged column can go, the resize handle under the pointer</td>\n<td><code>rgba(129, 127, 127, 0.7)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(151, 148, 148, 0.4)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-focus-color</code></td>\n<td>the outline of the element with the keyboard focus</td>\n<td><code>#005fcc</code></td>\n<td><code>#b3d7ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-accent-color</code></td>\n<td>the filled controls of the filter menu&#39;s value list and the filter editor: Apply and Apply &amp; close, the AND / OR a group uses, the checkboxes of the value list (<code>accent-color</code>), the find bar&#39;s <em>Match case</em> and <em>Filter rows</em> while they are on. At least 3:1 to the menus and the dialog</td>\n<td><code>#2563eb</code></td>\n<td><code>#93c5fd</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-accent-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the text on the accent colour, at least 4.5:1 to it</td>\n<td><code>#ffffff</code></td>\n<td><code>#111827</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-control-border-color</code></td>\n<td>the frame of the controls of the value list and the filter editor: the search boxes (the column chooser&#39;s too), the pickers, the value inputs, the buttons; a field of the detail form under the pointer and with the focus. At least 3:1 to the menus and the dialog (WCAG 1.4.11)</td>\n<td><code>#8c8c8c</code></td>\n<td><code>#9ca3af</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-detail-input-border-color</code></td>\n<td>the frame of a field of the detail form at rest: quiet, near the form&#39;s background - the field is seen by its own background (<code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code>). The controls&#39; frame under the pointer, the focus outline with the focus, the error colour with an error</td>\n<td><code>#d4d4d4</code></td>\n<td><code>#5f6b7a</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-range-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>laid over the cells of a range of cells, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</a></td>\n<td><code>rgba(37, 99, 235, 0.12)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.2)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-range-border-color</code></td>\n<td>the line around a range of cells</td>\n<td><code>#1e40af</code></td>\n<td><code>#dbeafe</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-find-match-color</code></td>\n<td>laid over a cell that matches Find (Ctrl+F), see <a href=\"#guide/theming/find\">Find</a></td>\n<td><code>rgba(250, 204, 21, 0.4)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(180, 83, 9, 0.45)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-find-current-color</code></td>\n<td>laid over the current match of Find</td>\n<td><code>rgba(251, 146, 60, 0.6)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(194, 65, 12, 0.65)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-find-current-border-color</code></td>\n<td>the line around the current match of Find</td>\n<td><code>#9a3412</code></td>\n<td><code>#fed7aa</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code></td>\n<td>a validation error: the border of the cell, the bar and the exclamation mark of the row selector</td>\n<td><code>#dc2626</code></td>\n<td><code>#fca5a5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-error-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>a validation error: laid over the cell and the row selector</td>\n<td><code>rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.12)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.22)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-edited-color</code></td>\n<td>the marker of an edited cell, the pencil of an edited row (<code>editedMarker</code>, <code>rowStatus</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#9a3412</code></td>\n<td><code>#fdba74</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-new-color</code></td>\n<td>the plus of a new row (<code>rowStatus</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#166534</code></td>\n<td><code>#86efac</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-deleted-color</code></td>\n<td>the trash can of a row marked for deletion (<code>rowStatus</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#57534e</code></td>\n<td><code>#d1d5db</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-conflict-color</code></td>\n<td>a conflict with the server: the arrows of the row number, the marker of a field in conflict</td>\n<td><code>#6d28d9</code></td>\n<td><code>#ddd6fe</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-merged-color</code></td>\n<td>the flash of a cell a merge changed (<code>mergeHighlight</code>), fading out</td>\n<td><code>rgba(250, 204, 21, 0.45)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(250, 204, 21, 0.35)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-multiline-color</code></td>\n<td>the marker of a cell with more lines than it shows (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#475569</code></td>\n<td><code>#e2e8f0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-button-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the button of a lookup column (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#e9edf2</code></td>\n<td><code>#374151</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-icon-color</code></td>\n<td>the triangle on it, at least 3:1 to it</td>\n<td><code>#475569</code></td>\n<td><code>#e2e8f0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-active-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the active option of the select of a lookup column</td>\n<td><code>#dbe7f3</code></td>\n<td><code>#2f4f80</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-link-color</code></td>\n<td>the text of a link: a cell of a link column (<a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">Templates and links</a>), underlined, and the link in the preview of the column settings. At least 4.5:1 to the rows, the selected rows, under the tints of an error, a range and Find, and to the inputs and the bars</td>\n<td><code>#1e3a8a</code></td>\n<td><code>#e8f1ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-red-color</code></td>\n<td>the text colours of the <a href=\"#guide/theming/colour-rules\">colour rules</a>: red. At least 4.5:1 to the rows, the selected rows (a range&#39;s tint over them too), the inputs and every background of the palette</td>\n<td><code>#90101a</code></td>\n<td><code>#ffe4dd</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-orange-color</code></td>\n<td>orange</td>\n<td><code>#7c2f00</code></td>\n<td><code>#ffe7c4</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-amber-color</code></td>\n<td>amber</td>\n<td><code>#6b3a00</code></td>\n<td><code>#ffeab0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-yellow-color</code></td>\n<td>yellow</td>\n<td><code>#654100</code></td>\n<td><code>#ffec6c</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-lime-color</code></td>\n<td>lime</td>\n<td><code>#2f4a00</code></td>\n<td><code>#e4ffb0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-green-color</code></td>\n<td>green</td>\n<td><code>#00541c</code></td>\n<td><code>#9dffb3</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-teal-color</code></td>\n<td>teal</td>\n<td><code>#00504a</code></td>\n<td><code>#b8fff0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-cyan-color</code></td>\n<td>cyan</td>\n<td><code>#004a60</code></td>\n<td><code>#c8f8ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-blue-color</code></td>\n<td>blue</td>\n<td><code>#12429f</code></td>\n<td><code>#ccf1ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-indigo-color</code></td>\n<td>indigo</td>\n<td><code>#3426a0</code></td>\n<td><code>#e4e6ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-purple-color</code></td>\n<td>purple</td>\n<td><code>#681aa4</code></td>\n<td><code>#fae2ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-pink-color</code></td>\n<td>pink</td>\n<td><code>#8a1050</code></td>\n<td><code>#ffe0f0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-brown-color</code></td>\n<td>brown</td>\n<td><code>#5c3317</code></td>\n<td><code>#fbe8d8</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-grey-color</code></td>\n<td>grey</td>\n<td><code>#414853</code></td>\n<td><code>#e3ecf9</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-red-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the backgrounds of the colour rules: red. The text and a link at least 4.5:1 on it, the marks 3:1</td>\n<td><code>#fecaca</code></td>\n<td><code>#7f1d1d</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-orange-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>orange</td>\n<td><code>#fed7aa</code></td>\n<td><code>#7c2d12</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-amber-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>amber</td>\n<td><code>#fcd34d</code></td>\n<td><code>#805500</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-yellow-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>yellow</td>\n<td><code>#fef08a</code></td>\n<td><code>#713f12</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-lime-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>lime</td>\n<td><code>#d9f99d</code></td>\n<td><code>#365314</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-green-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>green</td>\n<td><code>#bbf7d0</code></td>\n<td><code>#14532d</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-teal-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>teal</td>\n<td><code>#99f6e4</code></td>\n<td><code>#134e4a</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-cyan-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>cyan</td>\n<td><code>#67e8f9</code></td>\n<td><code>#155e75</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-blue-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>blue</td>\n<td><code>#bae6fd</code></td>\n<td><code>#0c4a6e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-indigo-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>indigo</td>\n<td><code>#c7d2fe</code></td>\n<td><code>#312e81</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-purple-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>purple</td>\n<td><code>#e9d5ff</code></td>\n<td><code>#581c87</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-pink-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>pink</td>\n<td><code>#fbcfe8</code></td>\n<td><code>#831843</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-brown-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>brown</td>\n<td><code>#e8d5c4</code></td>\n<td><code>#4a2c1a</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-palette-grey-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>grey</td>\n<td><code>#d1d5db</code></td>\n<td><code>#2b3544</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-icon-green-color</code></td>\n<td>the high icon of an icon set (<code>ColumnFormat.icons</code>): ▲, a green light, ✓. At least 3:1 to the rows, the selected rows and the palette&#39;s backgrounds</td>\n<td><code>#166534</code></td>\n<td><code>#86efac</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-icon-amber-color</code></td>\n<td>the middle icon: ►, an amber light, !</td>\n<td><code>#92400e</code></td>\n<td><code>#fcd34d</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-icon-red-color</code></td>\n<td>the low icon: ▼, a red light, ✕</td>\n<td><code>#b91c1c</code></td>\n<td><code>#fecaca</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-totals-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the totals row under the rows (<code>totalsRow</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a></td>\n<td><code>#e8edf3</code></td>\n<td><code>#2a3441</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-totals-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the text of the totals row</td>\n<td><code>#1f2328</code></td>\n<td><code>#f9fafb</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-aggregate-label-color</code></td>\n<td>the label in front of an aggregate (<code>Sum</code>) in a group row and the totals row. At least 4.5:1 to both</td>\n<td><code>#57606a</code></td>\n<td><code>#c9d1d9</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-group-line-color</code></td>\n<td>the grouping columns: the line of each level down its group&#39;s rows, the frame of a group row&#39;s <code>+</code> / <code>-</code> box. At least 3:1 to the group rows</td>\n<td><code>#8c8c8c</code></td>\n<td><code>#9ca3af</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-warning-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the &quot;only the first 100 values&quot; warning of the filter menu&#39;s value list</td>\n<td><code>red</code></td>\n<td><code>#fca5a5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-danger-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the footer&#39;s Clear filter button under the pointer: its text. A problem of a template in the grid config dialog, at least 4.5:1 to the dialog and the inputs</td>\n<td><code>#c0392b</code></td>\n<td><code>#fecaca</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-danger-border</code></td>\n<td>the footer&#39;s Clear filter button under the pointer: its border</td>\n<td><code>#d98282</code></td>\n<td><code>#f87171</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-readonly-stripe-color</code></td>\n<td>the red stripes of a readonly cell in an editable grid (<code>.simple-html-readonly</code>)</td>\n<td><code>rgb(255, 0, 0)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgb(255, 0, 0)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-required-stripe-color</code></td>\n<td>the blue stripes of an empty required cell (<code>.simple-html-mandatory</code>)</td>\n<td><code>rgb(0, 8, 255)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgb(120, 140, 255)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-stripe-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>between the stripes</td>\n<td><code>rgb(255, 255, 255)</code></td>\n<td><code>transparent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-menu-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the drop shadow of the menus and the other popovers (column chooser, date picker, dropdowns)</td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.28)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-dialog-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the shadow of the filter editor and the grid config dialog</td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.32)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-tooltip-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the shadow of the tooltips</td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.28)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-tooltip-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the background of the tooltips</td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-tooltip-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the text of the tooltips</td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-font-color</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-tooltip-border-color</code></td>\n<td>the frame of the tooltips</td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-pinned-border</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-scrollbar-thumb-color</code></td>\n<td>the scrollbar thumbs (the tracks are <code>--simple-html-grid-alt-bg-color</code>)</td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-odd</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-scrollbar-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the shadow in a scrollbar thumb (Safari, the other browsers use <code>scrollbar-color</code>)</td>\n<td><code>rgb(0 0 0 / 30%)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgb(0 0 0 / 30%)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-backdrop-color</code></td>\n<td>the layer over the page behind the filter editor and the grid config dialog</td>\n<td><code>rgba(129, 127, 127, 0.2)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>--simple-html-grid-grouping-border</code> and <code>--simple-html-grid-main-hr-border</code> are still set, but\nnothing in <code>grid.css</code> uses them. The four that name another variable are that one&#39;s colour, light\nand dark: a theme can give the tooltips and the scrollbar thumbs colours of their own (TreDeSpace\ndoes), and an override of the other one still changes them.</p>\n<p>The stripes are a pattern drawn at 20% opacity over the cell, so their colours show much lighter\nthan they are.</p>\n<p>The fonts:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>variable</th>\n<th>for</th>\n<th>default</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-family</code></td>\n<td>all text</td>\n<td><code>&#39;Roboto&#39;, &#39;Helvetica&#39;, &#39;Arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-size</code></td>\n<td>all text</td>\n<td><code>11px</code>. A larger font needs a larger <code>cellHeight</code> and <code>multilineLineHeight</code> (grid options, in px)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-touch-row-height</code></td>\n<td>on a touch screen (<code>pointer: coarse</code>): the height of a menu item, a column chooser field, a row of the filter menu&#39;s value list</td>\n<td><code>28px</code>. With a mouse they keep their own height</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight</code></td>\n<td>the cells, the inputs</td>\n<td><code>500</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight-header</code></td>\n<td>the labels, the menu sections, group rows, buttons</td>\n<td><code>700</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight-cell</code></td>\n<td>the menu items, the filter editor&#39;s title and captions</td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-date-picker\">The date picker</h2>\n<p>On <code>.simple-html-date</code>, in <code>date.css</code>: the grid&#39;s date picker, and the date picker or calendar on\nits own (<code>&lt;simple-html-date&gt;</code>).</p>\n<p>It looks like the grid: flat, square corners, thin lines, the grid&#39;s font. On top the month and the\nyear on one line, between square buttons to the year and the month before (« ‹) and after (› »).\nThen the week numbers and the days: the days of the months before and after are quieter, today is\nbold with a bar under it, the selected day has a frame and a fill. At the bottom the hour and the\nminutes, each a number between a step down and a step up (the arrow keys step it too), and Clear,\nNow and Use in a bar of buttons of the same width - Use in the colours of the selected day.</p>\n<p>A range picker (<code>rangepicker: true</code>, the grid&#39;s date header filter with Between) has two months side\nby side with « ‹ before the first and › » after the last (<code>.simple-html-date-nav-space</code> holds their\nplace in the other header). From and To have the frame and fill of the selected day\n(<code>.simple-html-date-day-selected</code>), the days between them a fill of their own\n(<code>.simple-html-date-day-in-range</code>). Under the months, as wide as both, the line of From and To\n(<code>.simple-html-date-range-ends</code>: a label over the day picked) and Clear and Use - no hours, minutes\nor Now. The bottom is <code>.simple-html-date-range</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>variable</th>\n<th>colours</th>\n<th>light</th>\n<th>dark</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the background, the buttons, the hour and minute inputs</td>\n<td><code>white</code></td>\n<td><code>#374151</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-color</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>#2e2d2d</code></td>\n<td><code>#f9f7f7</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-dimmed-color</code></td>\n<td>the days of the months before and after</td>\n<td><code>#767676</code></td>\n<td><code>#adadad</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-week-color</code></td>\n<td>the week numbers, the names of the days, the hour and minute labels</td>\n<td><code>#6b6b6b</code></td>\n<td><code>#b4b4b4</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-bg-border</code></td>\n<td>the lines: under the names of the days, right of the week numbers, over the time</td>\n<td><code>#e6e5e5</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-button-border</code></td>\n<td>the frame of the buttons and of the hour and minute inputs</td>\n<td><code>#d1d0d0</code></td>\n<td><code>#6b7280</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-hover-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>a day or a button under the pointer</td>\n<td><code>#e5ebf1</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-bg-selected</code></td>\n<td>the selected day, the Use button</td>\n<td><code>#bdd4e7</code></td>\n<td><code>#234882</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-in-range-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>a range picker: the days between From and To - midway between the background and the selected day</td>\n<td><code>#dee9f3</code></td>\n<td><code>#2d4469</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-selected-border</code></td>\n<td>the frame of the selected day and of the Use button</td>\n<td><code>#3f73a8</code></td>\n<td><code>#7fb0ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-today-color</code></td>\n<td>the bar under today</td>\n<td><code>#005fcc</code></td>\n<td><code>#93c5fd</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-focus-color</code></td>\n<td>the outline of the element with the keyboard focus (in the grid: the grid&#39;s)</td>\n<td><code>#005fcc</code></td>\n<td><code>#b3d7ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-font-size</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>11px</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-font-family</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>&#39;Roboto&#39;, &#39;Helvetica&#39;, &#39;Arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The text has a contrast of at least 4.5:1 to its background, in light and in dark - the fill of the\ndays of a range too; the frame of the selected day and the bar under today at least 3:1 to the\nbackground, the selected day&#39;s fill and the fill of a range (WCAG 1.4.3 and 1.4.11). Keep that when you change them. The dimmed days and the week numbers had\nlighter colours before v6 (<code>#dfdfdf</code>, <code>#8b8b8b</code>): too light to read.</p>\n<p><code>--simple-html-date-header-bg-border</code> is still set, but nothing in <code>date.css</code> uses it.</p>\n"},{"id":"accessibility","title":"Keyboard and accessibility","html":"<p>The grid follows the WAI-ARIA <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/grid/\">grid pattern</a>: a\nscreen reader gets one grid of rows and cells, with column headers, where each row and column is in\nthe whole grid, how the rows are sorted and which are selected. Grouped, it is a\n<a href=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/treegrid/\">treegrid</a>: the group rows expand and collapse.\nThis page has what the grid exposes, every key, and what it does not do (yet).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-screen-reader-gets\">What a screen reader gets</h2>\n<p>This is Chrome&#39;s accessibility tree of the column layout sample (a pinned column on each side, a\ncolumn of two fields), scrolled down and to the right: columns 3 and 4 are scrolled away, and the\nnumbers in parentheses are the <code>aria-colindex</code>es.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>grid &quot;Data grid&quot;  aria-rowcount=503  aria-colcount=10\n  row  aria-rowindex=1\n    columnheader &quot;row selector&quot; (1), &quot;Company&quot; (2), &quot;Phone&quot; (5), &quot;date&quot; (6) ... &quot;Balance&quot; (10)\n  row  aria-rowindex=2\n    gridcell (2) &gt; textbox &quot;Filter Company&quot;, gridcell (5) &gt; textbox &quot;Filter Phone&quot; ...\n  row  aria-rowindex=3\n    gridcell &quot;1&quot; (2) selected=false, gridcell &quot;4&quot; (5, colspan 2) selected=false ... gridcell &quot;8&quot; (10) selected=false\n  row  aria-rowindex=72  selected=false\n    gridcell &quot;69&quot; (1), gridcell (2) &gt; textbox &quot;Company&quot;: MASSIVE DYNAMIC,\n    gridcell (5) &gt; textbox &quot;Phone&quot;: +47 45911795 ... gridcell (10) &gt; textbox &quot;Balance&quot;: 2178.34</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>The grid</strong> has <code>role=&quot;grid&quot;</code>, <code>role=&quot;treegrid&quot;</code> while the rows are grouped. Its name is the text\n<code>grid.label</code> (see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/labels-and-translating\">Labels</a>). <code>aria-rowcount</code> is the number of header rows\nplus the displayed rows (after the filter, the group rows that are shown included), <code>aria-colcount</code>\nthe row numbers plus every field of the layout. <code>aria-multiselectable=&quot;true&quot;</code> with the selection\nmode <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The header rows</strong>: the labels are <code>columnheader</code>s, the header filters are <code>gridcell</code>s with the\nfilter input in them. The top left corner is the column header of the row numbers. A hidden row of\nlabels or filters (<code>hideLabels</code>, <code>hideFilter</code>) is not counted.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The column numbers</strong> under the filters (a click selects a column, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/selecting-columns\">Selecting columns</a>) are the last header row: a\n<code>gridcell</code> per column of the layout, at the <code>aria-colindex</code> of its first field - a column of two\nstacked fields is one number, <code>aria-colspan=&quot;2&quot;</code> - with <code>aria-selected</code> <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Cells\nand not column headers, as the row numbers are cells: the label stays the one header of a column.\nA number can have the focus, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/a-column-number\">A column number</a>. <code>selectSizeHeight: 0</code> hides\nthe row, it is not counted then.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The column header of the sorted column</strong> has <code>aria-sort=&quot;ascending&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;descending&quot;</code>. Sorted on\nmore columns, only the first one has it: a grid has one sorted column for a screen reader.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A row</strong> has <code>aria-rowindex</code>: where it is in the whole grid, the header rows included - the first\ndata row is 4 under a header of labels, filters and column numbers. With a selection mode it has <code>aria-selected</code>,\n<code>true</code> or <code>false</code>; with <code>&#39;none&#39;</code> it has none. A group row can not be selected.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A cell</strong> is a <code>gridcell</code> with <code>aria-colindex</code>; the input (or checkbox) in it is named with the\ncolumn&#39;s label, the same as the header shows. The row number is the first cell of a row. A column of\ntwo fields (<code>fields: [&#39;a&#39;, &#39;b&#39;]</code>) is two columns here, one per field.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A cell in a range of cells</strong> (see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>) has\n<code>aria-selected=&quot;true&quot;</code>: <code>gridcell &quot;Company&quot; selected</code>. The other cells have no <code>aria-selected</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/known-limits\">Known limits</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The state of a row</strong> is text in its row number cell, hidden on screen (<code>simple-html-visually-hidden</code>):\n<code>gridcell &quot;3 Marked for deletion&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;2 Edited, Error: Age: Must be at least 18&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;5 New row&quot;</code>. A\nrow as it was is its number only. The icons on screen are hidden from a screen reader, the text says\nthe same - the texts <code>rowStatus.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/row-state-and-edited-cells\">Texts</a>. Without the\nrow status (<code>rowStatus: false</code>) the row number is the number only: show the state another way, a\ncolumn of <code>__rowState</code> say.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>An edited cell</strong>: its marker is hidden from a screen reader and is no stop of Tab; the row&#39;s state\nsays the row is edited. Ctrl+Z takes an edit back, the row menu&#39;s <em>Undo cell edit</em> and a click on the\nmarker are for the mouse.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A cell of several lines</strong> (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>, or a value with a line break) is a readonly\ntextbox with its first line as the value. When it has more lines, its description\n(<code>aria-describedby</code>) says so: <code>textbox &quot;Notes&quot; readonly: &quot;Call back on Monday&quot;</code>, described &quot;More\nlines, Enter shows all&quot; (the text <code>cell.multiline.more</code>). The marker on screen is hidden from a\nscreen reader. Enter opens the editor, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-editor-of-a-multiline-cell\">The editor of a multiline cell</a>.\nIn a row made taller (<code>fitRows()</code>, the row menu&#39;s &quot;Fit row height&quot;) the cell shows its other lines\nunder the first one: they are for the eye and hidden from a screen reader, which reads the cell as\nin a row of one line - its first line and the description - and Enter shows all of the text. A\ntaller row changes nothing else: the same cells, the same keys.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A lookup cell</strong> (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>) is a readonly textbox with the label as its value, and\n<code>aria-haspopup</code> - <code>listbox</code> or <code>grid</code> - where it can be edited: <code>textbox &quot;Country&quot; readonly: &quot;Norway&quot;</code>. Its button, <code>button &quot;Open the list&quot;</code>, is no stop of Tab; the cell&#39;s name is made of\nboth, <code>gridcell &quot;Norway Open the list&quot;</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-list-of-a-lookup-column\">The list of a lookup column</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The state is not colour only</strong> (WCAG 1.4.1): each state has a shape of its own - a plus, a pencil,\na trash can, an exclamation mark - and a row marked for deletion is struck through.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A group row</strong>, grouped, is a row with <code>aria-level</code> (1 for the first grouping) and\n<code>aria-expanded</code>; a data row is one level below the last grouping. Its one cell spans the columns\nafter the row number, with a button that expands and collapses it: named with the text the row\nshows (<code>Country: Norway (12)</code>), <code>aria-expanded</code> says its state.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The aggregates of a group row</strong> (<code>ColumnConfig.aggregate</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>):\nthe aggregates of a field are one <code>gridcell</code> with the <code>aria-colindex</code> of its field, named with all its\nlines (<code>Sum 1234.5, Avg 123.4</code>), so a screen reader reads them with the column&#39;s header. The name has\na line of its own on screen; for a screen reader it is the cell of the first column and spans the\ncolumns up to the first aggregate - the aggregates of the first column are in its text\n(<code>Country: Norway (12) Count 10</code>).</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The totals row</strong> (<code>totalsRow</code>) is the last row of the grid, after the rows on screen: its first\ncell is a <code>rowheader</code>, &quot;Total&quot; (the text <code>aggregate.total</code>), then a <code>gridcell</code> per aggregate.\n<code>aria-rowcount</code> counts it, its <code>aria-rowindex</code> is the last.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The row count</strong> in the footer is a status (<code>role=&quot;status&quot;</code>): a screen reader reads it out when it\nchanges - after a filter, an expand or a collapse.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The footer&#39;s buttons</strong>: &quot;✕ Clear&quot; and the filter chips (a button, its tooltip &quot;Edit filter&quot;)\nwhile the rows are filtered, else &quot;No filter&quot; - its tooltip &quot;Open the filter editor&quot; is its\ndescription. Clear and &quot;No filter&quot; are one <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> whose name changes with the filter. The gear\nat the right end (<code>settingsButton</code>) is a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> named &quot;Column settings&quot; (the text\n<code>footer.settings</code>), <code>aria-haspopup=&quot;dialog&quot;</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A link cell</strong> (a text column with <code>ColumnFormat.link</code>): the cell is the <code>gridcell</code>, its host the\nfocus target, the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> in it a link named by its text - in browse mode a screen reader follows it.\nA row without an address the grid links to has no link, only the text. The host has\n<code>aria-keyshortcuts=&quot;Alt+Enter&quot;</code>: Alt+Enter opens the link. See\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-link-cell\">Templates and links</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The grid config dialog</strong>: a modal dialog named by its title. Its tabs are the WAI-ARIA tabs\npattern: a <code>tablist</code> named by the title, a <code>tab</code> each (<code>aria-selected</code>, <code>aria-controls</code> its\n<code>tabpanel</code>). The Detail form tab&#39;s list of the fields is a <code>listbox</code>, each field an <code>option</code> with\n<code>aria-checked</code> (in the form or not) and <code>aria-selected</code> (picked). The Columns tab: the list of the columns a\n<code>listbox</code> (its picked column <code>aria-selected</code>, <code>aria-activedescendant</code> on the list), each setting an\ninput or a checkbox with its <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>, the hint and the problems of a template its description\n(<code>aria-describedby</code>), <code>aria-invalid</code> while it has a problem; the problems and the preview&#39;s are live\nregions (<code>aria-live=&quot;polite&quot;</code>): read out as they come while typing. The colour rules are a <code>group</code>\nnamed <em>Colour rules</em> and described by its hint, each rule a <code>group</code> (<em>Rule 1</em>): its operator picker,\nits value input(s), the swatches of its text colour and of its background - radio buttons in a\n<code>radiogroup</code> named by its label, each named by its colour (<em>None</em>, <em>Red</em> ...), not by the colour\nalone - Bold, and Move up / Move down / Remove rule, named and with a tooltip (<code>aria-disabled</code> past an\nend).</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The detail form</strong> (see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/the-detail-form\">The detail form</a>): a <code>region</code> named by\nits title, <em>Details</em>; each value an input, a textarea or a checkbox with its <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>, <code>aria-readonly</code>\n/ <code>readonly</code> where it can not be edited, <code>aria-required</code>, its errors its description\n(<code>aria-describedby</code>) with <code>aria-invalid</code>. Its title is a button (<code>aria-expanded</code>, it controls the\nform&#39;s body) that collapses and expands it; its left edge a <code>separator</code> (focusable, named <em>Width of\nthe details</em>, its width in px as <code>aria-valuenow</code> with <code>aria-valuemin</code> / <code>aria-valuemax</code>, the arrow keys\nmove it) - the WAI-ARIA window splitter.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The panel</strong>: the x of a group chip is a button, &quot;Remove the grouping on Country&quot;.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The find bar</strong> (Ctrl+F, see <a href=\"#guide/find/what-a-screen-reader-gets\">Find</a>): a search region named\n&quot;Find in the grid&quot; before the grid, with an input named &quot;Find&quot;, the count of the matches as a status\n(<code>3 of 17</code>, read out when it changes), the toggle buttons &quot;Match case&quot; (it shows <code>Aa</code>) and &quot;Filter\nrows&quot; (<code>aria-pressed</code>) and the buttons &quot;Previous match&quot;, &quot;Next match&quot; and &quot;Close&quot;. The cells of the matches have no state of their\nown for a screen reader, the count says where you are; with &quot;Filter rows&quot; on the footer&#39;s row count\n(a status) says how many rows are left.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The keyboard shortcuts help</strong> (<code>?</code>, Ctrl+/, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">below</a>): a modal\ndialog named by its title, <em>Keyboard shortcuts</em>, with a search - a textbox named <em>Search</em>, described\nby the hint under it - and a table per group of keys, its caption the title of the group: each row\nhas the keys as its row header and what they do as its cell. &quot;No key matches the search&quot; is a status.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rows and the columns are virtual: only the rows on screen (and a few more) are in the dom, and\nof the middle section only the columns on screen. That is what <code>aria-rowindex</code> and <code>aria-colindex</code>\nare for, the screen reader knows where it is without having every row.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-how\">How</h3>\n<p>A row of the grid is not one element in the dom. The grid has a section for the row numbers, the\npinned left columns, the middle columns that scroll sideways and the pinned right columns, and a row\nhas an element in each. The row and column elements are reused while scrolling, so their order in the\ndom is not the order of the rows and columns either.</p>\n<p><code>aria-owns</code> gives a screen reader the right shape without moving anything: an element owns other\nelements, which become its children in the accessibility tree, in the order of the list. The grid is\nan element of its own: its children are the header rows, it owns the rows in the order of the rows. A\nrow is the row element of the row numbers: it owns the group row&#39;s cell and the column elements of\nthe left, middle and right sections, in the order of the columns. The other row and column elements\nare <code>role=&quot;none&quot;</code>. The grid updates the lists after every scroll. The elements aria-owns points to\nhave ids of the grid&#39;s own, <code>simple-html-grid-&lt;n&gt;-...</code>, one <code>&lt;n&gt;</code> per grid on the page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keyboard\">The keyboard</h2>\n<p>Every key the grid handles, by where the focus is. The cells are inputs, always ready to be typed in:\nthe arrow keys, Home and End move the caret in them, as in a spreadsheet&#39;s cell being edited. Alt is\nthe key of the grid: Alt with an arrow moves between the cells, Alt+Home / Alt+End to the first / last\ncell of the row - see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keys-that-jump\">The keys that jump</a>. A custom cell (<code>ColumnConfig.renderer</code>)\nhas no caret: there the plain arrows move between the cells too, see <a href=\"#guide/custom-cells/the-keys-and-the-focus\">Custom cells</a>.\nCtrl is Cmd on a Mac, Alt is Option.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-cell\">A cell</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the next / previous cell, across the sections, then the next / previous row. A group row between them is a stop of its own, its button. Shift+Tab in the first row goes up to the header filters. Tab on the last cell of the last row goes on to the footer&#39;s buttons - Clear and the filter chips while the rows are filtered, else &quot;No filter&quot; - and leaves the grid in a footer of no height; Shift+Tab on the first cell with the filters hidden leaves it. See <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/focus\">Focus</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowLeft / Alt+ArrowRight</td>\n<td>the cell to the left / right, across the sections. Past the end of a row: the next row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End</td>\n<td>the first cell of the first row / the last cell of the last row, scrolled to - in the order of Tab: the left pinned columns first, the right pinned ones last. The middle columns scroll to their start / end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Home / Alt+End</td>\n<td>the first / last cell of the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Home / End</td>\n<td>the input&#39;s: the caret to the start / end of the text (Shift+Home selects to the start)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PageUp / PageDown</td>\n<td>the cell a page of rows up / down, the same column: the rows scroll as far, the cell stays where it was on screen. At the first / last row it stops there</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+PageUp / Alt+PageDown</td>\n<td>the cell a page of the middle columns left / right, the same row: the middle columns scroll as far. In a pinned column the middle columns scroll, the focus stays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+ArrowUp / Ctrl+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the edge of the data in the column, as in a spreadsheet: the last cell with a value before a blank one, from a blank one the next cell with a value, else the first / last row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+ArrowLeft / Ctrl+ArrowRight</td>\n<td>the input&#39;s: the caret a word left / right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the cell above / below: the next field of a column of two fields, then the next row. Up from the first row: the header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>in a text cell that can be edited: the cell below (Alt+ArrowDown). In a multiline cell: opens its editor, also when the cell is readonly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F2</td>\n<td>in a multiline cell: opens its editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a character</td>\n<td>in a multiline cell that can be edited: opens its editor, the character at the end of the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Space</td>\n<td>in a cell with a <code>focusButton</code>: sends <code>cell-focus-button-click</code>, as a click on the button does. In a lookup cell that can be edited: opens its list. In a checkbox: checks it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowDown, F4, F2</td>\n<td>in a lookup cell (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>) that can be edited: opens its list - not the cell below. See <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-list-of-a-lookup-column\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a character</td>\n<td>in a lookup cell that can be edited: opens its list, the character as its search. The cell is not typed in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter, F2, a character</td>\n<td>in a custom cell that can be edited: opens the grid&#39;s editor of one line over it (the renderer&#39;s own <code>edit()</code> when it has one) - Enter saves it, Esc cancels. See <a href=\"#guide/custom-cells/editing\">Custom cells</a>. A link cell too: the editor has the value, not the text of the template</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Enter</td>\n<td>in a link cell (<code>ColumnFormat.link</code>): opens the link, as a click on its text - in a new tab with <code>newTab</code>. A row without an address: nothing. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-link-cell\">Templates and links</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowUp / ArrowDown / ArrowLeft / ArrowRight</td>\n<td>on a custom cell itself: as with Alt. On a control the renderer put in the cell, the control&#39;s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the row menu (the browser sends <code>contextmenu</code>), the focus on its first item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+arrows</td>\n<td>a range of cells: from this cell, the end one cell further each time - a row up or down, a field left or right (the fields of a column of two are two). The focus stays in the cell. See <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-keyboard\">Selection and clipboard</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+PageUp / Shift+PageDown, Ctrl+Shift+Home / Ctrl+Shift+End, Alt+Shift+Home / Alt+Shift+End, Ctrl+Shift+ArrowUp / Ctrl+Shift+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the end of the range where the key without Shift goes: a page, the first / last cell of the grid, of the row, the edge of the data. Not Alt+Shift+PageUp / PageDown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>clears the ranges of cells (when there are some, and no menu is open)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C, Cmd+C</td>\n<td>with no text selected in the cell: the range of cells as tab separated text, or the cell&#39;s value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+V, Cmd+V</td>\n<td>a block of cells (copied in a spreadsheet, or here) from this cell or the top left of the range; one value into every cell of the ranges. One value with no range is the input&#39;s own paste</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete / Backspace on a row number</td>\n<td>marks the selected rows for deletion (the number&#39;s row alone when it is not selected); a click on a row number puts the focus there. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/deleting-rows\">Deleting rows</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key on a row number</td>\n<td>the row menu of the row, without a cell (the entries of a cell are greyed out), the focus on its first item. Its <em>Select ›</em> adds the row, or the rows from the anchor - see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete / Backspace</td>\n<td>with a range of more than one cell and no text selected: empties its cells. In a lookup cell that can be edited: empties it, and the fields its <code>set</code> copies. Else the input&#39;s own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+F, Cmd+F</td>\n<td>opens the find bar, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-find-bar\">below</a>. Anywhere in the grid: a header filter, a column header too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F3 / Shift+F3</td>\n<td>while the find bar is open: the next / previous match. Anywhere in the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+D (Cmd+D)</td>\n<td>fill down: each range gets the values of its top row, a range of one row or the cell alone the row above. See <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/fill-down\">Selection and clipboard</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y / Ctrl+Shift+Z</td>\n<td>in an editable grid: undo, redo - and the focus goes to the cell it changed, scrolled to. While the cell is typed in Ctrl+Z is the input&#39;s own. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>?, Ctrl+/ (Cmd+/)</td>\n<td>the keyboard shortcuts help, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">below</a>: <code>?</code> where it types nothing - a readonly cell, a checkbox, a lookup or custom cell that can not be edited - Ctrl+/ also while typing in a cell. Anywhere in the grid: a header filter, a column header, a row number, the find bar too. Not with <code>shortcutHelp: false</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The grid scrolls to the cell the focus goes to. Moving to another cell (Tab, Alt+arrows, Enter, the keys\nthat jump) clears the ranges of cells, and so does typing in a cell. The focus moves in the keydown: the\nrows are scrolled and rendered first, so the next key - a key held down, a character typed at once -\ngoes on from the new cell.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-keys-that-jump\">The keys that jump</h3>\n<p>The keys of a spreadsheet that go further than the next cell, in the table above:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>goes to</th>\n<th>with Shift</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End</td>\n<td>the first cell of the first row / the last cell of the last row</td>\n<td>the end of the range there</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Home / Alt+End</td>\n<td>the first / last cell of the row</td>\n<td>the end of the range there</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PageUp / PageDown</td>\n<td>a page of rows up / down, the same column</td>\n<td>the end of the range a page further</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+PageUp / Alt+PageDown</td>\n<td>a page of the middle columns left / right, the same row</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+ArrowUp / Ctrl+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the edge of the data in the column</td>\n<td>the end of the range there</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Home and End stay the caret&#39;s.</strong> The WAI-ARIA grid pattern and a spreadsheet move to the first /\nlast cell of the row with Home / End - but only while no cell is being edited: in a cell being edited\nthey move the caret, in Excel and Google Sheets too. The cells here are always being edited (an\ninput, the caret in it), so Home / End are the input&#39;s, and Alt - the key that moves between the cells\nwith the arrows - makes them the grid&#39;s: Alt+Home / Alt+End. Home / End only when the caret is at the\nstart / end already was considered: pressed twice to be sure, a key that also moves the caret would\njump away from the cell. Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End do in a text input what Home / End do: the grid takes\nthem (Cmd+Home / Cmd+End on a Mac too).</li>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+ArrowLeft / Ctrl+ArrowRight stay the input&#39;s</strong>: a word left / right (Option+arrows on a Mac).\nCtrl+ArrowUp / Ctrl+ArrowDown do nothing in an input of one line (Cmd+ArrowUp / Cmd+ArrowDown move the\ncaret to its start / end on a Mac, as Cmd+ArrowLeft / Cmd+ArrowRight do): they are the grid&#39;s, to the\nedge of the data as in Excel. On a Mac Ctrl+ArrowUp / Ctrl+ArrowDown are the system&#39;s (Mission\nControl): Cmd+ArrowUp / Cmd+ArrowDown. A blank is <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> or an invalid date; 0 and\nfalse are values.</li>\n<li><strong>PageUp / PageDown</strong> have nothing to do in an input of one line: the grid&#39;s, a page being the rows the\nview shows. The rows scroll as far as the focus goes, so the cell stays where it was on screen; down a\npage and up again is the same row. Ctrl+PageUp / Ctrl+PageDown are left to the browser (its tabs).</li>\n<li><strong>Group rows are skipped</strong>, as the arrows skip them: a key landing on one goes on to the data row after\nit. Tab stops on their buttons, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/focus\">Focus</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>With Shift</strong> the key moves the end of the last range of cells instead, as Alt+Shift+arrows do - the\nkey without Shift moves the focus, with Shift the range follows it; see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-keyboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>. The focus stays in the active\ncell while it is on screen; when the range scrolled it away (Ctrl+Shift+End from the top) it goes to\nthe end of the range, and the keys go on from there - copy, Delete, the next Shift key.</li>\n<li><strong>From the header</strong>: a header filter has Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End, Alt+Home / Alt+End (the first / last\nfilter) and PageDown; a column header and the button of a group row Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-header-filter\">A header filter</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>filters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the next / previous filter. Tab on the last one goes to the first row (the button of its group row, when the rows are grouped; the footer&#39;s buttons when there are no rows), Shift+Tab on the first one leaves the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowLeft / Alt+ArrowRight</td>\n<td>the filter to the left / right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp</td>\n<td>the filter above in a column of two fields, else the column header (label) above it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the filter below in a column of two fields, else the first row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Home / Alt+End</td>\n<td>the first / last filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End</td>\n<td>the first cell of the first row / the last cell of the last row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PageDown</td>\n<td>the cell under the filter in the last row on screen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Home / End</td>\n<td>the input&#39;s: the caret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the filter menu, the focus on its first item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F4</td>\n<td>the operator choice under the operator indicator (under the cell of a column too narrow for one), the focus on its first item: Enter picks, the focus back in the filter; Esc closes it. Nothing on a column with one operator, nor with <code>filterOperatorIndicator: false</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The operator indicator in the cell - the symbol of the filter&#39;s operator - is a button (<code>aria-haspopup=&quot;menu&quot;</code>,\nnamed <code>Operator: Contains</code>) that is no stop of Tab: F4 does what its click does. The input is described\nby it (<code>aria-describedby</code>): a screen reader reads <code>Filter Company, Operator: Contains</code>. On a column\nwith one operator it is no button, the input is described by it still.</p>\n<p>The current row stays as it is when the focus goes up into the filters (Alt+ArrowUp, Shift+Tab,\nAlt+ArrowLeft from the first row): the filter row is no row of the data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-column-header\">A column header</h3>\n<p>A column header can have the focus, but is not a stop of Tab: Alt+ArrowUp from its filter gets there\n(a click too).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>sorts on the column, again for the other direction - as a click. The focus stays on it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+Enter / Shift+Space</td>\n<td>adds the column to the sort - as shift+click</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the label above / below in a column of two fields; down from the last one, the column&#39;s filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End</td>\n<td>the first cell of the first row / the last cell of the last row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the header menu, the focus on its first item</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-column-number\">A column number</h3>\n<p>A column number - the row of numbers under the header filters - can have the focus, but is not a stop\nof Tab: a click puts it there, and selects columns (Ctrl+click, Shift+click, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/selecting-columns\">Selecting columns</a>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the column menu, the focus on its first item. When it closes the focus is back on the number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C, Cmd+C</td>\n<td>copies the selected columns - the number&#39;s own when none is selected - as tab separated text and an html table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>no column selected. With a menu open, Esc closes the menu first</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-rest\">The rest</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>where</th>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>the button of a group row</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>expands or collapses the group. The focus stays on the button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the button of a group row</td>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the next / previous stop: the first cell of the row after it (the button of a group row after it), the last cell of the row before it (the last header filter before the first row). Out of the grid at its ends, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/focus\">Focus</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the button of a group row</td>\n<td>Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End</td>\n<td>the first cell of the first row / the last cell of the last row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the x of a group chip in the panel</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>removes the grouping. It is in the tab order, before the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the footer: Clear and the filter chips, or &quot;No filter&quot;, the settings button</td>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the stops after the last row: Clear and the chips while the rows are filtered, else &quot;No filter&quot;, then the gear (<code>settingsButton</code>). Tab from the last cell of the last row gets there, Tab from the last of them leaves the grid. Shift+Tab from the first goes back to the last row. None in a footer of no height (<code>footerHeight: 0</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the settings button in the footer</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>opens the grid config dialog</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the detail form</td>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab, Enter / Space, ArrowLeft / ArrowRight, Ctrl+Z (Cmd+Z)</td>\n<td>after the footer in the order of Tab: Tab from the footer&#39;s last stop goes into the form - the button of its title first (Enter or Space collapses and expands the form), then its left edge (ArrowLeft 10px wider, ArrowRight narrower), then its fields - from its last field out of the grid. Shift+Tab from its title button goes back to the footer. Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y undo and redo as in a cell, the focus stays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the filter chips in the footer, &quot;No filter&quot;</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>opens the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clear in the footer</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>removes the filter. The focus stays on the button, now &quot;No filter&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a menu</td>\n<td>ArrowDown / ArrowUp, Home / End, a letter</td>\n<td>the next / previous / first / last item, the next one starting with the letter. A menu opened with the mouse leaves the focus on the cell: ArrowDown / ArrowUp get into it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a menu</td>\n<td>Enter, Space</td>\n<td>uses the item: the menu closes, the focus goes back where it was. On an item with a submenu (<code>›</code>): opens it, as ArrowRight does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a submenu</td>\n<td>ArrowLeft, Esc</td>\n<td>closes it, the focus back on its item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a menu</td>\n<td>Esc, Tab</td>\n<td>closes it, the focus goes back where it was. Tab in the filter menu goes on into its value list: the search field, the checkboxes, Cancel, Apply. ArrowDown / ArrowUp move between the search field and the checkboxes, Enter applies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the editor of a multiline cell</td>\n<td>Esc / Ctrl+Enter / Tab</td>\n<td>see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-editor-of-a-multiline-cell\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the list of a lookup column</td>\n<td>the arrows, Enter, Tab, Esc</td>\n<td>see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-list-of-a-lookup-column\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the filter editor</td>\n<td>Tab, Enter / Space, Esc</td>\n<td>every control is a button or an input, in the order shown: the <code>✕</code>, each group&#39;s AND / OR, + Condition, + Group, wrap and delete, each condition&#39;s field, operator, value, switch and delete, then Undo, Redo, Cancel, Apply, Apply &amp; close. A picker opens its menu, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-editor\">Filtering</a>. Esc closes the menu open in it, else the editor without filtering (a modal dialog)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the filter editor</td>\n<td>Ctrl+Z (Cmd+Z), Ctrl+Y / Ctrl+Shift+Z (Cmd+Y, Cmd+Shift+Z)</td>\n<td>undoes / redoes an edit of the editor, the focus on the control it was made with (the dialog when it is gone). In a value input typed in since it got the focus, the input&#39;s own undo of the typing - Ctrl+Z until it is back where it started. Undo and Redo, the icons in the footer, are <code>aria-disabled</code> with nothing to undo / redo and keep the focus. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/undo-and-redo-in-the-filter-editor\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the tabs of the grid config dialog</td>\n<td>ArrowLeft / ArrowRight, Home / End</td>\n<td>shows the tab before / after (round), the first / last; the focus on it. One stop of Tab: the tab shown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the list of the fields on the Detail form tab</td>\n<td>ArrowDown / ArrowUp, Home / End, PageDown / PageUp, Space, Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>picks a field, puts it in the form or takes it out, moves it up / down - Move up / Move down are buttons too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the grid config dialog</td>\n<td>Tab, Enter / Space, Esc</td>\n<td>the <code>✕</code>, the tabs (one stop), the list of the columns (one stop), the settings of the column picked - a format picker, or the text, its <code>{ }</code> field button, Show as link, the address and its <code>{ }</code>, Open in a new tab - the link of the preview, then Reset column, Reset all, Cancel, Apply, Apply &amp; close. The focus is on the dialog when it opens, Esc closes the menu open in it, else the dialog without applying (a modal dialog); the focus goes back to where it was</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the list of the columns in the column settings</td>\n<td>ArrowDown / ArrowUp, Home / End, PageDown / PageUp, a letter</td>\n<td>picks the next / previous / first / last column, ten further, the next one whose label starts with the letter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the swatches of a colour rule</td>\n<td>ArrowRight / ArrowDown, ArrowLeft / ArrowUp</td>\n<td>picks the next / previous colour (the browser&#39;s radio buttons): one stop of Tab, the colour picked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the find bar</td>\n<td>Enter, F3, Esc</td>\n<td>the next match, closes it: see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-find-bar\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the keyboard shortcuts help</td>\n<td>Tab, typing, Esc</td>\n<td>a modal dialog: Tab goes through its ✕ and its search, typing in the search keeps the keys that have the text. Esc closes it, the focus goes back to where it was. See <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-list-of-a-lookup-column\">The list of a lookup column</h3>\n<p>A select or a dropdown grid under a lookup cell (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>), see\n<a href=\"#guide/lookups/the-keys\">Lookups</a> for every key. The cell&#39;s input has <code>aria-haspopup</code>: <code>listbox</code> for\nthe select, <code>grid</code> for the dropdown grid. Its button, &quot;Open the list&quot; (the text <code>lookup.open</code>), is for\nthe mouse: it is no stop of Tab.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The select</strong> is the WAI-ARIA <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/combobox/\">combobox pattern</a>\nwith a listbox popup: the focus is in its search field, a <code>combobox</code> named with the column&#39;s label\n(<code>aria-expanded</code>, <code>aria-controls</code> the list, <code>aria-autocomplete=&quot;list&quot;</code>). The list is a <code>listbox</code> of\n<code>option</code>s; the active option is the search field&#39;s <code>aria-activedescendant</code> and has\n<code>aria-selected=&quot;true&quot;</code>. ArrowDown / ArrowUp, PageDown / PageUp, Home / End move it, Enter picks,\nTab picks and goes to the next cell, Esc closes. &quot;No matches&quot; is a <code>status</code>. The cell&#39;s value is\nthe active option when it opens, marked with a check on screen.</li>\n<li><strong>The dropdown grid</strong> is a grid, with the focus in its filter: ArrowDown goes to the highlighted\nrow, ArrowDown / ArrowUp move between the rows (no Alt needed), Enter picks, Esc closes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After a pick, Enter and Esc the focus is back in the cell; Ctrl+Z there undoes the pick.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-find-bar\">The find bar</h3>\n<p>Ctrl+F (Cmd+F) with the focus in the grid opens it over the grid&#39;s top right corner, the focus in its\ninput - see <a href=\"#guide/find\">Find</a>. Outside the grid Ctrl+F is the browser&#39;s.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>typing</td>\n<td>finds the text when the typing stops: the cells are marked, the grid scrolls to the current match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter, F3</td>\n<td>the next match, wrapping to the first. The focus stays in the input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+Enter, Shift+F3</td>\n<td>the previous match, wrapping to the last</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes it: the focus goes to the cell of the current match (its row is the current row), else back to where Ctrl+F was pressed. &quot;Filter rows&quot; is turned off, every row is shown again. In a modal dialog of your own, the dialog stays open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+F, Cmd+F</td>\n<td>the focus back in the input, its text selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+C</td>\n<td>&quot;Match case&quot; on or off - the text as it is typed, or in any case. The focus stays where it is. Not on a Mac, where Option+C types <code>ç</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the input, &quot;Match case&quot; (<code>Aa</code>), &quot;Filter rows&quot; (the funnel), previous (↑), next (↓), close (✕). Shift+Tab from the input leaves the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Space, Enter</td>\n<td>on &quot;Match case&quot;: turns it on or off. On &quot;Filter rows&quot;: only the rows with a match, or every row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>&quot;Filter rows&quot; has no key of its own: Tab from the input gets to it, Space turns it on or off, Shift+Tab\ngoes back to the input. Alt+R was considered: on a Mac Option+R types <code>®</code> in the input, and find bars\n(VS Code&#39;s) use Alt+R for regular expressions. &quot;Match case&quot; has Alt+C, as in the find bars of Firefox and\nVS Code - by the letter the key types, so Option+C on a Mac stays <code>ç</code> (there: Tab, Space).</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-editor-of-a-multiline-cell\">The editor of a multiline cell</h3>\n<p>A textarea in a popover over the cell, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>. It gets the\nfocus when it opens: a <code>textbox</code> named with the column&#39;s label (<code>Notes</code>), multi-line, described by the\nhint under it (<code>Ctrl+Enter to save, Esc to cancel</code>, <code>Esc to close</code> when it is readonly - the texts\n<code>cell.multiline.hint</code> and <code>cell.multiline.hintReadonly</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>a new line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Enter, Cmd+Enter</td>\n<td>saves and closes it, the focus goes back to the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes it without saving, the focus goes back to the cell. In a modal dialog of your own, the dialog stays open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>saves and closes it, and goes on to the next / previous cell as Tab in the cell does</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The keys of an input method (IME) are its own while it composes: Enter and Esc go to it. The other\nkeys are the textarea&#39;s own: the arrows move the caret, Ctrl+Z is the textarea&#39;s undo while it is open.\nA readonly editor (a readonly grid or cell) shows the text, nothing can be typed in it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keyboard-shortcuts-help\">The keyboard shortcuts help</h2>\n<p>The keys above, in short: a dialog that lists them in groups, with a search. <code>?</code> opens it where the key\ntypes nothing - a readonly cell, a checkbox, a column header, a row number, a button of the grid - and\nCtrl+/ (Cmd+/) anywhere in the grid, also while typing in a cell or a header filter. The header menu has\n<em>Keyboard shortcuts…</em> (<code>header.shortcuts</code>, its hint Ctrl+/), and <code>gridInterface.openShortcutHelp()</code> opens\nit from a button of your own. <code>shortcutHelp: false</code> turns the keys and the menu entry off - for an app\nwith a help of its own, or one that uses the keys itself; <code>openShortcutHelp()</code> still opens it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] },\n    shortcutHelp: false\n};\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });\ndocument.getElementById('keys-help')!.onclick = () =&gt; gridInterface.openShortcutHelp();</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A modal dialog</strong> of the grid&#39;s look, as the filter editor: named by its title, <em>Keyboard shortcuts</em>,\nmoved by its title bar and resized from every edge and corner. Esc or its ✕ closes it, the focus goes\nback to where it was. Opened by a key the focus is in its search, to type in at once; opened with a\nclick (the header menu, a button) on the dialog, as the other dialogs - a focused input would open the\nkeyboard of a phone. On a phone it is as wide as the window, and its list scrolls.</li>\n<li><strong>The keys as the platform names them</strong>: on a Mac ⌘ for Ctrl, ⌥ for Alt and ⇧ for Shift, in the order\nof a Mac&#39;s menus and without the <code>+</code> - ⇧⌘Z. The names of the keys are texts too (<code>shortcuts.key.*</code>): a\ntranslation can name them as its keyboards do, Strg, Entf. A letter, F2 and the arrows are shown as\nthey are.</li>\n<li><strong>Only the keys that do something in the grid</strong>: a readonly grid leaves out the keys of an editable\ngrid (the column <em>shown</em> below), a Mac leaves out Alt+C, <code>filterOperatorIndicator: false</code> F4 of a\nheader filter, <code>shortcutHelp: false</code> the keys of the help itself.</li>\n<li><strong>The search</strong> keeps the keys whose text, keys or group have what is typed, in any case: <code>undo</code>,\n<code>ctrl</code>, <code>f3</code>. When none has it, the dialog says so.</li>\n<li><strong>Its texts</strong> are <code>shortcuts.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/keyboard-shortcuts-help\">Texts</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-every-key-of-the-help\">Every key of the help</h3>\n<p>The help&#39;s list, with the English texts. Each key is in the tables of <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard\">The keyboard</a> above,\ndescribed in full there. The docs and the dialog have one list: a test checks this one is the help&#39;s.</p>\n<h4 id=\"section-moving-around\">Moving around</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>The next / previous cell</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowLeft / Alt+ArrowRight</td>\n<td>The cell to the left / right</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>The cell above / below</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>The cell below, from a cell that can be edited</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Home / Alt+End</td>\n<td>The first / last cell of the row</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End</td>\n<td>The first / last cell of the grid</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PageUp / PageDown</td>\n<td>A page of rows up / down</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+PageUp / Alt+PageDown</td>\n<td>A page of columns left / right</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+ArrowUp / Ctrl+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>The edge of the data in the column, up / down</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-selecting-cells\">Selecting cells</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+arrows</td>\n<td>A range of cells, one cell further each time</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+PageUp / Shift+PageDown</td>\n<td>The range a page up / down</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+Home / Alt+Shift+End</td>\n<td>The range to the first / last cell of the row</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Shift+Home / Ctrl+Shift+End</td>\n<td>The range to the first / last cell of the grid</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Shift+ArrowUp / Ctrl+Shift+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>The range to the edge of the data, up / down</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>No range of cells any more</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-in-a-cell\">In a cell</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter / F2</td>\n<td>Open the editor of a cell of several lines, or of a custom cell</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Enter</td>\n<td>In the editor of several lines: save the text</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>Close an editor or the list of a lookup cell, nothing saved</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowDown / F4</td>\n<td>Open the list of a lookup cell</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Space</td>\n<td>Check or uncheck a checkbox</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Enter</td>\n<td>Open the link of a link cell</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-changing-values\">Changing values</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Z</td>\n<td>Undo, the focus to the cell it changed</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Y / Ctrl+Shift+Z</td>\n<td>Redo</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+D</td>\n<td>Fill down: the values of the row above, or of the top row of the range</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete / Backspace</td>\n<td>Empty the cells of a range</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete / Backspace</td>\n<td>On a row number: mark its row for deletion, or the selected rows</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-copy-and-paste\">Copy and paste</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C</td>\n<td>Copy the range of cells, or the value of the cell</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+V</td>\n<td>Paste cells copied here or in a spreadsheet</td>\n<td>an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C</td>\n<td>On a column number: copy the selected columns</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-find\">Find</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Ctrl+F</td>\n<td>Open the find bar</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter / F3</td>\n<td>The next match</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+Enter / Shift+F3</td>\n<td>The previous match</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+C</td>\n<td>Match case on or off</td>\n<td>not on a Mac</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>Close the find bar</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-filters-headers-and-groups\">Filters, headers and groups</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>In a header filter: filter</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F4</td>\n<td>In a header filter: choose the operator</td>\n<td>with <code>filterOperatorIndicator</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp</td>\n<td>From a header filter: up to its column header</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>On a column header: sort, again the other way</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+Enter / Shift+Space</td>\n<td>On a column header: add the column to the sort</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>On a group row: expand or collapse it</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-menus\">Menus</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Shift+F10 / the menu key</td>\n<td>The menu of the cell, header, filter or number with the focus</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowDown / ArrowUp</td>\n<td>The next / previous item</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>Use the item, or open its submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowRight / ArrowLeft</td>\n<td>Open / close a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>Close the menu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4 id=\"section-dialogs-and-this-help\">Dialogs and this help</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>keys</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>?</td>\n<td>This help, where the key types nothing</td>\n<td>with <code>shortcutHelp</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+/</td>\n<td>This help, also while typing in a cell</td>\n<td>with <code>shortcutHelp</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>Close a dialog</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y</td>\n<td>In the filter editor: undo / redo</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowLeft / ArrowRight</td>\n<td>In the grid config dialog: the tab before / after</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>The fields of the detail form: move the field up / down</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Space</td>\n<td>The fields of the detail form: in the form or out</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-focus\">Focus</h2>\n<p>What can have the focus: the cell inputs and checkboxes, the host of a custom cell (and what its renderer\nmakes focusable), the header filters, the column headers, the\nbuttons of the group rows, the x of the group chips, the buttons of the footer (Clear and the filter\nchips, or &quot;No filter&quot;, and the gear), the input and\nbuttons of the find bar, and the items of a menu (see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-keyboard\">Menus</a>: the WAI-ARIA\nmenu pattern). Each gets a\nvisible focus ring from the theme (<code>:focus-visible</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/the-keyboard-focus\">Theming</a>).</p>\n<p>Tab goes through the grid in the order it is shown, as through a form - every cell is a stop, not\nthe one stop of the WAI-ARIA grid pattern (see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/known-limits\">Known limits</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the x of the group chips in the panel (the browser&#39;s Tab, before the grid)</li>\n<li>the header filters, left to right</li>\n<li>the rows, top to bottom: the cells of a data row left to right, across the pinned sections; a group\nrow is one stop, its button</li>\n<li>the footer&#39;s Clear and filter chips while the rows are filtered, else its &quot;No filter&quot;, then the\nsettings button (none in a footer of no height)</li>\n<li>out of the grid</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Shift+Tab goes the same way back. The column headers are no stops (Alt+ArrowUp from a filter gets\nthere), nor are the button of a lookup cell, the operator indicator of a header filter (F4 opens its\nchoice), the marker of an edited cell and the focus button.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sorting from the keyboard and expanding a group rebuild the header or the rows: the focus is put\nback on the new column header / group button.</li>\n<li>At the ends of the grid Tab is left to the browser: the focus first goes to an empty element at the\nstart or the end of the grid (<code>tabindex=&quot;-1&quot;</code>, hidden from a screen reader), and the browser&#39;s Tab\ngoes on from there to what is before or after the grid.</li>\n<li>The wheel and a swipe take the focus from the cell being edited: its row element is about to show\nanother row. The wheel uses a hidden input for it, out of the tab order; a swipe blurs the cell,\nonce when it starts (focusing an input could open a phone&#39;s keyboard). Not from the find bar: no\ncell is edited, and Enter goes on finding. They save and close the\neditor of a multiline cell too, and a click outside it does: the focus is where the click put it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-labels-and-translating\">Labels and translating</h2>\n<p>The texts a screen reader reads are texts like the others, with an id - see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/screen-readers\">Texts</a>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>grid.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Data grid</code> - the name of the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.rowSelector</code></td>\n<td><code>row selector</code> - the column header of the row numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter {label}</code> - a header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.groupToggle</code></td>\n<td><code>{name} ({total})</code> - the button of a group row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.removeGroup</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove the grouping on {title}</code> - the x of a group chip</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The cells and the column headers use the column&#39;s label (<code>columns[field].label</code>, else the field&#39;s\nlabel, else the field name).</p>\n<p>Give each grid a name of its own, a page with two grids called &quot;Data grid&quot; is hard to find your way in:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] },\n    texts: { 'grid.label': 'Customers' }\n};\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<p>Translated for the whole page, with the other texts:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({\n    'grid.label': 'Tabell',\n    'grid.rowSelector': 'radnummer',\n    'grid.filter': 'Filtrer {label}',\n    'grid.groupToggle': '{name}, {total} rader',\n    'grid.removeGroup': 'Fjern grupperingen på {title}'\n});</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-known-limits\">Known limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The column numbers by keyboard</strong>: no key gets to them from a cell, a filter or a column header:\na click does. Their menu&#39;s copies of a whole column have no other key; a range of cells copies\nthe cells of a column too (Ctrl+C). <code>aria-multiselectable</code> on the grid follows the row selection\n(the selection mode <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code>), while more than one column can be selected in every mode.</li>\n<li><strong>Checked in Chrome only</strong>: its accessibility tree (through the DevTools protocol) has the\nstructure above, after scrolling both ways, sorted, grouped, expanded and collapsed. Firefox,\nSafari and real screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver) have not been tried. The structure depends on\n<code>aria-owns</code>, which Safari supports less well than Chrome and Firefox.</li>\n<li><strong>Not the keyboard of the grid pattern</strong>: there every cell is reached with the arrow keys and only\none cell is a stop of Tab. Here every cell input is a stop of Tab for the browser (the grid moves\nthe focus on Tab itself), and the plain arrow keys, Home and End move the caret in the input; Alt+arrows\nmove between the cells, Alt+Home / Alt+End to the ends of the row, Alt+Shift+arrows select a range of\ncells - see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keys-that-jump\">The keys that jump</a>. There is no key to select a whole row or column\n(Shift+Space, Ctrl+Space), and Ctrl+ArrowLeft / Ctrl+ArrowRight are the input&#39;s (a word), not the edge\nof the data sideways. Alt+Shift+PageUp / Alt+Shift+PageDown do not extend a range.</li>\n<li><strong>Shift+Tab into the grid from after it</strong> is the browser&#39;s: the last input in the dom, the last cell of\na row the grid rendered at the bottom of the view (row 20 at the top of 500 rows, checked in Chrome) -\nnot the last row, and not the footer&#39;s buttons. Tab into the grid from before goes to the first header\nfilter, as it should.</li>\n<li><strong>Ranges of cells</strong>: only the cells in a range have <code>aria-selected</code> (<code>true</code>). WAI-ARIA asks for\n<code>true</code> or <code>false</code> on every cell that can be selected, in a grid with <code>aria-multiselectable</code>: the\ngrid keeps <code>aria-multiselectable</code> for the row selection (the selection mode <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code>), and gives\nthe other cells no <code>false</code>: every cell of the grid would get a &quot;not selected&quot; state, the way the\nrows have one with a selection mode. Not tried with a screen reader yet, see above. A range\nmade with Alt+Shift+arrows (Shift+PageDown ...) is not read out while it grows: the focus stays in the\nactive cell, or goes to the end once the active cell scrolled away. Only the cells on screen are in\nthe dom, so a screen reader reads only those of a large range.</li>\n<li><strong>Only the rows on screen are in the dom</strong>: a screen reader reading the page (browse mode) reads\nthe rows on screen, not row 400. The counts and the indexes say where they are.</li>\n<li><strong>Group rows by keyboard</strong>: Tab stops on the button of a group row; Alt+arrows, Enter and the keys\nthat jump skip the group rows (they move between cells), so a group row is reached with Tab only.\nThe treegrid pattern&#39;s ArrowRight / ArrowLeft to expand / collapse a row are not there: Enter / Space\non the button.</li>\n<li><strong>The menus</strong>: the right click menus and <code>openMenu()</code> follow the WAI-ARIA menu pattern (roles,\n<code>aria-checked</code>, <code>aria-expanded</code>, the keys above), checked in Chrome&#39;s accessibility tree but not with\na screen reader yet. A menu has no name of its own (<code>aria-label</code>), a submenu is labelled by its item.\nThe field and operator pickers of the filter editor are menus of the grid too: buttons\n(<code>aria-haspopup=&quot;menu&quot;</code>, named <code>Field: Country</code>) that open them, the keys above in them. The\ncolumn chooser is for the mouse - its search (named <em>Search</em>) and the ✎ of each field (a button named\n<em>Rename Firm</em>, Enter saves the label typed, Esc cancels) are reached with Tab, a field is dragged with a\npointer only - and so is the date picker of a date cell or filter - a date can be\ntyped - and its range picker. The date picker opens on a click in the cell, under it at the end of the\npage&#39;s body: no key opens it and Tab does not get into it (its days have no keys, so taking the focus\ninto it would leave a keyboard user in a calendar that can not pick a day). Opened from an input of\nthe filter menu&#39;s range block it leaves the focus in the input: type, Enter applies; Esc closes the\npicker. The From and To line of the range picker is read out when it changes (<code>aria-live</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>The list of a lookup column</strong>: the select is checked in Chrome&#39;s accessibility tree, not with a\nscreen reader yet. The cell&#39;s input is a textbox with <code>aria-haspopup</code>, not a combobox: it is not\n<code>aria-expanded</code> while its list is open - the focus is in the list then. The select shows at most 500\noptions; the search finds the rest.</li>\n<li><strong>Only the first sort column</strong> has <code>aria-sort</code>; the number of a column in a sort on several\ncolumns is shown, but not read out.</li>\n<li><strong>The column numbers</strong> under the filters are hidden: selecting a column is for the mouse, and a\nselected column is not read out.</li>\n<li><strong>The two scrollbars</strong> are elements of their own, Chrome makes them stops of Tab (an element that\nscrolls and has nothing focusable in it); they have no name.</li>\n<li><code>setSelectionMode()</code> shows (<code>aria-multiselectable</code>, <code>aria-selected</code>) on the next render of the rows:\na click or <code>refresh()</code> (a scroll only renders the rows that scroll in).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"export","title":"Export","html":"<p>The grid exports its rows as an Excel file (.xlsx) and as CSV. By default it is what the user sees:\nthe rows the filter lets through, in the sort order, the columns of the layout in their order, with\na header of the column labels.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Excel</strong>: <code>getExcel()</code> gives the file as a <code>Blob</code>, <code>downloadExcel()</code> saves it. The cells are\ntyped - numbers, dates, booleans, text - so Excel can sum, sort and filter them, in the columns&#39;\nnumber formats and colours. The row menu exports Excel.</li>\n<li><strong>CSV</strong>: <code>getCsv()</code> gives the text, <code>downloadCsv()</code> saves it. Each value as the cell shows it. In\nthe API only, not in the row menu.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The other way, a CSV or Excel file read into new rows, is the <a href=\"#guide/import\">Import</a>: what the grid\nexports, it imports back with the same values.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-excel\">Excel</h2>\n<p>Every Excel file the grid writes follows two rules, there are no options to change them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The header row is frozen, with an autofilter.</strong> Row 1 has the column labels, bold, and stays\nin place while the rows scroll. The autofilter covers the header and every row (<code>A1</code> to the last\ncolumn and the last row), so each column has Excel&#39;s filter and sort button.</li>\n<li><strong>The file is a flat list.</strong> One header row, then one plain row per data row - also when the grid\nis grouped. No group rows or group headers, no Excel outline or row grouping, no subtotal rows,\nno totals row. Grouped, every data row is in the file - the rows of collapsed groups too - in the\ngrouped order.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-getexcel-and-downloadexcel\"><code>getExcel()</code> and <code>downloadExcel()</code></h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// a file: the browser downloads export.xlsx\ndocument.getElementById('export')!.onclick = () =&gt; gridInterface.downloadExcel();\n\n// the selected rows, as selected.xlsx, on a sheet named Orders\nawait gridInterface.downloadExcel('selected.xlsx', { rows: 'selected', sheetName: 'Orders' });\n\n// the file, for code of your own: upload it, attach it to a mail ...\nconst blob = await gridInterface.getExcel();\nawait fetch('/api/reports', { method: 'POST', body: blob });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getExcel(options?)</code> returns a promise of the <code>Blob</code>, of the type\n<code>application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet</code>.</li>\n<li><code>downloadExcel(filename?, options?)</code> saves it: the <code>Blob</code> downloaded by the click of an\n<code>&lt;a download&gt;</code> link, as <code>downloadCsv()</code> does. The filename defaults to <code>export.xlsx</code>; give it the\n<code>.xlsx</code> ending yourself, it is not added. The promise is fulfilled when the download has started.</li>\n<li>Both are async: the file is a ZIP, compressed with the browser&#39;s <code>CompressionStream</code>. The rows and\ntheir values are read when they are called, before the promise is returned: an edit while the\nfile is being made is not in it.</li>\n<li>Both work without the grid on the page: they read the config and the datasource.</li>\n<li>A bad option is a rejected promise, see <a href=\"#guide/export/the-excel-options\">the options</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"export\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-from-the-row-menu\">From the row menu</h3>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell) has an <strong>Export ›</strong> submenu with <em>Excel, all rows</em> and <em>Excel,\nselected rows</em> (greyed out when no row is selected): <code>downloadExcel()</code> with the default options, as\n<code>export.xlsx</code>. It is there in every grid, readonly ones too. The ids are <code>row.export</code> (the submenu),\n<code>row.export.excel</code> and <code>row.export.excelSelected</code>. To give them other options - a sheet name, a\nfile name - replace their actions in the submenu with the <a href=\"#guide/menus\">menus hook</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridHooks, MenuEntry } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: {\n        row: (defaults, context) =&gt;\n            defaults.map((entry): MenuEntry =&gt; {\n                if (entry.type !== 'submenu' || entry.id !== 'row.export') {\n                    return entry;\n                }\n                const entries = entry.entries.map((inner) =&gt;\n                    inner.id === 'row.export.excel'\n                        ? { ...inner, action: () =&gt; context.gridInterface.downloadExcel('orders.xlsx', { sheetName: 'Orders' }) }\n                        : inner\n                );\n                return { ...entry, entries };\n            })\n    }\n};\ngridInterface.updateHooks(hooks);</code></pre>\n<p>CSV is not in the row menu. To have it there, add an entry of your own to the export submenu, after\nthe Excel ones:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridHooks, MenuEntry } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: {\n        row: (defaults, context) =&gt; {\n            const csv: MenuEntry = {\n                id: 'my.export.csv',\n                label: 'CSV, all rows',\n                action: () =&gt; context.gridInterface.downloadCsv('export.csv', { separator: ';' })\n            };\n            return defaults.map((entry) =&gt;\n                entry.type === 'submenu' &amp;&amp; entry.id === 'row.export' ? { ...entry, entries: [...entry.entries, csv] } : entry\n            );\n        }\n    }\n};\ngridInterface.updateHooks(hooks);</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-typed-cells\">Typed cells</h3>\n<p>The cells hold the values themselves, not the texts the grid shows, so a number is a number in\nExcel: it sums, sorts as a number, and is shown with the decimal separator of the user&#39;s locale. The\ntype of the cell is the field&#39;s type (see <a href=\"#guide/datasource\">the datasource</a>), or the row&#39;s with\n<code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field type</th>\n<th>value</th>\n<th>Excel cell</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>number</code></td>\n<td>a number</td>\n<td>a number, General format: every digit, as the grid&#39;s number formatters show them. <code>numberComma</code>&#39;s <code>12,5</code> is the number 12.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>date</code></td>\n<td>a <code>Date</code></td>\n<td>a date: the local day, formatted <code>yyyy-mm-dd</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n<td>a <code>Date</code></td>\n<td>a date and time, to the second, <code>yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>\n<td>TRUE / FALSE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>text</code></td>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>text; a text with line breaks gets wrapped lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>any</td>\n<td><code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid <code>Date</code></td>\n<td>an empty cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>a value of another type</td>\n<td>text: what the cell shows</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A value of another type is text</strong>: the text <code>&#39;12&#39;</code> in a number field, a number in a text field, a\ndate string in a date field. Excel then marks a number stored as text. Give the fields their type\n(<code>fields: { amount: &#39;number&#39; }</code>) and keep the values of that type to get numbers and dates.</li>\n<li>With <code>dateDDMMYYYY</code> or <code>datetimeDDMMYYYY</code> as the formatter the dates are <code>dd.mm.yyyy</code>, as the grid\nshows them; with any other formatter, your own too, they are <code>yyyy-mm-dd</code>. A datetime has a space\nbefore the time where the grid&#39;s formatter has a <code>T</code>.</li>\n<li>A column&#39;s <code>numberOverride</code> is followed, as in the cells: <code>BLANK_TO_ZERO</code> writes 0 for a blank,\n<code>ZERO_TO_BLANK</code> an empty cell for 0.</li>\n<li>A column&#39;s display format (<code>ColumnConfig.format</code>, the header menu&#39;s Format ›, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>) gives its cells an Excel number format like\nit - the value stays the value, a date a date and a number a number. See the table below.</li>\n<li>A number has at most 15 significant digits in Excel: a longer id is better kept as text.</li>\n<li>A text longer than 32 767 characters, what an Excel cell holds, is cut there. Control characters\n(U+0000 to U+001F but tab, CR and LF), which XML can not hold, are left out.</li>\n<li><code>formatted</code> and <code>escapeFormulas</code>, the CSV options, have no place here: the values are typed, and\ntext is never a formula (see <a href=\"#guide/export/no-formulas\">No formulas</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"section-display-formats-in-excel\">Display formats in Excel</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>display format</th>\n<th>Excel number format</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;iso&#39; }</code></td>\n<td><code>yyyy-mm-dd</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;norwegian&#39; }</code>, <code>&#39;norwegianShort&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>dd\\.mm\\.yyyy</code>, <code>dd\\.mm\\.yy</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;us&#39; }</code>, <code>&#39;uk&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>mm\\/dd\\/yyyy</code>, <code>dd\\/mm\\/yyyy</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;isoTime&#39; }</code>, <code>&#39;isoSeconds&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm</code>, <code>yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;norwegianTime&#39; }</code>, <code>&#39;norwegianSeconds&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>dd\\.mm\\.yyyy hh:mm</code>, <code>dd\\.mm\\.yyyy hh:mm:ss</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;time&#39; }</code>, <code>&#39;timeSeconds&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>hh:mm</code>, <code>hh:mm:ss</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;weekday&#39; }</code></td>\n<td><code>dddd</code>: the day&#39;s name in Excel&#39;s language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;month&#39; }</code>, <code>&#39;year&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>yyyy-mm</code>, <code>yyyy</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ date: &#39;medium&#39; }</code>, <code>&#39;long&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;full&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;weekIso&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;weekUs&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;quarter&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;daysUntil&#39;</code></td>\n<td>none of Excel&#39;s: the date in the field&#39;s order, as without a format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ number: { decimals: 2 } }</code></td>\n<td><code>0.00</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ number: { decimals: 2, thousands: &#39; &#39; } }</code> (any separator)</td>\n<td><code>#,##0.00</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ number: { decimals: 1, percent: true } }</code></td>\n<td><code>0.0%</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ number: {} }</code>, a number format without <code>decimals</code></td>\n<td>General: every decimal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The <code>.</code> and <code>/</code> of a date are escaped: unescaped, they are the decimal mark and the date separator\nof the computer&#39;s language. A number&#39;s separators are Excel&#39;s: <code>,</code> and <code>.</code> in a number format are the\nthousands separator and the decimal mark of the computer Excel runs on, so <code>#,##0.00</code> is\n<code>1 234 567,89</code> in a Norwegian Excel and <code>1,234,567.89</code> in an English one - the characters picked in\nthe grid are not written.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-dates-and-time-zones\">Dates and time zones</h3>\n<p>A <code>Date</code> is a moment in time, an Excel date is a day and a time of day without a time zone. The file\nhas the date and time <strong>in the user&#39;s local time</strong> - what the grid shows them: a date at midnight in\nNorway is that day in Excel, not the day before (as <code>toISOString()</code> would make it, in UTC). The days\nsummer time starts and ends are days like any other.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <code>date</code> field is the day: its time of day, if it has one, is left out - the grid shows and\nfilters a date field by the day too.</li>\n<li>A <code>datetime</code> field is the date and the time to the second; the milliseconds are left out, as the\ngrid leaves them out.</li>\n<li>The workbook uses Excel&#39;s 1900 date system, the default. Excel can not hold a date before\n1900-01-01: such a date is written as the text the cell shows.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-widths\">Column widths</h3>\n<p>Each column is as wide as in the grid: a column of stacked fields gives each of its fields its\nwidth, a field that is not in the layout (given in <code>fields</code>) gets Excel&#39;s default width. The px are\nconverted to Excel&#39;s width with the formulas of the standard (ECMA-376 Part 1, 18.3.1.13), for the\nsheet&#39;s font, Calibri 11, whose widest digit is 7 px at 96 dpi: 100 px is 13.57 characters. On a\nscreen with another scale Excel scales the columns with the font.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-sheet-name\">The sheet name</h3>\n<p><code>sheetName</code> names the sheet, default <code>Sheet1</code>. Excel&#39;s rules apply - 1 to 31 characters, none of\n<code>[ ] : * ? / \\</code>, not starting or ending with <code>&#39;</code>, not <code>History</code> - and a name that breaks one is a\n<code>TypeError</code>, not quietly changed:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>await gridInterface.downloadExcel('orders.xlsx', { sheetName: 'Orders 2026' });</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-no-formulas\">No formulas</h3>\n<p>A CSV file is read by a spreadsheet that takes text starting with <code>=</code> as a formula, which is why\n<code>getCsv()</code> escapes it (see <a href=\"#guide/export/formulas-csv-injection\">Formulas</a>). An .xlsx file can not have that\nproblem: text is written as text (an inline string), and a formula would be an element of its own,\nwhich the grid never writes. A value a user typed, <code>=HYPERLINK(&quot;http://evil.example&quot;, &quot;Click me&quot;)</code>,\nshows in Excel as exactly that text. Nothing is escaped, nothing is added.</p>\n<p>A link column (<a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">Templates and links</a>) is written as its value, as\ncopy and the CSV export write it: no <code>HYPERLINK</code> formula, no hyperlink, not the text of its template -\n<code>Acme Corp</code>, not <code>Acme Corp (12)</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-colours\">Colours</h3>\n<p>The colours of the columns are in the file, as the grid shows them in its <strong>light look</strong> - the default\ntheme&#39;s palette (a sheet has no dark mode, nor the page&#39;s theme):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>in the grid</th>\n<th>in Excel</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">colour rule</a>: text colour, background, bold</td>\n<td>the cell&#39;s own font colour, fill and bold - fixed: the rule that matched when the file was made. A rule can be a formula over the row or a condition on other fields, Excel could not test it again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <a href=\"#guide/formatting/data-bars\">data bar</a></td>\n<td>Excel&#39;s own data bar (conditional formatting): from the column&#39;s smallest value or 0 to its largest (<code>MIN(0,MIN(...))</code>, <code>MAX(0,MAX(...))</code>), or the fixed <code>min</code> / <code>max</code>. Excel draws it as a gradient to white, in a stronger colour than the grid&#39;s tint; a negative value&#39;s bar is not drawn left of an axis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-scales\">colour scale</a></td>\n<td>Excel&#39;s own colour scale, the same two or three colours at the lowest value, the middle and the highest (or the fixed values). Not on the cells whose rule has a background: it wins, as in the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an <a href=\"#guide/formatting/icon-sets\">icon set</a></td>\n<td>Excel&#39;s own: <code>3Arrows</code>, <code>3TrafficLights1</code>, <code>3Flags</code>, <code>3Symbols2</code> - the thresholds in percent or as numbers, reversed with <code>reverse</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Excel computes</strong> the bars, scales and icons from the values, over the column&#39;s rows in the sheet -\nand again when a value is changed in Excel. Their thresholds are numbers and formulas of the column&#39;s\ncells the grid writes, never text of a cell.</li>\n<li><strong>A custom colour</strong> is as picked for a rule; a scale&#39;s is its tint in light, as the grid shows it.</li>\n<li><strong>The number formats</strong> of the columns&#39; display formats are written too, colours or not - see\n<a href=\"#guide/export/display-formats-in-excel\">Display formats in Excel</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>colors: false</code></strong> writes the values without any of them - no fill, no font colour, no conditional\nformatting:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>await gridInterface.downloadExcel('plain.xlsx', { colors: false });</code></pre>\n<p>The CSV has no colours at all: it is text.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-excel-options\">The Excel options</h3>\n<p><code>ExcelOptions</code>, every option optional. It is plain JSON: it can be stored, or come from a menu of\nyour own. An option the grid does not know (<code>header</code>: the header row is always there), or a value\nan option does not take (<code>rows: &#39;some&#39;</code>, a sheet name Excel does not take), rejects the promise\nwith a <code>TypeError</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>rows</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;all&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;all&#39;</code>: the rows the filter lets through, sorted. <code>&#39;selected&#39;</code>: the selected rows. <code>&#39;range&#39;</code>: the rows of the selected range of cells. See <a href=\"#guide/export/the-rows\">the rows</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fields</code></td>\n<td>the layout (<code>&#39;range&#39;</code>: the range&#39;s)</td>\n<td>the fields, in order, see <a href=\"#guide/export/the-columns-and-their-labels\">the columns</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sheetName</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;Sheet1&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the name of the sheet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>colors</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the colours of the columns: the rules&#39; as the cells&#39; own, the bars, scales and icons as Excel&#39;s conditional formatting. <code>false</code>: none, see <a href=\"#guide/export/colours\">Colours</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { ExcelOptions } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst options: ExcelOptions = { rows: 'selected', fields: ['name', 'born'], sheetName: 'People' };\nawait gridInterface.downloadExcel('people.xlsx', options);</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-file\">The file</h3>\n<p>The file is SpreadsheetML (ECMA-376, Office Open XML), what Excel writes, made by the grid itself -\nno library: a ZIP of <code>[Content_Types].xml</code>, <code>_rels/.rels</code>, <code>xl/workbook.xml</code>,\n<code>xl/_rels/workbook.xml.rels</code>, <code>xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml</code> and <code>xl/styles.xml</code>. The ZIP is deflated\nwith the browser&#39;s <code>CompressionStream(&#39;deflate-raw&#39;)</code>; a browser without it (before Chrome 103,\nFirefox 113, Safari 16.4) stores the parts uncompressed - the same file, about ten times larger\n(10 000 rows of 8 columns: 0.44 MB deflated, 4.7 MB stored).</p>\n<p>An Excel sheet holds 1 048 576 rows, the header row included, and 16 384 columns: more rejects the\npromise with a <code>RangeError</code> - export those as CSV.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-rows\">The rows</h2>\n<p>The Excel and the CSV export take the same rows. <code>rows: &#39;all&#39;</code>, the default, is every row the\nfilter lets through, in the sort order - what <code>datasource.getDataRows()</code> gives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filtered and sorted</strong>: the header filters, the filter editor and a filter set from code all\ncount, and so does <code>filterChangedOnly()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Grouped</strong>: every data row, in the grouped order. The rows of a collapsed group are exported too,\nthe group rows are not.</li>\n<li><strong>Marked for deletion</strong>: those rows are still rows of the grid (struck through) until they are\nsaved, they are exported.</li>\n<li><strong>No totals</strong>: the totals row (<code>totalsRow</code>) and the aggregates of the group rows are not exported, a\nspreadsheet sums the rows up itself - and a line of totals would be read back as a row. See\n<a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>rows: &#39;selected&#39;</code> is the selected rows, in the same order - what <code>datasource.getSelectedRows()</code>\ngives. That is the selected rows that are displayed: a selected row in a collapsed group, or one the\nfilter leaves out, is not exported, as the row menu&#39;s actions on the selected rows leave it out.\nWith nothing selected the export is the header only.</p>\n<p><code>rows: &#39;range&#39;</code> is the selected range of cells - the last of <code>gridInterface.getRanges()</code>, the one\nthe user made last, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>: its data rows top to\nbottom (a group row in it is left out) and, unless <code>fields</code> is given, its fields left to right, with\ntheir labels as the header. With no range the CSV text is empty and the Excel sheet has no columns.\nCtrl+C copies the same cells as tab separated text, without the header.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// the cells the user selected, as a file\nawait gridInterface.downloadExcel('range.xlsx', { rows: 'range' });\ngridInterface.downloadCsv('range.csv', { rows: 'range' });</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-columns-and-their-labels\">The columns and their labels</h2>\n<p>By default the columns are the layout&#39;s, in the order the user sees them: pinned left, center,\npinned right, as <code>getLayoutFields()</code> gives them. A column with stacked fields gives a column in the\nexport for each of its fields, top to bottom. A hidden column is not in the layout, so it is not\nexported.</p>\n<p><code>fields</code> picks the fields and their order. Any field of the rows can be given, also one the grid has\nno column for:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const text = gridInterface.getCsv({ fields: ['id', 'name', 'email'] });\nconst file = await gridInterface.getExcel({ fields: ['id', 'name', 'email'] });</code></pre>\n<p>The header has the labels the grid&#39;s header shows: the label the user gave the column in the column\nsettings (<code>format.label</code>), else the column&#39;s <code>label</code>, else the datasource field&#39;s <code>label</code>, else the\nfield name prettified (<code>first_name</code> is <code>First Name</code>). The CSV export can\nleave its header line out with <code>header: false</code>; an Excel file always has its header row.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-csv\">CSV</h2>\n<h3 id=\"section-getcsv-and-downloadcsv\"><code>getCsv()</code> and <code>downloadCsv()</code></h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// the text, for code of your own: send it to a server, put it on the clipboard ...\nconst text = gridInterface.getCsv(); // 'Name,Born\\r\\nAnn,2001-05-17\\r\\n...'\n\n// a file: the browser downloads export.csv\ndocument.getElementById('export')!.onclick = () =&gt; gridInterface.downloadCsv();\n\n// the selected rows, as selected.csv, for Excel in Norway\ngridInterface.downloadCsv('selected.csv', { rows: 'selected', separator: ';' });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getCsv(options?)</code> returns the text.</li>\n<li><code>downloadCsv(filename?, options?)</code> saves it: a <code>Blob</code> downloaded by the click of an\n<code>&lt;a download&gt;</code> link, like a link the user clicks. The filename defaults to <code>export.csv</code>; give it\nthe <code>.csv</code> ending yourself, it is not added.</li>\n<li>Both take the same options, see <a href=\"#guide/export/the-csv-options\">the CSV options</a>. The only difference is the\n<a href=\"#guide/export/csv-in-excel-the-separator-and-the-bom\">BOM</a>: <code>downloadCsv()</code> writes one by default, <code>getCsv()</code>\ndoes not.</li>\n<li>Both work without the grid on the page: they read the config and the datasource.</li>\n<li>They take <a href=\"#guide/export/the-rows\">the rows</a> and <a href=\"#guide/export/the-columns-and-their-labels\">the columns</a> as the Excel\nexport does.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-formatted-or-raw-values\">Formatted or raw values</h3>\n<p>By default (<code>formatted: true</code>) each value is written as the cell shows it, the text of the field&#39;s\nformatter (see <a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>) - not a column&#39;s display format (<code>2026-W47</code>,\n<code>1 234 567,89</code>): the CSV is the values, a program or a paste reads them back:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>value</th>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>written</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>12.5</code></td>\n<td><code>number</code>, <code>numberComma</code></td>\n<td><code>12,5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <code>Date</code></td>\n<td><code>date</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code>, or as its formatter writes it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>null</code></td>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A column&#39;s <code>numberOverride</code> and <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code> are followed, as in the cells. The template\nand the link of a text column are not: its value is written.</p>\n<p><code>formatted: false</code> writes the values as they are stored: for a file a program reads.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>value</th>\n<th>written</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a number</td>\n<td>with a dot, <code>String(value)</code>: <code>12.5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <code>Date</code></td>\n<td><code>toISOString()</code>: <code>2026-11-20T08:15:00.000Z</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a boolean</td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid <code>Date</code></td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>as it is</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A raw date is UTC: a date at midnight in Norway is the day before at 23:00 (22:00 in summer) in\nthe file. Export formatted values to get the dates as the user sees them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-csv-in-excel-the-separator-and-the-bom\">CSV in Excel: the separator and the BOM</h3>\n<p>For Excel, export <a href=\"#guide/export/excel\">Excel</a>: a CSV file is text, and Excel guesses the columns and the types.\nWhen it has to be CSV:</p>\n<p>Excel opens a <code>.csv</code> file with the list separator of the computer&#39;s locale. In a locale with a\ndecimal comma - Norwegian, German, French ... - that is <code>;</code>, and a file with <code>,</code> opens with every row\nin one column. For those users export with <code>separator: &#39;;&#39;</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.downloadCsv('export.csv', { separator: ';' });</code></pre>\n<p>With <code>;</code> a formatted <code>12,5</code> is written as it is; with <code>,</code> it is put in quotes (<code>&quot;12,5&quot;</code>).\n<code>separator: &#39;\\t&#39;</code> gives tab separated text.</p>\n<p>Excel also needs a UTF-8 byte order mark (BOM, the character U+FEFF) at the start of the file to read\nit as UTF-8: without one <code>æøå</code> show up garbled. <code>downloadCsv()</code> writes one by default, <code>bom: false</code>\nleaves it out. <code>getCsv()</code> does not write one by default, <code>bom: true</code> adds it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-formulas-csv-injection\">Formulas (CSV injection)</h3>\n<p>A spreadsheet takes a cell that starts with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code> or <code>@</code> as a formula. A value a user typed\ninto the grid - <code>=HYPERLINK(&quot;http://evil.example&quot;, &quot;Click me&quot;)</code> - would then run on the computer\nof whoever opens the file. That is &quot;CSV injection&quot;, see\n<a href=\"https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/CSV_Injection\">OWASP</a>.</p>\n<p>So by default (<code>escapeFormulas: true</code>) text that starts with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>@</code>, a tab, CR or LF, or\nwith the full width <code>＝ ＋ － ＠</code>, gets a <code>&#39;</code> in front: <code>&#39;=HYPERLINK(...)</code>, which a spreadsheet shows\nas text.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only text is escaped: a value that is text in the row (or an object), and the labels. A number, a\nboolean, a <code>Date</code> and a blank never are, whatever their formatter writes: <code>-5</code> stays <code>-5</code>.</li>\n<li>The value decides, not the field&#39;s type: a number field holding the text <code>&#39;-5&#39;</code> is written as\n<code>&#39;-5</code>. Keep the values of a number field numbers.</li>\n<li>Text that only looks like a formula gets the <code>&#39;</code> too: a phone number <code>+47 912 34 567</code> is written\n<code>&#39;+47 912 34 567</code>, a note <code>- see above</code> is <code>&#39;- see above</code>.</li>\n<li>The <code>&#39;</code> is part of the text in the file: a program that reads it gets it too. For a file a\nprogram reads, not a spreadsheet, turn it off with <code>escapeFormulas: false</code>.</li>\n<li>OWASP notes the escaping is not reliable in Excel once the file is saved and opened again: treat a\nfile of data users typed with care anyway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Excel export has none of this: <a href=\"#guide/export/no-formulas\">no formulas</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-file-format\">The file format</h3>\n<p>The text follows <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180\">RFC 4180</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a line per row, after the header line; every line ends with CRLF (<code>\\r\\n</code>), the last one too</li>\n<li>a value with the separator, a double quote, CR or LF is put in double quotes, and a double quote\nin it is doubled: <code>say &quot;hi&quot;</code> is written <code>&quot;say &quot;&quot;hi&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></li>\n<li>spaces are part of a value: they are kept, without quotes</li>\n<li>a row of a single empty value is written <code>&quot;&quot;</code>: many programs read an empty line as no value at all</li>\n<li>with no columns the text is empty</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-csv-options\">The CSV options</h3>\n<p><code>CsvOptions</code>, every option optional. It is plain JSON: it can be stored, or come from a menu of your\nown. An option the grid does not know, or a value an option does not take (<code>rows: &#39;some&#39;</code>,\n<code>separator: &#39;|&#39;</code>), throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>rows</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;all&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;all&#39;</code>: the rows the filter lets through, sorted. <code>&#39;selected&#39;</code>: the selected rows. <code>&#39;range&#39;</code>: the rows of the selected range of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fields</code></td>\n<td>the layout (<code>&#39;range&#39;</code>: the range&#39;s)</td>\n<td>the fields, in order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a first line with the column labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formatted</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the values as the cells show them; <code>false</code> as they are stored</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>separator</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;,&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;;&#39;</code> for Excel in a locale with a decimal comma, <code>&#39;\\t&#39;</code> tab separated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bom</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> for <code>downloadCsv()</code>, <code>false</code> for <code>getCsv()</code></td>\n<td>a UTF-8 BOM first, for Excel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>escapeFormulas</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a <code>&#39;</code> in front of text that starts with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>@</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { CsvOptions } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst forExcel: CsvOptions = { separator: ';' };\nconst forAProgram: CsvOptions = { formatted: false, escapeFormulas: false };\n\ngridInterface.downloadCsv('for-excel.csv', forExcel);\nconst text = gridInterface.getCsv(forAProgram);</code></pre>\n"},{"id":"live-data","title":"Live data","html":"<p>Rows that change while the grid shows them: prices from a server every second, the rows another user\nsaved. <code>datasource.upsert()</code> merges what the server sends by key, the cells show the new values on\ntheir own, and an edit the user has not saved is never overwritten - when the server changed it too,\nit is a conflict, for the user to resolve.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"live-data\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-values-show-on-their-own\">Values show on their own</h2>\n<p>A value set on a row shows in the grid without <code>gridInterface.refresh()</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nrow.price = 10; // shows in the next animation frame</code></pre>\n<p>The datasource sends <code>rows-changed</code> once the code that changed the rows is done (in a microtask):\none event, however many rows and fields it set, with the rows and the fields of each. The grid\nrenders the rows of it that are on screen in the next animation frame - once per frame, also for a\nloop over 100 000 rows. A row off screen is rendered when it scrolls in.</p>\n<p>A row is in <code>rows-changed</code> when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a field was set on it: from code, by the user, by <code>undo()</code> / <code>redo()</code>, <code>resetField()</code>,\n<code>resetRows()</code>, <code>resetData()</code>, <code>resolveConflict()</code>, or by <code>upsert()</code>. The validation of the edit is\ndone by then: its errors show with it</li>\n<li>its deletion mark changed, or its server error (<code>getDataContainer().setErrors()</code>)</li>\n<li>one of its conflicts came or went</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Still call <code>refresh()</code> when what the cells show changed without a row changing: what the\n<code>isReadonly</code> or <code>cellClass</code> hooks read, a value written to your own object instead of the row the\ndatasource hands out (that is no edit either, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/edits-from-code\">Edits from code</a>).\nA formatter that shows values differently needs <code>rebuild()</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>The cell being typed in is left as it is.</strong> Rendering it would put the formatted value into what\nthe user is typing - <code>12,</code> would become <code>12</code> - so no render touches the input the user typed in\nsince it got the focus, not even <code>refresh()</code>. Once the focus leaves it, the grid renders the rows on\nscreen again. A cell with the focus the user has not typed in yet is rendered: its text is its value,\nnothing is lost.</p>\n<p><strong>Scrolling renders only the rows that scroll in.</strong> A row that stays on screen is up to date already:\nits changes render themselves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-merging-rows-by-key-upsert\">Merging rows by key: <code>upsert()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Stock {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n    price: number;\n}\ndeclare function fetchStocks(): Promise&lt;Stock[]&gt;;\n\nconst stocks = new Datasource&lt;Stock&gt;({ key: 'id', fields: { price: 'number' } });\nstocks.setData(await fetchStocks());\n\n// a full poll: every row the server has. A row it no longer has goes, unless it has changes here\nsetInterval(async () =&gt; {\n    stocks.upsert(await fetchStocks(), { removeMissing: true });\n}, 1000);\n\n// a push of what changed: only the fields sent change\nstocks.upsert([{ id: 7, price: 12.5 }]);</code></pre>\n<p><code>upsert()</code> needs a key (<code>new Datasource({ key: &#39;id&#39; })</code>), it throws a <code>TypeError</code> without one, and\nfor a row without its key. For each row it gets:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>the row</th>\n<th>what happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>its key is in the data</td>\n<td>the fields it has get its values, as the server&#39;s: the value and its original, so the row is not edited by them. The fields it does not have stay as they are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a field the user has edited</td>\n<td>the user&#39;s value stays, the server&#39;s becomes its original - see <a href=\"#guide/live-data/conflicts\">Conflicts</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a row marked for deletion</td>\n<td>its fields get the values, it stays marked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>its key is not in the data</td>\n<td>added at the end: a loaded row, not new, not edited - the object itself, as <code>setData()</code> adds it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The keys are compared as text</strong>: <code>7</code> and <code>&#39;7&#39;</code> are the same key. The key field is never written.</li>\n<li><strong>A new row</strong> (<code>addNewEmpty()</code>) is not on the server yet: its key is a generated one, it never\nmatches a row of the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Not in the undo history</strong>: the server&#39;s values are no edits. The fields that change are\nvalidated, as for an edit. Undoing the user&#39;s own edits of a field the server changed goes back to\nthe server&#39;s value, not to the value the field had before - that would be an edit of the server&#39;s\nvalue, and a save would send the old value back.</li>\n<li><strong><code>removeMissing: true</code></strong> (default <code>false</code>): the rows are every row there is, and a row whose key\nis not among them is removed for good. Not a new row, and not a row with changes of the user&#39;s - an\nedit, a deletion mark, a cell being typed in: that one stays, in conflict. Use it for a full poll,\nleave it out for a push of changes.</li>\n<li><strong><code>refilter: false</code></strong> (default <code>true</code>): the filter does not run, so a row whose new values the\nfilter leaves out stays shown until it runs again - as for <code>setData(rows, { refilter: false })</code>.\nThe sorting and grouping still run.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then the displayed rows are rebuilt once - filtered (<code>filterChangedOnly()</code> too), sorted, grouped. The\nselection (it follows the key), the expanded groups and the scroll position stay. A row removed\nleaves the selection, and <code>currentEntity</code> is set to <code>null</code> when it was the current row. The grid keeps\nits row elements: the input the user types in keeps its focus and its text.</p>\n<p>It returns what it did: the rows <code>added</code>, <code>updated</code> and <code>removed</code>, and the <code>conflicts</code> it made.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const rowsFromServer: object[];\n\nconst { added, updated, removed, conflicts } = datasource.upsert(rowsFromServer, { removeMissing: true });\nconsole.log(`${added.length} new, ${updated.length} changed, ${removed.length} removed, ${conflicts.length} conflicts`);</code></pre>\n<p>It is cheap on many rows: one look up by key per row. 100 000 rows of which 1% changed take about 55\nms in node, 1000 rows into 100 000 about 16 ms - plus the sorting and grouping, which run as for\n<code>filter()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-conflicts\">Conflicts</h2>\n<p>A conflict is a change of the server&#39;s that <code>upsert()</code> did not write because the user changed the\nsame thing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A field</strong>: the server changed a field the user has edited and not saved (or is typing in). The\nuser&#39;s value stays; the server&#39;s value becomes the field&#39;s original, so <code>getChanges()</code> sends the\nuser&#39;s value as an edit of what the server has now, and reverting the field goes to the server&#39;s\nvalue. When the server has the user&#39;s value, the edit is done (not edited any more), and when it\nis back at the value the edit started from, nothing has to be chosen: neither is a conflict.</li>\n<li><strong>A row</strong>: <code>removeMissing</code> did not get a row that has changes here. It stays.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.on('conflicts-changed', () =&gt; {\n    for (const conflict of datasource.getConflicts()) {\n        if (conflict.field === null) {\n            console.log(`row ${conflict.row.__KEY} was removed on the server, it has changes here`);\n        } else {\n            console.log(`${conflict.field}: yours ${conflict.local}, the server's ${conflict.remote}, it was ${conflict.original}`);\n        }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>method, event</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>getConflicts(rows?)</code></td>\n<td>the conflicts, every row&#39;s or the rows given: <code>{ row, field, local, remote, original }</code> for a field, <code>{ row, field: null, remote: &#39;removed&#39; }</code> for a row. <code>local</code> is the value the row has when it is called</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hasConflicts()</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> when there is one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>resolveConflict(row, field, &#39;local&#39;)</code></td>\n<td>the user&#39;s value stays, still edited: a save sends it. A row the server removed stays, and is not in conflict again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>resolveConflict(row, field, &#39;remote&#39;)</code></td>\n<td>the server&#39;s value, as <code>resetField()</code> sets it: an edit, a step of the undo history. A row the server removed is removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conflicts-changed</code></td>\n<td>sent when they changed, once per run of code</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A conflict also goes when what it is about goes: the field is not edited any more (reverted, undone,\ntyped back to the server&#39;s value, saved with <code>acceptChanges()</code>), the row has no changes any more, or\nit leaves the data (<code>removeData()</code>, <code>setData()</code> without <code>add</code> ...). <code>resetData()</code> clears every one.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// the server wins every conflict\nfor (const conflict of datasource.getConflicts()) {\n    datasource.resolveConflict(conflict.row, conflict.field, 'remote');\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-the-grid-shows-them\">How the grid shows them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A cell a merge changed flashes</strong>: a tint that fades out over a second\n(<code>--simple-html-grid-merged-color</code>), when the cell is rendered in the second after the <code>upsert()</code>\nchanged its value. The cell element has the class <code>simple-html-cell-merged</code> meanwhile.\n<code>mergeHighlight: false</code> in the config turns it off.</li>\n<li><strong>A field in conflict</strong> has a marker in its top left corner in the conflict colour\n(<code>--simple-html-grid-conflict-color</code>), where an edited cell has the edited marker - also with\n<code>editedMarker: false</code>. Its tooltip has the server&#39;s value, and in an editable grid a click on it takes\nthe server&#39;s value (as <code>resolveConflict(row, field, &#39;remote&#39;)</code>, a step of the undo history). The cell\nelement has the class <code>simple-html-cell-conflict</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A row in conflict</strong> has two arrows on its row number (with <code>rowStatus</code>, the default), and its tooltip\nsays which fields the server changed too, or that the server removed the row. A screen reader gets it\nas text with the row number. An error wins the icon: it stops the save; a row marked for deletion\nkeeps its trash can.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To keep the user&#39;s value, or to take the server&#39;s for every field at once, call <code>resolveConflict()</code> -\nfrom a button, as the &quot;Live data&quot; sample does.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-showing-what-a-merge-changed-your-own-way\">Showing what a merge changed, your own way</h2>\n<p><code>datasource.getMergedAt(row, field)</code> is the time (<code>Date.now()</code>) the last <code>upsert()</code> changed the value\nof a field - <code>undefined</code> when no upsert changed it. A field the user has edited keeps the user&#39;s value,\nan upsert does not change it (a conflict is no change of the value). The grid renders the rows an\nupsert changed, so a <code>cellClass</code> hook can give those cells a class of your own - with\n<code>mergeHighlight: false</code>, for another look than the flash:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.updateHooks({\n    cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; {\n        const at = datasource.getMergedAt(row, field);\n        return at !== undefined &amp;&amp; Date.now() - at &lt; 1000 ? { input: 'just-changed' } : null;\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>@keyframes just-changed {\n    from { background: #ffe58a; }\n    to { background: transparent; }\n}\n.just-changed {\n    animation: just-changed 1s ease-out;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The animation runs when the class is added, once: a cell rendered again keeps it until it is\nrendered after the second is over.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-events\">Events</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>rows-changed</code></td>\n<td>values of rows changed (see above), once per run of code</td>\n<td><code>{ rows, fields }</code>: the rows, and <code>fields.get(row)</code> the fields set on each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conflicts-changed</code></td>\n<td>conflicts came or went, once per run of code, after <code>rows-changed</code></td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>getConflicts()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-changed</code></td>\n<td><code>upsert()</code> added or removed rows, or the displayed rows changed (filter, sort order, groups)</td>\n<td><code>{ info: &#39;upsert&#39;, added, removed? }</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>An upsert that only changed values sends no <code>collection-changed</code>: the grid renders the cells from\n<code>rows-changed</code>. Both live data events are the data container&#39;s: every datasource on the same\n<code>DataContainer</code> sends them. See <a href=\"#guide/events/datasource-events\">Events</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"selection-and-clipboard","title":"Selection and clipboard","html":"<p>The grid has three kinds of selection. <strong>Rows</strong> are selected in the datasource: a click on the row\nnumber, the rows a delete or an export works on. <strong>Columns</strong> are selected with the column numbers\nunder the header filters, and copied from their menu. <strong>Ranges of cells</strong> are selected in the grid, as\nin a spreadsheet: drag over the cells, then copy them (they paste into Excel or Google Sheets), paste\ncells copied there into the grid, or empty them with Delete.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"ranges\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-selecting-rows\">Selecting rows</h2>\n<p>A click on the row number selects the row, Ctrl+click (Cmd+click on a Mac) adds or removes one,\nShift+click selects the rows from the last one clicked without Shift - and keeps the rows the\nCtrl+clicks before it selected, so a second Shift+click changes the range only. That is the\ndatasource&#39;s selection, <code>datasource.getSelectedRows()</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/selection\">Selection</a>. The row menu&#39;s Copy, Paste cell, Clear cell, Delete and the\nexport of the selected rows work on these rows. A finger&#39;s tap adds or removes a row, and the row\nmenu&#39;s <em>Select ›</em> does what Shift and Ctrl do, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>.</p>\n<p>A right click on a row number - Shift+F10 or the menu key on one with the focus, a long press - opens\nthe row menu of the row, without a cell: what is about rows - undo and redo, select, copy, delete and\nsizes - see <a href=\"#guide/menus/row-menu-menu_idsrow\">Row menu</a>.</p>\n<p>A range of cells selects no rows, and selecting rows changes no range: they are two things.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-selecting-columns\">Selecting columns</h2>\n<p>The row of numbers under the header filters numbers the columns 1, 2, 3 ... across the pinned left,\nthe center and the pinned right columns, in the order of <code>getLayoutFields()</code>. A column of stacked\nfields (<code>fields: [&#39;phone&#39;, &#39;age&#39;]</code>) is one number. A click on a number selects columns as a click on\nthe row numbers selects rows:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a click</td>\n<td>that column only. Clicked again it stays selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+click, Cmd+click</td>\n<td>that column in or out, the others stay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+click</td>\n<td>the columns from the one clicked last without Shift to this one, either way, across the pinned sections. Shift+click again changes the range, from the same column; after Ctrl+clicks the range is added to the columns they selected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The selected numbers have the colour of a selected row, <code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-odd</code>. A\nclick puts the focus on the number - not a stop of Tab - and it has keys of its own there:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the column menu, the focus on its first item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C, Cmd+C</td>\n<td>copies the selected columns, as <em>Copy selected columns</em> below - the number&#39;s own column when none is selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>no column selected. With a menu open, Esc closes the menu first</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The columns are selected by their numbers, so the selection is cleared when the fields of the layout\nchange - a column moved, pinned, unpinned, hidden or added, <code>setState({ layout })</code>,\n<code>updateConfig({ layout })</code> - and <code>column-selection-change</code> is sent. It is kept when a column is\nresized, on a filter, a sort, grouping and <code>refresh()</code>. It selects no rows and no cells, and it is not\npart of <code>getState()</code>. <code>selectSizeHeight: 0</code> hides the numbers.</p>\n<p>The row menu&#39;s <em>Copy › Row (selected rows/col)</em> copies the selected rows in the selected columns,\nevery column when none is selected.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-column-menu\">The column menu</h3>\n<p>A right click on a column number - Shift+F10 or the menu key on one with the focus, a long press -\nopens the column menu: <em>Select ›</em> (what Ctrl and Shift do on a number, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>), then the copies of columns:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>copies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Copy column (all rows)</td>\n<td>the column right clicked, every displayed row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy selected columns (all rows)</td>\n<td>the selected columns, in the order of the layout. Greyed out with none selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy header names</td>\n<td>the label of every column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy header name</td>\n<td>the label of the column right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy selected header names</td>\n<td>the labels of the selected columns. Greyed out with none selected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The columns</strong> are copied as the row menu copies rows: tab separated text with a header line of\nthe labels (unless <code>copyHeaders</code> is false), a line per row ended with CRLF, and an html table of the\nsame cells - see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/copy\">Copy</a>. Every displayed data row: the rows the filter lets through, no group\nrows, and not the rows of a collapsed group (the CSV export has those, <code>getCsv()</code>). A column of\nstacked fields gives a column of text per field, the top one first.</li>\n<li><strong>The names</strong> are one line of text, a tab between them and no line end: pasted into a spreadsheet\nthey are a row of cells, into a text a line. A label with a tab, a line break or a double quote is\nput in double quotes, as in the header line of a copy. No html, and <code>copyHeaders</code> does not change\nthem: they are what is copied.</li>\n<li>A right click does not change the selection: the column right clicked is copied, selected or not.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The copies are flat, without a submenu: every entry is a copy, a <em>Copy ›</em> around them would only add\na step. Its ids are <code>column.select.*</code> and <code>column.copy.*</code> (<code>MENU_IDS.column</code>), the <code>menus</code> hook\nchanges it with its <code>column</code> function - see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-built-in-ids\">Menus</a> - and the texts have\nthe same ids, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/column-menu\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-api-of-the-columns\">The API of the columns</h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.selectColumns([2, 3]);\ngridInterface.getSelectedColumns(); // [2, 3]\n// their fields, a column of two stacked fields gives both\ngridInterface.getLayoutFields({ selectedOnly: true }); // ['country', 'phone', 'age']\ngridInterface.selectColumns([]); // none\n\ngridInterface.on('column-selection-change', ({ columns, fields }) =&gt; {\n    console.log(columns.length ? `columns ${columns.join(', ')}: ${fields.join(', ')}` : 'no column selected');\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>getSelectedColumns()</code></strong> gives the numbers, ascending, as a copy. <code>[]</code> when none is selected.</li>\n<li><strong><code>selectColumns(columns)</code></strong> replaces the selection, as if the user had clicked; a Shift+click after\nit selects from the last number given. A number that is no column - not a whole number from 1 to\nthe number of columns - throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>column-selection-change</code></strong> is sent each time the selected columns change: a click, Esc,\n<code>selectColumns()</code>, the layout changed. <code>{ columns, fields }</code>: the numbers and their fields. Not\nwhen nothing changed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</h2>\n<p>A range is the rectangle between two cells, both included:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>its rows</strong> are the displayed rows between them (<code>datasource.getRows()</code>, 0 based - the <code>row</code> of\nthe grid events). A group row in between is part of the rectangle, but has no cells: it is left out\nof a copy, a paste and a clear</li>\n<li><strong>its fields</strong> are the fields between them in the layout: pinned left, center, pinned right, in the\norder of <code>gridInterface.getLayoutFields()</code>. A column of two stacked fields is two columns here, the\ntop one first - as in the CSV export and for a screen reader</li>\n<li><strong>it starts</strong> at the cell the mouse was pressed in, or the cell with the focus - and ends at the\nopposite corner, which can be above it or left of it</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There can be several ranges: Ctrl+click adds one. The last one is the one made last; where it starts\nis the <strong>active cell</strong>, the cell with the focus.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-mouse\">The mouse</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>press in a cell and drag</td>\n<td>a range from that cell to the cell under the pointer, across the pinned sections. Inside the cell pressed it is the input&#39;s own text selection; once the pointer is on another cell it is a range, and the text the press selected is not selected any more</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+click, Shift+drag</td>\n<td>the last range from where it starts to the cell clicked - with no range, from the cell with the focus. The focus stays in the active cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+click, Cmd+click</td>\n<td>another range, of the cell clicked; Ctrl+drag makes it larger. With no range yet the cell with the focus stays selected, as a range of its own (as in Excel)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a plain click</td>\n<td>clears the ranges, and does what it did before: the cell gets the focus, its row is the current row, a date cell opens its picker</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Dragged past the top or the bottom of the rows the grid scrolls, a row at a time while the pointer\nstays out there; past the left or right edge of the grid the middle columns scroll.</p>\n<p>A click on a cell does not select its row - it never did: the row number does that. Shift+click does\nnot make the row clicked the current row (the active cell stays where it was), Ctrl+click and\nShift+click do not open the date picker of a date cell and do not check a checkbox.</p>\n<p>On the text of a link cell (<a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-link-cell\">Templates and links</a>) a plain click and\nCtrl/Cmd+click open the link - Ctrl/Cmd+click there makes no range. Shift+click makes the range and does\nnot open it; a press beside the text is the cell&#39;s, as anywhere. Copy, paste and fill down are the value,\nnot the text of the template.</p>\n<p><strong>A pen</strong> works as the mouse, where the browser does not take its drag for scrolling. <strong>A finger</strong>\ndoes not drag a range - a swipe scrolls the grid - it makes ranges with the row menu&#39;s <em>Select ›</em>,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-keyboard\">The keyboard</h3>\n<p>In a cell (see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard\">Keyboard and accessibility</a> for every key):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+arrows</td>\n<td>starts a range at the cell, or moves the end of the last range one cell: a row up or down (group rows are skipped), a field left or right in the order of the layout - in a column of two stacked fields, right of the top field is the field under it. The focus stays in the active cell, the grid scrolls to the end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+PageDown / Shift+PageUp</td>\n<td>the end a page of rows down / up, the same field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Shift+End / Ctrl+Shift+Home</td>\n<td>the end to the last field of the last row / the first field of the first row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+End / Alt+Shift+Home</td>\n<td>the end to the last / first field of its row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Shift+ArrowDown / Ctrl+Shift+ArrowUp</td>\n<td>the end to the edge of the data in its field: the last cell with a value before a blank one, from a blank one the next cell with a value, else the last / first row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>clears the ranges. With a menu open, Esc closes the menu first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V, Delete</td>\n<td>see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/copy\">Copy</a>, <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/paste\">Paste</a>, <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/delete\">Delete</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The arrows move the caret in the cell&#39;s input and Shift+arrows select text in it, as always; Alt+arrows\nmove to the next cell. On a Mac, Alt is the Option key: Option+Shift+arrows select a range, not words,\nand Ctrl is Cmd: Cmd+Shift+End.</p>\n<p>Each key with Shift moves the end of the range where the key without it moves the focus - see\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keys-that-jump\">The keys that jump</a> - and starts a range at the cell when there\nis none. The focus stays in the active cell; when the range scrolls it away (Ctrl+Shift+End from the\nfirst row) the focus goes to the end of the range, which is on screen, so the keys go on working:\nCtrl+C copies the range, the next Shift key moves its end again. The ranges stay.</p>\n<p>Moving to another cell - Tab, Alt+arrows, Enter, a click - clears the ranges, and so does typing in a\ncell: as moving the active cell does in a spreadsheet. A right click does not, the row menu works on\nthem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-how-a-range-looks\">How a range looks</h3>\n<p>The cells of a range get a tint, and the range a line around it; the empty cells under a column with\nfewer stacked fields than the others go with the field above them, so a range is a rectangle on screen\ntoo. The colours are <code>--simple-html-grid-range-bg-color</code> and <code>--simple-html-grid-range-border-color</code>,\nlight and dark, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</a>. The cells have the class\n<code>simple-html-cell-in-range</code>, the cells at its edges <code>simple-html-cell-range-top</code>, <code>-bottom</code>, <code>-left</code>\nand <code>-right</code>.</p>\n<p>The cells are reused while the grid scrolls: the classes follow the rows and fields they show.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-summary-in-the-footer\">The summary in the footer</h3>\n<p>With ranges of more than one cell the footer shows a summary of them at its right, like the status bar\nof a spreadsheet: <code>Count: 20   Sum: 201   Average: 40.2   Min: 26   Max: 53</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Count</strong> is the cells with a value, of any type: a blank (<code>null</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>) is left out, <code>0</code> and\n<code>false</code> count.</li>\n<li><strong>Sum, Average, Min, Max</strong> are of the numbers of the number fields (<code>type: &#39;number&#39;</code>) - a text of\ndigits is no number. Without numbers only the count shows.</li>\n<li>Every cell once: ranges that overlap count their common cells once; group rows have no cells.</li>\n<li>The numbers are shown with the formatter of the number field furthest left in the ranges, two decimals\nat most - with its display format when it has one (<code>1 234,50</code>), and that format&#39;s decimals.</li>\n<li>It follows the ranges and the values in them: an edit, a paste, an upsert. One cell, or no range,\nshows none.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is a group named &quot;Selected cells&quot; for a screen reader, its tooltip has all of it when a narrow grid\ncuts it. The texts are <code>footer.summary.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a>. It is on the line of the filter\n(Clear and the chips, or the button &quot;No filter&quot;): the filter chips collapse to one box when they would\nreach under it, and the summary is cut where that line ends.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-when-the-ranges-are-cleared\">When the ranges are cleared</h3>\n<p>The rows of a range are indexes of the displayed rows, and its fields are good for the layout it was\nmade in. So the grid clears the ranges, and sends <code>range-change</code>, when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>the displayed rows change</strong>: a filter, a sort, grouping, a group expanded or collapsed, rows set,\nadded or removed (<code>setData()</code>, <code>addNewEmpty()</code>, <code>removeData()</code>, an <code>upsert()</code> that adds, removes or\nmoves rows), rows marked for deletion, <code>setState()</code> / <code>resetState()</code> with any of these</li>\n<li><strong>the layout changes</strong>: a column moved, pinned, unpinned, hidden, added, its fields stacked another\nway, <code>setState({ layout })</code>, <code>updateConfig({ layout })</code></li>\n<li><strong>the grid is put back on the page</strong>: its rows may have changed while it was away</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are kept when a value changes (an edit, a paste, <code>upsert()</code> of values only), when rows are\nselected or the current row changes, when a column is resized, and on <code>refresh()</code> and <code>rebuild()</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-api\">The API</h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { CellRange } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// rows 0 to 4, every field from name to age in the layout\nconst range: CellRange = { fromRow: 0, fromField: 'name', toRow: 4, toField: 'age' };\ngridInterface.setRanges([range]);\n\ngridInterface.getRanges(); // [{ fromRow: 0, fromField: 'name', toRow: 4, toField: 'age' }]\ngridInterface.clearRanges();\n\ngridInterface.on('range-change', ({ ranges }) =&gt; {\n    console.log(ranges.length ? `${ranges.length} ranges` : 'no cells selected');\n});\n\n// the cells of the last range as CSV, with their labels\nconst csv = gridInterface.getCsv({ rows: 'range' });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>getRanges()</code></strong> gives copies: changing them changes nothing. <code>[]</code> when no cells are selected.</li>\n<li><strong><code>setRanges(ranges)</code></strong> replaces the ranges, as if the user had selected them. It does not move the\nfocus or scroll. A row that is not displayed (0 to <code>datasource.length() - 1</code>) or a field that is\nnot in the layout throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>range-change</code></strong> is sent each time they change, with what <code>getRanges()</code> gives - while the mouse\ndrags, each time the range reaches another cell. Not when nothing changed.</li>\n<li>A range is plain JSON. Its row indexes are only good for the rows as they are displayed now, so a\nrange is not part of <code>getState()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The export</strong>: <code>getCsv({ rows: &#39;range&#39; })</code> and <code>downloadCsv(filename, { rows: &#39;range&#39; })</code> export\nthe last range, see <a href=\"#guide/export/the-rows\">Export</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-with-a-finger\">With a finger</h2>\n<p>A finger has no Shift and no Ctrl. So on a phone or a tablet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A tap on a row number toggles the row</strong>, as Ctrl+click does: the first tap selects it, more taps\nadd rows or take them out again - as in a phone&#39;s own lists. In <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode a tap selects the\nrow as a click does (the one selected before is not). A tap on a group row&#39;s number adds the rows of\nthe group.</li>\n<li><strong>A tap on a column number toggles the column</strong>, in every selection mode: the column numbers have\nnone, Ctrl+click selects more columns in every mode.</li>\n<li><strong>The menus are Shift and Ctrl</strong>: a long press on a cell or a row number opens the row menu, on a\ncolumn number the column menu - both have <strong>Select ›</strong>. The mouse and the keyboard have it too.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The mouse and the pen are not changed: a plain click selects the one row or column. The grid tells a\nfinger&#39;s tap by the click&#39;s <code>pointerType</code> (<code>&#39;touch&#39;</code>): a click is a <code>PointerEvent</code> in Chrome 92,\nFirefox 129 and Safari 18.2 and later; before that, the pointer that pressed last in the grid.</p>\n<p><em>Select ›</em> in the row menu, opened on a cell or a row number:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>greyed out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>This row</td>\n<td>the row in or out of the selection, as Ctrl+click on its number. A ✓ while it is selected. In <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode it replaces the row selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rows from N to here</td>\n<td>the rows from the anchor - N, the row selected last without Shift - to this one, as Shift+click on its number. After taps (or <em>This row</em>) the range is added to the rows they selected</td>\n<td>without an anchor (N is this row then), when the anchor is this row, in <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All rows</td>\n<td>every row, <code>datasource.selectAll()</code></td>\n<td>when every row is selected, in <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Range from the active cell to here</td>\n<td>a range of cells, as Shift+click on the cell: from where the last range starts, or from the active cell - the cell with the focus (the one tapped last) - to this cell</td>\n<td>when there is neither, when it would start at this cell, on a row number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add this cell to the ranges</td>\n<td>a range of this cell, as Ctrl+click on it. With no range yet the active cell stays selected, a range of its own</td>\n<td>when the cell is in a range, on a row number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clear selection</td>\n<td>no row selected and no range of cells</td>\n<td>when there is neither</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>In <code>&#39;none&#39;</code> mode rows can not be selected: <em>This row</em>, <em>Rows from N to here</em> and <em>All rows</em> are left\nout, the ranges and <em>Clear selection</em> stay. Otherwise an entry that can do nothing is greyed out, not\nleft out: the menu keeps its shape.</p>\n<p>So a finger makes a range of cells: tap the first cell (it gets the focus), long press the last one,\n<em>Select › Range from the active cell to here</em>. Or long press the first cell, <em>Add this cell to the\nranges</em>, then the last one, <em>Range from the active cell to here</em>. <em>Copy › Range</em>, <em>Paste range</em>,\n<em>Clear range</em> work on them.</p>\n<p><em>Select ›</em> in the column menu, opened on a column number:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>does</th>\n<th>greyed out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>This column</td>\n<td>the column in or out, as Ctrl+click. A ✓ while it is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Columns from N to here</td>\n<td>the columns from the anchor - N, the column clicked last without Shift - to this one, as Shift+click</td>\n<td>without an anchor (N is this column then), when the anchor is this column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All columns</td>\n<td>every column</td>\n<td>when every column is selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clear column selection</td>\n<td>no column selected, <code>selectColumns([])</code></td>\n<td>with none selected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The entries send what a click sends: <code>selection-change</code>, <code>column-selection-change</code>, <code>range-change</code>.\nTheir ids are <code>row.select.*</code> and <code>column.select.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-built-in-ids\">Menus</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-copy\">Copy</h2>\n<p>Ctrl+C (Cmd+C) in a cell, with no text selected in it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>with a range of more than one cell, or several ranges</strong>: the cells as a table of tab separated\ntext - <strong>a header line of the column labels first</strong>, then a line per data row, each ended with CRLF,\na tab between the fields, as Excel puts cells on the clipboard. A value with a tab, a line break or a\ndouble quote is put in double quotes, a quote in it doubled. Every value is the text the cell shows,\nthrough the field&#39;s formatter: <code>12,5</code> with a decimal comma, a date as its formatter writes it. Not a\ncolumn&#39;s display format (<code>2026-W47</code>, <code>1 234 567,89</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>): the value as the field writes it, which a paste\nreads back.</li>\n<li><strong>else</strong>: the value of the cell with the focus, as the cell shows it, without a line end - as the\nrow menu&#39;s <em>Copy › Cell Value</em>. Before v6 this copied nothing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An html table of the same cells goes on the clipboard too, with the labels as its header row (<code>&lt;th&gt;</code>)\nand thin borders - Word, Google Docs and Excel paste the html, so the table has its headers there. A\nline break in a cell is a <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> with the style Excel reads as a line break in the cell\n(<code>mso-data-placement:same-cell</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>The header line</strong> is there for every copy of more than one cell: Ctrl+C on ranges, the row\nmenu&#39;s <em>Copy › Column</em>, <em>Row</em>, <em>Row (selected rows/col)</em> and <em>Range</em>, and the column menu&#39;s <em>Copy\ncolumn</em> and <em>Copy selected columns</em> (Ctrl+C on a column number). Not for one cell (Ctrl+C on a\ncell, <em>Copy › Cell Value</em>): that is a value. <code>copyHeaders: false</code> in the config copies the values only:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.updateConfig({ copyHeaders: false });</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Text selected in the cell&#39;s input</strong> is copied as before: the input&#39;s own copy. Making a range ends\nthe text selection of the active cell, so after a drag or Alt+Shift+arrows Ctrl+C copies the range.</p>\n<p><strong>Several ranges are copied as one table</strong>: the rows of every range, in the order of the grid, and\nthe fields of every range, in the order of the layout. A cell that is in none of the ranges is\nempty. So two ranges side by side (Name and Age, rows 1-2) are one table of Name and Age - the\ncolumns between them are not copied - and two ranges one above the other (City rows 1-2 and row 5)\none longer table; ranges that cross leave the cells they do not cover empty. Excel refuses to copy\nranges that do not make a rectangle together; the grid copies them all.</p>\n<p>Text that looks like a formula (<code>=SUM(A1:A2)</code>) is copied as it is: what is copied is what is in the\ncells, and a paste back into the grid gives the same values. A spreadsheet may take it as a formula\nwhen it is pasted there - the CSV export escapes it (<code>escapeFormulas</code>), the clipboard does not.</p>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell) has <em>Copy › Range</em> while there are ranges: every range, as Ctrl+C.\n<em>Copy › Column</em> and <em>Row</em> copy the selected rows, with the header line, values as the cells show them.\nThat also works when the focus is not in a cell, after the wheel scrolled the grid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-paste\">Paste</h2>\n<p>Ctrl+V (Cmd+V) in a cell of an editable grid reads the plain text of the clipboard as tab separated\ncells: what Excel and Google Sheets copy, and what the grid copies. A line per row (CRLF, LF or CR);\na value in double quotes can hold tabs and line breaks, <code>&quot;&quot;</code> is a quote; the line end Excel puts after\nthe last row is not a row.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A header line is left out</strong>: when every cell of the first line is the label of one of the grid&#39;s\ncolumns (any case, spaces around ignored), it is a copy&#39;s header and is not written into cells - so\na copy of the grid pasted back into it, into the same columns or others, writes the values. A\nfirst line of data that is all labels is left out too; that is rare, and the paste can be undone.</li>\n<li><strong>One value, ranges of more than one cell</strong>: every cell of every range gets it - copy one cell,\nselect a range, paste.</li>\n<li><strong>A block of cells</strong>: from the top left cell of the last range - or the cell with the focus when\nthere is no range - down and right, a row of the block per data row. With no range the cells pasted\ninto become a range, so you see where they went. A block smaller than the range is pasted once, it\nis not repeated to fill the range; a larger one is pasted whole.</li>\n<li><strong>One value, no range</strong>: the input&#39;s own paste, at the caret. Unless the input can not take it: a\nvalue with a line break (an input has none, the browser would make it a space), a checkbox, a\nmultiline cell (its input is readonly, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>), a\ncolumn with <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code> or a custom cell with the focus on it (it has no input, see\n<a href=\"#guide/custom-cells/the-keys-and-the-focus\">Custom cells</a>) - the grid writes the value into the cell\nthen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every cell is written as typing it does: the text through the field&#39;s formatter (<code>parse</code>) - <code>12,5</code>\nis 12.5 in a number field, <code>2026-11-20</code> a <code>Date</code> in a date field, <code>true</code> in a boolean field. An\n<strong>empty text empties the cell</strong> (<code>null</code>), as Delete does: a blank cell copied in Excel is blank in the\ngrid, also in a number field.</p>\n<p>What is skipped:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>readonly cells</strong>: the grid&#39;s <code>readonly</code>, the column&#39;s <code>readonly</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> hook, and the\nrows the <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">edit permissions</a> lock (<code>change: false</code>:\nthe rows that are not new). A readonly grid pastes nothing, nor does one where no\nvalue can change (<code>change</code> and <code>add</code> both <code>false</code>) - Ctrl+V is the browser&#39;s there</li>\n<li><strong>group rows</strong>: the next row of the block goes to the next data row</li>\n<li><strong>rows past the last displayed row</strong>: a paste adds no rows. Paste fewer, add rows first\n(<code>addNewEmpty()</code>), or paste them as new rows (below)</li>\n<li><strong>fields past the last field of the layout</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The validation runs on every cell written, as for an edit: the errors show on the cells and the row\nnumbers. The edited markers and the row states follow too. A paste sends no <code>paste</code> event (that is the\nrow menu&#39;s <em>Paste cell</em>): the rows changed are in <code>rows-changed</code>.</p>\n<p>The row menu has <em>Paste: Paste range</em> while there are ranges: it pastes as Ctrl+V does with ranges.\nIt reads the clipboard with <code>navigator.clipboard.readText()</code>, which the browser may ask the user to\nallow first (Ctrl+V needs no permission).</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-paste-as-new-rows\">Paste as new rows</h3>\n<p>Rows copied in Excel or Google Sheets can be added as new rows: with <code>importMenu</code> in the config, the row\nmenu&#39;s Paste › has <em>As new rows…</em>. It opens the <a href=\"#guide/import\">import dialog</a> with the rows on the\nclipboard - their columns from the right clicked cell&#39;s column on, as a paste of a block, or by name when\nthe first line names the columns (the dialog says which, and a picker over each column changes it). The\nvalues are read as typing reads them, the problems are listed before anything is added, and <em>Import</em> adds\nthe rows as new rows: one step of the undo history. Only where the user may add rows (<code>edit.add</code>).</p>\n<p>The rows can also be pasted into the dialog itself (Ctrl+V), when the browser does not give the\nclipboard to <code>readText()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-delete\">Delete</h2>\n<p>Delete or Backspace in a cell, with a range of more than one cell (or several ranges) and no text\nselected in the input: every cell of every range is emptied (<code>null</code>), the readonly cells skipped, in\nan editable grid. In one cell they are the input&#39;s own keys, as before. The row menu&#39;s <em>Clear: Clear\nrange</em> does the same.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-fill-down\">Fill down</h2>\n<p><strong>Ctrl+D</strong> (Cmd+D on a Mac) in a cell fills down, like a spreadsheet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>with ranges</strong>: in every range the values of its top row go into the rows under it</li>\n<li><strong>a range of one row</strong>, or <strong>the cell alone</strong> (no range): it gets the values of the data row above it</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The fill handle</strong> is the small square at the bottom right corner of the last range. Drag it down: the\nrange grows to the row under the pointer (past the bottom of the rows the grid scrolls), and when it\nis let go the rows it grew by get the values of the range&#39;s rows, repeated from its top - two rows\n<code>open</code>, <code>done</code> dragged over four more rows give <code>open</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>done</code>. Down only.</p>\n<p>The values are copied as they are, a date as a date of its own; the cells that can not be edited are\nskipped, group rows have no cells, and it is one step of the undo history (Ctrl+Z). In a readonly grid\nthere is no handle, and Ctrl+D is the browser&#39;s - and so where the edit permissions let no value change\n(<code>change</code> and <code>add</code> both <code>false</code>). With <code>change: false</code> only the new rows are filled. From code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.setRanges([{ fromRow: 0, fromField: 'status', toRow: 4, toField: 'status' }]);\ngridInterface.fillDown();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-undo\">Undo</h2>\n<p>A paste, a Delete and a clear of ranges are written in one go: <strong>one Ctrl+Z</strong> takes all of it back,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// what a paste or a Delete did, from code: one step\ndatasource.undo();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-find\">Find</h2>\n<p>Ctrl+F in the grid finds a text in the cells and marks them - a match in a range shows both tints. It\nselects nothing: the ranges and the selected rows stay as they are; Esc in the find bar puts the focus in\nthe cell of the current match, which clears the ranges as moving to another cell does. See\n<a href=\"#guide/find\">Find</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-screen-reader-gets\">What a screen reader gets</h2>\n<p>The cells of a range have <code>aria-selected=&quot;true&quot;</code>; the other cells have none. The column numbers are a\nheader row of their own, a cell per column with <code>aria-selected</code> <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/what-a-screen-reader-gets\">Keyboard and accessibility</a>. The keys are in the\ntable of <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/a-cell\">Keyboard and accessibility</a>, the limits in its\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/known-limits\">Known limits</a>: a range made with Alt+Shift+arrows is not read out\nwhile it grows, the focus stays in the active cell.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-known-limits\">Known limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A finger drags no range</strong>: it makes them with the row menu&#39;s <em>Select ›</em>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/with-a-finger\">With a finger</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy and paste listen while the focus is in a cell of the grid.</strong> The wheel takes the focus from\nthe cell (its row element is reused for other rows), then Ctrl+C goes to the page: use the row\nmenu&#39;s <em>Copy › Range</em>, or Shift+click the cell at the end of the range again - the range stays as it\nis and the cell gets the focus.</li>\n<li><strong>Cut</strong> (Ctrl+X) is the input&#39;s own: it cuts the text selected in the cell, not a range. Copy, then\nDelete.</li>\n<li><strong>Several ranges</strong> copy as one table (see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/copy\">Copy</a>); a block is pasted into the last one.</li>\n<li><strong>No fill</strong>: a block is not repeated to fill a larger range, only one value fills it.</li>\n<li><strong>Checked in Chrome</strong>: the drag over the cells, the text selection it ends, the clipboard. Firefox\nand Safari have not been tried.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"aggregates","title":"Aggregates","html":"<p>A column can be summed up: its sum, average, smallest or largest number, or the number of values it\nhas - one of them, or several. The grid shows them in the column&#39;s place in every group row - over the\nrows of the group - and in a totals row under the rows, over every row the filter lets through. The\nuser turns them on and off in the header menu (right click a column label), and they are part of the\ngrid&#39;s state. The datasource computes the\nvalues, so they are there without a grid too: <code>datasource.aggregate()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"aggregates\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-in-the-config\">In the config</h2>\n<p><code>aggregate</code> is an option of a column, plain JSON like the rest of the config:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        balance: { aggregate: ['sum', 'avg'] }, // two functions: two lines\n        age: { aggregate: 'avg' },\n        email: { aggregate: 'count' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 160 },\n            { fields: ['email'], width: 200 },\n            { fields: ['age'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['balance'], width: 120 }\n        ]\n    },\n    totalsRow: true, // off by default\n    initialState: { grouping: [{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>function</th>\n<th>the value</th>\n<th>of no values</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;sum&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the sum of the numbers</td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;avg&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the average of the numbers</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;min&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the smallest number</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;max&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the largest number</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;count&#39;</code></td>\n<td>how many values are not blank, of any type</td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> are for <code>number</code> fields</strong>, the type the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code> give\nthem. On another field the grid throws a <code>TypeError</code> when it is made, or when <code>updateConfig()</code> is\ngiven the column - a config from JSON could otherwise show nothing without a word. They take the\nnumbers: a blank (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>) and what is no number (text, even <code>&#39;12&#39;</code>, <code>NaN</code>) are\nleft out, of the average too. <code>0</code> is a number.</li>\n<li><strong><code>count</code></strong> counts the values that are not blank, in any field: <code>0</code> and <code>false</code> are values.</li>\n<li><strong>Rows marked for deletion count</strong> until they are saved (<code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code>, or\n<code>acceptChanges()</code> after the server took them): they are rows of the grid until then, as in the\nfooter&#39;s count and the export.</li>\n<li><strong>One function or several</strong>: <code>aggregate: &#39;sum&#39;</code> or <code>aggregate: [&#39;sum&#39;, &#39;avg&#39;]</code>. Each is a line of\nits own, always in the order sum, avg, min, max, count. A field shown in two columns of the layout\nhas the same functions in both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The two options of the grid:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>groupAggregates</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the aggregates in the group rows. <code>false</code> shows the name of the group only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>totalsRow</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>the totals row under the rows</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-from-the-header-menu\">From the header menu</h2>\n<p>Right click a column label: the <strong>Aggregate:</strong> section has <em>Sum</em>, <em>Average</em>, <em>Min</em>, <em>Max</em> - on a number\ncolumn - and <em>Count</em>, each a toggle with a ✓ when it is on, and <em>Totals row</em>, a toggle of the totals row.\nWhat the user turns on is part of the grid&#39;s state:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.getState().aggregates; // { balance: ['sum', 'avg'], email: ['count'] }\ngridInterface.getState().totalsRow; // true\n\n// the same from code - every field listed gets these, the others none\ngridInterface.setState({ aggregates: { balance: ['sum'] }, totalsRow: false });\n\n// back to the config's: columns.&lt;field&gt;.aggregate and totalsRow\ngridInterface.resetState();</code></pre>\n<p><code>setState()</code> checks the functions as the config&#39;s are checked: a function that is none, or <code>sum</code> on a\nfield that is not a number, throws a <code>TypeError</code> and changes nothing. <code>updateConfig()</code> changes the\ntotals row only when it is given <code>totalsRow</code>, so a toggle the user made stays. The ids are\n<code>header.aggregate.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-they-are-shown\">How they are shown</h2>\n<p>Each value is a line in its column: the label at the left - <code>Sum</code>, the texts <code>aggregate.sum</code>,\n<code>aggregate.avg</code>, <code>aggregate.min</code>, <code>aggregate.max</code> and <code>aggregate.count</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/aggregates\">Texts</a>. Translate them like the other texts, or make them shorter:\n<code>setTexts({ &#39;aggregate.sum&#39;: &#39;Σ&#39; })</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The field&#39;s formatter formats the value</strong>: a <code>balance</code> field with a formatter of two decimals\nshows its sum with two decimals. A column&#39;s display format (<code>ColumnConfig.format</code>, the header menu&#39;s\nFormat ›) does too: <code>{ number: { decimals: 2, thousands: &#39; &#39;, decimal: &#39;,&#39; } }</code> shows the sum as\n<code>1 234 565,39</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">Display formats</a>. Nothing is rounded before that - an average of whole numbers is\n<code>34.666666666666664</code> until the formatter makes it <code>34.7</code>. A formatter that shows whole numbers as\nthey are and the rest with one decimal suits a field of whole numbers, see the sample. <code>count</code> is\nshown as a whole number.</li>\n<li><strong>The label at the left, the value at the right</strong> of the column, so the labels line up and so do\nthe numbers. In a narrow column the label gives way first; a value still too long ends in <code>...</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The sum does not drift</strong> over many rows: it is added up with a compensated sum, 100 000 times\n<code>0.1</code> is <code>10000</code>, not <code>10000.000000018848</code>. What is left is what a number in javascript is:\n<code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is <code>0.30000000000000004</code> - the formatter shows it.</li>\n<li><strong>A line per function</strong>: a column&#39;s lines are its fields&#39; functions one under the other, from the\ntop. In a column of stacked fields (<code>fields: [&#39;debit&#39;, &#39;credit&#39;]</code>) the label says which field it is:\n<code>Sum Debit</code>, <code>Sum Credit</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-in-the-group-rows\">In the group rows</h2>\n<p>A group row with aggregates has the group&#39;s name on a line of its own, and the aggregates on the lines\nunder it, each in its column, over every row of the group: the rows of its sub groups, and of\ncollapsed groups too. A collapsed group shows the same values as an expanded one. So a group row is\n1 + n lines high, n the most lines a column needs - a column with sum and avg makes it three lines.\nThe name&#39;s line is a row high (<code>cellHeight</code>), each line of aggregates a line of text\n(<code>multilineLineHeight</code>, 15px by default), as the lines of a <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">multiline cell</a>: with the\ndefaults a group row with sum and avg is <code>22 + 2 × 15</code> = 52px. The name never covers a value, and it is in\nthe header&#39;s bold - the values are in the weight of the cells. Without aggregates (or\n<code>groupAggregates: false</code>) a group row is one line.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-totals-row\">The totals row</h2>\n<p><code>totalsRow: true</code> shows a row under the rows: &quot;Total&quot; under the row numbers (the text\n<code>aggregate.total</code>) and each aggregate in its column, over every row the filter lets through - when\ngrouped, the rows of collapsed groups too. It follows the columns when the middle scrolls sideways,\nand the pinned columns stay where they are, as in the rows. It is as many lines high as the column with\nthe most functions needs.</p>\n<p>The horizontal scrollbar is between the rows and the totals row. The totals row takes its own room:\nthe rows get less, the footer stays as it is.</p>\n<p>The totals row is not part of an export: <code>getCsv()</code> and <code>downloadCsv()</code> give the rows, a spreadsheet\nsums them up itself, and a line of totals would be read back as a row.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-when-they-change\">When they change</h2>\n<p>The values follow the rows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An edit</strong>, in the grid or from code (<code>row.balance = 10</code>), <code>undo()</code>, <code>redo()</code>, <code>upsert()</code>,\n<code>resetData()</code> ...: the group rows on screen and the totals row show the new values in the next\nanimation frame, with the cells - the datasource&#39;s <code>rows-changed</code> event.</li>\n<li><strong>The filter</strong>: the totals and the groups are over the rows it lets through.</li>\n<li><strong>Sorting</strong> changes no value, <strong>grouping</strong> makes new group rows.</li>\n<li>A value set on your own object, not on the row the datasource hands out (<code>getDataRows()</code> ...), is\nnot seen: neither the grid nor the datasource knows it changed. Set it on the row.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-from-code\">From code</h2>\n<p><code>datasource.aggregate(field, fn, rows?)</code> gives the value the grid shows, without a grid too:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.aggregate('balance', 'sum'); // the rows the filter lets through\ndatasource.aggregate('age', 'avg'); // null when no row has an age\n\n// a group's rows: the rows of its sub groups and of collapsed groups included\nfor (const row of datasource.getRows()) {\n    if (isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log(row.__groupName, datasource.aggregate('balance', 'sum', row));\n    }\n}\n\n// rows of your own, the selected ones say - formatted as the field shows its values\nconst sum = datasource.aggregate('balance', 'sum', datasource.getSelectedRows());\ndatasource.getFormatter('balance').format(sum);</code></pre>\n<p>The value of the filtered rows and of a group row is kept until a value of its field changes or\nother rows are filtered, so the grid can ask on every rebuild and every render of a group row: an\nedit of another field changes nothing. Computing it again reads the field of every row: measured in\nChrome, 4ms for 100 000 rows, about 18ms once they are grouped. A list of rows you give is computed\non each call.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-theming-and-screen-readers\">Theming and screen readers</h2>\n<p>The totals row has colours of its own, <code>--simple-html-grid-totals-bg-color</code> and\n<code>--simple-html-grid-totals-font-color</code>; the label in front of a value is\n<code>--simple-html-grid-aggregate-label-color</code>. The group rows keep their colours. See\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/the-variables-of-the-grid\">Theming</a>.</p>\n<p>For a screen reader the aggregates of a field are one cell of its column, named with all its lines\n(<code>Sum 12 500, Avg 1 250</code>) and read with the column&#39;s header; the group&#39;s name is the cell of the first\ncolumn. The totals row is the last row of the grid with &quot;Total&quot; as its row header. See\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility\">Accessibility</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"find","title":"Find","html":"<p>Ctrl+F (Cmd+F on a Mac) with the focus in the grid opens a find bar over the grid&#39;s top right corner:\ntype a text, and every cell that shows it is marked - the current match more - with the count beside\nit, <code>3 of 17</code>. Enter goes to the next match, Shift+Enter to the previous one, and the grid scrolls to\nit. Esc closes the bar and puts the focus in the cell of the current match. The button <code>Aa</code>, <em>Match\ncase</em>, finds the text as it is typed (see <a href=\"#guide/find/match-case\">below</a>); the funnel button, <em>Filter rows</em>, shows\nonly the rows with a match (see <a href=\"#guide/find/filter-rows\">below</a>). A browser&#39;s find does not work in a grid:\nonly the rows on screen are in the page, and the text is in inputs.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"find\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keys\">The keys</h2>\n<p>With the focus anywhere in the grid - a cell, a header filter, a column header:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Ctrl+F, Cmd+F</td>\n<td>opens the find bar, the focus in its input with the text it had selected. Again while it is open: the focus back in its input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F3 / Shift+F3</td>\n<td>while the find bar is open: the next / previous match</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Outside the grid Ctrl+F is the browser&#39;s own: the page around it, and the grid&#39;s menus, the editor of a\nmultiline cell and the filter editor, which are on the body. Ctrl+Shift+F and Ctrl+Alt+F are left alone.\nIt is the key that types f; on a keyboard layout without Latin letters (Cyrillic, Greek) the key where\nF is on a US keyboard.</p>\n<p>In the find bar:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>typing</td>\n<td>finds the text, 150ms after the last key: the cells are marked, the grid scrolls to the current match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter, F3</td>\n<td>the next match. After the last one, the first. Right after typing, before the matches showed: the first match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+Enter, Shift+F3</td>\n<td>the previous match. Before the first one, the last</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes the bar. The focus goes to the cell of the current match, which is the active cell then and its row the current row - as moving to it with the keys makes it. Without a match, back to the cell (or the header filter) Ctrl+F was pressed in. <em>Filter rows</em> is turned off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+C</td>\n<td><em>Match case</em> on or off, the focus stays where it is - as in the find bars of Firefox and VS Code. Not on a Mac: Option+C types <code>ç</code> there</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab, Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the buttons: <em>Match case</em> (<code>Aa</code>), <em>Filter rows</em> (the funnel), previous (↑), next (↓), close (✕). Shift+Tab from the input leaves the grid, Tab from the close button goes on to the header filters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Space, Enter</td>\n<td>on <em>Match case</em> or <em>Filter rows</em>: turns it on or off</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The focus stays in the input while you go from match to match, as in a browser: a click on ↑ or ↓\n(or <code>Aa</code>, the funnel) keeps it there too. <em>Filter rows</em> has no key of its own: Tab from the input,\nSpace, Shift+Tab back. Alt+R would type <code>®</code> on a Mac (Option+R), and is the key of regular expressions in\nother find bars (VS Code). On a Mac <em>Match case</em> is Tab and Space too, its tooltip leaves out Alt+C. The\nwheel scrolls the grid and leaves the focus in the bar. A click in a cell puts the focus there and leaves\nthe bar open; Ctrl+F gets back to it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-is-found\">What is found</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The text a cell shows</strong>: its value through the field&#39;s formatter - a date as <code>2026-03-14</code> (or\n<code>14.03.2026</code> with that formatter), <code>12,5</code> with a decimal comma, a <code>0</code> shown blank (<code>numberOverride</code>)\nnot as <code>0</code>. The text contains what you typed, in any case: <code>acme</code> finds <code>ACME CORP</code> - with <em>Match\ncase</em> as it is typed.</li>\n<li><strong>The text as it is typed</strong>: <code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>?</code> are characters, no wildcards - <code>50%</code> finds\n<code>50% off</code>, not <code>500</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Every line</strong> of a cell of several lines, also those it does not show.</li>\n<li><strong>The displayed rows</strong>: after the filter, in the sort order. When grouped, the rows of the expanded\ngroups; the rows of a collapsed group are not displayed, they are not found. The group rows (their\nnames, <code>Country: Norway (12)</code>) are not searched.</li>\n<li><strong>The fields of the layout</strong>: a field not in it (hidden) is not searched. Not the row numbers, and not\na checkbox - a boolean cell shows no text.</li>\n<li><strong>The order</strong> of the matches, for next and previous: the rows top to bottom, and in a row the fields\nin the order of <code>gridInterface.getLayoutFields()</code> - pinned left, center, pinned right, the stacked\nfields of a column top to bottom.</li>\n<li><strong>Where it starts</strong>: at the cell that had the focus when the bar opened, the first match at or after\nit - else the first match. While you type, the current match stays while it still matches (<code>acm</code>,\n<code>acme</code>), else it is the next one after it.</li>\n<li>An empty text finds nothing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-match-case\">Match case</h2>\n<p>The button <code>Aa</code> in the bar, <em>Match case</em>, is a toggle (<code>aria-pressed</code>), filled in the accent colour while\nit is on - as <em>Filter rows</em>. On, the text is found as it is typed: <code>Acme</code> finds <code>Acme 2</code>, not <code>ACME CORP</code> or <code>acme</code>. Off (the default), in any case.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everything follows it</strong>: the matches, the count, the marked cells, the current match (it stays while\nit still matches, else the next one) - and <em>Filter rows</em>, which filters the rows again with it. The\ngrid scrolls to the current match, as for typing.</li>\n<li><strong>Alt+C</strong> in the bar turns it on and off, Space or Enter on the button; a click keeps the focus in the\ninput.</li>\n<li><strong>It is kept</strong> when the bar closes, as the text: Ctrl+F opens the bar with it as it was. It is not\npart of the grid&#39;s state.</li>\n<li><strong>The chip</strong> of <em>Filter rows</em> in the footer has the marker <code>Aa</code> (the text <code>footer.matchCase</code>) while it\nmatches case, as a condition of the filter that matches case.</li>\n<li>Case is the case of JavaScript (<code>toLowerCase()</code>): <code>Ø</code> and <code>ø</code> are one letter in any case.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.find('Acme', { caseSensitive: true }); // Acme as it is, not ACME\ngridInterface.getFind().caseSensitive; // true\ngridInterface.find('acme', { caseSensitive: false }); // in any case again</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-the-matches-look\">How the matches look</h2>\n<p>Every match has a tint, the current one a stronger tint and a 2px line inside its edge. The colours are\n<code>--simple-html-grid-find-match-color</code>, <code>--simple-html-grid-find-current-color</code> and\n<code>--simple-html-grid-find-current-border-color</code>, light and dark, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/find\">Find</a>. The cells\nhave the classes <code>simple-html-cell-find-match</code> and <code>simple-html-cell-find-current</code> (the current match has\nboth); the cells are reused while the grid scrolls, the classes follow the rows and fields they show. A\nmatch in a range of cells shows both tints.</p>\n<p>The find bar is an element of the grid, <code>simple-html-grid-find</code>, the first one in it: over the top right\nof the grouping panel - or of the column labels, without a panel (<code>panelHeight: 0</code>). It gets narrower\nwith a narrow grid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-rows\">Filter rows</h2>\n<p>The funnel button in the bar, <em>Filter rows</em>, is a toggle: on, only the rows with at least one match are\nshown; off, every row the filter lets through again. The matches, the count and the current match work\nas before, in the rows shown - the current match stays the current one, the grid scrolls to it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The same matches</strong>: a row is shown when a cell of it is marked - the text as the cells show it\n(formatted, <code>numberOverride</code>, the label of a lookup, every line of a multiline cell; not a checkbox),\nthe fields of the layout. The rows shown and the marked cells agree.</li>\n<li><strong>On top of the filter</strong> (AND), as <em>Changed rows only</em> is - and after it: not part of the filter.\n<code>datasource.getFilter()</code>, <code>getFilterString()</code>, the filter editor and the grid&#39;s state\n(<code>getState()</code>) do not have it. The footer shows a chip for it, <code>Find: denma</code> (the text\n<code>footer.findFilter</code>), after the one of <em>Changed rows only</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>When the rows are filtered</strong>: when the text changes - 150ms after the last key, as the search -\nwhen the columns change (a column hidden or shown), and whenever the datasource filters the rows: a\nfilter, a header filter, <code>setData()</code>, <code>upsert()</code>, <code>reloadDatasource()</code> ... A sort or a grouping keeps\nthe rows, they only move.</li>\n<li><strong>Not when a value is edited</strong>, as for every filter here: a row edited so it no longer shows the text\nstays, without a mark, until the rows are filtered next. A row does not disappear while you type in it.</li>\n<li><strong>As any filter</strong>: the groups keep the rows with a match (their counts too, the rows of a collapsed\ngroup as well), the aggregates and the footer&#39;s count are of the rows shown, the ranges of cells are\ncleared, the current row and the selection are left as a filter leaves them.</li>\n<li><strong>An empty text</strong> shows every row, the toggle stays on: the rows are filtered once you type.</li>\n<li><strong>It is turned off</strong> when the bar closes - Esc, ✕, <code>closeFind()</code>, the grid taken off the page - and by\nthe footer&#39;s &quot;✕ Clear&quot; (and the filter menu&#39;s Clear All Filters), which leave the bar open with its\nmatches. Ctrl+F opens the bar with it off.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.find('denma', { filterRows: true }); // only the rows with a cell that shows &quot;denma&quot;\ngridInterface.getFind().filterRows; // true\ngridInterface.find('norway'); // another text: it stays on\ngridInterface.find('norway', { filterRows: false }); // every row, the bar stays open</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-rows-and-a-backend\">Filter rows and a backend</h2>\n<p><em>Filter rows</em> is a filter of the browser only: it tests the text the cells show, which a database does\nnot have. It is not part of the filter, so <code>datasource.getFilter()</code> and <code>gridInterface.getServerFilter()</code>\ndo not have it, and no SQL is made of it. When a backend loads the rows, it loads them by the filter;\n<em>Filter rows</em> then filters what came back - <code>setData()</code> with the new rows filters them again with it, as\nlong as it is on.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { FilterExpression } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare function loadRows(filter: FilterExpression | null): Promise&lt;Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;[]&gt;; // your backend\n\ngridInterface.find('acme', { filterRows: true });\ndatasource.setData(await loadRows(gridInterface.getServerFilter())); // only the rows with &quot;acme&quot; shown</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-it-follows-the-data\">It follows the data</h2>\n<p>While the bar is open the text is found again when what the grid shows changes: rows set, added or\nremoved, a filter, a sort, grouping, a group expanded or collapsed, a value edited (in the grid, from\ncode, by undo, by an upsert), a column moved, hidden or pinned, the texts of the grid. The current match\nstays the current one while it still matches - also when a sort moved it - else it is the next one\nafter it. The grid does not scroll for it: you may be typing in a cell somewhere. It waits until the\nchanges stop, 150ms, as for the typing.</p>\n<p>Closed, the bar finds nothing and <em>Filter rows</em> is off. Its text is kept: Ctrl+F shows it again,\nselected, and finds it. The bar is closed when the grid is taken off the page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-api\">The API</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { FindResult } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// as typing in the find bar: opens it with the text, marks the cells, the current match scrolled to\nconst count = gridInterface.find('acme'); // how many cells show it\n\ngridInterface.findNext(); // { row: 5, field: 'company' }, the cell of the current match\ngridInterface.findPrevious();\n\nconst result: FindResult = gridInterface.getFind();\n// { open: true, text: 'acme', filterRows: false, count: 17, position: 3, current: { row: 5, field: 'company' } }\n\n// only the rows with a match, as the funnel button of the bar; the text as it is, as Aa\ngridInterface.find('acme', { filterRows: true });\ngridInterface.find('Acme', { caseSensitive: true });\n\ngridInterface.on('find-change', ({ open, count, position }) =&gt; {\n    console.log(open ? `${position} of ${count}` : 'find closed');\n});\n\n// as Ctrl+F: the focus into the bar - for a button or a menu entry of your own\ngridInterface.openFind();\ngridInterface.closeFind();</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>find(text)</code></strong> opens the bar with the text and returns how many cells show it. The focus does not\nmove. The current match is the first one at or after the cell with the focus; while the bar is open,\nthe current match stays while it still matches. A text that is not a string throws a <code>TypeError</code>.\n<strong><code>find(text, { filterRows })</code></strong> turns <em>Filter rows</em> on or off as well; left out, it stays as it is.</li>\n<li><strong><code>openFind()</code></strong> does what Ctrl+F does: the bar opens, the focus goes into its input.</li>\n<li><strong><code>findNext()</code></strong> and <strong><code>findPrevious()</code></strong> move the current match, as Enter and Shift+Enter, and\nreturn its cell: <code>{ row, field }</code>, <code>row</code> an index in the displayed rows (<code>datasource.getRows()</code>) as in\nthe grid events. <code>null</code> while the bar is closed or nothing matches.</li>\n<li><strong><code>find(text, { caseSensitive })</code></strong> turns <em>Match case</em> on or off; left out, it stays as it is.</li>\n<li><strong><code>closeFind()</code></strong> closes it, as Esc: when the focus was in the bar it goes to the current match.\n<em>Filter rows</em> is turned off.</li>\n<li><strong><code>getFind()</code></strong> gives what it has found, see <code>FindResult</code>: <code>open</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>filterRows</code>,\n<code>caseSensitive</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>position</code> (1 to <code>count</code>, 0 without a match) and <code>current</code>. Plain JSON.</li>\n<li><strong><code>find-change</code></strong> is sent with the same each time it changes: the bar opened or closed, the typing\nstopped, the current match moved, <em>Filter rows</em> or <em>Match case</em> turned on or off, the matches were\nfound again after the data changed. Not when nothing changed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without the grid on the page they do nothing: <code>find()</code> returns 0, <code>getFind()</code> gives a closed find. The matches, <em>Match case</em> and <em>Filter rows</em> are not part of <code>getState()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-screen-reader-gets\">What a screen reader gets</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The find bar is a search region (<code>role=&quot;search&quot;</code>) named &quot;Find in the grid&quot; (<code>find.label</code>); its input is\nnamed &quot;Find&quot; (<code>find.placeholder</code>).</li>\n<li>The count is a status (<code>role=&quot;status&quot;</code>): a screen reader reads it out when it changes - after the\ntyping stopped, at each next and previous: <code>3 of 17</code>, <code>No matches</code>.</li>\n<li>The buttons are buttons, named &quot;Previous match&quot;, &quot;Next match&quot; and &quot;Close&quot;, with the key in their\ntooltip. <em>Match case</em> is a toggle button named &quot;Match case&quot; (<code>find.matchCase</code>) - it shows <code>Aa</code> - and\n<em>Filter rows</em> one named &quot;Filter rows&quot; (<code>find.filterRows</code>), <code>aria-pressed</code> true while they are on; the\nfunnel is hidden from a screen reader. A change of the count is read out after <em>Match case</em> too. While it filters, the footer&#39;s row\ncount (<code>3/500</code>, a status) is read out when it changes.</li>\n<li>The texts are <code>find.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/find\">Texts</a>. The keys are in the table of\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-find-bar\">Keyboard and accessibility</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-known-limits\">Known limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+F needs the focus in the grid.</strong> The wheel takes the focus from the cell (its row element is\nreused for other rows): Ctrl+F then is the browser&#39;s. Click a cell first, or call <code>openFind()</code> from a\nbutton.</li>\n<li><strong>The current match is not read out</strong>, only the count: the focus stays in the input. Esc puts the\nfocus in its cell, which is read then.</li>\n<li><strong>Contains</strong>, in any case or with <em>Match case</em>: no whole word or regular expression. Not the group\nrows, the rows of collapsed groups, the hidden fields, the checkboxes.</li>\n<li><strong>No Cmd+G / Ctrl+G</strong> for the next match: F3, Enter in the bar, <code>findNext()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A large grid</strong>: each search makes the text of every cell, 100 000 rows of 10 fields are a million\ntexts - some hundred ms. It runs once the typing (or the changes of the data) stop. <em>Filter rows</em>\nmakes the texts once more when it filters the rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Filter rows and an edit</strong>: a row edited so it no longer shows the text stays until the rows are\nfiltered again (another text, a filter ...), as for every filter.</li>\n<li><strong>One datasource in two grids</strong>: <em>Filter rows</em> filters the datasource, so both grids show the rows of\nthe one whose find bar has it on; the chip is in the footer of that one only.</li>\n<li><strong>Checked in Chrome</strong> (the keys, the bar, the look in light and dark). Firefox, Safari and screen\nreaders have not been tried.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"lookups","title":"Lookups: select and dropdown grid","html":"<p>A column whose value is picked from a list: a country stored as <code>NO</code> and shown as <code>Norway</code>, a customer\nstored as its id and shown as its name. Register the list once as a <strong>lookup</strong>, with an id, and name\nit in the column&#39;s <code>editor</code> - the config stays plain JSON. The cell shows the label, and a button at its\nright opens the list: a <strong>select</strong> (a search field over the options) or a <strong>dropdown grid</strong> (a small\ngrid of the rows, with a filter row). Picking writes the value, and other fields of the picked row if\nyou like, as one step of the undo history.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"lookups\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-registering-a-lookup\">Registering a lookup</h2>\n<p><code>registerLookup(id, lookup)</code> when the page loads, before the grid that names it is made. A lookup is\nthe rows to pick from, the field stored in the cell (<code>valueField</code>) and the field shown\n(<code>labelField</code>, default <code>valueField</code>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, registerLookup } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst countries = new Datasource();\ncountries.setData([\n    { code: 'NO', name: 'Norway' },\n    { code: 'SE', name: 'Sweden' },\n    { code: 'DK', name: 'Denmark' }\n]);\nregisterLookup('countries', { datasource: countries, valueField: 'code', labelField: 'name' });\n\nconst customers = new Datasource({ fields: { id: 'number' } });\ncustomers.setData([{ id: 1, name: 'ACME CORP', city: 'Oslo' }]);\nregisterLookup('customers', {\n    datasource: customers,\n    valueField: 'id',\n    labelField: 'name',\n    // the dropdown grid: its columns and options, plain JSON\n    config: {\n        layout: {\n            center: [\n                { fields: ['id'], width: 60 },\n                { fields: ['name'], width: 190 },\n                { fields: ['city'], width: 110 }\n            ]\n        }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A grid looks its lookups up when it is made</strong>, and when <code>updateConfig()</code> is given <code>columns</code>: an id\nthat is not registered throws a <code>TypeError</code> then, naming the ids that are. It keeps the lookups it\nfound: registering an id again changes the grids made after. There is one registry for the page.</li>\n<li><strong>The lookup follows its datasource</strong>: rows set, added, removed or changed show in the cells right\naway. The rows can come from a server after the grid is shown - the cells show the values until they\ndo.</li>\n<li><strong>A value is found by <code>===</code></strong>: the number <code>17</code> is not the text <code>&#39;17&#39;</code>. A lookup column is a <code>text</code>\nor <code>number</code> field; the first row with the value wins.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-grid-interface-of-your-own\">A grid interface of your own</h3>\n<p>The dropdown grid can be a <code>GridInterface</code> you made, with its own config and hooks (a <code>cellClass</code> that\ngreys out blocked customers, say): link it instead of a datasource and a config.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, GridInterface, registerLookup } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst customers = new Datasource({ fields: { id: 'number' } });\nconst picker = new GridInterface(\n    {\n        columns: { name: { operator: 'CONTAINS' } },\n        layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 200 }] },\n        panelHeight: 0\n    },\n    customers,\n    { cellClass: (row) =&gt; (row.blocked ? { input: 'blocked' } : null) }\n);\nregisterLookup('customers', { gridInterface: picker, valueField: 'id', labelField: 'name' });</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Show it in the dropdown only.</strong> An interface shows one grid at a time: while the dropdown is open,\nthe interface is connected to the dropdown&#39;s grid element, and when it closes it is disconnected\nagain. One that is shown in a grid on the page when the dropdown opens throws an <code>Error</code> - taking it\nwould leave that grid without its datasource. Each time the dropdown opens, the interface goes back to\nthe state it started in (<code>resetState()</code>): the filter typed the last time is gone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-column-option\">The column option</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    columns: {\n        country: { label: 'Country', editor: { type: 'select', lookup: 'countries' } },\n        customerId: {\n            label: 'Customer',\n            editor: { type: 'grid', lookup: 'customers', set: { customerName: 'name', customerCity: 'city' } }\n        },\n        customerName: { readonly: true }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['country'], width: 120 },\n            { fields: ['customerId'], width: 180 },\n            { fields: ['customerName'], width: 180 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>type</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;select&#39;</code>: a list with a search field. <code>&#39;grid&#39;</code>: a dropdown grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lookup</code></td>\n<td>the id of a registered lookup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>set</code></td>\n<td>optional, more fields to copy from the picked row: <code>{ &lt;field of the row&gt;: &lt;field of the picked row&gt; }</code>. <code>{ customerName: &#39;name&#39; }</code> gives the row&#39;s <code>customerName</code> the picked customer&#39;s <code>name</code>. Emptying the cell empties them too. They are written as data, also where those cells are readonly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>What is wrong throws a <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made (or <code>updateConfig()</code> is given the column),\nnaming the column: an editor that is no object, a <code>type</code> that is no <code>&#39;select&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;grid&#39;</code>, a lookup\nthat is not registered, a <code>set</code> that is no object of field names or sets the column&#39;s own field, a\nfield that is no <code>text</code> or <code>number</code>, and a <code>focusButton</code> or <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code> next to it - the\neditor has its own button, the column one type.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-cell\">The cell</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It shows the label</strong> of the row whose value it has, as the lookup&#39;s datasource formats the label\nfield. A value no row has is shown as it is (the field&#39;s formatter), a blank as nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not typed in</strong>: the input is readonly. A character typed opens the list with the character as\nits search - the way a multiline cell opens its editor.</li>\n<li><strong>The button</strong> at its right opens the list: a small triangle, shown while the pointer is over the\ncell and while the cell has the focus. A click on it again closes the list.</li>\n<li><strong>In a readonly grid or cell</strong> (<code>readonly</code>, the column&#39;s <code>readonly</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> hook, a row the\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">edit permissions</a> lock) it shows the label only: no\nbutton, no list, the keys are those of any cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keys\">The keys</h2>\n<p>In a lookup cell that can be edited:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowDown, F4</td>\n<td>opens the list, as in Excel and Windows. Not Alt+ArrowDown&#39;s &quot;the cell below&quot; here: Enter or Tab move on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F2, Space, a double click</td>\n<td>opens the list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a character</td>\n<td>opens the list with the character as its search</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete, Backspace</td>\n<td>empties the cell, and the fields of <code>set</code>: one step of the undo history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>the cell below, as in any cell that can be edited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Z</td>\n<td>undoes the last pick</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>In the select, the focus is in the search field:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>typing</td>\n<td>finds the options whose label has the text, ignoring case. The first one found is the active one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowDown / ArrowUp</td>\n<td>the next / previous option</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PageDown / PageUp</td>\n<td>ten options down / up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Home / End</td>\n<td>the first / last option. The caret of the search moves with ArrowLeft / ArrowRight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>picks the active option. With no option, it closes the list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>picks the active option and goes on to the next / previous cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes the list, the value as it was</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A click on an option picks it; the pointer over an option makes it the active one. The list opens with\nthe cell&#39;s value active and marked with a check.</p>\n<p>In the dropdown grid, the focus is in the filter of the label&#39;s column:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>typing</td>\n<td>filters the rows as you type, with the filter&#39;s operator. The first row is the highlighted one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter in a filter</td>\n<td>picks the highlighted row: the cell&#39;s value when it opened, the first row after a search</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowDown in a filter</td>\n<td>goes to the highlighted row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ArrowDown / ArrowUp in the rows</td>\n<td>the row below / above. Up from the first row: the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter in a row</td>\n<td>picks the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab</td>\n<td>the grid&#39;s own: the next filter or cell of the dropdown grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes it, the value as it was</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A click on a row picks it.</p>\n<p>The list closes, the value as it was, on a click outside it, when the grid scrolls, when a menu opens\nand when the grid leaves the page. One list is open at a time. After a pick, Enter and Esc, the focus is\nback in the cell.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-select\">The select</h2>\n<p>A list under the cell, at least as wide as the cell, kept in the window: above the cell when there is\nno room under it, moved in from the right edge. It has the options of the rows the lookup&#39;s datasource\ndisplays - its filter and sort decide which and in what order; the labels of the cells are found in all\nof its rows.</p>\n<p><strong>At most 500 options are shown</strong>, with a line under them: <code>500 of 10000 shown, type to search</code> - the\nsearch finds the rest. Rendering is what costs: in Chrome, a list of 10 000 options took 190ms to open\nand 50ms for each arrow key, cut at 500 it is 14ms and 11ms (a search 10-15ms).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-dropdown-grid\">The dropdown grid</h2>\n<p>A grid of the lookup under the cell, kept in the window like the select. Made from the lookup&#39;s\ndatasource and <code>config</code> - each time it opens, and done with when it closes - or the lookup&#39;s own grid\ninterface, shown as it is.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The config</strong>: without one, a column of the label, or of the value and the label when they differ.\nWhat the config leaves out is set for a picker: no grouping panel (<code>panelHeight: 0</code>), no footer\n(<code>footerHeight: 0</code>), no column numbers (<code>selectSizeHeight: 0</code>), no row state (<code>rowStatus: false</code>), no\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-operator-in-the-cell\">operator indicators</a> in its filters (<code>filterOperatorIndicator: false</code>),\nand a text column without an <code>operator</code> searches anywhere in the text (<code>CONTAINS</code>) - a number column\nkeeps <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, it has no <code>CONTAINS</code>. It is readonly, as every grid by default.</li>\n<li><strong>Its size</strong> follows its columns, and it is nine rows high (fewer when the lookup has fewer rows, at\nleast three) with its header.</li>\n<li><strong>It filters and sorts the datasource</strong>: a grid applies its state to its datasource, its config&#39;s\n<code>initialState</code> or none. Give a lookup a datasource of its own, or one that shares the rows of another\nthrough a <code>DataContainer</code>, so a grid on the page that shows the same datasource keeps its filter.</li>\n<li><strong>The row of the cell&#39;s value is selected</strong> and scrolled to when it opens; after a search the first\nrow found is.</li>\n<li>It has no right click menus: they are left out in a picker.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-picking\">Picking</h2>\n<p>A pick writes the value of the picked row to the cell, and the fields of <code>set</code> from it, in one run of\ncode: <strong>one step of the undo history</strong>, Ctrl+Z in the cell takes all of it back. Picking the value the\ncell has changes nothing, there is no step. The fields are validated as an edit, the row is edited\n(<code>__rowState</code>), the edited marker shows the label of the original value.</p>\n<p>While the list is open the cell counts as being edited: an <code>upsert()</code> from a server does not overwrite\nit, a value for it is a conflict - see <a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-value-and-label\">Value and label</h2>\n<p>What the user sees is the label; the rows hold the value. The grid uses the label where the user\nworks with what the cell shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sorting</strong> the column sorts on the labels: <code>Denmark, Norway, Sweden</code>, not <code>DK, NO, SE</code>. Two values\nwith the same label are sorted on the value after it. The grid gives the datasource the labels to sort\non (<code>datasource.sort()</code> from code too); <code>destroy()</code> takes them back.</li>\n<li><strong>The header filter</strong> is a text filter on the labels, also for a number field: the text typed finds\nthe lookup rows whose label matches with the column&#39;s operator (<code>EQUAL</code> by default, the whole label\nignoring case; set <code>operator: &#39;CONTAINS&#39;</code> to search in it), and the rows are filtered on their values<ul>\n<li>the condition is <code>IN</code> the values found. A value no lookup row has does not match a typed text. The\nfilter menu offers the operators of a text and its Match case: on, the label is matched with its case\nand the <code>IN</code> of the values found has <code>caseSensitive: true</code>. Its Is blank / Is not blank are on the\ncell&#39;s value. In the filter editor a lookup column&#39;s condition is on the values: the operators of the\nfield&#39;s type (a lookup on a number field: the number operators, not the column&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The value list of the filter menu</strong> lists the labels (<code>Denmark</code>, <code>Norway</code>, <code>Sweden</code>), in their\norder, and its search searches them - also on a number field, which has no value list without a\nlookup. Two values with one label are one entry. Apply filters on the values behind the checked\nlabels, as the header filter does: <code>IN [&#39;NO&#39;]</code>, or <code>NOT_IN</code> the unchecked ones. A value no lookup\nrow has is listed as the cell shows it (<code>XX</code>). See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-value-list\">the value list</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The footer&#39;s filter chip</strong> shows the labels of an <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code> of values (<code>Country in Norway</code>)\nand of a condition&#39;s value (the grid gives <code>getFilterTree()</code> the column&#39;s formatter).</li>\n<li><strong>The CSV and Excel export</strong> write the label, as text. <code>formatted: false</code> writes the value.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy</strong> gives the labels. <strong>Paste</strong> takes a label (ignoring case) and writes its value, and the\nfields of <code>set</code>; a text no label has is written as it is. <strong>Clear</strong> (the row menu, Delete in a range)\nempties the fields of <code>set</code> too, and so does <strong>fill down</strong> copy them with the value.</li>\n<li><strong>Find</strong> (Ctrl+F) and fitting the column widths read the labels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Where the value shows, and not the label:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grouped</strong> on a lookup column, the rows are grouped on the value and the group row shows it\n(<code>Country: NO (12)</code>). The groups are in the order of the labels.</li>\n<li><strong>The filter</strong> the header filter and the value list make is an <code>IN</code> (or <code>NOT_IN</code>) of values: the\nfilter editor - where they can be edited - <code>getFilterString()</code> and the state have the values.</li>\n<li><strong>The row menu&#39;s &quot;Filter: Replace / Add to filter&quot;</strong> filters on the cell&#39;s value (<code>EQUAL NO</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-doing-it-yourself\">Doing it yourself</h2>\n<p>For a picker of your own - a dialog, a tree, a search on a server - give the column a <code>focusButton</code>\nand open it from the <code>cell-focus-button-click</code> event, as a block of <code>openMenu()</code>: see\n<a href=\"#guide/menus/a-dropdown-grid-as-a-block\">A dropdown grid as a block</a>. The button is a <code>...</code>, Space in the\ncell clicks it, and writing the value back is yours: one write in one run of code is one step of the\nundo history. <code>allowPasteClearOnly: true</code> keeps the cell from being typed in. A column has either a\n<code>focusButton</code> or an <code>editor</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-for-a-screen-reader\">For a screen reader</h2>\n<p>The cell&#39;s input has <code>aria-haspopup</code> - <code>listbox</code> for the select, <code>grid</code> for the dropdown grid - and the\nbutton is named &quot;Open the list&quot; (the text <code>lookup.open</code>); it is no stop of Tab. The select is the\nWAI-ARIA combobox pattern: its search field is a <code>combobox</code> (<code>aria-expanded</code>, <code>aria-controls</code>, named with\nthe column&#39;s label) with the list as its <code>listbox</code>, and the focus stays in the search field - the active\noption is its <code>aria-activedescendant</code>, and the one with <code>aria-selected=&quot;true&quot;</code>. &quot;No matches&quot; is a\n<code>status</code>. The dropdown grid is a grid, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility\">Keyboard and accessibility</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-texts\">Texts</h2>\n<p><code>lookup.open</code>, <code>lookup.search</code> (the placeholder of the search), <code>lookup.noMatch</code> and <code>lookup.more</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/lookup-columns\">Texts</a>. The dropdown grid has the texts of a grid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-theming\">Theming</h2>\n<p>The button is <code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-button-bg-color</code> with its triangle in\n<code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-icon-color</code> (at least 3:1 to it), the pointer over it\n<code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-even</code>. The active option of the select is\n<code>--simple-html-grid-lookup-active-bg-color</code>; the rest are the colours of the menus. See\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/lookup-columns\">Theming</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"custom-cells","title":"Custom cells","html":"<p>A column can draw its cells with code of your own: a badge, a progress bar, a link, an icon, a select\nthat edits the value - written with plain DOM, lit-html, React, Solid or anything else that renders\ninto an element. Register the code once as a <strong>cell renderer</strong>, with an id, and name the id in the\ncolumn&#39;s <code>renderer</code>: the config stays plain JSON. The cell keeps everything else a cell of the grid\ndoes - the keys, the focus, copy and paste, ranges, Find, errors and the edited marker.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"custom-cells\"></div>\n<p>A link needs no renderer: <code>format: { link: { url: &#39;https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}&#39; } }</code> on a text\ncolumn draws its cells as links - a custom cell of the grid&#39;s own, with these keys and the focus, its\naddress checked. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">Templates and links</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-renderer\">A renderer</h2>\n<p><code>registerCellRenderer(id, renderer)</code> when the page loads, before the grid that names it is made. A\nrenderer is a function: the grid calls it with an element to render into, the <strong>host</strong>, and it returns\nan object with an <code>update()</code> the grid calls every time the cell shows a row.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, registerCellRenderer } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nregisterCellRenderer('badge', (host) =&gt; {\n    // made once per cell element\n    const span = document.createElement('span');\n    host.appendChild(span);\n    return {\n        // every time the cell is rendered: change what differs\n        update({ value, text }) {\n            span.textContent = text;\n            span.className = `badge badge-${String(value)}`;\n        }\n    };\n});\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: { status: { renderer: 'badge' } },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['status'], width: 100 }] }\n};\ncreateGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Once per cell element, not per row.</strong> The grid shows a few more cell elements than fit on screen\nand reuses them while it scrolls: a cell element shows another row on every scroll step. The renderer\nis called once for each cell element that shows the column, <code>update()</code> every time the cell is\nrendered - another row scrolled in, a value changed, <code>refresh()</code>, a sort or a filter. With 10 000\nrows there are as many renderers as cell elements of the column, about the rows on screen. Make the\nelements in the renderer, change them in <code>update()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>destroy()</code></strong>, when the instance has one, is called once when the cell element is done with it: it\nshows a column without the renderer now (scrolled sideways), the column lost its <code>renderer</code>\n(<code>updateConfig()</code>), the cells are made again (a resize, <code>cellHeight</code>, a new layout, grouping), the grid\nis taken off the page or <code>gridInterface.destroy()</code> is called. Undo there what lives outside the host -\na framework&#39;s root, a subscription, a timer: else it keeps the grid alive. The grid empties the host\nafter it.</li>\n<li><strong>An id that is not registered throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made, or when <code>updateConfig()</code>\nis given the column - naming the ids that are. So does a renderer that is not a function, and an\ninstance without <code>update()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Registering an id again replaces the renderer</strong> for the cells that mount it after; the cells that\nshow it keep theirs until they are done with it. There is one registry for the page.</li>\n<li>Two columns with the same renderer share the instances: a cell element that scrolls from one to the\nother keeps its instance, <code>update()</code> gets the other field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-update-gets\">What update() gets</h2>\n<p>A new <code>CellRenderContext</code> every call:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>row</code></td>\n<td>the row: always a data row, a group row has no cells. Writing to it is an edit, see <a href=\"#guide/custom-cells/a-cell-that-edits-its-value\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>field</code></td>\n<td>the cell&#39;s field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>row[field]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>text</code></td>\n<td>the text the default cell would show: the value through the field&#39;s formatter, with <code>numberOverride</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>type</code></td>\n<td>the cell&#39;s type: the field&#39;s, or the one <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code> gives this row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>readonly</code></td>\n<td>the cell can not be edited: the grid is readonly, the <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">edit permissions</a> lock the row, the column is, or the <code>isReadonly</code> hook says so</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowIndex</code></td>\n<td>the row&#39;s index in the displayed rows (<code>datasource.getRows()</code>), as in the grid&#39;s events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gridInterface</code></td>\n<td>the grid: the registry is for the page, so this is how a renderer knows its grid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>update()</code> is called on every render, also when nothing changed: compare and skip if your drawing is\nexpensive. A renderer may read other fields of the row.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-lit-html\">lit-html</h2>\n<p>The grid renders with lit-html, so it is installed already. <code>render()</code> into the host on every\n<code>update()</code>: lit-html keeps the elements and changes only what differs.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { registerCellRenderer } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport { html, render } from 'lit-html';\n\nregisterCellRenderer('progress', (host) =&gt; ({\n    update({ value }) {\n        const percent = Math.min(Math.max(Number(value) || 0, 0), 100);\n        render(html`&lt;span class=&quot;bar&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=${`width:${percent}%`}&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; ${percent}%`, host);\n    }\n}));</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-react\">React</h2>\n<p>A root per cell element, rendered again on every <code>update()</code>, unmounted in <code>destroy()</code> (React 18 and\n19, <code>react-dom/client</code>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><code>import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';\nimport { registerCellRenderer } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nfunction Owner({ name, readonly }: { name: string; readonly: boolean }) {\n    return &lt;span className={readonly ? 'owner readonly' : 'owner'}&gt;{name}&lt;/span&gt;;\n}\n\nregisterCellRenderer('owner', (host) =&gt; {\n    const root = createRoot(host);\n    return {\n        update: (context) =&gt; root.render(&lt;Owner name={context.text} readonly={context.readonly} /&gt;),\n        destroy: () =&gt; root.unmount()\n    };\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Pass the values the component shows as props. The row is the same object while its values change -\nthe datasource changes it in place - so a component memoized on <code>row</code> would not see an edit.</li>\n<li><code>root.render()</code> is not synchronous: React writes the DOM a moment later. That is fine, the grid never\nreads what the renderer drew.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-solid\">Solid</h2>\n<p>Solid 2.0 (<code>render</code> from <code>@solidjs/web</code>, <code>createSignal</code> and <code>Show</code> from <code>solid-js</code> - checked against\n<a href=\"https://docs.solidjs.com/\">docs.solidjs.com</a>, which covers 2.0). The context goes into a signal, the\ncomponent reads it: Solid updates only what depends on what changed.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><code>import { createSignal, Show } from 'solid-js';\nimport { render } from '@solidjs/web';\nimport { type CellRenderContext, registerCellRenderer } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nfunction Owner(props: { name: string }) {\n    return &lt;span class=&quot;owner&quot;&gt;{props.name}&lt;/span&gt;;\n}\n\nregisterCellRenderer('owner', (host) =&gt; {\n    const [context, setContext] = createSignal&lt;CellRenderContext&gt;();\n    // render() returns the function that disposes of the root and removes what it rendered\n    const dispose = render(() =&gt; &lt;Show when={context()}&gt;{(c) =&gt; &lt;Owner name={c().text} /&gt;}&lt;/Show&gt;, host);\n    return {\n        update: (next) =&gt; setContext(next),\n        destroy: dispose\n    };\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Solid 2 applies a write to a signal on the next microtask, so the cell shows the row a moment after\n<code>update()</code>. As for React, that is fine.</li>\n<li><code>Show</code>&#39;s function child gets an accessor (<code>c()</code>), not the value: without <code>keyed</code> the content is kept\nwhen the cell shows another row, only what reads the context runs again.</li>\n<li>Solid 1.x imports <code>render</code> from <code>solid-js/web</code> instead; the rest is the same. Neither is checked by\nthe docs build: it typechecks the <code>ts</code> examples of this guide, not these.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-cell-that-edits-its-value\">A cell that edits its value</h2>\n<p><code>context.row[context.field] = value</code> writes the row - as a write from code does: the edit is tracked\n(the row state, the edited marker), the field is validated, it is one step of the undo history, and the\ncell is rendered again in the next frame. Write only when <code>context.readonly</code> is false.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type CellRenderContext, registerCellRenderer } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nregisterCellRenderer('priority', (host) =&gt; {\n    const select = document.createElement('select');\n    // the cell is the stop of Tab: edit() below focuses the select\n    select.tabIndex = -1;\n    // a control of your own needs a name for a screen reader\n    select.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Priority');\n    for (const priority of ['Low', 'Normal', 'High']) {\n        select.add(new Option(priority, priority));\n    }\n    let context: CellRenderContext | undefined;\n    select.addEventListener('change', () =&gt; {\n        if (context &amp;&amp; !context.readonly) {\n            context.row[context.field] = select.value;\n        }\n    });\n    select.addEventListener('keydown', (event) =&gt; {\n        // its own key: Esc goes back to the cell, not to the grid (it clears the ranges there)\n        if (event.key === 'Escape') {\n            event.stopPropagation();\n            host.focus();\n        }\n    });\n    host.appendChild(select);\n    return {\n        update(next) {\n            context = next;\n            select.value = String(next.value ?? '');\n            select.disabled = next.readonly;\n        },\n        // Enter, F2, typing or a double click on the cell: the select is the editor\n        edit() {\n            select.focus();\n        }\n    };\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Keep the value in your control as <code>update()</code> gives it: undo, a paste, a write from code and a merge\nfrom the server change it too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-editing\">Editing</h2>\n<p>A cell the renderer only draws is edited by the grid, where it can be edited - the grid and the cell are\nnot readonly, the column is not <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>how</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter, F2, a double click</td>\n<td>opens the grid&#39;s editor over the cell, with the text of the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a character typed</td>\n<td>opens it with the character at the end of the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a click on a date cell</td>\n<td>opens the date picker, as the default date cell does. A date can be typed in the editor too</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The editor is an input in a popover over the cell, <code>Enter to save, Esc to cancel</code> under it (the text\n<code>cell.editor.hint</code>): Enter saves and closes it, Esc closes it without a write, Tab saves and goes on to\nthe next cell, a click outside it or scrolling saves. The text is read with the formatter&#39;s <code>parse</code>, the\nwrite is one step of the undo history; the focus goes back to the cell. A text of several lines (a\n<code>multiline</code> field, or a value with a line break) gets the editor of a multiline cell instead - Ctrl+Enter\nsaves - and that one opens read-only in a readonly cell, to read the text, as in a multiline cell. See\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>.</p>\n<p>A boolean has no editor: draw a control of your own, or let the cell be read only.</p>\n<p>A renderer that edits the value itself gives its instance an <code>edit()</code>: the grid calls it instead of\nopening its editor (and the date picker), with the character typed or <code>undefined</code>, and only where the\ncell can be edited. Focus your control there, open it, step the value.</p>\n<p>The keys and the double click are the grid&#39;s when they are on the cell itself. On what your renderer put\nin the cell - a select with the focus, an input - Enter and typing are the control&#39;s: the grid opens\nnothing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keys-and-the-focus\">The keys and the focus</h2>\n<p>The host is what gets the focus: it has the cell&#39;s <code>cellpos-</code> class and <code>tabindex=&quot;0&quot;</code>, the keys find\nit as they find the input of another cell. It has the keyboard focus ring of every control of the grid.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tab, Alt+arrows</strong>, the ranges (Alt+Shift+arrows, Esc), Delete on a range, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y\nand the row menu (Shift+F10) work as in any cell - also from what your renderer put in it, the keys\nbubble to the host.</li>\n<li><strong>The plain arrows</strong> move to the next cell too when the host has the focus: it has no caret. On your\nown control they are the control&#39;s.</li>\n<li><strong>The keys that jump</strong> - Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End, Alt+Home / Alt+End, PageUp / PageDown, Ctrl+ArrowUp /\nCtrl+ArrowDown, with Shift for a range, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keys-that-jump\">Keyboard</a> - work\non the host. On your own control they are the control&#39;s: a select has its own PageDown.</li>\n<li><strong>Space</strong> on the host does not scroll the page (a typed space opens the editor).</li>\n<li><strong>Copy</strong> (Ctrl+C) copies the cell&#39;s text - the formatter&#39;s, not what the renderer drew. <strong>Paste</strong> writes\nthe value, as into a checkbox: the host is no input.</li>\n<li>A click makes the row the current row and sends <code>cell-click</code>, a double click <code>cell-dblclick</code>; the focus moving into the cell sends\n<code>cell-row-focus</code> once - moving on from the host into your control is not a new focus of the cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stop a key your control handles itself, <code>event.stopPropagation()</code>, when the grid should not get it: a\ntext input of your own, say, where Ctrl+Z is the input&#39;s undo and the arrows move the caret.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-the-grid-shows-on-a-custom-cell\">What the grid shows on a custom cell</h2>\n<p>What the grid puts on the cell element shows on a custom cell as on any other: the red border and tooltip\nof a validation error, the edited marker, a range of cells and its fill handle, the tint of a match of\nFind, the readonly and required stripes, the <code>cellClass</code> hook&#39;s <code>background</code> class (behind the host) and\n<code>input</code> class (on the host), the flash of a merge, the colours of a <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">colour rule</a>\n(the background behind the host, the host&#39;s text in the rule&#39;s colour - what you give a colour of your\nown keeps it). A row marked for deletion strikes the host&#39;s text through. A repeated value (<code>dimRepeatedValues</code>) dims the host, your content with it.</p>\n<p>The host fills the cell: <code>position: absolute</code>, a flex row with its content in the middle of the line and\n3px of padding at the left, the grid&#39;s font and text colour, <code>overflow: hidden</code>. In a row made taller by\n<code>fitRows()</code> its content stays on the first line, as the text of the other cells. It is transparent: an\nerror or a range shows through it. Style what you put in it with your own classes; the host&#39;s own\n<code>class</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>tabindex</code> and <code>data-</code> attributes are the grid&#39;s and are set again on a render. The class\nof the host is <code>simple-html-grid-cell-custom</code>.</p>\n<p>Sort, filter, group, the aggregates, Find, the CSV and Excel export and copy use the value and the\nfield&#39;s formatter, not what the renderer drew: give the field a formatter for the text you want there.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-accessibility\">Accessibility</h2>\n<p>The cell element stays the <code>gridcell</code>, the host inside it is the focus target. The host has no\n<code>aria-label</code> - it would hide your content: what the renderer puts in it is what a screen reader reads.\nRender readable text, not only colour or an icon (a badge with the word in it, an icon with an\n<code>aria-label</code> or text next to it), and label a control of your own. Mark what is only decoration with\n<code>aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot;</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-known-limits\">Known limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No renderer with a lookup editor</strong>: a column with an <code>editor</code> (<a href=\"#guide/lookups\">Lookups</a>) and a\n<code>renderer</code> throws a <code>TypeError</code> - the lookup column draws its own cell, the label and its button.</li>\n<li><strong>The default cell&#39;s extras</strong> are not on a custom cell: <code>placeholderRow</code>, <code>focusButton</code>, and the other\nlines and marker of a multiline cell. A text of several lines opens the multiline editor all the same.</li>\n<li><strong><code>fitColumns()</code></strong> measures the text of the field&#39;s formatter, which may be narrower than a badge or a\nbar: set the width of the column in the layout.</li>\n<li><strong>Only the body cells</strong>: no renderer for group rows, the totals row or the column headers, and a column\nhas one renderer for every row - draw each row your own way in <code>update()</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"sql","title":"SQL","html":"<p>The grid filters the rows it has. A backend that loads only the rows a filter lets through needs the\nfilter as SQL: <code>toSqlWhere()</code> turns a filter - <code>datasource.getFilter()</code>, sent as JSON - into the\ncondition of a <code>WHERE</code> and its parameters, for <strong>PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQLite and SQL Server</strong>. It selects\nthe rows the grid&#39;s own filter selects, by the rules of <a href=\"#guide/filtering/operators\">Filtering</a>, and it\ntakes the filter as hostile input: no value is ever part of the SQL.</p>\n<p>It is a separate entry of the package, <code>@simple-html/grid/sql</code>, with no DOM and no lit-html: Node loads\nit with <code>import</code> and with <code>require</code>, without a bundler. Find&#39;s <em>Filter rows</em> is not sent: it filters in the\nbrowser, see <a href=\"#guide/find/filter-rows-and-a-backend\">Find</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"sql\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-where-from-the-filter\">A WHERE from the filter</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst filter = {\n    group: 'AND',\n    children: [\n        { field: 'name', operator: 'BEGIN_WITH', value: 'an' },\n        { field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18 }\n    ]\n};\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere(filter, {\n    dialect: 'postgres', // 'oracle' | 'sqlite' | 'mssql'\n    // the whitelist: the only fields a filter may use, their columns and types\n    columns: {\n        name: 'customer_name', // a text column\n        age: { column: 'age', type: 'number' },\n        born: { column: 'born', type: 'date' },\n        active: { column: 'is_active', type: 'boolean' }\n    },\n    timeZone: 'Europe/Oslo' // the user's, for the dates\n});\n// sql:    ((&quot;customer_name&quot; IS NOT NULL AND LOWER(&quot;customer_name&quot;) LIKE $1 ESCAPE '!')\n//          AND (&quot;age&quot; IS NOT NULL AND &quot;age&quot; &gt; $2))\n// params: ['an%', 18]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>sql</code></strong> is one condition, without the <code>WHERE</code>: in brackets when it has more than one part, so it can\nbe ANDed with your own (<code>WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND ${sql}</code>). When the filter leaves no condition - no\nfilter (<code>null</code>), empty groups, only conditions the grid ignores - it is <strong><code>1=1</code></strong>, which is valid after\n<code>WHERE</code> in every dialect: there is no empty case to handle.</li>\n<li><strong><code>params</code></strong> are the values of its placeholders, in their order: texts, numbers and booleans, which\nevery driver takes as they are (dates are text or numbers, see <a href=\"#guide/sql/dates-and-time-zones\">Dates</a>).</li>\n<li><strong><code>columns</code></strong> maps each field a filter may use to its column: a name (a text column), or\n<code>{ column, type, dateStorage, booleanValues }</code>. The <code>type</code> is one of the grid&#39;s field types: <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>\n(the default), <code>&#39;number&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;date&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;datetime&#39;</code> - give each the type its field has in the\ngrid&#39;s <code>fields</code>. Several fields can name one column.</li>\n<li><strong><code>firstParam</code></strong> numbers the placeholders from another number: <code>firstParam: 2</code> gives <code>$2</code>, <code>$3</code> ... for a\nquery with a parameter of its own before them.</li>\n<li>The options are checked on every call: an unknown option (<code>timezone</code> for <code>timeZone</code>) throws, so does a\ncolumn that is not a plain name.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-columns-from-the-grids-fields\">The columns from the grid&#39;s fields</h3>\n<p><code>sqlColumnsFromFields()</code> makes <code>columns</code> of the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code> - one list of the fields for the\nbrowser and the backend, so their types stay the same:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { sqlColumnsFromFields, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\n// the same object as the grid's `new Datasource({ fields })`\nconst fields = { project: 'text', born: 'date', amount: { type: 'number', label: 'Amount' } } as const;\nconst columns = sqlColumnsFromFields(fields, { nameCase: 'upper' });\n// { project: { column: 'PROJECT', type: 'text' }, born: { column: 'BORN', type: 'date' },\n//   amount: { column: 'AMOUNT', type: 'number' } }\nconst { sql } = toSqlWhere({ field: 'project', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'x' }, { dialect: 'oracle', columns, timeZone: 'UTC' });</code></pre>\n<p>Each field is a column of its type, <code>&#39;text&#39;</code> without one, named as the field. <code>nameCase</code> (<code>&#39;upper&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;lower&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;asIs&#39;</code>) sets the case of the names - they are quoted, and Oracle&#39;s unquoted names are upper\ncase - <code>column: (field) =&gt; ...</code> names them another way (<code>&#39;crm.&#39; + field</code>), and <code>dateStorage</code> is the\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/dates-and-time-zones\">storage</a> of every date and datetime column. Of a field <code>type</code>, <code>caseSensitive</code>\nand <code>clobType</code> are read - the column&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/sql/a-column-that-keeps-its-case-casesensitive\">locked case</a> and\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/a-lob-column-clobtype\">LOB</a>, as the grid has them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-errors-answer-with-a-400\">Errors: answer with a 400</h3>\n<p>A filter that is no filter, a field that is no column, a filter over a limit and a <code>timeZone</code> that is\nnone throw a <code>TypeError</code> or a <code>RangeError</code>: the request&#39;s mistake. <strong><code>isSqlFilterError(error)</code></strong> is true\nfor them - and false for the other options that are wrong, a mistake of your code (a 500):</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isSqlFilterError, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const body: { filter: unknown; timeZone: string };\ntry {\n    toSqlWhere(body.filter, { dialect: 'postgres', columns: { name: 'name' }, timeZone: body.timeZone });\n} catch (error) {\n    if (!isSqlFilterError(error)) {\n        throw error; // a 500\n    }\n    // a 400, with error.message\n}</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-links-and-bookmarks-of-v5\">Links and bookmarks of v5</h3>\n<p>A filter of v5 - <code>{ type: &#39;GROUP&#39;, logicalOperator, filterArguments }</code>, <code>{ attribute, operator, value }</code></p>\n<ul>\n<li>from an old <code>?filter=</code> link or a saved config: <strong><code>normalizeFilter()</code></strong> takes either shape, also as\nJSON text, and gives the v6 filter (null for none); <code>convertV5Filter()</code> converts a v5 one, <code>isV5Filter()</code>\ntells which it is. All three are in <code>@simple-html/grid</code> too, for the browser:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { normalizeFilter, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst filter = normalizeFilter('{&quot;attribute&quot;:&quot;name&quot;,&quot;operator&quot;:&quot;BEGIN_WITH&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:&quot;an&quot;}');\n// { field: 'name', operator: 'BEGIN_WITH', value: 'an' }\nconst { sql } = toSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'postgres', columns: { name: 'name' } });</code></pre>\n<p>They take hostile input as <code>toSqlWhere()</code> does: what is neither shape throws a <code>TypeError</code>\n(<code>isSqlFilterError</code> is true for it) - it is not taken as no filter, which would select every row. See\n<a href=\"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/blob/master/MIGRATION2V6.md#saved-configs\">Migration</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-security\">Security</h2>\n<p>The filter comes from a browser, so <code>toSqlWhere()</code> takes it as hostile JSON.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Values are parameters, never SQL.</strong> The SQL is only fixed keywords and functions, placeholders,\n<code>&#39;&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;!&#39;</code> (the escape of LIKE), Oracle&#39;s date formats, and the quoted names of your <code>columns</code>. A value\nof <code>&#39;; DROP TABLE customer; --</code> is a parameter like any other text.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The columns are a whitelist.</strong> A <code>field</code> or <code>valueField</code> that is not in <code>columns</code> <strong>throws</strong> - it is\nnever left out, which would show more rows than the filter asks for. Only own properties count:\n<code>__proto__</code>, <code>constructor</code> and <code>toString</code> are unknown fields unless you list them, and a polluted\n<code>Object.prototype</code> adds none.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The names are checked and quoted.</strong> A column is letters (a-z, A-Z), digits, <code>_</code> and <code>#</code> (Oracle&#39;s\n<code>PROJECT#</code>), first a letter or <code>_</code> - not a digit, not <code>#</code>, a temporary table on SQL Server - at most 128, with up to two prefixes (<code>schema.table.column</code>); anything else throws when the\noptions are read. It is quoted - <code>&quot;name&quot;</code> on PostgreSQL, Oracle and SQLite, <code>[name]</code> on SQL Server - and\ncan hold no quote or bracket that would need escaping. A quoted name keeps its case: PostgreSQL folds a\nname made without quotes to lower case, Oracle to upper case, so on <strong>Oracle write the name in upper\ncase</strong> (<code>&#39;CUSTOMER_NAME&#39;</code>), as the database has it.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The shape is checked.</strong> A group is <code>{ group?: &#39;AND&#39; | &#39;OR&#39;, children: [...] }</code>, a condition\n<code>{ field, operator, value?, valueField?, caseSensitive?, literal? }</code> with a known operator and a value of a type\nits column takes. Anything else throws a <code>TypeError</code>: an array or a class where an object goes, a\nproperty of neither (<code>caseSensitiv</code>), an unknown operator, an object as a value, a number on a text\ncolumn, a text that is no date, <code>NaN</code>, a text with a NUL character. Each property is read once, so a\ngetter can not give one value to the check and another to the SQL.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Limits</strong> keep a filter from making a query that is too large: over one, a <code>RangeError</code>. <code>limits</code>\nchanges them:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>limit</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>depth</code></td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>how deep groups nest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conditions</code></td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>the conditions and groups, also the ones left out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>listLength</code></td>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>the values of one <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>textLength</code></td>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>the characters of one text value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>params</code></td>\n<td>2000</td>\n<td>the parameters of the WHERE</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>SQL Server takes <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/maximum-capacity-specifications-for-sql-server\">2100 parameters</a>\nin a request, the 2000 leave room for your own. Oracle takes 1000 values in an <code>IN</code> list before 23ai\n(<a href=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/error-help/db/ora-01795/\">ORA-01795</a>): a longer list is split into\n<code>IN (...) OR IN (...)</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>The messages</strong> say where in the filter the problem is (<code>filter.children[1].value</code>) and show at most\n40 characters of it. Answer a <code>TypeError</code> or a <code>RangeError</code> with a 400.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What it does not do, and you still must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authenticate the user, and decide which rows they may see.</strong> The filter is the user&#39;s, it narrows\nwhat they see: AND your own conditions to it - <code>WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND ${sql}</code> - and keep the columns\na user may not filter on out of <code>columns</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Use the params.</strong> Pass them to the driver as the parameters of the query; never put them into the\nSQL text yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit the rows</strong> (<code>LIMIT</code>, <code>FETCH FIRST</code>, <code>TOP</code>) and the time a query may take: a <code>CONTAINS</code> is a\n<code>LIKE &#39;%...%&#39;</code>, which reads every row.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-dialects\">The dialects</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>dialect</th>\n<th>placeholders</th>\n<th>a name</th>\n<th>the driver</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;postgres&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>&quot;name&quot;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"https://node-postgres.com/features/queries\"><code>pg</code></a>: <code>client.query(sql, params)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;oracle&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>:1</code>, <code>:2</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>&quot;NAME&quot;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"https://node-oracledb.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user_guide/bind.html\"><code>oracledb</code></a>: <code>connection.execute(sql, params)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;sqlite&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>?</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;name&quot;</code></td>\n<td><code>node:sqlite</code>, <code>better-sqlite3</code>: <code>db.prepare(sql).all(...params)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;mssql&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>@p1</code>, <code>@p2</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>[name]</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/tediousjs/node-mssql\"><code>mssql</code></a>: <code>request.input(&#39;p1&#39;, params[0])</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\n// PostgreSQL, pg: $1 is params[0]\nconst pgWhere = toSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'postgres', columns, timeZone });\nawait pool.query(`SELECT * FROM customer WHERE ${pgWhere.sql}`, pgWhere.params);\n\n// Oracle, oracledb: an array binds by position - the order the placeholders have in the SQL text,\n// whatever their names. Put your own placeholders and values in the same order\nconst oraWhere = toSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'oracle', columns, timeZone });\nawait connection.execute(`SELECT * FROM CUSTOMER WHERE ${oraWhere.sql}`, oraWhere.params);\n\n// SQLite, node:sqlite or better-sqlite3: the ? in order\nconst liteWhere = toSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'sqlite', columns, timeZone });\ndb.prepare(`SELECT * FROM customer WHERE ${liteWhere.sql}`).all(...liteWhere.params);\n\n// SQL Server, mssql: @p1 ... are named, from firstParam\nconst msWhere = toSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'mssql', columns, timeZone });\nconst request = pool.request();\nmsWhere.params.forEach((value, index) =&gt; request.input(`p${index + 1}`, value));\nawait request.query(`SELECT * FROM customer WHERE ${msWhere.sql}`);</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>mssql</strong> picks the type of a parameter from the JS value when <code>input()</code> gets none: a text is\n<code>NVarChar</code>, which the text comparisons need (see <a href=\"#guide/sql/text\">Text</a>), a boolean <code>Bit</code>, a whole number <code>Int</code>\n(<code>BigInt</code> past 32 bits), another number <code>Float</code>. With <code>tedious</code> itself, give a text <code>TYPES.NVarChar</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>LOB columns are not supported</strong> - <code>CLOB</code>, <code>BLOB</code>, <code>NCLOB</code>: a column is a text, number, date or\nboolean of the database (<code>VARCHAR2</code>, <code>NVARCHAR</code>, <code>TEXT</code> ...). Oracle compares no CLOB with the <code>=</code> of a\ntext equal to a value (ORA-22848). A <code>CLOB</code> / <code>NCLOB</code> can be filtered as plain text with <code>LIKE</code> only,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/sql/a-lob-column-clobtype\">A LOB column: <code>clobType</code></a>.</li>\n<li><strong>sqlite</strong>: the <code>?</code> are taken in order, <code>firstParam</code> does not change them: put your own values before\nor after the params as your <code>?</code> are before or after the WHERE.</li>\n<li><strong>pg</strong> sends every parameter as text and PostgreSQL takes the type of the column it is compared with\n(<a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/typeconv-oper.html\">type resolution</a>), so <code>toSqlWhere()</code>\ncasts where that would fail, see <a href=\"#guide/sql/numbers\">Numbers</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-each-type-compares\">How each type compares</h2>\n<p>The rules are the grid&#39;s, <a href=\"#guide/filtering/operators\">Filtering › Operators</a>. Here is how the SQL does\nthem, and where each dialect needs its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Every condition is true or false, never null.</strong> SQL compares null as unknown, and <code>NOT</code> of unknown is\nunknown: <code>NOT (name = &#39;Ann&#39;)</code> leaves out the rows without a name, where the grid&#39;s <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> keeps\nthem. So each condition decides about the blank (null) rows itself, with <code>col IS NULL OR ...</code> or\n<code>col IS NOT NULL AND ...</code> - which one is the grid&#39;s answer for a blank row, worked out when the SQL is\nmade. <code>NOT (sql)</code> of any WHERE selects exactly the other rows.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-text\">Text</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Case</strong>: ignored by default: the column in <code>LOWER()</code> (<code>UPPER()</code> for <code>IN</code>, as the grid), and the\nvalue folded before it is bound when it is ASCII only - <code>UPPER(&quot;country&quot;) IN ($1, $2)</code> with the\nparams <code>[&#39;FINLAND&#39;, &#39;FRANCE&#39;]</code>. A value with a letter that is not ASCII is folded by the database, as\nits column is - <code>UPPER($1)</code> - since JavaScript and the database fold some letters differently\n(<code>ß</code> is <code>SS</code> in JavaScript, SQLite folds no letter that is not ASCII) and a text must always match\nthe same text. A to Z and a to z fold the same everywhere, except in a Turkish collation, where <code>i</code>\nand <code>I</code> are not a pair: there use <code>caseSensitive</code>, or fold the column yourself.\n<code>caseSensitive: true</code> compares as they are.</li>\n<li><strong><code>*</code> and <code>%</code> are wildcards</strong>, any text: they become the wildcard of <code>LIKE</code> (<code>%</code>), or of <code>GLOB</code> on\nSQLite (<code>*</code>). <strong><code>_</code> is one character</strong>: <code>_</code> of <code>LIKE</code>, <code>?</code> of <code>GLOB</code>. Every other character is\nliteral: the escape, and a <code>[</code> or <code>?</code> of <code>GLOB</code>, are escaped in the parameter.\n<code>EQUAL</code> is the whole text, <code>CONTAINS</code> <code>%v%</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code> <code>v%</code>, <code>END_WITH</code> <code>%v</code>; <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> and\n<code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code> their <code>NOT</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>\\</code> makes the next <code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> or <code>\\</code> a character</strong>, as in the grid&#39;s filter: <code>S\\_\\_den</code> is\n<code>LIKE &#39;s!_!_den&#39; ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code>, <code>50\\%*</code> is <code>LIKE &#39;50!%%&#39;</code>. A <code>\\</code> before anything else is a <code>\\</code>. The\n<code>\\</code> is the grid&#39;s escape, not the database&#39;s: what reaches the database is a plain <code>\\</code> where the\nvalue has one (<code>C:\\temp</code>), never an escape of <code>LIKE</code> - <code>ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code> is always given.</li>\n<li><strong><code>literal: true</code></strong> takes <code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>\\</code> as characters, as the grid does then: they are escaped\nwhere they are wildcards of the pattern - <code>!%</code> and <code>!_</code> with <code>ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code>, <code>[%]</code> and <code>[_]</code> on SQL\nServer, <code>[*]</code> in <code>GLOB</code> - and the rest is as above: <code>CONTAINS &#39;50%&#39;</code> is <code>LIKE &#39;%50!%%&#39; ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A blank row</strong> - null, and <code>&#39;&#39;</code> - is the text <code>&#39;&#39;</code>: it matches a pattern of wildcards only, and\n<code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> / <code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code> of anything else.</li>\n<li><code>IS_BLANK</code> is null or <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code> the others.</li>\n<li>The comparisons a text does not offer (<code>LESS_THAN</code> ... <code>BETWEEN</code>, from filters of v5) compare code\npoint by code point, as JavaScript does, in a binary collation.</li>\n<li>A number, a <code>Date</code> or a boolean as the value of a text column throws: the grid compares those as\nnumbers and dates. Give the column the type its values have.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>PostgreSQL</th>\n<th>Oracle</th>\n<th>SQLite</th>\n<th>SQL Server</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>match</td>\n<td><code>LIKE $1 ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>LIKE :1 ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>GLOB ?</code></td>\n<td><code>COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2 LIKE @p1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code> / <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> without a wildcard</td>\n<td><code>= $1</code></td>\n<td><code>= :1</code></td>\n<td><code>COLLATE BINARY = ?</code></td>\n<td><code>LIKE</code> as above: <code>=</code> ignores the spaces at the end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>one character (<code>_</code>)</td>\n<td><code>_</code></td>\n<td><code>_</code></td>\n<td><code>?</code></td>\n<td><code>_</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>literal <code>!</code></td>\n<td><code>!!</code></td>\n<td><code>!!</code></td>\n<td><code>!</code></td>\n<td><code>!</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>literal <code>[</code>, <code>?</code></td>\n<td>as is</td>\n<td>as is</td>\n<td><code>[[]</code>, <code>[?]</code></td>\n<td><code>[[]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> with <code>literal</code>, or <code>\\*</code>, <code>\\%</code>, <code>\\_</code></td>\n<td><code>*</code>, <code>!%</code>, <code>!_</code></td>\n<td><code>*</code>, <code>!%</code>, <code>!_</code></td>\n<td><code>[*]</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code></td>\n<td><code>*</code>, <code>[%]</code>, <code>[_]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>blank</td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL OR col = &#39;&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL</code></td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL OR col = &#39;&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>col IS NULL OR DATALENGTH(col) = 0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>order</td>\n<td><code>COLLATE &quot;C&quot;</code></td>\n<td>binary</td>\n<td><code>COLLATE BINARY</code></td>\n<td><code>COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>: <code>LIKE</code> keeps case with a deterministic collation (the default), <code>LOWER()</code> folds by the\n<a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/collation.html\">collation</a> of the column. <code>COLLATE &quot;C&quot;</code>\ncompares bytes, which in UTF-8 is code point order. The default escape of <code>LIKE</code> is a backslash, which\nis <code>ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code> here (<a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/functions-matching.html\">pattern matching</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Oracle</strong>: <a href=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/23/sqlrf/Nulls.html\"><code>&#39;&#39;</code> is null</a>,\nso a blank is <code>IS NULL</code> and no condition compares with <code>&#39;&#39;</code>. <code>LIKE</code> and <code>&lt;</code> compare binary with the\ndefault <code>NLS_COMP=BINARY</code>; with <code>NLS_COMP=LINGUISTIC</code>, or a column with a case-insensitive collation,\nthey follow that (<a href=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/21/nlspg/linguistic-sorting-and-matching.html\">linguistic matching</a>).\nAfter the escape only <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> and the escape itself may come\n(<a href=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/error-help/db/ora-01424/\">ORA-01424</a>): nothing else is escaped.</li>\n<li><strong>SQLite</strong>: <code>LIKE</code> ignores the case of ASCII letters and has no way to keep it, so the patterns are\n<code>GLOB</code>, which keeps case; a special character of <code>GLOB</code> is literal in brackets. <code>LOWER()</code> and <code>UPPER()</code>\nfold ASCII letters only (<a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_corefunc.html\">core functions</a>) - see\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/where-the-sql-differs-from-the-grid\">the differences</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SQL Server</strong>: case follows the collation of the column, and <code>=</code> ignores trailing spaces\n(<code>&#39;abc&#39; = &#39;abc &#39;</code> is true, <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/language-elements/string-comparison-assignment\">string comparison</a>).\nSo a text is matched with <code>LIKE</code> in the binary collation <code>Latin1_General_100_BIN2</code> (code point order,\ncase as it is); with an <code>nvarchar</code> parameter <code>LIKE</code> counts trailing spaces\n(<a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/language-elements/like-transact-sql\">LIKE</a>), and so does\n<code>DATALENGTH</code>: <code>&#39;   &#39;</code> is not blank. <code>LIKE</code>&#39;s wildcards are literal in brackets, <code>[%]</code>, <code>[_]</code>, <code>[[]</code>.\nA <code>char(n)</code> column is padded with spaces: use <code>varchar</code> / <code>nvarchar</code> for text the grid filters.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-numbers\">Numbers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A blank (null) row is 0</strong>, as in the grid: <code>LESS_THAN 5</code> matches it, <code>EQUAL 0</code> too. Whether 0\nmatches is worked out when the SQL is made, and the column is compared as it is:\n<code>(&quot;age&quot; IS NULL OR &quot;age&quot; &lt; $1)</code> for <code>LESS_THAN 5</code>, <code>(&quot;age&quot; IS NOT NULL AND &quot;age&quot; &gt; $1)</code> for\n<code>GREATER_THAN 5</code>. The same rows as <code>COALESCE(age, 0) &lt; 5</code>, but an index on the column is used.</li>\n<li><code>IS_BLANK</code> is null or 0.</li>\n<li>A text value is read as <code>Number()</code> reads it (<code>&#39;12&#39;</code>, as the grid does); a text that is no number,\n<code>NaN</code> or <code>Infinity</code> throws.</li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>: a parameter takes the type of the column, and <code>&#39;12.5&#39;</code> is no integer - an integer\ncolumn would fail. A number that is not whole is <code>CAST($1 AS numeric)</code>; a whole number stays as it is,\nso an index on an integer column is used for it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-booleans\">Booleans</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A blank (null) row is false</strong>: <code>EQUAL false</code> matches it, <code>EQUAL true</code> does not. <code>IS_BLANK</code> is null\nonly - <code>false</code> is a value.</li>\n<li>Any operator but <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> is <code>EQUAL</code>, as the grid takes it.</li>\n<li>The parameters are <code>true</code> / <code>false</code> on PostgreSQL (<code>boolean</code>) and SQL Server (<code>bit</code>), <code>1</code> / <code>0</code> on\nOracle and SQLite (<code>NUMBER(1)</code>, <code>INTEGER</code>). A column that holds other values says so:\n<code>{ column: &#39;IS_ACTIVE&#39;, type: &#39;boolean&#39;, booleanValues: { true: &#39;Y&#39;, false: &#39;N&#39; } }</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-dates-and-time-zones\">Dates and time zones</h3>\n<p>The grid reads a date in the user&#39;s browser, on its wall clock: a <code>date</code> is compared on the day, a\n<code>datetime</code> to the second. A <code>Date</code> of the filter becomes ISO text in UTC in JSON -\n<code>2026-11-21T23:00:00.000Z</code> is 22 November in Oslo - so the day is read in the <strong>user&#39;s time zone,\n<code>timeZone</code></strong>, an IANA name, not in the server&#39;s. The browser has it:\n<code>Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone</code>; send it with the filter. It is required when a column\nis a date.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A day is a range</strong>: <code>EQUAL</code> 22 November is <code>col &gt;= &lt;22 Nov 00:00&gt; AND col &lt; &lt;23 Nov 00:00&gt;</code>,\n<code>LESS_THAN</code> is <code>col &lt; &lt;the start&gt;</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN</code> <code>col &gt;= &lt;the next day&gt;</code>, a <code>datetime</code> the same with\nthe second. The column is compared as it is, so its index is used.</li>\n<li><strong>A blank row date</strong> (null) is no date: only <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> matches it.</li>\n<li><strong>A value</strong> is a <code>Date</code>, or ISO 8601 text: with <code>Z</code> or an offset it is an instant; without one the wall\nclock in <code>timeZone</code> (<code>&#39;2026-11-22T10:30&#39;</code>, read as JavaScript does - a time the clock skips moves on,\n<code>02:30</code> the night summer time starts is <code>03:30</code>). A <strong>date-only text</strong> (<code>&#39;2026-11-22&#39;</code>) is UTC midnight\nin a comparison, as the grid&#39;s <code>new Date(&#39;2026-11-22&#39;)</code> reads it: the day before west of UTC. In an <code>IN</code>\nlist it is the day itself, the text the grid shows. Send a <code>Date</code> - it is ISO text with <code>Z</code> in JSON. The\nyears are 1000 to 9998; other text throws.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>How the column holds its dates decides the parameters, <code>dateStorage</code> (on the options, or a column):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>dateStorage</code></th>\n<th>the column</th>\n<th>22 November in Oslo, the start</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;local&#39;</code> (default)</td>\n<td>a type without a time zone, holding the wall clock of <code>timeZone</code>: PostgreSQL <code>date</code> / <code>timestamp</code>, Oracle <code>DATE</code> / <code>TIMESTAMP</code>, SQL Server <code>date</code> / <code>datetime</code> / <code>datetime2</code>, SQLite text <code>&#39;2026-11-22 08:30:00&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;2026-11-22 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;utc&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the same types, holding UTC</td>\n<td><code>&#39;2026-11-21 23:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;zoned&#39;</code></td>\n<td>an instant: PostgreSQL <code>timestamptz</code>, Oracle <code>TIMESTAMP WITH (LOCAL) TIME ZONE</code>, SQL Server <code>datetimeoffset</code>, SQLite ISO text with <code>Z</code> or an offset</td>\n<td><code>&#39;2026-11-21 23:00:00+00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;epochSeconds&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;epochMillis&#39;</code></td>\n<td>a number since 1970-01-01 UTC</td>\n<td><code>1795302000</code>, <code>1795302000000</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The text is converted with a fixed format, so no session setting changes it:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><code>local</code>, <code>utc</code></th>\n<th><code>zoned</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>PostgreSQL</td>\n<td><code>CAST($1 AS timestamp)</code></td>\n<td><code>CAST($1 AS timestamptz)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oracle</td>\n<td><code>TO_DATE(:1, &#39;YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS&#39;)</code></td>\n<td><code>TO_TIMESTAMP_TZ(:1, &#39;YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS TZH:TZM&#39;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL Server</td>\n<td><code>CAST(@p1 AS datetime2(0))</code>, the text <code>2026-11-22T00:00:00</code></td>\n<td><code>CAST(@p1 AS datetimeoffset(0))</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQLite</td>\n<td><code>julianday(col) &gt;= julianday(?)</code></td>\n<td>the same, the text in UTC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong> reads ISO 8601 text in any <code>DateStyle</code>, and ignores an offset in a <code>timestamp</code> - so a\n<code>timestamptz</code> column is <code>&#39;zoned&#39;</code>, with the offset written out, and its session <code>TimeZone</code> does not\nmatter (<a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-datetime.html\">date/time types</a>). A <code>date</code>\ncolumn compares with the <code>timestamp</code> at midnight.</li>\n<li><strong>Oracle</strong>: <code>TO_DATE</code> with its format gives a <code>DATE</code>, whatever <code>NLS_DATE_FORMAT</code> is\n(<a href=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/23/sqlrf/TO_DATE.html\">TO_DATE</a>). Not a\n<code>TIMESTAMP</code>: compared with one, a <code>DATE</code> column is the side that is converted, and its index is not used.\nA <code>TIMESTAMP</code> column takes the <code>DATE</code> as it is. <code>TO_TIMESTAMP_TZ</code> for the zoned types\n(<a href=\"https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/23/sqlrf/TO_TIMESTAMP_TZ.html\">TO_TIMESTAMP_TZ</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>SQL Server</strong> reads <code>&#39;YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss&#39;</code>, with the <code>T</code>, the same whatever <code>SET LANGUAGE</code> and\n<code>SET DATEFORMAT</code> are; <code>&#39;YYYY-MM-DD&#39;</code> alone is read by them for <code>datetime</code>\n(<a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/data-types/datetime-transact-sql\">datetime</a>,\n<a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/data-types/datetimeoffset-transact-sql\">datetimeoffset</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>SQLite</strong> has no date type: <code>julianday()</code> reads every ISO 8601 text - with <code>T</code> or a space, with or\nwithout seconds, <code>Z</code> and offsets made UTC - and is null for a text that is none, which is blank then\n(<a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_datefunc.html\">date functions</a>). An index for it is an index on the\nexpression: <code>CREATE INDEX customer_born ON customer (julianday(born))</code>. Numbers are <code>epochSeconds</code> or\n<code>epochMillis</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Summer time</strong>: a day the clocks change is 23 or 25 hours long, and the range of an instant column\nis that long. Where midnight is skipped (Brazil until 2019) the day starts at 01:00, as JavaScript&#39;s\n<code>new Date(y, m, d)</code> does. The tests compare every instant with JavaScript&#39;s own <code>Date</code> in a dozen time\nzones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-week-typed-in-the-grid\">A week typed in the grid</h3>\n<p>A column shown as weeks, months, quarters (<a href=\"#guide/formatting/display-formats\">display formats</a>) takes\nits header filter as the cells show it: <code>2026-W47</code>. The grid makes <strong>ordinary date conditions</strong> of it -\nthe week is a <code>BETWEEN</code> of its Monday and Sunday (<a href=\"#guide/formatting/filter-as-shown\">Filter as shown</a>) -\nso the SQL needs no <code>EXTRACT(WEEK ...)</code>, <code>DATEPART(ISO_WEEK ...)</code> or <code>strftime(&#39;%W&#39;)</code>, and each dialect\nwrites the range it writes for any day - an index on the column works:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>typed in the grid</th>\n<th>the filter</th>\n<th>PostgreSQL, a <code>date</code> column</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>= 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td><code>BETWEEN [2026-11-16, 2026-11-22]</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;due&quot; &gt;= &#39;2026-11-16 00:00:00&#39; AND &quot;due&quot; &lt; &#39;2026-11-23 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≥ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO 2026-11-16</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;due&quot; &gt;= &#39;2026-11-16 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&gt; 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN 2026-11-22</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;due&quot; &gt;= &#39;2026-11-23 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≤ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO 2026-11-22</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;due&quot; &lt; &#39;2026-11-23 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠ 2026-W47</code></td>\n<td>an OR group: <code>LESS_THAN 2026-11-16</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN 2026-11-22</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>(&quot;due&quot; &lt; &#39;2026-11-16 00:00:00&#39;) OR (&quot;due&quot; &gt;= &#39;2026-11-23 00:00:00&#39;) OR &quot;due&quot; IS NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>2026-W40..2026-W47</code></td>\n<td><code>BETWEEN [2026-09-28, 2026-11-22]</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;due&quot; &gt;= &#39;2026-09-28 00:00:00&#39; AND &quot;due&quot; &lt; &#39;2026-11-23 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>(The values are parameters, <code>$1</code>, and each comparison has <code>&quot;due&quot; IS NOT NULL AND</code> in front, as above.) A\n<code>datetime</code> column gets Sunday 23:59:59 as the end, the SQL is the same: <code>&lt; &#39;2026-11-23 00:00:00&#39;</code>. The\nweek is the user&#39;s: its days are in the browser&#39;s time zone, as <code>timeZone</code> reads them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-quick-date\">A quick date</h3>\n<p>A <a href=\"#guide/filtering/quick-dates\">quick date</a> of the grid - This month, Last 7 days, Overdue - is relative,\nthe grid computes its days from today when it filters. The filter it makes, and so <code>toSqlWhere()</code>, is a\nplain <code>BETWEEN</code> of those days: <code>toSqlWhere()</code> never sees <code>&#39;thisMonth&#39;</code>, and a WHERE made today has\ntoday&#39;s dates. On 22 November 2026 (PostgreSQL, a <code>date</code> column):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>quick date</th>\n<th>the filter</th>\n<th>the WHERE</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Today</td>\n<td><code>BETWEEN [2026-11-22, 2026-11-22]</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;order_date&quot; &gt;= &#39;2026-11-22 00:00:00&#39; AND &quot;order_date&quot; &lt; &#39;2026-11-23 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>This month</td>\n<td><code>BETWEEN [2026-11-01, 2026-11-30]</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;order_date&quot; &gt;= &#39;2026-11-01 00:00:00&#39; AND &quot;order_date&quot; &lt; &#39;2026-12-01 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overdue</td>\n<td><code>BETWEEN [null, 2026-11-21]</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;order_date&quot; &lt; &#39;2026-11-22 00:00:00&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A <code>datetime</code> column gets the last day at 23:59:59, the WHERE is the same. As for a week typed, the values\nare parameters with <code>IS NOT NULL AND</code> in front, an index on the column works, and the days are the\nuser&#39;s: the browser&#39;s time zone, as <code>timeZone</code> reads them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-between\">BETWEEN</h3>\n<p><code>[from, to]</code>, both included: <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO from AND LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO to</code>, each with\nthe rules of the type - a <code>date</code> from the start of the first day to the end of the last. A blank end\n(null, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>) is open, both blank leave the condition out; 0 is an end. <code>from</code> after <code>to</code> is no row. A\nvalue that is not <code>[from, to]</code> throws (the grid leaves it out).</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-in-and-not_in\">IN and NOT_IN</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A list, or a text with one value per line. <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code> (any case; with <code>caseSensitive</code> as it is) and\n<code>null</code> in it are the blank rows. No list or <code>[]</code>: <code>IN</code> is <code>1=0</code>, no row; <code>NOT_IN</code> <code>1=1</code>, every row.</li>\n<li><strong>Text</strong>: in upper case, or as they are with <code>caseSensitive</code>; a number or boolean in the list as its\ntext. SQL Server matches each with <code>LIKE</code>, its wildcards literal, for the trailing spaces.</li>\n<li><strong>A number</strong> compares as a number: <code>[2]</code> finds 2. A text in the list is read as a number (<code>&#39;2.00&#39;</code> is\n2).</li>\n<li><strong>A date</strong> compares the day (<code>date</code>) or the second (<code>datetime</code>) of each: <code>&#39;2026-11-22&#39;</code> - the text the\ngrid shows - is that day in <code>timeZone</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A boolean</strong>: <code>true</code> / <code>&#39;TRUE&#39;</code>, <code>false</code> / <code>&#39;FALSE&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code>.</li>\n<li>A <code>valueField</code> is not used, as in the grid.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-valuefield\">valueField</h3>\n<p>A condition with <code>valueField</code> compares the column with another column of the row. The two have the same\ntype (a <code>date</code> and a <code>datetime</code> go together), and supported are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>number</strong>: every comparison, a blank as 0 on both sides: <code>COALESCE(a, 0) &gt; COALESCE(b, 0)</code></li>\n<li><strong>text</strong>: <code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> and the comparisons, a blank as <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, no wildcards. A pattern\n(<code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code>, <code>END_WITH</code>, <code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code>) with a <code>valueField</code> throws</li>\n<li><strong>boolean</strong>: <code>EQUAL</code> / <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, a blank row as false; a blank other column matches nothing (but\n<code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>), as the grid. Both columns have the same <code>booleanValues</code></li>\n<li><strong>date / datetime</strong>: both columns have the same <code>dateStorage</code>; a blank on either side only matches\n<code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>. A <code>datetime</code> compares the columns as they are. A <code>date</code> compares the days, which needs\n<code>dateStorage: &#39;local&#39;</code> (the day of an instant is in the user&#39;s time zone, which the SQL does not have):\n<code>CAST(col AS date)</code> on PostgreSQL and SQL Server, <code>TRUNC(col)</code> on Oracle, <code>date(col)</code> on SQLite. With\nanother storage it throws</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-column-that-keeps-its-case-casesensitive\">A column that keeps its case: <code>caseSensitive</code></h3>\n<p><code>{ column: &#39;PROJECT_CODE&#39;, caseSensitive: true }</code> - a text column every condition compares with its\ncase, also one the filter sends without <code>caseSensitive</code> or with <code>false</code>: <code>&quot;PROJECT_CODE&quot; = :1</code>, not\n<code>LOWER(&quot;PROJECT_CODE&quot;) = :1</code>, so an index on the column is used (on SQL Server the case is kept with\n<code>COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2</code>, which an index of another collation does not serve). The grid&#39;s field\nhas it too (<code>fields: { projectCode: { caseSensitive: true } }</code>, <a href=\"#guide/filtering/match-case\">Filtering</a>):\nMatch case is always on there, the user can not turn it off. Set it in both, or once in the fields with\n<code>sqlColumnsFromFields()</code>. A column that is no text throws.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-lob-column-clobtype\">A LOB column: <code>clobType</code></h3>\n<p><code>{ column: &#39;NOTES&#39;, clobType: true }</code> - an Oracle <code>CLOB</code> or <code>NCLOB</code>, which SQL compares with <code>LIKE</code> only:\n<code>=</code>, <code>&lt;&gt;</code>, <code>IN</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code> on one are an error (ORA-22848). LOB columns are not supported as such;\nthis lets a filter compare one as plain text:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>SQL (oracle)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code> / <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>LOWER(&quot;NOTES&quot;) LIKE :1 ESCAPE &#39;!&#39;</code> - the whole text, its <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> escaped - / its <code>NOT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code>, <code>END_WITH</code>, <code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>LIKE</code> as on any text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code> / <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;NOTES&quot; IS NULL</code> / <code>IS NOT NULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Every other operator (<code>IN</code>, <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code> ...), a <code>valueField</code> on either side, and a\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/calculated-columns\">calculated column</a> or a <a href=\"#guide/sql/a-column-filtered-on-its-template\">template</a> whose formula\nreads it throw a <code>TypeError</code> that <code>isSqlFilterError()</code> knows - a 400. Run on Oracle 23: <code>LIKE</code> finds the\ntext at the end of a CLOB of 8004 characters, there is no limit of 4000. The grid&#39;s field has it too\n(<code>fields: { notes: { clobType: true } }</code>): its filters offer only these operators - no value list -, no\nformula reads it, and a template that reads it is filtered on the value. A column that is no text throws.\n<code>BLOB</code> holds bytes, not text: not supported.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-operators-a-type-does-not-offer\">The operators a type does not offer</h3>\n<p>As the grid takes them (<a href=\"#guide/filtering/operators-a-type-does-not-offer\">the table</a>): on a number or a\ndate <code>CONTAINS</code> and <code>BEGIN_WITH</code> are <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>END_WITH</code> is <code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>,\n<code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code> is <code>LESS_THAN</code>; a text compares <code>LESS_THAN</code> ... and <code>BETWEEN</code> code point by code point;\na boolean takes every operator as <code>EQUAL</code>, and <code>IN</code> of <code>TRUE</code> / <code>FALSE</code> / <code>NULL</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-conditions-that-are-left-out\">Conditions that are left out</h3>\n<p>As the grid leaves them out (<a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-format\">the filter format</a>): a condition\nwithout a field or an operator (null, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>), one without the value it needs (null, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid\n<code>Date</code>) and no <code>valueField</code>, a <code>BETWEEN</code> with both ends blank, an empty group. A field that is not in\n<code>columns</code> throws also then.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-column-filtered-on-its-template\">A column filtered on its template</h2>\n<p>A text column with a template (<code>format.text</code>: <code>{first} {last}</code>, <code>order {value} ({id})</code>, the text of a link)\nis filtered on the text it shows. <strong><code>gridInterface.getShownFormulas()</code></strong> gives each such column&#39;s text as\na formula - <code>CONCAT({first}, &quot; &quot;, {last})</code> - and <strong><code>toSqlWhere({ shown })</code></strong> compares that formula&#39;s SQL\nfor a condition on the field, as the grid compares the text:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// in the browser\nconst fields = ['first', 'last', 'order', 'id'];\nconst body = JSON.stringify({ filter: gridInterface.getServerFilter({ fields }), shown: gridInterface.getShownFormulas({ fields }) });</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const body: { filter: unknown; shown: Record&lt;string, string&gt; };\n// in the backend: first = 'ann berg' is LOWER(first || ' ' || last) = $1\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere(body.filter, {\n    dialect: 'postgres',\n    columns: { first: 'first', last: 'last', order: 'order_no', id: { column: 'id', type: 'number' } },\n    shown: body.shown\n});</code></pre>\n<p>A formula writes a text as it is, a number as <code>12.5</code>, a date as <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, true / false as <code>true</code> /\n<code>false</code> - the default formatters&#39;. A template with a field of another formatter (<code>numberComma</code>,\n<code>DDMMYYYY</code>, one of your own) or a <code>datetime</code> field gets no formula, nor does one that reads a field not in\n<code>fields</code>: <code>getServerFilter()</code> takes the conditions on that column out, and the browser filters them in the\nrows that come back. <code>filterAsShown: false</code> in a column&#39;s format filters its value, in the grid and in SQL.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</h2>\n<p>A <a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns\">calculated column</a> (<code>GridState.calculatedColumns</code>) is computed in the\nbrowser, from the row: the database has no column of it. Give <code>toSqlWhere()</code> the calculated columns with\n<strong><code>calculated</code></strong>, and a filter on one compares its formula, written as SQL, as it compares a column:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst filter = { field: 'total', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 100 };\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere(filter, {\n    dialect: 'postgres',\n    columns: { price: { column: 'price', type: 'number' }, qty: { column: 'qty', type: 'number' } },\n    calculated: { total: '{price} * {qty}' } // or the grid's: gridInterface.getState().calculatedColumns\n});\n// sql: (((&quot;price&quot; * &quot;qty&quot;)) IS NOT NULL AND ((&quot;price&quot; * &quot;qty&quot;)) &gt; $1)</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>calculated</code></strong> is <code>getState().calculatedColumns</code> as it is - <code>{ total: { formula, label, type } }</code> - or\na formula by field: <code>{ total: &#39;{price} * {qty}&#39; }</code>. The browser sends it with the filter\n(<code>getServerFilter({ fields, calculated: &#39;keep&#39; })</code> keeps the conditions on them - each one whose formula\nreads only fields the backend has).</li>\n<li>A formula reads the fields of <code>columns</code> and the other calculated columns; a field that is not in\n<code>columns</code> throws, as in a filter. The name of a calculated column is not one of <code>columns</code>.</li>\n<li>A formula is hostile input, as the filter is: it is read by the grid&#39;s own parser, its texts are\nliterals with their quotes doubled (PostgreSQL gets a text with a <code>\\</code> as its bytes in hex), its numbers\ndigits - never pasted into the SQL. What is wrong with it is a <code>TypeError</code> that <code>isSqlFilterError()</code>\nknows: answer it with a 400. The SQL of one calculated column is at most 100 000 characters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dates.</strong> A date of a formula is its day in the user&#39;s <code>timeZone</code>, as the grid reads it in the browser:\n<code>DAYS()</code>, <code>YEAR()</code>, <code>MONTH()</code>, the comparisons and a date as text (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>) take the day, and\n<code>TODAY()</code> is the day it is there when the SQL is made. A date column becomes its day by how it holds its\ndates (<code>dateStorage</code>):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>PostgreSQL</th>\n<th>Oracle</th>\n<th>SQLite</th>\n<th>SQL Server</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;local&#39;</code> (a wall clock)</td>\n<td><code>CAST(col AS date)</code></td>\n<td><code>TRUNC(col)</code></td>\n<td><code>date(col)</code></td>\n<td><code>CAST(col AS date)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;utc&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;zoned&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>timezone(&#39;Europe/Oslo&#39;, …)</code></td>\n<td><code>FROM_TZ(…) AT TIME ZONE &#39;Europe/Oslo&#39;</code></td>\n<td>in UTC only</td>\n<td>in UTC only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;epochSeconds&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;epochMillis&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>to_timestamp(col)</code> in the zone</td>\n<td><code>NUMTODSINTERVAL</code> in the zone</td>\n<td>in UTC only</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>SQLite has no time zones, SQL Server&#39;s <code>AT TIME ZONE</code> takes Windows names: there an instant can be read in\nUTC only, any other <code>timeZone</code> throws a <code>TypeError</code> - store the wall clock, or let the database compute\nthe column. A calculated column of dates is a <code>date</code> column of days: a filter on it compares the day, as\non any date column.</p>\n<p>The SQL gives the values the grid computes - the tests run every function and 2000 random formulas on\nSQLite and compare each row with the grid, and the SQL of each database was run on PostgreSQL 17, Oracle\n23 and SQL Server 2017 and 2022 with the same rows - the dates around midnight, the changes of the clock\nand today, in every <code>dateStorage</code> the database reads. What it takes to get there, so you can read the SQL:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>the grid</th>\n<th>the SQL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a blank: null, <code>&#39;&#39;</code></td>\n<td>NULL - a text column&#39;s <code>&#39;&#39;</code> is <code>NULLIF(col, &#39;&#39;)</code> (<code>DATALENGTH</code> on SQL Server, where <code>&#39;  &#39; = &#39;&#39;</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a comparison with a blank is false, <code>=</code> true for two blanks</td>\n<td><code>a IS NOT NULL AND b IS NOT NULL AND ...</code>: never NULL, so <code>NOT</code> is right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a text compares in any case</td>\n<td><code>LOWER()</code> on both sides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/</code> of numbers</td>\n<td>as numbers: <code>7 / 2</code> is 3.5 - PostgreSQL, SQLite and SQL Server would divide whole numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a division by 0 blanks the whole value</td>\n<td>the value is NULL when a part divides by 0: <code>COALESCE({a} / {b}, 0)</code> is blank, not 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ROUND halves away from 0, digits from a field too (blank is 0, not a whole number from -10 to 10 blanks the value)</td>\n<td>a float rounded as a decimal: PostgreSQL and Oracle round a float half to even, SQL Server&#39;s <code>decimal(38, 10)</code> keeps a literal <code>9.5</code> from overflowing. SQLite&#39;s <code>round()</code> takes no negative digits: tens are a division. Digits of the row: a <code>CASE</code> of the 21 digits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MIN, MAX skip a blank</td>\n<td>PostgreSQL&#39;s LEAST; COALESCE of the others in front of each on Oracle and SQLite; <code>MIN</code> of a <code>VALUES</code> list on SQL Server (no LEAST before 2022)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a number as text (<code>&amp;</code>, LEFT, LEN): <code>2.5</code>, <code>4</code>, <code>0.3</code></td>\n<td>PostgreSQL <code>trim_scale</code> (13 or later), Oracle <code>TO_CHAR</code> with the <code>0</code> in front of <code>.5</code>, SQLite <code>printf(&#39;%.15g&#39;)</code>, SQL Server <code>FORMAT(…, &#39;G15&#39;, &#39;en-US&#39;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LEFT, MID start at 1</td>\n<td>a start below 1 is 1: PostgreSQL and SQL Server would count from 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TRIM takes spaces, tabs and line breaks</td>\n<td>the same four characters (SQL Server 2017 or later)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Where they still differ:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>the grid</th>\n<th>the SQL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>numbers</td>\n<td>each step kept to 15 significant digits: <code>4 / 3 - 1.5</code> is <code>-0.16666666666667</code></td>\n<td>the database&#39;s: <code>-0.166666666666667</code>. The same for numbers that are exact in binary (halves, quarters) and decimal columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a number as text past 15 digits, or below 0.0001</td>\n<td><code>1e+21</code>, <code>0.00001</code></td>\n<td>PostgreSQL and Oracle write the digits, SQLite <code>1e-05</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;</code> on text</td>\n<td>the browser&#39;s order of letters</td>\n<td>the database&#39;s (<code>COLLATE &quot;C&quot;</code>, binary): code points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LEN, LEFT, MID of an emoji</td>\n<td>one character</td>\n<td>two on SQL Server, unless the column has a collation of supplementary characters (<code>_SC</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>=</code> on text with spaces at the end</td>\n<td>they count</td>\n<td>SQL Server ignores them, Oracle too between two texts of the formula itself (<code>&quot;a &quot; = &quot;a&quot;</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TRIM</td>\n<td>every white space of Unicode</td>\n<td>spaces, tabs and line breaks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>case of letters that are not ASCII</td>\n<td>JavaScript&#39;s</td>\n<td>the database&#39;s <code>LOWER()</code> - SQLite folds ASCII only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>=</code> on text in a formula</td>\n<td>the browser&#39;s collator: <code>Ａ</code> is <code>a</code>, <code>ﬁ</code> is <code>fi</code>, <code>é</code> written in two code points is <code>é</code>, a soft hyphen is nothing</td>\n<td><code>LOWER()</code> of both: the characters as they are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a text of more than 10 000 characters (<code>&amp;</code>, CONCAT, LEFT, UPPER ...)</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n<td>the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a number past ±1.8 × 10³⁰⁸ (<code>{a} * {a}</code>)</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n<td>the database&#39;s: SQLite <code>Inf</code>, the others an overflow error</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Without <code>calculated</code>, <code>toSqlWhere()</code> refuses a condition on a calculated column, as any field it does not\nknow, with a <code>TypeError</code> that names it - it is never left out, that would load more rows than the user\nfiltered for. A database that computes the column itself - a generated column, a column of a view - lists\nit in <code>columns</code> instead, with its type: <code>columns: { total: { column: &#39;total&#39;, type: &#39;number&#39; } }</code>.</p>\n<p>To filter a calculated column in the browser only, send the filter without its conditions, and let the\ngrid filter the rows that came back - <code>datasource.filter()</code> with the whole filter:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { withoutFields } from '@simple-html/grid'; // and from '@simple-html/grid/sql'\n\nconst calculated = Object.keys(gridInterface.getState().calculatedColumns);\nconst forTheBackend = withoutFields(datasource.getFilter(), calculated);</code></pre>\n<p>A condition taken out matches every row: it is left out of an AND group, and an OR group with one is\nleft out whole - <code>country = &#39;Norway&#39; OR total &gt; 100</code> can be true for any row. So the backend sends every\nrow the whole filter lets through, maybe more, and the browser filters the rest. <code>null</code> when nothing is\nleft. <code>fields</code> can be a list, a Set, or a function: <code>(field) =&gt; !Object.hasOwn(columns, field)</code> takes out\nevery field the database does not have.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-filter-to-send-getserverfilter\">The filter to send: <code>getServerFilter()</code></h2>\n<p>One call gives what a button that loads rows sends: the filter, without the conditions the backend can\nnot run. Find&#39;s <em>Filter rows</em> is not in it - it filters the rows that come back, in the browser.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const columns = { company: 'company', country: 'country', age: { column: 'age', type: 'number' as const } };\n\n// the filter, without the calculated columns and the fields not in columns\nconst filter = gridInterface.getServerFilter({ fields: Object.keys(columns) });\nawait fetch('/api/customers', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ filter }) });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>fields</code>: the fields the backend has. A condition on another field is taken out, as\n<a href=\"#guide/sql/calculated-columns\"><code>withoutFields()</code></a> takes it out - it matches every row. Default every field.</li>\n<li><code>calculated: &#39;keep&#39;</code> keeps the conditions on the calculated\ncolumns - for <code>toSqlWhere({ calculated })</code>, or a database that computes them too (default <code>&#39;strip&#39;</code>).</li>\n<li><code>null</code> is every row. What comes back is filtered by the datasource as it is: the browser drops the\nrows the backend could not.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-where-the-sql-differs-from-the-grid\">Where the SQL differs from the grid</h2>\n<p>Where SQL can do what the grid does, the SQL selects the same rows: the tests run thousands of filters\nthrough the grid&#39;s <code>Datasource</code> and a real SQLite database and compare the rows. Where it can not, or a\ndatabase decides:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>the grid</th>\n<th>the SQL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>case of letters that are not ASCII</td>\n<td>JavaScript&#39;s <code>toLowerCase()</code> / <code>toUpperCase()</code></td>\n<td>the database&#39;s <code>LOWER()</code> / <code>UPPER()</code>: by the collation (PostgreSQL), NLS (Oracle), ASCII only (SQLite: <code>&#39;Æ&#39;</code> and <code>&#39;æ&#39;</code> differ); <code>ß</code>, the Turkish <code>İ</code> can differ everywhere</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IN</code> on a number</td>\n<td>the text the formatter shows: with two decimals <code>2.004</code> is <code>&#39;2.00&#39;</code>, in <code>[2]</code></td>\n<td>the numbers: <code>2.004</code> is not 2, <code>&#39;2.00&#39;</code> is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IN</code> on a date</td>\n<td>the text the formatter shows; a <code>Date</code> in the list never matches</td>\n<td>the day or second of each value, a <code>Date</code> too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <code>datetime</code> stored as an instant, the hour the clocks go back</td>\n<td>the wall clock, which shows that hour twice: both 02:30 match</td>\n<td>the instant: the first 02:30 (<code>&#39;local&#39;</code> storage is the wall clock, the same as the grid)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datetime</code> with a <code>valueField</code></td>\n<td>to the second</td>\n<td>the columns as they are: milliseconds count</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a text <code>valueField</code></td>\n<td><code>*</code>, <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> in the other column are wildcards (with <code>literal: true</code> not: the same)</td>\n<td>no wildcards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL Server, a text <code>valueField</code></td>\n<td>trailing spaces count</td>\n<td><code>=</code> ignores them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>comparisons of text (<code>LESS_THAN</code> ...)</td>\n<td>UTF-16 code units</td>\n<td>code points: differs only for letters past U+FFFF against U+E000-U+FFFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>what is no filter</td>\n<td>an unknown operator is <code>EQUAL</code> (text) or <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>&#39;or&#39;</code> is AND, <code>&#39;abc&#39;</code> on a number is 0, a malformed <code>BETWEEN</code> is left out</td>\n<td>a <code>TypeError</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-full-example\">A full example</h2>\n<p>The client sends the filter and its time zone, the backend answers with the rows:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const response = await fetch('/api/customers', {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n    body: JSON.stringify({ filter: datasource.getFilter(), timeZone: Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone })\n});\nif (response.ok) {\n    // dates come as text in JSON: parse makes them Dates, as the fields of the grid need\n    datasource.setData(await response.json(), { parse: true });\n}</code></pre>\n<p>An Express backend with <code>pg</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import express from 'express';\nimport pg from 'pg';\nimport { isSqlFilterError, normalizeFilter, toSqlWhere, type SqlOptions } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst pool = new pg.Pool();\nconst app = express();\napp.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' }));\n\n// the fields the grid has and a user may filter on - not the tenant, not the internal columns\nconst columns: SqlOptions['columns'] = {\n    name: 'customer_name',\n    country: 'country',\n    age: { column: 'age', type: 'number' },\n    born: { column: 'born', type: 'date' },\n    active: { column: 'is_active', type: 'boolean' }\n};\n\napp.post('/api/customers', async (req, res) =&gt; {\n    const user = await authenticate(req); // yours: who is it, which tenant\n    if (!user) {\n        return res.sendStatus(401);\n    }\n    let where;\n    try {\n        // a v5 filter of an old link too\n        where = toSqlWhere(normalizeFilter(req.body.filter), {\n            dialect: 'postgres',\n            columns,\n            timeZone: typeof req.body.timeZone === 'string' ? req.body.timeZone : 'UTC',\n            firstParam: 2 // $1 is the tenant\n        });\n    } catch (error) {\n        // a filter that is no filter, a field that is no column, a filter too large, a time zone that is none\n        if (isSqlFilterError(error)) {\n            return res.status(400).json({ error: error.message });\n        }\n        throw error;\n    }\n    const { rows } = await pool.query(\n        `SELECT id, customer_name AS name, country, age, born, is_active AS active\n         FROM customer\n         WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND ${where.sql}\n         ORDER BY id\n         LIMIT 5000`,\n        [user.tenantId, ...where.params]\n    );\n    res.json(rows);\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>An invalid <code>timeZone</code> throws a <code>TypeError</code> too: the 400 says so. The time zone only moves the user&#39;s\nown days.</li>\n<li>For Oracle the same with <code>dialect: &#39;oracle&#39;</code>, the names in upper case, <code>:1</code> for the tenant (the\ntenant&#39;s value first in the array, as <code>:1</code> is first in the text) and <code>FETCH FIRST 5000 ROWS ONLY</code>;\nfor SQL Server <code>@p1</code>, <code>request.input(&#39;p1&#39;, tenantId)</code> and <code>TOP (5000)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"calculated-columns","title":"Calculated columns","html":"<p>A calculated column is a column whose value a formula computes from the other fields of its row: the\ntotal of an order (<code>{price} * {qty}</code>), the days it is late (<code>DAYS({due}, TODAY())</code>), a label from a\ncondition (<code>IF({qty} &gt;= 10, &quot;bulk&quot;, &quot;single&quot;)</code>). It is read only, and otherwise a column like any other:\nin the layout, pinned, moved, resized and hidden; sorted, filtered, grouped and summed; formatted and\ncoloured; in the detail form, copied, exported and found.</p>\n<p>The developer starts the grid with some in the config, the user adds, changes and removes them in the\ngrid config dialog. They are part of the state (<code>GridState.calculatedColumns</code>): saved with\n<code>getState()</code>, given back with <code>setState()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"calculated-columns\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-calculated-column-in-the-config\">A calculated column in the config</h2>\n<p><code>GridConfig.calculatedColumns</code> has them by field name. Put the field in the <code>layout</code> as any field, and\ngive it <code>columns</code> options - a label, an aggregate, a display format, colour rules - as any column:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ key: 'id', fields: { price: 'number', qty: 'number', due: 'date' } });\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    calculatedColumns: {\n        total: { formula: '{price} * {qty}', label: 'Total' },\n        daysLate: { formula: 'MAX(0, DAYS({due}, TODAY()))', label: 'Days late', type: 'number' },\n        size: { formula: 'IF({qty} &gt;= 10, &quot;bulk&quot;, &quot;single&quot;)' }\n    },\n    columns: {\n        total: { aggregate: 'sum', format: { number: { decimals: 2 } } },\n        daysLate: { format: { rules: [{ operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: '0', background: 'red' }] } }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['price'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['qty'], width: 60 },\n            { fields: ['total'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['daysLate'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['size'], width: 80 }\n        ]\n    }\n};\ncreateGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>the key</td>\n<td>its field name: a letter, then letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>#</code>, 64 at most - <code>total</code>, <code>days_late</code>, <code>line#</code>. Not a field of the datasource (<code>fields</code>) or of the rows, not one of the grid&#39;s own (<code>__...</code>), not a name every object has (<code>constructor</code>, <code>toString</code> ...)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formula</code></td>\n<td>the formula, see <a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns/the-formulas\">The formulas</a>. At most 1000 characters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>label</code></td>\n<td>its label, default the field name made readable (<code>days_late</code> is <em>Days Late</em>). <code>ColumnConfig.label</code> and the user&#39;s label (<code>ColumnFormat.label</code>) win over it, as over a field&#39;s label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>type</code></td>\n<td>the type of its values: <code>&#39;number&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;date&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code>. Left out, the type the formula gives; given, it must be that one - a saved state keeps saying what it holds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The type is the formula&#39;s: <code>{price} * {qty}</code> is a number, <code>IF(...)</code> the type of its two values, a\ncomparison true / false. A <code>number</code> column sorts and filters as numbers and can be summed, a <code>date</code>\ncolumn has the date formats, a <code>boolean</code> column shows checkboxes.</p>\n<p>A formula with a problem - a syntax error, a field the grid does not know, a type that does not fit -\nthrows a <code>TypeError</code> when the grid is made, or <code>setState()</code> or <code>updateConfig()</code> is given it, before\nanything changes: <code>GridConfig: calculatedColumns.total.formula: * takes a number: {name} is a text</code>.\nSo does a name it can not have, a format that does not fit its type (a date format on a number column),\nand more than 50 calculated columns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-formulas\">The formulas</h2>\n<p>A formula is text: fields in braces, numbers, texts in quotes, operators and a few functions.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>{price} * {qty}\nROUND({price} * {qty} * 1.25, 2)\nIF({status} = &quot;done&quot;, 0, MAX(0, DAYS({due}, TODAY())))\n{firstname} &amp; &quot; &quot; &amp; {lastname}\nCOALESCE({discount}, 0)</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>{field}</code></td>\n<td>the value of a field of the row: a field of the datasource, a column of the grid, another calculated column. As in the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/templates-and-links\">templates</a>: any characters but braces, <code>{first name}</code> too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>12</code>, <code>0.25</code>, <code>.5</code></td>\n<td>a number, with a <code>.</code> for the decimals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&quot;late&quot;</code>, <code>&#39;late&#39;</code></td>\n<td>a text; the quote doubled inside it: <code>&quot;say &quot;&quot;hi&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TRUE</code>, <code>FALSE</code></td>\n<td>true and false</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>+ - * /</code></td>\n<td>numbers. A minus in front: <code>-{qty}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&amp;</code></td>\n<td>joins values as text: <code>{name} &amp; &quot; (&quot; &amp; {id} &amp; &quot;)&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>= &lt;&gt; &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=</code></td>\n<td>compares two values of one type: true or false. One at a time: <code>a &lt; b &lt; c</code> is a problem, write <code>a &lt; b AND b &lt; c</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>!=</code>, <code>==</code></td>\n<td>the same as <code>&lt;&gt;</code> and <code>=</code>: <code>{shipped} != {ordered}</code> compares two columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, <code>NOT</code></td>\n<td>true / false: <code>{qty} &gt; 10 AND NOT {paid}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>( )</code></td>\n<td>brackets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The keywords and the functions are in any case (<code>and</code>, <code>If</code>). Spaces and line breaks between the parts\ndo not matter. Precedence, the first binding the loosest: <code>OR</code>, <code>AND</code>, <code>NOT</code>, the comparisons, <code>&amp;</code>,\n<code>+ -</code>, <code>* /</code>, a minus in front.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-functions\">The functions</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>function</th>\n<th>gives</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>IF(condition, a, b)</code></td>\n<td>a when the condition is true, else b - a and b of one type. Only the one taken is computed: <code>IF({qty} = 0, 0, {price} / {qty})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ROUND(x, n)</code></td>\n<td>x rounded to n decimals, halves away from 0 (<code>ROUND(2.5)</code> is 3, <code>ROUND(-2.5)</code> -3). n left out is 0, a negative n rounds to tens, hundreds: <code>ROUND(1250, -2)</code> is 1300. n a whole number from -10 to 10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ABS(x)</code></td>\n<td>x without its sign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MIN(a, b, …)</code>, <code>MAX(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the least / the greatest of numbers, or of dates. A blank is skipped: <code>MAX(0, {balance})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TODAY()</code></td>\n<td>today, at 00:00 - the day the value is read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DAYS(from, to)</code></td>\n<td>the days from the day of <code>from</code> to the day of <code>to</code>, a whole number: <code>DAYS({due}, TODAY())</code> is how many days late, negative before the due date. The time of day is left out, a day of summer time counts as one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>YEAR(d)</code>, <code>MONTH(d)</code></td>\n<td>the year, the month (1 to 12) of a date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONCAT(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the values as one text, as <code>&amp;</code> joins them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>COALESCE(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the first value that is not blank - all of one type: <code>COALESCE({qty}, 0)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ISBLANK(x)</code></td>\n<td>true when x is blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LEFT(text, n)</code>, <code>RIGHT(text, n)</code></td>\n<td>the first / the last n characters: <code>LEFT({zip}, 2)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MID(text, start, n)</code>, <code>SUBSTRING(text, start, n)</code></td>\n<td>n characters from the start-th, the first is 1: <code>MID(&quot;2026-11-24&quot;, 6, 2)</code> is <code>11</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LEN(text)</code></td>\n<td>how many characters - 0 for a blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UPPER(text)</code>, <code>LOWER(text)</code></td>\n<td>the text in upper / lower case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TRIM(text)</code></td>\n<td>the text without the spaces around it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONTAINS(text, part)</code></td>\n<td>true when the text has the part, in any case: <code>CONTAINS({note}, &quot;urgent&quot;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The functions of text take a value of any type, as <code>&amp;</code> does: a number as it is written, a date as\n<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> - <code>LEFT({born}, 4)</code> is the year as text. A count or a position that is not a whole number is\ncut to one (<code>LEFT(x, 2.9)</code> is 2 characters), a negative one is 0, a start before 1 is 1. A letter made of\ntwo UTF-16 units (an emoji) is one character.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-types\">Types</h3>\n<p>Every part of a formula has its type before it runs - number, text, date or true / false - so a formula\nthat can not work is a problem at once, not a wrong value in some rows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A field has its type in the datasource (<code>fields</code>): a <code>datetime</code> is a date, a field that is not listed\nis a text. <strong>List a number field as a number</strong> to compute with it: <code>{age} * 2</code> on a field not listed\nis the problem <em>* takes a number: {age} is a text</em>.</li>\n<li><code>+ - * /</code>, ROUND, ABS take numbers; DAYS, YEAR, MONTH dates; AND, OR, NOT, IF&#39;s condition true / false.</li>\n<li><code>=</code>, <code>&lt;&gt;</code>, the comparisons, IF&#39;s two values, COALESCE, MIN and MAX take values of one type.</li>\n<li><code>&amp;</code>, CONCAT and the functions of text take any: a number as it is written (<code>12.5</code>), a date as <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, true / false as\n<code>true</code> / <code>false</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-blanks\">Blanks</h3>\n<p>A field that is empty - null, undefined, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid date - is blank.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In a sum or a function of numbers or dates, a blank is a blank out</strong>: <code>{price} * {qty}</code> is blank\nwithout a qty, <code>DAYS({due}, TODAY())</code> without a due date. To count a blank as 0:\n<code>{price} * COALESCE({qty}, 0)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A comparison with a blank is false</strong>; <code>=</code> is true for two blanks and <code>&lt;&gt;</code> the other way round, so\n<code>{note} = &quot;&quot;</code> asks whether the note is blank. AND, OR, NOT and IF take a blank as false.</li>\n<li><code>&amp;</code>, CONCAT and the functions of text take a blank as <code>&#39;&#39;</code> (<code>LEN</code> of a blank is 0, <code>LEFT</code> of one is\nblank); MIN and MAX skip it.</li>\n<li>0 is a number, not a blank: <code>ISBLANK(0)</code> is false (the header filter takes 0 as blank, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/0-is-blank\">0 is blank</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-comparisons-and-numbers\">Comparisons and numbers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A text compares without its case</strong>: <code>{status} = &quot;late&quot;</code> is true for <code>Late</code> and <code>LATE</code>. An accent\ncounts: <code>&quot;é&quot; = &quot;e&quot;</code> is false.</li>\n<li><strong>A date compares its day</strong>: <code>{due} &lt; TODAY()</code> is true for a due date of yesterday, whatever its time.</li>\n<li><strong>A number computed keeps 15 significant digits</strong>, as a spreadsheet: <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is 0.3, and\n<code>406.09 * 10</code> 4060.9 - not the noise of binary numbers. Show it with a display format\n(<code>format: { number: { decimals: 2 } }</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-row-where-it-can-not-be-computed\">A row where it can not be computed</h3>\n<p>A division by 0, a value that is not of its field&#39;s type (a text in a number field), a number too big,\na text of more than 10 000 characters: that row has no value, its cell is empty - nothing is thrown\nwhile the grid renders. The grid config dialog&#39;s preview says why for the current row.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-the-values-are-provided\">How the values are provided</h2>\n<p>The rows are your objects in the datasource&#39;s proxy (see <a href=\"#guide/datasource\">Datasource</a>). The grid gives\nthe datasource a calculated field for each calculated column, and <strong>the proxy answers a read of it with\nits value, computed then</strong>. Nothing is stored on a row:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It is never an edit.</strong> A row is not edited or dirty by it, <code>getChanges()</code> does not have it, and so\nit is not sent to a server. <code>JSON.stringify(row)</code> and <code>Object.keys(row)</code> show your fields only.\nSetting it (<code>row.total = 5</code>) does nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>It is always the value of the row as it is</strong>: after an edit of a field it reads, undo and redo,\n<code>upsert()</code> and live data, <code>resetData()</code> - there is nothing to compute again. The cells of a changed\nrow are rendered again, the totals row follows.</li>\n<li><strong>Everything that reads a field reads it</strong>: <code>datasource.filter()</code>, <code>sort()</code>, <code>group()</code>,\n<code>aggregate()</code>, <code>getField()</code> (its type and label); the cells, copy, the CSV and Excel exports, Find,\nthe detail form.</li>\n<li><strong>The filter and the sort are not run again by an edit</strong>, as for any field: the rows stay where they\nare until the next filter or sort.</li>\n<li>A formula runs on every read: it is read and checked once, then each read walks its parts - cheap.\nAn aggregate of a calculated column is kept until a field it reads changes (or the day, with TODAY()).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The calculated fields are the datasource&#39;s container&#39;s: a second datasource on the same container\nreads them too, and may not list one of their names in its <code>fields</code>. <code>gridInterface.destroy()</code> takes\nthe grid&#39;s calculated fields back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-in-the-state\">In the state</h2>\n<p><code>GridState.calculatedColumns</code> has them by field name, as plain JSON:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridState } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst calculatedColumns: GridState['calculatedColumns'] = {\n    total: { formula: '{price} * {qty}', label: 'Total' },\n    daysLate: { formula: 'DAYS({due}, TODAY())' }\n};\ngridInterface.setState({\n    calculatedColumns,\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['price', 'qty'], width: 80 }, { fields: ['total'], width: 90 }, { fields: ['daysLate'], width: 80 }] }\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>It starts as <code>GridConfig.calculatedColumns</code> (or <code>initialState.calculatedColumns</code>), and <code>resetState()</code>\ngoes back to it. <code>updateConfig({ calculatedColumns })</code> shows new ones at once and makes them what\n<code>resetState()</code> goes back to.</li>\n<li><code>setState({ calculatedColumns })</code> replaces all of them - start from <code>getState().calculatedColumns</code> to\nchange one. The rows are filtered, sorted and grouped again with their values.</li>\n<li><strong>A calculated column left out is taken out</strong>: of the layout, the sort, the grouping, the filter (its\nconditions), the header filters, the detail form, and its format and aggregates go - it is no field\nanymore. The column chooser does not list it.</li>\n<li><strong>A saved state runs nothing</strong>: a formula is text read by the grid&#39;s own small language, see\n<a href=\"#guide/calculated-columns/security\">Security</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-in-the-grid-config-dialog\">In the grid config dialog</h2>\n<p>The Columns tab of the <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-grid-config-dialog\">grid config dialog</a> (the gear in the\nfooter):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Add calculated column</strong>, under the list of the columns, adds one at the end of the list - <code>calc_1</code>,\nlabelled <em>Calculated 1</em> - and picks it; the focus is in its formula.</li>\n<li>A calculated column has its settings at the top, in place of the label: <strong>Field name</strong> (an input\nwhile it is new; once applied it stays), <strong>Label</strong>, <strong>Formula</strong> with the <code>{ }</code> button that puts a field\nin at the caret (the columns and the calculated columns, by their labels, with a search) and the\n<strong>ƒ</strong> button: a menu of the functions in four submenus - <em>Logic</em>, <em>Numbers</em>, <em>Dates</em>, <em>Text</em> - each as\nit is written (<code>LEFT(text, n)</code>) with a tooltip of what it does. Picked, it is put in at the caret\nbetween its brackets, or around the text selected (<code>{name}</code> selected is <code>LEFT({name})</code>). The type it\ngives (<em>Gives: Number</em>), its value in the row of the preview, and its problem - at once, the column is\nmarked <em>Error</em> and Apply waits. A problem of the row (a division by 0) is said, it holds nothing back.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove calculated column</strong> takes it out of the dialog; Apply takes it out of the state and the\nlayout. A colour rule that reads one removed is a problem until it is changed.</li>\n<li>Its other settings - format, filter, colour rules - are those of its type, once it is applied with\nthat type: before that, a hint says to apply first.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply</strong> puts a new one at the end of the layout (center, 100px). <em>Reset column</em> gives a calculated\ncolumn of the config its formula back, <em>Reset all</em> the calculated columns the grid starts with.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-colour-rules-on-several-fields-and-formulas\">Colour rules on several fields, and formulas</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"#guide/formatting/colour-rules\">colour rules</a> of a column can look at the whole row: a rule of the\nkind <em>Conditions</em> tests fields of the row - a calculated column too - all of them or any; one of the\nkind <em>Formula</em> is a formula of this language that is true or false:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    calculatedColumns: { daysLate: { formula: 'DAYS({due}, TODAY())' } },\n    columns: {\n        customer: {\n            format: {\n                rules: [\n                    {\n                        conditions: [\n                            { field: 'daysLate', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: '7' },\n                            { field: 'balance', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: '0' }\n                        ],\n                        background: 'red'\n                    },\n                    { expression: '{status} = &quot;waiting&quot; AND {qty} &gt;= 10', background: '#c4b5fd' }\n                ]\n            }\n        }\n    },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['customer'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['daysLate'], width: 80 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-in-a-backend-filter-sql\">In a backend filter (SQL)</h2>\n<p>A calculated column is computed in the browser: the database has no column of it. Send the calculated\ncolumns with the filter, and <a href=\"#guide/sql/calculated-columns\"><code>toSqlWhere({ calculated })</code></a> writes each\nformula as SQL - a filter on <code>total</code> compares <code>&quot;price&quot; * &quot;qty&quot;</code> - giving the values the grid computes, on\nPostgreSQL, Oracle, SQLite and SQL Server:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// in the browser\nconst body = JSON.stringify({\n    filter: gridInterface.getServerFilter({ calculated: 'keep' }),\n    calculated: gridInterface.getState().calculatedColumns\n});</code></pre>\n<p>A formula with dates takes the day in the user&#39;s time zone (<code>timeZone</code>), as the grid does. The SQL guide\nhas what differs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-security\">Security</h2>\n<p>A formula is part of the state, and a state can come from storage, a server or another user: it is\ntaken as hostile input.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No code runs.</strong> The grid reads a formula with a tokenizer and a parser of its own into parts of a\nfixed set of kinds, checks it and runs it by a switch on the kind of each part: never <code>eval</code>,\n<code>new Function</code>, a timer of text, or a regular expression or css or html made from it.</li>\n<li><strong>Only its own names.</strong> A function is one of the twelve above, looked up in a <code>Map</code>: <code>constructor</code>,\n<code>toString</code>, <code>__proto__</code> are no functions. There is no <code>.</code> or <code>[ ]</code>: nothing reaches an object.</li>\n<li><strong>Only fields.</strong> A field is one the grid knows - of the datasource, a column, a calculated column -\nnever one of the grid&#39;s own (<code>{__KEY}</code>, <code>{__proto__}</code>), and it is read as an own property of the row:\nnothing of a prototype.</li>\n<li><strong>Limits.</strong> At most 1000 characters and 500 parts, nested at most 32 deep; a text it makes at most\n10 000 characters; a number finite. The language has no loops, and a calculated column computed from\nitself (straight or through others) is a problem: nothing can run long or recurse.</li>\n<li><strong>Data, never markup.</strong> What a formula gives is a number, a text, a date or true / false, shown as\ntext (an input&#39;s value, <code>textContent</code>): <code>&quot;&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;&quot;</code> shows as those characters.</li>\n<li><strong>The exports</strong>: a text that starts with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>@</code>, a tab or a line break is a formula to a\nspreadsheet that opens a CSV file - <code>getCsv()</code> puts a <code>&#39;</code> in front of it by default\n(<code>escapeFormulas</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/export/formulas-csv-injection\">Formulas (CSV injection)</a>), a calculated\nvalue as any. The Excel export writes text as text, never as a formula. The clipboard does not escape\nit: a copy is pasted by the user, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A field name</strong> of a calculated column is a strict pattern (a letter, then letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>#</code>),\nnever the name of a field of the rows or of every object.</li>\n<li><strong>The colours</strong> of a colour rule are a name of the palette or <code>#</code> and six hex digits - nothing else\nreaches css, and a custom colour is set as the value of a css custom property.</li>\n<li>A state with anything else throws a <code>TypeError</code> in <code>setState()</code>, before anything changes.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"import","title":"Import","html":"<p>Rows from a CSV file, an Excel workbook (.xlsx) or a spreadsheet&#39;s clipboard become new rows of the\ngrid: the user picks a file - or drops it, or pastes rows - in the import dialog, sees which field each\ncolumn goes to and the problems found, and imports them. From code, <code>importRows()</code> does the same without\nthe dialog. The counterpart of <a href=\"#guide/export\">Export</a>: what the grid exports, it imports back.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"import\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-turning-it-on\">Turning it on</h2>\n<p>The import adds rows, so it is for an editable grid where the user may add them - <code>readonly: false</code> and\nthe <a href=\"#guide/editing/edit-permissions-add-change-delete\">edit permission</a> <code>add</code> (the default). The grid&#39;s\nown menus offer it with <code>importMenu</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    importMenu: true,\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['born'], width: 110 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Header menu</strong> (right click a column label): <em>Import rows…</em> (<code>header.import</code>) opens the dialog - also\nin a grid without rows, which has no cell to right click.</li>\n<li><strong>Row menu</strong>, Paste ›: <em>As new rows…</em> (<code>row.paste.newRows</code>) opens it with the rows on the clipboard,\ntheir columns from the right clicked cell&#39;s column on. The browser may ask the user to let it read the\nclipboard; when it does not give the text, the dialog asks for a paste (Ctrl+V) into it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>importMenu</code> is <code>false</code> by default: many rows at once is for the app to allow. Without it, a button of\nyour own opens the dialog - under the same rules, it does nothing in a readonly grid or with\n<code>edit: { add: false }</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>document.getElementById('import')!.onclick = () =&gt; gridInterface.openImport();\n\n// with a file of your own: an &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt;, a drop zone\ndeclare const file: File;\ngridInterface.openImport(file);\n\n// with text: CSV, or tab separated as a spreadsheet copies it\ngridInterface.openImport('Name\\tBorn\\r\\nAnn\\t2001-05-17\\r\\n');</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-dialog\">The dialog</h2>\n<p>The dialog has the filter editor&#39;s look: it is moved by its title bar, resized from its edges, Esc closes\nit. From the top:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Where the rows come from</strong>: <em>Choose file…</em>, a file dropped on the dialog, or rows pasted into it.</li>\n<li><strong>The options</strong>: <em>First row is a header</em> - on when the first row names a field; the separator of a CSV\nfile, found from its first lines (<em>Found: Semicolon</em>) or picked; the sheet of a workbook of more\nsheets; the decimal mark when a number field is picked, the order of dates with <code>/</code> when a date field\nis (see <a href=\"#guide/import/the-values\">The values</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The columns</strong>: the first rows of the file, with a picker over each column - a field, or <em>Skip</em>. A\nline over them says how they were matched: <em>by name</em>, from the header, or <em>by position</em>, from a field.\nA field picked for a second column is taken off the first. A cell with a problem is marked, its\ntooltip says what is wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>The rows and their problems</strong>: how many rows, how many with problems, the first problems in words -\n<em>Row 3, Age: Not a number: abc</em>. The row is the file&#39;s: a sheet&#39;s row number as Excel shows it, a CSV\nfile&#39;s record. <em>Rows with problems</em>: imported (and marked) or skipped.</li>\n<li><strong>Import N rows</strong> adds them and closes. Cancel, the ✕ and Esc close without.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every text comes from the <a href=\"#guide/texts/import-dialog\">texts</a>, the Norwegian ones are in the Translation\nsample.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-columns\">The columns</h2>\n<p>Each column of the file goes to a field, or is skipped:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>By name</strong>, with a header: a header cell that is the label of a column as the grid shows it, the name\nof a field or the label of a datasource field - in any case, spaces around it ignored. A column whose\nname is none of them is skipped, and so is a second column of the same field. The dialog turns the\nheader on by itself when the first row names a field; then the columns can be in any order.</li>\n<li><strong>By position</strong>, without: the columns go to the fields of the layout left to right, from the first -\nfrom the right clicked cell&#39;s column for <em>Paste › As new rows…</em>, as a paste puts a block of cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A field can be imported when a user could type into it on a new row: any field of the datasource but a\nreadonly column (<code>ColumnConfig.readonly</code>). The pickers list the fields of the layout, then the other\ncolumns of the config (hidden ones). A lookup column (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>) takes the label its cells\nshow, as a paste does, and fills the fields of its <code>set</code>. The <code>isReadonly</code> hook is not asked: it decides\nabout the rows there are.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-values\">The values</h2>\n<p>Each value is read as typing reads it - through the field&#39;s formatter, the label of a lookup column as\nthe value of its row - with what a file has that typing has not:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>the import reads</th>\n<th>a problem</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>the text as it is - <code>=1+1</code> is the text <code>=1+1</code>, never a formula</td>\n<td>none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>number</td>\n<td><code>1234.5</code>, <code>-5</code>, <code>3e2</code>, with spaces or <code>&#39;</code> between the digits; the decimal mark of the option (default each column&#39;s own: <code>,</code> for <code>numberComma</code>), the other mark between groups of three digits: <code>1 234,5</code> and <code>1.234,5</code> with a decimal comma, <code>1,234.5</code> with a dot. The other mark once and not between groups is the decimal mark: <code>12,5</code> is 12.5 also with a dot, as typing reads it. A number cell of a workbook as it is</td>\n<td><code>12 kg</code>, <code>1.2.3</code>, <code>=1+1</code>, <code>abc</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>date, datetime</td>\n<td><code>2026-12-31</code>, <code>31.12.2026</code>, <code>31/12/2026</code> (or <code>12/31/2026</code> with <em>Month first</em>), each with a time <code>13:45</code> or <code>13:45:12</code> after it - local time; ISO with <code>Z</code> or an offset is that instant. Any other form as the field&#39;s formatter reads it, when it writes the date back as the text. A date cell of a workbook as it is</td>\n<td><code>31.02.2026</code>, <code>20.11.26</code>, <code>tomorrow</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code>, <code>1</code> / <code>0</code>, what the formatter writes for true and false (<code>yes</code> / <code>no</code>) - in any case. A boolean cell as it is</td>\n<td><code>maybe</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A value that is not read is never guessed from a part of it: the field is left empty, and the problem is\nlisted. An empty cell is an empty field. A row with nothing in it is left out.</p>\n<p>The validation runs on each row before it is added, with the rules of every field of the datasource (see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>): a required field the file does not have is a problem of every\nrow, <em>Required</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-rows-added\">The rows added</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New rows</strong>, at the end (<code>datasource.addNewRows()</code>): <code>getChanges().newEntities</code> lists them, the save\nsends them as rows the user added. The first one is selected, the grid scrolls to it. When grouped they\ngo into their groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Validated</strong>: a rule broken shows on the cell, as after an edit. A value that was not read is in the\nrow&#39;s error (<code>__rowError</code>, <em>Not imported: Age: Not a number: abc</em>): its row number shows it, with the\nexclamation mark - until <code>acceptChanges()</code>, or <code>setErrors()</code> from a save replaces it.</li>\n<li><strong>One step of the undo history</strong>: Ctrl+Z takes the rows out again - it marks them for deletion, as\nDelete takes out a new row, so they are in none of the lists of <code>getChanges()</code> - and Ctrl+Y brings\nthem back.</li>\n<li>With <em>Rows with problems: Skip them</em> (<code>invalidRows: &#39;skip&#39;</code>) a row with a problem is not added.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-from-code\">From code</h2>\n<p><strong><code>importFile(file)</code></strong> does it in one call: it reads an Excel workbook (.xlsx) or a CSV file - the first\nsheet, unless <code>sheet</code> names another - and adds its rows, the first row that is not empty the header.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const file: File;\n\nconst result = await gridInterface.importFile(file); // the first sheet, a header row\nawait gridInterface.importFile(file, { sheet: 'Customers', invalidRows: 'skip' }); // another sheet</code></pre>\n<p>It takes <code>sheet</code> and <code>separator</code> of <code>readImportFile()</code> and the options of <code>importRows()</code> below, <code>header</code>\n<code>true</code> by default. The rows are new rows, one step of the undo history, as the dialog adds them.</p>\n<p><code>importRows()</code> imports rows of cells - an array of rows, each an array of text, numbers, booleans, <code>Date</code>\nor <code>null</code>. <code>readImportFile()</code> reads a file into such rows:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { readImportFile } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const file: File;\n\nconst table = await readImportFile(file); // CSV or .xlsx, the first sheet\nconst result = gridInterface.importRows(table.rows, { header: true });\nconsole.log(`${result.rows.length} rows imported, ${result.skipped} skipped`);\nfor (const problem of result.problems) {\n    console.log(`row ${problem.row + 1}, ${problem.field}: ${problem.message}`);\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// no header: the fields by position, null skips a column\ngridInterface.importRows(\n    [\n        ['Ann', 'ignored', '1 234,5'],\n        ['Bob', '', '7']\n    ],\n    { fields: ['name', null, 'balance'], decimalMark: ',', invalidRows: 'skip' }\n);</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>header</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>the first row that is not empty names the columns, it is not imported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fields</code></td>\n<td>by the header&#39;s names, or by position</td>\n<td>the field of each column, <code>null</code> skips it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>decimalMark</code></td>\n<td>each number column&#39;s own</td>\n<td><code>&#39;.&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;,&#39;</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/import/the-values\">The values</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dateOrder</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;DMY&#39;</code></td>\n<td>a date with <code>/</code>: <code>&#39;DMY&#39;</code> 31/12/2026 or <code>&#39;MDY&#39;</code> 12/31/2026</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>invalidRows</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;import&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;skip&#39;</code> leaves out the rows with problems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The result has the new rows, every problem (<code>{ row, field, value, kind, message }</code> - <code>kind</code> is <code>&#39;value&#39;</code>\nfor a value that was not read, <code>&#39;rule&#39;</code> for a validation rule) and how many rows were skipped. An option\nit does not know throws a <code>TypeError</code>. Code is not stopped by <code>readonly</code> or the edit permissions, as\n<code>addNewEmpty()</code> is not: check them yourself when the rows come from the user.</p>\n<p><code>readImportFile(file, { sheet, separator })</code> reads a sheet by its index or name (default the first) and\na CSV file with the separator given (default the one found). It gives the format, the names of the\nsheets, the separator and the rows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-files\">The files</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CSV</strong> (<code>.csv</code>, <code>.tsv</code>, <code>.txt</code>): RFC 4180 - a field in double quotes can hold the separator, line\nbreaks and <code>&quot;&quot;</code> for a quote. The separator is <code>,</code>, <code>;</code> or tab, the one that splits the first lines into\nthe same number of fields. UTF-8 (with or without a byte order mark), UTF-16 with its byte order mark\n(Excel&#39;s <em>Unicode text</em>), else Windows-1252 - what Excel saves as <em>CSV</em> in a Western language, so æøå\ncome through. Every field is text.</li>\n<li><strong>Excel</strong> (<code>.xlsx</code>): the cells of one sheet, typed - text (shared, inline, rich text), numbers,\nbooleans, errors (<code>#N/A</code>, as text), a <code>Date</code> for a number with a date or time format (the 1900 and the\n1904 date systems). A formula is never run: its value as Excel saved it is read. Reading needs the\nbrowser&#39;s <code>DecompressionStream(&#39;deflate-raw&#39;)</code> - Chrome 103, Firefox 113, Safari 16.4.</li>\n<li><strong>Not read</strong>: <code>.xls</code> (Excel 97-2003, a binary format) and a workbook protected by a password - save\nthem as .xlsx or CSV; <code>.ods</code>, <code>.numbers</code> and anything else.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What a file is, is read from its first bytes, not from its name.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-file-is-not-trusted\">A file is not trusted</h2>\n<p>A file a user picks can be anything - made to hurt, or just broken. The import reads it within limits,\nand says in words what it does not read (<code>ImportFileError</code>, its <code>problem</code>):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>limit (<code>IMPORT_LIMITS</code>)</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>fileSize</code></td>\n<td>20 MB, checked before a byte is read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>unzippedSize</code></td>\n<td>100 MB: all the parts of a workbook read, inflated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rows</code></td>\n<td>100 000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columns</code></td>\n<td>1 000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cells</code></td>\n<td>1 000 000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A zip bomb</strong> - kilobytes that inflate to gigabytes - is not inflated: a part larger than the limit is\nrefused by its size, and the inflating stops at the size the ZIP gives, so a ZIP that lies about it is\nnever held in memory. What comes out must have the size and the CRC-32 the ZIP gives.</li>\n<li><strong>XML</strong> is read with the browser&#39;s <code>DOMParser</code>, as XML: no html, no scripts, nothing of the file is put\nin the page as markup - the dialog shows every value as text. A part with a <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE</code> is not read at\nall: its entities could expand (&quot;billion laughs&quot;), and a workbook never has one. XML that is not\nwell-formed is a damaged file.</li>\n<li><strong>A CSV file</strong> stops at the limits while it is read: a million empty lines does not become a million\nrows first.</li>\n<li><strong>Formulas stay text</strong>: <code>=HYPERLINK(...)</code> in a cell is imported as the text it is, the grid never runs\nit. When the rows are exported again the CSV export escapes it (see\n<a href=\"#guide/export/formulas-csv-injection\">Formulas</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n"}]