[{"id":"getting-started","title":"Getting started","html":"<p>A grid shows rows of data in columns, like a spreadsheet. You can filter, sort and group the rows,\nfind a text, copy cells and export to Excel - and, when your app allows it, edit the rows. This page\nshows the parts of a grid and how to move around in it.</p>\n<p>On a Mac, <strong>Ctrl</strong> is <strong>⌘</strong> (Cmd) and <strong>Alt</strong> is <strong>⌥</strong> (Option).</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"getting-started\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-know-the-parts-of-a-grid\">Know the parts of a grid</h2>\n<p>From the top:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The grouping panel</strong>: a thin strip above the column headers. Drag a column header to it to group\nthe rows, see <a href=\"#user-guide/grouping\">Grouping</a>. Your app may hide it.</li>\n<li><strong>The column headers</strong>: the name of each column. Click one to sort, see <a href=\"#user-guide/sorting\">Sorting</a>.\nAn arrow shows the sort, with a small number when you sort by more than one column.</li>\n<li><strong>The filter row</strong>: a box under each header. Type in it to filter the rows, see\n<a href=\"#user-guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>. A small symbol in the box (<code>=</code>, <code>≥</code>, <code>*a*</code> ...) shows how it\ncompares.</li>\n<li><strong>The column numbers</strong>: a row of numbers under the filters. Click one to select the whole column,\nsee <a href=\"#user-guide/selecting-and-copying\">Selecting and copying</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The rows</strong>: one line per record. Each cell is an input box, so you can put the cursor in it.</li>\n<li><strong>The row numbers</strong>: at the left of each row. Click one to select the row. A small icon on the\nnumber shows a row that is new, changed, marked for deletion or has an error. The corner above\nthem selects all rows.</li>\n<li><strong>The footer</strong>, under the rows:<ul>\n<li>at the left, the <strong>views</strong> button, if your app shows it: the name of the view you are on, see\n<a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a></li>\n<li>the <strong>row count</strong>, such as <code>12/500</code>: 12 rows shown of 500</li>\n<li>the <strong>filter</strong> as small chips, with <strong>✕ Clear</strong> - or the button <strong>No filter</strong> when nothing is\nfiltered</li>\n<li>at the right, a <strong>summary</strong> (count, sum, average ...) of the cells you have selected</li>\n<li>the <strong>gear ⚙</strong>, which opens <strong>Grid config</strong>: how each column shows its values, the detail form\nand more, see <a href=\"#user-guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some columns can be pinned to the left or right side, so they stay in place while you scroll\nsideways. You can also move, resize, hide and pin columns yourself, see <a href=\"#user-guide/columns\">Columns</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-scroll-through-the-rows\">Scroll through the rows</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>mouse wheel</strong> scrolls the rows up and down.</li>\n<li>A <strong>trackpad</strong> swipe sideways, or <strong>Ctrl+Shift</strong> with the wheel, scrolls the columns sideways.</li>\n<li>The <strong>scrollbars</strong> at the right and the bottom work as usual.</li>\n<li>On a <strong>touch screen</strong>, swipe with one finger. Let go while moving and it coasts on; touch to stop it.</li>\n<li>From the keyboard: <strong>PageUp</strong> / <strong>PageDown</strong> scroll a page of rows, <strong>Ctrl+Home</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+End</strong> go\nto the first / last cell. See <a href=\"#user-guide/keyboard\">Keyboard</a> for all keys.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only the rows on screen are drawn, so even many thousands of rows scroll fast.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-pick-a-cell\">Pick a cell</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Click</strong> a cell to put the cursor in it. Its row becomes the current row.</li>\n<li><strong>Tab</strong> / <strong>Shift+Tab</strong> go to the next / previous cell.</li>\n<li><strong>Alt</strong> with an arrow key moves to the cell beside, above or below. The plain arrow keys move the\ncursor inside the cell&#39;s text, as in a spreadsheet cell you are editing.</li>\n<li>Whether you can change a value depends on your app: many grids are read only, or have only some\ncolumns you can edit. See <a href=\"#user-guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-open-a-right-click-menu\">Open a right-click menu</h2>\n<p>Right-click a part of the grid to see what you can do there. Most menu items are in submenus, marked\nwith <code>›</code>: point at one to open it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Right-click on</th>\n<th>Menu</th>\n<th>What is in it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a cell or a row number</td>\n<td>row menu</td>\n<td>Undo / Redo, Select, Copy, Paste, Clear, Delete, Filter, View, Size, Export</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a column header</td>\n<td>header menu</td>\n<td>Pin, Hide column, Remove sorting, Column chooser…, Size, Format, Column settings…, Keyboard shortcuts…, Aggregate, Grouping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a filter box</td>\n<td>filter menu</td>\n<td>Filter (the operator, Match case, Blank, Clear Filter, Advanced Filter…) and a list of values or a date and number range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a column number</td>\n<td>column menu</td>\n<td>Select, Copy column, Copy header names</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Menu items you can not use right now are greyed out. Items for editing are only there in a grid you\ncan edit. Your app may also hide items or add its own.</p>\n<p>From the keyboard, <strong>Shift+F10</strong> (or the menu key) opens the menu of the cell, header or filter that\nhas the focus. The arrow keys move in the menu, <strong>Enter</strong> picks, <strong>Esc</strong> closes it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-use-a-touch-screen\">Use a touch screen</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Long press</strong> (hold your finger still for half a second) on a cell, a row number, a header, a\nfilter box or a column number: the same menu a right-click opens.</li>\n<li><strong>Tap</strong> a header to sort. Hold a header for a moment, then drag it to move the column.</li>\n<li><strong>Tap</strong> a row number to select or unselect the row. A finger has no Shift or Ctrl key: use\n<strong>Select ›</strong> in the row menu to select several rows or a range of cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-read-the-tooltips\">Read the tooltips</h2>\n<p>Hold the mouse pointer still over a button or a symbol to see a short tip on what it does. A tooltip\nalso shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the error message of a cell or row with a value that is not valid</li>\n<li>the original value of a changed cell (the small marker in its corner)</li>\n<li>the rest of the text of a cell that has more lines than it shows</li>\n<li>the operator of a filter box, such as <code>Operator: Contains (F4)</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-see-all-keyboard-shortcuts\">See all keyboard shortcuts</h2>\n<p>Press <strong>Ctrl+/</strong> (⌘/ on a Mac) anywhere in the grid, or <strong>?</strong> where the key types nothing (a read only\ncell, a column header). You can also pick <strong>Keyboard shortcuts…</strong> in the header menu.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Keyboard shortcuts</strong> window lists every key of the grid in groups. Type in its search box to\nfind one, such as <code>undo</code> or <code>F3</code>. <strong>Esc</strong> closes it. It only lists keys that work in your grid. See\nalso <a href=\"#user-guide/keyboard\">Keyboard</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-know-what-your-app-decides\">Know what your app decides</h2>\n<p>The grid can do much more than a given app may want. The app you use decides, among other things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>whether you can edit, add or delete rows - or only look at them</li>\n<li>which columns are read only</li>\n<li>whether <strong>Import rows…</strong> is in the menus, see <a href=\"#user-guide/import\">Import</a></li>\n<li>whether the views button is in the footer, see <a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a></li>\n<li>whether the grouping panel, the footer or the gear ⚙ are shown</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a menu item or button in this guide is missing in your grid, your app has turned it off.</p>\n"},{"id":"filtering","title":"Filtering","html":"<p>A filter shows only the rows you want to see. The quickest way is to type in the filter row under the\ncolumn headers. For more, right-click a filter box for its menu, or build any filter in the filter\neditor. The footer always shows what the rows are filtered by.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"filtering\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-on-a-value\">Filter on a value</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click the filter box under a column header.</li>\n<li>Type a value.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Enter</strong>, or leave the box with <strong>Tab</strong> or a click. The rows are filtered.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Filters on several columns work together: a row must match all of them. To remove a column&#39;s filter,\nempty its box and press <strong>Enter</strong>.</p>\n<p>How the value is compared depends on the column:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Text</strong>: the whole text, upper or lower case alike. <code>ann</code> finds <code>Ann</code> and <code>ANN</code>, but not <code>Anna</code>.\nUse a <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering/use-wildcards\">wildcard</a> or the operator <em>Contains</em> to search inside the text.</li>\n<li><strong>Number</strong>: the number or more (<code>≥</code>). <code>30</code> shows 30 and up.</li>\n<li><strong>Date</strong>: that day or later (<code>≥</code>). Type the date as the placeholder in the box shows it, or click\nthe box and pick a day in the calendar, then <strong>Use</strong>. <strong>Clear</strong> removes the filter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-change-the-operator\">Change the operator</h2>\n<p>The small symbol in the filter box is the operator: how the value is compared.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Operator</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>=</code></td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>*a*</code></td>\n<td>Contains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>≠</code></td>\n<td>Not Equal</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>!*a*</code></td>\n<td>Does not contain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;</code> <code>≤</code></td>\n<td>Less than (or equal)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>a*</code></td>\n<td>Begins with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&gt;</code> <code>≥</code></td>\n<td>Greater than (or equal)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>*a</code></td>\n<td>Ends with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a..b</code></td>\n<td>Between</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>To change it, click the symbol (or press <strong>F4</strong> in the filter box) and pick one. You can also\nright-click the filter box and pick <strong>Filter › Operator ›</strong>. The filter runs again with the new\noperator. Text columns have the text operators, number and date columns the others.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-as-you-type\">Filter as you type</h2>\n<p>Normally a filter runs when you press <strong>Enter</strong>. With <strong>Filter as you type</strong> it runs by itself after a\nshort pause. Turn it on or off per column, at the bottom of the operator list (click the symbol), or in\nthe filter menu under <strong>Filter ›</strong>. A ✓ shows it is on. A number or date filter waits until what you\ntyped is a whole number or date.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-use-wildcards\">Use wildcards</h2>\n<p>In a text filter:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>*</code> or <code>%</code> stands for any text, also none: <code>ann*</code> begins with &quot;ann&quot;, <code>*ann*</code> has &quot;ann&quot; in it.</li>\n<li><code>_</code> stands for exactly one character: <code>p_mp</code> finds <code>pump</code> and <code>pimp</code>.</li>\n<li><code>\\</code> before <code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> or <code>\\</code> makes it a plain character: <code>50\\%*</code> finds <code>50% off</code> but not <code>500</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-match-case\">Match case</h2>\n<p>A text filter ignores upper and lower case. To match the case exactly, right-click the filter box\nand pick <strong>Filter › Match case</strong> (a ✓ shows it is on), or pick <strong>Match case</strong> at the bottom of the\noperator list. The box then shows <strong>Aa</strong> before the operator: <code>Ann</code> no longer finds <code>ANN</code>. Your app\ncan make a column always match case - a code column, <code>AB-1</code> is not <code>ab-1</code>; <strong>Match case</strong> is then greyed\nout with the hint <em>always</em>, and in the grid config dialog its hint says <em>set by the application</em>.</p>\n<p>Some long text columns (notes, descriptions) can only be filtered on their text: Equal, Not equal,\nContains, Does not contain, Begins with, Ends with and Blank. Their filter menu has no list of values, and a\ncalculated column can not use them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-find-blank-values\">Find blank values</h2>\n<p>Right-click the filter box and pick <strong>Filter › Blank › Is blank</strong> or <strong>Is not blank</strong>. In a number\ncolumn, 0 counts as blank too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-as-the-cells-show-it\">Filter as the cells show it</h2>\n<p>A date or number column can show its values in a format of its own (see\n<a href=\"#user-guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>): a week, a month, <code>1 234,5</code>. Type the filter the same way the cells\nshow it, and the grid filters on the whole period:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>2026-W47</code> in a column shown as weeks is that week, Monday to Sunday.</li>\n<li><code>2026-11</code> is November, <code>2026 Q4</code> the fourth quarter, <code>2026</code> the year.</li>\n<li><code>1 234,5</code> or <code>50 %</code> in a number column is read with the column&#39;s own separators.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The operator works on the whole period: with <code>=</code> you get only week 47, with <code>≥</code> week 47 and later,\nwith <code>≠</code> every other week. The number and date columns start with <code>≥</code>, so change the operator to <code>=</code>\nto see one period only. This is called <strong>Filter as shown</strong> in <strong>Grid config</strong>, where it can be\nturned off for a column (see <a href=\"#user-guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-a-range-with-between\">Filter a range with Between</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Change the operator to <strong>Between</strong> (<code>a..b</code>).</li>\n<li>Type both ends with <code>..</code> between them: <code>10..20</code>, or <code>2026-01-01..2026-03-31</code>. Both ends are\nincluded. Leave one out for an open end: <code>10..</code> is 10 or more, <code>..20</code> is 20 or less.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In a date column, a click in the box opens two months side by side: click the first day, then the\nlast day, then <strong>Use</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-use-the-filter-menu\">Use the filter menu</h2>\n<p>Right-click a filter box. Under <strong>Filter ›</strong> are the operator, Match case, Filter as you type, Blank,\n<strong>Clear Filter</strong> (this column) and <strong>Clear All Filters</strong> (every column). Under it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A number or date column</strong> has a range block: pick <code>=</code>, <code>≠</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>≤</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>≥</code> or <strong>Between</strong>,\ntype a value - or <strong>From</strong> and <strong>To</strong> - and press <strong>Apply</strong> (or <strong>Enter</strong>). A click in a date\ninput opens the calendar.</li>\n<li><strong>A date column</strong> also has quick dates, see <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering/pick-a-quick-date\">below</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A text column</strong> has a list of its values, see <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering/check-values-in-a-list\">below</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cancel</strong>, <strong>Esc</strong> or a click outside closes the menu and leaves the filter as it was.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-pick-a-quick-date\">Pick a quick date</h2>\n<p>In the filter menu of a date column, click one of <strong>Today</strong>, <strong>Yesterday</strong>, <strong>This week</strong>, <strong>Last\nweek</strong>, <strong>This month</strong>, <strong>Last month</strong>, <strong>This quarter</strong>, <strong>Last quarter</strong>, <strong>This year</strong>, <strong>Last year</strong>,\n<strong>Last 7 days</strong>, <strong>Last 30 days</strong> or <strong>Next 7 days</strong> - and on some columns <strong>Overdue</strong> (every day\nbefore today). The rows are filtered at once. Hold the pointer over a button to see its dates.</p>\n<p>A quick date stays relative: a filter on <em>This month</em> saved in a view shows the current month every\ntime you open it. You can also type the name in the filter box, such as <code>today</code> or <code>last week</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-check-values-in-a-list\">Check values in a list</h2>\n<p>The filter menu of a text column lists the values its cells show, each with the number of rows, like\nthe filter of a spreadsheet.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Uncheck the values you do not want, or uncheck <strong>Select all</strong> and check the ones you want.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blank</strong>, at the top in italics, stands for the empty cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Search values</strong> narrows the list. Check <strong>Filter on the text: contains &quot;…&quot;</strong> under it to filter\non the text you typed instead of on the values checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Only values in filtered rows</strong>: while other columns are filtered, the list has only their rows&#39;\nvalues. Uncheck it to list the values of every row.</li>\n<li>The list shows the first 100 values. Search to find the others.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-build-a-filter-in-the-filter-editor\">Build a filter in the filter editor</h2>\n<p>The filter editor (<strong>Filter Editor</strong>) builds any filter, with AND and OR. Open it with <strong>Filter ›\nAdvanced Filter…</strong> in the filter menu, a click on the filter chips in the footer, or <strong>No filter</strong> in\nthe footer.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each line is a condition: pick a <strong>Field</strong>, an <strong>Operator</strong>, and type a <strong>Value</strong>. The arrows beside\nthe value switch to comparing with another column of the same row.</li>\n<li><strong>Aa</strong> after a text value turns Match case on for that condition.</li>\n<li>A group is a set of conditions with <strong>AND</strong> (all must match) or <strong>OR</strong> (one is enough). <strong>+\nCondition</strong> adds a condition to it, <strong>+ Group</strong> a group inside it.</li>\n<li>The trash can deletes a condition. The icons on a group&#39;s line wrap it in a new group or delete it.</li>\n<li><strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong> (<strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> and <strong>Ctrl+Y</strong>, <strong>⌘Z</strong> and <strong>⇧⌘Z</strong> on a Mac) take back what you\ndid in the editor, step by step. They do not change the grid&#39;s filter until you apply.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply</strong> filters and keeps the window open, <strong>Apply &amp; close</strong> filters and closes it, <strong>Cancel</strong>\ncloses it and keeps the filter as it was.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-read-and-clear-the-filter-in-the-footer\">Read and clear the filter in the footer</h2>\n<p>The footer shows the filter as chips, such as <code>Age between 10 and 20</code> or <code>Order date = This month</code>.\nClick the chips to change the filter in the filter editor. <strong>✕ Clear</strong> removes the whole filter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-on-a-cells-value\">Filter on a cell&#39;s value</h2>\n<p>Right-click a cell and open <strong>Filter ›</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Replace filter</strong> (<em>all</em>) shows only the rows with this value, and removes every other filter.</li>\n<li><strong>Add to filter</strong> (<em>and</em>) keeps the filter and adds this value to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On an empty cell it filters on blank values.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-special-columns\">Filter special columns</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A checkbox column</strong> has a checkbox in its filter box. Click once: checked rows only. Again:\nunchecked rows only (empty ones too). A third time: no filter.</li>\n<li><strong>A column with a text built from several fields, or a link</strong>, such as <code>Ann Berg (12)</code>, is\nfiltered on the text you see: <code>*(12)*</code> finds it. Turn off <strong>Filter as shown</strong> in <strong>Grid config</strong> to\nfilter on the column&#39;s own value instead.</li>\n<li><strong>A column where you pick from a list</strong> (such as a country code shown as <code>Norway</code>) is filtered on\nthe name you see: type <code>Norway</code>, not <code>NO</code>, and the value list lists the names. The filter editor\nshows the codes, which is what the rows hold.</li>\n<li><strong>A calculated column</strong> (see <a href=\"#user-guide/calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</a>) is filtered like any\nother column of its type: a number, a date or a text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To see only the rows you have changed, pick <strong>View › Changed rows only</strong> in the row menu. To see only\nrows with a certain text in any column, use <strong>Filter rows</strong> in the find bar, see <a href=\"#user-guide/find\">Find</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"sorting","title":"Sorting","html":"<p>Sort the rows by clicking a column header. You can sort by one column, or by several: first by one,\nthen by the next within equal values. The sort stays when the rows are filtered or reloaded.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"sorting\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-sort-by-a-column\">Sort by a column</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click the column header (the name of the column). The rows are sorted A to Z, smallest to\nlargest, oldest to newest.</li>\n<li>Click it again to sort the other way. Each click after that turns the order around again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>An arrow in the header shows the sort: pointing up for ascending, down for descending. Clicking a\nheader sorts by that column only - the sort there was is replaced.</p>\n<p>On a touch screen, tap the header. A header you hold still for a moment is dragged instead, to move\nthe column (see <a href=\"#user-guide/columns\">Columns</a>).</p>\n<p>From the keyboard: put the focus on the header (<strong>Alt+ArrowUp</strong> from its filter box) and press\n<strong>Enter</strong> or <strong>Space</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-sort-by-several-columns\">Sort by several columns</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click the first column&#39;s header.</li>\n<li>Hold <strong>Shift</strong> and click the next column&#39;s header. Its rows are sorted within the equal values of\nthe first column.</li>\n<li>Shift+click more headers to add more columns.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each sorted header shows its arrow with a small number: 1 for the first column of the sort, 2 for the\nnext, and so on. Shift+click on a column that is in the sort already turns its order around and keeps\nits place. From the keyboard, press <strong>Shift+Enter</strong> or <strong>Shift+Space</strong> on a header.</p>\n<p>There is no Shift key on a touch screen, so there a tap always sorts by one column.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-remove-the-sort\">Remove the sort</h2>\n<p>Right-click any column header and pick <strong>Remove sorting</strong>. The arrows go away. The rows keep the order\nthey have until they are filtered again; then they come back in the order they were loaded.</p>\n<p>When the rows are grouped, the grouped columns stay sorted: grouping needs it (see\n<a href=\"#user-guide/grouping\">Grouping</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-know-how-values-are-sorted\">Know how values are sorted</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blank cells</strong> come first when you sort ascending, and last when you sort descending. A 0 is a\nnumber, not blank.</li>\n<li><strong>Text</strong> is sorted alphabetically, with upper and lower case together: <code>apple</code> and <code>Apple</code> end up\nnext to each other.</li>\n<li><strong>Dates</strong> are sorted by day. A date and time column is sorted by the full time.</li>\n<li><strong>Yes/no</strong> (checkbox) columns sort unchecked before checked.</li>\n<li><strong>A column where you pick from a list</strong> - such as a country code shown as <code>Norway</code> - is sorted by\nthe name you see: Denmark, Norway, Sweden. Not by the codes behind them.</li>\n<li><strong>A column shown in a format of its own</strong> (a week, a month, a number with separators) is sorted by\nits real value, the date or the number - not by the text shown.</li>\n<li><strong>A column that shows a text built from several fields</strong>, such as <code>Berg, Ann (12)</code>, is sorted by the\ntext you see: by the last name here. While you group by it, its groups are in the order of its own\nvalue.</li>\n<li><strong>A calculated column</strong> (see <a href=\"#user-guide/calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</a>) is sorted as its type:\na number, a date or a text.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-sort-while-grouped\">Sort while grouped</h2>\n<p>When the rows are grouped (see <a href=\"#user-guide/grouping\">Grouping</a>), the groups always stay together:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Click the header of the grouped column to turn the order of the groups around.</li>\n<li>Click another column&#39;s header to sort the rows inside each group.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-keep-a-sort\">Keep a sort</h2>\n<p>The sort is part of the grid&#39;s state. When your app keeps the state, or you save a view (see\n<a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a>), the sort comes back the next time.</p>\n"},{"id":"grouping","title":"Grouping","html":"<p>Grouping puts rows with the same value together under a group row, such as all the orders of one\ncustomer. You can group by one column, or by several for groups inside groups. Group rows can show\ntotals of the rows in them, and a totals row under all the rows can show the totals of everything you\nhave filtered.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"grouping\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-group-by-a-column\">Group by a column</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the grouping panel: the thin strip above the column headers.</li>\n<li>Drag a column header up into the panel and drop it there.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The rows are grouped by that column. The panel shows a chip with the column&#39;s name. On a touch\nscreen, hold the header still for a moment, then drag it.</p>\n<p>Your app may hide the grouping panel. Then you can not group the rows yourself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-group-inside-groups\">Group inside groups</h2>\n<p>Drag a second column header into the panel, after the first chip. Each group then has groups of its\nown: customers, and within each customer the order dates, say.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Change the order of the levels</strong>: drag a chip in the panel to another place. Dropped on the left\nside of a chip, it goes before that chip.</li>\n<li>A column header dropped on the left side of a chip is put in at that place, too.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-open-and-close-groups\">Open and close groups</h2>\n<p>Each group row shows the column&#39;s name, the value and the number of rows: <code>Country: Norway (12)</code>.\nThe rows with an empty value are one group at the top, named <code>blank</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Groups start closed. Click the <strong>+</strong> box on a group row to open it, <strong>-</strong> to close it.</li>\n<li>From the keyboard, press <strong>Enter</strong> or <strong>Space</strong> on a group row&#39;s box.</li>\n<li>To open or close all groups at once, right-click a column header and pick <strong>Grouping › Expand all</strong>\nor <strong>Grouping › Collapse all</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lines at the left of the rows show which group each row belongs to, like a tree.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-group-by-week-month-quarter-or-year\">Group by week, month, quarter or year</h2>\n<p>A date column groups by day. To group by a longer period, show the column in that format first:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Right-click the date column&#39;s header.</li>\n<li>Pick <strong>Format ›</strong> and one of <strong>Week, ISO</strong>, <strong>Week, US</strong>, <strong>Month</strong>, <strong>Quarter</strong> or <strong>Year</strong>.</li>\n<li>Group by the column as above.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>There is now one group per period: <code>Due: 2026-W47 (8)</code>. Pick another format and the rows are grouped\nagain. See <a href=\"#user-guide/formatting\">Formatting</a> for the formats.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-show-totals-in-the-group-rows\">Show totals in the group rows</h2>\n<p>Right-click the header of the column you want a total of, and open <strong>Aggregate ›</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Shows</th>\n<th>Columns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Sum</strong></td>\n<td>the sum of the numbers</td>\n<td>number columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Average</strong></td>\n<td>the average of the numbers</td>\n<td>number columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Min</strong></td>\n<td>the smallest number</td>\n<td>number columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Max</strong></td>\n<td>the largest number</td>\n<td>number columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Count</strong></td>\n<td>how many cells are not empty</td>\n<td>every column</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Each is turned on or off with a click; a ✓ shows it is on. You can turn on several for one column.</p>\n<p>Every group row then shows the value in that column, over all the rows of the group (also of the\ngroups inside it, open or closed). Each value has its own line with a short label: <code>Sum</code>, <code>Avg</code>,\n<code>Min</code>, <code>Max</code> or <code>Count</code>. Your app may turn the totals in the group rows off.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-show-a-totals-row\">Show a totals row</h2>\n<p>Right-click a column header and pick <strong>Aggregate › Totals row</strong>. A row with <strong>Total</strong> at its left\nappears under the rows, with the sum, average and so on of each column that has one turned on. It\ncounts every row of the filter, also the rows of closed groups - so it changes when you filter. The\ntotals row is not part of an export to Excel or CSV.</p>\n<p>The totals row also works when the rows are not grouped.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-sort-the-groups\">Sort the groups</h2>\n<p>The groups are sorted by their value, A to Z. Click the header of the grouped column to turn the\norder of the groups around. Click another header to sort the rows inside each group. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/sorting/sort-while-grouped\">Sorting</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-remove-grouping\">Remove grouping</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One level</strong>: click the <strong>✕</strong> on its chip in the grouping panel. From the keyboard, <strong>Tab</strong> to the\n✕ and press <strong>Enter</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>All grouping</strong>: right-click a column header and pick <strong>Grouping › Clear all</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-know-a-few-details\">Know a few details</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A column where you pick from a list (such as a country code shown as <code>Norway</code>) groups by the code\nthe rows hold: <code>Country: NO (12)</code>. The groups are in the order of the names.</li>\n<li>A group row can not be selected, edited or deleted.</li>\n<li>Find (<strong>Ctrl+F</strong>) only searches the rows of open groups, see <a href=\"#user-guide/find\">Find</a>.</li>\n<li>The grouping, the open groups and the totals are kept with the grid&#39;s state, and in a saved view\n(see <a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"columns","title":"Columns","html":"<p>You can change how the columns are laid out: their order, their width, which ones stay in place at\nthe side, and which ones are shown at all. You do it in the header, with the mouse or a finger, and\nin the header menu - right click a column&#39;s label. The grid keeps what you change while you work; if\nyour app saves views, a view keeps it too, see <a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"columns\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-move-a-column\">Move a column</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Press on the column&#39;s label and drag it. On a touch screen, hold the label still a moment, then drag.</li>\n<li>While you drag, the header shows where the column will go.</li>\n<li>Let go:<ul>\n<li>on the <strong>left or right edge</strong> of another label: the column goes before or after that column</li>\n<li>on the <strong>top or bottom edge</strong> of another label: the two are stacked in one column, one above the other</li>\n<li>on the <strong>middle</strong> of another label: the two columns swap places</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Drag past the left or right side of the grid and the columns scroll. Drop the label on the panel\nabove the header and the rows are grouped on it instead, see <a href=\"#user-guide/grouping\">Grouping</a>.</p>\n<p>A short click on a label sorts on it, see <a href=\"#user-guide/sorting\">Sorting</a>. It only becomes a drag once the\nmouse has moved a little.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-change-the-width-of-a-column\">Change the width of a column</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drag the edge</strong> of the column&#39;s label: the right edge, or the left edge for a column pinned right.\nA column is at least 25 pixels wide.</li>\n<li><strong>Double click the edge</strong> to make the column as wide as its content, like AutoFit in Excel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-fit-columns-to-their-content\">Fit columns to their content</h2>\n<p>Right click a label and open <strong>Size ›</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Fit column width</td>\n<td>this column as wide as its widest text and its label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fit all columns</td>\n<td>every column as wide as its content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reset column width</td>\n<td>this column back to the width it started with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reset all column widths</td>\n<td>every column back to the width it started with</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Right click a cell and open <strong>Size ›</strong> for <strong>Fit column to this cell</strong>: the column as wide as the text\nof that one cell. The same menu makes rows taller for cells with several lines (<strong>Fit row height</strong>,\n<strong>Fit all rows</strong>, <strong>Reset row height</strong>, <strong>Reset all row heights</strong>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-pin-a-column-to-the-left-or-right\">Pin a column to the left or right</h2>\n<p>A pinned column stays in place while the other columns scroll sideways - handy for a name or a number\nyou always want to see.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Right click the column&#39;s label.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>Pin ›</strong> and pick one:<ul>\n<li><strong>Pin left</strong> or <strong>Pin right</strong>: the column moves to that side.</li>\n<li><strong>Pin left (copy)</strong> or <strong>Pin right (copy)</strong>: a copy is pinned to that side, and the column also\nstays where it was.</li>\n<li><strong>Unpin</strong>: the column goes back among the columns that scroll.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You can also drag a label onto the edge of a pinned column&#39;s label: it is pinned beside it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-hide-a-column\">Hide a column</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Right click the column&#39;s label.</li>\n<li>Pick <strong>Hide column</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In a column with two stacked fields, only the field you right clicked is hidden. Hiding a column\nchanges nothing in the data: the column is just not shown.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-show-a-hidden-column\">Show a hidden column</h2>\n<p>Use the column chooser:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Right click any label and pick <strong>Column chooser…</strong>.</li>\n<li>A list opens with every column, by its label. Type in <strong>Search</strong> to find one by its name.</li>\n<li>Drag the column from the list into the header, to the place where you want it. On a touch screen,\nhold it still a moment first.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Close</strong> when you are done.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-rename-a-column\">Rename a column</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the <strong>Column chooser…</strong> from the header menu.</li>\n<li>Click the ✎ at the right of the column (it shows when you point at the column).</li>\n<li>Type the new label and press <strong>Enter</strong>. <strong>Esc</strong> keeps the old one.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Leave the label empty to give the column its own label back. The new label is used everywhere the\ngrid names the column: the header, the menus, the filter and the exports. You can also set it in\n<strong>Grid config</strong>, see <a href=\"#user-guide/formatting/the-label-and-the-placeholder\">Formatting</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-select-columns-with-the-column-numbers\">Select columns with the column numbers</h2>\n<p>Under the header filters there is a row of numbers, 1, 2, 3 ... one for each column (if your app shows\nit). Click them to select whole columns:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a click</td>\n<td>selects that column only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+click</strong> (<strong>⌘</strong>+click on a Mac)</td>\n<td>adds the column, or takes it out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shift+click</strong></td>\n<td>selects the columns from the one you clicked last up to this one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>no column selected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>On a touch screen a tap adds or removes a column.</p>\n<p>Right click a column number for the column menu:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Select ›</strong>: <strong>This column</strong>, <strong>Columns from N to here</strong>, <strong>All columns</strong>, <strong>Clear column selection</strong><ul>\n<li>what Ctrl and Shift do, also for a finger.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Copy column</strong>: the column you right clicked, every row shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy selected columns</strong>: the selected columns, every row shown. <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> on a column number does\nthe same.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy header names</strong>, <strong>Copy header name</strong>, <strong>Copy selected header names</strong>: only the labels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The selection is cleared when you move, pin, hide or add a column. See also\n<a href=\"#user-guide/selecting-and-copying\">Selecting and copying</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-reset-the-columns\">Reset the columns</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Size › Reset column width</strong> and <strong>Reset all column widths</strong> in the header menu give the columns\ntheir first widths back.</li>\n<li>To get the whole layout back as it started - order, pinning and hidden columns - pick <strong>Standard</strong>\nin the views menu at the left end of the footer, if your app shows it. Note that Standard also puts\nback the sorting, the filters and the rest of the grid as it started. See <a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"formatting","title":"Formatting","html":"<p>Formatting changes how the cells look, not what they hold: a date shown as a week, a number with two\ndecimals, a text shown as a link, a negative balance in red. Copy, paste and the exports still use the\nreal value, and a cell you edit shows the value as it is.</p>\n<p>Most of it is done in the <strong>Grid config</strong> dialog. Open it with the gear at the right end of the footer,\nor right click a column&#39;s label and pick <strong>Column settings…</strong> to open it on that column. The\n<strong>Columns</strong> tab lists the columns at the left; pick one, and its settings are at the right in four\ntabs: <strong>General</strong>, <strong>Display</strong>, <strong>Header filter</strong> and <strong>Colours</strong>. A preview under them shows how the\ncell of the current row (or the first row) will look. Click <strong>Apply</strong> to use the changes, <strong>Apply &amp;\nclose</strong> to use them and close, <strong>Cancel</strong> to drop them. <strong>Reset column</strong> and <strong>Reset all</strong> go back to\nhow the columns started. See also <a href=\"#user-guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"formatting\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-pick-a-date-or-number-format\">Pick a date or number format</h2>\n<p>The quickest way: right click the label of a date or number column and open <strong>Format ›</strong>. Each entry\nshows an example.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Date columns</strong>: <strong>ISO</strong> (2026-11-22), <strong>Norwegian</strong> (22.11.2026), <strong>Norwegian, short year</strong>, <strong>US</strong>\n(11/22/2026), <strong>UK</strong> (22/11/2026), <strong>Short month name</strong>, <strong>Month name</strong>, <strong>Day and month name</strong>.\nThen parts of the date: <strong>Day of week</strong>, <strong>Week, ISO</strong> (2026-W47), <strong>Week, US</strong>, <strong>Month</strong> (2026-11),\n<strong>Quarter</strong> (2026 Q4), <strong>Year</strong>, and <strong>Days until</strong> (how many days from today: 1 is tomorrow, -1\nyesterday).</li>\n<li><strong>Date and time columns</strong> also have <strong>ISO with time</strong>, <strong>ISO with seconds</strong>, <strong>Norwegian with time</strong>,\n<strong>Norwegian with seconds</strong>, <strong>Time</strong> and <strong>Time with seconds</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Number columns</strong>: <strong>Norwegian</strong> (1 234 567,891), <strong>English</strong> (1,234,567.891), <strong>Swiss</strong>\n(1&#39;234&#39;567.891) or <strong>Plain</strong> (1234567.891). Then <strong>Decimals ›</strong> (<strong>As is</strong>, or 0 to 4), <strong>Thousands\nseparator ›</strong>, <strong>Decimal mark ›</strong>, and <strong>Percent</strong> (the number times 100, with %).</li>\n<li><strong>Default</strong> goes back to the column&#39;s normal format.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same list is the <strong>Format</strong> picker on the <strong>Display</strong> tab of Grid config. Text and checkbox columns\nhave no format.</p>\n<p>The header filter takes text as the cells show it: in a column shown as weeks, type <code>2026-W47</code> to get\nthat week. See <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-set-a-format-for-every-column-of-a-type\">Set a format for every column of a type</h2>\n<p>On the <strong>Defaults</strong> tab of Grid config, <strong>Formats by type</strong> has a picker for <strong>Date</strong>, <strong>Date and\ntime</strong> and <strong>Number</strong>. Every column of that type without a format of its own shows it. A column&#39;s own\nformat wins; its picker then says, for example, <code>Default: Norwegian</code>. <strong>Reset formats</strong> goes back to\nhow they started.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-show-blanks-as-0-or-0-as-blank\">Show blanks as 0, or 0 as blank</h2>\n<p>For a number column, on the <strong>Display</strong> tab:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Show blank as 0</strong>: an empty cell shows 0.</li>\n<li><strong>Show 0 as blank</strong>: a 0 shows as an empty cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only one of them can be on. The value itself does not change.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-show-a-column-as-checkboxes\">Show a column as checkboxes</h2>\n<p>A text or number column that holds a flag, such as <code>1</code> / <code>0</code> or <code>Y</code> / <code>N</code>, can show checkboxes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>On the <strong>Display</strong> tab, tick <strong>Show as checkbox</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fill in the <strong>Checked value</strong> (for example <code>1</code>) and the <strong>Unchecked value</strong> (for example <code>0</code>). An\nempty unchecked value empties the cell.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A click on a checkbox writes the other value. The header filter becomes a checkbox too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-show-a-column-as-links\">Show a column as links</h2>\n<p>A text or number column can link to a web page, an email address or a phone number:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>On the <strong>Display</strong> tab, tick <strong>Show as link</strong>.</li>\n<li>In <strong>Address</strong>, write where the link goes. It starts with <code>https://</code>, <code>http://</code>, <code>mailto:</code> or\n<code>tel:</code>, and takes values of the row in braces: <code>https://crm.example.com/customer/{id}</code>,\n<code>mailto:{email}</code>. Or write one field alone, such as <code>{website}</code>, when that field holds the whole address.</li>\n<li><strong>Open in a new tab</strong> is on at first; untick it to open the link in the same page.</li>\n<li><strong>Text shown</strong> is the text of the link. Leave it empty for the cell&#39;s own text, or write a\ntemplate: <code>{company} ({id})</code> shows the company and the id. <code>{value}</code> is the cell&#39;s own value,\n<code>{field}</code> the value of another field of the row.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>{ }</strong> button beside the input opens a list of the fields, with a search: pick one and it is put\nin where the cursor is, so you do not need to know the field names. A problem, such as an address that\ndoes not start right, is shown under the input at once, and <strong>Apply</strong> waits until it is fixed.</p>\n<p>A click on the link&#39;s text opens it; a click beside the text selects the cell. With the keyboard,\n<strong>Alt+Enter</strong> opens the link. A row whose address is not valid shows the text without a link.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-show-text-of-several-lines\">Show text of several lines</h2>\n<p>A text column with notes or addresses can be <strong>Multiline</strong> (the <strong>General</strong> tab). The cell shows the\nfirst line, its tooltip shows more, and the text is edited in a bigger editor. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/editing/edit-text-of-several-lines\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-label-and-the-placeholder\">The label and the placeholder</h2>\n<p>On the <strong>General</strong> tab:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Label</strong>: the name of the column wherever the grid shows it. Leave it empty for the column&#39;s own.</li>\n<li><strong>Placeholder</strong>: a grey text shown in an empty cell, as a hint of what to fill in. Or tick <strong>Use the\ncolumn label</strong> to show the label there.</li>\n<li><strong>Read only</strong>: the column&#39;s cells can not be edited. When it is checked and greyed out, the\napplication has locked the column: it is never edited.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-colour-rules\">Colour rules</h2>\n<p>Colour rules colour a cell by its value or its row: red text for a negative balance, an orange\nbackground for orders that wait. Every column can have them, on the <strong>Colours</strong> tab.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Click <strong>Add rule</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pick the kind of <strong>Condition</strong>:<ul>\n<li><strong>Value</strong>: the cell&#39;s own value, with an operator - <strong>Equal</strong>, <strong>Greater than</strong>, <strong>Contains</strong>,\n<strong>Between</strong>, <strong>Is blank</strong> ... - and a value, typed as you type it in the header filter. A date\ncolumn can compare with a typed date or period, or a quick date such as <strong>This week</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditions</strong>: one or more conditions on fields of the row, each with its <strong>Field</strong>, operator\nand value. <strong>Add condition</strong> adds one; with two or more, pick <strong>All of them</strong> or <strong>Any of them</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Formula</strong>: a formula that is true or false, such as <code>{balance} &lt; 0 AND {status} = &quot;late&quot;</code>. The\n<strong>{ }</strong> and <strong>ƒ</strong> buttons insert a field or a function, see <a href=\"#user-guide/calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</a>.\nUnder it you see whether it is true in the preview&#39;s row.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Pick a <strong>Text colour</strong> and a <strong>Background</strong> from the swatches - <strong>None</strong> or one of 14 colours -\nor <strong>+</strong> for a custom colour. Tick <strong>Bold</strong> for bold text.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>One rule is open at a time. The others are a line each, with a sample of their colours (<strong>Abc</strong>) and\nwhat they match; click a line to open that rule, <strong>▾</strong> to close it. <strong>Move up</strong> and <strong>Move down</strong>\nchange the order, <strong>Remove rule</strong> removes one.</p>\n<p><strong>The first rule that matches wins.</strong> Put the most specific rules at the top. A cell no rule matches\nkeeps the grid&#39;s colours. A cell with a validation error, or a match of Find, shows without the rule&#39;s\ncolours so it stands out.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-bars-colour-scales-and-icons\">Data bars, colour scales and icons</h2>\n<p>A number column has more on its <strong>Colours</strong> tab, in four panels: <strong>Colour rules</strong>, <strong>Data bar</strong>,\n<strong>Colour scale</strong> and <strong>Icons</strong>. One panel is open at a time. Each closed panel shows what it has, such\nas <strong>2 rules</strong>, <strong>On</strong> or <strong>Off</strong>; a red square means a problem in it. Click a panel to open it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data bar</strong>: tick <strong>Data bar</strong> to draw a bar in each cell, as long as the value&#39;s place among the\ncolumn&#39;s numbers - negative values to the left of 0. Pick its <strong>Colour</strong>. <strong>Min</strong> and <strong>Max</strong> set the\nends of the bar; leave them empty for <strong>Automatic</strong>, from the rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Colour scale</strong>: tick <strong>Colour scale</strong> to colour the background from the <strong>Lowest</strong> value to the\n<strong>Highest</strong>, with a <strong>Middle</strong> colour if you want three (<strong>None</strong> gives two). <strong>Min</strong>, <strong>Midpoint</strong>\nand <strong>Max</strong> are optional.</li>\n<li><strong>Icons</strong>: tick <strong>Icons</strong> and pick an <strong>Icon set</strong>: <strong>Arrows</strong>, <strong>Traffic lights</strong>, <strong>Flags</strong> or\n<strong>Symbols</strong>. The high icon is shown from <strong>High from</strong>, the middle one from <strong>Middle from</strong>, the low\none below. <strong>Thresholds</strong> are in <strong>% of the range</strong> or <strong>Values</strong>. <strong>Icon</strong> puts it <strong>Before the\nvalue</strong> or <strong>After the value</strong>. <strong>Reverse</strong> gives the low values the high icon, for a column where\nlower is better.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Five sample cells, from the lowest value of the rows to the highest, show how the column will look.\nAutomatic ends follow the rows the filter lets through. A colour rule&#39;s background wins over a colour\nscale; the bar and the icons show with any rule. The Excel export keeps all of them.</p>\n"},{"id":"calculated-columns","title":"Calculated columns","html":"<p>A calculated column shows a value worked out from the other fields of the row, with a formula: the\ntotal of an order (<code>{price} * {qty}</code>), how many days an invoice is late (<code>DAYS({due}, TODAY())</code>), a\nword from a condition (<code>IF({qty} &gt;= 10, &quot;bulk&quot;, &quot;single&quot;)</code>).</p>\n<p>A calculated column works like any other column: you can move, pin, hide, sort, filter, group, format\nand colour it, and it is copied and exported. It is <strong>read only</strong>: you can not type in it, and it is\nnever saved as data - the value is worked out again whenever the row changes.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"calculated-columns\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-add-a-calculated-column\">Add a calculated column</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <strong>Grid config</strong> (the gear at the right end of the footer).</li>\n<li>On the <strong>Columns</strong> tab, click <strong>Add calculated column</strong> under the list of columns. A new column,\n<strong>Calculated 1</strong>, is added and picked.</li>\n<li>Fill in:<ul>\n<li><strong>Field name</strong>: the column&#39;s name inside the grid - a letter, then letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>#</code>,\nsuch as <code>total</code>, <code>days_late</code> or <code>line#</code>. It can not be changed once applied.</li>\n<li><strong>Label</strong>: the name shown in the header, such as <em>Total</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Formula</strong>: what to calculate, such as <code>{price} * {qty}</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Apply</strong>. The column is added at the end of the columns.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Once it is applied, it gets the settings of its type - a format and colour rules - like any column.\nBefore that, a hint says to apply first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-write-a-formula\">Write a formula</h2>\n<p>A formula is made of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fields in braces</strong>: <code>{price}</code> is the value of the field <em>price</em> in the row. It can be any field,\nalso another calculated column.</li>\n<li><strong>Numbers</strong>: <code>12</code>, <code>0.25</code> - with a <code>.</code> for the decimals.</li>\n<li><strong>Texts in quotes</strong>: <code>&quot;late&quot;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Operators</strong> and <strong>functions</strong>, below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You do not have to remember the field names. The <strong>{ }</strong> button beside the formula opens a list of the\nfields, by their labels, with a search: pick one and it is put in where the cursor is.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ƒ</strong> button opens a menu of the functions in four groups - <strong>Logic</strong>, <strong>Numbers</strong>, <strong>Dates</strong> and\n<strong>Text</strong>. Point at a function to see what it does. Pick it and it is put in with the cursor between its\nbrackets; if you had selected some text, the function is put around it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-operators\">The operators</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n<th>example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>+ - * /</code></td>\n<td>add, subtract, multiply, divide</td>\n<td><code>{price} * {qty}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&amp;</code></td>\n<td>joins values as one text</td>\n<td><code>{firstname} &amp; &quot; &quot; &amp; {lastname}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>=</code> <code>&lt;&gt;</code> <code>!=</code></td>\n<td>equal, not equal (<code>!=</code> is the same as <code>&lt;&gt;</code>)</td>\n<td><code>{status} = &quot;done&quot;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&gt;=</code></td>\n<td>less than, at most, greater than, at least</td>\n<td><code>{qty} &gt;= 10</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AND</code> <code>OR</code> <code>NOT</code></td>\n<td>both, either, not</td>\n<td><code>{qty} &gt; 10 AND NOT {paid}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>( )</code></td>\n<td>brackets, to group</td>\n<td><code>({price} - {cost}) * {qty}</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Compare two values at a time: write <code>{a} &lt; {b} AND {b} &lt; {c}</code>, not <code>{a} &lt; {b} &lt; {c}</code>. A text compares\nwithout its case: <code>{status} = &quot;late&quot;</code> is also true for <em>Late</em>. Function names and AND, OR, NOT can be\nwritten in any case.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-functions\">The functions</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>function</th>\n<th>what it gives</th>\n<th>example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>IF(condition, a, b)</code></td>\n<td>a when the condition is true, else b</td>\n<td><code>IF({qty} &gt;= 10, &quot;bulk&quot;, &quot;single&quot;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>COALESCE(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the first value that is not blank</td>\n<td><code>COALESCE({discount}, 0)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ISBLANK(x)</code></td>\n<td>true when x is blank</td>\n<td><code>ISBLANK({email})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ROUND(x, n)</code></td>\n<td>x rounded to n decimals; a negative n rounds to tens, hundreds</td>\n<td><code>ROUND({price} * 1.25, 2)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ABS(x)</code></td>\n<td>x without its minus</td>\n<td><code>ABS({balance})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MIN(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the smallest of numbers or dates</td>\n<td><code>MIN({stock}, 100)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MAX(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the largest of numbers or dates</td>\n<td><code>MAX(0, {balance})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TODAY()</code></td>\n<td>today&#39;s date</td>\n<td><code>{due} &lt; TODAY()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DAYS(from, to)</code></td>\n<td>the days from one date to another, negative when <em>to</em> is earlier</td>\n<td><code>DAYS({due}, TODAY())</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>YEAR(date)</code></td>\n<td>the year of a date</td>\n<td><code>YEAR({born})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MONTH(date)</code></td>\n<td>the month of a date, 1 to 12</td>\n<td><code>MONTH({ordered})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONCAT(a, b, …)</code></td>\n<td>the values as one text, as <code>&amp;</code> joins them</td>\n<td><code>CONCAT({city}, &quot;, &quot;, {country})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LEFT(text, n)</code></td>\n<td>the first n characters</td>\n<td><code>LEFT({zip}, 2)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>RIGHT(text, n)</code></td>\n<td>the last n characters</td>\n<td><code>RIGHT({phone}, 4)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MID(text, start, n)</code></td>\n<td>n characters from the start-th, the first is 1</td>\n<td><code>MID({code}, 3, 2)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SUBSTRING(text, start, n)</code></td>\n<td>the same as MID</td>\n<td><code>SUBSTRING({code}, 3, 2)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LEN(text)</code></td>\n<td>how many characters - 0 for a blank</td>\n<td><code>LEN({comment})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UPPER(text)</code></td>\n<td>the text in upper case</td>\n<td><code>UPPER({country})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LOWER(text)</code></td>\n<td>the text in lower case</td>\n<td><code>LOWER({email})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TRIM(text)</code></td>\n<td>the text without the spaces around it</td>\n<td><code>TRIM({name})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONTAINS(text, part)</code></td>\n<td>true when the text has the part, in any case</td>\n<td><code>CONTAINS({note}, &quot;urgent&quot;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The text functions also take a number or a date: a date is read as <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, so <code>LEFT({born}, 4)</code>\nis the year as text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-blank-values\">Blank values</h2>\n<p>A field with no value is blank. What happens to it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sums and number or date functions give a blank</strong>: <code>{price} * {qty}</code> is empty when <em>qty</em> is empty.\nTo count a blank as 0, write <code>{price} * COALESCE({qty}, 0)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A comparison with a blank is false</strong>. <code>{note} = &quot;&quot;</code> asks whether the note is blank.</li>\n<li><strong>Joining text</strong> takes a blank as nothing, and <strong>MIN</strong> and <strong>MAX</strong> skip it.</li>\n<li>0 is a number, not a blank.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-check-what-the-formula-gives\">Check what the formula gives</h2>\n<p>Under the formula the dialog shows, while you type:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gives:</strong> the type of the result: <strong>Gives: Number</strong>, <strong>Text</strong>, <strong>Date</strong>, or <strong>Checkbox</strong> for a\nformula that is true or false (the column then shows checkboxes).</li>\n<li><strong>In this row:</strong> the value in the row of the preview - the current row, or the first row shown.\n<strong>(blank)</strong> means it is empty there.</li>\n<li><strong>A problem</strong>, at once, such as <em>No field {qty}</em>, or <code>* takes a number: {name} is a text</code> when a\nfield of text is used in a sum. The column\nis marked <strong>Error</strong> in the list, and <strong>Apply</strong> waits until it is fixed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A problem of one row only, such as <em>Divides by 0 in this row: no value</em>, is shown too, but does not\nstop you: that row&#39;s cell is just empty. A number field must be a number for sums: if the grid treats\na field as text, ask whoever set up your app.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-change-or-remove-a-calculated-column\">Change or remove a calculated column</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>To <strong>change</strong> one, pick it in the list on the <strong>Columns</strong> tab, change its label or formula, and\nclick <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>\n<li>To <strong>remove</strong> one, pick it and click <strong>Remove calculated column</strong>, then <strong>Apply</strong>. A colour rule that\nused it shows a problem until you change the rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Reset all</strong> gives back the calculated columns the grid started with - and the settings every\ncolumn started with.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can also use formulas in <a href=\"#user-guide/formatting/colour-rules\">colour rules</a>, with the kind <strong>Formula</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"editing","title":"Editing","html":"<p>If your app allows it, you can change values right in the cells, add rows and delete rows. Your app\ndecides what you may do: some grids are read only, some let you add rows but not change the ones that\nare there. The grid keeps track of every change until it is saved, and <strong>saving is done by your app</strong> -\nusually with a Save button of its own. Until then, every change can be undone.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"editing\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-edit-a-cell\">Edit a cell</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click the cell. It is ready to type in at once, with the cursor where you clicked.</li>\n<li>Type. The value is written to the row as you type: a number column takes a number, a date column a\ndate.</li>\n<li>Move on with <strong>Enter</strong> (the cell below), <strong>Tab</strong> (the next cell) or <strong>Shift+Tab</strong> (the cell before).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In a cell the arrow keys, <strong>Home</strong> and <strong>End</strong> move the cursor in the text. To move to another cell\nwith the arrows, hold <strong>Alt</strong> (<strong>⌥</strong> on a Mac): <strong>Alt+↓</strong> is the cell below. There is no <strong>Esc</strong> to\ncancel what you typed: press <strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> (<strong>⌘Z</strong> on a Mac) instead.</p>\n<p>A cell with stripes (red in the normal look) is <strong>read only</strong>: you can not change it. A whole column can\nbe read only, or only some cells, as your app decides.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-edit-text-of-several-lines\">Edit text of several lines</h2>\n<p>A cell with notes or an address can hold several lines. It shows the first line, with a small marker\nwhen there are more; point at it to see more lines in its tooltip.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Press <strong>Enter</strong> or <strong>F2</strong>, double click the cell, or just start typing. A bigger editor opens over\nthe cell.</li>\n<li>In the editor <strong>Enter</strong> starts a new line.</li>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+Enter</strong> (<strong>⌘Enter</strong> on a Mac) saves and closes. So do <strong>Tab</strong>, a click outside the editor\nand the <strong>✕</strong> under it, at the right. <strong>Esc</strong> closes it without saving.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In a read only cell the editor opens too, to read the whole text; <strong>Esc</strong> closes it. To make rows tall\nenough for all their lines, right click a cell and pick <strong>Size › Fit row height</strong> or <strong>Fit all rows</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-pick-a-date\">Pick a date</h2>\n<p>Click a date cell and a date picker opens under it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Click a day. Use the arrows beside the month and the year to go to another month or year.</li>\n<li>For a date with a time, set the hours (<strong>HH</strong>) and minutes (<strong>MM</strong>).</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Use</strong>. <strong>Now</strong> goes to the current date and time, <strong>Clear</strong> empties the cell.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You can also type the date in the cell, as the cells show dates.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-pick-a-value-from-a-list\">Pick a value from a list</h2>\n<p>Some columns take their value from a list - a customer, a country, a status. The cell shows a name,\nand is not typed in. To open the list:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>click the small triangle at the right of the cell, or</li>\n<li>press <strong>Alt+↓</strong>, <strong>F4</strong>, <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Space</strong>, or just start typing - what you type searches the list.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Type to search, use <strong>↑</strong> and <strong>↓</strong> to move, and <strong>Enter</strong> or a click to pick. <strong>Tab</strong> picks and moves on\nto the next cell. <strong>Esc</strong> closes the list and keeps the value as it was. A long list shows its first\n500 entries: type to find the rest. Some lists are a small grid of their own, with more columns and a\nfilter: type in its filter, then pick a row.</p>\n<p><strong>Delete</strong> in the cell empties it. Picking a value may fill in other fields of the row too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-tick-a-checkbox\">Tick a checkbox</h2>\n<p>Click a checkbox, or press <strong>Space</strong> in it, to check or uncheck it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-add-a-row\">Add a row</h2>\n<p>The grid has no add button of its own: if your app allows adding rows, it has a button for it. A new\nrow has a <strong>+</strong> on its row number. Rows can also be added from a spreadsheet or a file, see\n<a href=\"#user-guide/import\">Import</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-delete-rows\">Delete rows</h2>\n<p>Deleting a row only <strong>marks it for deletion</strong>: it stays in the grid, struck through, with a trash can\non its row number, until your app saves. Until then you can take the mark off.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right click a cell and open <strong>Delete ›</strong>: <strong>Row</strong> marks the row you right clicked, <strong>Rows (selected\nrows)</strong> marks the selected rows, <strong>Undo (selected rows)</strong> takes the mark off again.</li>\n<li>Or click a row number to select the row and press <strong>Delete</strong> or <strong>Backspace</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> also takes a delete back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> (<strong>⌘Z</strong> on a Mac) undoes the last change, and takes you to the cell it changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+Y</strong> or <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Z</strong> (<strong>⇧⌘Z</strong> on a Mac) does it again.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One undo takes back one step: what you typed in one cell, a whole paste, a fill down, a clear or a\ndelete. The last 100 steps are kept. After a save there is nothing to undo.</p>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell) starts with <strong>Undo / Redo ›</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Undo last change</td>\n<td>as <strong>Ctrl+Z</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Redo</td>\n<td>as <strong>Ctrl+Y</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undo cell edit</td>\n<td>puts back the original value of the cell you right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undo row edits</td>\n<td>puts back every value of the row you right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undo row edits (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the same for the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-see-what-you-changed\">See what you changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>edited cell</strong> has a small triangle in its top left corner. Point at it to see <strong>Original value:</strong>\nand the value it had. Click the triangle to put that value back.</li>\n<li>The <strong>row number</strong> shows the state of the row: <strong>+</strong> a new row, a pencil an edited row, a trash can a\nrow marked for deletion, <strong>!</strong> a row with an error. Point at it for the details.</li>\n<li>To see only the changed rows before you save, right click a cell and pick <strong>View › Changed rows\nonly</strong>. Pick it again to see every row.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-fill-down\">Fill down</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+D</strong> (<strong>⌘D</strong> on a Mac) fills down, as in a spreadsheet. In a range of cells, the top row is\ncopied into the rows under it. In a single cell, the value of the cell above is copied in.</li>\n<li>Or drag the <strong>fill handle</strong>, the small square at the bottom right corner of a range, down over the\nrows to fill. The values of the range are repeated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The row menu has <strong>Paste › Fill down</strong> too. Read only cells are skipped.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-paste-a-block-of-cells\">Paste a block of cells</h2>\n<p>Copy cells in Excel, Google Sheets or the grid, click a cell and press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong> (<strong>⌘V</strong> on a Mac).\nThe block is pasted from that cell down and to the right. One value pasted over a selected range of\ncells fills every cell of it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read only cells are skipped.</li>\n<li>A paste adds no rows: rows past the last row are left out. To paste rows as new rows, use <strong>Paste ›\nAs new rows…</strong> in the row menu, if your app allows it, see <a href=\"#user-guide/import\">Import</a>.</li>\n<li>A whole paste is one step: one <strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> takes it back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>See <a href=\"#user-guide/selecting-and-copying\">Selecting and copying</a> for how to select ranges of cells.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-clear-cells\">Clear cells</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Select a range of cells and press <strong>Delete</strong> or <strong>Backspace</strong>: every cell in it is emptied.</li>\n<li>Or right click a cell and open <strong>Clear ›</strong>: <strong>Clear cell</strong>, <strong>Clear cell (selected rows)</strong> for that\ncolumn in the selected rows, or <strong>Clear range</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Read only cells are skipped. In a single cell <strong>Delete</strong> just deletes text, as in any text box. In a\nnumber cell, deleting all the text may give 0; <strong>Clear › Clear cell</strong> leaves it truly empty.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-errors-in-what-you-typed\">Errors in what you typed</h2>\n<p>Your app can have rules for the values: a field that must be filled in, a number that must be at least\n1 or a whole number, a text of at most 50 characters, one of a list of values, an email address, a\nvalue no other row has. You can not change them, but you can see them: in the grid config dialog, on a\ncolumn&#39;s <strong>General</strong> tab, under <strong>Validation</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>empty cell that must be filled in</strong> has stripes (blue in the normal look) until it has a value.\nOnly spaces count as empty.</li>\n<li>A <strong>value that must be unique</strong>: when two rows have it, both cells show the error <strong>Another row has\nthe same value</strong>. Change one of them, or delete the row, and it goes from both.</li>\n<li>A <strong>cell with an error</strong> has a red border. Point at it to read the message, such as <strong>Required</strong> or\n<strong>Must be at least 1</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>row number</strong> of a row with an error has a red bar and <strong>!</strong>. Point at it to see every error of\nthe row.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fix the value and the error goes away. Your app may not save rows with errors, and it can show errors\nfrom the server on the row numbers too.</p>\n"},{"id":"selecting-and-copying","title":"Selecting and copying","html":"<p>You can select whole rows, whole columns, or a block of cells, much as in a spreadsheet. What you\nselect can be copied to Excel, Google Sheets or Word, and cells copied there can be pasted into the\ngrid. Rows, columns and cells are selected separately: selecting cells does not select their rows.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"selecting-and-copying\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-select-rows\">Select rows</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One row</strong>: click the row number at the left of the row. The row turns a selected colour.</li>\n<li><strong>One more row</strong>: hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> (<strong>⌘</strong> on a Mac) and click another row number. Ctrl+click a\nselected row to take it out again.</li>\n<li><strong>Rows next to each other</strong>: click the first row number, then hold <strong>Shift</strong> and click the last one.\nEvery row between them is selected.</li>\n<li><strong>All rows</strong>: click the empty top left corner of the grid, above the row numbers. <strong>Ctrl+click</strong> the\ncorner to select no rows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell or a row number) has <strong>Select › All rows</strong> too. Many actions in the\nrow menu work on the selected rows: they say <em>(selected rows)</em> after their name.</p>\n<p>Some apps let you select only one row at a time, or none. Then Ctrl and Shift select nothing more.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-select-columns\">Select columns</h2>\n<p>Under the header filters there is a row of numbers, 1, 2, 3 ... one for each column. Click a number to\nselect that column. <strong>Ctrl+click</strong> adds or removes a column, <strong>Shift+click</strong> selects the columns between\nthe one you clicked first and this one.</p>\n<p>Right click a column number to open the column menu:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>copies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Copy column (all rows)</td>\n<td>this column, every row shown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy selected columns (all rows)</td>\n<td>the selected columns, every row shown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy header names</td>\n<td>the names of all columns, on one line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy header name</td>\n<td>the name of this column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy selected header names</td>\n<td>the names of the selected columns</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>With the focus on a column number, <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> copies the selected columns and <strong>Esc</strong> selects none.\nMoving, hiding or adding a column clears the selected columns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-select-a-range-of-cells\">Select a range of cells</h2>\n<p>A range is a block of cells. It is shown with a tint and a line around it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drag</strong>: press the mouse in a cell and drag to another cell. Past the edge of the grid it scrolls.</li>\n<li><strong>Shift+click</strong>: click a cell, then hold <strong>Shift</strong> and click another cell. The range goes from the\nfirst cell to the one you Shift+clicked.</li>\n<li><strong>Several ranges</strong>: hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> (<strong>⌘</strong> on a Mac) and click or drag to add another range.</li>\n<li><strong>With the keyboard</strong>: in a cell, press <strong>Alt+Shift+arrow keys</strong> (<strong>Option+Shift+arrow keys</strong> on a\nMac) to make the range one cell larger each time. Plain <strong>Shift+arrow keys</strong> select text inside the\ncell, as in any text box.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More keys that make the range larger:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>the range reaches</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Shift+PageDown</strong> / <strong>Shift+PageUp</strong></td>\n<td>a page of rows down / up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Shift+End</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Home</strong></td>\n<td>the last cell / the first cell of the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+Shift+End</strong> / <strong>Alt+Shift+Home</strong></td>\n<td>the last / first cell of the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Shift+Arrow Down</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Arrow Up</strong></td>\n<td>the end of the data in the column</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Esc</strong> clears the ranges. So does a plain click in a cell, moving to another cell, typing, and any\nchange to which rows are shown (a filter, a sort, grouping).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-summary-of-the-selected-cells\">The summary of the selected cells</h2>\n<p>When a range has more than one cell, the footer shows a summary at its right, like the status bar of\na spreadsheet:</p>\n<p><em>Count: 20   Sum: 201   Average: 40.2   Min: 26   Max: 53</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Count</strong> is the number of cells with a value. Empty cells are not counted.</li>\n<li><strong>Sum</strong>, <strong>Average</strong>, <strong>Min</strong> and <strong>Max</strong> are of the number columns only. Without numbers only the\ncount shows.</li>\n<li>The summary follows the cells: edit or paste a value and it changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-copy-cells\">Copy cells</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Select the cells: a range, or just click one cell.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> (<strong>⌘C</strong> on a Mac).</li>\n<li>Paste into Excel, Google Sheets, Word or the grid.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With a range, the copy is a table: <strong>the column names come first</strong>, then a line per row. Excel and\nGoogle Sheets put each value in its own cell; Word makes a table. With one cell, only its value is\ncopied, without a column name. Several ranges are copied as one table.</p>\n<p>Values are copied as the cells show them, for example a date as the grid writes it. A column shown\nin a special format (such as 1 234 567,89) is copied with its plain value, so it can be pasted back.</p>\n<p>Text you have selected inside a cell is copied as plain text, as in any text box.</p>\n<p>If <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> does nothing, the focus has left the grid, for example after you scrolled with the mouse\nwheel. Shift+click the last cell of the range again, or use the row menu (below).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-copy-menu\">The Copy menu</h2>\n<p>Right click a cell and open <strong>Copy ›</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>copies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Cell Value</td>\n<td>the value of the cell you right clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Column (selected rows)</td>\n<td>this column, for the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Row (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the selected rows, every column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Row (selected rows/col)</td>\n<td>the selected rows, in the selected columns only (every column when none is selected)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Range</td>\n<td>the selected ranges, as <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> does</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>An entry is greyed out when there is nothing to copy, for example no selected rows. Every copy of more\nthan one cell starts with a line of column names. Your app may have turned that line off.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-paste-cells\">Paste cells</h2>\n<p>If your app allows editing, you can paste cells copied in the grid or in a spreadsheet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A block of cells</strong>: click the top left cell where it should go and press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong> (<strong>⌘V</strong> on a\nMac). The block fills the cells down and to the right, and is shown as a range afterwards.</li>\n<li><strong>One value into many cells</strong>: copy one cell, select a range, press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong>. Every cell of the\nrange gets the value.</li>\n<li><strong>From the menu</strong>: right click a cell, <strong>Paste › Paste range</strong> pastes into the selected ranges, and\n<strong>Paste › Paste cell (selected rows)</strong> pastes into this column of every selected row. The browser\nmay ask you to allow the page to read the clipboard.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Good to know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A first line of column names, as the grid copies it, is left out: only the values are pasted.</li>\n<li>Each value is read as if you typed it. An empty cell in the copy empties the cell in the grid.</li>\n<li>Read-only cells and group rows are skipped. A paste does not add rows: values past the last row are\nleft out. To add copied rows as new rows, see <a href=\"#user-guide/import\">Import</a>.</li>\n<li>One <strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> undoes the whole paste. See <a href=\"#user-guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Delete</strong> in a range empties every cell of it, if your app allows editing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-select-with-a-finger\">Select with a finger</h2>\n<p>On a phone or tablet there is no Shift or Ctrl, so:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tap a row number</strong> to add the row to the selection, tap again to take it out. A column number works\nthe same way.</li>\n<li><strong>Press and hold</strong> a cell or a row number to open the row menu. Its <strong>Select ›</strong> submenu does what\nShift and Ctrl do:</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>This row</td>\n<td>adds the row to the selection, or takes it out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rows from N to here</td>\n<td>selects the rows from row N (the one you selected last) to this one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All rows</td>\n<td>selects every row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Range from the active cell to here</td>\n<td>makes a range from the cell you tapped last to this cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add this cell to the ranges</td>\n<td>adds this cell as a range of its own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clear selection</td>\n<td>selects no rows and no cells</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>To make a range: tap the first cell, press and hold the last cell, then choose <strong>Select › Range from\nthe active cell to here</strong>. Hold a column number for the column menu, which has its own <strong>Select ›</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"find","title":"Find","html":"<p>Find looks for a text in the cells of the grid and marks every cell that shows it. It works like the\nfind bar of a browser or a text editor. The browser&#39;s own find does not work well in a grid, because\nonly the rows on the screen are in the page: use the grid&#39;s find instead.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"find\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-open-the-find-bar\">Open the find bar</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click a cell of the grid, so the focus is in the grid. A header filter or a column header works too.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Ctrl+F</strong> (<strong>⌘F</strong> on a Mac).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The find bar opens over the top right corner of the grid, with the focus in its input. If the focus\nis outside the grid, <strong>Ctrl+F</strong> opens the browser&#39;s own find instead. After you scroll with the mouse\nwheel the focus may have left the grid: click a cell first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-find-a-text\">Find a text</h2>\n<p>Type the text. A moment after you stop typing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>every cell that shows the text gets a tint</li>\n<li>the current match gets a stronger tint and a line around it, and the grid scrolls to it</li>\n<li>the bar shows the count, for example <em>3 of 17</em>: the third of 17 matches. <em>No matches</em> when there\nare none</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Find starts at the cell you were in when you opened the bar.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-go-from-match-to-match\">Go from match to match</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key or button</th>\n<th>goes to</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong>, <strong>F3</strong>, the ↓ button</td>\n<td>the next match. After the last one, the first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shift+Enter</strong>, <strong>Shift+F3</strong>, the ↑ button</td>\n<td>the previous match. Before the first one, the last</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The focus stays in the input, so you can go on typing. <strong>F3</strong> and <strong>Shift+F3</strong> also work while you are\nin a cell and the bar is open.</p>\n<p>You can click a cell to work in it; the bar stays open. <strong>Ctrl+F</strong> takes you back to the bar.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-match-case\">Match case</h2>\n<p>By default find ignores upper and lower case: <em>acme</em> finds <em>ACME Corp</em>. The <strong>Aa</strong> button, <em>Match case</em>,\nfinds the text exactly as you typed it: <em>Acme</em> then finds <em>Acme</em> but not <em>ACME</em> or <em>acme</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Click <strong>Aa</strong>, or press <strong>Alt+C</strong> in the bar, to turn it on or off. The button is filled while it is on.</li>\n<li>On a Mac, use <strong>Tab</strong> to reach the button and <strong>Space</strong> to turn it on or off.</li>\n<li>Match case is kept when you close the bar, until you turn it off.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-filter-rows\">Filter rows</h2>\n<p>The funnel button, <em>Filter rows</em>, shows only the rows that have a match. Click it again to see every\nrow again.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It works on top of your other filters: rows the filter hides stay hidden.</li>\n<li>While it is on, the footer shows a chip such as <em>Find: denma</em>. With Match case on, the chip has the\nmarker <strong>Aa</strong>.</li>\n<li>To reach the button from the keyboard, press <strong>Tab</strong> in the input, then <strong>Space</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is turned off when you close the bar, and when you click <strong>✕ Clear</strong> in the footer.</li>\n<li>A row you edit so it no longer has the text stays shown until the rows are filtered again. Rows do\nnot disappear while you type in them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For the grid&#39;s other filters, see <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-close-the-find-bar\">Close the find bar</h2>\n<p>Press <strong>Esc</strong>, or click <strong>✕</strong>. The focus goes to the cell of the current match, so you can start\nworking there. Without a match, it goes back to where you pressed <strong>Ctrl+F</strong>.</p>\n<p>The text you searched for is kept: the next <strong>Ctrl+F</strong> shows it again, selected, so you can type over\nit or press <strong>Enter</strong> to find it again.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-find-looks-in\">What find looks in</h2>\n<p>Find looks at <strong>the text the cells show</strong>, not at the data behind them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a date or a number as the column shows it, for example <em>14.03.2026</em> or <em>12,5</em></li>\n<li>the name a lookup column shows, not the code behind it</li>\n<li>every line of a cell with several lines, also lines you can not see</li>\n<li>the text contains what you typed, anywhere in the cell. A star, a percent sign or a question\nmark is found as that character: there are no wildcards</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Find does <strong>not</strong> look in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>checkboxes, because they show no text</li>\n<li>the rows of a collapsed group, and the group rows themselves. Expand a group to search in it</li>\n<li>rows the filter hides, and hidden columns</li>\n<li>the row numbers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Find marks cells only: it does not select rows or cells, and the selection stays as it was.</p>\n<p>The marks follow the data: when rows are added, edited, sorted or filtered while the bar is open, the\nmatches and the count are updated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-good-to-know\">Good to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>In a very large grid, find may take a moment after you stop typing.</li>\n<li>There is no search for whole words or patterns, only &quot;contains&quot;.</li>\n<li>For all keys of the grid, see <a href=\"#user-guide/keyboard\">Keyboard</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"export","title":"Export","html":"<p>You can save the rows of the grid as an Excel file (.xlsx). Some apps also offer CSV, a plain text\nfile most programs can read. The file has what you see: the rows your filter lets through, in your\nsort order, and the columns in the order you placed them.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"export\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-export-to-excel\">Export to Excel</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Right click any cell of the grid.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>Export ›</strong>.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Excel, all rows</strong> or <strong>Excel, selected rows</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The browser downloads a file named <em>export.xlsx</em>, to where it saves downloads. <strong>Excel, selected rows</strong>\nis greyed out until you select rows: see <a href=\"#user-guide/selecting-and-copying/select-rows\">Selecting and copying</a>.</p>\n<p>Export works in every grid, also one you can not edit. Your app may have its own export button, or\nname the file differently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-which-rows-are-exported\">Which rows are exported</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>you choose</th>\n<th>the file has</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Excel, all rows</td>\n<td>every row the filter lets through, in the sort order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excel, selected rows</td>\n<td>the selected rows that are shown, in the same order</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filters count</strong>: the header filters, the filter editor and <em>Changed rows only</em> all decide which\nrows are exported. Clear the filter first to export everything. See <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Grouped rows</strong>: the file is a plain list, without group rows. Every row is in it, also the rows\nof collapsed groups, in the grouped order.</li>\n<li><strong>No totals</strong>: totals and group sums are not exported. Excel can sum the rows itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Rows marked for deletion</strong> are still exported until they are saved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To get just a block of cells into Excel, select the cells and copy them with <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> (<strong>⌘C</strong> on a\nMac), then paste in Excel. See <a href=\"#user-guide/selecting-and-copying/copy-cells\">Copy cells</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-which-columns-are-exported\">Which columns are exported</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The columns of the grid, in the order you see them: pinned columns on the left first, then the\nothers, then the columns pinned on the right.</li>\n<li>A hidden column is not exported. Show it first if you need it. See <a href=\"#user-guide/columns\">Columns</a>.</li>\n<li>The first row has the column names as the grid shows them, also names you gave the columns.</li>\n<li>A column with two fields stacked in it becomes two columns in Excel.</li>\n<li>Each column is as wide in Excel as in the grid.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-the-excel-file-looks-like\">What the Excel file looks like</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The header row</strong> is bold and stays in place while you scroll. Each column has Excel&#39;s filter and\nsort button.</li>\n<li><strong>Numbers are numbers and dates are dates</strong>, so Excel can sum, sort and filter them. Numbers show with\nthe decimal mark of your computer. Dates show as year-month-day (2026-11-20), or as day.month.year\nwhen the grid shows them that way - your local dates, as in the grid.</li>\n<li><strong>Formats</strong>: a column you set to a special format (Format › in the column header menu) gets a\nmatching Excel format, such as two decimals, a thousands separator, a percent or a date style. The\nvalue in the cell stays the real number or date. See <a href=\"#user-guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Colours</strong>: the colours of colour rules are copied to the cells, as they were when you exported.\nData bars, colour scales and icons become Excel&#39;s own, and Excel updates them when you change a\nvalue. The colours are always those of the light look of the grid.</li>\n<li><strong>Links and templates</strong>: a column that shows a link or a text made from a template is exported with\nits value, not the text shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Calculated columns</strong> are exported like any other column, with their values.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An Excel sheet holds about one million rows. A larger export fails: ask for CSV instead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-text-that-looks-like-a-formula\">Text that looks like a formula</h2>\n<p>A value such as <em>=SUM(A1:A2)</em> or <em>+47 912 34 567</em> could be taken as a formula by a spreadsheet.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In the Excel file</strong> it is always plain text. Excel shows it exactly as it was in the grid and never\nruns it.</li>\n<li><strong>In a CSV file</strong> such text gets an apostrophe in front (&#39;=SUM(A1:A2)), so a spreadsheet shows it as\ntext. The apostrophe is part of the file.</li>\n<li><strong>When you copy</strong> cells, the text is copied as it is. Excel may take it as a formula when you paste.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-export-to-csv\">Export to CSV</h2>\n<p>CSV is not in the grid&#39;s menus. If your app has a button or menu entry for it, the CSV file has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the same rows and columns as the Excel export, with a first line of column names</li>\n<li>each value as the cell shows it, for example <em>12,5</em> or a date as the grid writes it</li>\n<li>no formats and no colours: it is text</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-open-a-csv-file-in-excel\">Open a CSV file in Excel</h2>\n<p>If you can, export to Excel instead: Excel then gets the numbers, dates and columns right. When you\nopen a CSV file in Excel:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everything in one column</strong>: Excel splits the columns with the list separator of your computer. In\ncountries that write a decimal comma (Norway, Germany, France ...) that is a semicolon. A file with\ncommas then opens with each row in one cell. Ask whoever set up your app for a CSV with semicolons,\nor open the file with Excel&#39;s <em>Data › From Text/CSV</em> and pick the separator there.</li>\n<li><strong>Letters such as æ, ø, å look wrong</strong>: the grid&#39;s CSV download marks the file as UTF-8, which Excel\nreads correctly. If you still see odd characters, import it with <em>Data › From Text/CSV</em> and choose\nUTF-8.</li>\n<li><strong>Dates and numbers</strong>: Excel guesses the type of each column, and may read a date or a decimal\nnumber differently than the grid shows it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-bring-the-rows-back\">Bring the rows back</h2>\n<p>A file exported from the grid can be read back in as new rows, if your app allows it. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/import\">Import</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"import","title":"Import","html":"<p>Import adds rows from a CSV file, an Excel workbook (.xlsx) or rows copied in a spreadsheet as new\nrows of the grid. Before anything is added, you see which column goes to which field and which\nvalues could not be read. Import is only there if your app allows it, and only where you may add rows.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"import\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-open-the-import-dialog\">Open the import dialog</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From a file</strong>: right click a column name in the header and choose <strong>Import rows…</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>From the clipboard</strong>: copy rows in Excel or Google Sheets, right click a cell of the grid and\nchoose <strong>Paste › As new rows…</strong>. The browser may ask you to allow the page to read the clipboard.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The dialog <em>Import rows</em> opens. You can move it by its title bar and make it larger from its edges.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-pick-the-rows\">Pick the rows</h2>\n<p>Do one of these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Click <strong>Choose file…</strong> and pick a file.</li>\n<li>Drag a file from your computer and drop it on the dialog.</li>\n<li>Copy rows in a spreadsheet and press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong> (<strong>⌘V</strong> on a Mac) in the dialog.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The grid reads these files:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>file</th>\n<th>read</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>CSV (.csv, .tsv, .txt)</td>\n<td>yes. The separator (comma, semicolon or tab) is found by itself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excel workbook (.xlsx)</td>\n<td>yes. With more than one sheet, pick the sheet under <strong>Sheet</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excel 97-2003 (.xls)</td>\n<td>no: open it in Excel and save it as .xlsx or CSV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A workbook protected by a password</td>\n<td>no: save it without the password</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OpenDocument (.ods), Numbers and others</td>\n<td>no: save it as .xlsx or CSV</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li>For a CSV file, <strong>Separator</strong> shows the one it found, for example <em>Found: Semicolon</em>. Pick\n<em>Comma</em>, <em>Semicolon</em> or <em>Tab</em> if the columns look wrong.</li>\n<li>CSV files saved by Excel, also as <em>Unicode text</em>, are read with their letters intact, such as æ, ø\nand å.</li>\n<li>In a workbook, formulas are not run: the value Excel saved is read.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-first-row-as-a-header\">The first row as a header</h2>\n<p>Tick <strong>First row is a header</strong> when the first row has the column names. The dialog turns it on by\nitself when the first row names fields of the grid. The header row is not imported.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-match-columns-to-fields\">Match columns to fields</h2>\n<p>The dialog shows the first rows of the file, with a picker over each column. In each picker, choose\nthe field the column goes to, or <strong>Skip</strong> to leave the column out. A line above says how the columns\nwere matched:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>By name</strong>: with a header, a column goes to the field with the same name, as the grid&#39;s column\nnames it. Upper and lower case do not matter. A column with a name the grid does not know is skipped.\nThe columns can be in any order.</li>\n<li><strong>By position</strong>: without a header, the first column goes to the first field of the grid, the second\nto the second, and so on. With <strong>Paste › As new rows…</strong> it starts at the column you right clicked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A field can go to one column only: pick it for a second column and the first one is set to <strong>Skip</strong>.\nRead-only columns can not be picked.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-numbers-and-dates\">Numbers and dates</h2>\n<p>Values are read as if you typed them into the grid. When a number or date field is picked, two more\noptions show:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Decimal mark</strong>: <em>As the columns</em> (the default, each column&#39;s own decimal mark), <em>Dot: 1,234.5</em>\nor <em>Comma: 1 234,5</em>. Pick the one the file uses.</li>\n<li><strong>Dates with /</strong>: <em>Day first: 31/12/2026</em> (the default) or <em>Month first: 12/31/2026</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>read</th>\n<th>not read</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>number</td>\n<td>1234.5, -5, 1 234,5, 1.234,5</td>\n<td>12 kg, abc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>date</td>\n<td>2026-12-31, 31.12.2026, 31/12/2026, also with a time 13:45 after it</td>\n<td>31.02.2026, 20.11.26, tomorrow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>yes / no</td>\n<td>true / false, 1 / 0, yes / no</td>\n<td>maybe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>any text, also text that starts with = (it stays text)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A value that can not be read is never guessed: the field is left empty and the problem is listed.\nEmpty cells give empty fields, and empty rows are left out.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-rows-with-problems\">Rows with problems</h2>\n<p>Under the columns the dialog shows how many rows it read and how many have problems, for example\n<em>120 rows, 3 with problems</em>, followed by the first problems in words:</p>\n<p><em>Row 3, Age: Not a number: abc</em></p>\n<p>The row number is the row in the file, as Excel numbers it. A cell with a problem is marked in the\npreview; point at it to see what is wrong. A required field the file does not have is a problem of\nevery row.</p>\n<p>Under <strong>Rows with problems</strong>, choose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Import them, marked</strong>: the rows are added, the values that could not be read are left empty, and\nthe row is marked so you can fix it.</li>\n<li><strong>Skip them</strong>: those rows are not added.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-import-the-rows\">Import the rows</h2>\n<p>Click <strong>Import N rows</strong>. The button counts the rows that will be added. <strong>Cancel</strong>, the <strong>✕</strong> and\n<strong>Esc</strong> close the dialog without importing.</p>\n<p>After the import:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The rows are added as new rows at the end, or in their groups when the grid is grouped. The first new\nrow is selected and the grid scrolls to it.</li>\n<li>A row that had a problem has an exclamation mark on its row number. Point at it to see what was not\nimported, for example <em>Not imported: Age: Not a number: abc</em>. Other errors show on the cells, as\nafter an edit.</li>\n<li>The rows are new rows, like rows you add yourself: they are saved when you save in your app.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-undo-an-import\">Undo an import</h2>\n<p>Press <strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> (<strong>⌘Z</strong> on a Mac) once, or use <strong>Undo last change</strong> in the row menu: all imported rows\nare taken out again. <strong>Ctrl+Y</strong> (<strong>⇧⌘Z</strong> on a Mac) brings them back. See <a href=\"#user-guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-limits\">Limits</h2>\n<p>The import refuses a file that is too large. It reads at most:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a file of 20 MB</li>\n<li>100 000 rows</li>\n<li>1 000 columns</li>\n<li>1 000 000 cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some browsers can not read Excel files. The dialog then says so: save the file as CSV instead. A file\nthat is damaged, or is not CSV or Excel, gives a message too. The file&#39;s name does not matter: the\ngrid looks at what is in it.</p>\n<p>To get rows out of the grid into a file, see <a href=\"#user-guide/export\">Export</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"views","title":"Views","html":"<p>A view is the way the grid looks, saved under a name: &quot;My open orders&quot;, &quot;Late deliveries&quot;. You set up\nthe grid the way you need it - columns, filters, sorting - save it as a view, and later switch back to it\nwith one click. You can keep as many views as you like.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"views\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-views-button\">The views button</h2>\n<p>If your app shows it, the views button is at the left end of the footer, under the rows. It shows the\nname of the view you are looking at. When no view is shown it says <strong>Standard</strong>: the grid as the app\nfirst shows it.</p>\n<p>Click the button to open the views menu. Click it again to close the menu.</p>\n<p>If you do not see the button, you may be able to turn it on yourself: open <strong>Grid config</strong> with the gear\n⚙ at the right end of the footer, go to the <strong>Defaults</strong> tab and check <strong>Show the views button</strong>. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/grid-config/the-defaults-tab\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-save-a-new-view\">Save a new view</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Set up the grid: move, hide and resize columns, filter, sort, group, change formats.</li>\n<li>Click the views button and choose <strong>Save as new view…</strong>.</li>\n<li>Type a name and press <strong>Enter</strong> (or click <strong>Save</strong>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The new view is now the one shown, and its name is on the button. A name must be new: if another view\nhas it (in any case), the line under the name says <em>Another view has this name</em>. An empty name is not\ntaken either. <strong>Esc</strong> or <strong>Cancel</strong> closes the box without saving.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-switch-to-another-view\">Switch to another view</h2>\n<p>Click the views button and click the view you want. The one shown has a ✓ in the menu.</p>\n<p>To go back to the grid as the app first shows it, choose <strong>Standard</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-save-changes-to-a-view\">Save changes to a view</h2>\n<p>When you change something in a view - a filter, a sort, a column width - a <code>*</code> appears after its name on\nthe button. It means the grid is no longer the same as the view you saved.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Save</strong> saves the grid as it is now over the view shown. It is greyed out when nothing has changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Revert changes</strong> throws your changes away and shows the view as it was saved.</li>\n<li><strong>Save as new view…</strong> keeps the old view and saves the changes as a new one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Opening and closing groups does not count as a change. Moving around in the rows, selecting and\nfinding do not either.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-rename-a-view\">Rename a view</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Switch to the view.</li>\n<li>Click the views button and choose <strong>Rename…</strong>.</li>\n<li>Type the new name and press <strong>Enter</strong> (or click <strong>Rename</strong>).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-delete-a-view\">Delete a view</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Switch to the view.</li>\n<li>Click the views button and choose <strong>Delete</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The view is deleted at once, without asking first. The grid stays as it was, but the button now says\n<strong>Standard</strong> with a <code>*</code>. If you deleted it by mistake, choose <strong>Save as new view…</strong> to save it again.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-default-view\">The default view</h2>\n<p>The default view is the one the grid opens with next time.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the views menu, switch to a view and choose <strong>Default view</strong>. A ✓ shows that it is on. Choose it\nagain to turn it off: the grid then opens in <strong>Standard</strong>.</li>\n<li>Or open <strong>Grid config</strong> (the gear ⚙), go to the <strong>Defaults</strong> tab and pick a view under <strong>Default\nview</strong>. Then click <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the views menu, the default view has the hint <em>default</em> after its name.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-shared-views\">Shared views</h2>\n<p>Your app may give you views made by your company. They have the hint <em>shared</em> in the menu. You can\nswitch to them, but not save over them, rename them or delete them: those entries are greyed out. To\nchange one, make your changes and use <strong>Save as new view…</strong> to keep them as your own view.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-view-keeps\">What a view keeps</h2>\n<p>A view keeps everything you set up in the grid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the columns: their order, widths, which are pinned and which are hidden</li>\n<li>sorting and grouping</li>\n<li>the filters, and what the header filters show</li>\n<li>the formats of the columns, their labels, colour rules, data bars and icons</li>\n<li>the formats by type from the <strong>Defaults</strong> tab</li>\n<li>calculated columns</li>\n<li>the <strong>Aggregate</strong> choices of the columns (Sum, Average ...) and the totals row</li>\n<li>the detail form: whether it is shown, its fields and its width</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A quick date filter such as <em>This month</em> stays relative: a view saved in November shows December when\nyou open it in December.</p>\n<p>A view does not keep the data itself, your edits, the selected rows, the current row or where you\nscrolled to.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-where-views-are-kept\">Where views are kept</h2>\n<p>Your views are usually kept in this browser on this computer. Then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>another browser, or another computer, does not have them</li>\n<li>clearing the browser&#39;s site data deletes them</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some apps keep your views for you on their server, so they follow you to other computers. If your app\ndoes not keep them anywhere, they last only until you leave the page. Ask whoever runs your app if\nyou are not sure.</p>\n"},{"id":"grid-config","title":"Grid config","html":"<p><strong>Grid config</strong> is the dialog where you set up the columns, the detail form and the grid&#39;s defaults in\none place. Nothing changes in the grid until you click <strong>Apply</strong>, so you can try things out and cancel.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"grid-config\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-open-the-dialog\">Open the dialog</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Click the gear ⚙ at the right end of the footer, under the rows. Its tooltip says <em>Grid config</em>.</li>\n<li>Or right click a column header and choose <strong>Column settings…</strong>. The dialog opens on that column.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If your app hides the gear, <strong>Column settings…</strong> in the header menu usually still opens the dialog.</p>\n<p>The dialog has three tabs under its title: <strong>Columns</strong>, <strong>Detail form</strong> and <strong>Defaults</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-columns-tab\">The Columns tab</h2>\n<p>The left side lists the columns of the grid, in their order. Each shows its label, its type (<em>Text</em>,\n<em>Number</em>, <em>Date</em>, <em>Date and time</em>, <em>Checkbox</em> or <em>Lookup</em>) and small badges for what it has set:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>badge</th>\n<th>the column has</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Calculated</td>\n<td>a value computed by a formula</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Format</td>\n<td>a date or number format of its own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>a text template</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Link</td>\n<td>its cells shown as links</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checkbox</td>\n<td>its cells shown as checkboxes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blank / 0</td>\n<td>Show blank as 0, or Show 0 as blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Label</td>\n<td>a label you gave it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Placeholder</td>\n<td>a placeholder for empty cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiline</td>\n<td>text of several lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read only</td>\n<td>cells that can not be edited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colours, Bar, Scale, Icons</td>\n<td>colour rules, a data bar, a colour scale, icons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Error</td>\n<td>a problem to fix before Apply</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Click a column to see its settings on the right. They are split into tabs:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>tab</th>\n<th>what you set there</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>General</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Label</strong>, <strong>Multiline</strong>, <strong>Read only</strong>, <strong>Placeholder</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Display</strong></td>\n<td>how the cells show the value: the <strong>Format</strong>, <strong>Filter as shown</strong>, <strong>Show blank as 0</strong> / <strong>Show 0 as blank</strong>, <strong>Show as link</strong> with <strong>Text shown</strong>, <strong>Address</strong> and <strong>Open in a new tab</strong>, <strong>Show as checkbox</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Header filter</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Filter operator</strong>, <strong>Filter as you type</strong>, and <strong>Match case</strong> for a text column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Colours</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Colour rules</strong>; a number column also has <strong>Data bar</strong>, <strong>Colour scale</strong> and <strong>Icons</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A checkbox column and a lookup column have no <strong>Display</strong> tab: they have no format. A checkbox column\nhas no <strong>Header filter</strong> tab. When you pick another column, the same tab stays open if that column has\nit.</p>\n<p>Under the tabs, the <strong>preview</strong> shows how the cell will look in the current row (or the first row), with\nits colours and link. Click a link in the preview to try it in a new tab.</p>\n<p>For the formats, templates, links, checkboxes and colours, see <a href=\"#user-guide/formatting\">Formatting</a> and\n<a href=\"#user-guide/formatting/colour-rules\">Colour rules</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-colour-rules-one-at-a-time\">Colour rules, one at a time</h2>\n<p>A column can have many colour rules. Only one rule is open at a time; the others are a line each, with\na sample <em>Abc</em> in their colours and what they match. Click a line to open that rule. Click <strong>▾</strong> to close\nit. <strong>Add rule</strong> adds a new rule and opens it.</p>\n<p>A number column has four panels on its <strong>Colours</strong> tab: <strong>Colour rules</strong>, <strong>Data bar</strong>, <strong>Colour\nscale</strong> and <strong>Icons</strong>. One panel is open at a time. The line of each closed panel says what it has:\n<em>2 rules</em>, <em>On</em> or <em>Off</em>. Click the line to open the panel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-add-a-calculated-column\">Add a calculated column</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click <strong>Add calculated column</strong> under the list of columns. A column named <em>Calculated 1</em> is added\nto the list.</li>\n<li>Write its <strong>Formula</strong>, such as <code>{price} * {qty}</code>. Change its <strong>Field name</strong> and <strong>Label</strong> if you like.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The column is added at the end of the grid. Its format and colour rules can be set after Apply. To take\nit away again, pick it and click <strong>Remove calculated column</strong>. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/calculated-columns\">Calculated columns</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-detail-form-tab\">The Detail form tab</h2>\n<p>Here you set up the detail form, the panel that shows the current row as a form beside the rows:\n<strong>Show the detail form</strong>, <strong>Expanded</strong>, its width, and which fields it shows in which order. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/detail-form/set-up-the-detail-form\">The detail form</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-defaults-tab\">The Defaults tab</h2>\n<p>Settings for the whole grid, not for one column:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Formats by type</strong>: one format for every date, date and time, and number column that has no format\nof its own. On the <strong>Columns</strong> tab such a column then says <em>Default: Norwegian</em> (or the format you\npicked).</li>\n<li><strong>Show the views button</strong>: shows the views button at the left end of the footer. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Default view</strong>: the view the grid opens with - <strong>Standard</strong> or one of your views. It is greyed out\nuntil you have saved a view.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-apply-or-cancel\">Apply or cancel</h2>\n<p>The buttons at the bottom of the dialog work on all three tabs at once:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Apply</strong> puts your changes in the grid. The dialog stays open, so you can go on.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply &amp; close</strong> puts your changes in the grid and closes the dialog.</li>\n<li><strong>Cancel</strong> closes the dialog and throws away what you changed since the last Apply. The ✕ in the\ntitle bar and <strong>Esc</strong> do the same.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What you apply is kept with the rest of the grid&#39;s look, and saved in your views. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-start-over\">Start over</h2>\n<p>The left end of the bottom row has the reset buttons of the tab shown:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reset column</strong>: the column picked, back to the settings it starts with.</li>\n<li><strong>Reset all</strong>: every column back to the settings it starts with.</li>\n<li><strong>Reset form</strong> on the <strong>Detail form</strong> tab, <strong>Reset formats</strong> on the <strong>Defaults</strong> tab.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A reset changes only the dialog. Click <strong>Apply</strong> to put it in the grid, or <strong>Cancel</strong> to keep what you\nhad.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-problems-block-apply\">Problems block Apply</h2>\n<p>When a setting has a problem - an address that does not start with <code>https://</code>, a formula with a\nmistake, a colour rule without a value - the dialog says what is wrong right under it. The column gets\nan <strong>Error</strong> badge in the list, and its tab (and the panel on the <strong>Colours</strong> tab) gets a small red\nsquare. <strong>Apply</strong> does nothing until every problem is fixed or the setting is taken away.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-move-and-resize-the-dialog\">Move and resize the dialog</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Drag the title bar to move the dialog.</li>\n<li>Drag an edge or a corner to make it bigger or smaller, up to the size of the window.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The dialog opens no larger than most of the window. What does not fit scrolls inside it, and the\nbuttons stay at the bottom.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-on-a-small-screen\">On a small screen</h2>\n<p>On a phone or a narrow window the list of columns and the settings of a column take turns:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tap a column in the list to see its settings.</li>\n<li>Tap <strong>‹ Columns</strong> at the top to go back to the list.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-keys-in-the-dialog\">Keys in the dialog</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong> / <strong>Shift+Tab</strong></td>\n<td>the next / previous control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>←</strong> / <strong>→</strong> on a row of tabs</td>\n<td>the tab before / after</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑</strong> / <strong>↓</strong>, <strong>Home</strong> / <strong>End</strong> in the list of columns</td>\n<td>another column; a letter jumps to the next column starting with it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>closes an open menu, else closes the dialog without applying</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>More keys: <a href=\"#user-guide/keyboard/dialogs\">Keyboard</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"detail-form","title":"The detail form","html":"<p>The detail form shows the current row as a form, beside the rows: a label and a value for each field.\nIt is handy for long notes that do not fit in a narrow column, and for fields that are not columns in\nthe grid at all. Your app may show it from the start, or you can turn it on yourself.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"detail-form\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-it-shows\">What it shows</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The form is a panel at the right of the rows. Its title says <em>Details</em> and the number of the row.</li>\n<li>It always shows the <strong>current row</strong>: the row you clicked or moved to with the keys. Move to another\nrow and the form follows.</li>\n<li>With no current row, or when a filter hides it, the form says <em>No row</em>.</li>\n<li>The values look as they do in the cells: the same formats, the same colours from the colour rules.\nA text of several lines is shown in full, a yes / no field is a checkbox, and a link is shown under its\nvalue.</li>\n<li>A form without fields says <em>No fields: pick them with the gear in the footer, tab Detail form</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-turn-it-on-or-off\">Turn it on or off</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click the gear ⚙ at the right end of the footer to open <strong>Grid config</strong>.</li>\n<li>Go to the <strong>Detail form</strong> tab.</li>\n<li>Check or uncheck <strong>Show the detail form</strong>.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Apply</strong> or <strong>Apply &amp; close</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-collapse-and-expand-it\">Collapse and expand it</h2>\n<p>Click the title of the form to collapse it. It becomes a narrow strip at the right of the rows, so the\nrows get the room. Click the strip&#39;s title to expand the form again.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Expanded</strong> checkbox on the <strong>Detail form</strong> tab of <strong>Grid config</strong> is the same setting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-make-it-wider-or-narrower\">Make it wider or narrower</h2>\n<p>Drag the left edge of the form. The rows follow as you drag. The form stops at its least and most\nwidth, and never takes more than half of the grid.</p>\n<p>With the keyboard: <strong>Tab</strong> to the edge, then <strong>←</strong> makes the form wider and <strong>→</strong> narrower.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-on-a-narrow-screen\">On a narrow screen</h2>\n<p>When there is not room for the form beside the rows - on a phone, say - the form is shown under the\nrows instead, above the footer. Its width can not be dragged there. When the grid gets wider, the form\nmoves back beside the rows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-edit-in-the-form\">Edit in the form</h2>\n<p>If your app lets you edit the grid, you can edit the fields of the form as you edit the cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type in a field. The cell in the grid changes as you type.</li>\n<li>The same rules apply as in the cells: a read only column can not be edited in the form either.</li>\n<li>A value that is not allowed shows its error under the field.</li>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> (<strong>⌘Z</strong> on a Mac) in a field undoes the last change. See <a href=\"#user-guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A lookup column, where you pick the value from a list, is shown in the form but not edited there: pick\nthe value in its cell in the grid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-sections\">Sections</h2>\n<p>The form can have sections, such as <em>Address</em> or <em>Notes</em>, each with a header. Click a section&#39;s header\nto collapse it, and again to expand it. Some sections start collapsed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-set-up-the-detail-form\">Set up the detail form</h2>\n<p>Open <strong>Grid config</strong> with the gear ⚙ in the footer and go to the <strong>Detail form</strong> tab:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Show the detail form</strong>: the form is shown or not.</li>\n<li><strong>Expanded</strong>: the form is open, or collapsed to a strip.</li>\n<li><strong>Width (px)</strong>, <strong>Min width (px)</strong> and <strong>Max width (px)</strong>: how wide the form is, and how far it can\nbe dragged. The width is at least 150. An empty max width means <em>None</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fields of the form</strong>: every field, each with a checkbox. Checked fields are in the form, in the\norder of the list.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the list, a field marked <em>Hidden</em> is not a column in the grid, but the form can show it. A field\nmarked <em>Several lines</em> shows text of several lines.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-choose-and-order-the-fields\">Choose and order the fields</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Put a field in or out</strong>: check or uncheck it. With the keyboard, <strong>Space</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Move a field</strong>: drag it by the handle at its right. A line shows where it will go.</li>\n<li>Or pick it and click <strong>Move up</strong> or <strong>Move down</strong>. With the keyboard, <strong>Alt+↑</strong> / <strong>Alt+↓</strong>\n(<strong>⌥↑</strong> / <strong>⌥↓</strong> on a Mac).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For the field picked you can also set <strong>Min lines</strong> (the least number of lines of a text of several\nlines), <strong>Space above (px)</strong> and <strong>Space below (px)</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-add-a-section\">Add a section</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In the list of fields, pick the field the section should come after.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Add section</strong>. A new row <em>Section</em> is added under it.</li>\n<li>Give it a <strong>Name</strong>.</li>\n<li>Move the fields you want under it. The fields under a section, down to the next section, are in it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A section also has <strong>Panel: a frame around it</strong>, <strong>Open at start</strong> and room above and below.\n<strong>Remove section</strong> takes the section away; its fields stay in the form.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-apply-your-changes\">Apply your changes</h2>\n<p>Click <strong>Apply</strong> (or <strong>Apply &amp; close</strong>) to see your changes in the form. <strong>Reset form</strong> goes back to the\nform your app started with. See <a href=\"#user-guide/grid-config/apply-or-cancel\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<p>The form - shown or not, its fields, its width, collapsed or expanded - is kept in your views. See\n<a href=\"#user-guide/views\">Views</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"keyboard","title":"Keyboard","html":"<p>You can do almost everything in the grid from the keyboard. This page lists the keys by where you are:\nin a cell, a header filter, a menu, the find bar or a dialog.</p>\n<p><strong>One thing to know first</strong>: a cell is like a small text box that is always ready to type in. The arrow\nkeys, <strong>Home</strong> and <strong>End</strong> move the text cursor inside the cell, as in a spreadsheet cell you are\nediting. To move from cell to cell, hold <strong>Alt</strong> with the arrow keys.</p>\n<p><strong>On a Mac</strong>, use <strong>⌘</strong> (Cmd) where this page says <strong>Ctrl</strong>, <strong>⌥</strong> (Option) where it says <strong>Alt</strong>, and\n<strong>⇧</strong> for <strong>Shift</strong>: <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> is <strong>⌘C</strong>, <strong>Alt+↓</strong> is <strong>⌥↓</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some keys only work if your app lets you edit the grid. They are marked <em>editing</em> below.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-tryit\" data-tryit=\"keyboard\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-see-every-key-in-the-grid\">See every key in the grid</h2>\n<p>Press <strong>Ctrl+/</strong> anywhere in the grid, also while you type in a cell. Or press <strong>?</strong> where it types\nnothing, such as on a read only cell or a column header. Or right click a column header and choose\n<strong>Keyboard shortcuts…</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Keyboard shortcuts</strong> dialog lists the keys in groups, the way your computer names them (⌘ and ⌥\non a Mac). Type in its <strong>Search</strong> box to find a key: <code>undo</code>, <code>ctrl</code>, <code>f3</code>. <strong>Esc</strong> closes it.</p>\n<p>Your app may have turned this help off.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-move-around\">Move around</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>goes to</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong> / <strong>Shift+Tab</strong></td>\n<td>the next / previous cell, then on to the next / previous row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+←</strong> / <strong>Alt+→</strong></td>\n<td>the cell to the left / right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+↑</strong> / <strong>Alt+↓</strong></td>\n<td>the cell above / below. Up from the first row: the header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong></td>\n<td>the cell below, from a cell you can edit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+Home</strong> / <strong>Alt+End</strong></td>\n<td>the first / last cell of the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Home</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+End</strong></td>\n<td>the first cell of the first row / the last cell of the last row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Page Up</strong> / <strong>Page Down</strong></td>\n<td>a page of rows up / down, in the same column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+Page Up</strong> / <strong>Alt+Page Down</strong></td>\n<td>a page of columns left / right, in the same row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+↑</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+↓</strong></td>\n<td>the edge of the data in the column, as in a spreadsheet: the last filled cell before an empty one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Home</strong> / <strong>End</strong></td>\n<td>the start / end of the text in the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+←</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+→</strong></td>\n<td>a word left / right in the text of the cell (<strong>⌥←</strong> / <strong>⌥→</strong> on a Mac)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The keys pass over group rows. <strong>Tab</strong> stops on them, see <a href=\"#user-guide/keyboard/groups-and-column-headers\">Groups</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-edit-cells\">Edit cells</h2>\n<p><em>Editing</em> - only if your app lets you edit the grid.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>type</td>\n<td>changes the value of the cell. There is no need to press a key first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong></td>\n<td>goes to the cell below. The value is kept as you typed it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Delete</strong> / <strong>Backspace</strong></td>\n<td>deletes text in the cell. With a block of cells selected: empties them all</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Space</strong></td>\n<td>checks or unchecks a checkbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong> or <strong>F2</strong></td>\n<td>in a cell of several lines: opens its editor (also to read a read only one)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+↓</strong> or <strong>F4</strong></td>\n<td>in a cell with a list: opens the list. Typing a letter opens it too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+Enter</strong></td>\n<td>in a link cell: opens the link</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+D</strong></td>\n<td>fill down: the value of the row above, or of the top row of the selected cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong></td>\n<td>undo, and go to the cell that changed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Y</strong> or <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Z</strong></td>\n<td>redo</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>See <a href=\"#user-guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-editor-of-a-cell-of-several-lines\">The editor of a cell of several lines</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong></td>\n<td>a new line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Enter</strong></td>\n<td>saves and closes the editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>closes the editor without saving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong> / <strong>Shift+Tab</strong></td>\n<td>saves, closes and goes to the next / previous cell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-list-of-a-cell\">The list of a cell</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>type</td>\n<td>searches the list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑</strong> / <strong>↓</strong>, <strong>Page Up</strong> / <strong>Page Down</strong>, <strong>Home</strong> / <strong>End</strong></td>\n<td>moves in the list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong></td>\n<td>picks the value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong></td>\n<td>picks the value and goes to the next cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>closes the list, nothing picked</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-dates\">Dates</h3>\n<p>Type a date straight into a date cell or a date filter. The date picker that opens on a click is for\nthe mouse: no key opens it, and the keyboard does not go into it. In the range of a filter menu, type\nthe date and press <strong>Enter</strong> to filter; <strong>Esc</strong> closes the picker.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-select-cells\">Select cells</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+Shift+arrow</strong></td>\n<td>a block of cells, one cell further each time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shift+Page Up</strong> / <strong>Shift+Page Down</strong></td>\n<td>the block a page up / down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+Shift+Home</strong> / <strong>Alt+Shift+End</strong></td>\n<td>the block to the first / last cell of the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Shift+Home</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+Shift+End</strong></td>\n<td>the block to the first / last cell of the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Shift+↑</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+Shift+↓</strong></td>\n<td>the block to the edge of the data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>no block of cells any more</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>There is no key to select whole rows or columns. Click the row numbers or column numbers, or use\n<strong>Select ›</strong> in the row menu. See <a href=\"#user-guide/selecting-and-copying\">Selecting and copying</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-copy-paste-undo-and-redo\">Copy, paste, undo and redo</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+C</strong></td>\n<td>copies the selected block of cells, or the value of the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+C</strong> on a column number</td>\n<td>copies the selected columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+V</strong></td>\n<td>pastes cells copied in the grid or in a spreadsheet (<em>editing</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong></td>\n<td>undo (<em>editing</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Y</strong> or <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Z</strong></td>\n<td>redo (<em>editing</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Delete</strong> / <strong>Backspace</strong> on a row number</td>\n<td>marks the row, or the selected rows, for deletion (<em>editing</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-header-filters\">Header filters</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong></td>\n<td>filters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong> / <strong>Shift+Tab</strong></td>\n<td>the next / previous filter. <strong>Tab</strong> on the last one goes to the rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+←</strong> / <strong>Alt+→</strong></td>\n<td>the filter to the left / right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+↑</strong></td>\n<td>up to the column header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+↓</strong></td>\n<td>down to the first row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+Home</strong> / <strong>Alt+End</strong></td>\n<td>the first / last filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>F4</strong></td>\n<td>choose the operator (=, ≥, contains ...)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shift+F10</strong> or the <strong>Menu</strong> key</td>\n<td>opens the filter menu</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>See <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-groups-and-column-headers\">Groups and column headers</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>where</th>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a column header</td>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong> / <strong>Space</strong></td>\n<td>sorts on the column; again for the other direction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a column header</td>\n<td><strong>Shift+Enter</strong> / <strong>Shift+Space</strong></td>\n<td>adds the column to the sort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a column header</td>\n<td><strong>Shift+F10</strong> or the <strong>Menu</strong> key</td>\n<td>opens the header menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a group row</td>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong> / <strong>Space</strong></td>\n<td>expands or collapses the group</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>To get to a column header, press <strong>Alt+↑</strong> in its header filter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-menus\">Menus</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Shift+F10</strong> or the <strong>Menu</strong> key</td>\n<td>opens the menu of the cell, header, filter or column number you are on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↓</strong> / <strong>↑</strong></td>\n<td>the next / previous item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Home</strong> / <strong>End</strong></td>\n<td>the first / last item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a letter</td>\n<td>the next item that starts with it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong> / <strong>Space</strong></td>\n<td>uses the item, or opens its submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>→</strong> / <strong>←</strong></td>\n<td>opens / closes a submenu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>closes the submenu, or the menu</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong></td>\n<td>closes the menu</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>In the filter menu, <strong>Tab</strong> goes on into its list of values (or the From and To of a number or date\ncolumn), then <strong>Cancel</strong> and <strong>Apply</strong>. See <a href=\"#user-guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-find\">Find</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+F</strong></td>\n<td>opens the find bar. Again: back to its box</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enter</strong> or <strong>F3</strong></td>\n<td>the next match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shift+Enter</strong> or <strong>Shift+F3</strong></td>\n<td>the previous match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+C</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Match case</strong> on or off (not on a Mac)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong></td>\n<td>the buttons of the bar: <strong>Match case</strong>, <strong>Filter rows</strong>, previous, next, close</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>closes the find bar and goes to the cell of the match, if there is one</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Ctrl+F</strong> finds in the grid only while you are in the grid. Outside it, it is the browser&#39;s own find.\nSee <a href=\"#user-guide/find\">Find</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-footer-and-the-detail-form\">The footer and the detail form</h2>\n<p>After the last cell of the last row, <strong>Tab</strong> goes on to the footer: the views button, the filter\nbuttons, then the gear ⚙. <strong>Enter</strong> or <strong>Space</strong> uses the button: the views button opens the views\nmenu, the gear opens <strong>Grid config</strong>.</p>\n<p>Then <strong>Tab</strong> goes into the detail form, if it is shown: first the button of its title (<strong>Enter</strong> or\n<strong>Space</strong> collapses and expands it), then its left edge (<strong>←</strong> wider, <strong>→</strong> narrower), then its fields.\nSee <a href=\"#user-guide/detail-form\">The detail form</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-dialogs\">Dialogs</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Esc</strong></td>\n<td>closes an open menu in the dialog, else the dialog, without applying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong> / <strong>Shift+Tab</strong></td>\n<td>the next / previous control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>←</strong> / <strong>→</strong>, <strong>Home</strong> / <strong>End</strong></td>\n<td>on the tabs of <strong>Grid config</strong>: the tab before / after, the first / last</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑</strong> / <strong>↓</strong>, a letter</td>\n<td>in the list of columns of <strong>Grid config</strong>: another column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Space</strong></td>\n<td>in the fields of the detail form (<strong>Grid config</strong>, <strong>Detail form</strong> tab): the field in the form or out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alt+↑</strong> / <strong>Alt+↓</strong></td>\n<td>in the fields of the detail form: moves the field up / down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> / <strong>Ctrl+Y</strong></td>\n<td>in the filter editor: undo / redo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ctrl+/</strong></td>\n<td>the keyboard shortcuts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>See <a href=\"#user-guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n"}]