{"build":{"branch":"master","commit":"9412b0d","sha":"9412b0d6332a3d09367ac9f1e87a3123d5a0f7fc","date":"2026-11-28T15:27:41+01:00","localChanges":false,"url":"https://github.com/vegarringdal/simple-html/commit/9412b0d6332a3d09367ac9f1e87a3123d5a0f7fc","version":"6.0.0","dev":false,"built":"2026-11-28T14:27:56.960Z"},"html":"<p>What is known and not done. Limits of one area are also listed in their guides:\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility\">accessibility</a>, <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">selection and clipboard</a>,\n<a href=\"#guide/find\">find</a>, <a href=\"#guide/custom-cells\">custom cells</a>, <a href=\"#guide/sql\">SQL</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-open-questions\">Open questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The start page of the docs site</strong>: the Feature overview (<code>#guide/features</code>) is the start page now; it\nwas Getting started (<code>samples/demo/index.ts</code>, <code>fromHash()</code>). Keep it?</li>\n<li><strong>The amber of the traffic lights</strong> (icon sets, <code>--simple-html-grid-icon-amber-color</code>): <code>#92400e</code> in light\nmode looks brown beside the red. It is that dark to keep 3:1 on selected rows and on the palette&#39;s\nbackgrounds. An outlined icon would allow a brighter fill. Is it worth it?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-on-hold\">On hold</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Validation rules in the grid config dialog</strong> (required, min / max, length, a pattern, a formula): on hold\nat the user&#39;s request. The rules are in the grid&#39;s config (<code>ColumnConfig.validate</code>), and the user of a grid\nshould not be allowed to change them, so the dialog shows them but does not edit them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-not-wanted\">Not wanted</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A server-side row model</strong> (rows loaded page by page from a backend, filtered and sorted there): not\nwanted. <code>toSqlWhere()</code> stays a helper for the app&#39;s own backend.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-bugs\">Bugs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Made for the desktop first</strong>: the grid was built and checked with a mouse and a keyboard. Touch works,\nbut some things may be odd on a phone or a tablet - see the touch items below and under Features.</li>\n<li><strong>Touch has not been tried on a device</strong>: the touch work was checked in Chromium&#39;s emulation only. Worth a\nlook on real phones:<ul>\n<li>Safari on iOS: there is no <code>contextmenu</code>, so the grid times the long press. A canceled touchend drops the\nclick. Check the callout and the loupe on a cell&#39;s input under <code>-webkit-touch-callout: none</code>.</li>\n<li>Chrome on Android: it has its own long-press <code>contextmenu</code>, which the check simulates by sending one from\nthe page. Does a long press on an editable input also start the browser&#39;s text selection?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-accessibility\">Accessibility</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The column numbers by keyboard</strong>: only a click reaches a number (Shift+F10, Ctrl+C and Esc work from\nthere). Alt+ArrowDown from a header filter goes to the first row. <code>aria-multiselectable</code> follows the row\nselection mode, while several columns can be selected in every mode.</li>\n<li><strong>Keyboard</strong>:<ul>\n<li>The days of the date picker and the column chooser are for the mouse. The picker&#39;s buttons and hour /\nminute fields can be reached, and a date can be typed.</li>\n<li>The picker opened from a cell is put at the end of the body, no key opens it, and it does not take the\nfocus. This is worth doing together with keys for its days.</li>\n<li>The two scrollbar elements are unnamed Tab stops in Chrome.</li>\n<li>Shift+Tab into the grid from after it lands on the last input in the DOM (a cell of a row rendered at the\nbottom of the view), not on the last row or the footer.</li>\n<li>Missing keys: Shift+Space / Ctrl+Space (a row, a column); Ctrl+Left / Ctrl+Right to the edge of the data\nsideways (they are the input&#39;s word moves); Alt+Shift+PageUp / PageDown; the treegrid&#39;s ArrowRight /\nArrowLeft on a group row.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Only Chrome&#39;s accessibility tree</strong> has been tried. Firefox, Safari (weaker <code>aria-owns</code>) and real screen\nreaders (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver) have not.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-tooling\">Tooling</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Test coverage</strong> was last measured at 91% of the statements: <code>src/datasource</code> 96%, <code>src/grid</code> 98%,\n<code>src/grid/gridFunctions</code> 88%, <code>src/date</code> 85%. The thinnest parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the key handlers of the header filter row (40%) and of the rows (59%)</li>\n<li><code>renderHeaderFilter</code> (62%)</li>\n<li><code>fillDown</code> (60%; the fill handle&#39;s drag is covered by <code>fill-check.js</code>)</li>\n<li><code>contextmenuLabel</code> (67%)</li>\n<li>the date picker&#39;s <code>day.ts</code> (78%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Measure with <code>npm run test:coverage</code>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-features\">Features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Column settings, more</strong>:<ul>\n<li>relative addresses within the app, resolved against a base the config gives, keeping the base&#39;s origin</li>\n<li>an &quot;Open link&quot; entry in the row menu</li>\n<li>a <code>HYPERLINK</code> in the Excel export (a formula of data, within Excel&#39;s 255 characters)</li>\n<li>the dialog&#39;s own undo (the filter editor has one)</li>\n<li><code>placeholderRow</code> and <code>focusButton</code> on a link cell</li>\n<li>more of the column in the dialog (its width, aggregates, readonly), so the whole of a column&#39;s view is in\none place</li>\n<li>group chips that follow a label changed after grouping (<code>GroupArgument.title</code> keeps the old one)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Display formats, more</strong>:<ul>\n<li>a value list of weeks for a date column (the header filter takes <code>2026-W47</code> already)</li>\n<li>copy and CSV of the text shown, as an option</li>\n<li>Days until kept up to date over midnight (a timer)</li>\n<li>more formats: US with time on a 12-hour clock, <code>november 2026</code>, a week without its year, short day\nnames, a currency, negatives in parentheses or red, more than 4 decimals</li>\n<li>the named formats in Excel with its locale codes (<code>[$-414]</code>; Excel&#39;s names and order differ from Intl&#39;s)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Touch, smaller things</strong>:<ul>\n<li>A finger can make ranges of cells with the row menu&#39;s Select › (Range from the active cell to here, Add\nthis cell), but can not drag one. A hold and a drag could be one gesture.</li>\n<li>The fill handle (7px) is small for a finger.</li>\n<li>The multiline cell&#39;s editor hints &quot;Ctrl+Enter to save, Esc to cancel&quot;, keys a phone does not have (a tap\noutside saves).</li>\n<li>The filter editor&#39;s AND / OR and icon buttons are 24px. This is kept: the user found taller buttons too\ntall, so a finger gets taller list rows only.</li>\n<li>The docs site is not made for a phone: its sidebar takes half the width (<code>touch-check.js</code> gives the grid\nthe page with CSS of its own).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Custom cells, later</strong>: <code>focusButton</code> and <code>placeholderRow</code> on a custom cell; a <code>measure()</code> for\n<code>fitColumns()</code>; renderers for group rows, the totals row and header labels.</li>\n<li><strong>Formatters are fixed when the datasource is made</strong>: there is no setter to switch a field&#39;s formatter.</li>\n</ul>\n"}