h.april\" | \"datePicker.month.may\" | \"datePicker.month.june\" | \"datePicker.month.july\" | \"datePicker.month.august\" | \"datePicker.month.september\" | \"datePicker.month.october\" | \"datePicker.month.november\" | \"datePicker.month.december\" | \"datePicker.day.sunday\" | \"datePicker.day.monday\" | \"datePicker.day.tuesday\" | \"datePicker.day.wednesday\" | \"datePicker.day.thursday\" | \"datePicker.day.friday\" | \"datePicker.day.saturday\" | \"validation.required\" | \"validation.min\" | \"validation.max\" | \"validation.minLength\" | \"validation.maxLength\" | \"validation.pattern\" | \"validation.integer\" | \"validation.decimals\" | \"validation.oneOf\" | \"validation.email\" | \"validation.unique\" | \"validation.uniqueWith\" | \"filterString.and\" | \"filterString.or\" | \"filterString.operator.EQUAL\" | \"filterString.operator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO\" | \"filterString.operator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO\" | \"filterString.operator.LESS_THAN\" | \"filterString.operator.GREATER_THAN\" | \"filterString.operator.BETWEEN\" | \"filterString.operator.CONTAINS\" | \"filterString.operator.NOT_EQUAL_TO\" | \"filterString.operator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN\" | \"filterString.operator.BEGIN_WITH\" | \"filterString.operator.END_WITH\" | \"filterString.operator.IN\" | \"filterString.operator.NOT_IN\" | \"filterString.operator.IS_BLANK\" | \"filterString.operator.IS_NOT_BLANK\" | \"filterString.betweenAnd\" | \"filterString.matchCase\" | \"grouping.blank\""],"doc":"<p>The id of a text of the grid or the datasource: a key of <a href=\"#api/index.TEXTS\">TEXTS</a>, e.g. <code>&#39;row.copy&#39;</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { TextId } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst id: TextId = 'filterEditor.title';</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"Texts","group":"Type Aliases","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type Texts = { [id in TextId]?: string } & { [id: string]: string }"],"doc":"<p>Texts by id, for <a href=\"#api/index.setTexts\">setTexts</a> and <code>GridConfig.texts</code>: plain JSON, a text for each id to\nchange. An id left out shows the text it had: the one set with <code>setTexts()</code>, else the default in\n<a href=\"#api/index.TEXTS\">TEXTS</a>.</p>\n<p>The ids of <a href=\"#api/index.TEXTS\">TEXTS</a> are suggested by the editor. An id of your own is allowed too: a validator\n(or a rule&#39;s <code>message</code>) can give an id, and its text is looked up the same way - see\n<a href=\"#api/index.registerValidator\">registerValidator</a>.</p>\n<p><code>{name}</code> in a text is filled in when it is shown: <code>&#39;Count: {count}&#39;</code>. Keep the names of the\ndefault text, a name the text does not use is not shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { Texts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst norwegian: Texts = {\n    'row.copy': 'Kopier',\n    'footer.summary.count': 'Antall: {count}',\n    'validation.required': 'Må fylles ut',\n    'validation.phoneNO': 'Må være 8 siffer' // an id of your own, returned by a validator\n};</code></pre>\n","samples":["translation"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"ValidationOption","group":"Type Aliases","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type ValidationOption = string | number | boolean | null | ValidationOption[] | { [key: string]: ValidationOption }"],"doc":"<p>The value of an option of a <a href=\"#api/index.ValidationRuleObject\">ValidationRuleObject</a>: plain JSON - text, a number, <code>true</code> /\n<code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, or a list or an object of them. So the rules can be stored, e.g. in a backend.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { ValidationOption } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst values: ValidationOption = ['Norway', 'Sweden'];</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"ValidationRule","group":"Type Aliases","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type ValidationRule = string | ValidationRuleObject"],"doc":"<p>A validation rule of a field, in <code>FieldConfig.validate</code>: the id of a validator as text, for a rule\nwithout options (<code>&#39;required&#39;</code>), or a <a href=\"#api/index.ValidationRuleObject\">ValidationRuleObject</a> with the options\n(<code>{ id: &#39;min&#39;, value: 0 }</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type ValidationRule } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst ageRules: ValidationRule[] = ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }, { id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'too old' }];\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { age: { type: 'number', validate: ageRules } } });</code></pre>\n","samples":["validation"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"Validator","group":"Type Aliases","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type Validator = (value: unknown, row: Entity, rule: ValidationRuleObject) => string | null"],"doc":"<p>Checks one value of a field: returns <code>null</code> when the value is valid (<code>&#39;&#39;</code> counts as valid too),\nelse the error. Register it under an id with <a href=\"#api/index.registerValidator\">registerValidator</a>, and name it in a field&#39;s\nrules.</p>\n<p>The error is a text id - a <code>validation.*</code> id of <a href=\"#api/index.TEXTS\">TEXTS</a>, like the built-ins return, or an id\nof your own given a text with <code>setTexts()</code> - or the message itself. The rule&#39;s own <code>message</code> is used\ninstead when it has one, and <code>{name}</code> in the message is replaced by the rule&#39;s option <code>name</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>value</code> - the field&#39;s value in the row</li>\n<li><code>row</code> - the row, to compare with its other fields. Such a validator runs when its own field is\nedited, and on <code>validate()</code></li>\n<li><code>rule</code> - the rule written out, with its options: <code>{ id, ...options, message? }</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A blank value (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>) should pass, the way the built-ins other than\n<code>required</code> do: an optional field can be empty, the <code>required</code> rule makes it fail. Do not use\n<code>this</code>, keep it fast (it runs on every key the user types), and do not change the row.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { registerValidator, type Validator } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// { id: 'afterStart' }: a date field `end` not before the row's `start`\nconst afterStart: Validator = (value, row) =&gt; {\n    if (!(value instanceof Date) || !(row.start instanceof Date)) {\n        return null; // an empty value is the required rule's job\n    }\n    return value &lt; row.start ? 'Must not be before the start' : null;\n};\nregisterValidator('afterStart', afterStart);</code></pre>\n","samples":["validation"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"BooleanFormatter","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const BooleanFormatter: { format: any; parse: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for booleans, the default for <code>boolean</code> fields. Registered as <code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code>, the options\nname it by that id (see <a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>). The grid shows a boolean field as a\ncheckbox from the raw value, this formatter is for the places that show it as text: the filter\nstring, group headers, and a value pasted into the column.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - <code>true</code> -&gt; <code>&#39;true&#39;</code>, <code>false</code> -&gt; <code>&#39;false&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for null and undefined</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> / <code>parseFilter</code> - <code>&#39;true&#39;</code> (any case, spaces around it ignored) is true, blank text,\nnull and undefined are null, any other text is false. A boolean passes through</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { BooleanFormatter, Datasource, registerFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nBooleanFormatter.format(true); // 'true'\nBooleanFormatter.parse('TRUE'); // true\nBooleanFormatter.parse(''); // null\n\n// show yes/no in the filter string and group headers\nconst yesNo = { ...BooleanFormatter, format: (value: unknown) =&gt; (value === true ? 'yes' : value === false ? 'no' : '') };\nregisterFormatter('yesNo', yesNo);\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { active: { type: 'boolean', formatter: 'yesNo' } }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["formatters"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"DATE_PRESETS","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const DATE_PRESETS: readonly DatePreset[]"],"doc":"<p>Every quick date filter, in the order of the filter menu: in pairs, this one and the one before -\nthe menu shows them in two columns. See <a href=\"#api/index.DatePreset\">DatePreset</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { DATE_PRESETS, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// every quick date filter on the due date, Overdue too\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: { dueDate: { datePresets: [...DATE_PRESETS] } },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['dueDate'], width: 110 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"DateFormatterDDMMYYYY","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const DateFormatterDDMMYYYY: { placeholder: string; format: any; parse: any; parseFilter: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for dates as <code>DD.MM.YYYY</code>, e.g. <code>20.11.2026</code>. Registered as <code>&#39;dateDDMMYYYY&#39;</code>, the options\nname it by that id (see <a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - a <code>Date</code> (or a date string/timestamp) as <code>20.11.2026</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for a blank or invalid date</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> - <code>&#39;20.11.2026&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code>. A month or year left out is the current one (<code>&#39;20&#39;</code> is the\n20th of this month), and the time of day is the current time, so an edited date keeps a time.\nA <code>Date</code> passes through, null and undefined are returned as they are, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> gives null and text\nthat is no date gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>parseFilter</code> - like <code>parse</code>, but at the start of the day (00:00:00.000). A <code>Date</code> is moved to the\nstart of its day</li>\n<li><code>placeholder</code> - <code>&#39;DD.MM.YYYY&#39;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, DateFormatterDDMMYYYY } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nDateFormatterDDMMYYYY.format(new Date(2026, 10, 20)); // '20.11.2026'\nDateFormatterDDMMYYYY.parseFilter('20.11.2026'); // Date 2026-11-20 00:00:00\n\n// one field, by its id\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { born: { type: 'date', formatter: 'dateDDMMYYYY' } }\n});\n\n// or every date field\nconst datasource2 = new Datasource({\n    fields: { born: 'date', hired: 'date' },\n    formatters: { date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["formatters"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"DateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const DateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS: { placeholder: string; format: any; parse: any; parseFilter: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for a date with time as <code>DD.MM.YYYYTHH:MM:SS</code>, e.g. <code>20.11.2026T13:45:12</code>. Registered as\n<code>&#39;datetimeDDMMYYYY&#39;</code>, the options name it by that id (see <a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - a <code>Date</code> (or a date string/timestamp) as <code>20.11.2026T13:45:12</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for a blank or\ninvalid date</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> - <code>&#39;20.11.2026T13:45:12&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code>. A month or year left out is the current one, and so\nare the hours, minutes and seconds left out. A <code>Date</code> passes through, null and undefined are\nreturned as they are, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> gives null and text that is no date gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>parseFilter</code> - like <code>parse</code>, but the time parts left out are 0, and so are the milliseconds:\n<code>&#39;20.11.2026&#39;</code> is 2026-11-20 00:00:00.000. A <code>Date</code> passes through</li>\n<li><code>placeholder</code> - <code>&#39;DD.MM.YYYYTHH:MM:SS&#39;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, DateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nDateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS.format(new Date(2026, 10, 20, 13, 45, 12)); // '20.11.2026T13:45:12'\n\n// a field names it by its id\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { lastLogin: { type: 'datetime', formatter: 'datetimeDDMMYYYY' } }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["formatters"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"DateFormatterYYYYMMDD","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const DateFormatterYYYYMMDD: { placeholder: string; format: any; parse: any; parseFilter: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for dates as <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, e.g. <code>2026-11-20</code>. The default for <code>date</code> fields. Registered as\n<code>&#39;dateYYYYMMDD&#39;</code>, the options name it by that id (see <a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - a <code>Date</code> (or a date string/timestamp) as <code>2026-11-20</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for a blank or invalid date</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> - <code>&#39;2026-11-20&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code>. A month or day left out is the current one\n(<code>&#39;2026&#39;</code> is today&#39;s month and day in 2026), and the time of day is the current time, so an\nedited date keeps a time. A <code>Date</code> passes through, null and undefined are returned as they are,\n<code>&#39;&#39;</code> gives null and text that is no date gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>parseFilter</code> - like <code>parse</code>, but at the start of the day (00:00:00.000). A <code>Date</code> is moved to the\nstart of its day</li>\n<li><code>placeholder</code> - <code>&#39;YYYY-MM-DD&#39;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, DateFormatterYYYYMMDD } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nDateFormatterYYYYMMDD.format(new Date(2026, 10, 20, 13, 45)); // '2026-11-20'\nDateFormatterYYYYMMDD.parse('2026-11-20'); // Date 2026-11-20, current time of day\nDateFormatterYYYYMMDD.parseFilter('2026-11-20'); // Date 2026-11-20 00:00:00\n\n// the default for date fields, set it by its id on one field or as the default to be explicit\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { born: { type: 'date', formatter: 'dateYYYYMMDD' } },\n    formatters: { date: 'dateYYYYMMDD' }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["formatters"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS: { placeholder: string; format: any; parse: any; parseFilter: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for a date with time as <code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS</code>, e.g. <code>2026-11-20T13:45:12</code>. The default\nfor <code>datetime</code> fields. Registered as <code>&#39;datetimeYYYYMMDD&#39;</code>, the options name it by that id (see\n<a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - a <code>Date</code> (or a date string/timestamp) as <code>2026-11-20T13:45:12</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for a blank or\ninvalid date</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> - <code>&#39;2026-11-20T13:45:12&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code>. A month or day left out is the current one, and so\nare the hours, minutes and seconds left out (<code>&#39;2026-11-20T13&#39;</code> is 13 o&#39;clock and the current\nminutes and seconds). A <code>Date</code> passes through, null and undefined are returned as they are,\n<code>&#39;&#39;</code> gives null and text that is no date gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>parseFilter</code> - like <code>parse</code>, but the time parts left out are 0, and so are the milliseconds:\n<code>&#39;2026-11-20&#39;</code> is 2026-11-20 00:00:00.000. A <code>Date</code> passes through</li>\n<li><code>placeholder</code> - <code>&#39;YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS&#39;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nDateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS.format(new Date(2026, 10, 20, 13, 45, 12)); // '2026-11-20T13:45:12'\nDateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS.parseFilter('2026-11-20T13'); // Date 2026-11-20 13:00:00\n\n// the default for datetime fields\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { lastLogin: 'datetime' }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["formatters"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"GRID_CONFIG_VERSION","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const GRID_CONFIG_VERSION: 2"],"doc":"<p>The version of the saved grid <code>exportConfig()</code> writes. A saved grid without a version is version 1:\na config v5 saved (<code>saveConfig()</code>) or was written for. <code>convertGridConfig()</code> makes the one of this\nversion of every older one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1</strong> - v5: <code>attributes</code>, <code>columnsPinnedLeft</code> / <code>columnsCenter</code> / <code>columnsPinnedRight</code>, and the\nstate - <code>sorting</code>, <code>grouping</code>, <code>expandedGroups</code>, <code>filter</code>, a column&#39;s <code>currentFilterValue</code> - in the\nconfig. No <code>version</code></li>\n<li><strong>2</strong> - v6: <code>{ version: 2, config, state, views }</code> - the <a href=\"#api/index.GridConfig\">GridConfig</a>, the <a href=\"#api/index.GridState\">GridState</a> and\nthe named views (<code>views</code>, optional: every view, the ones the user saved too)</li>\n</ul>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"IMPORT_LIMITS","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const IMPORT_LIMITS: { cells: 1000000; columns: 1000; fileSize: number; rows: 100000; unzippedSize: number }"],"doc":"<p>The most a file the grid imports may hold. A file over a limit is not read: <a href=\"#api/index.readImportFile\">readImportFile</a>\nrejects with an <a href=\"#api/index.ImportFileError\">ImportFileError</a> of the problem <code>&#39;tooLarge&#39;</code>, the import dialog says so.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>limit</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>fileSize</code></td>\n<td>20 MB: the bytes of the file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>unzippedSize</code></td>\n<td>100 MB: an .xlsx file is a ZIP, the parts read from it inflated - all of them together</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rows</code></td>\n<td>100 000 rows of a sheet or a CSV file, a header row included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columns</code></td>\n<td>1 000 columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cells</code></td>\n<td>1 000 000 cells: the CSV fields, the cells of the sheet that have a value</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"MENU_IDS","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const MENU_IDS: { column: readonly [\"column.select\", \"column.select.column\", \"column.select.columnsTo\", \"column.select.all\", \"column.select.clear\", \"column.copy.separator\", \"column.copy.column\", \"column.copy.selectedColumns\", \"column.copy.namesSeparator\", \"column.copy.headers\", \"column.copy.header\", \"column.copy.selectedHeaders\"]; filter: readonly [\"filter.filter\", \"filter.operator\", \"filter.operator.equal\", \"filter.operator.notEqualTo\", \"filter.operator.lessThan\", \"filter.operator.lessThanOrEqualTo\", \"filter.operator.greaterThan\", \"filter.operator.greaterThanOrEqualTo\", \"filter.operator.between\", \"filter.operator.contains\", \"filter.operator.doesNotContain\", \"filter.operator.beginWith\", \"filter.operator.endWith\", \"filter.matchCase\", \"filter.filterOnType\", \"filter.filter.blank\", \"filter.filter.isBlank\", \"filter.filter.isNotBlank\", \"filter.filter.clear\", \"filter.filter.clearAll\", \"filter.filter.advancedSeparator\", \"filter.filter.advanced\", \"filter.values\", \"filter.range\", \"filter.dates\"]; header: readonly [\"header.column.pin\", \"header.column.pinLeft\", \"header.column.pinLeftCopy\", \"header.column.pinRight\", \"header.column.pinRightCopy\", \"header.column.unpin\", \"header.column.hide\", \"header.column.removeSorting\", \"header.column.chooserSeparator\", \"header.column.chooser\", \"header.size\", \"header.size.column\", \"header.size.all\", \"header.size.resetColumn\", \"header.size.resetAll\", \"header.format\", \"header.format.default\", \"header.format.defaultSeparator\", \"header.format.date.iso\", \"header.format.date.norwegian\", \"header.format.date.norwegianShort\", \"header.format.date.us\", \"header.format.date.uk\", \"header.format.date.medium\", \"header.format.date.long\", \"header.format.date.full\", \"header.format.timeSeparator\", \"header.format.date.isoTime\", \"header.format.date.isoSeconds\", \"header.format.date.norwegianTime\", \"header.format.date.norwegianSeconds\", \"header.format.date.time\", \"header.format.date.timeSeconds\", \"header.format.partSeparator\", \"header.format.date.weekday\", \"header.format.date.weekIso\", \"header.format.date.weekUs\", \"header.format.date.month\", \"header.format.date.quarter\", \"header.format.date.year\", \"header.format.date.daysUntil\", \"header.format.number.norwegian\", \"header.format.number.english\", \"header.format.number.swiss\", \"header.format.number.plain\", \"header.format.settingsSeparator\", \"header.format.decimals\", \"header.format.decimals.asIs\", \"header.format.decimals.0\", \"header.format.decimals.1\", \"header.format.decimals.2\", \"header.format.decimals.3\", \"header.format.decimals.4\", \"header.format.thousands\", \"header.format.thousands.none\", \"header.format.thousands.space\", \"header.format.thousands.comma\", \"header.format.thousands.dot\", \"header.format.thousands.apostrophe\", \"header.format.decimalMark\", \"header.format.decimalMark.dot\", \"header.format.decimalMark.comma\", \"header.format.percent\", \"header.settings\", \"header.shortcuts\", \"header.import\", \"header.aggregate\", \"header.aggregate.sum\", \"header.aggregate.avg\", \"header.aggregate.min\", \"header.aggregate.max\", \"header.aggregate.count\", \"header.aggregate.totalsSeparator\", \"header.aggregate.totalsRow\", \"header.grouping\", \"header.grouping.expandAll\", \"header.grouping.collapseAll\", \"header.grouping.clear\"]; row: readonly [\"row.edit\", \"row.edit.undo\", \"row.edit.redo\", \"row.edit.revertCell\", \"row.edit.revertRow\", \"row.edit.revertRows\", \"row.select\", \"row.select.row\", \"row.select.rowsTo\", \"row.select.allRows\", \"row.select.cellsSeparator\", \"row.select.rangeTo\", \"row.select.addCell\", \"row.select.clearSeparator\", \"row.select.clear\", \"row.copy\", \"row.copy.cell\", \"row.copy.column\", \"row.copy.row\", \"row.copy.selectedColumns\", \"row.copy.range\", \"row.paste\", \"row.paste.current\", \"row.paste.cell\", \"row.paste.range\", \"row.paste.fillDown\", \"row.paste.newRowsSeparator\", \"row.paste.newRows\", \"row.clear\", \"row.clear.current\", \"row.clear.cell\", \"row.clear.range\", \"row.delete\", \"row.delete.row\", \"row.delete.rows\", \"row.delete.undo\", \"row.filter\", \"row.filter.replace\", \"row.filter.add\", \"row.view\", \"row.view.dimRepeatedValues\", \"row.view.changedOnly\", \"row.size\", \"row.size.fitColumn\", \"row.size.fitRow\", \"row.size.fitRows\", \"row.size.fitAllRows\", \"row.size.resetRow\", \"row.size.resetAllRows\", \"row.export\", \"row.export.excel\", \"row.export.excelSelected\"] }"],"doc":"<p>The ids of the grid&#39;s own menu entries, per menu: the <code>defaults</code> a <a href=\"#api/index.GridMenus\">GridMenus</a> function\ngets. The lists are flat, in the order of the tree: a submenu, then what is in it. Every entry is\nalways in <code>defaults</code>: the ones that do not apply (paste on a readonly cell, the range entries\nwithout a range ...) have a <code>visible</code> callback that hides them, and a submenu with nothing left to show is\nhidden too.</p>\n<p>The ids are <code>menu.part.entry</code>: an entry in a submenu starts with the id of the submenu\n(<code>row.copy.cell</code> is in <code>row.copy</code>), and the entries of a part of the menu share its start\n(<code>header.column.*</code>: pin, hide, remove sorting, the column chooser). The ids stay the same between\nversions, so a menu changed by id keeps working. They are text ids too: the label of an entry is\nthe text of its id, its hint the text <code>&lt;id&gt;.hint</code> - see <a href=\"#api/index.TEXTS\">TEXTS</a> and <a href=\"#api/index.setTexts\">setTexts</a>. The\n&quot;shown as&quot; below is the English text; the separators and the blocks <code>filter.values</code> and <code>filter.range</code>\nhave no text of their own.</p>\n<p>The menus as they open, a <code>›</code> is a submenu:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>row menu                          header menu                     filter menu\nUndo / Redo ›                     Pin ›                           Filter ›\nSelect ›                          Hide column                     the value list / the range block\nCopy ›                            Remove sorting\nPaste ›                           ----                            in Filter ›:\nClear ›                           Column chooser…                 Operator ›\nDelete ›                          Size ›                          Blank ›\nFilter ›                          Format ›                        Clear Filter\nView ›                            Aggregate ›                     Clear All Filters\nSize ›                            Grouping ›                      ----\nExport ›                                                          Advanced Filter…\n\ncolumn menu                       in Select › of the row menu:     in Select › of the column menu:\nSelect ›                          This row                        This column\n----                              Rows from N to here             Columns from N to here\nCopy column (all rows)            All rows                        All columns\nCopy selected columns (all rows)  ----                            Clear column selection\n----                              Range from the active cell to here\nCopy header names                 Add this cell to the ranges\nCopy header name                  ----\nCopy selected header names        Clear selection</code></pre>\n<p>Row menu (<code>GridMenus.row</code>), right click on a cell - or on a row number: the same menu without a cell,\n<code>context.field</code> is <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, the entries of the cell and its column are greyed out (Undo cell edit, Copy\n› Cell Value and Column, Paste cell and Clear cell, Filter ›, Fit column to this cell, the cell\nentries of Select ›):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>row.edit</code></td>\n<td>Undo / Redo › - a submenu, first in the menu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo</code></td>\n<td>Undo last change (Ctrl+Z) - <code>datasource.undo()</code>, the last edit, paste, clear, delete or revert. Greyed out when there is nothing to undo</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo</code></td>\n<td>Redo (Ctrl+Y) - <code>datasource.redo()</code>. Greyed out when there is nothing to redo</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertCell</code></td>\n<td>Undo cell edit - the cell&#39;s original value (<code>datasource.resetField()</code>). Greyed out when the cell is not edited</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRow</code></td>\n<td>Undo row edits - the row right clicked back to its original values, unmarked for deletion (<code>datasource.resetRows(row)</code>), no selection needed; a new row is marked for deletion (it is not on the server). Greyed out when the row is not edited, not marked and not new</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRows</code></td>\n<td>Undo row edits (selected rows) - the selected rows back, unmarked for deletion (<code>datasource.resetRows()</code>); the new ones marked for deletion. Greyed out when no selected row is edited, marked or new</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select</code></td>\n<td>Select › - a submenu: what Shift and Ctrl do with the mouse, for a finger (and every input)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.row</code></td>\n<td>This row - the row in or out of the selection, as Ctrl+click on its number; a ✓ while it is selected. In <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode it replaces the row selected</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.rowsTo</code></td>\n<td>Rows from N to here - from the anchor (N, the row selected last without Shift) to this row, as Shift+click on its number. Greyed out without an anchor (N is this row then), when the anchor is this row, and in <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.allRows</code></td>\n<td>All rows - <code>datasource.selectAll()</code>. Greyed out in <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> mode and when every row is selected</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.cellsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>the selection mode is not <code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.rangeTo</code></td>\n<td>Range from the active cell to here - as Shift+click on the cell: the last range from where it starts, or from the cell with the focus, to this cell. Greyed out when there is neither, when it would start at this cell, and on a row number</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.addCell</code></td>\n<td>Add this cell to the ranges - as Ctrl+click on the cell: a range of the cell, the active cell kept as a range when there is none yet. Greyed out when the cell is in a range, and on a row number</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.clearSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.select.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear selection - no row selected, no range of cells. Greyed out when there is neither</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy</code></td>\n<td>Copy › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.cell</code></td>\n<td>Cell Value</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.column</code></td>\n<td>Column (selected rows). Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.row</code></td>\n<td>Row (selected rows). Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td>Row (selected rows/col) - the selected columns, all when none is selected. Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.range</code></td>\n<td>Range - the ranges of cells, as Ctrl+C copies them: tab separated text and an html table. Greyed out without a range</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste</code></td>\n<td>Paste › - a submenu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.current</code></td>\n<td>Paste cell - the cell right clicked. Greyed out when it is readonly - also on a row <code>edit</code> does not let the user change (<code>change: false</code>, a row that is not new)</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.cell</code></td>\n<td>Paste cell (selected rows) - the column of the selected rows, the readonly cells skipped. Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.range</code></td>\n<td>Paste range - the clipboard as Ctrl+V pastes it with ranges: one value into every cell of them, a block from the top left of the last one. Greyed out without a range</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.fillDown</code></td>\n<td>Fill down (Ctrl+D) - as Ctrl+D (<code>gridInterface.fillDown()</code>): in every range the values of its top row go into the rows under it, a range of one row gets the row above it; without a range the cell right clicked gets the value of the cell above it. Readonly cells skipped. Greyed out without a range when the cell is readonly</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.newRowsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td><code>GridConfig.importMenu</code>, the grid is editable and <code>edit</code> allows <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.newRows</code></td>\n<td>As new rows… - the import dialog with the rows on the clipboard (<code>gridInterface.openImport(text)</code>): their columns from this cell&#39;s column on, or by the names of a header row; new rows on Import</td>\n<td><code>GridConfig.importMenu</code>, the grid is editable and <code>edit</code> allows <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear › - a submenu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.current</code></td>\n<td>Clear cell - empties the cell right clicked (<code>null</code>, also a number field: emptying the cell by typing stores 0). Greyed out when it is readonly, as Paste cell</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.cell</code></td>\n<td>Clear cell (selected rows) - empties the column of the selected rows, the readonly cells skipped. Greyed out with no row selected</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.range</code></td>\n<td>Clear range - every cell of the ranges, as Delete: <code>null</code>, the readonly cells skipped. Greyed out without a range</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and <code>edit</code> allows <code>change</code> or <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete</code></td>\n<td>Delete › - a submenu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.row</code></td>\n<td>Row - marks the row right clicked for deletion. Greyed out when it is marked, when <code>edit.delete</code> is <code>false</code> and the row is not new (a new row can always be taken out), or the <code>isReadonly</code> hook locks it</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.rows</code></td>\n<td>Rows (selected rows) - marks them for deletion (<code>markForDeletion()</code>), they stay in the grid until the changes are accepted. Skips a row the <code>isReadonly</code> hook returns <code>true</code> for in every column of the layout, and with <code>edit.delete: false</code> the rows that are not new. Greyed out when no selected row is left to mark</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.undo</code></td>\n<td>Undo (selected rows) - takes the mark off the selected rows marked for deletion (<code>unmarkForDeletion()</code>). Greyed out when no selected row is marked</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter</code></td>\n<td>Filter › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.replace</code></td>\n<td>Replace filter (all)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.add</code></td>\n<td>Add to filter (and)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view</code></td>\n<td>View › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.dimRepeatedValues</code></td>\n<td>Use dimmed values, a toggle with a ✓ when on</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.changedOnly</code></td>\n<td>Changed rows only, a toggle with a ✓ when on - the new, edited and deleted rows of the filter (<code>datasource.filterChangedOnly()</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size</code></td>\n<td>Size › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitColumn</code></td>\n<td>Fit column to this cell - the column as wide as the cell&#39;s text (<code>gridInterface.fitColumns([field], { rows: [row] })</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitRow</code></td>\n<td>Fit row height - the row right clicked as high as its multiline cells need (<code>gridInterface.fitRows(row)</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitRows</code></td>\n<td>Fit row height (selected rows) - the selected rows and the rows of the ranges of cells. Greyed out without either</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.fitAllRows</code></td>\n<td>Fit all rows - every row the filter lets through (<code>fitRows()</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.resetRow</code></td>\n<td>Reset row height - the row right clicked (<code>resetRowHeights(row)</code>). Greyed out when it has the height of the layout</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.size.resetAllRows</code></td>\n<td>Reset all row heights (<code>resetRowHeights()</code>). Greyed out when no row has a height of its own</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export</code></td>\n<td>Export › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.excel</code></td>\n<td>Excel, all rows - <code>gridInterface.downloadExcel()</code>: the rows the filter lets through, as an .xlsx file</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.excelSelected</code></td>\n<td>Excel, selected rows - <code>downloadExcel(&#39;export.xlsx&#39;, { rows: &#39;selected&#39; })</code>. Greyed out when no row is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Header menu (<code>GridMenus.header</code>), right click on a header label:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pin</code></td>\n<td>Pin › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeft</code></td>\n<td>Pin left</td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeftCopy</code></td>\n<td>Pin left (copy)</td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRight</code></td>\n<td>Pin right</td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRightCopy</code></td>\n<td>Pin right (copy)</td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.unpin</code></td>\n<td>Unpin</td>\n<td>pinned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.hide</code></td>\n<td>Hide column</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.removeSorting</code></td>\n<td>Remove sorting - keeps the sort of the grouped fields</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooserSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooser</code></td>\n<td>Column chooser…</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size</code></td>\n<td>Size › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.column</code></td>\n<td>Fit column width - to its content and label (<code>gridInterface.fitColumns([field])</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.all</code></td>\n<td>Fit all columns (<code>fitColumns()</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.resetColumn</code></td>\n<td>Reset column width - the width of the config (<code>resetColumnWidths([field])</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.resetAll</code></td>\n<td>Reset all column widths (<code>resetColumnWidths()</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format</code></td>\n<td>Format › - a submenu: how the cells show the value, the display format in use with a ✓ (<code>GridState.formats</code>, see <a href=\"#api/index.ColumnFormat\">ColumnFormat</a>). Each hint is an example: today, or 1234567.891</td>\n<td>a date, datetime or number field, not a lookup column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.default</code></td>\n<td>Default - no display format: the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.defaultSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.iso</code> ... <code>header.format.date.full</code></td>\n<td>ISO, Norwegian, Norwegian short year, US, UK, Short month name, Month name, Day and month name - the date written out (<code>ColumnFormat.date</code>)</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.timeSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.isoTime</code> ... <code>header.format.date.timeSeconds</code></td>\n<td>ISO with time, ISO with seconds, Norwegian with time, Norwegian with seconds, Time, Time with seconds</td>\n<td>a datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.partSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.date.weekday</code> ... <code>header.format.date.daysUntil</code></td>\n<td>Day of week, Week ISO, Week US, Month, Quarter, Year, Days until</td>\n<td>a date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.number.norwegian</code> ... <code>header.format.number.plain</code></td>\n<td>Norwegian (1 234 567,891), English, Swiss, Plain: the separators (<code>ColumnFormat.number</code>), the decimals kept</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.settingsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimals</code></td>\n<td>Decimals › - As is, 0 to 4 (<code>header.format.decimals.asIs</code>, <code>.0</code> ... <code>.4</code>)</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.thousands</code></td>\n<td>Thousands separator › - None, Space, Comma, Dot, Apostrophe (<code>.none</code>, <code>.space</code>, <code>.comma</code>, <code>.dot</code>, <code>.apostrophe</code>). The character of the decimal mark greyed out</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.decimalMark</code></td>\n<td>Decimal mark › - Dot, Comma (<code>.dot</code>, <code>.comma</code>). The character of the thousands separator greyed out</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.format.percent</code></td>\n<td>Percent, a toggle: the number times 100 with <code> %</code></td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.settings</code></td>\n<td>Column settings… - the grid config dialog on this column (<code>gridInterface.openColumnSettings(field)</code>): its display format, a text column&#39;s template and link</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.shortcuts</code></td>\n<td>Keyboard shortcuts… - the keyboard shortcuts help (<code>gridInterface.openShortcutHelp()</code>), its hint the key that opens it: Ctrl+/ (<code>header.shortcuts.hint</code>, ⌘/ on a Mac)</td>\n<td><code>GridConfig.shortcutHelp</code> (the default)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.import</code></td>\n<td>Import rows… - the import dialog (<code>gridInterface.openImport()</code>): a CSV or Excel file, or rows pasted from a spreadsheet, as new rows</td>\n<td><code>GridConfig.importMenu</code>, the grid is editable and <code>edit</code> allows <code>add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate</code></td>\n<td>Aggregate › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.sum</code></td>\n<td>Sum - a toggle with a ✓ when on: the column&#39;s sum in the group rows and the totals row (<code>GridState.aggregates</code>)</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.avg</code></td>\n<td>Average, a toggle</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.min</code></td>\n<td>Min, a toggle</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.max</code></td>\n<td>Max, a toggle</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.count</code></td>\n<td>Count, a toggle: the values that are not blank</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.totalsSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.aggregate.totalsRow</code></td>\n<td>Totals row, a toggle (<code>GridState.totalsRow</code>)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping</code></td>\n<td>Grouping › - a submenu</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.expandAll</code></td>\n<td>Expand all</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.collapseAll</code></td>\n<td>Collapse all</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear all</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Filter menu (<code>GridMenus.filter</code>), right click on a header filter:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter</code></td>\n<td>Filter › - a submenu, first in the menu: the entries below up to the value list</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator</code></td>\n<td>Operator › - a submenu in Filter ›, the operator in use has a ✓. The column&#39;s operators the header filter&#39;s input takes (<code>ColumnConfig.filterOperators</code>, else its type&#39;s, see <a href=\"#api/index.operatorsFor\">operatorsFor</a>)</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.equal</code></td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.notEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Not Equal</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThan</code></td>\n<td>Less than</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Less than or equal</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThan</code></td>\n<td>Greater than</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Greater than or equal</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.between</code></td>\n<td>Between - typed as <code>from..to</code></td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.contains</code></td>\n<td>Contains</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.doesNotContain</code></td>\n<td>Does not contain</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.beginWith</code></td>\n<td>Begins with</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.endWith</code></td>\n<td>Ends with</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.matchCase</code></td>\n<td>Match case - a toggle with a ✓ (<code>GridState.headerFilters[field].caseSensitive</code>). On and greyed out, with the hint &quot;always&quot;, for a column with <code>filterCaseSensitive</code></td>\n<td>a text field, a lookup column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filterOnType</code></td>\n<td>Filter as you type - a toggle with a ✓: the header filter filters after a pause in typing (<code>GridState.headerFilters[field].filterOnType</code>, <code>ColumnConfig.filterOnType</code>)</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.blank</code></td>\n<td>Blank › - a submenu in Filter ›</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isBlank</code></td>\n<td>Is blank - the column&#39;s conditions replaced with <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>the column has <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isNotBlank</code></td>\n<td>Is not blank</td>\n<td>the column has <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear Filter - the column&#39;s conditions</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clearAll</code></td>\n<td>Clear All Filters</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advancedSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advanced</code></td>\n<td>Advanced Filter… - the filter editor</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values</code></td>\n<td>the value list: search box, &quot;Select all&quot; and a checkbox per value, &quot;Cancel&quot; and &quot;Apply&quot; - a <a href=\"#api/index.MenuBlock\">MenuBlock</a>, its texts are <code>filter.values.*</code></td>\n<td>a text field, a lookup column - with <code>IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.range</code></td>\n<td>the range block: the operators of Operator › as a row of buttons (=, ≠, &lt;, ≤, &gt;, ≥, Between), an input - From and To for Between - &quot;Cancel&quot; and &quot;Apply&quot;: as the operator picked in Operator › and the value typed in the header filter. A <a href=\"#api/index.MenuBlock\">MenuBlock</a>, its texts are <code>filter.range.*</code></td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field, not a lookup column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.dates</code></td>\n<td>the quick dates, under the range block: a button each - Today, Yesterday, This week, Last week ... (<code>ColumnConfig.datePresets</code>) - one click filters the column on its days, from today, and the header filter keeps it (<code>GridState.headerFilters[field].datePreset</code>). A <a href=\"#api/index.MenuBlock\">MenuBlock</a>, its texts are <code>filter.dates.*</code></td>\n<td>a date or datetime field whose header filter has <code>BETWEEN</code>, not a lookup column, <code>datePresets</code> not <code>false</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The items of Operator › are the operator choice of the header filter&#39;s operator indicator too\n(<code>GridConfig.filterOperatorIndicator</code>), a menu of its own: <code>GridMenus.filter</code> is not called for it. Under\nthem, after a line (<code>filter.filterOnTypeSeparator</code>), it has <code>filter.filterOnType</code>.</p>\n<p>Column menu (<code>GridMenus.column</code>), right click on a column number - the row of numbers under the\nheader filters - or Shift+F10 on one with the focus. Select ›, then the copies, flat: a Copy › around\nthem would only add a step. The columns are copied as the row menu copies rows - tab separated text\nand an html table, a header line of the labels with <code>copyHeaders</code> - the names as one line of text, a\ntab between them. The column numbers have no selection mode: Select › is always shown:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>column.select</code></td>\n<td>Select › - a submenu: what Shift and Ctrl do on a column number, for a finger (and every input)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.column</code></td>\n<td>This column - the column in or out, as Ctrl+click; a ✓ while it is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.columnsTo</code></td>\n<td>Columns from N to here - from the anchor (N, the column clicked last without Shift) to this one, as Shift+click. Greyed out without an anchor (N is this column then) and when the anchor is this column</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.all</code></td>\n<td>All columns - <code>selectColumns()</code> with every number. Greyed out when every column is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.select.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear column selection - <code>selectColumns([])</code>. Greyed out with none selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.separator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.column</code></td>\n<td>Copy column (all rows) - the column right clicked, every displayed data row. A column of stacked fields: each of them</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td>Copy selected columns (all rows) - the selected columns (<code>getSelectedColumns()</code>), every displayed data row. Greyed out with no column selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.namesSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.headers</code></td>\n<td>Copy header names - the label of every field of the layout</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.header</code></td>\n<td>Copy header name - the label of the column right clicked, of each of its fields</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column.copy.selectedHeaders</code></td>\n<td>Copy selected header names - the labels of the selected columns. Greyed out with no column selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type GridHooks, MENU_IDS } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a row menu without the copy submenu\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: { row: (defaults) =&gt; defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; !entry.id.startsWith('row.copy')) }\n};\n\nMENU_IDS.row.includes('row.paste.cell'); // true</code></pre>\n","samples":["context-menu"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"NumberFormatterComma","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const NumberFormatterComma: { format: any; parse: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for numbers with a comma as the decimal separator: <code>12,5</code>. Registered as <code>&#39;numberComma&#39;</code>,\nthe options name it by that id (see <a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - a number as text with a comma, <code>12.5</code> -&gt; <code>&#39;12,5&#39;</code>. Anything that does not read as a\nnumber (null, undefined, <code>&#39;abc&#39;</code>) gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>parse</code> / <code>parseFilter</code> - <code>&#39;12,5&#39;</code> and <code>&#39;12.5&#39;</code> both give 12.5. A number passes through,\nnull and undefined give null, text that is no number (<code>&#39;&#39;</code> too) gives 0. So emptying a number\ncell in the grid stores 0; the row menu&#39;s Clear stores null. A formatter whose <code>parse</code> gives null\nfor <code>&#39;&#39;</code> stores null</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, NumberFormatterComma } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nNumberFormatterComma.format(1234.5); // '1234,5'\nNumberFormatterComma.parse('12,5'); // 12.5\n\n// one field, by its id\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberComma' } }\n});\n\n// or every number field\nconst datasource2 = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: 'number', amount: 'number' },\n    formatters: { number: 'numberComma' }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["formatters"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"NumberFormatterDot","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const NumberFormatterDot: { format: any; parse: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for numbers with a dot as the decimal separator: <code>12.5</code>. The default for <code>number</code> fields.\nRegistered as <code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code>, the options name it by that id (see <a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - a number as text, <code>12.5</code> -&gt; <code>&#39;12.5&#39;</code>. Anything that does not read as a number\n(null, undefined, <code>&#39;abc&#39;</code>) gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>parse</code> / <code>parseFilter</code> - <code>&#39;12.5&#39;</code> and <code>&#39;12,5&#39;</code> both give 12.5. A number passes through,\nnull and undefined give null, text that is no number (<code>&#39;&#39;</code> too) gives 0. So emptying a number\ncell in the grid stores 0; the row menu&#39;s Clear stores null. A formatter whose <code>parse</code> gives null\nfor <code>&#39;&#39;</code> stores null</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, NumberFormatterDot, registerFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nNumberFormatterDot.format(1234.5); // '1234.5'\nNumberFormatterDot.parse('12,5'); // 12.5\n\n// the default, set it by its id to be explicit\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberDot' } }\n});\n\n// a variant: spread it, replace what you need and register it under an id of your own\nconst twoDecimals = { ...NumberFormatterDot, format: (value: unknown) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? value.toFixed(2) : '') };\nregisterFormatter('twoDecimals', twoDecimals);</code></pre>\n","samples":["formatters"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"TextFormatter","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const TextFormatter: { format: any; parse: any }"],"doc":"<p>Formatter for text, the default for <code>text</code> fields (and so for every field not listed in <code>fields</code>).\nRegistered as <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>, the options name it by that id (see <a href=\"#api/index.registerFormatter\">registerFormatter</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> - the value as text, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for null and undefined, a date <code>2026-11-25</code> (with a time of day\n<code>2026-11-25T14:30:00</code>). Anything else goes through <code>String()</code>: 0 is <code>&#39;0&#39;</code>, false is <code>&#39;false&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>parse</code> / <code>parseFilter</code> - the text as it is</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, registerFormatter, TextFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nTextFormatter.format(null); // ''\nTextFormatter.format(42); // '42'\n\n// a variant: show the text in upper case, edit and filter it as it is\nconst upperCase = { ...TextFormatter, format: (value: unknown) =&gt; TextFormatter.format(value).toUpperCase() };\nregisterFormatter('upperCase', upperCase);\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { country: { formatter: 'upperCase' } }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["custom-formatter"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"TEXTS","group":"Variables","kind":"Variable","signatures":["const TEXTS: Readonly<Record<TextId, string>>"],"doc":"<p>Every text the grid and the datasource show, by id: the English defaults. Change them with\n<a href=\"#api/index.setTexts\">setTexts</a> (for the page) and <code>GridConfig.texts</code> (for one grid); this object stays as it is.</p>\n<p>The ids are grouped by area, the area first:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ids</th>\n<th>where</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>row.*</code>, <code>header.*</code>, <code>filter.*</code>, <code>column.*</code></td>\n<td>the right click menus: the ids of <a href=\"#api/index.MENU_IDS\">MENU_IDS</a>. A hint (the text in parentheses) is <code>&lt;id&gt;.hint</code>. <code>filter.values.*</code>: the value list of the filter menu, <code>filter.range.*</code> its range block, <code>filter.dates.*</code> its quick dates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.*</code></td>\n<td>the column chooser</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.*</code></td>\n<td>the filter editor, its field and operator menus. <code>filterEditor.operator.&lt;FilterOperator&gt;</code>: the names of the operators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.*</code></td>\n<td>the footer: the row count, the filter chips, the &quot;No filter&quot; button. <code>footer.operator.&lt;FilterOperator&gt;</code>: the short names of the operators (<code>=</code>, <code>contains</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>views.*</code></td>\n<td>the named views (<code>GridConfig.views</code>): the views button at the footer&#39;s left end, its menu and the name asked for by <em>Save as new view…</em> and <em>Rename…</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.*</code></td>\n<td>the aggregates of the columns: the label in front of a value in the group rows and the totals row (<code>aggregate.&lt;AggregateFunction&gt;</code>), and the label of the totals row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>format.*</code></td>\n<td>the display formats of the columns (<code>ColumnConfig.format</code>): <code>format.locale</code> the language of the names of a date, a BCP 47 tag for <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> (<code>&#39;en-US&#39;</code>; not shown, <code>&#39;nb-NO&#39;</code> shows <code>søndag 22. november 2026</code>), and how a week and a quarter are written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.*</code></td>\n<td>labels for screen readers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find.*</code></td>\n<td>the find bar of Ctrl+F: the input, the count of the matches, &quot;Match case&quot;, &quot;Filter rows&quot;, the buttons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>shortcuts.*</code></td>\n<td>the keyboard shortcuts help: its title, search and hint, the names of the keys (<code>shortcuts.key.*</code>), the title of each group of keys (<code>shortcuts.group.*</code>) and what each key does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.*</code></td>\n<td>the state of a row on its row number: the tooltip, and the text for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.*</code></td>\n<td>the cells: the marker of an edited cell, a cell of several lines and its editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lookup.*</code></td>\n<td>a lookup column (<code>ColumnConfig.editor</code>): the button of the cell, the select under it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.*</code></td>\n<td>the date picker: the buttons, the names of the months and the days, From and To of a range picker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.*</code></td>\n<td>the messages of the built-in validators. Made by the datasource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.*</code></td>\n<td><code>datasource.getFilterString()</code>: AND, OR and the operators. Made by the datasource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grouping.blank</code></td>\n<td>the value in the name of the group of blank values. Made by the datasource</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The tooltip of an element is <code>&lt;id&gt;.tooltip</code>: <code>filterEditor.addCondition.tooltip</code>. <code>{name}</code> in a text\nis filled in when it is shown (<code>&#39;Count: {count}&#39;</code>). A text that names a key - <code>row.edit.undo.hint</code>,\n<code>Ctrl+Z</code> - has a text of its own for a Mac, <code>&lt;id&gt;.mac</code> (<code>⌘Z</code>): the grid shows that one on a Mac.</p>\n<p>The texts guide has every id with its text and where it is shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts, TEXTS } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nTEXTS['footer.summary.count']; // 'Count: {count}'\n\n// every id with its English text, e.g. to hand to a translator\nconst english: string = JSON.stringify(TEXTS, null, 2);\nconsole.log(english);\n\nsetTexts({ 'footer.summary.count': 'Antall: {count}' });</code></pre>\n","samples":["translation"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"and","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function and(...children: FilterExpression[]): FilterGroup"],"doc":"<p>A group where every child must match.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>children</code> conditions and groups. None gives an empty group, which filters out nothing</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { and, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// country = 'Norway' AND age &lt; 30\ndatasource.filter(and(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'), where('age', 'LESS_THAN', 30)));</code></pre>\n","samples":["filtering"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"convertGridConfig","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function convertGridConfig(saved: unknown): ExportedGrid"],"doc":"<p>A saved grid of any version as one of this version (<a href=\"#api/index.GRID_CONFIG_VERSION\">GRID_CONFIG_VERSION</a>): what\n<code>importConfig()</code> reads, and for a grid that is made from it - <code>createGrid()</code> with its <code>config</code>, then\n<code>setState()</code> with its <code>state</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No <code>version</code>, or 1</strong>: a config v5 saved or was written for, converted - <code>attributes</code> to\n<code>columns</code>, the <code>columns*</code> to <code>layout</code>, its sort, grouping, groups expanded, filter and header filter\nvalues to <code>initialState</code> and <code>state</code>. A column&#39;s <code>type</code> and <code>mandatory</code> are left out: they are the\ndatasource&#39;s fields&#39; in v6 (<code>{ type: &#39;date&#39; }</code>, <code>validate: [&#39;required&#39;]</code>). So are <code>tooltipText</code> and\n<code>selectionMode</code> (the texts, the datasource)</li>\n<li><strong>2</strong>: as it is, copied</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The config is checked when the grid takes it, as any config.</p>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for what is no saved grid, a version that is no whole number from 1, one newer\nthan this grid&#39;s, and a saved grid without a version that has <code>layout</code>, <code>columns</code> or <code>initialState</code> - a\nv6 config: give it <code>{ version: 2, config }</code></p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { convertGridConfig, createGrid, Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const host: HTMLElement;\nconst datasource = new Datasource();\nconst saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('my-grid') ?? 'null'); // v5's saveConfig(), or exportConfig()\nconst { config, state } = convertGridConfig(saved);\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });\nif (state) {\n    gridInterface.setState(state);\n}</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"convertV5Filter","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function convertV5Filter(filter: unknown): FilterExpression | null"],"doc":"<p>A filter of v5 as a filter of v6: a group&#39;s <code>logicalOperator</code> <code>&#39;OR&#39;</code> is an OR group, anything else an\nAND group (as v5 did); a condition&#39;s <code>attribute</code> is its <code>field</code>, <code>valueType: &#39;ATTRIBUTE&#39;</code> a\n<code>valueField</code>. A condition without a field or an operator is left out - v5 ignored it too. <code>null</code> for\n<code>null</code> / <code>undefined</code> and for a condition left out.</p>\n<p>Takes hostile input (a link, a request): what is no v5 filter throws.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>filter</code> the v5 filter, e.g. <code>JSON.parse(params.get(&#39;filter&#39;))</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Returns</strong> the v6 filter, or null for none</p>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for what is no v5 filter - a node that is no object, <code>filterArguments</code> that is no\narray, an operator that is none, a <code>BETWEEN</code> without <code>[from, to]</code> - and a <code>RangeError</code> for groups nested\ndeeper than 100</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { convertV5Filter } from '@simple-html/grid'; // or '@simple-html/grid/sql' on a backend\n\nconvertV5Filter({\n    type: 'GROUP',\n    logicalOperator: 'OR',\n    filterArguments: [\n        { attribute: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Norway' },\n        { attribute: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 'minAge', valueType: 'ATTRIBUTE' }\n    ]\n});\n// { group: 'OR', children: [\n//     { field: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Norway' },\n//     { field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', valueField: 'minAge' } ] }</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"createGrid","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function createGrid<T>(host: HTMLElement, options: CreateGridOptions<T>): GridInterface<T>"],"doc":"<p>Puts a grid in a container on the page, in one call: makes the <a href=\"#api/index.GridInterface\">GridInterface</a> for the config\nand the datasource, and a <code>&lt;simple-html-grid&gt;</code> element that fills the container (width and height\n100%), connects them and adds the element to the container.</p>\n<p>The container needs a size: the grid fills it, it does not grow with its rows.</p>\n<p>Its look is <code>@simple-html/grid/grid.css</code> (and <code>date.css</code> for the date picker of date cells), light\nor dark by the page&#39;s <code>color-scheme</code>. <code>@simple-html/grid/theme-tredespace.css</code> is a second theme,\nthe colours of the TreDeSpace UI widget library, on with <code>data-grid-theme=&quot;tredespace&quot;</code> on the\nroot of the page - see the theming guide.</p>\n<p><code>gridInterface.destroy()</code> takes the grid off the page again. To place and size the element yourself,\nsee <a href=\"#api/index.GridElement\">GridElement</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>host</code> the container, the element is added as its last child</li>\n<li><code>options</code> the config, the datasource and the hooks</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Returns</strong> the grid interface, to work with the grid</p>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> when the config has <code>isReadonly</code>, <code>cellClass</code> or <code>menus</code> (those are hooks), or\na column has v5&#39;s <code>mandatory</code> (it is the field&#39;s <code>required</code> rule)</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { age: 'number' } });\ndatasource.setData([{ name: 'Ann', age: 30 }, { name: 'Bob', age: 42 }]);\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['age'], width: 80 }] }\n};\n\n// &lt;div id=&quot;grid&quot; style=&quot;height: 400px&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(document.getElementById('grid')!, { config, datasource });\n\n// later, when the grid is not needed any more\ngridInterface.destroy();</code></pre>\n","samples":["basic-grid"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"escapeTextPattern","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function escapeTextPattern(text: string): string"],"doc":"<p>a text as a filter value that matches it as it is: <code>*</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>\\</code> escaped with ``.\nFor a value put into a header filter, where the user can see and change it</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { escapeTextPattern } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nescapeTextPattern('50% off'); // '50\\\\% off'</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"isGroupRow","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function isGroupRow(row: Entity | GroupRow | null | undefined): row is GroupRow"],"doc":"<p><code>true</code> when the row is a group header row, <code>false</code> for a data row - and for <code>null</code> or\n<code>undefined</code>, so it can be given <code>datasource.getRow(n)</code> or <code>datasource.currentEntity</code> as they\nare. It narrows the type, a data row&#39;s own fields can be read after it.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst row = datasource.getRow(0);\nif (isGroupRow(row)) {\n    datasource.expandGroup(row.__groupID);\n} else {\n    console.log(row.name);\n}\n\n// only the data rows - getDataRows() is the same, plus the rows of collapsed groups\nconst dataRows = datasource.getRows().filter((row) =&gt; !isGroupRow(row));</code></pre>\n","samples":["sorting-grouping"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"isV5Filter","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function isV5Filter(value: unknown): value is V5Filter"],"doc":"<p><code>true</code> for a filter of v5: an object with a key only v5 had - <code>filterArguments</code>, <code>attribute</code>,\n<code>logicalOperator</code>, <code>valueType</code>, <code>attributeType</code>, or <code>type: &#39;GROUP&#39;</code> / <code>&#39;CONDITION&#39;</code> - and none only\nv6 has (<code>group</code>, <code>children</code>, <code>field</code>). Only the top is looked at: <a href=\"#api/index.convertV5Filter\">convertV5Filter</a> checks the\nrest.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isV5Filter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nisV5Filter({ attribute: 'age', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 30 }); // true\nisV5Filter({ field: 'age', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 30 }); // false: v6\nisV5Filter(null); // false</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"normalizeFilter","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function normalizeFilter(filter: unknown): FilterExpression | null"],"doc":"<p>A filter of either shape as a filter of v6 - the one place that decides the shape of a filter from a\nrequest, a link or a saved config:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code> and <code>&#39;&#39;</code>: <code>null</code>, no filter</li>\n<li>text: read as JSON first (<code>?filter=</code> of a link), then as below</li>\n<li>a v5 filter (<a href=\"#api/index.isV5Filter\">isV5Filter</a>): <a href=\"#api/index.convertV5Filter\">convertV5Filter</a></li>\n<li>a v6 filter - an object with <code>group</code> / <code>children</code>, or <code>field</code>: as it is, the same object. Check it\nwhere it is used: <code>toSqlWhere()</code> on a backend, <code>datasource.filter()</code> in the browser</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for anything else - text that is no JSON, a number, an array, an object of\nneither shape - and what convertV5Filter throws: a filter that can not be read is not taken as no\nfilter, which would show every row</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { normalizeFilter } from '@simple-html/grid'; // or '@simple-html/grid/sql' on a backend\n\nnormalizeFilter(new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('filter')); // a link of v5 or v6\nnormalizeFilter({ attribute: 'age', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 30 }); // { field: 'age', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 30 }\nnormalizeFilter({ field: 'age', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 30 }); // the same object\nnormalizeFilter(null); // null</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"operatorsFor","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function operatorsFor(type: FieldType): FilterOperator[]"],"doc":"<p>The operators of a field type: the ones that mean something for it, in the order the grid shows\nthem. The grid offers these, and only these, in a header filter&#39;s menu and in the filter editor - a\ncolumn&#39;s <code>filterOperators</code> can offer fewer.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>operators</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>text</code></td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code>, <code>BEGIN_WITH</code>, <code>END_WITH</code>, <code>IN</code>, <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>number</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>datetime</code></td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>LESS_THAN</code>, <code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN</code>, <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>IN</code>, <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>, <code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code>, <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The datasource still filters on an operator a type does not offer - <code>CONTAINS</code> on a number, in a\nfilter saved with v5 - the way it did, see <a href=\"#api/index.FilterOperator\">FilterOperator</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>type</code> a field type. One that is no type gives the operators of <code>text</code>, the type of a field\nthat is not listed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Returns</strong> a new list each call</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { operatorsFor } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\noperatorsFor('boolean'); // ['EQUAL', 'NOT_EQUAL_TO', 'IS_BLANK', 'IS_NOT_BLANK']\noperatorsFor(datasource.getField('age').type).includes('BETWEEN'); // true for a number field</code></pre>\n","samples":["filtering"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"or","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function or(...children: FilterExpression[]): FilterGroup"],"doc":"<p>A group where one matching child is enough.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>children</code> conditions and groups. None gives an empty group, which filters out nothing</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { or, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// country = 'Norway' OR country = 'Sweden'\ndatasource.filter(or(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'), where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Sweden')));</code></pre>\n","samples":["filtering"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"readImportFile","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function readImportFile(file: Blob, options?: ReadImportFileOptions): Promise<ImportTable>"],"doc":"<p>Reads a CSV file or an Excel workbook (.xlsx) into rows of cells, for <code>gridInterface.importRows()</code> or\ncode of your own. The same reader as the grid&#39;s import dialog, with the same limits (<a href=\"#api/index.IMPORT_LIMITS\">IMPORT_LIMITS</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CSV</strong>: UTF-8 (a byte order mark is left out), UTF-16 with its byte order mark, else Windows-1252 -\nwhat Excel saves as &quot;CSV&quot; in a Western language, æøå included. RFC 4180: fields in quotes can\nhold the separator, quotes and line breaks. The separator is found from the first lines, unless it\nis given. Every field is text, as it is written - <code>=1+1</code> is the text <code>=1+1</code>, never a formula</li>\n<li><strong>.xlsx</strong>: the cells of one sheet, typed - text, numbers, booleans, a <code>Date</code> for a cell with a date\nformat. A formula is never run: its value as Excel saved it is read. Needs the browser&#39;s\n<code>DecompressionStream(&#39;deflate-raw&#39;)</code></li>\n<li><strong>.xls</strong> (Excel 97-2003) is not read: save it as .xlsx or CSV</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>file</code> the file: from an <code>&lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt;</code>, a drop, <code>fetch()</code> - any <code>Blob</code></li>\n<li><code>options</code> which sheet, which separator, see <a href=\"#api/index.ReadImportFileOptions\">ReadImportFileOptions</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> (the promise rejects with) an <a href=\"#api/index.ImportFileError\">ImportFileError</a> for a file it does not read: over a limit,\nan .xls file, a damaged or unknown file, a browser that can not inflate a workbook. A <code>RangeError</code> for a\nsheet the workbook does not have</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { readImportFile } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst input = document.querySelector&lt;HTMLInputElement&gt;('#file')!;\ninput.onchange = async () =&gt; {\n    const table = await readImportFile(input.files![0]);\n    // the first row names the columns: matched to the fields by label or name\n    gridInterface.importRows(table.rows, { header: true });\n};</code></pre>\n","samples":["import"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"reconcileColumns","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function reconcileColumns(saved: ExportedGrid, columns: readonly SourceColumn[], options?: ReconcileOptions): ReconciledGrid"],"doc":"<p>The grid of a saved one with the columns its source has now. A grid made from a source - the columns of\na database view, say - and saved (<code>exportConfig()</code>, its views) names the fields the source had then; the\nsource changed since: columns added, columns removed. This makes the saved grid fit it, before the grid\nis made from it (or <code>gridInterface.updateColumns()</code> on a grid that is shown):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>config.columns</code></strong> is the source&#39;s columns: each one&#39;s options as given, over the options the saved\nconfig had for it. A column the source has not is taken out - not a calculated column, it is the grid&#39;s</li>\n<li><strong>A field that is gone</strong> is taken out of everything that names it - the config, its <code>initialState</code>, the\nstate and every view - of <code>config.views</code> and the saved grid&#39;s <code>views</code> (the user&#39;s): the layout (a column left without a field goes), the sort, the\ngrouping, the header filters, the conditions of the filter (an AND / OR group left empty goes, a\ncondition comparing with it - <code>valueField</code> - too), the formats, the aggregates, the detail form&#39;s\nfields and options. A colour rule: its conditions on the field go, a rule left without one goes, and\nso does a formula that names the field</li>\n<li><strong>A calculated column</strong> whose formula names a field that is gone goes too, the same way - and one that\nnames that one, and so on</li>\n<li><strong>A new field</strong> is in <code>config.columns</code>: the column chooser lists it. Hidden, unless <code>showNew</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The saved grid is not changed: a new one is returned, with a report of what changed. It is of the\nversion <a href=\"#api/index.GRID_CONFIG_VERSION\">GRID_CONFIG_VERSION</a>: an older saved grid is converted first (<code>convertGridConfig()</code>).\nGive the datasource the types of the new fields (<code>new Datasource({ fields })</code>): the grid shows a field it\nhas no type of as text.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>saved</code> a saved grid: what <code>exportConfig()</code> made, or a <code>{ version: 2, config }</code></li>\n<li><code>columns</code> the columns of the source now, in their order</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { convertGridConfig, createGrid, Datasource, reconcileColumns, type SourceColumn } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const host: HTMLElement;\n// the columns of the view now - one was added, one removed since the grid was saved\nconst columns: SourceColumn[] = [\n    { field: 'company', label: 'Company' },\n    { field: 'country', label: 'Country' },\n    { field: 'region', label: 'Region' } // new\n];\nconst saved = convertGridConfig(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('my-grid') ?? 'null'));\nconst { grid, report } = reconcileColumns(saved, columns);\nreport; // { added: ['region'], removed: ['city'], removedCalculated: [], removedRules: [] }\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { region: 'text' } });\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config: grid.config, datasource });\nif (grid.state) {\n    gridInterface.setState(grid.state);\n}</code></pre>\n","samples":["reconcile-columns"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"registerCellRenderer","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function registerCellRenderer(id: string, renderer: CellRenderer<any>): void"],"doc":"<p>Registers a cell renderer under an id, for a column to name it: <code>ColumnConfig.renderer</code> is the id, so\nthe config stays plain JSON. The renderer is code: register it when the page loads, before the grid\nthat names it is made. It draws the cells of the column with what you like - plain DOM, lit-html,\nReact, Solid: a badge, a progress bar, a link, a select that edits the value.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Called once per cell element</strong>, not per row: the grid reuses its cell elements while scrolling,\nso a cell shows another row on every scroll step. The renderer renders into <code>host</code> and returns an\nobject whose <code>update(context)</code> the grid calls every time the cell is rendered, with the row, the\nvalue and the text the default cell would show (see <a href=\"#api/index.CellRenderContext\">CellRenderContext</a>). There are as many\nas cell elements show the column - the rows on screen and a few more - not one per row.</li>\n<li><strong><code>destroy()</code></strong> is called when the cell element is done with it, see <a href=\"#api/index.CellRendererInstance\">CellRendererInstance</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The host</strong> fills the cell and is what gets the focus: the keys of the grid work in it - Tab,\nAlt+arrows and the arrows, copy, paste, Delete, Ctrl+Z, the ranges. Put your content in it; its\nclass, <code>tabindex</code> and data attributes are the grid&#39;s. Keys and clicks on your content go to the\ncell too: stop the ones your content handles itself (<code>event.stopPropagation()</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Writing the value</strong>: <code>context.row[context.field] = value</code>, when <code>context.readonly</code> is false - a\nselect or buttons. It is tracked, validated and undone as any edit.</li>\n<li><strong>The grid&#39;s editor</strong>: in a cell that can be edited, Enter, F2, typing and a double click open the\ngrid&#39;s text editor over the cell, and a click on a date cell the date picker - unless the instance\nhas an <code>edit()</code> of its own.</li>\n<li><strong>Registering an id again replaces the renderer</strong>, for the cells mounted after; the cells showing it\nkeep theirs until they are done with it. There is one registry for the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Errors, the edited marker, ranges, Find, the fill handle, the readonly and required stripes and the\n<code>cellClass</code> hook show on a custom cell as on any other. Copy, export, sort, filter and group use the\nvalue and the formatter, not what the renderer draws.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id</code> the name the config uses, e.g. <code>&#39;badge&#39;</code>. Any text but <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>renderer</code> called with the host of each cell element, returns its instance</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> when the id is not a text, or the renderer is not a function</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, registerCellRenderer } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// plain DOM: the element is made once per cell element, update() changes its text and class\nregisterCellRenderer('badge', (host) =&gt; {\n    const span = document.createElement('span');\n    host.appendChild(span);\n    return {\n        update({ value, text }) {\n            span.textContent = text;\n            span.className = `badge badge-${String(value)}`;\n        }\n    };\n});\n\n// the config names it: plain JSON\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: { status: { renderer: 'badge' } },\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['status'], width: 100 }] }\n};\ncreateGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n","samples":["custom-cells"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"registerFormatter","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function registerFormatter(id: string, formatter: Formatter): void"],"doc":"<p>Registers a formatter under an id, for the datasource options to name it: a field&#39;s <code>formatter</code>\nand the default per type in <code>formatters</code> are ids, so the options are plain JSON and can be stored,\ne.g. in a backend. The formatter itself is code: register it when the page loads, before the\ndatasource that names it is made.</p>\n<p>The built-in formatters are registered from the start:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>formatter</th>\n<th>shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;text&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.TextFormatter\">TextFormatter</a></td>\n<td>the text, the default for <code>text</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.BooleanFormatter\">BooleanFormatter</a></td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code>, the default for <code>boolean</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.NumberFormatterDot\">NumberFormatterDot</a></td>\n<td><code>1234.5</code>, the default for <code>number</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;numberComma&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.NumberFormatterComma\">NumberFormatterComma</a></td>\n<td><code>1234,5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;dateYYYYMMDD&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.DateFormatterYYYYMMDD\">DateFormatterYYYYMMDD</a></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code>, the default for <code>date</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;dateDDMMYYYY&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.DateFormatterDDMMYYYY\">DateFormatterDDMMYYYY</a></td>\n<td><code>20.11.2026</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeYYYYMMDD&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS\">DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS</a></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20T13:45:12</code>, the default for <code>datetime</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeDDMMYYYY&#39;</code></td>\n<td><a href=\"#api/index.DateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS\">DateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS</a></td>\n<td><code>20.11.2026T13:45:12</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Registering an id again replaces the formatter</strong> - a built-in one too, and replacing a built-in\ndefault (<code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code> ...) changes every field of that type without a formatter of its own. Pick\nan id of your own unless that is what you want.</li>\n<li><strong>A datasource looks its ids up when it is made</strong> and keeps the formatters it found: replacing a\nformatter later changes the datasources made after, not the ones made before.</li>\n<li>There is one registry for the page, shared by every datasource.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id</code> the name the options use, e.g. <code>&#39;money&#39;</code>. Any text but <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>formatter</code> the formatter, see <a href=\"#api/index.Formatter\">Formatter</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> when the id is not a text, or the formatter has no <code>format</code> or <code>parse</code></p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type Formatter, NumberFormatterDot, registerFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// two decimals and a space between the thousands: 12345.6 -&gt; '12 345.60'; edited as the plain number\nconst money: Formatter = {\n    ...NumberFormatterDot,\n    format: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? value.toFixed(2).replace(/\\B(?=(\\d{3})+(?!\\d))/g, ' ') : ''),\n    formatForEdit: (value) =&gt; NumberFormatterDot.format(value),\n    parse: (text) =&gt; NumberFormatterDot.parse(text.replaceAll(' ', ''))\n};\nregisterFormatter('money', money);\n\n// the options name it: plain JSON, they can come from a backend\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'money' } },\n    formatters: { date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' } // a built-in, the default for every date field\n});\ndatasource.getFormatter('price').format(12345.6); // '12 345.60'</code></pre>\n","samples":["custom-formatter"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"registerLookup","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function registerLookup(id: string, lookup: Lookup): void"],"doc":"<p>Registers a lookup under an id, for a column&#39;s <code>editor</code> to name it: <code>editor: { type: &#39;select&#39;, lookup: &#39;countries&#39; }</code>. The config stays plain JSON - it can be stored, e.g. in a backend - and the\nlookup, which holds a datasource or a grid interface, is code: register it when the page loads,\nbefore the grid that names it is made.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A grid looks its ids up when it is made</strong> (and when <code>updateConfig()</code> is given <code>columns</code>): an id\nthat is not registered throws a <code>TypeError</code> then. It keeps the lookups it found: registering an id\nagain changes the grids made after, not the ones made before.</li>\n<li><strong>The lookup follows its datasource</strong>: rows added, removed, replaced or changed show in the cells\non their own - load the rows after the grid is made, if you like.</li>\n<li>There is one registry for the page, shared by every grid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id</code> the name the columns use, e.g. <code>&#39;countries&#39;</code>. Any text but <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>lookup</code> the rows to pick from, see <a href=\"#api/index.Lookup\">Lookup</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> when the id is not a text, the lookup has neither or both of <code>datasource</code> and\n<code>gridInterface</code>, <code>valueField</code> or <code>labelField</code> is not a text, or <code>config</code> is given with a\n<code>gridInterface</code> (it has its own)</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, registerLookup } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst countries = new Datasource();\ncountries.setData([\n    { code: 'NO', name: 'Norway' },\n    { code: 'SE', name: 'Sweden' },\n    { code: 'DK', name: 'Denmark' }\n]);\nregisterLookup('countries', { datasource: countries, valueField: 'code', labelField: 'name' });\n\nconst customers = new Datasource({ fields: { id: 'number' } });\ncustomers.setData([{ id: 1, name: 'ACME CORP', city: 'Oslo' }]);\nregisterLookup('customers', {\n    datasource: customers,\n    valueField: 'id',\n    labelField: 'name',\n    config: { layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 180 }, { fields: ['city'], width: 100 }] } }\n});\n\n// the config names them: plain JSON\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, {\n    datasource,\n    config: {\n        readonly: false,\n        columns: {\n            country: { editor: { type: 'select', lookup: 'countries' } },\n            // picking a customer stores its id, and copies its name into the row's customerName\n            customerId: { label: 'Customer', editor: { type: 'grid', lookup: 'customers', set: { customerName: 'name' } } }\n        },\n        layout: { center: [{ fields: ['country'], width: 120 }, { fields: ['customerId'], width: 180 }] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["lookups"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"registerValidator","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function registerValidator(id: string, validator: Validator): void"],"doc":"<p>Registers a validator under an id, for the fields&#39; rules to name it: a field&#39;s <code>validate</code> lists\nrules by id (<code>validate: [&#39;required&#39;, { id: &#39;min&#39;, value: 0 }]</code>), so the fields are plain JSON and\ncan be stored, e.g. in a backend. The validator itself is code: register it when the page loads,\nbefore the datasource that names it is made.</p>\n<p>The built-in validators are registered from the start. They take their option as <code>value</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>the value fails when</th>\n<th>message id</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;required&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, only spaces, an invalid Date or 0</td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.required&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;unique&#39;</code></td>\n<td>another row has the same value - with <code>with: [&#39;year&#39;]</code> the same value and <code>year</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.unique&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.uniqueWith&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;min&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is a number below <code>value</code>, or a date before the day <code>value</code> (<code>&#39;2026-01-01&#39;</code>)</td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.min&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;max&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is a number above <code>value</code>, or a date after the day <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.max&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;integer&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is a number with decimals</td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.integer&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;decimals&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is a number with more decimals than <code>value</code> (0 to 20)</td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.decimals&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;oneOf&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is not in the list <code>value</code> (<code>[&#39;Norway&#39;, &#39;Sweden&#39;]</code>), compared as text</td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.oneOf&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;email&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is text that is no email address (something <code>@</code> something with a dot, no spaces)</td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.email&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;minLength&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is text shorter than <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.minLength&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;maxLength&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is text longer than <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.maxLength&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;pattern&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is text the regular expression <code>value</code> (as text) does not find</td>\n<td><code>&#39;validation.pattern&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Only spaces fail <code>required</code></strong>: <code>&#39;  &#39;</code> is as empty as <code>&#39;&#39;</code> - tabs and line breaks too.</li>\n<li><strong><code>unique</code> compares the rows</strong> of the datasource&#39;s container, the rows marked for deletion left\nout: every row with the value has the error, and it goes from all of them once one of them is\nchanged (or deleted). <code>with</code> lists other fields whose values go with it - <code>{ id: &#39;unique&#39;, with: [&#39;year&#39;] }</code> on <code>code</code>: a code once per year, the error on <code>code</code>; give the rule to <code>year</code> too for\nthe error on both. A blank value is no duplicate (as NULL in SQL): add <code>&#39;required&#39;</code>. Text is\ncompared as it is, <code>ignoreCase: true</code> takes <code>Ann</code> and <code>ANN</code> as the same; a date by its time, other\nvalues as text. The import compares its rows with the grid&#39;s and with each other.</li>\n<li><strong>0 fails <code>required</code></strong>, like the filter&#39;s <code>IS_BLANK</code> takes 0 as blank: an emptied number cell\nstores 0 (the number formatters parse <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as 0), so a required number must not take it. <code>false</code>\npasses, it is a value. For a number where 0 is a real value, register a rule of your own (below).</li>\n<li><strong>The other built-ins pass a blank value</strong> (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>): an optional field can be\nempty, add <code>&#39;required&#39;</code> to make it fail. They check only values of their kind - <code>min</code> / <code>max</code>\nnumbers and dates (a datetime on the day of <code>max</code> is within it), <code>integer</code> / <code>decimals</code> numbers,\n<code>oneOf</code> any value, the others text - and pass the rest.</li>\n<li><strong><code>pattern</code> is not anchored</strong>: <code>&#39;[0-9]&#39;</code> finds a digit anywhere, write <code>&#39;^[0-9]+$&#39;</code> for the whole\ntext. It is a <code>RegExp</code> without flags.</li>\n<li><strong>The messages are texts</strong>: the built-ins return the ids <code>validation.*</code> of <a href=\"#api/index.TEXTS\">TEXTS</a>, changed\nwith <a href=\"#api/index.setTexts\">setTexts</a>. A rule&#39;s <code>message</code> replaces what the validator returns. Either is the\nmessage itself or a text id: an id of your own gets its text from <code>setTexts()</code>, so it is translated\nlike the built-ins&#39; (<code>&#39;validation.phoneNO&#39;</code> below).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Registering an id again replaces the validator - a built-in one too, for the fields that name\nit (its options are not checked then, and the grid&#39;s stripes for <code>required</code> still mark what the\nbuilt-in takes as missing). <strong>A datasource looks its ids up when it is made</strong> and keeps the validators it found, like the\nformatters. There is one registry for the page, shared by every datasource.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id</code> the name the rules use, e.g. <code>&#39;phone&#39;</code>. Any text but <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>validator</code> the function that checks a value, see <a href=\"#api/index.Validator\">Validator</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> when the id is not a text, or the validator is not a function</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, registerValidator, setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a Norwegian phone number: 8 digits, spaces allowed. Returns a text id, its text is set below\nregisterValidator('phoneNO', (value) =&gt;\n    typeof value === 'string' &amp;&amp; value !== '' &amp;&amp; !/^(\\d\\s*){8}$/.test(value) ? 'validation.phoneNO' : null\n);\nsetTexts({ 'validation.phoneNO': 'Must be 8 digits' });\n// a number that must be there, 0 included (the built-in required takes 0 as missing)\nregisterValidator('present', (value) =&gt; (value === null || value === undefined ? 'validation.required' : null));\n\n// the fields name them: plain JSON, they can come from a backend\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        name: { validate: ['required', { id: 'maxLength', value: 50 }] },\n        age: { type: 'number', validate: ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }, { id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'too old' }] },\n        phone: { validate: ['phoneNO'] },\n        discount: { type: 'number', validate: ['present'] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":["validation"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"setTexts","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function setTexts(texts: Texts): void"],"doc":"<p>Sets the texts of the page: every grid and datasource uses them. An app with one language calls\nit once, before it makes its grids. A text for one grid only is <code>GridConfig.texts</code>, which wins over\nthese.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It replaces the texts set before</strong>: an id left out shows its default again, <code>setTexts({})</code> goes\nback to the defaults (English). The object is copied.</li>\n<li><strong>Nothing is rendered again</strong>: a grid shows the new texts after its next rebuild\n(<code>gridInterface.rebuild()</code>), a menu, the filter editor and the date picker the next time they\nopen. <code>getFilterString()</code> uses them from its next call. A validation message is made when a value\nis validated: an error already shown keeps its text until the field is validated again. The name\nof the group of blank values changes the next time the rows are grouped.</li>\n<li>The texts of the datasource - <code>validation.*</code>, <code>filterString.*</code>, <code>grouping.blank</code> - are set here\nonly: the datasource has no grid, <code>GridConfig.texts</code> does not change them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>texts</code> a text for each id to change, see <a href=\"#api/index.TEXTS\">TEXTS</a> for the ids. Plain JSON, it can come\nfrom a backend</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> when <code>texts</code> is not an object, or a text is not a string</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const config: GridConfig;\n\n// once, before the grids are made\nsetTexts({\n    'row.copy': 'Kopier',\n    'footer.summary.count': 'Antall: {count}',\n    'validation.required': 'Må fylles ut'\n});\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });\n\n// later: back to English\nsetTexts({});\ngridInterface.rebuild();</code></pre>\n","samples":["translation"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"where","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function where(field: string, operator: FilterOperator, value?: unknown, options?: { caseSensitive?: boolean; literal?: boolean }): FilterCondition"],"doc":"<p>A condition: <code>field</code> <code>operator</code> <code>value</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>field</code> the field to test, the property on the row</li>\n<li><code>operator</code> how to compare, see <a href=\"#api/index.FilterOperator\">FilterOperator</a></li>\n<li><code>value</code> what to compare with. Leave it out for <code>IS_BLANK</code> / <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code>, the condition\nthen has no <code>value</code> property. <code>[from, to]</code> for <code>BETWEEN</code>, an end can be null</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for a <code>BETWEEN</code> whose value is not <code>[from, to]</code>, and for options that are not\nan object (<code>where(field, operator, value, true)</code>)</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nwhere('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 18); // { field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18 }\nwhere('born', 'LESS_THAN', new Date(2000, 0, 1));\nwhere('age', 'BETWEEN', [18, 30]); // 18 to 30, both included\nwhere('age', 'BETWEEN', [18, null]); // 18 or more\nwhere('country', 'NOT_IN', ['Norway', 'Sweden']);\nwhere('name', 'BEGIN_WITH', 'Mc', { caseSensitive: true }); // { ..., caseSensitive: true }\nwhere('email', 'IS_BLANK'); // { field: 'email', operator: 'IS_BLANK' }\nwhere('discount', 'CONTAINS', '50%', { literal: true }); // finds '50%', not '500'</code></pre>\n","samples":["filtering"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"withoutFields","group":"Functions","kind":"Function","signatures":["function withoutFields(filter: FilterExpression | null | undefined, fields: Iterable<string, any, any> | ((field: string) => boolean)): FilterExpression | null"],"doc":"<p>A filter without the conditions on some fields - the calculated columns, the fields a backend does not\nhave - for a backend that filters the rest. The browser filters on all of it; the backend&#39;s part must\nlet every row through that the whole filter lets through, so a condition taken out matches every row:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>in an AND group it is left out: <code>a AND calc</code> is <code>a</code></li>\n<li>an OR group with one is left out whole: <code>a OR calc</code> can be true for any row</li>\n<li>a group left with nothing, and the top, is <code>null</code>: no filter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The backend then sends some rows the browser&#39;s filter drops, never fewer. A condition is taken out when\nits <code>field</code> or its <code>valueField</code> is one of the fields. This file has no DOM: the sql entry\n(<code>@simple-html/grid/sql</code>) bundles it.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>filter</code> the filter: <code>datasource.getFilter()</code>, null for none</li>\n<li><code>fields</code> the fields whose conditions go - a list, a Set, or a function true for each of them</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Returns</strong> the filter without them, a copy where something was taken out - null when nothing is left</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { withoutFields } from '@simple-html/grid'; // or '@simple-html/grid/sql' on a backend\n\nconst calculated = Object.keys(gridInterface.getState().calculatedColumns); // ['total']\nwithoutFields(datasource.getFilter(), calculated);\n// AND(country = Norway, total &gt; 100)  -&gt; country = Norway\n// OR(country = Norway, total &gt; 100)   -&gt; null: any row can have a total over 100</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"convertV5Filter","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Function","signatures":["function convertV5Filter(filter: unknown): SqlFilter | null"],"doc":"<p>A filter of v5 as one of v6, for the old links and bookmarks a backend still gets - <code>convertV5Filter</code>\nof <code>@simple-html/grid</code>, the same function. <code>null</code> for none. Hand the result to <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for what is no v5 filter, a <code>RangeError</code> for groups nested deeper than 100 -\nanswer both with a 400 (<a href=\"#api/sql.isSqlFilterError\">isSqlFilterError</a>)</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { convertV5Filter, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst filter = convertV5Filter({ attribute: 'name', operator: 'BEGIN_WITH', value: 'an' });\n// { field: 'name', operator: 'BEGIN_WITH', value: 'an' }\ntoSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'postgres', columns: { name: 'name' } });</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"isSqlFilterError","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Function","signatures":["function isSqlFilterError(error: unknown): error is TypeError | RangeError"],"doc":"<p><code>true</code> for an error of a filter that came from outside: what <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a>, <a href=\"#api/sql.convertV5Filter\">convertV5Filter</a>\nand <a href=\"#api/sql.normalizeFilter\">normalizeFilter</a> throw for the filter - its shape, its values, a limit - and for a\n<code>timeZone</code> that is none (the user&#39;s, sent with the filter): the request&#39;s mistake, answer it with a 400.\n<code>false</code> for anything else, among them a <code>TypeError</code> of the other <code>options</code> that are wrong: a mistake of\nthe backend&#39;s code. The error stays the <code>TypeError</code> or <code>RangeError</code> it was.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isSqlFilterError, normalizeFilter, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nfunction where(body: { filter: unknown; timeZone: string }) {\n    try {\n        return toSqlWhere(normalizeFilter(body.filter), { dialect: 'postgres', columns: { name: 'name' }, timeZone: body.timeZone });\n    } catch (error) {\n        if (isSqlFilterError(error)) {\n            return { status: 400, message: error.message };\n        }\n        throw error; // a 500\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"isV5Filter","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Function","signatures":["function isV5Filter(value: unknown): value is SqlV5Filter"],"doc":"<p><code>true</code> for a filter of v5 - <code>convertV5Filter</code> of <code>@simple-html/grid</code>, the same function: an object with\na key only v5 had (<code>filterArguments</code>, <code>attribute</code>, <code>logicalOperator</code> ...) and none only v6 has.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isV5Filter } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nisV5Filter({ attribute: 'age', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 30 }); // true</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"normalizeFilter","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Function","signatures":["function normalizeFilter(filter: unknown): SqlFilter | null"],"doc":"<p>A filter of either shape - v5, v6, or either as JSON text (<code>?filter=</code>) - as one of v6, <code>null</code> for none:\n<code>normalizeFilter</code> of <code>@simple-html/grid</code>, the same function. A v6 filter is returned as it is:\n<a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a> checks it.</p>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for what is neither - text that is no JSON, a number, an object of neither shape:\nit is not taken as no filter, which would select every row. Answer it with a 400 (<a href=\"#api/sql.isSqlFilterError\">isSqlFilterError</a>)</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { normalizeFilter, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const body: string; // the request's\nconst filter = normalizeFilter(JSON.parse(body).filter); // a v5 or a v6 filter, or none\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'postgres', columns: { name: 'name' } });</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"sqlColumnsFromFields","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Function","signatures":["function sqlColumnsFromFields(fields: Readonly<Record<string, SqlColumnType | { caseSensitive?: boolean; clobType?: boolean; type?: SqlColumnType }>>, options?: SqlFieldsOptions): Record<string, SqlColumn>"],"doc":"<p>The <code>columns</code> of <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a> from the fields of the grid&#39;s datasource - the same object as\n<code>DatasourceOptions.fields</code>: <code>{ born: &#39;date&#39;, age: { type: &#39;number&#39;, label: &#39;Age&#39; } }</code>. Each field is a\ncolumn of its type (a field without one is <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>), named as the field - <code>nameCase</code> and <code>column</code>\nchange that. A field&#39;s <code>caseSensitive</code> and <code>clobType</code> are the column&#39;s (SqlColumn). One list of the\nfields for the browser and the backend keeps their types the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>fields</code> the datasource&#39;s fields: a type, or a config with <code>type</code>, <code>caseSensitive</code> and <code>clobType</code>\n(the rest is not read)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for fields that are no object, a type that is none, wrong options</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type SqlFilter, sqlColumnsFromFields, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const filter: SqlFilter;\nconst fields = { project: 'text', born: 'date', amount: { type: 'number', label: 'Amount' } } as const;\nconst columns = sqlColumnsFromFields(fields, { nameCase: 'upper' });\n// { project: { column: 'PROJECT', type: 'text' }, born: { column: 'BORN', type: 'date' },\n//   amount: { column: 'AMOUNT', type: 'number' } }\ntoSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'oracle', columns });</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"toSqlWhere","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Function","signatures":["function toSqlWhere(filter: unknown, options: SqlOptions): SqlWhere"],"doc":"<p>Turns a filter of the grid into the condition of a SQL <code>WHERE</code>, with its values as parameters: the\nrows the grid&#39;s own filter shows, selected by the database. For a backend that gets the filter from\nthe browser - <code>datasource.getFilter()</code>, <code>GridState.filter</code> - and loads only the rows it lets through.</p>\n<p><strong>The filter is taken as hostile input.</strong> Every value is a parameter, never part of the SQL: the SQL\nis fixed keywords, placeholders and the quoted columns of <code>columns</code>. A field (or <code>valueField</code>) that\nis not in <code>columns</code> throws, so does anything that is no filter: a wrong shape, an unknown operator or\nproperty, a value of the wrong type. A filter over a limit (<code>limits</code>) throws a <code>RangeError</code>. A message\nshows at most 40 characters of what it is about. Answer both with a 400: <a href=\"#api/sql.isSqlFilterError\">isSqlFilterError</a> is\ntrue for them and for a <code>timeZone</code> that is none (the user&#39;s, sent with the filter) - not for the other\noptions that are wrong, a mistake of the backend&#39;s code.</p>\n<p>It does not decide who may see which rows: AND your own conditions to it -\n<code>WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND ${sql}</code>, with <code>firstParam: 2</code>.</p>\n<p>The rules are the grid&#39;s, see the filtering guide &quot;Operators&quot;: a text ignores case unless the\ncondition is <code>caseSensitive</code> (<code>*</code> and <code>%</code> are wildcards, <code>_</code> one character, unless it is <code>literal</code>), a\nblank number is 0, a blank boolean false, a blank date is no date, a date is its day in the user&#39;s\n<code>timeZone</code>. A condition\nthe grid ignores (no field, no operator, no value) is left out. Where SQL does not do exactly what the\ngrid does, the SQL guide says so.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>filter</code> the filter as it came, e.g. <code>JSON.parse(body).filter</code>: a <code>FilterExpression</code>, or null /\nundefined for no filter</li>\n<li><code>options</code> the dialect, the columns, the user&#39;s time zone, see <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlOptions\">SqlOptions</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Returns</strong> the SQL and the values of its placeholders, see <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlWhere\">SqlWhere</a>. <code>1=1</code> when no condition is\nleft</p>\n<p><strong>Throws</strong> a <code>TypeError</code> for a filter that is no filter, a field that is not in <code>columns</code>, and for options\nthat are wrong; a <code>RangeError</code> for a filter over a limit</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst filter = {\n    group: 'AND',\n    children: [\n        { field: 'name', operator: 'BEGIN_WITH', value: 'an' },\n        { field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18 }\n    ]\n};\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere(filter, {\n    dialect: 'postgres',\n    columns: { name: 'customer_name', age: { column: 'age', type: 'number' } }\n});\n// sql: ((&quot;customer_name&quot; IS NOT NULL AND LOWER(&quot;customer_name&quot;) LIKE LOWER($1) ESCAPE '!')\n//       AND (&quot;age&quot; IS NOT NULL AND &quot;age&quot; &gt; $2))\n// params: ['an%', 18]\n// await client.query(`SELECT * FROM customer WHERE ${sql}`, params);</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"withoutFields","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Function","signatures":["function withoutFields(filter: SqlFilter | null | undefined, fields: Iterable<string, any, any> | ((field: string) => boolean)): SqlFilter | null"],"doc":"<p>A filter without the conditions on some fields - <code>withoutFields</code> of <code>@simple-html/grid</code>, the same\nfunction: a condition taken out matches every row (left out of an AND group, its OR group left out\nwhole), so the SQL selects every row the whole filter lets through, maybe more. For the calculated\ncolumns, and the fields the database does not have. <code>null</code> when nothing is left.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>fields</code> the fields whose conditions go: a list, a Set, or a function true for each of them</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type SqlFilter, toSqlWhere, withoutFields } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const body: { filter: SqlFilter };\nconst columns = { name: 'name', age: 'age' };\n// the fields the database has not: out, the browser filters on them\nconst filter = withoutFields(body.filter, (field) =&gt; !Object.hasOwn(columns, field));\ntoSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'postgres', columns });</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"SqlCalculatedColumn","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlCalculatedColumn"],"doc":"<p>A calculated column for <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlOptions\">SqlOptions.calculated</a>: <code>CalculatedColumn</code> of the grid - the formula\n(<code>&#39;{price} * {qty}&#39;</code>), and the type and label it may have (the label is not used). A formula is text\nfrom the user&#39;s state, taken as hostile as a filter: what is wrong with it is a <code>TypeError</code> that\n<code>isSqlFilterError()</code> knows - a 400.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type SqlCalculatedColumn, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const body: { filter: unknown; calculated: Record&lt;string, SqlCalculatedColumn&gt; };\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere(body.filter, {\n    dialect: 'postgres',\n    columns: { price: { column: 'price', type: 'number' }, qty: { column: 'qty', type: 'number' } },\n    calculated: body.calculated // { total: { formula: '{price} * {qty}' } }\n});</code></pre>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"formula","signatures":["formula: string"],"doc":"<p>the formula, in the language of the grid&#39;s calculated columns: <code>{price} * {qty}</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"label","signatures":["label?: string"],"doc":"<p>the column&#39;s label in the grid: not used</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"type","signatures":["type?: \"number\" | \"boolean\" | \"text\" | \"date\""],"doc":"<p>the type of its values: left out the formula&#39;s, given it must be the formula&#39;s</p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlColumn","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlColumn"],"doc":"<p>One field a filter may use, in <code>SqlOptions.columns</code>: its column and its type. Writing just the column\n(<code>name: &#39;customer_name&#39;</code>) is a text column.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { SqlColumn } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst active: SqlColumn = { column: 'is_active', type: 'boolean', booleanValues: { true: 'Y', false: 'N' } };\nconst changed: SqlColumn = { column: 'audit.changes.changed_at', type: 'datetime', dateStorage: 'utc' };\nconst code: SqlColumn = { column: 'PROJECT_CODE', caseSensitive: true }; // its index is used\nconst notes: SqlColumn = { column: 'NOTES', clobType: true }; // an Oracle CLOB: LIKE only</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"booleanValues","signatures":["booleanValues?: { false: SqlParam; true: SqlParam }"],"doc":"<p><code>boolean</code> only: the values the column holds for true and false - <code>{ true: &#39;Y&#39;, false: &#39;N&#39; }</code> for a\n<code>CHAR(1)</code>. Default: <code>true</code> / <code>false</code> on postgres and mssql (<code>boolean</code>, <code>bit</code>), <code>1</code> / <code>0</code> on oracle and\nsqlite. A column that is null is false, as a blank boolean in the grid</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"caseSensitive","signatures":["caseSensitive?: boolean"],"doc":"<p><code>text</code> only: <code>true</code> - every condition on the column compares with its case, also one the filter\nsends without <code>caseSensitive</code> (or with <code>false</code>): <code>&quot;PROJECT_CODE&quot; = :1</code>, so an index on the column is\nused - <code>LOWER(&quot;PROJECT_CODE&quot;)</code> would not use it. The grid&#39;s <code>FieldConfig.caseSensitive</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"clobType","signatures":["clobType?: boolean"],"doc":"<p><code>text</code> only: <code>true</code> - the column is a LOB, an Oracle <code>CLOB</code> / <code>NCLOB</code>, which SQL compares with <code>LIKE</code>\nonly. LOB columns are not supported as such; this filters one as plain text: <code>EQUAL</code> and\n<code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code> are a <code>LIKE</code> of the whole text (its wildcards escaped), <code>CONTAINS</code>, <code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code>,\n<code>BEGIN_WITH</code>, <code>END_WITH</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code> and <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code> as on any text. Any other operator (<code>IN</code>, <code>&lt;</code>,\n<code>BETWEEN</code> ...), a <code>valueField</code> on either side and a formula that reads it (<code>calculated</code>, <code>shown</code>)\nthrow a <code>TypeError</code> that <code>isSqlFilterError()</code> knows. The grid&#39;s <code>FieldConfig.clobType</code>, whose filters\noffer only those</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"column","signatures":["column: string"],"doc":"<p>The column&#39;s name: letters (a-z, A-Z), digits and <code>_</code>, not starting with a digit, at most 128 of\nthem - with up to two prefixes, <code>schema.table.column</code>. It is quoted (<code>&quot;name&quot;</code>, <code>[name]</code> on SQL\nServer), so write it as the database has it: PostgreSQL folds a name created without quotes to\nlower case, Oracle to UPPER case (<code>&#39;CUSTOMER_NAME&#39;</code>).</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"dateStorage","signatures":["dateStorage?: SqlDateStorage"],"doc":"<p><code>date</code> / <code>datetime</code> only: how the column holds a date. Default <code>SqlOptions.dateStorage</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"type","signatures":["type?: SqlColumnType"],"doc":"<p>how a condition on it compares, default <code>&#39;text&#39;</code></p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlFieldsOptions","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlFieldsOptions"],"doc":"<p>The options of <a href=\"#api/sql.sqlColumnsFromFields\">sqlColumnsFromFields</a></p>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"column","signatures":["column?: (field: string) => string"],"doc":"<p>the name of a field&#39;s column, before <code>nameCase</code>: <code>(field) =&gt; &#39;customer.&#39; + field</code> - default the field</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"dateStorage","signatures":["dateStorage?: SqlDateStorage"],"doc":"<p>how the <code>date</code> and <code>datetime</code> columns hold their dates, see <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlDateStorage\">SqlDateStorage</a>. Left out: <code>&#39;local&#39;</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"nameCase","signatures":["nameCase?: \"asIs\" | \"upper\" | \"lower\""],"doc":"<p>the case of the column names: <code>&#39;asIs&#39;</code> (the default) the field&#39;s name, <code>&#39;upper&#39;</code> / <code>&#39;lower&#39;</code>. The\nnames are quoted: Oracle&#39;s unquoted names are upper case - <code>&#39;upper&#39;</code> - PostgreSQL&#39;s lower case</p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlFilterCondition","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlFilterCondition"],"doc":"<p>a condition of a <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlFilter\">SqlFilter</a>: <code>FilterCondition</code> of the grid</p>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"caseSensitive","signatures":["caseSensitive?: boolean"],"doc":"<p>a text compared with the case of its letters</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"field","signatures":["field: string"],"doc":"<p>the field to test</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"literal","signatures":["literal?: boolean"],"doc":"<p><code>*</code>, <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> are characters like any other</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"operator","signatures":["operator: SqlFilterOperator"],"doc":"<p>how to compare</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"value","signatures":["value?: unknown"],"doc":"<p>what to compare with: an array for <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>[from, to]</code> for <code>BETWEEN</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"valueField","signatures":["valueField?: string"],"doc":"<p>another field, whose value is compared with</p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlFilterGroup","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlFilterGroup"],"doc":"<p>a group of a <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlFilter\">SqlFilter</a>: <code>FilterGroup</code> of the grid</p>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"children","signatures":["children: SqlFilter[]"],"doc":"<p>the conditions and groups in the group</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"group","signatures":["group: \"AND\" | \"OR\""],"doc":"<p><code>&#39;AND&#39;</code>: every child must match, <code>&#39;OR&#39;</code>: one is enough</p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlLimits","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlLimits"],"doc":"<p>The limits <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a> puts on a filter, so a filter from a browser can not make a query that\nis too large. A filter over a limit throws a <code>RangeError</code>. Each is a whole number of at least 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const filter: unknown;\ntoSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'sqlite', columns: { name: 'name' }, limits: { listLength: 200, conditions: 20 } });</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"conditions","signatures":["conditions?: number"],"doc":"<p>the conditions and groups of the filter, also the ones left out. Default 100</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"depth","signatures":["depth?: number"],"doc":"<p>how deep groups nest, the top group is 1. Default 10</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"listLength","signatures":["listLength?: number"],"doc":"<p>the values of one <code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code>. Default 1000 (an Oracle <code>IN</code> list takes 1000 before 23ai)</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"params","signatures":["params?: number"],"doc":"<p>the parameters of the WHERE clause. Default 2000 (SQL Server takes 2100 in one request)</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"textLength","signatures":["textLength?: number"],"doc":"<p>the characters (UTF-16 code units) of one text value. Default 1000</p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlOptions","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlOptions"],"doc":"<p>The options of <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type SqlOptions, toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst options: SqlOptions = {\n    dialect: 'postgres',\n    columns: {\n        name: 'customer_name',\n        age: { column: 'age', type: 'number' },\n        born: { column: 'born', type: 'date' },\n        active: { column: 'is_active', type: 'boolean' }\n    },\n    timeZone: 'Europe/Oslo'\n};\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere({ field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18 }, options);\n// (&quot;age&quot; IS NOT NULL AND &quot;age&quot; &gt; $1), [18]</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"calculated","signatures":["calculated?: Record<string, string | SqlCalculatedColumn>"],"doc":"<p>The calculated columns of the grid (<code>gridInterface.getState().calculatedColumns</code>), by field: a filter\non one compares its formula, written as SQL, as it compares a column. Its formula reads the fields of\n<code>columns</code> and the other calculated columns - no field <code>columns</code> has not. See <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlCalculatedColumn\">SqlCalculatedColumn</a>.\nA formula with dates needs <code>timeZone</code>: a date is its day in the user&#39;s time zone, as in the grid.</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"columns","signatures":["columns: Record<string, string | SqlColumn>"],"doc":"<p>The whitelist: the only fields a filter may use, each with its column. A filter with any other\nfield (or <code>valueField</code>) throws. Own properties only: <code>__proto__</code>, <code>constructor</code> or <code>toString</code> are\nfields like any other, unknown unless you list them. Several fields can name one column</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"dateStorage","signatures":["dateStorage?: SqlDateStorage"],"doc":"<p>how the <code>date</code> / <code>datetime</code> columns hold their dates, unless a column says. Default <code>&#39;local&#39;</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"dialect","signatures":["dialect: SqlDialect"],"doc":"<p>the database, see <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlDialect\">SqlDialect</a></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"firstParam","signatures":["firstParam?: number"],"doc":"<p>The number of the first placeholder, default 1: <code>3</code> gives <code>$3</code>, <code>$4</code> ... - for a query that has two\nparameters of its own before. Not used by sqlite, whose <code>?</code> are taken in order</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"limits","signatures":["limits?: SqlLimits"],"doc":"<p>the limits on the size of the filter, see <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlLimits\">SqlLimits</a></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"shown","signatures":["shown?: Record<string, string>"],"doc":"<p>The columns the grid filters on the text their template shows (a text column with <code>format.text</code>:\n<code>{first} {last}</code>), each as a formula of that text - <code>gridInterface.getShownFormulas()</code> gives them. A\ncondition on such a field compares the formula&#39;s SQL, as the grid compares the text: <code>= &#39;Ann Berg&#39;</code>\nis <code>CONCAT(&quot;first&quot;, &#39; &#39;, &quot;last&quot;)</code> in SQL. The formula reads the columns (the field&#39;s own too) and the\ncalculated columns. What is wrong with one is a <code>TypeError</code> that <code>isSqlFilterError()</code> knows</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"timeZone","signatures":["timeZone?: string"],"doc":"<p>The user&#39;s time zone, an IANA name (<code>&#39;Europe/Oslo&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;UTC&#39;</code>): a date of the filter is on the day\nand at the time it is in this zone, as the grid in the user&#39;s browser reads it. Required when a\ncolumn is a <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code>. The browser has it: <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone</code></p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlV5Filter","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlV5Filter"],"doc":"<p>A filter as v5 had it - <code>V5Filter</code> of <code>@simple-html/grid</code>: a group has <code>type: &#39;GROUP&#39;</code> and\n<code>filterArguments</code>, a condition <code>attribute</code>, <code>operator</code>, <code>value</code></p>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"attribute","signatures":["attribute?: string | null"],"doc":"<p>a condition&#39;s field</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"attributeType","signatures":["attributeType?: string | null"],"doc":"<p>the field&#39;s type: dropped, the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code> have it in v6</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"filterArguments","signatures":["filterArguments?: SqlV5Filter[]"],"doc":"<p>a group&#39;s conditions and groups</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"logicalOperator","signatures":["logicalOperator?: \"AND\" | \"OR\" | \"NONE\""],"doc":"<p>a group&#39;s: <code>&#39;OR&#39;</code> is an OR group, anything else AND</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"operator","signatures":["operator?: string | null"],"doc":"<p>a condition&#39;s operator: the names of v6</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"type","signatures":["type?: \"GROUP\" | \"CONDITION\""],"doc":"<p><code>&#39;GROUP&#39;</code> for a group, <code>&#39;CONDITION&#39;</code> for a condition</p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"value","signatures":["value?: unknown"],"doc":"<p>a condition&#39;s value - the name of another field with <code>valueType: &#39;ATTRIBUTE&#39;</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"valueType","signatures":["valueType?: \"VALUE\" | \"ATTRIBUTE\" | null"],"doc":"<p><code>&#39;ATTRIBUTE&#39;</code>: <code>value</code> is another field, a <code>valueField</code> in v6</p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlWhere","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Interface","signatures":["interface SqlWhere"],"doc":"<p>What <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a> returns: the condition of a <code>WHERE</code> and its parameters.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>sql</code> - one condition, in brackets when it has more than one part, so it can be ANDed with your\nown: <code>WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND ${sql}</code>. <code>1=1</code> when the filter leaves no condition (no filter, empty\ngroups, only conditions the grid ignores)</li>\n<li><code>params</code> - the values for the placeholders of <code>sql</code>, in their order: <code>params[0]</code> is <code>$1</code> (or the\nfirst <code>?</code>, <code>:1</code>, <code>@p1</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ndeclare const filter: unknown;\nconst { sql, params } = toSqlWhere(filter, { dialect: 'postgres', columns: { name: 'name' }, firstParam: 2 });\nconst query = `SELECT * FROM customer WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND ${sql}`;\nconst values = [42, ...params];</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[{"title":"Properties","members":[{"name":"params","signatures":["params: SqlParam[]"],"doc":"<p>the values of the placeholders, in the order they are in <code>sql</code></p>\n","samples":[]},{"name":"sql","signatures":["sql: string"],"doc":"<p>the condition, without the <code>WHERE</code></p>\n","samples":[]}]}]},{"name":"SqlColumnType","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type SqlColumnType = \"text\" | \"number\" | \"boolean\" | \"date\" | \"datetime\""],"doc":"<p>The type of a column for <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a>: how a condition on it compares, as the grid compares a\nfield of that type - the same names as the grid&#39;s <code>FieldType</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>&#39;text&#39;</code> - texts, ignoring case unless the condition is <code>caseSensitive</code>, <code>*</code> and <code>%</code> are wildcards,\n<code>_</code> one character - characters like any other when the condition is <code>literal</code></li>\n<li><code>&#39;number&#39;</code> - numbers, a blank (null) is 0</li>\n<li><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code> - true and false, a blank is false</li>\n<li><code>&#39;date&#39;</code> - the day, in the user&#39;s time zone (<code>SqlOptions.timeZone</code>)</li>\n<li><code>&#39;datetime&#39;</code> - the second, in the user&#39;s time zone</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { SqlOptions } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst columns: SqlOptions['columns'] = {\n    name: 'name', // text\n    age: { column: 'age', type: 'number' },\n    born: { column: 'born', type: 'date' }\n};</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"SqlDateStorage","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type SqlDateStorage = \"local\" | \"utc\" | \"zoned\" | \"epochSeconds\" | \"epochMillis\""],"doc":"<p>How a <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code> column holds its dates, see the SQL guide &quot;Dates and time zones&quot;. It\ndecides what the date parameters are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>&#39;local&#39;</code> (the default) - a type without a time zone (PostgreSQL <code>date</code> / <code>timestamp</code>, Oracle <code>DATE</code>\n/ <code>TIMESTAMP</code>, SQL Server <code>date</code> / <code>datetime</code> / <code>datetime2</code>, SQLite text such as\n<code>&#39;2026-11-22 08:30:00&#39;</code>), holding the wall clock of the user&#39;s <code>timeZone</code>. The parameters are that\nwall clock: <code>&#39;2026-11-22 00:00:00&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>&#39;utc&#39;</code> - the same types, holding the time in UTC. The parameters are the UTC wall clock: the start\nof 22 November in Oslo is <code>&#39;2026-11-21 23:00:00&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>&#39;zoned&#39;</code> - a type with a time zone: PostgreSQL <code>timestamptz</code>, Oracle <code>TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE</code> /\n<code>WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE</code>, SQL Server <code>datetimeoffset</code>, SQLite ISO text with <code>Z</code> or an offset\n(<code>toISOString()</code>). The parameters are UTC with the offset written out: <code>&#39;2026-11-21 23:00:00+00:00&#39;</code></li>\n<li><code>&#39;epochSeconds&#39;</code> / <code>&#39;epochMillis&#39;</code> - a number: the seconds / milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC (a JS\n<code>getTime()</code> is milliseconds). The parameters are numbers</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\nconst created = { field: 'created', operator: 'EQUAL', value: '2026-11-21T23:00:00.000Z' };\ntoSqlWhere(created, {\n    dialect: 'postgres',\n    timeZone: 'Europe/Oslo',\n    columns: { created: { column: 'created_at', type: 'date', dateStorage: 'zoned' } }\n});\n// sql: (&quot;created_at&quot; IS NOT NULL AND &quot;created_at&quot; &gt;= CAST($1 AS timestamptz)\n//       AND &quot;created_at&quot; &lt; CAST($2 AS timestamptz))\n// params: ['2026-11-21 23:00:00+00:00', '2026-11-22 23:00:00+00:00'] - 22 November in Oslo</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"SqlDialect","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type SqlDialect = \"postgres\" | \"oracle\" | \"sqlite\" | \"mssql\""],"doc":"<p>The database <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a> writes SQL for. It decides the placeholders, how a name is quoted,\nhow texts are matched and how dates are written:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>dialect</th>\n<th>placeholders</th>\n<th>a name</th>\n<th>the driver</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;postgres&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>&quot;name&quot;</code></td>\n<td><code>pg</code>: <code>client.query(sql, params)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;oracle&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>:1</code>, <code>:2</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>&quot;NAME&quot;</code></td>\n<td><code>oracledb</code>: <code>connection.execute(sql, params)</code>, by position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;sqlite&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>?</code></td>\n<td><code>&quot;name&quot;</code></td>\n<td><code>node:sqlite</code>, <code>better-sqlite3</code>: <code>db.prepare(sql).all(...params)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;mssql&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>@p1</code>, <code>@p2</code> ...</td>\n<td><code>[name]</code></td>\n<td><code>mssql</code>: <code>request.input(&#39;p1&#39;, params[0])</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { toSqlWhere } from '@simple-html/grid/sql';\n\ntoSqlWhere({ field: 'name', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Ann' }, { dialect: 'mssql', columns: { name: 'name' } });\n// { sql: '([name] IS NOT NULL AND LOWER([name]) COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2 LIKE @p1)', params: ['ann'] }</code></pre>\n","samples":["sql"],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"SqlFilter","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type SqlFilter = SqlFilterGroup | SqlFilterCondition"],"doc":"<p>A filter of the grid - the same shape as <code>FilterExpression</code> of <code>@simple-html/grid</code> (a test keeps them\none): a condition, or an AND / OR group of them. Its own name here, so the typings of this entry are one\nfile. See the filtering guide.</p>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"SqlFilterOperator","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type SqlFilterOperator = \"EQUAL\" | \"NOT_EQUAL_TO\" | \"LESS_THAN\" | \"LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO\" | \"GREATER_THAN\" | \"GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO\" | \"BETWEEN\" | \"CONTAINS\" | \"DOES_NOT_CONTAIN\" | \"BEGIN_WITH\" | \"END_WITH\" | \"IN\" | \"NOT_IN\" | \"IS_BLANK\" | \"IS_NOT_BLANK\""],"doc":"<p>the operators of a condition: <code>FilterOperator</code> of the grid</p>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]},{"name":"SqlParam","group":"@simple-html/grid/sql","kind":"Type Alias","signatures":["type SqlParam = string | number | boolean"],"doc":"<p>A value <a href=\"#api/sql.toSqlWhere\">toSqlWhere</a> binds as a parameter: a text, a number or a boolean. Dates are text or\nnumbers (see <a href=\"#api/sql.SqlDateStorage\">SqlDateStorage</a>), so every driver takes them as they are.</p>\n","samples":[],"memberGroups":[]}]}